APPENDIX A
\par
SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS: HOW DO WE KNOW WHAT'S IMPORTANT?
\par
A.0 INTRODUCTION
\par
Sensitivity analysis, as it is applied to risk assessment, is any systematic, common sense
\par
technique used to understand how risk estimates and, in particular, risk-based decisions, are dependent on
\par
variability and uncertainty in the factors contributing to risk. In short, sensitivity analysis identifies what
\par
is ``driving'' the risk estimates. It is used in both point estimate and probabilistic approaches to identify
\par
and rank important sources of variability as well as important sources of uncertainty. The quantitative
\par
information provided by sensitivity analysis is important for guiding the complexity of the analysis and
\par
communicating important results (see Chapter 6). As such, sensitivity analysis plays a central role in the
\par
tiered process for PRA (see Chapter 2). This Appendix focuses on a set of graphical and statistical
\par
techniques that can be used to determine which variables in the risk model contribute most to the
\par
variation in estimates of risk. This variation in risk could represent variability, uncertainty, or both,
\par
depending on the type of risk model and characterization of input variables.
\par
There is a wide array of analytical methods that may be referred to as sensitivity analysis, some of
\par
which are very simple and intuitive. For example, a risk assessor may have two comparable studies from
\par
which to estimate a reasonable maximum exposure (RME) for childhood soil ingestion. One approach to
\par
evaluating this uncertainty would be to calculate the corresponding RME risk twice, each time using a
\par
different plausible point estimate for soil ingestion rate. Similarly, in a probabilistic model, there may be
\par
uncertainty regarding the choice of a probability distribution. For example, lognormal and gamma
\par
distributions may be equally plausible for characterizing variability in an input variable. A simple
\par
exploratory approach would be to run separate Monte Carlo simulations with each distribution in order to
\par
determine the effect that this particular source of uncertainty may have on risk estimates within the RME
\par
range (90th to 99.9th percentile, see Chapter 1).
\par
Sensitivity analysis can also involve more complex mathematical and statistical techniques such
\par
as correlation and regression analysis to determine which factors in a risk model contribute most to the
\par
variance in the risk estimate. The complexity generally stems from the fact that multiple sources of
\par
variability and uncertainty are influencing a risk estimate at the same time, and sources may not act
\par
independently. An input variable contributes significantly to the output risk distribution if it is both
\par
highly variable and the variability propagates through the algebraic risk equation to the model output (i.e.,
\par
risk). Changes to the distribution of a variable with a high sensitivity could have a profound impact on
\par
the risk estimate, whereas even large changes to the distribution of a low sensitivity variable may have a
\par
minimal impact on the final result. Information from sensitivity analysis can be important when trying to
\par
determine where to focus additional resources. The choice of technique(s) should be determined by the
\par
information needs for risk management decision making.
\par
This appendix presents guidance on both practical decision making and theoretical concepts
\par
associated with the sensitivity analysis that are commonly applied in risk assessment. An overview of the
\par
type of information provided by sensitivity analysis is presented first, followed by guidance on how to
\par
decide what method to use in each of the tiers. A straightforward example of applications of Tier 1 and
\par
Tier 2 sensitivity analysis methods is shown, followed by a more detailed discussion of the theory and
\par
equations associated with the different methods.
\par
