DEFINITIONS FOR APPENDIX A
\par
Continuous Variables - A random variable that can assume any value within an interval of real numbers (e.g., body
\par
weight).
\par
Correlation - A quantitative expression of the statistical association between two variables; usually represented by the
\par
Pearson correlation coefficient for linear models, and the Spearman rank correlation coefficient (see below) for
\par
nonlinear models.
\par
Discrete Variables - A random variable that can assume any value within a finite set of values (e.g., number of visits to a
\par
site in one year) or at most a countably infinite set of values, meaning that you can count observations, but there is no
\par
defined upper limit. An example of countably infinite would be the number of dust particles in a volume of air (a
\par
Poisson distribution), whereas uncountably infinite would be the number of points in a line segment.
\par
Local Sensitivity Analysis - Evaluation of the model sensitivity at some nominal points within the range of values of input
\par
variable(s).
\par
Monte Carlo Analysis (MCA) or Monte Carlo Simulation - The process of repeatedly sampling from probability
\par
distributions to derive a distribution of outcomes. MCA is one of several techniques that may be used in PRA.
\par
Multiple Regression Analysis - A statistical method that describes the extent, direction, and strength of the relationship
\par
between several (usually continuous) independent variables (e.g., exposure duration, ingestion rate) and a single
\par
continuous dependent variable (e.g., risk).
\par
Nonparametric Tests - Statistical tests that do not require assumptions about the form of the population probability
\par
distribution.
\par
Range Sensitivity Analysis - Evaluation of the model sensitivity across the entire range of values of the input variable(s).
\par
Rank - If a set of values is sorted in ascending order (smallest to largest), the rank corresponds to the relative position of a
\par
number in the sequence. For example, the set \textbraceleft 7, 5, 9, 12\textbraceright{} when sorted gives the following sequence \textbraceleft 5, 7, 9, 12\textbraceright{}
\par
with ranks ranging from 1 to 4 (i.e., rank of 5 is 1, rank of 7 is 2, rank of 9 is 3, and rank of 12 is 4).
\par
Sensitivity Analysis - Sensitivity generally refers to the variation in output of a model with respect to changes in the values
\par
of the model's input(s). Sensitivity analysis attempts to provide a ranking of the model inputs based on their relative
\par
contributions to model output variability and uncertainty. Common metrics of sensitivity include:
\par
{$<$} Pearson Correlation Coefficient - A statistic r that measures the strength and direction of linear association
\par
between the values of two quantitative variables. The square of the coefficient (r2) is the fraction of the variance
\par
of one variable that is explained by least-squares regression on the other variable, also called the coefficient of
\par
determination..
\par
{$<$} Sensitivity Ratio - Ratio of the change in model output per unit change in an input variable; also called elasticity.
\par
{$<$} Sensitivity Score - A sensitivity ratio that is weighted by some characteristic of the input variable (e.g., variance,
\par
coefficient of variation, range).
\par
{$<$} Spearman Rank Order Correlation Coefficient - A ``distribution free'' or nonparametric statistic r that measures
\par
the strength and direction of association between the ranks of the values (not the values themselves) of two
\par
quantitative variables. See Pearson (above) for r2
\par
.
