\par
A.1.0 UTILITY OF SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS
\par
As highlighted in Exhibit A-2, sensitivity
\par
analysis can provide valuable information for both risk
\par
assessors and risk management decision makers
\par
throughout the tiered process for PRA. By
\par
highlighting important sources of variability and
\par
uncertainty in the risk assessment, sensitivity analysis
\par
is generally an important component of the overall
\par
uncertainty analysis. For example, methods that
\par
quantify parameter uncertainty and model uncertainty
\par
may yield different estimates of the RME risk. This
\par
information can be used to guide the tiered process by
\par
supporting decisions to conduct additional analyses or
\par
prioritize resource allocations for additional data
\par
collection efforts. Results of sensitivity analysis can
\par
also facilitate the risk communication process by
\par
focusing discussions on the important features of the
\par
risk assessment (e.g., constraints of available data,
\par
state of knowledge, significant scientific issues, and
\par
significant policy choices that were made when
\par
alternative interpretations of data existed).
\par
Decision Making with the Tiered Approach
\par
In general, the type of information provided by a sensitivity analysis will vary with each tier of a
\par
PRA. Table A-1 provides an overview of the methods that may be applied in each tier based on the type
\par
of information needed. In Tier 1, sensitivity analysis typically involves changing one or more input
\par
variables or assumptions and evaluating the corresponding changes in the risk estimates. Ideally, the
\par
results for Tier 1 would be useful in deciding which exposure pathways, variables, and assumptions are
\par
carried forward for further consideration in subsequent tiers of analysis. By identifying the variables that
\par
are most important in determining risk, one can also decide whether point estimates, rather than
\par
probability distribution functions (PDFs), can be used with little consequence to the model output. This
\par
information is important not only for designing 1-D MCA models of variability, but also for designing
\par
more complex analyses of uncertainty discussed in Appendix D (e.g., 2-D MCA models, geostatistical
\par
analysis, Bayesian analysis). Section A.2.2 provides an overview of the Tier 1 methods and some
\par
insights regarding their limitations. Methods associated with Monte Carlo simulations used in Tiers 2 and
\par
3 can take advantage of the ability to vary multiple inputs simultaneously and account for correlations.
\par
Sections A.2.3 and A.3 provide an overview of the sensitivity analysis methods that can be applied in a
\par
probabilistic analysis.
\par
