\par
Resource Allocation
\par
Decisions regarding allocation of future resources and data collection efforts to reduce lack of
\par
knowledge generally should take into consideration the most influential input factors in the model, and
\par
the cost of gaining new information about the factors. Sensitivity analysis is a key feature of determining
\par
the expected value of information (EVOI) (see Appendix D). Once a sensitivity analysis is used to
\par
identify an input variable as being important, the source of its variability generally should be determined.
\par
If an input factor has a significant uncertainty component, further research and/or data collection can be
\par
conducted to reduce this uncertainty. Reducing major sources of uncertainty, such as the most relevant
\par
probability model for variability or the parameter estimates for the model, will generally improve
\par
confidence in the model output, such as the estimated 95th percentile of the risk distribution. An input
\par
factor may contribute little to the variability in risk, but greatly to the uncertainty in risk (e.g., the
\par
concentration term). Likewise, a variable may contribute greatly to the variability in risk, but, because
\par
the data are from a well characterized population, the uncertainty is relatively low (e.g., adult tap water
\par
ingestion rate).
\par
An example of the output from a 2-D MCA of uncertainty and variability (see Appendix D) is
\par
shown in Figure A-1. Assume for this example that the decision makers choose the 95th percentile risk as
\par
the RME risk, and that a sensitivity analysis is run to identify and quantitatively rank the important
\par
source(s) of parameter uncertainty. The bar chart (top panel) in Figure A-1 indicates that the mean soil
\par
concentration contributes most to the uncertainty in the 95th percentile risk estimate. In addition, the
\par
mean exposure frequency is a greater source of uncertainty than the standard deviation exposure
\par
frequency. Since both the sample size and variance impact the magnitude of the confidence limits for an
\par
arithmetic mean soil concentration, one way to reduce the confidence limits (i.e., the uncertainty) would
\par
be to collect additional soil samples. As shown by the box-and-whisker plots (bottom panel) in
\par
Figure A-1, increasing the sample size (from n=25 to n=50) reduced the 90\% confidence limits for the
\par
95th percentile risk to below 1E-05, assuming the additional observations support the same estimate of the
\par
mean and standard deviation as the original sample.
\par
Although the uncertainty in a risk estimate can be reduced by further data collection if the
\par
sensitive input distribution represents uncertainty, this is not necessarily true for input distributions that
\par
represent variability. For example, variability in the distribution of body weights can be better
\par
characterized with additional data, but the coefficient of variation (i.e., standard deviation divided by the
\par
mean) will not in general be reduced.
\par
Risk Communication
\par
Even if additional data are not collected to reduce uncertainty, identifying the exposure factors
\par
that contribute most to risk or hazard may be useful for risk communication. For example, assume that
\par
the input for exposure frequency has the strongest effect on the risk estimate for a future recreational open
\par
space. Further examination of this exposure variable reveals that the wide spread (i.e., variance) of the
\par
PDF is a result of multiple users (e.g., mountain bikers, hikers, individuals who bring picnics, etc.) of the
\par
open space who may spend very different amounts of time recreating. As a result of this analysis, the
\par
decision makers and community may decide to focus remediation efforts on protecting the high-risk
\par
subpopulation that is expected to spend the most time in the open space.
\par
After determining which contaminants, media, and exposure pathways to carry into a PRA,
\par
numerical experiments generally should be performed to determine the sensitivity of the output to various
\par
distributions and parameter estimates that may be supported by the available information. Variables that
\par
Page A-7RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix A \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
do not strongly affect the risk estimates may be characterized with point estimates without significantly
\par
altering the risk estimates. This guidance document does not recommend a quantitative metric or rule of
\par
thumb for determining when a variable strongly affects the output; this would generally be determined on
\par
a case-by-case basis. A qualitative or quantitative analysis may be used depending on the complexity of
\par
the risk assessment at this point. For example, incidental ingestion of soil by children is often an
\par
influential factor in determining risk from soil, a factor recognized by risk assessors. This recognition is a
\par
de facto informal sensitivity analysis. An array of quantitative techniques is also available, ranging from
\par
something as simple as comparing the range of possible values (i.e., maximum-minimum) for each
\par
variable, to more complex statistical methods such as multiple regression analysis. Several of these
\par
methods are discussed in more detail in this appendix.
\par
Often, sufficient information is available to characterize a PDF for a minor variable without
\par
significant effort. This situation raises a question of whether the variable should be characterized with a
\par
point estimate or a PDF. The results of sensitivity analysis should be viewed as supplemental
\par
information, rather than an absolute rule for determining when to use a PDF. There are at least two issues
\par
to consider related to risk communication. First, the risk communication process may be facilitated by
\par
narrowing the focus of the evaluation to the key factors. More attention can be given to the discussion of
\par
key variables quantified by PDFs by describing the minor variables with point estimates. However, the
\par
decision to use a point estimate should be balanced by considering a second issue regarding perception
\par
and trust. There may be a concern that by reducing sources of variability to point estimates, there would
\par
be a reduction (however small) in the variability in risk, especially if multiple small sources of variability
\par
add up to a nonnegligible contribution. To address these concerns, it may be prudent to leave the PDFs in
\par
the calculations despite the results of a sensitivity analysis.
