A.2.0 COMMON METHODS OF SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS
\par
Of the numerous approaches to sensitivity
\par
analysis that are available (see Exhibit A-3), no single
\par
approach will serve as the best analysis for all
\par
modeling efforts. Often, it will make sense to apply
\par
multiple approaches. The best choice(s) for a
\par
particular situation will depend on a number of
\par
factors, including the nature and complexity of the
\par
model and the resources available. A brief description
\par
of the more common approaches is provided in this
\par
appendix. Sensitivity analysis need not be limited to
\par
the methods discussed in this guidance, which focuses
\par
on the more common approaches. A large body of
\par
scientific literature on various other methods is
\par
available (e.g., Iman et al., 1988, 1991; Morgan and
\par
Henrion, 1990; Saltelli and Marivort, 1990; Rose et
\par
al., 1991; Merz, Small, and Fischbeck, 1992;
\par
Shevenell and Hoffman 1993; Hamby, 1994; U.S.
\par
EPA, 1997). Any method used, however, generally
\par
should be documented clearly and concisely. This
\par
includes providing all information needed by a third
\par
party to repeat the procedure and corroborate the
\par
results. Relevant information might include the following: exposure pathways and equations; a table with
\par
the input variables with point estimates, probability distributions and parameters; and tables or graphs
\par
giving the results of the sensitivity analysis and description of the method used. A hypothetical example
\par
is presented in this appendix to illustrate how to apply and present the results of selected approaches to
\par
sensitivity analysis.
\par
