A.2.1.1 PERCENTAGE CONTRIBUTION OF EXPOSURE PATHWAYS TO TOTAL RISK
\par
For cancer and noncancer risk assessments central tendency exposure (CTE) and RME risk is
\par
typically calculated as the sum of risks from multiple exposure pathways. Risks may be dominated by
\par
one or two exposure pathways, which can be determined through a simple calculation as shown below.
\par
The relative contributions of exposure pathways are likely to differ between the CTE risk and RME risk.
\par
The point estimates in Table A-2 were applied to Equation A-2 to obtain CTE and RME point
\par
estimates of HI. Table A-3 gives the percent contributions of soil ingestion and tap water ingestion using
\par
Equations A-3 and A-4. Tap water ingestion contributes at least 90\% to HI, and the total HI is greater
\par
than 1.0 for both CTE and RME point estimates. If 1.0 is the level of concern for HI, and a decision was
\par
made to explore variability and uncertainty in a probabilistic analysis, this result might support
\par
prioritizing the evaluation of data and assumptions associated with the tap water ingestion pathway.
