A.2.1.2 INSPECTION OF RISK EQUATION
\par
For many Superfund risk assessments, risk equations can be characterized as relatively simple
\par
algebraic expressions involving addition, multiplication, and division of input variables. The term
\par
``product-quotient'' model is often applied to describe equations such as Equation A-1. For these risk
\par
equations, the input variables that are likely to contribute most to the variability or uncertainty in risk can
\par
be identified by inspection. In addition, inspection of the risk equation can help to identify which
\par
sensitivity analysis methods are unlikely to reveal the relative importance of the input variables. This
\par
concept is illustrated by comparing the results of the sensitivity ratio approach (Section A.2.1.3) with the
\par
Tier 2 approaches (Section A.2.2) applied to the hypothetical example in Section A.2.0.
\par
Some risk equations can be more complex, involving conditional probabilities, or expressions
\par
with exponents (e.g., y=x2, or y=exp(1- x)). In these cases, the Tier 1 sensitivity analysis methods may be
\par
effective and highlighting the variables that contribute most to the risk estimates.
\par
A.2.1.3 SENSITIVITY RATIO (SR)
\par
A method of sensitivity analysis applied in many different models in science, engineering, and
\par
economics is the Sensitivity Ratio (SR), otherwise know as the elasticity equation. The approach is easy
\par
to understand and apply. The ratio is equal to the percentage change in output (e.g., risk) divided by the
\par
percentage change in input for a specific input variable, as shown in Equation A-5.
