Risk estimates are considered most sensitive to input variables that yield the highest absolute value for
\par
SR. The basis for this equation can be understood by examining the fundamental concepts associated
\par
with partial derivatives (see Section A.3.2). In fact, SR is equivalent to the normalized partial derivative
\par
(see Equation A-12).
\par
Sensitivity ratios can generally be grouped into two categories---local SR and range SR. For the
\par
local SR method, an input variable is varied by a small amount, usually \textpm 5\% of the nominal (default)
\par
point estimate, and the corresponding change in the model output is observed. For the range sensitivity
\par
ratio method, an input variable is varied across the entire range (plausible minimum and maximum
\par
values). Usually, the results of local and range SR calculations are the same. When the results differ, the
\par
risk assessor can conclude that different exposure variables are driving risk near the high-end (i.e.,
\par
extreme tails of the risk distribution) than at the central tendency region.
