The following observations can be made for the four forms of the risk equation, based on one of the five
\par
cases described in Exhibit A-4:
\par
(1) Additive in Numerator
\par
{$<$} Case 2: SR values for variables in the numerator depend exclusively on the nominal point
\par
estimates for all variables in the numerator. The values are independent of the choice of percent
\par
change in the inputs ()).
\par
{$<$} Case 3: SR values for variables in the denominator depend exclusively on ), and are negative
\par
(i.e., inversely related to the output). Also, the lower the choice for ), the higher the resulting SR
\par
values. Therefore, SR is somewhat arbitrary, especially for the range SR approach since input
\par
variables may have different plausible minimum and maximum values.
\par
(2) Additive in Denominator
\par
{$<$} Case 1: SR values for variables in the numerator are always equal to 1.0. Since they are
\par
independent of the nominal values and ), there is no way to distinguish the relative contributions
\par
to the output.
\par
{$<$} Case 4: SR values for variables in the denominator are a function of both the nominal values of
\par
variables in the denominator and ).
\par
(3) Multiplicative in Numerator and (4) Multiplicative in Denominator
\par
{$<$} Case 1: SR values for variables in the numerator are always equal to 1.0. Since they are
\par
independent of the nominal values and ), there is no way to distinguish the relative contributions
\par
to the output.
\par
{$<$} Case 3: SR values for variables in the denominator depend exclusively on the ), and are negative
\par
(i.e., inversely related to the output). Also, the lower the choice for ), the higher the resulting SR
\par
values. Therefore, SR is somewhat arbitrary, especially for range SR since input variables may
\par
have different plausible minimum and maximum values.
\par
These generalized results highlight a major limitation in the use of the SR approach for obtaining
\par
information from sensitivity analysis. For simple exposure models in which the relationship between
\par
exposure and risk is linear (e.g., multiplicative), the ratio offers little information regarding the relative
\par
contributions of each input variable to the risk estimate. In many cases, all of the input variables will
\par
have the same constant, either equal to 1.0 (in the case of a single exposure pathway) or equal to the
\par
relative contributions of the exposure pathways. For more complex models that combine additive,
\par
multiplicative, and nonlinear relationships between inputs and outputs (e.g., environmental fate and
\par
transport models, pharmacokinetic models), the ratio is likely to be an effective screening tool for
\par
identifying potentially influential input variables and assumptions.
\par
Another difficulty with the SR approach is that it generally requires an assumption that the input
\par
variables are independent. Two variables may actually be positively correlated (e.g., high values of X1
\par
correspond with high values of X2) or negatively correlated (e.g., high values of X1 correspond with low
\par
values of X2). If input variables are correlated, holding the value for one variable fixed while allowing
\par
the other to vary may produce misleading results, especially with the range sensitivity ratio approach. For
\par
example, it may not be realistic to hold body weight fixed at a central tendency while allowing skin
\par
surface area to vary from the minimum to maximum values. An improvement over the sensitivity ratio
\par
approach would be to allow correlated input variables to vary simultaneously.
\par
