\par
A.2.2 TIER 2 APPROACHES
\par
Approaches for sensitivity analysis in Tier 2 of a PRA utilize the results of Monte Carlo
\par
simulations, which allows multiple input variables to vary simultaneously. The methods are relatively
\par
simple to perform with spreadsheets or commercial statistical software. The results are generally easy to
\par
communicate, although the details of the methodology are more complex than Tier 1 approaches. As
\par
given by Table A-1, goals for the sensitivity analysis in Tier 2 are the same as Tier 1:quantifying the
\par
relative contributions of the exposure pathways, identifying potential nonlinear relationships that may
\par
exist between input variables and the risk estimate, and rank ordering the relative contribution of
\par
exposure variables to variability or uncertainty in the risk estimate. In addition, since the output is a
\par
distribution, Tier 2 sensitivity analysis methods can also utilize graphical techniques to observe nonlinear
\par
relationships, as well as evaluate potential changes in relative importance of variables and assumptions
\par
for risks in the RME risk range. Methods are applied to the hypothetical example presented in
\par
Section A.2.0 in order to demonstrate the advantages over the Tier 1 methods.
\par
A.2.2.1 GRAPHICAL TECHNIQUES
\par
Simple scatter plots of the simulated input and output (e.g., risk vs. exposure frequency, or risk
\par
vs. arithmetic mean soil concentration) can be used to qualitatively and quantitatively evaluate influential
\par
variables. A ``tight'' best-fit line through the scatter plot, as indicated by the magnitude of the r2, suggests
\par
that a variable may significantly influence the variance in risk. Hypothetical scatter plots used to identify
\par
sensitive and insensitive variables are shown in Figure A-2. Another method for visualizing the
\par
relationship between all of the inputs and outputs is to generate a scatterplot matrix (Helsel and Hirsch,
\par
1992). This graphic shows both histograms and scatter plots for all variables on the same page.
\par
Figure A-3 illustrates scatter plots for the 1-D MCA simulations associated with the example
\par
from Section A.2.0. Based on the r2 values (i.e., coefficient of determination for simple linear regression
\par
analysis), the relationship between HI and I\_w is very strong (r2 = 0.47) while the relationship between HI
\par
and I\_s is very weak (r2 {$<$} 0.01), suggesting that HI is more sensitive to variability in I\_w than I\_s.
\par
A.2.2.2 CORRELATION COEFFICIENTS
\par
The variance in a risk estimate from a Monte Carlo simulation is due to the variance in the
\par
probability distributions used in the risk equation. It is commonly said that a Monte Carlo model
\par
propagates sources of variability simultaneously in a risk equation. Numerous statistical techniques,
\par
known collectively as correlation analysis and regression analysis, can be applied to a linear equation to
\par
estimate the relative change in the output of a Monte Carlo simulation based on changes in the input
\par
variables. Examples of metrics of sensitivity include the simple correlation coefficient, the rank
\par
correlation coefficient, and a variety of coefficients from multiple regression techniques. The underlying
\par
assumptions associated with these approaches are discussed in greater detail in Section A.3. As explained
\par
in Section A.3.3.1, correlation coefficients and regression coefficients are based on different
\par
interpretations of the input variables, but they can be calculated with similar equations.
\par
When the output distribution is compared with the distribution for one input variable at a time,
\par
two of the more common approaches are to calculate the Pearson product moment correlation and the
\par
Spearman rank correlation. Correlation analysis with one input variable will generally yield reasonable
\par
results when the input variables are sampled independently in a Monte Carlo simulation. Some statistical
\par
packages offer the correlation coefficient as an index of sensitivity, so it is important to identify which
\par
Page A-21RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix A \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
coefficient is being calculated. Crystal Ball\textregistered{} and @Risk can be used to calculate the Spearman rank
\par
correlation, which tends to be more robust when the relationships between inputs and outputs are
\par
nonlinear. If the relationships are linear, such as with the product quotient models presented in this
\par
appendix, the two metrics of correlation will yield similar rankings of input variables. Rank correlation
\par
coefficients shown in Crystal Ball\textregistered{} and @Risk are calculated by the standard method provided in most
\par
statistics texts. Crystal Ball\textregistered{} also indicates that sensitivity can be determined as contribution to variance.
\par
This is not the relative partial sum of squares techniques discussed in Section A.3.3.2 (Equation A-19).
\par
Instead, Crystal Ball\textregistered{} calculates the contribution to the variance by squaring the rank correlation
\par
coefficients and normalizing them to 100\%. Many other commonly used commercial software packages
\par
will perform Spearman rank correlation. Pearson product moment correlations (r) can be calculated in
\par
Microsoft Excel using the trendline feature in a scatter plot chart, or by using the function Correl(X array,
\par
Y array), where X array corresponds with the Monte Carlo simulation of an input variable, and Y array
\par
corresponds with the output of the simulation.
\par
Figure A-4 illustrates results of the correlation analysis for the 1-D MCA simulations associated
\par
with the example from Section A.2.0. The graphics were generated using Crystal Ball\textregistered{} 2000. The results
\par
are summarized in Table A-9. If the model output variable (e.g., HI) and input variable are highly
\par
correlated, it means that the output is sensitive to that input variable. By squaring the coefficient, the
\par
results can be expressed in terms of the percentage contribution to variance in the output (Figure A-4, top
\par
panel). To determine if the correlation is positive or negative, the correlation coefficient should not be
\par
squared (Figure A-4, bottom panel). For risk equations, in general, variables in the numerator of the
\par
equation (ingestion rate, absorption fraction, exposure frequency, etc.) will tend to be positively
\par
correlated with risk, while variables in the denominator (body weight) will tend to be negatively
\par
correlated with risk. The greater the absolute value of the correlation coefficient, the stronger the
\par
relationship.
