The Tier 1 sensitivity scores and Tier 2 correlation coefficients yield similar results, suggesting
\par
that, if sufficient information is available to estimate the coefficient of variation in the input variables, a
\par
Tier 1 analysis can help to focus efforts on the variables that contribute most to the variance in risk. By
\par
contrast, the Tier 1 sensitivity ratio approach suggested that EF was the most influential variable, when in
\par
fact it contributes less than 5\% to the variance in the HI. These results suggest that Tier 1 sensitivity
\par
ratios are best applied to identify dominant exposure pathways, rather than dominant exposure variables
\par
in the risk equation.
