A.2.2.3 FOCUSING ON THE RME RANGE OF THE RISK DISTRIBUTION
\par
Monte Carlo methods can also be used to determine the sensitivity over a subset of the output
\par
distribution, such as the RME range (i.e., 90th to 99.9th percentiles). For some exposure models, the
\par
relative contribution of exposure variables may be different for the high-end exposed individuals than for
\par
the entire range of exposures. The general strategy for exploring sensitivity over subsets of risk estimates
\par
is to first sort the distribution of simulated output values in ascending (or descending) order, and then
\par
apply a sensitivity analysis to the subset of interest (e.g., {$>$} 90th percentile). For the hypothetical example
\par
presented in this appendix, there was no difference in the relative rankings of inputs in the RME range.
\par
A.2.2.4 INSPECTION
\par
With Monte Carlo analysis, the probability distributions assumed for the various input variables
\par
are used to generate a sample of a large number of points. Statistical methods are applied to this sample
\par
to evaluate the influence of the inputs on the model output. A number of different ``indices'' of sensitivity
\par
can be derived from the simulated sample to quantify the influence of the inputs and identify the key
\par
contributors. Most of these are based on an assumption that the model output Y varies in a monotonic,
\par
linear fashion with respect to various input variables (X1, X2, etc.). For example, an estimate of average
\par
daily intake (mg/kg-day) from multiple exposure pathways is linear with respect to the intake from each
\par
pathway. Since most risk models are linear with respect to the input variables, the output distribution
\par
(particularly its upper percentiles) tends to be dictated by the input variables with the largest coefficient of
\par
variation (CV), or the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean. For example, Equation A-9 represents a
\par
simple expression for intake rate as a function of random variables X1 and X2 :
\par
Y X X 1 2 = + Equation A-9
\par
where X1 and X2 may represent dietary intake associated with prey species 1 and 2, respectively. If the
\par
same probability distribution was used to characterize X1 and X2, such as a lognormal distribution with an
\par
arithmetic mean of 100 and standard deviation of 50 (i.e., CV=50/100=0.5), each variable would
\par
contribute equally to variance in Y. If, however, X2 was characterized by a lognormal distribution with
\par
an arithmetic mean of 100 and standard deviation of 200 (i.e., CV=200/100=2.0), we would expect Y to
\par
be more sensitive to X2. That is, X2 would be a greater contributor to variance in Y.
\par
While the coefficient of variation may be a useful screening tool to develop a sense of the relative
\par
contributions of the different input variables, a common exception is the case when X1 and X2 have
\par
different scales. For example, Equation A-10 is an extension of Equation A-9:
