A.3.0 ADVANCED CONCEPTS IN SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS
\par
This section provides additional information on the underlying principles of sensitivity analysis,
\par
although it is not a comprehensive summary and is not intended to substitute for the numerous statistical
\par
texts and journal articles on sensitivity analysis. Section A.3.1 begins with a general framework for
\par
relating model output to model input. Section A.3.2 explains the sensitivity ratio approach and highlights
\par
some of its limitations. Section A.3.3 reviews some of the metrics reported by the commercial software
\par
that report results of sensitivity analysis following Monte Carlo simulations (e.g., Crystal Ball\textregistered{}
\par
, @Risk).
\par
While statistical software for MCA provides convenient metrics for quantifying and ranking these
\par
sources, it is strongly recommended that risk assessors and risk managers develop an understanding of the
\par
underlying principles associated with these metrics.
\par
A.3.1 RELATING THE CHANGE IN RISK TO THE CHANGE IN INPUT VARIABLE X
\par
For purposes of discussion, let Y denote a model output (e.g., risk) and suppose that it depends on
\par
the input variable X. In general, a risk assessment model may use any number of inputs; however, for
\par
purposes of illustrating concepts, it is convenient to restrict this discussion to one variable. The model
\par
relates the output Y to values of X (i.e., x0, x1, , xn) based on the function expressed as Y=F(x). The
\par
sensitivity of Y to X can be interpreted as the slope of the tangent to the response surface F(X) at any point
\par
xi. This two-dimensional surface can be a simple straight line, or it may be very complex with changing
\par
slopes as shown in Figure A-5a. The sensitivity, therefore, may depend on both the value of X and the
\par
amount of the change )x about that point. This concept can be extended to two input variables, X1 and
\par
X2, where the response is characterized by a three-dimensional surface. The shape may be a simple plane
\par
(Figure A-5c) or it may be very complex with many ``hills'' and ``valleys'' depending on the defining
\par
function F(X1, X2). In a typical risk assessment with ten or more variables, the surface can be very
\par
complex, but the shape is likely to be dominated by a small subset of the input variables.
\par
A sensitivity analysis based on a relatively small deviation about the point may be referred to as a
\par
local sensitivity analysis, while a large deviation may be referred to as range sensitivity analysis. In either
\par
case, the objective is to evaluate the sensitivity at some nominal point (X1*, X2
\par
*) such as the point defined
\par
by the mean or median of X1 and X2. At any point, the sensitivity of the model output, Y* = F(X1*, X2
\par
*),
\par
to one of the inputs (X1 or X2), is represented by the rate of change in Y per unit change in X. This is the
\par
slope of the surface at that nominal point in the direction of X and is expressed as M Y/M Xi, the partial
\par
derivative of Y with respect to X.
