A.3.2 NORMALIZED PARTIAL DERIVATIVE
\par
Classical sensitivity analysis methods use estimates of the partial derivatives of the model output
\par
with respect to each variable. For the purpose of evaluating the relative influence of the various input
\par
variables on the model output at a single point, the normalized partial derivative provides a useful
\par
index.
\par
If the input variables are all discrete and take on a small number of values, then it is possible to
\par
evaluate the influence of the various input variables at each of the points defined by considering all
\par
possible combinations of the inputs. Then the influence can be evaluated for each input by computing
\par
normalized partial derivatives at each point. This approach is limited to situations where the number of
\par
inputs as well as the number of possible values for each input is relatively small; otherwise, the number of
\par
combinations to be evaluated will be unmanageable. Furthermore, when evaluating the influence at
\par
different points on the input-output surface simultaneously, it is important to take into account the
\par
probability associated with each of those points. For example, the fact that a particular input has a large
\par
influence on the model output at a particular point would be discounted if the probability associated with
\par
that particular point is very low.
\par
A similar approach may be used to analyze inputs that are continuous variables if a few points
\par
representing the range of values are selected. For example, low, medium (or nominal), and high values
\par
may be selected for each of the continuous input variables and then the relative influence of each of the
\par
input variables can be computed as in the case of discrete inputs. One limitation of this approach,
\par
however, is that the continuous nature of the inputs makes it impossible to calculate an exact probability
\par
for each of the points. Generally, in a PRA, many if not all of the inputs will be random variables
\par
described by probability distributions and it will be necessary to quantify the influence of each input, Xi,
\par
over the entire range of Xi.
\par
An estimate of the partial derivative can be obtained by incrementing Xi by a small amount, say
\par
) Xi while keeping the other inputs fixed and reevaluating the model output Y. The resulting change in Y
\par
divided by )Xi will approximate M Y/M Xi at the nominal point.
