\par
total variation due to random variation (see discussion of random error above). This is an important
\par
consideration when trying to interpret the results of regression analyses that incorporate data
\par
averaged over different spatial scales (e.g., regression of PbB on soil lead concentrations taken at the
\par
city block level may give an inflated R2 value if the sampling data are averaged over a larger spatial
\par
scale, such as the census tract level).
\par
A multiple regression analysis can also be performed to estimate the regression coefficients (see
\par
Appendix A.3.3). Each coefficient essentially represents an ``average'' value of the partial derivative
\par
across the entire distribution of the input. The regression coefficient, like the partial derivative, depends
\par
on the units of measurement so, as in the case of the partial derivative, it must be normalized. This can be
\par
accomplished by multiplying the regression coefficient by the ratio of estimated standard deviations sy/sx.
\par
A convenient way to carry out a sensitivity analysis is to perform a stepwise regression analysis.
\par
Some statistical software packages (e.g., SAS, SPSS) offer a variety of different approaches for this;
\par
however, in general, they can be classified into two general categories: forward selection and backward
\par
elimination. In the forward selection, the inputs are added to the model one by one in the order of their
\par
contribution. In the backward elimination, all of the inputs are used in the model initially and then they
\par
are dropped one by one, eliminating the least important input at each step. A true stepwise procedure is a
\par
variation on the forward selection approach where an input can drop out again once it has been selected
\par
into the model if at some point other inputs enter the model that account for the same information.
\par
A.3.3.2 RELATIVE PARTIAL SUM OF SQUARES (RPSS)
\par
The relative partial sum of squares (RPSS) measures the sensitivity of the model output to each
\par
of the input variables by partitioning the variance in the output attributable to each variable using multiple
\par
regression techniques (Rose et al., 1991). The RPSS is presented as a percentage reflecting the proportion
\par
of influence a given variable has on risk. The results of RPSS are intuitive and generally easy to
\par
understand.
\par
Briefly, the RPSS represents the percentage of the total sum of squares attributable to each of the
\par
variables. To calculate RPSS for variable Vi, the difference between the regression sum of squares (RSS)
\par
for the full model and the regression sum of squares for the model with Vi missing (RSS-i) is divided by
\par
the total sum of squares (TSS) and expressed as a percentage:
