A.3.3.3 SPEARMAN'S RANK CORRELATION COEFFICIENT (RHO)
\par
The validity of using indices such as regression coefficients, correlation coefficients, and partial
\par
correlation coefficients depends on the assumptions of the underlying linear model being met. If there is
\par
any doubt that a data set satisfies the model assumptions, a nonparametric measure of correlation based on
\par
the rank orders of the inputs and associated outputs can be used. The Spearman Rank correlation
\par
coefficient is a nonparametric statistic; it measures an association between variables that are either count
\par
data or data measured on an ordinal scale, as opposed to data measured on an interval or ratio scale. An
\par
example of an ordinal scale would be the ranking of sites based on their relative mean soil concentrations.
\par
For example, if there are four categories of soil contaminant concentrations, sites with the highest
\par
concentrations may receive a rank of 1 while sites with lowest concentrations may receive a rank of 4.
\par
Ordinal scales indicate relative positions in an ordered series, not ``how much'' of a difference exists
\par
between successive positions on a scale.
\par
To calculate the Spearman rank correlation coefficient, assign a rank to each of the input
\par
variables (Xj) and output variables (Yk). For each ranked pair (Xj, Yk), calculate the difference, d, between
\par
the ranks. For example, if the first observation for variable X has a ranking of 5 (relative to all of the
\par
observations of X), and the corresponding value of Y has a ranking of 3 (relative to all of the observations
\par
of Y), the difference (d) is equal to 5--3=2. Spearman rho (rs) is calculated as:
