APPENDIX B
\par
SELECTION AND FITTING OF DISTRIBUTIONS
\par
B.0 INTRODUCTION
\par
An important step in Monte Carlo analysis (MCA) is to select the most appropriate distributions
\par
to represent the factors that have a strong influence on the risk estimates. This step in the development of
\par
a Monte Carlo model can be very challenging and resource intensive.
\par
L Specifying probability distributions for all of the input variables and
\par
parameters in a probabilistic risk assessment (PRA) will generally not be
\par
necessary.
\par
If the sensitivity analysis indicates that a particular input variable does not contribute significantly
\par
to the overall variability and uncertainty, then this variable may be represented as a point estimate. As
\par
discussed in Appendix A, however, different approaches to sensitivity analysis may be applied
\par
throughout the tiered approach (e.g., sensitivity ratios, correlation analysis), and the ability to reliably
\par
identify variables as being minor or major can vary. Sometimes it can be helpful to develop probability
\par
distributions based on preliminary information that is available from Tier 1 in order to explore alternative
\par
options for characterizing variability and uncertainty. Likewise, sometimes the important ``risk drivers''
\par
are apparent, and resources can be allocated to fully characterize the variability and uncertainty in those
\par
input variables. Therefore, the process of selecting and fitting distributions may also be viewed as a
\par
tiered approach. This appendix reviews the methods available to select and fit distributions and provides
\par
guidance on the process for determining appropriate choices depending on the information needed from
\par
the assessment and the information available to define the input variables.
\par
In PRA, there are some important distinctions in the terminology used to describe probability
\par
distributions. A probability density function (PDF), sometimes referred to as a probability model,
\par
characterizes the probability of each value occurring from a range of possible values. Probability
\par
distributions may be used to characterize variability (PDFv) or uncertainty (PDFu). One advantage of
\par
using a PDFv and PDFu is that distributions represent a large set of data values in a compact way (Law
\par
and Kelton, 1991). For example, a lognormal distribution provides a good fit to a large data set of tap
\par
water ingestion rates (n=5,600) among children ages 1 to 11 years (Roseberry and Burmaster, 1992).
\par
Therefore, the distribution type (lognormal) and associated parameters (mean and standard deviation)
\par
fully describes the PDFv for intake rates, from which other statistics of interest can be calculated (e.g.,
\par
median, and 95th percentile). Reducing a complex exposure model to a series of representative and well-
\par
fitting distributions can facilitate both the quantitative analysis and the communication of the modeling
\par
methodology. Alternatively, a PDFu may be specified to characterize parameter uncertainty. For
\par
example, the sample mean ( x
\par
) is generally an uncertain estimate of the population mean (:) due to
\par
measurement error, small sample sizes, and other issues regarding representativeness (see Section B.3.1).
\par
A PDFu can be used to represent the distribution of possible values for the true, but unknown parameter.
\par
Understanding whether uncertainty or variability is being represented by a PDF is critical to determining
\par
how the distribution and parameters should be specified and used in a PRA.
\par
