DEFINITIONS FOR APPENDIX B
\par
Bayesian Analysis - Statistical analysis that describes the probability of an event as the degree of belief or confidence
\par
that a person has, given some state of knowledge, that the event will occur. Bayesian Monte Carlo combines a
\par
prior probability distribution and a likelihood function to yield a posterior distribution (see Appendix D for
\par
examples). Also called subjective view of probability, in contrast to the frequentist view of probability.
\par
Bin - Regarding a histogram or frequency distribution, an interval within the range of a random variable for which a
\par
count (or percentage) of the observations is made. The number of bins for a histogram is determined on a case-
\par
by-case basis. In general, equal interval widths are used for each bin; however, in some cases (e.g., Chi-square
\par
test), individual bin widths are calculated so as to divide the distribution into intervals of equal probability.
\par
Countably Infinite - Used to describe some discrete random variables, this term refers to a set of numbers that can be
\par
counted with integers (e.g., one, two, three) and that has no upper limit. Examples include the number of tosses
\par
required for a coin to show a head---we can count each toss, but it is possible that at least one more toss is
\par
needed. The number of dust particles in a volume of air is another example. Countably finite implies there is an
\par
upper limit (e.g., days of work per year).
\par
Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) - Obtained by integrating the PDF, gives the cumulative probability of
\par
occurrence for a random independent variable. Each value c of the function is the probability that a random
\par
observation x will be less than or equal to c.
\par
Empirical Distribution Function (EDF) -The EDF, also called the empirical CDF (ECDF), is based on the frequency
\par
distribution of observed values for a random variable. It is a stepwise distribution function calculated directly
\par
from the sample, in which each data point is assigned an equal probability.
\par
Frequency Distribution or Histogram - A graphic (plot) summarizing the frequency of the values observed or
\par
measured from a population. It conveys the range of values and the count (or proportion of the sample) that was
\par
observed across that range.
\par
Goodness-of-Fit (GoF) Test - A method for examining how well (or poorly) a sample of data can be described by a
\par
hypothesized probability distribution for the population. Generally involves an hypothesis test in which the null
\par
hypothesis H0 is that a random variable X follows a specific probability distribution F0. That is, H0: F=F0 and
\par
Ha: F \dots{} F0.
\par
Independence - Two events A and B are independent if whether or not A occurs does not change the probability that B
\par
occurs. Likewise, knowing the value of B does not affect the value of A. Input variables, X and Y, are
\par
independent if the probability of any paired values (X, Y) is equal to the probability of X multiplied by the
\par
probability of Y. In mathematical terms, X and Y are independent if f(X, Y)=f(X) x f(Y). Independence is not
\par
synonymous with correlation. If X and Y are independent, then their correlation is zero, Cor(X, Y)= 0. But, the
\par
converse is not always true. There may be a nonlinear relationship between X and Y that yields Cor(X, Y)=0, but
\par
the variables are highly dependent.
\par
Nonparametric Method - Also called a distribution-free method, a procedure for making statistical inferences without
\par
assuming that the population distribution fits a theoretical distribution such as normal or lognormal. Common
\par
examples are the Spearman rank correlation, (see Appendix A) and the bootstrap-t approach..
\par
Parameter - In PRA, a parameter is a quantity that characterizes the probability distribution of a random variable. For
\par
example, a normal probability distribution may be defined by two parameters (e.g., arithmetic mean and standard
\par
deviation).
\par
Parametric Distribution - A theoretical distribution specified by a distribution type and one or more parameters.
\par
Examples include the normal, Poisson, and beta distributions.
