B.2.0 PRELIMINARY SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS
\par
Selecting and fitting probability distributions for all of the input variables can be resource
\par
intensive and is generally unnecessary. Ideally, a subset of variables could be identified that contribute to
\par
most of the variability and uncertainty in a risk estimate. Sensitivity analysis can play an important role
\par
in helping to identify and quantitatively rank the major exposure pathways and variables. Since the
\par
information obtained from a sensitivity analysis may vary, depending on the approach(es) used and the
\par
information available to characterize the input variables, risk assessors should understand inherent
\par
limitations of each approach. A variety of approaches that are common for Tier 1 and 2 analyses are
\par
described and applied to a hypothetical example in Appendix A.
\par
In a Tier 1 assessment, sensitivity analysis is typically limited to exploring the effect of
\par
alternative point estimates on the risk estimate. These methods can be helpful if additional information
\par
regarding the variability in the input variables is incorporated into the analysis (i.e., sensitivity scores).
\par
Alternatively, a reasonable approach is to specify preliminary probability distributions for one or more
\par
inputs in order to maximize the advantages of probabilistic methods. The difference between a
\par
preliminary distribution and a subsequent distribution reflects the level of effort invested in characterizing
\par
variability and uncertainty. If a robust data set is available in Tier 1 to define point estimates, then a
\par
preliminary distribution may, in fact, fully characterize variability with very high confidence. For other
\par
variables, summary statistics, rather than sample data, may be available, allowing for estimates of central
\par
tendency or plausible ranges. The use of preliminary distributions reflects an effort to employ more
\par
robust sensitivity analysis techniques without expending the effort and resources that might otherwise be
\par
applied to a PRA in Tier 2. The goal of the preliminary analysis would not be necessarily to evaluate
\par
risks and/or develop a PRG; rather, the focus would be on identifying input variables that may be
\par
important to explore more fully. Preliminary sensitivity analysis can provide insight into the importance
\par
of selecting among alternative probability distributions and exposure scenarios.
\par
One-dimensional Monte Carlo simulations with preliminary (or screening-level) distributions can
\par
be run prior to engaging in a more involved process of selecting and fitting distributions. The
\par
distributions can be selected based on knowledge regarding the mechanisms that result in variability, and
\par
information already available for determining point estimates (e.g., summary statistics, U.S. EPA
\par
guidance, etc.). Table B-1 provides examples of preliminary distributions that might be selected based on
\par
the type of information available, sometimes referred to as the state of knowledge. In many cases, the
\par
distribution is intended to estimate the plausible bounds of a variable, while requiring no additional data
\par
collection effort. For example, given estimates of a lower bound [min], upper bound [max], and the
\par
assumption that each value is equally likely, a uniform distribution would be used to represent variability
\par
(or parameter uncertainty). If no mechanistic basis for selecting a distribution exists, then the preliminary
\par
distribution would be chosen based on the available information. For example, given the estimates of the
\par
arithmetic mean [:] and a percentile value [a] for a random variable, an exponential distribution might be
\par
recommended with 8=1/:.
\par
Guidance on matching the choice of the distribution to the state of knowledge is extended to a
\par
more diverse array of scenarios later in this appendix (see Table B-4).
