B.3.0 WHAT DOES THE DISTRIBUTION REPRESENT?
\par
Distributions may be specified to characterize variability or uncertainty. Often, a Monte Carlo
\par
simulation of variability will focus on describing differences between individuals in a population (i.e.,
\par
inter-individual variability). In this case, the goal is to select a distribution that is representative of the
\par
target population---the set of all receptors that are potentially at risk. There may be uncertainty that the
\par
choice of PDFv reflects variability in the target population. In general, risk assessors should fully
\par
disclose uncertainties in the PDFv, especially because the use of a distribution instead of a point estimate
\par
may inappropriately suggest that there is a greater state of knowledge. Following the tiered process (see
\par
Chapter 2, Figure 2-1), there are multiple opportunities to consider consequences of alternative modeling
\par
approaches early in the process of developing a probabilistic model. The importance of relating the
\par
distribution to the target population, clearly distinguishing between variability and uncertainty, and
\par
evaluating data representativeness is emphasized in Sections B.3.1, B.3.2 and B.4.
