B.3.1 CONCEPTS OF POPULATION AND SAMPLING
\par
The distinction between a target population, a sampled population, and a statistical population
\par
should be considered carefully when evaluating information for use in both Tier 1 and Tier 2 of a PRA.
\par
The target population is often considered to be the ``population of concern''. A risk assessor is often
\par
interested in quantifying specific attributes of the population (e.g., exposure duration, exposure
\par
frequency, etc.). A sampled population is the set of receptors available for selection and measurement.
\par
For purposes of this appendix/guidance, the sampled population may be the target population or it may be
\par
a different population that is thought to be representative of the target population. For purposes of this
\par
guidance, a statistical population is an approximation of the target population based on information
\par
obtained from the sampled population.
\par
Distributions are generated from representative sample populations to make inferences about the
\par
target population. Ideally, a sampled population should be a subset of a target population and should be
\par
selected for measurement to provide accurate and representative information about the exposure factor
\par
being studied. However, defining representative samples is a matter of interpretation.
