\par
B.3.2 CONSIDERING VARIABILITY AND UNCERTAINTY IN SELECTING AND FITTING DISTRIBUTIONS
\par
Multiple probability distributions may be used to describe variability and uncertainty in an input
\par
variable. For example, a normal probability distribution may be selected to characterize variability in
\par
body weight, whereas a uniform distribution may selected to characterize uncertainty in the estimate of
\par
the arithmetic mean of the normal distribution. The appropriate interpretation and analysis of data for an
\par
exposure variable will depend on whether one is specifying a PDFv or PDFu. Figure B-1 outlines one
\par
useful process for selecting distributions for variability, whereas Figure B-2 (three pages) outlines a
\par
useful process for quantifying both model and parameter uncertainty.
\par
Variability generally refers to observed differences attributable to true heterogeneity or diversity
\par
in a population (U.S. EPA, 1997b). Variability results from natural random processes. Inter-individual
\par
variability may stem from environmental, lifestyle, and genetic differences. Examples include human
\par
physiological variation (e.g., natural variation in body weight, height, breathing rates, drinking water
\par
intake rates), changes in weather, variation in soil types, and differences in contaminant concentrations in
\par
the environment. Intra-individual variability may reflect age-specific changes (e.g., body weight and
\par
height). Variability is not reducible by further measurement or study. A PDF for variability can usually
\par
be obtained by fitting a distribution to the sample measurements.
\par
Sources of Uncertainty
\par
Uncertainty generally refers to the lack of knowledge about specific factors, parameters, or
\par
models (U.S. EPA, 1997b). Although uncertainty in exposure and risk assessment may be unavoidable
\par
due to the necessary simplification of real-world processes, it generally can be reduced by further
\par
measurement and study. Parameter uncertainty may stem in part from measurement errors, sampling
\par
errors, or other systematic errors in the collection and aggregation of data. Model uncertainty may reflect
\par
the simplification of a complex process, a mis-specification of the exposure model structure, a misuse or
\par
misapplication of an exposure model, use of the wrong distributional model, and the use of surrogate data
\par
or variables. Scenario uncertainty may reflect uncertainty in an exposure model, such as the relevance of
\par
specific exposure pathways to the target population. A conceptual exposure model can be used to provide
\par
direction in specifying a probability distribution for uncertainty. For example, the concentration term in a
\par
Superfund risk assessment typically represents the long-term average concentration to which a receptor is
\par
exposed (see Chapter 5). An uncertainty distribution for the concentration term could be developed in
\par
part from ideas about the statistical uncertainty of estimating the long-term average from a small sample,
\par
and the assumption of random movement of the receptors within a defined exposure unit.
\par
Probability Distributions and Model Uncertainty
\par
This appendix primarily focuses on methods for quantifying uncertainty associated with both the
\par
selection of a variability distribution, and estimating parameters of a distribution. A probability
\par
distribution can be referred to as a type of model in the sense that it is an approximation, and often a
\par
simplified representation of variability or uncertainty that combines both data and judgment. A broader
\par
use of the term model refers to a representation of a chemical, physical, or biological process. In risk
\par
assessment, many different models have been developed, with varying objectives, major defining and
\par
limiting components, and theoretical basis. Figure B-2b provides a general process for exploring model
\par
uncertainty of this type. This figure reflects the concepts and spirit of the Agency Guidance for
\par
Conducting External Peer Review of Environmental Regulatory Modeling (U.S. EPA, 1994). In general,
\par
EPA regional risk assessors should be consulted in order to determine the types of exposure and risk
\par
models that may be plausible for quantifying exposure at a particular site.
\par
Page B-12RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
Parameter Uncertainty
\par
Quantifying parameter uncertainty in a probabilistic model typically requires judgment (see
\par
Appendix C). When data are uncertain due to, for example, small sample sizes or questionable
\par
representativeness (Section B.3.1), Monte Carlo simulation can be a useful tool for demonstrating the
\par
effect of the uncertainty on the risk estimates. It is most important to model uncertainty when the
\par
sensitive input variables are uncertain. Uncertainty can be quantified in both the point estimate approach
\par
(e.g., a range of possible central tendency exposure values) or a probabilistic approach (e.g., a range of
\par
possible values for the arithmetic mean of a distribution). While a quantitative uncertainty analysis may
\par
complicate a risk management decision by suggesting that risk estimates are highly uncertain, this
\par
information can be helpful by focusing additional efforts towards collecting data and reducing uncertainty
\par
in the most sensitive input variables. Likewise, if an estimated risk is below a regulatory level of concern,
\par
even after quantifying highly uncertain inputs to the exposure model, the risk manager may be more
\par
confident in a decision. As emphasized in Figures B-2a, B-2b, and B-2c, risk assessors should generally
\par
refrain from setting ad hoc probabilities to different candidate distributions in a single Monte Carlo
\par
simulation. Instead, this guidance strongly recommends exploring model or parameter uncertainty by
\par
running a separate simulation with each candidate model. For example, rather than randomly assigning a
\par
beta distribution or a lognormal distribution to an exposure variable for each iteration of a simulation,
\par
separate simulations should be run with the candidate probability distributions. Similarly, if a range of
\par
temporal or spatial scales is plausible for quantifying exposure, multiple simulations should be designed
\par
to demonstrate the importance of these assumptions on the risk estimates.
\par
Uncertainty in parameter estimates may be characterized using a variety of methods. Similar to a
\par
PDF for variability, a PDF for parameter uncertainty may be represented by a probability distribution
\par
with a unique set of parameters. Sometimes the distribution for uncertainty can be specified by knowing
\par
(or assuming) a distribution for variability. For example, if X is a normally distributed random variable,
\par
the Student's t distribution and the Chi-square (P2) distribution can be used to develop PDFu's for random
\par
measurement error uncertainty in the sample mean and variance, respectively. The PDFu for both the
\par
Student's t and Chi-square distributions is determined by the sample size (n). If a PDFu cannot be
\par
determined from the PDF for variability, or assumptions regarding the underlying distribution for
\par
variability are not supportable, nonparametric or ``distribution free'' techniques may be used (e.g.,
\par
bootstrapping). Both parametric and nonparametric techniques may yield confidence intervals for
\par
estimates of population parameters.
\par
B.4.0 DO DATA EXIST TO SELECT DISTRIBUTIONS?
\par
Developing site-specific PDFs for every exposure assumption (or toxicity value, in the case of
\par
ecological risk) can be time and resource intensive, and in many cases, may not add value to the risk
\par
management decision. For those exposure variables that do exert a significant influence on risk, a PDF
\par
may be developed from site-specific data, data sets available in the open literature (e.g., EPA's Exposure
\par
Factors Handbook, U.S. EPA 1997a), or from existing PDFs in the literature (e.g., Oregon DEQ, 1998).
\par
At Superfund sites, perhaps the most common exposure variable that will be described by site-
\par
specific data will be the media concentration term. The sample (i.e., collection of empirical
\par
measurements) will most often be used to estimate either a point estimate of uncertainty (e.g., an upper
\par
confidence limit for the arithmetic mean concentration---the 95\% UCL), or a distribution that
\par
characterizes the full distribution of uncertainty in the mean. Exposure variables such as ingestion rates,
\par
exposure duration, and exposure frequency will most likely be derived from existing PDFs or data sets in
\par
the open literature. The Agency supports the development PDFs that may be generally applicable to
\par
Page B-13RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
different sites (e.g., body weight, water intake, and exposure duration) (U.S. EPA, 1999b, 2001). Until
\par
final recommendations of PDFs are available for the more generic exposure variables, PDFs for exposure
\par
variables that lack adequate site-specific data will typically be selected from: (1) existing PDFs; (2) data
\par
on the entire U.S. population; or (3) data on subsets of the U.S. population that most closely represent the
\par
target population at a site. If risks to a sensitive subpopulation, such as young children, elderly adults,
\par
ethnic groups, or subsistence fishermen, are a concern at a site, then existing PDFs or data sets that best
\par
characterize these subpopulations would be preferable to national distributions based on the entire U.S.
\par
population. If adequate site-specific data are available to characterize any of the exposure variables,
\par
distributions can be fit to those data.
\par
Uncertainty Associated with Sample Size
\par
An appropriate question to consider when evaluating data sets for use in exposure and risk
\par
assessment is, ``What sample size is sufficient?'' Generally, the larger the sample size (n), the greater
\par
one's confidence in the choice of a probability distribution and the corresponding parameter estimates.
\par
Conversely, for small n, Goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests (see Section B.6.2) will often fail to reject many of
\par
the hypothesized PDFs. In general, there is no rule of thumb for the minimum sample size needed to
\par
specify a distribution for variability or uncertainty. Increasing a sample size may be an appropriate option
\par
to consider when evaluating risk management strategies to reduce uncertainty.
\par
Statistical sampling, in general, is important to consider when estimating parameters of a
\par
probability distribution. One rule of thumb is that the parameters that reflect the central tendency of a
\par
distribution (e.g., arithmetic mean, median, mode) can be estimated with greater confidence than
\par
parameters that reflect the extremes of the distribution (e.g., 95th percentile). When deciding on
\par
appropriate truncation limits (minimum and maximum values), it is unlikely that the statistical sample
\par
actually includes the plausible bounds. See Section B.5.7 for more detailed guidance on specifying
\par
truncation limits for probability distributions.
\par
B.4.1 WHAT ARE REPRESENTATIVE DATA?
\par
The question, ``What is a representative sample?'', is important to address when selecting and
\par
fitting distributions to data. Many of the factors that may determine representativeness (e.g., sample size
\par
and the method of selecting the target, and sample population (Section B.3.1)) are relevant to both point
\par
estimate and PRA. EPA's Guidance for Data Usability in Risk Assessment, Part A (U.S. EPA, 1992)
\par
describes representativeness for risk assessment as the extent to which data define the true risk to human
\par
health and the environment.
\par
The goal of representativeness is easy to understand. However, evaluating data to determine if
\par
they are representative is more difficult, especially if the problem and decision objectives have not been
\par
clearly defined.
\par
The importance of representativeness also varies with the level of complexity of the assessment.
\par
If a screening level assessment is desired, for example, to determine if concentrations exceed a health
\par
protective exposure level, then representativeness may not be as important as health protectiveness.
\par
However, if a complete baseline risk assessment is planned, the risk assessor should generally consider
\par
the value added by more complex analyses (e.g., site-specific data collection, sensitivity analysis, and
\par
exposure modeling). A tiered approach for making these decisions for a PRA is presented in Chapter 2,
\par
and examples of more complex analyses are presented in Appendix D. In addition, the Agency (U.S.
\par
Page B-14RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
EPA, 1999a) summarizes the advantages and weaknesses of proposed checklists for risk assessors to
\par
evaluate representativeness of exposure factors data.
\par
For purposes of this guidance, a surrogate study is one conducted on a sampled population that is
\par
similar to, but not a subset of, the target population. When using surrogate data, the risk assessor should
\par
generally exercise judgment about the representativeness of the data to the target population. For
\par
example, the distribution of body weights of deer mice from two independent samples from similar
\par
ecosystems may differ depending on the age structure, proportion of males and females, and the time of
\par
year that the samples were obtained. When in doubt about which study results to use in defining a
\par
probability distribution, one option is to develop a distribution and calculate risks with each sample
\par
independently, and compare the results. This approach can be a simple, but effective type of uncertainty
\par
analysis. At a minimum, uncertainties associated with the use of surrogate studies should be discussed in
\par
the assessment.
\par
In many cases, the surrogate population shares common attributes with the target population, but
\par
is not truly representative. The risk assessor should then determine the importance of the discrepancies
\par
and whether adjustments can be made to reduce those differences. There are a wide variety of methods
\par
that can be used to account for such discrepancies, depending on the available information. Summary
\par
statistics (e.g., as presented by the Exposure Factors Handbook, U.S. EPA, 1997a) can be used to
\par
estimate linear characteristics of the target population from the sample population. For example, if the
\par
mean, standard deviation, and various percentiles of the sample population are known, then the mean or
\par
proportion exceeding a fixed threshold can be calculated using a simple weighted average. Adjustment
\par
options are more numerous if the risk assessor has access to the raw data. Adjustments for raw data
\par
include: weighted averages, weighted proportions, transformations, and grouping of the data based on the
\par
available information (e.g., empirical data, and professional judgment).
\par
In most cases, the evaluation of data representativeness will necessarily involve judgment. The
\par
workplan should generally include a description of the data, the basis for the selection of each
\par
distribution, and the method used to estimate parameters (see Chapter 2). Empirical data (i.e.,
\par
observations) are typically used to select distributions and derive parameter estimates. However, it may
\par
be necessary to use expert judgment or elicitation in cases where the quality or quantity of available data
\par
are found to be inadequate.
\par
B.4.2 THE ROLE OF EXPERT JUDGMENT
\par
Expert judgment refers to inferential opinion of a specialist or group of specialists within an area
\par
of their expertise. When there is uncertainty associated with an input variable, such as a data gap, expert
\par
judgment may be appropriate for obtaining distributions. Note that distributions elicited from experts
\par
reflect individual or group inferences, rather than empirical evidence. Distributions based on expert
\par
judgment can serve as Bayesian priors in a decision-analytic framework. The distributions and Bayesian
\par
priors can be modified as new empirical data become available. There is a rich literature base regarding
\par
the protocol for conducting expert elicitations and using the results to support decisions (Morgan and
\par
Henrion, 1990). Elicitation of expert judgment has been used to obtain distributions for risk assessments
\par
(Morgan and Henrion, 1990; Hora, 1992; U.S. EPA, 1997b) and for developing air quality standards
\par
(U.S. EPA, 1982).
\par
Bayesian analysis is a statistical approach that allows the current state of knowledge, expressed as
\par
a probability distribution, to be formally combined with new data to reach an updated information state.
\par
In PRA, Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis (Bayesian MCA) can be used to determine the reduction in
