B.5.0 FITTING DISTRIBUTIONS TO DATA
\par
Sometimes more than one probability
\par
distribution may adequately characterize
\par
variability or uncertainty. The choice of a
\par
distribution should be based on the available data
\par
and on knowledge of the mechanisms or
\par
processes that result in variability. In general, the
\par
preferred choice is the simplest probability model
\par
that adequately characterizes variability or
\par
uncertainty and is consistent with the mechanism
\par
underlying the data. For example, a log-logistic
\par
distribution would not necessarily be selected
\par
over a 2-parameter lognormal distribution simply
\par
because it was ranked higher in a GoF test by a
\par
statistical software package. Some distributions (e.g., normal, lognormal) are well known among risk
\par
assessors. The statistical properties for these distributions are well understood and the formal descriptions
\par
can often be brief.
\par
Important factors to consider in selecting a PDF are described in Exhibit B-3. An initial step in
\par
selecting a distribution should be to determine if the random variable is discrete or continuous.
\par
Continuous variables take any value over one or more intervals and generally represent measurements
\par
(e.g., height, weight, concentration). For a continuous variable, a mathematical function generally
\par
describes the probability for each value across an interval. Discrete variables take either a finite or
\par
countably infinite number of values. Unique probabilities are assigned to each value of a discrete
\par
variable. The number of rainfall events in a month is an example of a discrete random variable, whereas
\par
the amount of rainfall is a continuous variable. Similarly, the number of fish meals per month is a
\par
discrete variable, whereas the average size (mass) of a fish meal is continuous.
\par
Another important consideration is whether there are plausible bounds or limits for a variable.
\par
For example, it is highly unlikely that an American adult will weigh less than 30 kg or more than 180 kg.
\par
Most exposure variables may assume any nonnegative value within a plausible range. Therefore,
\par
distributions will generally be truncated at a minimum of zero (or higher), or a probability distribution
\par
that is theoretically bounded at a nonzero value may be specified (see Table B-3). A more detailed
\par
discussion of factors to consider in selecting a PDF and specifying parameter values is provided below.
\par
