\par
B.5.1 CONSIDERING THE UNDERLYING MECHANISM
\par
There may be mechanistic reasons depending on known physical or biological processes that
\par
dictate the shape of the distribution. For example, normal distributions result from processes that sum
\par
random variables whereas lognormal distributions result from multiplication of random variables. A
\par
Poisson distribution is used to characterize the number of independent and randomly distributed events in
\par
a unit of time or space. An exponential distribution would describe the inter-arrival times of independent
\par
and randomly distributed events occurring at a constant rate. If, instead, the elapsed time until arrival of
\par
the kth event is of interest, then the appropriate probability distribution would be the gamma distribution
\par
(Morgan and Henrion, 1990).
\par
L In all cases, it is incumbent on the risk assessor to explain clearly and fully the
\par
reasoning underlying the choice of a distribution for a given exposure
\par
variable---primarily from a mechanistic standpoint if possible.
\par
Table B-2 lists some of the probability distributions that may commonly be used in PRA. This is
\par
not an exhaustive list, and the scientific literature contains numerous examples with alternative
\par
distributions. Where practicable, a mechanistic basis is presented for the choice of the distribution. For
\par
some distributions, such as beta, triangular, and uniform, a mechanistic basis is not offered because it is
\par
unlikely that a chemical or biological process will yield a random variable with that particular shape.
\par
Nevertheless, such distributions may be appropriate for use in PRA because they reflect the extent of
\par
information that is available to characterize a specific random variable. Preliminary distributions are
\par
discussed in Section B.2.0 and Table B-4. Because many of the distributions given in Table B-2 can
\par
assume flexible shapes, they offer practical choices for characterizing variability.
\par
Table B-2 also illustrates probability distributions (both PDFs and CDFs) commonly used in
\par
PRA. While intuitively appealing, identifying a mechanistic basis for a distribution can be difficult for
\par
many exposure variables; however, it may be relatively apparent that the variable is bounded by a
\par
minimum (e.g., ingestion rate \$ 0 mg/day) and a maximum (e.g., absorption fraction \# 100\%), or that the
\par
relevant chance mechanism results in a discrete distribution rather than a continuous distribution, as
\par
described above.
\par
For each distribution, one or more examples with different parameter estimates are given to
\par
demonstrate the flexibility in the shape of the PDF. In addition to the descriptions of the distributions in
\par
Tables B-2, Table B-3 provides a summary of the parameters and theoretical bounds that define the PDFs.
\par
For a further discussion of characteristics of PDFs see Thompson, 1999. Figures (a-h) immediately
\par
following Table B-2 present examples of PDFs and the corresponding CDFs for distributions commonly
\par
used in PRA.
