VARIATIONS OF THE EDF
\par
Linearized - Linearly interpolates between two
\par
observations, yielding a linearized cumulative
\par
distribution pattern.
\par
Extended - In addition to linearizing (see above),
\par
adds lower and upper bounds based on expert
\par
judgment.
\par
Mixed Exponential - Adds an exponential upper
\par
and/or lower tail to the EDF.
\par
B. 5.2 EMPIRICAL DISTRIBUTION FUNCTIONS (EDFS)
\par
In some cases, an empirical distribution function (EDF) may be preferred over fitting the data set
\par
to a hypothesized distribution. EDFs, also called empirical cumulative distribution functions (ECDF),
\par
provide a way to use the data itself to define the distribution of the relevant variable. Briefly, an EDF for
\par
a random variable is described by a step function based on the frequency distribution of observed values.
\par
An EDF for a continuous random variable may be linearized by interpolating between levels of the
\par
various bins in a frequency distribution. The CDF for a linearized EDF appears as a line, rather than
\par
steps. Example B-3 at the end of this Appendix illustrates an EDF, linearized EDF, and beta distribution
\par
("1=0.63, "2=2.85, rescaled to min=0, max=364) fit to percentile data for soil ingestion rates in children
\par
(Stanek and Calabrese, 1995). A plausible range (i.e., minimum and maximum values) was imposed on
\par
the data set for this example.
\par
EDFs provide a complete representation
\par
of the data with no loss of information. They do
\par
not depend on the assumptions associated with
\par
estimating parameters for theoretical probability
\par
models. EDFs are designed to provide direct
\par
information about the shape of the distribution,
\par
which reveals skewness, multimodality, and
\par
other features of the data set. However, EDFs
\par
may not adequately represent the tails of a
\par
distribution due to limitations in data
\par
acquisition. In the simplest case, an EDF is
\par
constrained to the extremes of the data set. This
\par
may be an unreasonable restriction if limiting
\par
the EDF to the smallest and largest sample
\par
values is likely to greatly underestimate the
\par
distributional tails. If this is an important source
\par
of uncertainty, the risk assessor may choose to extend the tails of the distribution to plausible bounds or to
\par
describe the tails with another distribution (see Exhibit B-4). For example, an exponential distribution
\par
may be used to extend the tails based on the last 5\% of the data. This method is based on extreme value
\par
theory, and the observation that extreme values for many continuous, unbounded distributions follow an
\par
exponential distribution (Bratley et al., 1987). As with other probability models, uncertainty in the
\par
plausible bounds of an EDF may be reduced by obtaining additional information.
\par
Advantages and disadvantages of using EDFs in PRA are discussed in detail in the Report of the
\par
Workshop on Selecting Input Distributions for Probabilistic Assessments (U.S. EPA, 1999a).
\par
B.5.3 GRAPHICAL METHODS FOR SELECTING PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTIONS
\par
Graphical methods can provide valuable insights and generally should be used in conjunction
\par
with exploratory data analysis. Examples of graphical methods are frequency distributions (i.e.,
\par
histograms), stem-and-leaf plots, dot plots, line plots for discrete distributions, box-and-whisker plots, and
\par
scatter plots (Tukey, 1977; Conover, 1980; Morgan and Henrion, 1990).
\par
L Graphical methods are invaluable for exploring a data set to understand the
\par
characteristics of the underlying population.
\par
