B.5.5 DEALING WITH CORRELATIONS AMONG VARIABLES OR PARAMETERS
\par
Correlations between exposure variables or between parameters of the probability distribution
\par
may be important components of a probabilistic model. Correlation is a measure of association between
\par
two quantitative random variables. Two random variables may either be positively or negatively
\par
correlated. A positive correlation exists between two variables if the value of X1 increases as the value of
\par
X2 increases. For example, higher hand dust lead levels have been associated with higher pediatric blood
\par
lead levels (Charney et al., 1980). A negative correlation exists between two variables if the value of X1
\par
increases as the value of X2 decreases. For example, studies suggest the ingestion of soil and dust
\par
particles increases as particle size decreases (Calabrese et al., 1996).
\par
A first step in identifying correlations is to assess the possible physical and statistical
\par
relationships that exist between variables. In an ecological risk assessment (ERA), for example, the
\par
largest surf scoter (diving duck) does not consume the least amount of food, nor does the smallest surf
\par
scoter consume the greatest amount of food. Random sampling of body weight and ingestion rate as
\par
separate parameters, however, allows for these two possibilities. Neglecting a correlation between two
\par
variables may restrict (underestimate) the tails of the ecological Hazard Quotient (HQ) for each chemical
\par
of concern (COC), which are frequently the areas of the distribution of most interest.
\par
The degree to which correlations affect the output of a risk model depends on: (1) the strength of
\par
correlations between the two variables, and (2) the contribution of the correlated variables to overall
\par
variance in the output (Cullen and Frey, 1999). Therefore, it is useful to conduct a preliminary sensitivity
\par
analysis to assess the impact of alternative correlation assumptions on the model output. If the impact is
\par
significant, correlations should be identified and accounted for in the PRA.
\par
There are several approaches to account for dependencies in MCA including: (1) modifying the
\par
model to include the correlation; and (2) simulating dependence between variables for sample generation
\par
(Cullen and Frey, 1999). Modifying the model is preferred as simulation techniques cannot capture the
\par
full complexity between model inputs. However, when this is not possible, dependencies between
\par
variables can be simulated and approximated by correlation coefficients and bivariate normal
\par
distributions.
\par
Correlation coefficients are a numerical measure of the strength and direction of the relationship
\par
between two variables. Sample correlation coefficients measure the linear relationship between variables.
\par
However, if two variables are from different probability distributions, it is unlikely that they are linearly
\par
related. Consequently, simulation software programs such as Crystal Ball\textregistered{} and @Risk can be used to
\par
calculate and employ the nonparametric statistic, Spearman's Rank Correlation Coefficients (Rho) in
\par
simulating correlation between inputs. Rank Correlation Coefficients measure the linear dependence not
\par
of the data values themselves, but of the rank value of the data. The ranks indicate relative positions in an
\par
ordered series, not the quantitative differences between the positions. The disadvantage of losing
\par
information by using the rank values (rather than the actual values) is offset by the ability to correlate
\par
random variables from different distribution types (See Appendix A).
\par
Exhibit B-8 gives an example of a straightforward approach to specifying a rank correlation
\par
between two input variables in a one-dimensional Monte Carlo analysis (1-D MCA) for variability. A
\par
range of correlations is explored as a form of uncertainty analysis on the distribution of intakes given a
\par
fish advisory of 7.0 :g/day for a chemical.
