\par
Correlations may also be specified for parameters of a probability distribution. This is an
\par
important concept when designing a two-dimensional Monte Carlo analysis (2-D MCA) in which
\par
parameters of the same PDFv might be otherwise be described by independent PDFu's. A common
\par
approach for correlating two parameters is to specify a bivariate normal distribution (Nelsen, 1986, 1987;
\par
Brainard and Burmaster, 1992). A bivariate normal distribution allows for the distribution of one variable
\par
to be sampled conditional on the other. This is a special case of a joint distribution in which both x and y
\par
are random variables and normally distributed (as the conditional distribution of x or of y is always
\par
normal) (Wonnacott and Wonnacott, 1981). Example B-4 further explains bivariate normal distributions
\par
and demonstrates this approach applied to coefficients of a simple linear regression model that relates
\par
contaminant concentrations in soil and dust.
\par
The results of correlation analysis should be interpreted with caution. Two variables may be
\par
associated due to: (1) a dependency between the two variables; (2) chance (two independent variables
\par
appear dependent due to chance in the sampling procedure); and (3) variables not included in the analysis
\par
(lurking variables) are affecting the two variables being analyzed. Likewise, a low correlation measure
\par
does not necessarily mean the two variables are independent. As a lurking variable may cause the
\par
appearance of an association between the two independent variables, it may also mask the association
\par
between two dependent variables.
\par
L Correlation describes a degree of mathematical association, not a causal
\par
relationship between the two variables.
\par
Efforts to extrapolate or predict correlations outside the range of observed values should also be
\par
done with caution. For example, there may be a strong linear relationship between age and height in
\par
children; however, it would be inappropriate to apply this correlation to adults. Additional caution is
\par
needed when correlating more than two factors at a time. In general, because of the complexity of
\par
specifying a valid covariance matrix when correlating more than two factors at a time, risk assessors may
\par
need to consult a statistician to avoid generating misleading risk estimates.
\par
B.5.6 CENSORED DATA
\par
In order to define the exposure point concentration, estimates of summary statistics representative
\par
of the entire distribution of data are needed (Helsel and Hirsch, 1992). Censored data complicate the
\par
process of selecting and fitting PDFs and estimating parameter estimates. A censored data set is a data set
\par
for which measurements above or below a certain threshold are not available. Left censored data occurs
\par
frequently at Superfund sites, where samples for a number of chemicals are often below the reporting
\par
limit. A censored datum (often denoted by ND) commonly represents a value of half of the laboratory
\par
reporting limit.
\par
Three general methods for estimating summary statistics for left censored data sets include:
\par
(1) simple substitution; (2) distributional methods; and (3) robust methods (Helsel and Hirsch, 1992).
\par
These methods may be evaluated based on the root mean squared error (RMSE) estimate, a measure of
\par
the difference between the sample statistic (e.g., the sample mean, ) and the true population parameter x
\par
(e.g., population mean, \textmu ).
\par
