Methods which yield estimates closer to the true parameter value have lower bias, higher precision, and
\par
lower RMSEs.
\par
Simple Substitution Methods
\par
Simple substitution methods entail substituting values equal to or lower than the reporting limit in
\par
the data set. These surrogate values are then included in the calculation of the summary statistics and in
\par
determining the distributional shape of the data set. Although this method is frequently used, it is
\par
important to understand its limitations; depending on the surrogate value used (e.g., half the reporting
\par
limit) the simple substitution method may yield biased parameter estimates (e.g., low estimates of the
\par
mean) and may yield misleading distributional shapes. Studies such as those reported by Gilliom and
\par
Helsel (1986) have determined, in terms of the RMSE, that simple substitution methods perform more
\par
poorly than the distributional and robust methods described below.
\par
Distributional Methods
\par
With distributional methods, the entire data set is assumed to follow a theoretical distribution
\par
(e.g., normal distribution). Assuming a theoretical distribution, MLE and probability plotting (p-plot)
\par
methods provide summary statistics that best match the reported values of the data and the percentage of
\par
samples below the threshold value. If the data fit the theoretical distribution exactly, or if the sample size
\par
is large, both MLE and p-plots are unbiased methods. Often, however, the sample size is small and the
\par
distribution deviates from a theoretical distribution. In this case, the MLE and p-plot methods may yield
\par
biased and imprecise methods (Hesel and Hirsch, 1992).
\par
Robust Methods
\par
With robust methods, a theoretical distribution is needed. A theoretical distribution is fit to the
\par
data above the detection limit by MLE or p-plot methods. Based on this assumed PDF, the value of the
\par
data points below the detection limit are extrapolated and used in the summary statistics calculation.
\par
Unlike the simple substitution method, these extrapolated values are not estimates for the data points;
\par
rather, they are only used jointly to calculate summary statistics (Hesel and Hirsch, 1992). The method is
\par
considered robust as it uses the actual values of the sample data, rather than the distribution above the
\par
detection limit.
\par
B.5.7 TRUNCATION
\par
Truncation refers to imposing a minimum and/or maximum value on a probability distribution.
\par
The main purpose of truncation is to constrain the sample space to a set of ``plausible values''. For
\par
example, a probability distribution for adult body weight might be truncated at a minimum value of 30 kg
\par
and a maximum value of 180 kg in order to avoid the occasional selection of an unlikely value (e.g., 5 or
\par
500 kg). Given the subjectiveness involved in selecting truncation limits, such choices should clearly be
\par
made with caution, and involvement of stakeholders who may be aware of site-specific circumstances.
\par
Page B-29RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
For example, there may well be individuals who weigh more than 180 kg and less than 30 kg. The
\par
purpose for truncating the tails of a distribution is to confine each risk estimate of a Monte Carlo
\par
simulation to a combination of plausible input values. The advantage of truncating unbounded
\par
probability distributions in PRA is that central tendency and high-end risk estimates will not be biased by
\par
unrealistic values. The disadvantage is that the original parameter estimates of the nontruncated
\par
distribution are altered by constraining the sample space. The bias in the parameter estimates increases as
\par
the interval between the minimum and maximum truncation limit is reduced. For example, a normal
\par
distribution with an arithmetic mean of 100 may be fit to a data set; imposing a truncation limit of
\par
300 may result in a truncated normal distribution with an arithmetic mean of 85. The relationship
\par
between the truncated and nontruncated parameter estimates can be determined analytically (Johnson et
\par
al., 1995) or approximated using Monte Carlo simulations under both truncated and nontruncated
\par
scenarios.
\par
