\par
gamma, Weibull). Truncation is generally less important when a PDF is used to characterize uncertainty
\par
in a parameter estimate (e.g., arithmetic mean), since distributions for uncertainty are often bounded by
\par
definition (e.g., triangular, uniform). Bounded continuous distributions, such as the beta distribution or
\par
empirical distribution (see Section B.5.2) are not subject to the parameter bias of truncation, although
\par
plausible minimum and maximum values must still be identified.
\par
Identifying appropriate truncation limits that reflect ``plausible bounds'' for an exposure variable
\par
will often require judgment. Given that most data sets represent statistical samples of the target
\par
population, it is unlikely that the minimum and maximum observed values represent the true minimum
\par
and maximum values for the population. However, there may be physiological or physical factors that
\par
can aid in setting plausible truncation limits. For example, the maximum bioavailability of chemicals in
\par
the gastrointestinal (GI) tract is 100\%. Similarly, the solubility of chemicals in aquatic environments
\par
