\par
(accounting for effects of temperature) will generally be less than the chemical solubility in water free of
\par
particulates.
\par
In general, sensitivity analysis can be used to determine if truncation limits are an important
\par
source of parameter uncertainty in risk estimates. For exposure variables in the numerator of the risk
\par
equation, the maximum truncation limit is of greatest concern. For exposure variables in the denominator
\par
of the risk equation, the minimum truncation limit is of greatest concern. Details regarding the fit of the
\par
tails of the probability distribution and the effect of truncation on the parameter estimates should
\par
generally be included in the workplan.
