B.6.0 ASSESSING QUALITY OF THE FIT
\par
The quality of the fit of a distribution may be evaluated in several ways. Standard statistical
\par
approaches are available to test the fit of a theoretical distribution to a data set (i.e., GoF tests). In
\par
addition, alternative choices for distribution shapes and plausible bounds might be explored as a form of
\par
sensitivity analysis. Together with graphical exploration (Section B.5.3), this information may be useful
\par
when deciding whether or not to incorporate a specific type of distribution for an exposure variable into a
\par
PRA.
\par
L GoF tests are one tool among several to assess the quality of a distribution.
\par
Although GoF testing is a necessary part of distribution fitting, and tests are readily available with
\par
commercial software, it is less important than mechanistic considerations or graphical data exploration for
\par
choosing a candidate distribution. Examples of GoF tests are discussed below, and cautions regarding
\par
GoF are outlined in Section B.6.3.
\par
B.6.1 WHAT IS A GOODNESS-OF-FIT TEST?
\par
Goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests are formal statistical tests of the hypothesis that the data represent an
\par
independent sample from an assumed distribution. These tests involve a comparison between the actual
\par
data and the theoretical distribution under consideration.
\par
In statistical hypothesis testing the null hypothesis (H0) is assumed to be true unless it can be
\par
proven otherwise. The ``evidence'' upon which we base a decision to reject or not to reject H0 is a random
\par
sample. Typically, we seek to reject H0 in favor of Ha. For example, with the two sample t-test, the null
\par
hypothesis is that the means of two populations are equal (not different) and the alternative is that they are
\par
different. This is expressed as:
