B.6.2 WHAT ARE SOME COMMON GOODNESS-OF-FIT TECHNIQUES?
\par
The following GoF tests can also be found in most general statistical and spreadsheet software.
\par
Both Crystal Ball\textregistered{} and @Risk software present the results of chi-square, K-S, and Anderson-Darling tests
\par
in their fitting routines.
\par
Shapiro-Wilk Test
\par
The most widely used GoF test in risk assessment is the Shapiro-Wilk test for normality (Gilbert,
\par
1987). This simple hypothesis test can determine whether or not a small data set (n \# 50) is normally
\par
distributed. The test can also be run on log-transformed data to assess whether the data are lognormally
\par
distributed. D'Agostino's test may be used for samples sizes larger than those accommodated by the
\par
Shapiro-Wilk test (i.e., n {$>$} 50) (d'Agostino and Stephens, 1986). In addition, Royston (1982) developed
\par
an extension of the Shapiro-Wilk test for n as large as 2000 (Gilbert, 1987).
\par
Probability Plot Correlation Coefficient Test
\par
The correlation coefficient r (or the coefficient of determination, r2) between the data and the
\par
z-scores of a normal probability plot (Filliben, 1975; Helsel and Hirsch, 1992) is similar to the W statistic
\par
Page B-32RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
of the Shapiro-Wilk test. A detailed comparison of the Shapiro-Wilk test and the product correlation
\par
coefficient test is given by Filliben (1975) and d'Agostino and Stephens (1986). Helsel and Hirsch
\par
(1992) summarize critical r* values derived by Looney and Gulledge (1985) for the probability plot
\par
correlation coefficient test.
\par
Chi-Square Test
\par
The chi-square test is a general test that may be used to test any distribution (continuous or
\par
discrete), and for data that are ordinal (e.g., categories such as high/medium/low). Chi-square is a
\par
measure of the normalized difference between the square of the observed and expected frequencies. For
\par
example, by constructing a frequency distribution of the data with k adjacent bins, j=1...k, the number of
\par
data points in the jth bin can be compared with the expected number of data points according to the
\par
hypothesized distribution. Note that in the case of continuous, unbounded distributions (e.g., normal), the
\par
first and last intervals may include [- 4, a1] or [ak, + 4] (Law and Kelton, 1991). The chi-square test is
\par
very sensitive to the chosen number and interval width of bins---different conclusions can be reached
\par
depending on how the intervals are specified. Strategies for selecting bins (e.g., setting interval widths
\par
such that there are no fewer than 5 data points expected per bin) are given in the statistical literature
\par
(d'Agostino and Stephens, 1986; Law and Kelton, 1991). The test statistic is compared with a value of
\par
the chi-square distribution with (k - r - 1) degrees of freedom, where k is the number of sample values and
\par
r is the number of parameters of the hypothesized distribution. As described in Section B.6.1, in general,
\par
higher p-values suggest better fits.
\par
Kolmogorov-Smirnov (K-S) Test
\par
The K-S test is a nonparametric test that compares the maximum absolute difference between the
\par
step-wise empirical CDF and the theoretical CDF. Because the maximum discrepancy is compared with
\par
the test statistic, K-S is sometimes referred to as a supremum test (Cullen and Frey, 1999). In general,
\par
lower values of the test statistic indicate a closer fit. The K-S test is most sensitive around the median of
\par
a distribution, and, hence, it is of little use for regulatory purposes when the tails of distributions are most
\par
generally of concern (U. S. EPA, 1999a). Although it does not require grouping data into bins like the
\par
chi-square test, critical values for the K-S test depend on whether or not the parameters of the
\par
hypothesized distribution are estimated from the data set (Gilbert, 1987; Law and Kelton, 1991). The
\par
Lilliefors test was developed to surmount this problem when the hypothesized distribution is normal or
\par
lognormal (Gilbert, 1987).
\par
Anderson Darling Test
\par
The Anderson-Darling test assesses GoF in the tails (rather than the mid-ranges) of a PDF using a
\par
weighted average of the squared differences between the observed cumulative densities. The Anderson-
\par
Darling test is sometimes referred to as the quadratic test (Cullen and Frey, 1999). The test statistic
\par
should be modified based on sample size prior to comparison with the critical value. Like the K-S test, in
\par
general, lower values of the test statistic indicate a closer fit (i.e., if the adjusted test statistic is greater
\par
than the modified critical value for a specified ", the hypothesized distribution is rejected). The
\par
Anderson-Darling test may be particularly useful because it places more emphasis on fitting the tails of
\par
the distribution.
\par
