\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
B.6.3 CAUTIONS REGARDING GOODNESS-OF-FIT TESTS
\par
There are many statistical software programs that will run GoF tests against a long list of
\par
candidate distributions. It is tempting to use the computer to make the choice of distribution based on a
\par
test statistic. However, GoF tests have low statistical power and often provide acceptable fits to multiple
\par
distributions. Thus, GoF tests are better used for rejecting poorly fitting distributions than for ranking
\par
good fits. In addition, for many distributions, GoF statistics lack critical values when the parameters are
\par
unknown (i.e., estimated from the data). In practice, this limitation is often discounted and the critical
\par
values are interpreted as a semi-quantitative measure of the fit. It is most appropriate to form an idea of
\par
the candidate distributions based on some well reasoned assumptions about the nature of the process that
\par
led to the distribution, and then to apply a GoF test to ascertain the fit (U.S. EPA, 1999a). Whenever
\par
possible, mechanistic and process (i.e., phenomenologic) considerations should inform the risk assessor's
\par
choice of a particular distribution rather than the results of a comparison of GoF tests (Ott, 1995). In
\par
addition, the value of graphical evaluations of the fit cannot be overstated.
\par
B.6.4 ACCURACY OF THE TAILS OF THE DISTRIBUTION
\par
The tails of a distribution (e.g., {$<$} 5th and {$>$} 95th percentiles) for an input variable are often of
\par
greatest interest when characterizing variability in risk. Distributions fit to data may not characterize the
\par
tails of the distribution in a way that represents the target population. In general, the importance of
\par
uncertainty in the fit of the tails of particular distributions should be determined on a site-specific basis.
\par
For exposure variables in the numerator of the risk equation, the upper tail is of greatest concern. For
\par
exposure variables in the denominator of the risk equation, the lower tail is of greatest concern.
\par
The tails of the input PDFs generally have a significant influence on the tails of the risk
\par
distribution, especially for those variables that are ranked highest in a sensitivity analysis. Different
\par
distributions may share the same mean and variance, but assume very different shapes. Experiments with
\par
Monte Carlo simulations have demonstrated that the shape of the input PDFs may have a minimal effect
\par
on the risk estimates in the tails of the probability distribution when the mean and variance of the input
\par
PDFs are held constant (Hoffman and Hammonds, 1992; Finley and Paustenbach, 1994). Nevertheless, it
\par
is generally a good practice in PRA to demonstrate that alternative choices of PDFs do not have a
\par
significant effect on percentiles in the RME risk range.
\par
A common question when developing and evaluating Monte Carlo models is, ``How many
\par
iterations is enough?''. Since Monte Carlo sampling is approximately random, no two simulations will
\par
yield the same results (unless the same starting point, or seed, of the random number generator is used).
\par
A rule of thumb is that the stability of the output distribution improves with increasing numbers of
\par
iterations, although there will always remain some stochastic variability. The stability is generally better
\par
at the central tendency region of the output distribution than at the tails; therefore, more iterations may be
\par
needed when the risk management decision is associated with the higher percentiles
\par
(e.g., {$>$} 95th percentile). Risk assessors are encouraged to run multiple simulations (with the same inputs)
\par
using different numbers of iterations in order to evaluate the stability of the risk estimate of concern. The
\par
results of such an exercise should generally be reported to the Agency when submitting a PRA for review.
\par
Note that while the speed of modern computers has essentially eliminated the issue for 1-D MCA (e.g.,
\par
10,000 iterations of most 1-D MCA models can be run in less than 1 minute), it may still be an important
\par
issue for more complex modeling approaches such as Microexposure Event analysis (MEE) and
\par
2-D MCA (see Appendix D).
\par
