B.7.0 SELECTING PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTIONS BASED ON STATE OF KNOWLEDGE
\par
Table B-4 summarizes preliminary strategies for proceeding with a PRA based on the amount of
\par
available information. Recommended starting points for each of the three steps in the general process are
\par
provided. This table provides guidance on candidate distributions that are consistent with the available
\par
information, however, it is not intended to discourage the use or exploration of alternative choices.
\par
L Table B-4 provides recommended preliminary strategies, not steadfast rules.
\par
As an analyst works through the PRA, alternative distributions, estimation
\par
methods, consideration of mechanism, and GoF tests may better guide the
\par
selection process.
\par
Case 1 represents the best scenario, in which the analyst has access to the raw data and a
\par
sufficiently large sample size (or {$>$} 6 percentiles). In this case, the analyst has a variety of choices for
\par
distribution fitting and estimating parameters. However, frequently raw data are inaccessible to the
\par
analyst. Cases 2 and 3 have limited information available (i.e., mean and upper percentile) and, therefore,
\par
have a narrower set of starting points. Case 4 is the most extreme scenario of data availability requiring
\par
expert judgment on selecting and fitting distributions.
