EXAMPLES OF FITTING DISTRIBUTIONS USING
\par
GRAPHICAL METHODS, GOODNESS-OF-FIT, AND PARAMETER ESTIMATION
\par
Example B-1. Empirical Distribution Function (EDF) for Soil Ingestion Rates
\par
This hypothetical example illustrates how graphical methods can be used to select probability
\par
distributions for variability based on percentile data reported in the literature. Table B-5 gives the
\par
summary statistics that are reported by Stanek and Calabrese (1995) for average daily soil ingestion rates
\par
among young children. Three options are explored for selecting a distribution: (1) empirical distribution
\par
function (EDF) represented by a step function; (2) linearized and extended EDF; and (3) continuous
\par
parametric distributions (beta and lognormal).
\par
In order to specify an EDF, a plausible range (minimum and maximum) must be inferred using
\par
judgment. Exposure factors such as ingestion rate are nonnegative variables (i.e., minimum \$0); given
\par
the relatively low value for the 25th percentile (10 mg/day), it is assumed that 0 mg/day is a reasonable
\par
minimum value for this example. If children with pica for soil are excluded from the population of
\par
concern, the maximum value may be inferred from the relatively shallow slope at the high-end of the
\par
distribution. That is, the 90th percentile is reported as 186 mg/day while the 99th percentile is 225 mg/day,
\par
an increase of only 39 mg/day; it is assumed that 300 mg/day is a plausible maximum value for this
\par
example. Commercial software such as Crystal Ball\textregistered{} and @Risk can be used to input EDFs. Figure B-3
\par
illustrates the basic step-wise EDF represented by the reported percentile values, as well as the
\par
``linearized, extended EDF'' (i.e., linear interpolation between reported values and extended lower and
\par
upper tails).
\par
An alternative to relying on a linear interpolation between the percentile values is to fit a
\par
continuous probability distribution to the reported percentiles. Since the original data are unavailable,
\par
standard GoF tests for the EDF, such as K-S and Anderson-Darling (d'Agostino and Stephens, 1986),
\par
cannot be applied. Note that computer software (e.g., Crystal Ball\textregistered{}
\par
, @Risk) will provide test statistics
\par
and corresponding p-values, however, these results will (inappropriately) reflect the number of percentile
\par
values reported rather than the sample size of the original data. Nevertheless, graphical methods may be
\par
employed to assess the adequacy of the fit of various PDFs. In this example, a beta distribution and
\par
lognormal distribution were fit to the EDF using Crystal Ball\textregistered. Figure B-4 illustrates the selected
\par
statistics for both distributions.
\par
The beta distribution appears to more closely match the reported percentile values, especially at
\par
the upper tail of the distribution. The lognormal distribution has an unbounded maximum that, for this
\par
example, results in an extreme overestimate of the 95th and 99th percentiles. The beta distribution, by
\par
definition, is bounded at 0 and 1, and rescaled in this example to a maximum of 364 mg/day. This
\par
analysis would support the use of a beta distribution in a Monte Carlo simulation.
