Example B-2. Variability in Lead Concentrations in Quail Breast Tissue
\par
This hypothetical example demonstrates how the combination of graphical methods, GoF tests,
\par
and parameter estimation techniques provides strong evidence for selecting and fitting a lognormal
\par
distribution. Assume lead concentration in quail is an important variable for a food web model. Site-
\par
specific data (n=62) are used to estimate inter-individual variability in concentration (Table B-6). The
\par
histograms in Figure B-5 show lead concentrations in quail breast tissue collected near a settling pond at a
\par
plating works. Equation B-1 indicated that 7 bins is an appropriate starting point. The result (top left
\par
panel, Figure B-5) suggests that approximately 80\% of the values are {$<$} 200 ppm and that the probability
\par
distribution for variability may be described by a nonnegative, right-skewed distribution (e.g.,
\par
exponential, Weibull, lognormal, etc.). However, additional bins are needed to better understand the low-
\par
end of the distribution. After increasing the number of bins from 7 to 16 (top right panel, Figure B-5),
\par
graphical evaluation continues to suggest that the distribution is unimodal right skewed. The bottom
\par
panel of Figure B-5 illustrates that increasing the number of bins would not provide better resolution of
\par
the low-end of the distribution. For these data, 16 bins appear to provide a reasonable balance between
\par
too much smoothing and too much jaggedness.
\par
Probability plots can be used to visually inspect the GoF of a specified distribution to the data,
\par
and, because the hypothesized distribution yields a straight line, the plots are particularly useful for
\par
evaluating deviations at the tails. In addition, parameter estimates can be obtained from the regression
\par
lines fit to the data, as discussed below. For this example, two lognormal probability plots are explored to
\par
evaluate how well the data can be described by a lognormal distribution (Figure B-6). The top panel
\par
gives the z-score on the abscissa (the ``x'' axis) and ln[concentration] on the ordinate (the ``y'' axis), while
\par
the bottom panel gives ln[concentration] on the abscissa and z-score on the ordinate. Plotting positions
\par
for both methods were calculated using Equation B-2. Equally plausible parameter estimates can be
\par
obtained from regression lines using either plotting method; however, the approach shown in the top
\par
panel may be easier to implement and interpret.
\par
Despite the relatively large sample size of n=62, GoF tests generally fail to reject lognormality
\par
(i.e., normality of the log-transformed data) in this example. For the probability plot correlation
\par
coefficient test (Filliben, 1975; Looney and Gulledge, 1985), if r {$<$} r* (the value for r at a specified "),
\par
normality is rejected. For this example, r is 0.988, and r* is between 0.988 and 0.989 for n=62 and
\par
"=0.25; therefore, the p-value for the concentrations is approximately 0.25 and one fails to reject
\par
lognormality at " \# 0.25. D'Agostino's test yields essentially the same conclusion, with a calculated
\par
Y value of -1.9166. For this data set, with n=62 and "=0.10, one rejects normality if Y {$<$} -2.17 or
\par
Y {$>$} 0.997 (see Table 9.7 in d'Agostino and Stephens, 1986); therefore, since Y is within this interval, one
\par
fails to reject the normal distribution. However, for "=0.20, the rejection criteria is [Y {$<$} -1.64 or
\par
Y {$>$} 0.812], Y falls outside the low-end of the interval, resulting in a rejection of the normal distribution.
\par
For this data set, the p-value associated with d'Agostino's test is slightly less than 0.20 and one fails to
\par
reject normality at " {$<$} 0.20.
