Example B-4. Bivariate Normal Distributions
\par
This example introduces the bivariate normal
\par
distribution to illustrate two concepts: (1) use of
\par
information on correlations in a Monte Carlo
\par
simulation; and (2) specifying distributions for
\par
uncertainty in parameter estimates. A brief
\par
explanation of the bivariate distribution is presented
\par
followed by an example comparing assumptions of
\par
no correlation and perfect correlation. A less
\par
complex example of a method for addressing
\par
correlations in PRA is given in Exhibit B-8.
\par
Properties of a Bivariate Normal Distribution
\par
One approach that can be used to correlate
\par
two random variables is to specify a bivariate normal distribution, which allows for the distribution of one
\par
variable to be sampled conditional on the other. A bivariate normal distribution is a special case of a joint
\par
distribution in which both x and y are random independent normally distributed variables. A bivariate
\par
normal distribution can be specified for all correlation coefficients including {$\rho$}=0, {$\rho$}=1, and {$\rho$}=-1. The
\par
bivariate distribution has a three dimensional shape and for {$\rho$}=0, from a bird's-eye view, is perfectly
\par
circular. As correlation increases (i.e. moves towards -1 or 1) this circle narrows and flattens to an
\par
elliptical shape, and finally for perfect correlation {$\rightarrow$}=1 and {$\rho$}=-1) becomes a straight regression line with
\par
a r2=1. In three dimensional space the probability of obtaining measurement pairs (x, y) in the region is
\par
equal to the volume under the surface in that region. To completely specify the bivariate normal,
\par
estimates of the arithmetic mean and variance of the two parameters, as well as the correlation coefficient
\par
(:X and :Y, variances F2
\par
X and F2
\par
Y , and correlation coefficient D) are needed.
\par
Page B-45RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
In a bivariate normal distribution, values of y corresponding to each value of x follow a normal
\par
distribution (Snedecor and Cochran, 1989). Analogously, the values of x corresponding to each value of
\par
y follow a normal distribution. Furthermore, if two random variables, X and Y, jointly follow a bivariate
\par
normal distribution, the marginal distribution of X is normal with mean :X and variance F2
\par
X, and the
\par
marginal distribution of Y is normal with mean :Y and variance F2
\par
Y.
\par
