\par
Application of Bivariate Normal Distribution to
\par
Correlate Concentrations of Zinc in Soil and Dust
\par
Assume random sampling of soil and dust
\par
zinc concentrations occurs in a residential area. Composite samples of soil and dust are collected from
\par
21 locations such that samples are paired (i.e., each soil sample is co-located with a dust sample) (Table
\par
B-9). First the relationship between the zinc concentration in soil and dust is evaluated using simple
\par
least-squares regression. Next, the bivariate normal distribution for the slope (\$1) and intercept (\$0) is
\par
determined, yielding an arithmetic mean and standard deviation for each parameter (:b0, F2
\par
b0, :b1, and
\par
F2
\par
b1), and correlation coefficient D between \$1 and \$0. In this context, the bivariate normal distribution
\par
may be considered a distribution for uncertainty in the parameter estimates.
\par
Three simulation methods are employed to demonstrate the effect of assuming a bivariate normal
\par
distribution for parameters vs. perfect correlation, or independent parameters. Specifically:
\par
(1) The slope and intercept of the regression line are described by a specific form of the bivariate
\par
normal distribution (i.e., follow Steps 1, 2 in Exhibit B-9, and use Equation B-10 instead of
\par
Step 4).
\par
(2) The slope and intercept of the regression line are described by a general form of the bivariate
\par
normal distribution (i.e., follow Steps 1 to 4 in Exhibit B-9).
\par
(3) The slope and intercept of the regression line are described by independent normal distributions
\par
(i.e., follow Steps 1--4 in Exhibit B-9, but omit the correlation coefficient D in Steps 2 and 4).
\par
For each approach, Monte Carlo simulations with I=5,000 iterations were run to determine the set
\par
Page B-47RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix B \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
of parameter values (\$0, \$1) for a simple linear regression equation. Typically, the uncertainty in the
\par
parameter estimates is not accounted for when simple linear regression equations are used to relate to
\par
exposure variables in a model. Such an approach may fail to account for important sources of parameter
\par
uncertainty. Figure B-10 (middle panel) illustrates the preferred approach for characterizing parameter
\par
uncertainty based on the bivariate normal distribution. (Note that the correlation coefficient relating the
\par
intercepts and slopes generated from the simulation is consistent with the correlation coefficient that
\par
describes the bivariate normal distribution; this is a good check that the simulation was set up correctly
\par
and run for a sufficient number of iterations). These results are contrasted with results using a form of the
\par
bivariate normal (Equation B-10) that underestimates uncertainty (top panel) unless parameters are
\par
perfectly correlated. In addition, the simplistic approach of sampling from independent normal
\par
distributions (bottom panel), yields a ``shot gun'' scatter plot. Sampling from independent normal
\par
distributions results in unlikely extreme combinations of the slope and intercept more often than the
\par
correct bivariate normal approach; propagating this bias through a risk model may severely bias estimates
\par
of uncertainty in risk.
