APPENDIX C
\par
CHARACTERIZING VARIABILITY AND UNCERTAINTY
\par
IN THE CONCENTRATION TERM
\par
C.0 THE CONCENTRATION TERM AND THE EXPOSURE UNIT
\par
Incomplete knowledge of the concentration of one or more chemicals in various exposure media
\par
is often the major source of uncertainty in Superfund risk assessments. In any risk assessment, the
\par
derivation of the concentration term will reflect assumptions about: (1) properties of the contaminant,
\par
(2) the spatial and temporal variability in contamination, (3) the behavior of the receptor, and (4) the time
\par
scale of the toxicity of the chemical(s). This appendix expands upon concepts introduced in Chapter 5.
\par
This appendix does not provide detailed equations for performing calculations, but instead refers the
\par
reader to other Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) guidance documents in which both the
\par
recommended approaches and calculations are provided.
\par
The concentration term is linked to the concept of an exposure unit (EU). For Superfund risk
\par
assessments, an EU is the geographical area in which a receptor is randomly exposed to a contaminated
\par
medium for a relevant exposure duration. Environmental sampling provides information about the
\par
contamination within and around an EU. Multiple EUs may be defined at a site based on the choice of a
\par
receptor, the exposure medium, and the nature of contact with the medium. For example, residential
\par
exposures to children may involve exposures via soil and dust ingestion both at the primary residence and
\par
recreational areas at a day care facility. Site-specific information regarding the activities of receptors
\par
should guide assumptions about the receptor's contact with exposure media.
\par
L Defining the EU is critical to the success of the remedial strategy, as it
\par
affects the calculation of the concentration to which receptors are
\par
exposed.
\par
C.1.0 VARIABILITY IN PRA
\par
In general, variability and uncertainty should be kept separate to the extent possible in any
\par
probabilistic risk assessment (PRA). For example, assume a one-dimensional Monte Carlo Analysis
\par
(1-D MCA) was developed to characterize variability in risk, but it combined a distribution for
\par
uncertainty in mean concentration with distributions for variability in exposure variables. The result
\par
would yield a single distribution for risk, however, each risk estimate would reflect both uncertainty and
\par
variability and distinguishing between the two would not be possible. Therefore, EPA's Guiding
\par
Principles for Monte Carlo analysis recommends against mixing distributions of variability and
\par
uncertainty in a 1-D MCA (U.S. EPA, 1997b) to avoid such ambiguities.
\par
A fundamental concept in Monte Carlo analysis is that there is variability in exposure between
\par
receptors (inter-individual variability) as well as day-to-day variability for each individual (intra-
\par
individual variability). In most Tier 2 analyses (see Chapter 2), the goal of a 1-D MCA is to characterize
\par
inter-individual variability in exposure and risk. Typically, probability distributions for exposure
\par
represent variability (PDFv's) between individuals in the average value over the entire exposure duration.
\par
In this case, the exposure point concentration (EPC) should represent the average exposure concentration
\par
over the entire exposure duration. Because an EPC is calculated from a sample, there is uncertainty that
\par
Page C-1RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix C \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
the sample mean equals the true mean concentration within the EU; therefore, to account for associated
\par
uncertainty, the 95\% upper confidence limit for the mean (95\% UCL) is generally used for Superfund risk
\par
assessments (U.S. EPA, 1992).
\par
In a 1-D MCA, a point estimate for the EPC is combined with PDFv's for other variables to yield
\par
a probability distribution for risk. An alternative approach is to simulate long-term average exposures as
\par
a series of consecutive short-term exposure events. This approach is referred to as MicroExposure Event
\par
(MEE) Monte Carlo modeling, and is discussed in detail in Appendix D. In MEE modeling, the goal is to
\par
develop PDFv's for exposure variables that capture the event-to-event variability in exposures at the
\par
individual level. The concept of an averaging time still applies, but generally to a shorter time frame. For
\par
example, seasonal variability in exposure frequency might be expected among outdoor occupational
\par
workers so that different PDFv's are representative of inter-individual for each season. In this case, the
\par
EPC continues to represent an average concentration within the EU, but it would be linked to season-
\par
specific activity patterns. It may be important to develop two different weighted averages to reflect
\par
season-specific activity patterns and locations that are more frequently contacted in the summer compared
\par
with the winter, for example. As the time frame for the exposure scenario is shortened from the entire
\par
exposure duration, to a season, to a day, to an individual event, the concentration term should be
\par
reevaluated to assess the relevance of the assumption that concentrations contacted by the receptor are
\par
represented by the mean of the measured sample.
\par
The following discussion introduces concepts of temporal and spatial variability as they apply to
\par
the estimate of the EPC for different exposure media and exposure scenarios. While the general rule of
\par
thumb applies to all Monte Carlo models---use a measure of the average concentration within the EU over
\par
the time frame of exposure---it is important to apply the site sampling data in a way that is consistent with
\par
the exposure scenario.
\par
C.1.1 TEMPORAL VARIABILITY
\par
Temporal variability in chemical concentrations may be an important consideration when
\par
developing a preliminary remediation goal (PRG) for any exposure medium (refer to Chapter 5 for a
\par
comprehensive discussion of using PRA to evaluate PRGs). For example, wind erosion may change
\par
chemical concentrations in surface soil over time; leaching may change concentrations in both subsurface
\par
soil and groundwater; and bioaccumulation may result in increasing concentrations in predatory fish with
\par
time. If possible, such factors should be considered early in the risk assessment process and included in
\par
the conceptual site model.
\par
Development of the EPC normally will depend on the averaging time relevant to the exposure
\par
scenario and health endpoint of concern. In the shorter term, it may be unlikely that receptors are
\par
exposed throughout the entire EU due to temporal (and spatial) variability in the contaminant and inter-
\par
individual variability in activity patterns. Therefore, inter-individual variability in the EPC might be
\par
expected, and a distribution of EPCs may be developed to represent differences in exposure among the
\par
population. Variability in short-term exposure may be an important factor for assessing variability in
\par
acute toxicity. However, over time, short-term variability in the EPC will tend to smooth out and
\par
approach a long-term average concentration. A single estimate of the long-term average EPC may be
\par
reasonable to use in assessing risks to the receptor population. This is true regardless of the underlying
\par
distribution of the environmental sampling data (e.g., lognormal, normal, beta, etc.).
\par
While most chemicals regulated by the Superfund program are based on concerns for chronic
\par
toxicity (e.g., lifetime cancer risk from exposure to a carcinogen for ten or more years), for some
\par
