\par
chemicals, toxic effects occur with shorter exposure durations (e.g., nitrate in drinking water and
\par
methemoglobinemia in infants). Differences between acute and chronic health endpoints are important to
\par
consider for ecological receptors such as transient migratory species. Superfund guidance distinguishes
\par
between acute and chronic exposure to provide risk assessors the option of evaluating risk under different
\par
time frames. The EPC should be estimated within an EU during a period of time that has toxicological
\par
relevance for the exposed population.
\par
L The time scale of the concentration term should match the time scale of
\par
the toxicity criterion and exposure duration.
\par
C.1.2 SPATIAL VARIABILITY
\par
Spatial variability in chemical concentrations is also an important property to consider when
\par
developing a PRG. Spatial variability arises from many factors, including the mechanism of
\par
contamination, physical and chemical dilution and transformation processes, and physical characteristics
\par
of the site (Cullen and Frey, 1999). Similarly, receptors may exhibit spatial variability in their contact
\par
with an exposure medium. In general, receptors are assumed to have equal access to all areas within an
\par
EU so that the concept of a long-term average concentration is applicable.
\par
Often, the EPC is estimated without regard to the spatial patterns in contamination. The sampling
\par
design yields a measure of the variability in concentrations that is assumed to be representative of the
\par
receptor's contact with the exposure medium. However, even when the sampling design is representative
\par
(e.g., both are simple random samples within the EU), the concentrations may exhibit clear spatial
\par
patterns that could be used to reduce uncertainty in the EPC. Geostatistics (see Section C.5.2 and
\par
Appendix D) offers a wide range of techniques for incorporating spatial information into estimates of the
\par
EPC. These techniques are particularly useful when there is uncertainty in the representativeness of site
\par
sampling, due to a difference in scale between site sampling and the size of the EU, or the use of targeted
\par
sampling designs that oversample areas within an EU believed to contain the highest levels of
\par
contamination.
\par
In point estimate risk assessments (Tier 1 of the PRA), the EPC is most often characterized by a
\par
point estimate of the mean concentration, typically given by the 95\% UCL for the mean to account for
\par
uncertainty in the site characterization (U.S. EPA, 1992). Variability in concentrations is an important
\par
consideration for determining appropriate statistical methods used to estimate the 95\% UCL. In addition,
\par
for some Monte Carlo models, a PDFv may be developed to determine the EPC for the exposure model.
\par
A PDFv for the EPC may be warranted in short-term exposure scenarios, particularly when the sampling
\par
density is relatively sparse in relation to the size of the EU (i.e., poor site characterization). For example,
\par
a risk assessment may include a future use residential scenario (e.g., currently the site is undeveloped) in
\par
which the EPC that is relevant to a potentially exposed population of children is the average concentration
\par
within a 0.5 acre lot. If the soil sampling yields 100 measurements, but a small subset of the samples
\par
(e.g., less than three) are available for any 0.5 acre area, the most appropriate measure of the average
\par
concentration for a hypothetical residence may be the maximum detected concentration or a single value
\par
from the PDFv in concentration among hypothetical receptors. In general, for any of the EU's that define
\par
a randomly located residence, the poor site characterization would be a source of uncertainty in both a
\par
point estimate and probabilistic risk assessment.
\par
At the vast majority of sites, concentration data is the easiest data to obtain of all the exposure
\par
variables. In cases of poor site characterization, risk managers may opt to perform a point estimate risk
\par
assessment only using the maximum detected concentration and highly protective exposure assumptions.
\par
