\par
In the scenario described above for 0.5 acre residential lots, it is possible that a residence would be
\par
located in an area in which the average concentration is represented by the maximum detected
\par
concentration in the sample. Should the risk manager opt for a Tier 1 point estimate risk assessment, the
\par
use of the maximum detected concentration of a chemical on the site should ensure the performance of a
\par
health-protective risk assessment within a smaller EU.
\par
Consideration of variability is also warranted in short-term scenarios for ecological risk
\par
assessment (ERA) when the EU is much smaller than the site (see Section C.3.1.1). For example, the
\par
home range of the receptor populations may be relatively small in comparison to the spatial distribution
\par
of sampling locations (e.g., benthic invertebrates living in the sediment at the bottom of a river or soil
\par
invertebrates in a terrestrial habitat). In these cases, the receptor would be exposed to an area smaller than
\par
the sampling grid or measure of areal sampling density. A value from the PDFv that characterizes
\par
variability in the concentrations across a relatively large spatial scale may be used to define the EPC for a
\par
receptor population at a smaller scale. Again, risk assessors should take care in designing a 1-D Monte
\par
Carlo model when using a PDFv for the concentration term. It is unadvisable to mix a PDFv for the
\par
concentration term with PDFv's for other exposure scenarios when estimating risks within one EU. Use
\par
of the PDFv in this manner would incorrectly suggest that the mean concentration varied for each
\par
individual within the same EU according to the variability in concentration measured across a much
\par
larger area. A preferred approach is to use a PDFv to obtain a point estimate that represents the EPC, and
\par
then combine this point estimate with PDFv's for other variables in the Monte Carlo simulation to
\par
estimate risks in the small EU. If there are many EU's at a site, or if the boundaries of EUs are undefined,
\par
more advanced modeling approaches can be developed to efficiently run multiple scenarios. Methods for
\par
characterizing exposure point concentrations for ecological receptors are further discussed in Sections C.2
\par
and C.3.
\par
C.1.3 EXAMPLE OF TEMPORAL AND SPATIAL VARIABILITY
\par
Exposure scenarios often require consideration of both temporal and spatial variability. The
\par
MEE might be used to assess temporal variability by simulating long-term intake as the sum of individual
\par
exposure events. The time step for MEE is an important consideration and will depend on the rate of
\par
change of the most rapidly changing exposure variable. In addition, there should be a correspondence
\par
between the time periods over which data were obtained and the time step used in the MEE model. For
\par
example, when a MEE is used for the risk assessment, the concentration term selected at each time period
\par
should match the ``average'' concentration within the EU appropriate for that particular time period.
\par
Assume that the receptor is a residential child, and the time period is a single day, and the child may
\par
contact only 1,000 square feet within the 0.5 acre (20,000 square feet) residential EU. The specific
\par
1,000 square foot area may change with each day as the child chooses different areas in the yard to
\par
frequent. Hence, the variability in the sample may be a more appropriate measure of the concentration
\par
contacted by residential child receptor on a day-to-day basis than the long-term average within the
\par
0.5 acre EU. Over the long-term, this receptor will be exposed to the entire EU and hence the average
\par
contaminant concentration within the 0.5 acre EU. Note that the day-to-day variability in concentration
\par
undergoes the familiar phenomenon of ``regression to the mean'' when considered over the long-term.
\par
