C.1.4 SPATIAL AND TEMPORAL VARIABILITY FOR DIFFERENT EXPOSURE MEDIA
\par
C.1.4.1 VARIABILITY OF CONCENTRATIONS IN SOIL
\par
Surface soil is subject to erosion by wind and surface water runoff. Over time, concentrations in
\par
surface soil may change, but generally at a slow rate relative to other media. The spatial variability of
\par
chemical contamination is most often due to the mechanism by which the contamination occurred. For
\par
example, particulate stack emissions will tend to fall in an even pattern downwind of the stack whereas
\par
over-application of pesticides and chemical spills can result in a patchy pattern of contamination.
\par
Subsurface soil is not subject to wind erosion, so concentrations change mostly due to
\par
degradation processes or leaching of the contaminant to groundwater. At most Superfund sites,
\par
concentrations of chemicals in subsurface soil will remain relatively constant.
\par
C.1.4.2 VARIABILITY OF CONCENTRATIONS IN GROUNDWATER
\par
Exposure to groundwater contamination mostly occurs at a fixed point in space (e.g., the
\par
wellhead). Groundwater is subject to a variety of influences that can alter chemical concentrations within
\par
this medium such as aerobic and anaerobic biodegredation, volatization, and absorption. Due to these
\par
influences, monitored natural attenuation is an appropriate remedy under certain site conditions. If a risk
\par
assessor wishes to use a measure of the long-term average of a concentration in groundwater, a
\par
hydrogeologist should be consulted.
\par
C.1.4.3 VARIABILITY OF CONCENTRATIONS IN SURFACE WATER
\par
Concentrations in surface water can be very dynamic. Streams are constantly flowing and the
\par
effects of mixing, dilution and evaporation can change the chemical concentrations in surface water over
\par
relative short time periods. Any sampling of surface water is truly a ``snapshot'' in time. The sampling
\par
methods used to characterize spatial and temporal variability of concentrations in surface water will have
\par
a direct effect on the uncertainty in estimates of the average concentration over both short and long time
\par
frames.
\par
C.1.4.4 VARIABILITY OF CONCENTRATIONS IN SEDIMENT
\par
In some situations, sediment may be considered a relatively stable medium, similar to soil.
\par
Alternatively, sediment may be physically moved by currents, tides, the movement of ships and other
\par
events. Trend analysis may be used to establish the long-term average sediment transport at a site. This
\par
information could provide the basis for choosing a representative ``average'' concentration in the sediment
\par
available to ecological receptors (Piest and Miller, 1975; Van Sickel and Beschta, 1983; Walling, 1983;
\par
Meade et al., 1990).
\par
C.1.4.5 VARIABILITY OF CONCENTRATIONS IN FISH
\par
Concentrations in fish may vary due to a change in the availability of food and environmental
\par
conditions. Factors that may be used to model population dynamics may include intensity of angler
\par
harvest, death/attrition of the population, and the introduction of a predator species or a more adaptive
\par
species. In risk assessments that include a fish ingestion exposure pathway, the activities of the angler
\par
may be a more important factor in determining the EPC than the changes in concentrations in fish over
\par
time. For example, an avid recreational angler may harvest fish from different locations within a lake and
\par
Page C-5RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix C \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
consume fish of different sizes and species. In this way, with the consumption of contaminated fish, both
\par
the contaminated medium and the exposure point change throughout the exposure duration.
\par
Unless, samples of fish are collected over time, knowledge of these factors will generally be
\par
unknown. Concentrations of bioaccumulative chemicals in territorial fish (e.g., largemouth bass)
\par
obtained in different locations will generally reflect the concentrations in the sediment in the individual's
\par
home territory. Concentrations of bioaccumulative chemicals in migratory fish will be more difficult to
\par
predict as the fish will contact areas with varying sediment and surface water concentrations.
\par
