C.2.0 NONRANDOM EXPOSURES
\par
As discussed in Section C.1.2, in the long-term it is generally assumed receptors exhibit random
\par
movement, such that there is an equal probability of contacting any area within the entire EU. Therefore,
\par
the long-term exposure concentration will most likely be the arithmetic mean of the concentration within
\par
the EU. However, in many situations, the assumption of random exposures in space may clearly be an
\par
oversimplification. People's behavior and preferences will cause them to access specific areas within an
\par
EU with greater frequency than others. The same is true in terms of ecological receptors with specific
\par
habitat preferences.
