C.3.0 SOURCES OF UNCERTAINTY IN THE CONCENTRATION TERM
\par
There are numerous potential sources of uncertainty in the estimate of the true mean
\par
concentration within an EU. As discussed in Chapter 5 (Section 5.1.1), sources of uncertainty can be
\par
grouped into four broad categories: sample data, location of the EU, behavior of the receptor, and from
\par
miscellaneous sources (e.g., physical and chemical processes). Development of an uncertainty
\par
distribution for the average concentration requires knowledge of the variability in chemical
\par
concentrations within the EU (unless distribution-free approaches are used), the toxicity of the chemicals,
\par
and the receptor's behavior. These distributions should be developed by risk assessors with the concept
\par
of the EU in mind. Differences in scale (e.g., small home range of an ecological receptor population
\par
relative to the site sampling design) can be a major source of uncertainty in ecological risk assessments.
\par
Methods for addressing such uncertainties in the concentration term are presented below. By
\par
incorporating these methods into the quantitative uncertainty analysis, risk managers may more
\par
effectively evaluate the importance of data-gaps and design subsequent rounds of site sampling to reduce
\par
the uncertainty in the EPC.
\par
C.3.1 QUANTIFICATION OF UNCERTAINTY BASED ON THE SIZE OF THE EXPOSURE UNIT
\par
Site characterization sometimes occurs before an EU has been defined. Therefore, an EU may be
\par
smaller than an entire site, equal to the site itself, or larger than the site. These three conditions lead to
\par
different conclusions and methods about the determination of the EPC. The most complex situation is
\par
when the EU is smaller than the site and the site can contain multiple EUs. For future scenarios in which
\par
the land use differs from the current land use, the difficulty in predicting the exact size and location of
\par
EUs necessitates accounting for the uncertainty in the EU.
\par
Composite sampling is often used to maximize site information. However, it is important to note
\par
that the use of composite sampling influences the concentration term. If composite sampling is used
\par
exclusively at a site, the actual maximum concentration present or the best estimate of this maximum
\par
concentration will not be available. Depending on the time scale of the toxic effect or whether acute
\par
toxicity should be considered, this lack of knowledge of the maximum concentration present may be a
\par
large data gap. Risk assessors are urged to consider composite sampling and its ramifications for the
\par
concentration term.
\par
C.3.1.1 WHEN THE EXPOSURE UNIT IS SMALLER THAN THE SITE
\par
The size of the EU will be different depending on the length of exposure. A receptor can access a
\par
greater area if given more time. In almost all cases, the size of the EU for short-term exposure will be
\par
smaller than the EU for long-term exposure. Therefore, in addition to the uncertainty associated with
\par
sampling and analysis (which can be quantified with existing methods for calculating confidence
\par
intervals), there is uncertainty about the location of the EU within the site.
\par
