\par
Appendix C \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
If contamination is evenly spread across the site, the location of the EU may not have any bearing
\par
on the EPC. In such a case, uncertainty may depend on the sample size or density of measurements
\par
within the EU relative to the entire site. In point estimate risk assessments, the concentrations of
\par
chemicals at the sampling location that poses the greatest risk may be considered as estimates of the EPC
\par
for this small EU. Using this ``riskiest'' sampling location as an estimate of the mean within an EU of
\par
unknown location accounts for both the uncertainty associated with limited sampling within a single EU
\par
and the uncertainty of the location within the site of the EU.
\par
To express the uncertainty in location of the EU as a distribution, methods have been developed
\par
to place an EU of a given size randomly about a site (Burmaster and Thompson, 1997). A concentration
\par
term is developed for each of a large number of randomly located EUs. The distribution of these
\par
concentration terms will express the uncertainty in the location of the EU.
\par
Risk assessors are cautioned to consider whether the statistical method used to estimate the EPC
\par
in an EU accounts for all sources of uncertainty in the concentration term. If only a few samples are used
\par
to characterize the average concentration within an EU, then the uncertainty in the EPC is large and
\par
should be presented in the risk characterization. These conditions may warrant additional sampling or the
\par
use of analytical methods that account for spatial variability within the entire site.
\par
At some sites, geostatistical methods, pattern recognition, and geographical information systems
\par
(GIS) methods may provide additional insight and will aid in the development of the concentration term
\par
(see Section C.5.2). Although Table 3-1 shows several statistical methods for estimating both point
\par
estimates and distributions that encode uncertainty in the concentration term, a risk assessor's
\par
understanding of these uncertainties should be conceptual as opposed to purely statistical.
\par
C.3.1.2 WHEN THE EXPOSURE UNIT IS THE SAME SIZE AS THE SITE
\par
In this case, the entire environmental data set within the site boundaries can be used for the
\par
determination of the concentration term. Assuming the EU occupies the entire site, then the source of
\par
uncertainty associated with knowing the average concentration within the EU is the sampling and
\par
analytical uncertainty.
\par
C.3.1.3 WHEN THE EXPOSURE UNIT IS LARGER THAN THE SITE
\par
In this case, the EU extends beyond the site boundaries. Therefore, the entire environmental data
\par
set within the site boundaries can be used for determination of the concentration term. However, an
\par
additional term in the exposure assessment may be needed to account for the fraction of the exposures
\par
that are expected to occur off site. Essentially, the contribution of the chemical concentrations measured
\par
on and off site are weighted by the fraction ingested or contacted in each area. Similarly, the term ``area
\par
use factor'' is used in ecological risk assessments to refer to the percentage of time or area an animal
\par
inhabits a contaminated area. An exposure scenario in which the EU is defined by the multiple locations
\par
that may be visited would be a common extension of this concept. One reasonable assumption regarding
\par
off site exposures is that the concentrations would be equal to the ``background'' concentrations. If this
\par
assumption is made, a site risk assessor should be consulted to determine appropriate methods for
\par
incorporating background concentrations into the risk assessment. Alternatively, additional sampling at
\par
off site locations would be needed to estimate the concentrations.
\par
