C.5.0 METHODS FOR ESTIMATING UNCERTAINTY IN THE MEAN CONCENTRATION
\par
Confidence intervals (CIs) and UCLs are computed to characterize uncertainty in a parameter
\par
estimate. CIs can be computed for any parameter. The general method for estimating confidence
\par
intervals is presented in equation C-1.
\par
CI = parameter estimate \textpm{} (critical value) x SE Equation C-1
\par
The parameter estimate is the estimated value for the unknown population parameter. The critical
\par
value is the number, z, with probability, p, lying to its right (for an upper critical value) or left (for a lower
\par
critical value). For a standard normal distribution (i.e., arithmetic mean=0, standard deviation=1), critical
\par
values are referred to as the z-score or z-statistic. These values are commonly given in statistics texts, and
\par
Page C-10RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix C \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
may also be calculated using the Microsoft Excel function Normsinv(p), where p corresponds to the
\par
probability lying to the right of the value. Distributions that characterize parameter uncertainty are
\par
sometimes referred to as sampling distributions. The standard error (SE) is the standard deviation of the
\par
sampling distribution for the parameter estimate. The confidence interval conveys two concepts: (1) an
\par
upper and lower confidence limit (for a 2-sided CI), and (2) a confidence level (1--"), which gives the
\par
probability that the method yields an interval that encloses the parameter (Moore and McCabe, 1993).
\par
Methods for estimating SE vary for specific parameters. For example, the SE of a mean concentration
\par
may be calculated based on the sample variance and the sample size (due to Central Limit Theorem).
\par
Methods for calculating the SE for other parameters, such as the 95th percentile, are more complex, and
\par
may be estimated from a series of nested bootstrap simulations (Efron and Tibshirani, 1993; U.S. EPA,
\par
2001a).
\par
When comparing alternative approaches for quantifying parameter uncertainty, criteria that are
\par
important to consider include the variance of the original data set, and the bias and coverage of the CIs
\par
generated by each method. In statistics, a method is unbiased if the mean of the sampling distribution is
\par
equal to the true value of the parameter. Similarly, a method has accurate coverage if the probability p
\par
that a CI does not cover the true parameter is equal to the probability level used to construct the CI. For
\par
risk assessment, the most desirable method is one that deals well with high variance, yields CIs that are
\par
sufficiently wide (i.e., the CI does not underestimate the probability of enclosing the population
\par
parameter), and, more specifically, yields upper confidence limits that are not biased low. The choice of
\par
the most appropriate method will depend on the characteristics of the data set and a balance between two
\par
objectives: (1) the desire to be health protective and, therefore, have a low probability of underestimating
\par
the mean, and (2) a desire to be accurate, in the sense of choosing a method whose expected coverage
\par
equals the true coverage. As a general principle for quantitative uncertainty analysis, if alternative
\par
methods yield very different answers, it is helpful to explore the reasons for the differences. The
\par
objective is to explain why the estimates of the 95\% UCL differ, and to determine if the differences are
\par
sufficiently great that they could alter the risk management decision or PRG. This information should be
\par
presented as part of the risk communication process associated with the scientific management decision
\par
points of the tiered process for PRA (see Chapter 2).
\par
As discussed in Chapter 5, in Superfund risk assessment, the EPC is usually calculated as the
\par
95\% UCL for the mean to account for the uncertainty in estimating the average concentration within an
\par
EU. The 95\% UCL is defined as a value that, when repeatedly calculated for randomly drawn subsets of
\par
size (n), equals or exceeds the true population mean 95\% of the time. In other words, it is calculated and
\par
applied as a 1-sided confidence limit. The 95\% UCL is one percentile on the probability distribution that
\par
characterizes uncertainty in the mean (i.e., the PDFu for the mean). It is equal to the 95th percentile of the
\par
sampling distribution for the mean. EPA's guidance on calculating the concentration term describes the
\par
rationale and methodology for selecting the 95\% UCL as the point estimate for the concentration term
\par
(U.S. EPA, 1992).
\par
Common methodologies for characterizing the 95\% UCL for the arithmetic mean concentration
\par
include the following: (1) application of Equation C-1 using Student's t-statistic (for normal
\par
distributions), (2) Land method using H-statistic (for lognormal distributions) (Land 1971, 1975), and
\par
(3) bootstrap and Jacknife resampling techniques (Efron and Tibshirani, 1993). Details on these methods
\par
and on choosing an appropriate method are provided in the ORD/OSWER guidance bulletin, Lognormal
\par
Distribution in Environmental Applications (U.S. EPA, 1997a), and the more recent OSWER guidance
\par
bulletin, Guidance on Calculation of UCLs at Superfund Sites (U.S. EPA, 2001a). An overview of
\par
methods that may be used when data are not normal or lognormal is also provided by Schulz and Griffin
\par
(1999). It is the responsibility of the regional risk assessor to ensure that an appropriate method for
\par
Page C-11RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix C \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
calculating a UCL or for developing an uncertainty distribution is chosen. Chapter 3 (Table 3-1) provides
\par
an overview of approaches for characterizing uncertainty in the concentration term in both 1-D MCA and
\par
2-D MCA.
\par
C.5.1 QUANTIFYING UNCERTAINTY WITHOUT INFORMATION ABOUT LOCATIONS OF
\par
SAMPLES AND RECEPTORS
\par
Knowledge of both the sampling locations and the receptor's activity patterns with the EU can be
\par
used to derive a more representative estimate of the 95\% UCL. If a risk assessor has access to an
\par
environmental data set without information about the sample locations, the risk assessor is forced to
\par
assume that the sample consists of a number of independent observations. The validity of this assumption
\par
depends on the unknown spatial variability of contamination at the site. The size and location of an EU,
\par
as well as the choice of a statistical method for estimating the distribution of uncertainty around the mean
\par
concentration will require often implicit (and possibly incorrect) assumptions about the spatial
\par
distribution of contamination. Similarly, if information regarding receptor activity patterns is unavailable,
\par
one must assume that any area within the EU is equally representative of potential exposures. The risk
\par
assessor is urged to explore the effects of these various assumptions and to make choices that are
\par
protective of human health and the environment.
\par
C.5.2 QUANTIFYING UNCERTAINTY WITH INFORMATION ABOUT LOCATIONS OF SAMPLES
\par
AND RECEPTORS
\par
In classical statistics, observations are assumed to be independent. This assumption is often
\par
invalid at contaminated sites where the method by which a chemical is released into the environment
\par
(e.g., deposition from airborne emissions; migration of contaminant plume from a point source) results in
\par
positive spatial autocorrelation. In other words, observations located next to each other tend to contain
\par
similar levels of contamination (i.e., redundant information) (Griffith and Layne, 1999). For example, the
\par
higher the spatial autocorrelation, the less incremental information is provided by adding observations in
\par
close proximity to existing observations. This decrease in the information content of a site sample is
\par
exacerbated by the tendency to choose sampling locations in the most contaminated areas rather than
\par
distributed at regular spatial intervals or specified using random sampling methodology.
\par
At many hazardous waste sites, environmental sampling plans are designed with remedial actions
\par
rather than risk assessment in mind. Therefore, the risk assessor must establish a correspondence between
\par
the actual sampling locations and the locations a receptor would be expected to frequent. Geostatistics
\par
may provide information to establish this correspondence.
\par
Geostatistics is a branch of spatial statistics that can be used to model spatial variability and
\par
parameter uncertainty. Geostatistics offers two fundamental contributions to risk assessment: (1) a group
\par
of methods to describe the spatial distribution of a contaminant in a quantitative fashion, and (2) the
\par
ability to maximize the information available in the data set (Deutsch and Journel, 1988; Isaacs and
\par
Srivastava, 1989).
\par
Geostatistics is capable of using the information revealed by a correlation analysis of the data to
\par
estimate concentrations at unsampled locations. For example, geostatistics is able to use the spatial
\par
information contained in the data to model uncertainty in contaminant concentrations for areas where data
\par
are sparse, a situation commonly encountered in site assessment work. Using geostatistics, information
\par
from samples collected from outside an EU can be used to model the uncertainty in the mean
\par
concentration within an EU. Approaches that do not consider the geospatial information present in the
\par
Page C-12RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process for Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix C \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
data are limited to the subset of samples within an EU. However, this ability to model uncertainty in
\par
areas where data are sparse is also limited, and a well characterized site is still the best path to
\par
understanding the risk at that site.
\par
Geostatistical methods may be used to calculate a distribution of uncertainty in the mean of the
\par
concentration term for use in PRAs. In the past, geostatistics has not been widely applied to risk
\par
assessment, even though uncertainty in the exposure concentration is often a major source of uncertainty
\par
in risk estimates. Most risk assessors quantify uncertainty in the long-term average concentration without
\par
explicitly considering the spatial information present in data obtained from environmental sampling or
\par
knowledge of the receptor's movement and activities within the EU. When spatial information does not
\par
exist, the inherent assumption is that environmental sampling yields a data set that is representative of the
\par
spatial variability in concentrations encountered by a receptor. This assumption represents one source of
\par
uncertainty in the EPC. In addition, data collected outside an EU are often ignored in the analysis, even
\par
though they can provide a more comprehensive view of patterns of contamination across the site,
\par
including the EU of interest. Ignoring site-wide information may result in less informed estimates of risk
\par
and, therefore, less effective remedial designs (i.e., too little or too much remediation). In the past five
\par
years, with rapidly expanding software and hardware capabilities, some examples of the application of
\par
geostatistics can be found in exposure assessment and remedial design (e.g., Gomez-Hernandez, 1996;
\par
Goovaerts, 1996, 1997; Kriakidis, 1996; Ginevan and Splitstone, 1997; McKenna, 1997, 1998) as well as
\par
site assessment guidance (e.g., U.S. EPA, 2000).
\par
A limit to applying geostatistics at hazardous waste sites is that the method is resource intensive
\par
and requires personnel experienced with the software and techniques. Risk assessors and risk managers
\par
should ensure that contractors and other personnel have the necessary capabilities before applying
\par
geostatistical methods to risk assessment or site cleanup. Geostatistics is a powerful tool, but it cannot
\par
incorporate quantitative knowledge regarding all sources of uncertainty. The risk assessor is cautioned to
\par
consider all possible sources of uncertainty as described in Chapter 5. As indicated previously, a full
\par
discussion of geostatistics is beyond the scope of this guidance, and interested readers are urged to consult
\par
the OSWER guidance document, Guidance on Strategy for Surface Soil Cleanup at Superfund Sites (U.S.
\par
EPA, 2001b).
\par
EPA has produced several software packages used for geostatistical estimation. Among these are
\par
GEO-EAS and GEO-PACK. Expertise in geostatistics can be obtained from ORD/Las Vegas.
