APPENDIX D
\par
ADVANCED MODELING APPROACHES FOR
\par
CHARACTERIZING VARIABILITY AND UNCERTAINTY
\par
D.0 INTRODUCTION
\par
This appendix briefly describes the following advanced modeling approaches that can be used in
\par
probabilistic risk assessment (PRA) to characterize variability and uncertainty: two-dimensional MCA (2-
\par
D MCA), microexposure event analysis (MEE), geospatial statistics, and Bayesian analysis. Except for 2-
\par
D MCA, these approaches can also be applied to point estimate risk assessment. The application of many
\par
of these approaches will require access to expertise in specialized areas of statistics and, in some cases,
\par
specialized or even custom-designed computer software. The intent here is to introduce some of the basic
\par
concepts and terminology, as well as to provide references where the reader can find more exhaustive
\par
coverage of these topics.
\par
D.1.0 EXPRESSING VARIABILITY AND UNCERTAINTY SIMULTANEOUSLY
\par
A Monte Carlo analysis that characterizes either uncertainty or variability in each input variable
\par
(see Chapter 1) can be described as a one-dimensional Monte Carlo analysis (1-D MCA). A 2-D MCA is
\par
a term used to describe a model that simulates both uncertainty and variability in one or more input
\par
variables. All probability distributions that are used to describe variability in a PRA model have a certain
\par
degree of associated uncertainty. For example, suppose variability in soil concentration (ppm) is
\par
estimated using a normal probability density function (PDF) defined by a mean (:soil=5) and standard
\par
deviation (Fsoil=1), and subjectively truncated (min, max) at (0, 50). Uncertainty in the parameter
\par
estimates can be represented in a PRA model by assuming both parameters are also random variables. To
\par
illustrate this concept, assume normal PDFs for uncertainty can be specified for both parameters.
\par
Uncertainty in the mean is described by the normal PDF with parameters (:mean=5, Fmean=0.5); similarly,
\par
uncertainty in the standard deviation is described by the normal PDF with parameters (:SD =1, FSD =0.5).
\par
Model variables are represented in this manner when there is a compelling reason to believe that a unique
\par
probability distribution does not adequately describe one's knowledge of each variable in the model. A
\par
variable described in this way is called a second order random variable. Figure D-1 (Panel A) shows a
\par
collection of n=20 cumulative probability distributions (CDFs), each curve representing a unique set of
\par
(mean, SD) parameter estimates for the normal PDF for variability. Panel B shows the 90\% confidence
\par
interval1 based on 2,500 simulated CDFs. The 95\% lower and upper bounds correspond to the
\par
distribution of 5th percentiles and 95th percentiles, respectively (i.e., CDF for 2,500 5th percentiles and
\par
CDF for 2,500 95th percentiles). The 90\% credible interval (CI) for the 50th percentile is (3.4, 6.7).
\par
1Note that the term ``credible interval'' may be more appropriate than ``confidence interval'' given that the range is
\par
based on subjective as well as statistical considerations. Brattin, Barry, and Chiu (1996) provide additional examples of
\par
uncertain PDFs that illustrate this concept.
