DEFINITIONS FOR APPENDIX D
\par
Bayesian Statistics - A specialized branch of statistics that views the probability of an event occurring as the degree of belief
\par
or confidence in that occurrence.
\par
Geospatial Statistics - A specialized branch of statistics that explicitly takes into account the georeferenced context of data and
\par
the information (i.e., attributes) it contains.
\par
Frequentist - A term referring to classical statistics in which the probability of an event occurring is defined as the frequency
\par
of occurrence measured in an observed series of repeated trials.
\par
Image Analysis - A technique in geostatistics used to restore a degraded image or interpret images that have been contaminated
\par
by noise or possibly some nonlinear transformation.
\par
Kriging - A geostatistical method of spatial statistics for predicting values at unobserved locations.
\par
Likelihood Function - A Bayesian term referring to a probability distribution expressing the probability of observing a piece
\par
of new information given that a particular prior belief is true.
\par
Location Tag - The spatial coordinates of a sampling location (e.g., longitude, latitude).
\par
Microexposure Event Analysis (MEE) - An approach to modeling exposure in which long-term exposure of an individual is
\par
simulated as the sum of separate short-term exposure events.
\par
Point Pattern Analysis - A technique in geostatistics of restricting the analysis to location information, ignoring attribute
\par
information, addresses two location problems: (1) describing points according to spacing, and (2) describing points
\par
according to density.
\par
Posterior Distribution - A Bayesian term referring to a probability distribution that has been updated with new information.
\par
Prior Distribution - A Bayesian term referring to the hypothesized, expected, or calculated probability distribution for an event
\par
prior to the collection of new information.
\par
Spatial Autocorrelation - The tendency of data from locations that are relatively close together to be geographically correlated.
\par
Thiessen (Voronoi) Polygon Analysis - A method of spatial statistics in which an area is subdivided into subregions, or
\par
polygons, in order to predict values at unobserved locations.
\par
Time Step - A modeling term used to describe the time interval within which variable values do not change.
\par
Two-Dimensional Monte Carlo analysis (2-D MCA) - Separate representation of variability and uncertainty in an MCA,
\par
usually accomplished using nested computation loops.
\par
In the example shown in Figure D-1, the mean and standard deviation for soil concentration were allowed
\par
to vary independently. Thus, a distribution could be defined by a combination of a low mean and a high
\par
standard deviation, high mean and low standard deviation, or any other combination in between. The
\par
assumption of independence of variable parameters may not be valid in all cases. It may be unreasonable
\par
to assume that a high mean soil concentration would occur with a low standard deviation. An alternative
\par
assumption would be that the standard deviation of the mean is a constant proportion of the mean (i.e., a
\par
constant coefficient of variation). Correlations between parameters should be considered in the design of
\par
the PRA. One approach that is especially useful for characterizing relationships between the slope and
\par
intercept of a simple linear regression is to specify the bivariate normal distribution for the parameter
\par
estimates.
\par
D.2.0 TWO-DIMENSIONAL MONTE CARLO ANALYSIS (2-D MCA)
\par
Two-dimensional MCA is an approach for computing risk (or hazard) when combining
\par
distributions that represent variability and uncertainty. In 2-D MCA, distributions representing variability
\par
and uncertainty are sampled using nested computational loops (Figure D-2). The inner loop simulates
\par
variability by repeatedly sampling values for each variable from their defined probability distributions.
\par
With each circuit of the outer loop, new parameter values for each variable are selected, and the inner
\par
loop sampling is repeated. The result is a collection of inner loop simulations, one for each parameter
\par
value selected. If the inner loop samples 5,000 times, and the outer loop samples 1,000 times, then each
