D.3.0 MICROEXPOSURE EVENT ANALYSIS
\par
The standard dose equation
\par
generally used in Superfund site risk
\par
assessments represents exposures
\par
averaged over a specified time period
\par
that is relevant to the health endpoint of
\par
concern (Equation D-1). If the risk
\par
assessment is directed at assessing life-
\par
time risk to humans, the averaging time
\par
used in Equation D-1 would generally be
\par
70 years (i.e., estimated average human
\par
lifetime), and the calculated chemical
\par
intake would generally represent the life-
\par
time average daily dose (LADD). Where
\par
information is available to characterize
\par
variability on a smaller time scale than
\par
life-time, an alternative expression of
\par
dose that accommodates such variability
\par
may be desirable.
\par
Concentrations in various
\par
environmental media can be expected to
\par
vary over time. For example, wind
\par
erosion may change chemical
\par
concentrations in surface soil. Leaching may change concentrations in both subsurface soil and
\par
groundwater. The change in the concentration term is most readily apparent when considering anglers
\par
harvesting fish. If an angler consumes a large amount of fish from a single location (e.g., a specific lake,
\par
pond, or river), then the average chemical concentration in the fish consumed by that angler can be
\par
expected to be similar to the average of the chemical concentration of fish in the population. However, if
\par
an angler consumes fish only occasionally, or harvests fish from different locations, there will be
\par
considerably more uncertainty in the concentration term. In addition, a harvesting angler may consume
\par
varying amounts of fish over the period of the exposure duration due to changing tastes, changes in the
\par
fish population size or other factors.
\par
Daily activity patterns, food intake, soil ingestion and other behavioral factors are measured in a
\par
time period of less than a year. The extrapolation of these short term results to the chronic exposure
\par
situation is a source of uncertainty. Exposure events are real but unknowable, whereas data regarding the
\par
nature and magnitude of these events is known but its application to a real world situation is uncertain.
\par
Microexposure event analysis (MEE) attempts to explicitly quantify this uncertainty. Figure D-5 presents
\par
the general approach for MEE analysis. (Price et al., 1996, 2000). MEE modeling provides an alternative
\par
to the standard time-averaging approach represented by Equation D-1. In the MEE approach, long term
\par
intake is viewed as the sum of individual exposure events (Equation D-2). Implementing the MEE
\par
approach in a PRA requires dividing the exposure duration into short epochs, or time steps, within which
\par
the values assigned to exposure variables remain constant, but are allowed to vary from one time step to
\par
the next. In a PRA model, exposure variables are adjusted at each time step by selecting values from the
\par
probability distributions representing each variable (Figure D-4). Discussion of the implementation of
