\par
Two important issues related to time step should be considered in implementing the MEE
\par
approach in PRA models. The first is the relationship between the length of the time step and the number
\par
of times random values are generated from a defined probability distribution. As the time step decreases,
\par
more time steps are needed to simulate exposures over a specified duration. For example, given a time
\par
step of one year and an exposure duration of 30 years, each random variable will be sampled 30 times
\par
(once per year); for a time step of one month and an exposure duration of 30 years, each random variable
\par
would be sampled 360 times (i.e., 12 months/year x 30 years). The Central Limit Theorem indicates that
\par
as n increases, the distribution of sample means is approximately normal, and the standard deviation of
\par
the sample distribution is inversely proportional to the square root of n. Thus a highly skewed input
\par
distribution (e.g., lognormal) may tend to become less skewed with increasing n (Figure D-6). A biased
\par
estimate of the RME risk in a PRA model may result if an inappropriately small or large time step is used
\par
in the model. This emphasizes the importance of having an empirical basis for selecting the time step and
\par
of exploring the time step as a variable in a sensitivity analysis of the model.
\par
The second issue related to the time step concerns temporal correlations. Is it reasonable to
\par
assume that random values selected for consecutive time steps are completely independent? For example,
\par
consider body weight. The body weights of an individual measured at different times would be expected
\par
to show positive temporal autocorrelation; that is, body weight is likely to be similar (but not constant)
\par
from one time step to the next. For example, if an individual weighs 60 kg during one month, it is
\par
unlikely that they will weigh 80 kg the next month. If this scenario is accepted, then body weight should
\par
not be allowed to vary independently from one monthly time step to the next in the model. At shorter
\par
time steps, temporal correlation becomes more likely as a result of temporal autocorrelation. For
\par
example, one can expect a higher correlation between body weights on an individual measured on two
\par
successive days (one-day time step) than between weights measured at the midpoint of two successive
\par
years. Approaches to simulating temporal correlations in probabilistic models might include fixing an
\par
individual within a percentile range of a distribution (e.g., randomly assigned quartile) or using randomly
\par
assigned fluctuations (e.g., BWt = BWt-1 \textpm{} x).
