D.4.0 GEOSPATIAL STATISTICS
\par
Spatial statistics is a specialized branch
\par
of statistics, falling under the heading of
\par
multivariate statistics, that explicitly takes into
\par
account the georeferenced or locational tagged
\par
context of data. Generally, environmental
\par
samples collected at Superfund sites have this
\par
geolocational information By acknowledging the
\par
geography of site chemicals, information about
\par
the spatial distribution of contamination can be
\par
incorporated into an exposure assessment. In
\par
addition, knowledge about a receptors home
\par
range or patterns of movement may also be
\par
incorporated into the definition of the exposure unit (see Appendix C, Section C.2.0). Explicitly
\par
accounting for spatial relationships may lead to a more accurate estimate of the confidence limits for the
\par
arithmetic mean concentration. Geospatial statistics quantifies the spatial autocorrelation (Exhibit D-2) of
\par
sample measurements and allows for the exploration of the spatial distribution of exposure and risk using
\par
techniques of map generalization. By recording locational tags for each sample, information about spatial
\par
patterns within an exposure unit (EU) can be exploited to estimate both pre- and post-remediation
\par
exposure and risk.
\par
In the past five years, with rapidly expanding software and hardware capabilities, some examples
\par
of the application of geostatistics can be found in exposure assessment and remedial design (e.g.,
\par
Gomez-Hernandez, 1996; Goovaerts, 1996, 1997; Kriakidis, 1996; Ginevan and Splitstone, 1997;
\par
McKenna, 1998; Hope, 2000; 2001) as well as site assessment guidance (e.g., U.S. EPA, 2000).
\par
Several important risk assessment issues
\par
are closely linked to geospatial statistics, as
\par
described in Exhibit D-3. Geospatial statistics
\par
comprises:
\par
{$\bullet$} spatial autoregression
\par
{$\bullet$} geostatistics
\par
{$\bullet$} point pattern analysis
\par
{$\bullet$} image analysis
\par
The first three of these subjects can
\par
contribute to spatial statistical support of site risk
\par
assessments. The key concept linking all three is
\par
spatial autocorrelation, which refers to covariation
\par
among samples for a single chemical, or the
\par
tendency of data from locations that are relatively
\par
close together to be geographically correlated. By
\par
analogy, classical statistics treats soil samples as
\par
though they are balls, each having a battery of
\par
attributes, that can be placed into an urn for
\par
statistical analysis; geospatial statistics treats soil
\par
samples as though they are clusters of grapes,
