How is Geostatistics Different from Classical Statistics?
\par
In general, geostatistics provides information beyond that provided by classical statistical
\par
techniques for at least two reasons. First, in classical statistics, observations are assumed to be
\par
independent. This assumption is often invalid at contaminated sites where the method by which a
\par
chemical is released into the environment (e.g., deposition form airborne emissions; migration of
\par
contaminant plume from a point source) often results in positive spatial autocorrelation (see
\par
Section D.4.1). In other words, observations located next to each other tend to contain similar levels of
\par
contamination (i.e., redundant information). For example, the higher the spatial autocorrelation, the less
\par
incremental information is provided by adding observations in close proximity to existing observations.
\par
This issue is compounded when the sample locations have been preferentially determined (e.g., ``hot spot''
\par
sampling) rather than distributed at regular intervals or specified using random sampling methodology.
\par
Second, geostatistics is able to use the geospatial information contained in the data to model
\par
uncertainty in contaminant concentrations for areas where data are scarce, a situation commonly
\par
encountered in site assessment work. Using geostatistics, information from samples collected from
\par
outside an EU can be used to model the uncertainty in the mean concentration within an EU. Approaches
\par
that do not consider the geospatial information present in the data are limited to the subset of samples
\par
within an EU.
\par
D.4.1 CORRELATION AND SPATIAL AUTOCORRELATION
\par
Several simple bivariate statistical approaches may be used to introduce the concept of spatial
\par
autocorrelation. Consider two variables, X and Y. For positive correlation there is a tendency for high
\par
values of X to be paired with the high values of Y, medium values of X to be with the medium values of
\par
Y, and low values of X with the low values of Y. The tendency is in the opposite direction for negative
\par
correlation; high values of X tend to be paired with low values of Y, and so on. Spatial autocorrelation,
\par
which virtually always is positive, directly parallels these definitions, but is written in terms of a single
\par
variable as shown in Exhibit D-2.
\par
Just as the bivariate relationship between two variables, X and Y, can be portrayed by a scatter
\par
plot (Y versus X), the spatial autocorrelation relationship can be portrayed for a single variable, Y, (e.g.,
\par
Y versus Y). A good example is the Moran scatterplot, which plots the sum or average of nearby values
\par
of Y versus Y. This plot is most effective when Y has been converted to z-scores. As shown in
\par
Figure D-7 and Section D.4.2, scatter plots can be used to illustrate some important issues related to
\par
sample size.
