If no soil samples were collected at a site (n=0), there is no information about the chemical
\par
concentrations in soil, and any guess may be considered an estimate. However, if the chemical
\par
concentration of a single sample (n=1) is measured, some information is obtained that partly restricts this
\par
estimate. As each additional independent sample is taken, more information is obtained, and the
\par
restriction on the estimate becomes more binding. If the same location is selected repeatedly for
\par
sampling, then the repeated measures, which may vary through time, will tend to be highly positively
\par
correlated; part of the information obtained from each sample is the same, and should not be counted
\par
more than once in estimating the site-wide soil concentration. Similarly, if immediately adjacent
\par
locations are sampled, the measures will often tend to be highly positively correlated (spatial
\par
autocorrelation). Once the first sample is taken, each additional sample provides only a fractional
\par
increment of new information about the site in its entirety.
\par
D.4.2 EFFECTIVE SAMPLE SIZE (N*) AND DEGREES OF FREEDOM
\par
Repeated measures can result in data clustering, which can be illustrated in a scatter diagram.
\par
Because two points determine a straight line, if (n--1) points cluster together on a scatter diagram while a
\par
single additional point occurs far away from this cluster (i.e., an outlier), then the resulting bivariate
\par
correlation will be very high (see Figure D-7). This situation alludes to the notion of effective sample
\par
size (n*): the n* is no longer equal to the number of observations (n), but rather is dramatically reduced
\par
by the presence of inter-observational correlation. For the example shown in Figure D-7, n* is slightly
\par
greater than 2 rather than 100 (i.e., n).
