Spatial autocorrelation plays an analogous role in
\par
georeferenced data. If a sampling network is arranged as
\par
a 25-by-25 square grid (one sample point per grid cell),
\par
and superimposed over a large site so that a very large
\par
distance separates nearby sample locations, then
\par
essentially zero spatial autocorrelation should be present
\par
in the geographic distribution of the concentrations of any
\par
given chemical. Concentrations will appear to be
\par
haphazard across the site, rendering the effective sample
\par
size as n*=625. If the distance between nearby locations
\par
on the sampling mesh is decreased so that the spatial
\par
correlation is only r=0.050, then the effective sample size
\par
decreases to n*=514. The effect of reducing the inter-
\par
sample distance on spatial autocorrelation and n* for a
\par
25-by-25 grid is shown in Exhibit D-4. If r increases to
\par
1, then n* reduces to 1. Therefore, obtaining a measure
\par
of latent spatial autocorrelation is essential to estimating
\par
n*; this in turn is critical to determining confidence limits
\par
for estimates of mean concentrations, which are sensitive to sample size. The UCL for the mean will be
\par
biased only when very high levels of spatial autocorrelation are present; this is because the Student-t
\par
statistic used to estimate the UCL (assuming a normal distribution) changes very little as the degrees of
\par
freedom (related to sample size) increases above 10; part of the difference between n and n* is offset by
\par
an inflation of the variance.
\par
The concept of effective degrees of freedom is important in exposure assessment because high
\par
positive spatial autocorrelation can bias the estimate of the UCL concentration if geospatial statistics are
\par
not considered. This should be of particular concern when specific locations at a site are intensively
\par
sampled (e.g., suspected ``hot spots''), and other locations are relatively undersampled. Accordingly, the
\par
design of the sampling network itself can be evaluated from the perspective of geospatial statistics in
\par
order to ascertain the quality of sample information. The ideal sampling network should provide
\par
geographic representativeness, should be roughly uniformly distributed over a site, and is best
\par
implemented as a stratified random sampling design; that is, the site is partitioned into geographic stratum
\par
(e.g., EUs), and then a random sampling of points is selected within each strata. In practice, sample
\par
designs may need to focus on objectives that are in conflict with the above ideals. For example, intense
\par
sampling of suspected ``hotspots'' may be necessary at some sites, at the expense of a more representative
\par
spatial coverage of the site. In such cases, several statistical techniques are available for assessing the
\par
statistical benefit (in terms of reducing uncertainty) of additional sampling at undersampled locations.
\par
