D.4.4 MAP GENERALIZATION
\par
Another important application of geospatial statistics to risk assessment is that of map
\par
generalization, which draws on the subjects of geostatistics and spatial autoregression. Techniques
\par
developed for both topics exploit spatial autocorrelation in order to produce a map.
\par
Kriging and Semivariograms. Geostatistics may employ kriging, which yields statistical
\par
guesses at values of a chemical at unsampled locations based on information obtained from sampled
\par
locations. Kriging assumes that the underlying geographic distribution is continuous, evaluates spatial
\par
autocorrelation in terms of distance separating sample points, and employs a scatter diagram similar to the
\par
Moran scatter plot to portray this relationship (i.e., the semivariogram plot: half the squared difference
\par
between measured concentrations for two sampled locations versus distance separating these two
\par
locations). The best-fit line to this scatter of points is described by one of about a dozen equations
\par
(semivariogram models).
\par
Many different kriging approaches can be applied to quantify the spatial relationships among
\par
geographic attributes within an exposure unit. For example, site-specific chemical concentrations may be
\par
correlated with geologic information, such as glacial deposits, soil characteristics of core samples, and
\par
attributes that represent favorable habitats for ecological receptors. This information can be used to
\par
expand the available data and improve estimates of chemical concentrations at unsampled locations by
\par
employing a technique called co-kriging.
\par
Thiessen Polygons and Spatial Autoregression. Spatial autoregression assumes a discretized
\par
surface, uses the Thiessen polygon surface partitioning to construct a Moran scatter plot, and can be used
\par
to estimate values at selected points with a regression-type equation. Theoretically, the exponential
\par
semivariogram model relates to the conditional autoregressive model, and the Bessel function
\par
semivariogram model relates to the simultaneous autoregressive model; in practice, though, the spherical
\par
semivariogram model often provides the best description of a semivariogram plot. Regardless of which
\par
approach is taken to map generalization, one relevant contribution of these two subjects is the following
\par
observation:
\par
L Including positive spatial autocorrelation results in more accurate
\par
variance estimates; this in turn yields more accurate estimates of the
\par
95\% UCL for the mean concentration.
\par
