D.4.5 IMPLEMENTATION ISSUES RELATED TO GEOREFERENCED DATA
\par
Estimation of parameters, for either geostatistical or spatial autoregressive models, cannot be
\par
achieved with ordinary least squares (OLS) techniques; nonlinear least squares must be used. While OLS
\par
provides unbiased regression coefficients, these estimates are not necessarily sufficient (i.e., they do not
\par
summarize all of the information in a sample pertaining to the population), efficient (i.e., the standard
\par
errors often are incorrect), and consistent (i.e., the asymptotic sampling distribution concentration will not
\par
be at the parameter value). In other words, OLS essentially uses the wrong degrees of freedom in its
\par
calculations, as described in Section D.4.2. Two additional complications of georeferenced data that do
\par
not appear in other types of data are (1) spatial autocorrelation might be directional (i.e., directional
\par
dependency); and (2) variance might be nonconstant over space as well as over the magnitude of the
\par
dependent variable, Y (e.g., chemical concentration). Several statistical approaches, which are beyond
\par
the scope of this guidance, are available for analyzing these potential sources of bias in the exposure
\par
concentration estimates (Isaaks and Srivastava, 1989; Cressie, 1991; Griffith, 1993; Ginevan and
\par
Splitstone, 1997).
\par
D.5.0 EXPERT JUDGMENT AND BAYESIAN ANALYSIS
\par
Up to this point in RAGS Volume 3: Part A, risk has been characterized as having a population
\par
probability distribution with parameters (e.g., mean, standard deviation) that can, theoretically, be
\par
estimated from observation. In theory, risk estimates could be derived by repeatedly measuring risk in
\par
subsets of the population of interest (e.g., repeated measurements of site-related cancer risk). The
\par
unstated expectation, or goal, is that the PRA model will accurately simulate this real risk distribution.
\par
This approach derives from a classical view of probability. The classical or frequentist view defines the
\par
probability of an event as the frequency with which it occurs in a long sequence of similar trials. From
\par
the frequentist perspective, the probability of having a flipped coin land heads-up is given by the
\par
frequency distribution of heads-up results derived from repeated similar trials of coin flips. For real-
\par
world decisions such as those informed by Superfund risk assessments, there is uncertainty that the
\par
sample data are representative of the population (see Chapter 1, Section 1.2.4).
\par
Bayesian View of Probability. A Bayesian perspective on probability allows distributions to be
\par
constructed based on the judgment of an expert in the field. The subjectivist or Bayesian view is that the
\par
probability of an event occurring is the degree of belief a person has in the occurrence. Probabilities can
\par
be assessed by experts using scientific knowledge, judgment, data, past experience, and intuition.
\par
Different people may assign different probabilities to an event, and a single individual may assign
\par
different probabilities to the same event when considered at different times. The consequence is that
\par
probabilities become conditional and the conditions must be explicitly stated (Howson and Urbach, 1989;
\par
Morgan and Henrion, 1990; Ott, 1995; Sivia, 1996). These conditional probabilities can, of course, be
\par
updated with new information.
\par
Using the coin flip analogy above, a Bayesian perspective might be that, based on experience
\par
with coins, assuming that most coins are fair, and that a fair coin would be expected to land heads-up half
\par
the time, the expected probability of the tossed coin landing heads-up is 0.5. If the outcome of repeated
\par
trials was different from the expected, the Bayesian approach would be to update the probability based on
\par
the new data. In the coin flip example, both the Bayesian and frequentist approaches will arrive at the
\par
same conclusions, because the outcome is amenable to rigorous experimentation. Where the two
\par
approaches can be expected to differ is in the assignment of probabilities to events that cannot be
\par
rigorously measured; for example, the probability of a site-related cancer risk, or the probability of a child
\par
ingesting a specific amount of soil.
\par
Page D-16RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process For Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix D \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
The subjective judgment of experts is, therefore, an important tool in the Bayesian approach to
\par
risk assessment. For example, the input distributions for a PRA may be based upon the judgment of one
\par
or more experts who rely upon estimates from the literature, data from experimental studies, and any
\par
other information they consider relevant. Even when formal elicitations of expert opinion are not done,
\par
the final selection of the form and parameters of the input distributions usually involves some subjective
\par
judgment by the analyst. One of the challenges of incorporating judgments from experts or lay people is
\par
that there can be overconfidence bias (i.e., people tend to underestimate their uncertainty). There is a rich
\par
literature about the protocol for conducting expert elicitations and using the results to support decisions
\par
(Lichtenstein and Fischoff, 1977; Morgan and Henrion, 1990; Shlyakhter and Kammen, 1992).
\par
Elicitation of expert judgment has been used to obtain distributions for use in risk assessments (Morgan
\par
and Henrion, 1990; Hora, 1992; U.S. EPA, 1997;) and in developing air quality standards (U.S. EPA,
\par
1982).
