of serum TCDD. This is the posterior distribution (P(R\textbar D)).
\par
Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis. In the past, the use of Bayesian analysis was limited by the
\par
degree of mathematical complexity involved. Using Monte Carlo analysis to carry out the PRA, rather
\par
than mathematical equations to describe the distributions, allows the calculations to be done much more
\par
easily. This variation on traditional Bayesian methods is called Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis
\par
(Patwardan and Small, 1992; Dakins et al., 1996). In the TCDD example discussed above and illustrated
\par
in Figure D-7, the required calculations are carried out for each of the N iterations of the Monte Carlo
\par
analysis (I and j go from 1 to N).
\par
Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis is appropriate in several situations. If a model has been created
\par
and a distribution developed using PRA, new information may be incorporated without the need to repeat
\par
the entire analysis. This information could be on one of the uncertain parameters of the model or on the
\par
model output variable. Similarly, a generalized risk model with generic parameter distributions may be
\par
used for a Superfund risk assessment with the model predictions fine-tuned using data from a particular
\par
site of interest. Finally, after a distribution is developed, the amount of uncertainty that exists may be too
\par
large for the risk manager to make a decision. In this case, the risk manager might seek out new
\par
information that would refine the analysis and decrease the uncertainty.
\par
Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis can also be combined with techniques from decision analysis to
\par
help determine the type and quantity of data that should be collected to reduce uncertainty. Decision
\par
analysis is a technique used to help organize and structure the decision maker's thought process and
\par
identify a best strategy for action. To determine the appropriate action, one defines the range of possible
\par
decisions, evaluates the expected value of the utility or loss function associated with each decision, and
\par
selects the decision that maximizes the expected utility or minimizes the expected loss.
\par
L Decision analysis provides a quantitative approach for evaluating the
\par
benefits of including an expanded assessment of uncertainty and the
\par
subsequent benefits of reducing this uncertainty.
\par
Value of Information. Value of information (VOI) analysis involves estimating the value that
\par
new information can have to a risk manager before that information is actually obtained (Clemen, 1996).
\par
It's a measure of the importance of uncertainty in terms of the expected improvement in a risk
\par
management decision that might come from better information. Examples of VOI quantities are the
\par
expected value of including uncertainty (EVIU), the expected value of sample information (EVSI), the
\par
expected value of perfect information (EVPI). Calculation of these quantities can be done using
\par
mathematical methods, numerical integration (Finkel and Evans, 1987), or Monte Carlo techniques
\par
(Dakins, 1999)
\par
Page D-19RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process For Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix D \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
Value of information calculations require the specification of either a utility or a loss function. A
\par
loss function states the losses associated with making different types of decision errors including both
\par
direct monetary costs and losses associated with other consequences. Loss functions take various forms
\par
depending on the risk management situation (Morgan and Henrion, 1990).
\par
Expected Value of Including Uncertainty. The expected value of including uncertainty, EVIU,
\par
is a measure of the value of carrying out a PRA. It's the difference between the expected loss of a
\par
decision based on a point estimate risk assessment and the expected loss of the decision that considers
\par
uncertainty (Figure D-9). If uncertainty in a risk assessment has been estimated using Monte Carlo
\par
techniques and a loss function has been specified, the EVIU can be easily calculated. First, the
\par
management decision from the point estimate assessment is determined. The loss from making this
\par
decision is calculated for each iteration of the Monte Carlo, each time assuming that the risk estimate
\par
from that iteration is true. The expected loss is the average of these individual losses. The expected loss
\par
for the PRA is determined by calculating the expected loss for a full range of management decisions and
\par
selecting the decision with the lowest expected loss. The EVIU is calculated by subtracting the loss
\par
associated with the PRA from that associated with the point estimate risk assessment.
\par
Expected Value of Sample Information. The expected value of sample information is the
\par
difference between the expected loss of the decision based on the PRA and the expected loss of the
\par
decision from an improved information state. As such, the EVSI is a measure of the value that may result
\par
from the collection and use of new information (Figure D-9). Calculation of the EVSI involves a
\par
technique called preposterior analysis and is somewhat more complicated.
\par
This type of analysis is termed ``preposterior'' because it involves the possible posterior
\par
distributions resulting from potential samples that have not yet been taken. For each replication from the
\par
Monte Carlo simulation, the predicted value from the model is used to randomly generate a set of K data
\par
points. Each set of data points is then used to calculate the posterior probabilities for the N Monte Carlo
\par
simulated values. These posterior probabilities are then used to obtain the optimal answer to the
\par
management question at this new level of uncertainty by selecting the decision that minimizes the
\par
expected loss over all possible management decisions.
\par
This procedure is repeated for each of the N replications of the Monte Carlo analysis resulting in
\par
N posterior distributions, N management decisions, and N associated expected losses. Because each of
\par
these outcomes is equally weighted, the expected loss associated with the state of uncertainty expected to
\par
exist after the data collection program is carried out is simply the average of the N expected losses. The
\par
EVSI is the difference between the expected loss based on the results of the PRA and the expected loss
\par
from the updated information state.
\par
Expected Value of Perfect Information. The EVPI is the difference between the expected loss
\par
of the decision based on the results of the PRA and the expected loss of the optimal management decision
\par
if all uncertainty were eliminated. In actual application, no research plan or data collection program can
\par
completely eliminate uncertainty, only reduce it. The EVPI is an upper bound for the expected value of
\par
efforts to reduce uncertainty and so provides the ultimate bound on what should be spent on research and
\par
data collection efforts.
