\par
Uses of Value of Information in Risk Assessment. VOI analysis has many benefits for risk
\par
managers. First, VOI analysis makes the losses associated with decision errors explicit, balances
\par
competing probabilities and costs, and helps identify the decision alternative that minimizes the expected
\par
loss. VOI analysis can help a decision maker overcome a fear of uncertainty by developing a method to
\par
handle it. If the losses associated with making a poor decision are unclear, small uncertainties can take on
\par
major importance. Conversely, if the losses associated with different risk management decisions are
\par
similar, little additional effort need be expended to continue to consider the alternatives.
\par
In addition, VOI analysis helps prioritize spending on research. It provides insights into how
\par
resources could be spent to achieve the most cost-effective reduction in uncertainty by identifying which
\par
sources of uncertainty should be reduced, what type of data should be obtained, and how much data is
\par
Page D-21RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde{} Process For Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix D \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
needed. Finally, VOI analysis may help decision makers explain the rationale for their decisions to the
\par
public and help the public understand the multiple objectives considered in managing risks.
\par
Expected Loss is usually greatest when uncertainty in risk estimates is ignored. For example, by
\par
quantifying uncertainty in risk (e.g., 2-D MCA, Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis) a risk manager may
\par
determine that the cleanup level associated with the 90th percentile of the risk distribution (rather than the
\par
95th percentile) is adequately protective. Quantifying uncertainty may also result in lower expected loss
\par
when more soil remediation is required due to the losses associated with possible under-remediation, e.g.,
\par
cost of additional sampling or lost revenue due to failure to meet land use requirements. The expected
\par
loss may be further reduced by collecting additional soil samples, which would presumably reduce
\par
uncertainty in estimates of mean exposure point concentrations. The expected loss may be minimized by
\par
obtaining "perfect" information (i.e., no uncertainty); however, as shown in Figure D-9, EVPI spans a
\par
wide range of expected loss because the value associated with reducing uncertainty may be tempered by
\par
costs associated with additional sampling and analysis. In practice, risk assessors consider this issue
\par
when deciding to obtain additional samples for site characterization.
\par
The decision to obtain additional information in order to reduce uncertainty should be made on a
\par
site-specific basis, taking into account the potential impact that reducing uncertainty may have on the
\par
overall remedial decision. Important questions to consider include: (1) Are the risk estimates sufficiently
\par
sensitive to an exposure variable that collecting further data will reduce uncertainty? and (2) Are the
\par
confidence limits on the 95th percentile risk estimate sufficiently wide that reducing uncertainty may alter
\par
the cleanup goal? An example of decision framework applicable to PRA is presented in Figure D-10.
\par
The framework has three tiers. Tier 1 includes the point estimate approach and an assessment of the need
\par
for PRA. In Tier 2, the EVIU is calculated and, if warranted, a PRA is conducted. In Tier 3, the value of
\par
additional information is assessed and Bayes Theorem would be used to incorporate the new information
\par
and update probability distributions.
\par
Limitations of These Techniques. Figure D-10 illustrates situations where Bayesian analysis
\par
and value of information quantities may not be helpful. For example, if point estimate risk assessment is
\par
selected as the appropriate method, these techniques do not apply. In addition, as site-specific data
\par
become available that are increasingly comprehensive and representative of the population of interest,
\par
Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis and the Monte Carlo analysis using the classical (frequentist) methods
\par
will approach the same result. This is because the site-specific data are incorporated into both
\par
approaches. To be representative and comprehensive, the data set must be sufficiently large, randomly
\par
selected, and represent the full range of variability that exists in the population (e.g., temporal, spatial,
\par
inter-individual). However, data sets are rarely perfect, often too small, suffer from relatively high
\par
sampling and/or measurement errors, or don't represent the entire population variability over time, space,
\par
age, gender, or other important variables. If the data cannot be assumed to describe the population
\par
distribution sufficiently well, then PRA will help to more fully develop the entire range of the population
\par
distribution and the Bayesian Monte Carlo analysis will act to refine the model estimates.
