\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
This section presents a few questions and answers relating to probabilistic risk assessment (PRA).
\par
The purpose of the frequently asked questions (FAQs) is to facilitate the understanding of PRA using a
\par
comparison with the traditional point estimate approach to risk assessment.
\par
The FAQs presented here provide an overview of PRA with pointers to more detailed, and often
\par
more technical, discussions in other parts of the guidance.
\par
(1) What is a risk assessment?
\par
Risk assessment is a tool for organizing available information to make inferences about the
\par
potential human health or ecological effects associated with exposure to hazardous materials. The
\par
National Contingency Plan (NCP) addresses the use of a baseline risk assessment at Superfund sites to
\par
determine whether risks to human health and the environment are unacceptable. The NCP implements
\par
the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (CERCLA) of 1980.
\par
Risk assessments traditionally provide single point descriptors of risk (e.g., a central tendency
\par
exposure (CTE) risk descriptor or a reasonable maximum exposure (RME) risk descriptor). As such,
\par
these types of risk assessments have been referred to as point estimate risk assessments.
\par
In 1983, the National Research Council (NRC) described the following four steps for conducting
\par
human health risk assessments:
\par
{$\bullet$} Hazard identification: the determination of whether a particular chemical is or is not causally
\par
linked to a particular health effect.
\par
{$\bullet$} Dose-response assessment: the determination of the relation between the magnitude of
\par
exposure and the probability of occurrence of the health effects in question.
\par
{$\bullet$} Exposure assessment: the determination of the extent of human exposure before or after
\par
application of regulatory controls.
\par
{$\bullet$} Risk characterization: a description of the nature and often the magnitude of human risk,
\par
including attendant uncertainty (NRC, 1983).
\par
Readers are referred to risk assessment guidance documents such as Risk Assessment Guidance
\par
for Superfund (RAGS): Volume I. Human Health Evaluation Manual (HHEM) (Part A, Baseline Risk
\par
Assessment)(U.S. EPA, 1989a), Risk Assessment Guidance for Superfund: Volume II. Environmental
\par
Evaluation Manual (U.S. EPA, 1989b), and Ecological Risk Assessment Guidance for Superfund:
\par
Process for Designing and Conducting Ecological Risk Assessments (U.S. EPA, 1997a) for more
\par
information about point estimate risk assessment methods and policies.
\par
