\par
(2) What is a probabilistic risk assessment (PRA)?
\par
Superfund risk assessments have traditionally provided single point estimates of risk. More
\par
recently, PRAs have been developed. A PRA is a risk assessment that provides a probability distribution,
\par
rather than a point estimate, of risk. A probability distribution conveys both a range of values and a
\par
likelihood of occurrence of each value. This may allow a risk assessor to make statements about the
\par
likelihood that risks will exceed a level of concern. The probability distribution for risk often represents
\par
variability in risk estimates for a potentially exposed population. This variability may be due to
\par
variability in exposure and/or toxicity. PRA may also be used to quantify uncertainty in risk estimates.
\par
This can be useful because it allows a risk assessor to make statements about the level of confidence in
\par
the likelihood that risks will exceed a level of concern.
\par
Probabilistic methods often use computer simulations to combine multiple probabilistic
\par
distributions in a risk equation. Monte Carlo analysis (MCA) is perhaps the most widely used
\par
probabilistic method in PRA (see Question \#7).
\par
(3) How does PRA compare with the point estimate approach?
\par
A single point estimate of risk does not explicitly characterize associated variability or
\par
uncertainty. However, multiple point estimates of risk (e.g., CTE or RME) can begin to characterize
\par
variability in risk as they use different points on each input distribution for exposure). A PRA can
\par
characterize variability in risk by using the full distribution of variability in exposure parameters in the
\par
risk equations. Advanced PRA techniques can also quantitatively characterize uncertainty. In
\par
appropriate circumstances, results of a PRA can lead to more informed risk management decisions.
\par
A PRA can be more resource intensive than a point estimate risk assessment. Some PRAs can
\par
require greater effort than point estimate approaches to define model inputs (i.e., select and fit probability
\par
distributions), as well as additional steps in the planning, review, and communication of the risk
\par
assessment assumptions and results (see Chapter 6 and Appendix F). A PRA does not necessarily require
\par
more data than a point estimate approach, although it does provide a framework for incorporating more of
\par
the available information into the risk assessment. When information on important exposure variables is
\par
lacking, results from a point estimate approach and a probabilistic approach will be equally uncertain.
\par
If a decision is made to conduct a PRA, this does not replace a point estimate risk assessment.
\par
Results of point estimate approaches should still be presented along with results of probabilistic
\par
approaches in Tier 2 or Tier 3.
\par
(4) Why should I consider using PRA?
\par
PRA can have several advantages over the traditional point estimate approach to risk assessment.
\par
PRA can often provide a more complete characterization of risk; a quantitative description of the
\par
uncertainties in the risk estimates; more informative sensitivity analysis; the ability to make probabilistic
\par
statements about risk; the ability to know where specific risk levels are on the potential distribution of
\par
risk; an increased understanding of risks; and opportunities for improved communication and risk
\par
management decision making.
\par
