\par
(5) When should I consider using PRA?
\par
A PRA may be considered as early as the planning stages of a point estimate risk assessment or as
\par
late as after the completion of a point estimate risk assessment. Ideally, PRA should be considered as
\par
early as possible in the planning of risk assessment activities at a site so that sampling plans and data
\par
collection efforts may be appropriately directed. A PRA may be used when the risk management decision
\par
is not apparent and when the results of a PRA may inform the risk management decision. Often a risk
\par
management decision is not apparent when the site-specific risk estimate is close to the regulatory level of
\par
concern. The NCP discusses a generally acceptable range for cumulative excess cancer risk of 1E-06 to
\par
1E-04 for protecting human health (U.S. EPA, 1990). Noncancer risks to human health and ecological
\par
health are generally characterized by a ratio of exposure to toxicity, called a Hazard Quotient (HQ) or
\par
Hazard Index (HI) for multiple contaminants. The point of departure for evaluating noncancer risks may
\par
vary from site to site, but a HQ of 1 may be a good starting point for risk management decisions.
\par
PRA may also be considered when the results of the point estimate risk assessment suggest that
\par
risks are clearly above a risk level of concern, and a preliminary remediation goal (PRG) is needed.
\par
Because PRA and point estimate risk assessments use different techniques for quantifying variability and
\par
uncertainty, they may support different PRGs. If the results are dramatically different, further steps may
\par
be warranted to reevaluate the choices for input variables - both the point estimates, and the probability
\par
distributions and parameters (including truncation limits) for the 1-D MCA.
\par
PRA will not be needed in many cases. Point risk estimates often produce results which are
\par
sufficient for making remedial decisions (e.g., sites are usually either heavily contaminated or only
\par
marginally contaminated). A tiered approach to risk assessment has been developed by Environmental
\par
Protection Agency (EPA) and is recommended for use in deciding when to move from point estimate risk
\par
assessments to PRAs of varying complexities. A workplan should be developed and submitted for review
\par
before beginning a PRA at any stage in the tiered process. As a general rule, if the potential value added
\par
by a PRA outweighs the additional resource required to conduct it, PRA may be warranted (see
\par
Chapter 2).
\par
(6) How is the risk distribution from PRA used for decision making?
\par
The EPA's RAGS Volume I (U.S. EPA, 1989a) and the NCP Preamble (U.S. EPA, 1990) state
\par
that the RME will generally be the principal basis for evaluating potential human health risks at
\par
Superfund sites. Ecological assessments also often consider an RME endpoint. The point estimate
\par
Superfund risk assessments use a combination of average and high-end input values to arrive at the RME.
\par
In PRA, risks are described by a probability distribution instead of a point estimate. To use a risk
\par
distribution for decision making, one needs to identify a percentile value that corresponds to the RME.
\par
EPA's Guidelines for Exposure Assessment (U.S. EPA, 1992a) states that, ``the high-end risk means risks
\par
above the 90th percentile of the population distribution'', and ``the high-end estimator should not exceed
\par
the 99.9th percentile'' due to uncertainty in specifying the upper tail of the input distributions in a Monte
\par
Carlo analysis. Similarly, the 90th to 99.9th percentiles of the risk distribution are recommended in this
\par
guidance as the RME range for decision making in PRA. Selection of a single point within the RME
\par
range generally requires consideration of the level of uncertainty in the risk distribution. The EPA
\par
recommends that the 95th percentile of the risk distribution be used as a starting point for risk management
\par
decisions in the absence of site-specific information.
\par
