\par
(7) What is Monte Carlo Analysis (MCA)?
\par
MCA is a numerical technique for PRA. MCA was developed in the 1940's during the
\par
beginnings of the nuclear power industry. MCA combines statistical analysis with modern computational
\par
techniques to calculate risk estimates, by randomly choosing different sets of input values each time.
\par
Each calculation is an iteration and a set of iterations is called a simulation. The output of a simulation
\par
used for risk assessment is a continuous probability distribution, which can be displayed in a graph in the
\par
form of either a probability density function (PDF) or corresponding cumulative distribution function
\par
(CDF). Both displays represent the same distribution, but are useful for conveying different information.
\par
For example, the PDF for risk is a good way for displaying relative probability using an area under the
\par
bell-shaped curve. The CDF for risk is generally S-shaped and can be especially informative for
\par
illustrating the percentile corresponding to a particular risk level of concern (e.g., 95th percentile=1E-06).
\par
Other uses of PDFs and CDFs are presented in Chapter 1, Exhibit 1-3. In 1997, EPA published a policy
\par
accepting the use of MCA to perform human health and ecological risk assessments (U.S. EPA, 1997a).
\par
This guidance focuses on MCA as a method of quantifying variability and uncertainty.
\par
(8) What is the policy on using PRA to characterize variability or uncertainty in toxicity or dose
\par
response?
\par
In human health risk assessments, probability distributions for risk should reflect variability or
\par
uncertainty in exposure. In ecological risk assessments, risk distributions may reflect variability or
\par
uncertainty in exposure and/or toxicity (see Chapter 1, Sections 1.4 and 1.4.1, Item 3).
\par
Approaches to characterizing variability and uncertainty in toxicological information should
\par
reflect both the latest developments in the science of hazard and dose-response evaluation and consistent
\par
application of EPA science policy. This statement is consistent with the 1997 EPA Policy Statement
\par
presented in Section 1.4 above (U.S. EPA, 1997g). Probabilistic approaches to ecological dose-response
\par
assessment may be explored, as discussed and demonstrated in Chapter 4. This guidance does not
\par
develop or evaluate probabilistic approaches for dose-response in human health assessment and, further,
\par
discourages undertaking such activities on a site-by-site basis. Such activities require contaminant-
\par
specific national consensus development and national policy development. Parties wishing to undertake
\par
such activities should contact the OERR to explore ways in which they might contribute to a national
\par
process for the contaminant of interest to them.
\par
(9) What is the policy on using PRA at EPA and in Superfund?
\par
In the spring of 1997, EPA released the memorandum, Use of Probabilistic Techniques
\par
(including Monte Carlo Analysis) in Risk Assessment (U.S. EPA, 1997b). The policy states that
\par
probabilistic analysis techniques, ``given adequate supporting data and credible assumptions, can be
\par
viable statistical tools for analyzing variability and uncertainty in risk assessments.'' As such, a PRA,
\par
``will be evaluated and utilized in a manner that is consistent with other risk assessments submitted to the
\par
Agency.'' Together with this Policy Statement, the Agency released a set of guiding principles for use
\par
and review of probabilistic analyses. Hence, both RAGS and Agency-wide guidance emphasize the
\par
importance of review of the scientific and technical merit of a probabilistic analysis to determine whether
\par
or not the assessment is of sufficient quality to support a remedial decision. This guidance, RAGS
\par
Volume 3: Part A, provides risk assessors with comprehensive guidance on when and how to conduct
\par
PRAs using MCA within the Superfund program (see Preface and Chapter 1).
\par
(10) What are the challenges of using PRA?
\par
Page G-4RAGS Volume 3 Part A \textasciitilde Process For Conducting Probabilistic Risk Assessment
\par
Appendix G \textasciitilde{} December 31, 2001
\par
Although PRA may have several advantages over the traditional point estimate approach to risk
\par
assessment, the use of PRA tends to be more resource intensive and may introduce some additional
\par
challenges to risk communication efforts. Risk communication helps build trust with the stakeholders and
\par
disseminate the risk information. In general, EPA staff and stakeholders are accustomed to a point
\par
estimate of risk and are unfamiliar with PRA and the quantitative estimates of uncertainty that PRA can
\par
support. Although, quantitative risk estimates may be more informative, they also may be more difficult
\par
to communicate and may not be well received due to stakeholder desires for certainty (Slovic, et al.
\par
1979). Early and frequent communication with stakeholders is key in implementing PRA successfully.
\par
Often PRA requires additional data collection efforts as well as more time and resources to select and fit
\par
probability distributions.
\par
