\par
Risk Analysis, Vol. 20, No. 1, 2000
\par
Risk Estimation and Value-of-Information Analysis
\par
for Three Proposed Genetic Screening Programs
\par
for Chronic Beryllium Disease Prevention
\par
Scott M. Bartell,1,2 Rafael A. Ponce,1,2 Timothy K. Takaro,2,3 Richard O. Zerbe,4
\par
Gilbert S. Omenn,5 and Elaine M. Faustman1,2,6
\par
Genetic differences (polymorphisms) among members of a population are thought to influence
\par
susceptibility to various environmental exposures. In practice, however, this information is
\par
rarely incorporated into quantitative risk assessment and risk management. We describe an an-
\par
alytic framework for predicting the risk reduction and value-of-information (VOI) resulting
\par
from specific risk management applications of genetic biomarkers, and we apply the frame-
\par
work to the example of occupational chronic beryllium disease (CBD), an immune-mediated
\par
pulmonary granulomatous disease. One described Human Leukocyte Antigen gene variant,
\par
HLA-DP 1*0201, contains a substitution of glutamate for lysine at position 69 that appears to
\par
have high sensitivity ( 94\%) but low specificity ( 70\%) with respect to CBD among indi-
\par
viduals occupationally exposed to respirable beryllium. The expected postintervention CBD
\par
prevalence rates for using the genetic variant (1) as a required job placement screen, (2) as
\par
a medical screen for semiannual in place of annual lymphocyte proliferation testing, or (3) as a
\par
voluntary job placement screen are 0.08\%, 0.8\%, and 0.6\%, respectively, in a hypothetical co-
\par
hort with 1\% baseline CBD prevalence. VOI analysis is used to examine the reduction in total
\par
social cost, calculated as the net value of disease reduction and financial expenditures, expected
\par
for proposed CBD intervention programs based on the genetic susceptibility test. For the ex-
\par
ample cohort, the expected net VOI per beryllium worker for genetically based testing and in-
\par
tervention is \$13,000, \$1,800, and \$5,100, respectively, based on a health valuation of \$1.45 mil-
\par
lion per CBD case avoided. VOI results for alternative CBD valuations are also presented.
\par
Despite large parameter uncertainty, probabilistic analysis predicts generally positive utility for
\par
each of the three evaluated programs when avoidance of a CBD case is valued at \$1 million or
\par
higher. Although the utility of a proposed risk management program may be evaluated solely
\par
in terms of risk reduction and financial costs, decisions about genetic testing and program im-
\par
plementation must also consider serious social, legal, and ethical factors.
\par
KEY WORDS: Value-of-information, beryllium, polymorphism, occupational health, epidemiology
