2. BACKGROUND
\par
Beryllium is a rare metal primarily used in the
\par
ceramics, aircraft, and nuclear industries during the last
\par
half-century, though recent reports indicate new ap-
\par
plications in consumer products such as golf clubs
\par
and mountain bicycles.(6) Respirable beryllium parti-
\par
cles are toxic to humans; they cause pneumonitis
\par
(acute beryllium disease) at high exposures and chronic
\par
beryllium disease (CBD) at lower exposures. CBD is
\par
generally characterized by diffuse interstitial infiltra-
\par
tion, alveolitis, and fibrosis and often involves devel-
\par
opment of granulomas. Beryllium-induced granulo-
\par
mas are histologically indistinguishable from those
\par
found in sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, and Crohn's dis-
\par
ease,(7) or from granulomas caused by aluminum and
\par
titanium.(8) Though beryllium diseases including can-
\par
cer and acute beryllium disease are generally associ-
\par
ated with pulmonary dysfunction, other organs, in-
\par
cluding the lymphatic system, heart, skin, liver, and
\par
spleen, may be affected.(7,9)
\par
After 1949, when an exposure standard of 2 g/
\par
m3 in air was implemented by the U.S. Atomic Energy
\par
Commission, acute beryllium disease was essentially
\par
eliminated in the U.S. but the incidence of CBD con-
\par
tinued unabated. The Occupational Safety and Health
\par
Administration (OSHA) later adopted and currently
\par
enforces the 2 g/m3 standard. CBD, however, is still
\par
prevalent among workers exposed to beryllium dusts,
\par
even at exposures generally below 2 g/m3
\par
(10)
\par
.
\par
The overall prevalence rate of CBD among indi-
\par
viduals known to have worked in beryllium-contami-
\par
nated areas ranges from about 1\% to 5\%.(11) Among
\par
beryllium workers routinely engaged in certain work
\par
tasks, such as machining and ceramics dry pressing,
\par
CBD prevalence is as high as 10\% to 20\%,(10,12)
\par
though associated median daily weighted average be-
\par
ryllium air concentrations were below the OSHA
\par
standard. Though the ``beryllium worker'' character-
\par
ization varies from study to study, most studies now
\par
include administrators, engineers, janitors, and other
\par
nonmachinists if they are known to have regularly
\par
been present in buildings with ongoing or previous
\par
beryllium use or potential exposure. Cases of CBD
\par
have been identified among these individuals(10,12) as
\par
well as among workers' spouses presumably exposed
\par
through contaminated clothing,(13) despite their lack
\par
of direct involvement in high-risk activities.
\par
A beryllium worker health surveillance program
\par
was instituted at the beginning of the decade at the
\par
U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) Oak Ridge Y-12
\par
Plant and the Rocky Flats Environmental Technol-
\par
ogy Site. The program now includes dozens of other
\par
DOE sites where beryllium was used and has identi-
\par
fied approximately 9,000 workers with potential pre-
\par
vious occupational beryllium exposure.(14) Despite ex-
\par
posure reductions to levels generally at or below the
\par
OSHA standard, roughly 1\% of beryllium-exposed
\par
DOE workers have developed CBD.(14)
\par
The similar pathogenesis of CBD to other pul-
\par
monary granulomatous diseases, including sarcoido-
\par
sis, and available animal-based experimental data
\par
suggest that susceptibility to CBD involves a genetic
\par
component. Although a number of genetic polymor-
\par
phisms have been investigated to identify potential
\par
associations with CBD, the presence of a glutamate
\par
for lysine substitution at position 69 of HLA-DP 1
\par
(Glu-69 ) has been found to have the highest correla-
\par
tion with CBD,(2) and has been suggested as a poten-
\par
tial screening tool for occupational CBD risk man-
\par
agement.(15) Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) genes
\par
are a group of genes similar to Major Histocompat-
\par
ability Complex genes found in other species. Certain
\par
HLA genes located in the D-region on the short arm
\par
of chromosome 6 (DP 1, DP 2, DP 1, and DP 2)
\par
produce tetrameric proteins involved in antigen rec-Beryllium Disease and Genetic Screening ognition that bind to endocytosed fragments such as
\par
beryllium. Polymorphisms in the beta chains are
\par
frequent and are suspect contributors to immune-
\par
mediated metal responses.(2)
\par
Another screening biomarker is the beryllium
\par
lymphocyte proliferation test (LPT), for which a pos-
\par
itive result indicates immunologic sensitization to be-
\par
ryllium and heightened susceptibility to CBD. This in
\par
vitro assay consists of an evaluation of the prolifera-
\par
tive response of isolated lymphoctyes to the presence
\par
of beryllium. A positive LPT result is considered a
\par
sign of immunologic sensitization to beryllium and is
\par
usually followed by CBD.(16) At DOE sites, beryllium
\par
workers are currently tested with the LPT every 1 to
\par
3 years. Sensitized workers (as indicated by a con-
\par
firmed positive LPT) are reassigned to jobs without
\par
beryllium exposure and followed up with additional
\par
medical monitoring for CBD.(14)
\par
3. METHODS
\par
In the present analysis we use risk assessment
\par
techniques to assess the impact of using the Glu-69
\par
test as a placement or medical screen for occupa-
\par
tional beryllium exposure. One proposed interven-
\par
tion is the removal of genetically susceptible (i.e.,
\par
Glu-69 ) workers from beryllium-contaminated
\par
areas, which might require job reassignment. This in-
\par
tervention could be required or strictly voluntary.
\par
Another option is more aggressive medical monitor-
\par
ing for genetically susceptible individuals. This might
\par
consist of more frequent LPT, physical examinations,
\par
X-rays, or other clinical screens. Heightened medical
\par
surveillance of Glu-69 individuals might be a rea-
\par
sonable intervention strategy for CBD because indi-
\par
viduals in the early stages of disease would be identi-
\par
fied more quickly, thereby allowing rapid removal
\par
from further exposure to beryllium and earlier treat-
\par
ment where warranted.
\par
We evaluate three specific examples of Glu-
\par
69-based risk management options: (1) required
\par
screening of workers for job placement in beryl-
\par
lium-contaminated areas, (2) increased frequency of
\par
LPT monitoring for Glu-69 beryllium-exposed
\par
workers from annual to semiannual testing, and (3)
\par
voluntary genetic testing and informed decison
\par
making by workers with potential beryllium expo-
\par
sure. The expected reduction in CBD risk and the
\par
implementation cost per person are estimated for
\par
each of these three genetic interventions by using
\par
distributional analysis. We generated 20,000 simple
\par
89
\par
random samples for each variable during Monte
\par
Carlo simulation.
