3.3.2. Value of CBD Avoidance; Cost of Glu-69
\par
Screening and LPT Testing
\par
Bd indicates the economic preference for avoid-
\par
ing a single case of CBD and is typically determined
\par
by using ``cost of illness'' (COI) approaches, ``willing-
\par
ness to pay'' (WTP) measures, or other methods.(24)
\par
COI estimates are obtained by disaggregation of di-
\par
rect and indirect costs (e.g., health care costs and lost
\par
wages). COI estimates underestimate the societal
\par
value of disease avoidance because they place no value
\par
on lost time other than for wage losses. Though they
\par
also fail to value pain and suffering associated with dis-
\par
ease, COI techniques are useful for initially estimating
\par
the value of disease avoidance. WTP approaches im-
\par
plicitly include pain and suffering as well as the COI.
\par
In practice, health valuation estimates vary
\par
considerably according to the methods and assump-
\par
tions chosen. The choice of valuation method and
\par
discount rate strongly influence the final valuation.
\par
We select a variety of estimates based on different
\par
approaches and repeat the risk and VOI calculations
\par
for each estimate.
\par
We obtain a low estimate of the COI for CBD
\par
based on the medical cost savings per CBD case
\par
avoided according to a DOE economic analysis.(25)
\par
The mean valuation for the four potential treatment
\par
scenarios in that analysis is \$12,200. This valuation
\par
uses a discount rate of 7\% and assumes that workers
\par
are diagnosed with CBD at age 40 and live to age 70.
\par
A second COI estimate(21) is based on reported treat-
\par
ment costs and lost wages for general respiratory dis-
\par
eases. For this estimate we assumed 45 years would
\par
be spent with reduced activity and extended the daily
\par
respiratory disease costs throughout that entire pe-
\par
riod without discounting to obtain an expected value
\par
of \$1,450,000 per case of CBD avoided. With a 5-year
\par
latency and a 7\% discount rate, this value is reduced
\par
to approximately \$290,000 per CBD case. We evalu-
\par
ate the results by using both the discounted and non-
\par
discounted values (Table I).92 Bartell et al.
\par
No WTP estimates are currently available for
\par
CBD. However, most individuals diagnosed with
\par
CBD will die from beryllium-related disease.(25) Be-
\par
cause of a combination of high mortality and long-
\par
term morbidity, applying life valuations to CBD may
\par
be appropriate. Estimates for the statistical value of
\par
life run as high as \$16,300,000.(26) We include that fig-
\par
ure as the highest value in our analysis, without dis-
\par
counting for latency.
\par
We estimate that laboratory analysis for Glu-69
\par
will cost \$100 per sample.(21) Allowing an extra \$50
\par
per sample for sample collection and shipment, we
\par
estimate a total cost for Glu-69 testing of \$150/sample
\par
and assign bounds of \$100 and \$500 to allow for a
\par
large degree of error. This parameter (Cgenotype) is rep-
\par
resented by using a triangular distribution (Table I).
\par
Voluntary preplacement screening would re-
\par
quire some counseling for applicants testing Glu-69 .
\par
We suggest at least an hour's worth of counseling for
\par
each individual along with the production of written
\par
materials for the applicant to take home. Our best es-
\par
timate of this cost is \$100 per Glu-69 applicant. We
\par
assign a triangular distribution allowing possible
\par
values in the range of half to double the primary esti-
\par
mate (Table I).
\par
The present value of the costs from 50 years of
\par
additional LPTs in one individual (one extra test per
\par
year over a lifetime) is estimated to be \$2,200 per
\par
Glu-69 individual using a 7\% discount rate and \$231
\par
per LPT.(25) If a Glu-69 individual develops a positive
\par
LPT during the fifth year of exposure and is immedi-
\par
ately replaced by a Glu-69 individual, the dis-
\par
counted cost of additional LPTs is about \$700. The
\par
true value is likely to lie somewhere between these
\par
two estimates, so we represent CLPT by using a uni-
\par
form distribution (Table I).
\par
voluntary screening program is highly uncertain, we
\par
assign a uniform distribution allowing for anywhere
\par
between 0\% and 100\% removal likelihood for Glu-
\par
69 applicants.
\par
Medical or industrial hygiene intervention could
\par
reduce disease rates through a different use of ge-
\par
netic information. Rather than screening out geneti-
\par
cally susceptible applicants before they are exposed
\par
to beryllium, this type of intervention seeks to reduce
\par
the disease risk or severity through targeted exposure
\par
mitigation or heightened medical monitoring. We as-
\par
sume that increasing LPT frequency would reduce
\par
CBD risk by removing workers from beryllium expo-
\par
sure earlier in the process of disease development.
\par
No known evidence supports or contradicts the as-
\par
sumed benefits of early detection of beryllium sensi-
\par
tization, but it is reasonable based on other immune-
\par
mediated disease models to assume that CBD risk
\par
increases with exposure duration. Assuming a uniform
\par
likelihood of CBD development in the time interval
\par
between LPTs, increasing the LPT frequency from
\par
once a year to twice a year would reduce the average
\par
beryllium exposure duration by 3 months for sensi-
\par
tized individuals. What effect if any this would have
\par
on disease progression is unclear, but some evidence
\par
indicates that CBD risk increases with exposure du-
\par
ration.(27) Hypothesizing that the prevalence of dis-
\par
ease might be linearly related to exposure duration
\par
for Glu-69 individuals, detection and removal from
\par
exposure 3 months early in a cohort with a 5-year la-
\par
tency would reduce CBD prevalence by 5\% among
\par
individuals in that cohort assuming they are replaced
\par
by workers who do not become sensitized. Alterna-
\par
tively, the prevalence of disease could be propor-
\par
tional to the postsensitization exposure duration, in
\par
which case early detection would decrease the CBD
\par
prevalence by 50\%. Clearly a number of other models
\par
could be formed, including the possibility that early
\par
detection does not impact the likelihood of CBD de-
\par
velopment. We assign a triangular distribution to this
\par
parameter, with bounds of 0\% and 50\% and a mode
\par
of 5\% (Table I).
\par
