\par
may be as nearly effective as required screening in re-
\par
ducing disease. The major risk associated with this ap-
\par
proach is the possibility that few Glu-69 workers will
\par
decide to get tested or to avoid placement in beryllium-
\par
exposed job tasks. However, even if every worker de-
\par
cides to get tested for susceptibility but nobody with
\par
Glu-69 decides to act on the results, the financial
\par
costs are minimal ( \$290/worker). Furthermore,
\par
even if only one of every hundred Glu-69 applicants
\par
decides to remove themselves from exposure, the ex-
\par
pected value of the health benefits will still exceed the
\par
program costs at health valuations greater than
\par
\$500,000 per CBD case.
\par
Indirect benefits of biomarker development and
\par
use exist but have not been evaluated in this model.
\par
The identification of the polymorphism and develop-
\par
ment of the Glu-69 test, for example, may ultimately
\par
allow understanding of the mechanisms underlying
\par
CBD and thus lead perhaps to better prevention or
\par
treatment options.(20) Medical surveillance programs
\par
designed around the susceptibility biomarker might
\par
similarly lead to new insights regarding CBD etiol-
\par
ogy. Although it may not be appropriate to include
\par
these impacts in deciding whether or not to broadly
\par
implement a risk management program based on ge-
\par
netic susceptibility, they are important considerations
\par
when targeting biomarker research.
\par
5.1. Other Risk Management Options
\par
Not all risk management options have been eval-
\par
uated here, nor have combinations of the three op-
\par
tions. Process changes, personal protective equipment,
\par
and limitations on exposure duration are common
\par
methods used in industrial hygiene practice to reduce
\par
exposure.(29) Indeed, DOE has recently introduced
\par
substantial exposure reduction activities as part of a
\par
suite of hazard mitigation and communication activi-
\par
ties intended to reduce CBD incidence among its
\par
workers.(30) Because the dose--response relationship is
\par
unclear, whether exposure reductions beyond the
\par
current standard will lead to substantially reduced
\par
CBD incidence is uncertain. Although it is prudent to
\par
assume that further reductions in exposure concen-
\par
tration are likely to reduce disease incidence, for be-
\par
ryllium there is little direct evidence to support or
\par
contradict this assumption.
\par
Quantitative dose--response models for beryllium-
\par
induced CBD are currently unavailable. Such a
\par
model could easily be incorporated into this analysis
\par
95
\par
and would provide a means of estimating the risk re-
\par
duction and VOI for beryllium exposure controls.
\par
Here we chose not to consider the effectiveness of ex-
\par
posure reductions but rather to limit our analysis to
\par
the utility of the Glu-69 test at specific baseline CBD
\par
prevalence levels. If a Glu-69 genetic screening ap-
\par
proach is combined with exposure reductions to all
\par
workers, the value of the genetic information de-
\par
pends on the baseline prevalence following the expo-
\par
sure reduction. For example, current CBD preva-
\par
lence among beryllium machinists has been reported
\par
as approximately 10\% for one cohort.(23) If additional
\par
exposure controls in these machinists were expected
\par
to lead to a 90\% reduction in risk, the new baseline
\par
prevalence in this cohort would be 1\%, and the ex-
\par
pected VOI for the voluntary Glu-69 program
\par
would decrease from \$8,000 to \$560 per beryllium
\par
machinist based on \$240,000 per CBD case avoided
\par
(Table III).
\par
Other programs might be considered in addition
\par
to those heightened medical monitoring options eval-
\par
uated here. For example, the interval between succes-
\par
sive LPTs might be changed to other values, and
\par
when warranted, other clinical tests could be used to
\par
ascertain disease status before the appearance of
\par
frank CBD. Although the possibility has not been
\par
quantitatively evaluated here, any application of Glu-
\par
69 genetic screening might indirectly induce some
\par
Glu-69 beryllium workers to reduce their own beryl-
\par
lium exposures, thereby indirectly increasing the util-
\par
ity of the program. However, the disease reduction
\par
benefits of early detection of sensitization or disease
\par
are unlikely to be greater than the benefits of re-
\par
quired or voluntary preplacement screening, and the
\par
financial costs of heightened medical monitoring are
\par
relatively high. Nonetheless, heightened medical
\par
monitoring targeted to Glu-69 workers may be su-
\par
perior to not using Glu-69-based genetic testing at
\par
current occupational CBD prevalence rates. In addi-
\par
tion, heightened medical monitoring may be the only
\par
genetically based intervention available to former
\par
beryllium workers.
\par
We have not evaluated the possibility of using
\par
multiple genetic polymorphisms in screening. Poly-
\par
morphisms other than the Glu-69 substitution have
\par
been associated with CBD, albeit none have demon-
\par
strated as high sensitivity or specificity for predicting
\par
CBD. Use of information derived from multiple poly-
\par
morphisms might increase the sensitivity and speci-
\par
ficity of genetically based screening for CBD, and
\par
determination of possible genetic linkages would
\par
provide insight into whether the Glu-69 polymor-96 Bartell et al.
\par
phism is causally implicated in disease onset and
\par
progression.
\par
Richeldi et al.(2) investigated multiple polymor-
\par
phisms in the HLA-DPB1 gene after a screening
\par
study demonstrated no strong associations with poly-
\par
morphisms in the HLA-DR and -DQ genes. They re-
\par
port that an aspartic acid--glutamic acid substitution
\par
for alanine-alanine at position 55--56 showed signifi-
\par
cant association with disease (79\% sensitivity and
\par
59\% specificity). Because individual data were not
\par
provided in the publication, however, whether indi-
\par
viduals with both polymorphisms exhibited greater
\par
disease prevalence was unclear. A concomitant anal-
\par
ysis of genetic linkage between Glu-69 and polymor-
\par
phic sites within HLA-DRB4 and TNFB demon-
\par
strated no association with CBD.(2)
\par
In a study of genetic polymorphisms associated
\par
with beryllium hypersensitivity, Stubbs et al.(31) exam-
\par
ined HLA-DRB1, -DRB3, -DRB4, -DRB5, and -DPB1.
\par
They observed a significant association between be-
\par
ryllium sensitization and the HLA-DPB1 Glu-69
\par
polymorphism, supporting the observations of Rich-
\par
eldi et al.(2) In addition, they observed that five spe-
\par
cific polymorphisms in HLA-DRB1 were associated
\par
with susceptibility to beryllium sensitization and that
\par
four others within the same gene were associated
\par
with protection. Polymorphisms associated with re-
\par
duced prevalence of sensitization were also observed
\par
within HLA-DRB3 and -DRB5. Whether these poly-
\par
morphisms are associated with development of CBD
\par
has not been reported, and genetic linkage between
\par
HLA-DP genes and other MHC class II genes is
\par
low.(32) Finally, unpublished analyses by Saltini et al.
\par
reportedly demonstrate a significant association be-
\par
tween CBD and polymorphisms in TNF- ; however,
\par
the association is reportedly weaker than that ob-
\par
served with Glu-69,(8) and no information is currently
\par
available regarding disease prevalence among indi-
\par
viduals with both polymorphisms.
\par
5.2. Social Implications and Ethics
\par
of Genetic Screening
\par
If a decisionmaker decides that the social and
\par
ethical risks of required Glu-69 screening should be
\par
quantified and weighed against the financial and
\par
health risks, Eq. 1 can be expanded to include all of
\par
those risks. Because of the difficulty in assigning
\par
value to social risks, we have chosen to treat them
\par
qualitatively instead of quantitatively in this analysis.
\par
For this reason we have also calculated VOI sepa-
\par
rately for each risk management option, rather than
\par
taking the more typical approach of optimizing be-
\par
fore the VOI calculation.(33) We feel that our approach
\par
may be more informative to decisionmakers, particu-
\par
larly when they might wish to select a ``suboptimal''
\par
risk management option based on factors external to
\par
the quantitative analysis.
\par
Many authors have pointed out the social and
\par
ethical hazards of occupational genetic screening.(34)
\par
These hazards include potential loss of employment,
\par
benefits, employability, insurability, and privacy for
\par
individuals who are genetically susceptible but may
\par
not develop disease after beryllium exposure. Be-
\par
cause of the potential for these negative impacts, and
\par
because the majority of beryllium-exposed Glu-69
\par
individuals will not develop CBD, some beryllium re-
\par
searchers have recommended that Glu-69 not be
\par
used for employment screening.(23) We do not intend
\par
to belittle these concerns, but when the health risks
\par
are substantial the ethical hazards of failing to act are
\par
also worthy of consideration. This ethical hazard is
\par
heightened in this case by the uncertainty regarding
\par
the dose--response relationship for CBD, the absence
\par
of a complete cure, and the complications often en-
\par
countered when using long-term immunosuppressive
\par
therapy as is frequently required for CBD. Although
\par
the current 2 g/m3 standard is clearly not protective
\par
of all workers, further beryllium exposure reduc-
\par
tions are not guaranteed to substantially reduce
\par
CBD incidence because of the exquisite susceptibil-
\par
ity of some individuals. Hence, other options must
\par
be considered for use in conjunction with improved
\par
exposure controls.
\par
The social risks of taking action on the basis of
\par
genetic test results can be mitigated to some extent.
\par
For example, DOE has directed that its workers test-
\par
ing positive for sensitization to beryllium be removed
\par
from job tasks with known beryllium exposure but re-
\par
tain full employment and benefits for some time after
\par
diagnosis.(30) A similar rule could be applied to cur-
\par
rent workers testing positive for genetic susceptibility
\par
to beryllium, though this would increase the costs of
\par
the screening program and thereby reduce the ex-
\par
pected VOI. In addition, federal legislation disallow-
\par
ing the use of genetic susceptibility tests by insurance
\par
companies and preventing inappropriate use of ge-
\par
netic screening by employers has been proposed.(35,36)
\par
Although this issue is highly contentious and will
\par
likely be discussed for years to come, legal safeguards
\par
on the availability and use of genetic information
\par
might mitigate some of the concerns raised by bio-
\par
ethicists. For example, employers could hire an inde-Beryllium Disease and Genetic Screening pendent laboratory to conduct confidential genetic
\par
testing and provide confidential counseling to current
\par
and prospective beryllium workers. This approach
\par
would encourage individuals to get tested by protect-
\par
ing their privacy but would increase screening costs
\par
for employers. Revised estimates of the VOI for ex-
\par
panded programs such as these could be obtained by
\par
using our model equations.
\par
The success of any voluntary screening program
\par
depends on trust. Participation rates can increase
\par
over time as a program becomes established and
\par
gains credibility. One study reported that over 99.9\%
\par
of Maryland parents consented to newborn phe-
\par
nylketonuria screening for medical intervention after
\par
the state repealed a required screening law.(37) Any-
\par
thing close to this degree of participation could only
\par
be achieved in the occupational setting by carefully
\par
addressing the privacy and job security concerns of
\par
those tested and through well-conceived risk commu-
\par
nication. Although previous surveys found many
\par
people reluctant to share genetic information with
\par
employers,(38) it is reasonable to believe that some be-
\par
ryllium workers would give informed consent to sus-
\par
ceptibility testing and remove themselves from beryl-
\par
lium work on the basis of the results. Given the health
\par
risks, we believe that beryllium workers should at
\par
least be provided with information about the poten-
\par
tial uses of the test.
\par
5.3. Data Needs and Suggestions
\par
for Future Research
\par
The health valuation for CBD is clearly the most
\par
important aspect of this model in terms of impact on
\par
the predicted VOI. Readers are likely to have quite
\par
different opinions on the most appropriate value of
\par
Bd, and for policy makers to select an appropriate
\par
value may be difficult. Although some of these dis-
\par
crepancies could be resolved by COI and WTP studies
\par
conducted specifically for CBD, further research is
\par
unlikely to resolve basic differences of opinion.
\par
Of the other variables in the model, several
\par
stand out as crucial areas for additional research. The
\par
voluntary participation rate is unknown as we are un-
\par
aware of such a genetically based occupational
\par
screening model in current use. Even were data avail-
\par
able for another genetic screen, how applicable these
\par
data would be to the particular case of Glu-69 and
\par
CBD is questionable. The specific contextual factors
\par
are likely to strongly influence the participation rate
\par
for such a program; thus a small-scale pilot study to
\par
97
\par
preliminarily estimate the parameter would be re-
\par
quired. We suggest that Glu-69 may be appropriate
\par
for such a pilot study, on the basis of the VOI analysis
\par
conducted here.
\par
The medical value of higher LPT frequency is
\par
also open to question and is represented by a wide
\par
range of possibilities in the present model. A detailed
\par
quantitative model of beryllium sensitization and
\par
CBD progression is needed for this and other aspects
\par
of our model, but existing models are mostly qualita-
\par
tive and still open to debate. Future work should in-
\par
clude the development and testing of more detailed
\par
disease models for CBD, particularly those that take
\par
both genetic status and beryllium exposure charac-
\par
teristics into account. For example, we have hypothe-
\par
sized that the sensitivity and specificity rates are inde-
\par
pendent of baseline CBD prevalence by using a
\par
relatively simple model for the interaction of genetics
\par
and environment in producing CBD. More cohort
\par
study data would allow for the exploration of this
\par
model and alternative hypotheses.
\par
6. CONCLUSIONS
\par
The Glu-69 genetic biomarker, when used as a
\par
required or voluntary screen for occupational place-
\par
ment in beryllium-contaminated areas, is expected to
\par
be cost-effective in mitigating CBD in typical cohorts
\par
when CBD valuation is above \$100,000 per case. At
\par
lower valuations and lower baseline disease preva-
\par
lence rates, cost-effectiveness decreases. However,
\par
genetic screening has significant ethical and legal im-
\par
plications that may prevent implementation. Volun-
\par
tary screening may be nearly as cost effective as re-
\par
quired screening and is more likely to be accepted by
\par
stakeholders, but predicting the participation rate is
\par
difficult. Increasing the frequency of the LPT from
\par
once a year to twice a year for Glu-69 individuals
\par
may also decrease disease prevalence at a reasonable
\par
cost but is predicted to be less effective than either of
\par
the screening programs.
\par
We recommend a dialogue among stakeholders
\par
about the use of voluntary Glu-69 testing for beryl-
\par
lium work applicants or existing workers expected to
\par
enter areas with known beryllium contamination. If
\par
workers' privacy and employability concerns are ad-
\par
dressed, voluntary screening can achieve significant
\par
disease reduction without undesirable social conse-
\par
quences. Heightened medical monitoring may still be
\par
worthwhile, perhaps in conjunction with voluntary
\par
screening, but is not as amenable to privacy protec-98 Bartell et al.
\par
tion as voluntary genetic testing conducted by an out-
\par
side party.
\par
Many more biomarkers are available today than
\par
are actually used in clinical, occupational, or environ-
\par
mental risk management. Highly sensitive and spe-
\par
cific biomarkers are clearly valid for practice, but less
\par
obvious biomarkers may also offer significant oppor-
\par
tunities for disease reduction. Tools such as decision
\par
analysis and VOI analysis can assist in discriminating
\par
between good and poor biomarker opportunities by
\par
providing information about the predicted costs and
\par
benefits of using biomarkers in specific applications.
