C. Methodological Advantages of a Bayesian Approach
\par
In order to retain its credibility as a legitimate principle of envi-
\par
ronmental policymaking and not degenerate into an instrument of
\par
veiled protectionism, the Precautionary Principle must be imple-
\par
mented in a way that is as transparent as possible, does not deter fur-
\par
ther research and information gathering, and characterizes risk in a
\par
way amenable to regulatory decision-making and ongoing scientific
\par
debate. This paper argues that a Bayesian approach is not only the
\par
appropriate method to assess truly uncertain risks, but that it is
\par
uniquely suited to promoting intellectual due process principles. The
\par
latter characteristic is an important counterargument to the criticism
\par
that the Precautionary Principle is a fundamentally ``unscientific'' de-
\par
cision rule.26
