II. THE MERITS OF BAYESIAN APPROACH
\par
A. Background
\par
Bayes' Theorem28 is an extremely important development in the
\par
history of social science, as well as a somewhat controversial area
\par
within statistics.29 Bayes' original theory states that ``the probability
\par
of an event is the ratio between the value at which an expectation de-
\par
pending on the happening of the event ought to be computed, and the
\par
value of the thing expected upon its happening.''30 The Theorem
\par
therefore provides a way to combine prior information, or a prior be-
\par
lief about a risk or a population parameter with new information ob-
\par
tained through sampling or other experiments to guide a person's in-
\par
ferences in a rational way. It allows decision and game theorists to
\par
examine how rational actors would assimilate new information with
\par
their existing subjective beliefs.
\par
Its appeal to statisticians and social scientists is that its rules con-
\par
form to mathematical principles that reflect a certain amount of in-
\par
ternal consistency.31 The basic idea is that a person should not accept
\par
wholesale the results of new information that disconfirm their prior
\par
beliefs. The weight assigned to the new data versus the prior beliefs
\par
depends upon the person's degree of confidence in both their beliefs
\par
and the sampled data, which is graphically reflected in how ``tightly''
\par
the probability distribution is centered around certain values.
\par
