Mathematical Definition and Example
\par
In technical terms, Bayes' Theorem states that the subjective
\par
posterior odds (odds after being exposed to new data)32 that a hy-
\par
pothesis is true can be determined by multiplying the prior odds (or
\par
odds before exposure to the new data)33 by the ratio of (1) the prob-
\par
ability that the data would have been observed if the hypothesis were
\par
true to (2) the probability that the data would have been observed if
\par
the hypothesis were not true. The ratio of (1) to (2) above is referred
\par
to as the likelihood ratio.
\par
34 So, using L to represent this likelihood ra-
\par
tio, Bayes' Theorem is
\par
P(hypothesis is true after) = P(hypothesis is true before) x L.
\par
Alternatively, since the probability of obtaining the data and the pa-
\par
rameters can be written as:
\par
P(data and parameters) = P(data parameters) x P(parameters)
\par
Or:
\par
P(data and parameters) = P(parameters data) x P(data).
\par
Equating these expressions and rearranging gives us Bayes' Theorem:
\par
P(parameters data) = [P(data parameters) x P(parameters)] / P(data).35
\par
In this case, the prior hypothesis is the probability of the parameters
\par
prior to being exposed to the data.
\par
In practice, consider two groups, A and B, who are trying to an-
\par
ticipate the magnitude of a truly uncertain environmental harm. This
\par
will eventually be a real number, of course, but because of their un-
\par
certainty the best that A and B can do is assign a prior subjective
\par
probability distribution. The prior subjective probability distribution
\par
assigned by A and B is based on their current beliefs, given every-
