IV. CONCLUSION
\par
In adopting environmental regulations under conditions of true
\par
scientific uncertainty, many advocates of the Precautionary Principle
\par
have suggested that the use of Bayesian methods to assess probability
\par
distributions may be inappropriate. But as this paper demonstrates,
\par
even in cases of true uncertainty the Bayesian approach has charac-
\par
teristics that make it a more appropriate means of assessing even
\par
truly uncertain risks than its methodological rivals. Bayesian tech-
\par
niques are more transparent than classical hypothesis testing, and are
\par
consistent with well-established scientific principles such as falsifi-
\par
ability and simplicity when used to compare rival scientific hypothe-
\par
ses. Although Bayesian techniques can lead to perverse results when
\par
groups adopt inappropriately extreme prior assessments of risk, such
\par
problems can be discouraged through institutional innovations.
\par
Moreover, the Bayesian requirement that regulators adopt an explicit
\par
prior subjective distribution for an environmental risk subjects regula-
\par
tory judgment to scrutiny through subsequent experience, making the
\par
choice of ``doctored'' priors chosen to influence or defend policy or
\par
trade decisions less likely. Its transparency and consistency with well-
\par
established scientific principles such as simplicity and falsifiability
\par
demonstrate that the Bayesian approach possesses many attributes
\par
that make it an appealing risk assessment tool in operationalizing the
\par
sometimes controversial ideas behind the Precautionary Principal.
