\par
system to forecast future oil prices), with many vari-
\par
ables of interest, assessing and modeling expert
\par
knowledge about relationships become essential. The
\par
process of building a network representation, such as
\par
an influence diagram, belief network, or Bayes net, to
\par
represent the overall structure of the uncertainty in a
\par
problem is quite well understood, as is the assessment
\par
of probability distributions for individual variables in
\par
the network. Assessing dependence among the vari-
\par
ables has received less attention despite the fact that
\par
the ultimate probability distributions of interest may
\par
be quite sensitive to the level of dependence. When, as
\par
is often the case, "hard data" concerning relationships
\par
are limited or nonexistent, expert judgments become
\par
particularly crucial. For example, consider a product
\par
under development involving a technology that is so
\par
new that no directly relevant past data are available.
\par
The degree of dependence between variables such as
\par
time to market and availability of competing products,
\par
or advertising budget and sales, requires judgmental
\par
assessment and can have great impact on develop-
\par
ment and marketing decisions.
\par
Typically, conditional distributions have been used
\par
to represent an expert's knowledge about a set of
\par
interrelated random variables. However, the number
\par
of required probability assessments can grow expo-
\par
nentially as variables are added. Also, thinking about
