In this article, we report the results of two studies
\par
designed to investigate some methods for assessing
\par
dependence. Our orientation is prescriptive in that we
\par
are interested in developing and studying procedures
\par
that lead to better decisions by improving how we
\par
elicit and model expert knowledge regarding depen-
\par
dence. Our intent is not to propose and test behavioral
\par
theories. On the other hand, we ultimately want
\par
methods that have a sound probabilistic foundation
\par
for modeling and also take into account current
\par
knowledge in behavioral decision theory, as well as
\par
current practice in probability elicitation. For example,
\par
