\par
2. Dependence-Assessment
\par
Methods
\par
What are desirable characteristics for a dependence-
\par
assessment method? For modeling purposes, the meth-
\par
ods should have rigorous foundations that are defensible
\par
in terms of probability theory, they should be as general
\par
as possible to permit modeling of dependence in a wide
\par
variety of situations, and they should be able to be linked
\par
directly to the modeling procedure. For ease of assess-
\par
ment, they should have a clear intuitive interpretation,
\par
and assessors should view them as easy and credible. In
\par
terms of the resulting assessments, we would like to see
\par
coherent assessments, a reasonable amount of agree-
\par
ment among the assessments of individuals with similar
\par
knowledge, and accuracy (i.e., assessments that corre-
\par
spond well with actual levels of dependence in the real
\par
world). For any given method, we can examine the
\par
underlying foundations and connections with modeling.
\par
We can also consider interpretations and ask assessors if
\par
they find the method easy to use. Assessments can be
\par
constrained to be coherent. The degree of agreement
\par
among assessors can be empirically evaluated. Finally,
\par
where data are available (as they are for the pairs of
\par
variables considered in our experiments), we can use
\par
those data to calculate accuracy statistics for the various
\par
assessment methods.
\par
We used six different dependence assessment meth-
\par
ods in our experiments: S (strength of relationship), R
\par
(correlation), CF (conditional fractile), CNC (concor-
\par
dance probability), JP (joint probability), and CP (con-
\par
ditional probability). These methods represent six
\par
