3. Study 1
\par
Method. In the first study, we asked 90 students in
\par
Fuqua's Weekend Executive MBA program to re-
\par
spond to a questionnaire. All were enrolled in the
\par
second quantitative course in the MBA curriculum,
\par
and the exercise was done at a time when the curric-
\par
ulum focused on Monte Carlo simulation and the
\par
problem of modeling correlated variables. All stu-
\par
dents had taken a statistics course during the previous
\par
term, during which they studied probability, regres-
\par
sion, and correlation. The average age of the subjects
\par
was 36.5 years, 24\% were female, and 76\% were male.
\par
The design is a within-subjects design. The ques-
\par
tionnaire included the assessment questions for the six
\par
dependence measures described for five pairs of vari-
\par
ables about which we believed the respondents would
\par
be reasonably knowledgeable. In addition, we ob-
\par
tained data for these variables so that we could
\par
estimate the correlation and compare the students'
\par
assessments with the estimates in order to measure the
\par
accuracy of the assessments. (Thus, our variables
\par
satisfy Kunda and Nisbett's (1986) conditions of famil-
\par
iarity and codability.) The five pairs of variables and
\par
the estimated correlations are:
\par
1) Height and Weight (HW). This represents the
\par
height and weight of male MBA students enrolled in
\par
Fuqua's daytime MBA program. Based on a sample of
\par
218 individuals, the estimated Spearman correlation is
\par
0.530.
\par
2) Math and Verbal SAT Scores (MV). These are
\par
scores on the two portions of the Scholastic Aptitude
\par
Test (SAT) for Duke undergraduates. Based on data
\par
for 46,278 Duke undergraduates who matriculated
\par
between 1963 and 1998, the estimated Spearman cor-
\par
relation is 0.377.
\par
3) Standard and Poor's 500 and Dow Jones Industrial
\par
Average (SD). This represents the monthly returns for
\par
these two indexes of overall stock market perfor-
\par
mance. Based on monthly data from February 1945 to
\par
January 1995, the estimated Spearman correlation is
\par
0.950.
\par
4) Automotive Index and Chrysler Corporation (AC).
\par
This represents the monthly returns for Chrysler Cor-
\par
poration common stock and an index of stocks (de-
\par
fined by Compustat) from the automotive industry.
\par
Based on data from July 1976 through June 1996, the
\par
estimated Spearman correlation is 0.738.
\par
5) Eli Lilly and Chrysler Corporation (LC). These are
\par
monthly returns for Eli Lilly (a pharmaceutical firm)
\par
and Chrysler Corporation common stock. Based on
\par
data from July 1976 through June 1996, the estimated
\par
Spearman correlation is 0.173.
\par
For each pair of variables, we provided histograms
\par
of the marginal distributions as well as estimated
\par
means, standard deviations, and deciles of the distri-
\par
butions. No scatterplot or other indication of the joint
\par
distribution was provided, however, because we were
\par
interested in the students' ability to assess the level of
\par
dependence between the variables based on their own
\par
subjective knowledge.
\par
With each assessment question, we included a brief
\par
statement describing "how to think about" the assess-
\par
ment, including an indication of upper and lower
\par
bounds. This information was included on the basis of
\par
a previous pilot study in which such information was
\par
shown to improve accuracy and reduce the number of
\par
responses that fell outside mathematically feasible
\par
bounds.
\par
As an example, in the case of HW, the assessment
\par
question for the CF measure is:
\par
A randomly chosen male MBA student is 74 inches tall,
\par
which is the 90th percentile of the height distribution. (This
\par
means that 90\% of individuals are less than or equal to 74
\par
inches tall.) For the same student, what percentile would you
\par
estimate for his weight?
\par
(How to think about this: An estimate near the 50th percen-
\par
tile means you think the two variables have no relationship-
\par
even though you know what the height is, your best guess for
\par
the weight is still the median value. An estimate above the
\par
50th percentile means you think they have a positive rela-
\par
tionship, and the greater the estimate, the stronger the
\par
relationship. Likewise, an estimate of less than the 50th
\par
percentile indicates a negative relationship. For consistency,
\par
the estimate must fall between the 10th percentile (perfect
\par
negative relationship) and 90th percentile (perfect positive
\par
relationship) of the weight distribution, or between 142 and
\par
200 lbs.)
\par
