\par
The structure of each questionnaire was to group
\par
the assessment questions for each pair of variables.
\par
Thus, for example, in one condition a respondent
\par
would answer all assessment questions first for HW,
\par
then for MV, and so on. The ordering of the variable
\par
pairs was partially counterbalanced: The demographic
\par
variables (HW and MV) always appeared first, fol-
\par
lowed by the financial variables (SD, AC, LC), with
\par
randomized ordering within each of these groups. The
\par
ordering of the assessment questions also was par-
\par
tially counterbalanced. First, for a given respondent,
\par
the ordering of the assessment questions was the same
\par
for all variable pairs. Strength of relationship (S) was
\par
always asked first as a "conditioning" question to help
\par
respondents begin to think about the nature of the
\par
relationship between the variables. The ordering of
\par
the remaining assessment questions was varied using
\par
a Latin Square design. Complete details of the design
\par
and samples of the questionnaires are available from
\par
the authors.
\par
The entire task took 30 minutes to complete, and no
\par
compensation was given. In a follow-up session, stu-
\par
dents received feedback regarding how their re-
\par
sponses compared to the data-based correlation esti-
\par
mates and to the distribution of responses from all
\par
subjects.
\par
Nine of the 90 students indicated that they had
\par
some experience in the financial industry, ranging
\par
from 3 to 10 years, with an average of 6.2 years.
\par
Responses from these 9 subjects regarding the finan-
\par
cial variables have been eliminated from the following
\par
analysis because results from Study 2 indicate that
\par
experience does make a difference in terms of assess-
\par
ment accuracy.
\par
Results. To analyze the dependence assessments,
\par
we first converted the responses to equivalent Spear-
\par
man correlations. The responses were converted as
\par
follows:
\par
S: We transformed S linearly. Thus, the equivalent
\par
correlation was calculated as rs = (S - 4)/3.
\par
R: The response was taken to be the Spearman
\par
correlation.
\par
CF: Given random variables X and Y with distribu-
\par
tion functions F(x) and G(y), the standard nonpara-
\par
metric regression representation is
\par
E[F(X)ly] = rcF[G(y) - 0.5] + 0.5.
\par
The assessment question specifies G(y), and we
\par
take the response to be E [F(X) y]. Thus, we are able to
\par
solve for rCF.
\par
CNC: Given the probability of concordance Pc, we
\par
begin by calculating Kendall's T = 2Pc - 1. For
\par
purposes of the analysis, we assume that the bivariate
\par
normal model is a close representation of the partici-
\par
pants' beliefs about the joint distribution. The data-
\par
based joint distributions for our variable pairs are well
\par
approximated by the bivariate normal model, and we
\par
believe that the normality assumption is reasonable
\par
and robust. Thus, we are able to use Kruskal's (1958)
\par
relationships between the Pearson correlation (r*) and
\par
Spearman correlation (r): r* = 2sin(rr/6), and be-
\par
tween Pearson's correlation and Kendall's T: r:
\par
= sin(WT/2). Using these equations, we convert T to
\par
the equivalent Spearman correlation and label it as rc.
\par
JP and CP: In both cases we explicitly assumed that
\par
the assessed probability arose from a bivariate normal
\par
distribution, and we calculated r1p and rcp based on
\par
that assumption. Calculations were done using Math-
\par
ematica and Microsoft Excel.
\par
In all cases, if the assessed value fell outside math-
\par
ematically feasible bounds, we coded the equivalent
\par
correlation as a missing value. Across all variable
\par
pairs, such responses occurred 5 times for CF, 24 times
\par
for JP, 7 times for CP, and 0 for R, CNC, and S.
\par
