\par
Accuracy can be improved slightly in two ways.
\par
One is to provide some training to the expert regard-
\par
ing the measure of dependence being assessed, as we
\par
did in Study 2. The training we did was minimal; for
\par
R we discussed the meaning of a correlation as a
\par
measure of dependence ranging from +1.00 to -1.00,
\par
and we showed several scatterplots with different
\par
levels of correlation to help the subject visualize what
\par
different correlations would imply graphically. The
\par
increase in performance for R especially was accom-
\par
panied by a marked (more than 25\%) decrease in the
\par
standard deviation of absolute errors. An open ques-
\par
tion is whether more extensive training and practice
\par
would lead to further improvements.
\par
The second way to improve an expert's perfor-
\par
mance is to ask for several different dependence
\par
measures and average them. The improvement is
\par
slight, about 0.015 in terms of average absolute error,
\par
and is about the same order of magnitude as the effect
\par
of training. However, the averaging also led to a
\par
substantial decrease in the standard deviation of ab-
\par
solute errors, which implies a lower risk of getting a
\par
very large absolute error when multiple measures are
\par
assessed and averaged. These results are consistent
\par
with the findings of Makridakis and Winkler (1983)
\par
that averaging forecasts from different forecasting
\par
techniques led to some improvements in accuracy and
\par
dramatic reductions in the variability of accuracy
\par
measures. Thus, although R performs better than the
\par
other methods, it may be worthwhile to use other
\par
methods in addition to (not in place of) R. Standard
\par
practice in probability assessment in decision analysis
\par
is to ask questions in a variety of ways to ferret out
\par
inconsistencies and stimulate careful thought. It is also
\par
common to have the same probabilities assessed by
\par
more than one expert and to average the resulting
\par
probabilities, with the same types of gains noted
\par
above for averages of assessments from different
\par
methods. If multiple experts are available to assess a
\par
dependence measure for a given relationship, averag-
\par
ing their assessments is an attractive strategy.
\par
Another conclusion of our studies is that the ability
\par
of our subjects to assess correlations accurately is
\par
somewhat limited. Even the most accurate methods
\par
have overall average absolute errors around 0.20, and
\par
for particular variable pair/assessment method com-
\par
binations average absolute errors between 0.30 and
\par
0.40 are not unusual. Our results for experienced
\par
subjects, however, suggest that experience can im-
\par
prove accuracy, and the low average absolute error on
\par
financial variables (0.132) for experienced subjects
\par
trained to use R is encouraging if not conclusive. The
\par
effects of experience and training in correlation assess-
\par
ment accuracy parallel their roles in probability as-
\par
sessment, where the evidence suggests that expertise,
\par
training, and feedback can reduce the effect of over-
\par
confidence and improve calibration. (For a review, see
\par
Yates 1990.)
\par
One possible explanation for the generally good
\par
performance of R is simply that our subjects had been
\par
exposed to correlation fairly recently. Although this is
\par
true, they also had been exposed to probability con-
\par
cepts. Regardless, if familiarity with correlation is of
\par
value, this bodes well for the use of R in real-world
\par
applications, where many experts will have been
\par
trained in statistical methods and hence should be
\par
familiar with correlation. Also, we note that the Excel
\par
add-in Crystal Ball presents an example scatterplot for
\par
a specified correlation, thereby essentially mimicking
\par
the training procedure we used for R. For an expert
\par
who is knowledgeable about the variables in question
\par
and familiar with correlation, using Crystal Ball's
\par
correlation tool appears to be a suitable approach for
\par
correlation assessment.
\par
Although the results from our study are sound, we
\par
have considered only five variable pairs, albeit with a
\par
wide range of positive correlations. Given constraints
\par
on the availability of time for the subjects and appro-
\par
priate variables about which the subjects had some
\par
knowledge, we did not include any variables with
\par
negative relationships. We speculate that results for
\par
negative relationships would parallel those for posi-
\par
tive relationships, with the strength of the relationship
\par
being more crucial than the direction. However, a
\par
more general study, including negative as well as
\par
positive correlations, would be of value. Perhaps this
