\par
In preparing for our empirical studies, we searched
\par
the literature and worked diligently to develop
\par
dependence-assessment methods. We chose to include
\par
methods that seemed reasonable for experts to assess.
\par
Other measures of dependence are indeed available in
\par
the literature (e.g., see Schweizer and Wolff 1981,
\par
Genest and Rivest 1993), but they are difficult to
\par
interpret intuitively and do not readily lend them-
\par
selves to subjective assessment. Regardless, further
\par
work might consider other dependence measures and
\par
ways to assess dependence. Along these lines, a useful
\par
study would compare direct assessment of measures
\par
of dependence (as we have done here) with the
\par
assessment of dependence via conditional probability
\par
distributions, the conventional approach in decision-
\par
analysis modeling.
\par
Assessing dependence is not an easy judgmental
\par
task, and cognitive problems can influence the
\par
assessed measures. For instance, difficulties in judging
\par
a joint probability, often labeled the "conjunction
\par
fallacy," are well documented (Tversky and Kahne-
\par
man 1983). JP yielded more correlations outside the
\par
feasible bounds than any other technique and per-
\par
formed poorly in general. CNC also requires thinking
\par
about joint events and may cause assessments to be
\par
based on special pairs or limited (nonrandom) sam-
\par
ples of pairs. CF is susceptible to the nonregressive
\par
prediction effect (Kahneman and Tversky 1973), as
\par
suggested here by the high correlations obtained from
\par
CF. In Table 1, for example, CF had the highest
\par
average correlation for four of the five variable pairs
\par
and the second highest for the remaining pair. As
\par
noted in ?2, we believe that conditioning on an inter-
\par
val of values, as is done in CP, is difficult. Cognitive
\par
problems such as these may have contributed to the
\par
fact that JP, CF, and CP had the highest average
\par
absolute errors in Table 2 and the worst average
\par
difficulty ratings, with CNC next in line.
\par
For several of the assessment methods we stud-
\par
ied, variations in how the question is framed or in
\par
other details could be considered. For instance, the
\par
question in CF could be asked in terms of a value
\par
(e.g., a weight) instead of a percentile. We chose to
\par
use a percentile to disentangle judgments about
\par
dependence from judgments about marginal proba-
\par
bilities. In this study, the marginal distributions
\par
were given to the subjects, but in a real-world
\par
decision-making problem that is unlikely to be the
\par
case. However, conditioning on fractiles and asking
\par
for fractiles may be difficult cognitively. Similarly,
\par
the conditional probability in CP could be condi-
\par
tioned on a specific value or fractile instead of a
\par
range of values. For the methods involving the
\par
assessment of probabilities (CNC, JP, and CP),
\par
questions can be framed in different ways (e.g., in
\par
terms of frequencies), and an alternative to the
\par
direct assessment of probabilities is the use of
\par
lotteries or other indirect procedures for assessing
\par
probabilities. Such procedures are commonly used
\par
in decision analysis. Finer details can also be mod-
\par
ified. For instance, a percentile other than the 90th
\par
percentile could be used in CF. We chose the 90th
\par
percentile in order to have a wide range of feasible
\par
responses (from the 10th to the 90th percentile).
\par
Similarly, different percentiles could be used in JP
\par
and CP. However, even with modifications, meth-
\par
ods such as CF, CNC, JP, and CP might not perform
\par
as well as R.
\par
Finally, we have considered here methods for as-
\par
sessing dependence among two variables only. Even if
\par
we consider only bivariate measures of dependence,
\par
when more than two variables must be modeled, the
\par
matrix of such bivariate measures must obey certain
\par
constraints (e.g., a correlation matrix must be positive
\par
definite). Methods to ensure that pairwise assessments
\par
obey such constraints must be developed. In addition,
\par
in some cases pairwise dependence may be inade-
\par
quate for modeling the pattern of dependence among
\par
a set of variables.'
