JOURNAL OF MULTI-CRITERIA DECISION ANALYSIS
\par
J. Multi-Crit. Decis. Anal. 9: 243--262 (2000)
\par
Dynamic Decision Making: A Comparison of Approaches
\par
PAULO C.G. DA COSTA and DENNIS M. BUEDE*
\par
DepartmentofSystems Engineering andOperations Research , George Mason University, Fairfax, Virginia, USA
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
This paper is concerned with a specific type of problem, namely dynamic decisions, for which most techniques fail
\par
to provide adequate solutions. Here, we present two of the most promising optimization techniques, partially
\par
observable Markov decision processes (POMDP) and dynamic decision networks (DDN), while arguing which is
\par
the most suitable for this problem domain. Copyright \copyright{} 2000 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd.
\par
KEY WORDS: Bayesian updating; DDN; dynamic decisions comparison; decision support; multi-criteria;
\par
POMDP
\par
1. INTRODUCTION
\par
Taking decisions is an everyday activity for all of
\par
us, and we do spend a lot of time in our lives
\par
dealing with the art of deciding how and when to
\par
act in a decision situation. Fortunately, most of
\par
our problems are fairly simple and do not demand
\par
excessive effort and thought from us in order to
\par
achieve reasonable solutions. Nevertheless, there
\par
are times when choosing the best decision or set
\par
of interrelated decisions can be a very important,
\par
challenging, time-consuming task. This is where
\par
the use of scientific decision techniques is a valu-
\par
able asset.
\par
Most of these tools are based on the `divide and
\par
conquer' approach, in which a complex problem
\par
is optimally solved by dismantling it into smaller,
\par
less complicated parts through a process that we
\par
may call decision modelling. Usually, a model is
\par
more likely to be useful if it accurately reflects the
\par
central characteristics of the real situation. For
\par
decision procedures, these issues are the set of
\par
available options, the criteria to be followed when
\par
choosing an option within this set, and the uncer-
\par
tainty on the outcomes of each option. For the
\par
values, the theoretical support is provided by the
\par
postulates of Utility Theory (Von Newmann and
\par
Morgenstern, 1944; Savage, 1954, 1961) further
\par
extended to a more comprehensive set called
\par
Multi-Attribute Utility Theory (Keeney and
\par
Raiffa, 1976; French, 1986; Watson and Buede,
\par
1987), while the latter issue is better handled by
\par
the axioms of the Probability Theory (Laplace,
\par
1951; Keynes, 1957; Weatherford, 1982; French,
\par
1986).
\par
