\par
2. DYNAMIC DECISIONS
\par
The decision-maker should be aware of the match
\par
between the particulars of the modelling process
\par
and the problem's characteristics. Using a sophis-
\par
ticated decision analysis technique to solve a
\par
lesser problem can be a great waste of time and
\par
money.
\par
For most problems, there will be a handful of
\par
techniques that can be successfully applied. How-
\par
ever, in this paper, we are concerned with a
\par
specific class of problem, namely dynamic proba-
\par
bilistic systems. In such an environment, decisions
\par
are taken at different points in time, with uncer-
\par
tain outcomes or conflicting objectives affecting
\par
the decisions that are to be taken later; in a
\par
process that is usually called dynamic decisions.
\par
An insightful representation of the decision
\par
problem domain can be found in Howard (1998),
\par
where a given situation may be classified by the
\par
number of its variables, by its relationship with
\par
the time domain, and by its degree of uncertainty.
\par
Clearly, the problems with many variables in a
\par
dynamic situation with significant uncertainty are
\par
the most difficult; an `optimal' solution for this
\par
problem class is beyond our grasp for most prob-
\par
lems today.
\par
In addition to Howard's three problem dimen-
\par
sions, a dynamic probabilistic decision system
\par
ReceiTed 10 December 1999
\par
Accepted 20 September 2000244
\par
P.C.G. DA COSTA AND D.M. BUEDE
\par
would also have to cope with the `downstream
\par
decisions' (Howard, 1998), where an action made
\par
at time `t' would change the problem's character-
\par
istics for the next iteration of the system (`t+1').
\par
So a fourth axis would be the time dependency of
\par
the decisions taken, which brings a new source of
\par
complexity to the problem, and has prevented
\par
many decision systems from succeeding. In
\par
essence, evolving decisions are taken by analysing
\par
information that is usually gathered asyn-
\par
chronously.
\par
3. APPROACHES FOR DYNAMIC
\par
DECISIONS
\par
Decisions that evolve over time, depend upon
\par
asynchronous data, and address competing objec-
\par
tives are a huge challenge for every decision-
\par
maker or automated decision system. An
\par
appropriate formal approach for facing this prob-
\par
lem is dynamic programming (DP) (Bellman,
\par
1957; Bertsekas, 1995), a technique that makes a
\par
comprehensive analysis of all possible alternatives
\par
through an elegant, yet computationally hard
\par
algorithm.
\par
In short, the idea is to decompose the problem
\par
by ordering its decisions into segments. Then
\par
starting with the last segment, evaluate each deci-
\par
sion segment (moving backwards to the first seg-
\par
ment) for all possible prior segment sequences
\par
using a value function that can be segmented into
\par
additive (or multiplicative) components to match
\par
the decision segments. Ultimately, one decision
\par
alternative is selected for each segment; this set of
\par
selected alternatives is called a portfolio that pro-
\par
vides the highest value. Each possible portfolio is
\par
called a path, and a path that returns the maxi-
\par
mum value of all possible paths is considered an
\par
optimal path. As stated above, for dynamic pro-
\par
gramming to be applicable, the value function has
\par
to be separable for the chosen decision segments.
\par
This separable value function allows each path's
\par
overall value to be computed by the sum of the
\par
value of each of its decision segments.
\par
Dynamic programming can be considered a
\par
very precise, elegant approach for dynamic deci-
\par
sions that can be structured to meet its assump-
\par
tions, namely segmenting the decisions with a
\par
separable value function across segments. In
\par
essence, these assumptions permit all possible
\par
paths to be analysed without being computed.
\par
For example, if two subpaths over the same prior
\par
decision segment are computed and one is better,
\par
then the less valuable subpath can be dropped
\par
from consideration when additional segments are
\par
considered. Unfortunately, in spite of those ad-
\par
vantages, the lack of an efficient computational
\par
algorithm makes DP more a conceptual tool than
\par
a practical one for many real world dynamic
\par
decisions.
\par
In recent years, two optimization techniques
\par
have been developed to address dynamic deci-
\par
sions: partially observable Markov decision pro-
\par
cesses (POMDP) (Astrom, 1965; Sondik, 1971,
\par
1978; Smallwood and Sondik, 1973; Monahan,
\par
1982) and dynamic decision networks (DDN)
\par
(Buede, 1999; Buede and Costa, 1999; Costa,
\par
1999). The first is a special case of a Markov DP,
\par
while the latter combines Bayesian networks
\par
(BN), influence diagrams, and multi-attribute util-
\par
ity theory.
\par
Both techniques are powerful enough for mod-
\par
elling dynamic decisions. However, it is our view
\par
that the Markovian approach, while presenting
\par
serious limitations on its tractability, still lags
\par
behind the DDN technique in terms of overall
\par
flexibility for dealing with this specific kind of
\par
problem. In order to introduce this idea, we ini-
\par
tially provide a brief background on each ap-
\par
proach and then present different case studies.
\par
The first example, a very simple one, is intended
\par
to give a fairly straightforward view on how the
\par
solution process of each approach is conducted.
\par
The second case study involves multi-sensor data
\par
fusion with a time dependent decision, which is a
\par
special class of dynamic decision. This example is
\par
intended to provide a means for a more in-depth
\par
analysis of each technique.
\par
Following the case studies, Section 4 presents
\par
our comparative analysis of the POMDP and
\par
DDN approaches on the case studies. In that
\par
chapter, we ignore the issues regarding the
\par
tractability of the models so we can focus on the
\par
conceptual issues that deal directly with framing
\par
dynamic decisions. As a result of this scheme, our
\par
conclusions can be seen as independent of the
\par
current state-of-art of each approach.
\par
4. DECIDING WITH MARKOV PROCESSES
\par
4.1. Markov processes background
\par
In order to understand a Markov process, the
\par
concepts of state and state transitions are crucial.
\par
