\par
A state is any set of variables (thus called system
\par
state variables), the values of which fully describe
\par
the entire system at a given instant in time. A
\par
state transition represents the probability of going
\par
from a given state to another and the set of a
\par
system's state transitions is usually represented by
\par
an n\texttimes n square matrix called the state transition
\par
matrix, where n is the number of possible states of
\par
the system.
\par
The number of state variables and the number
\par
of values of each variable are directly related to
\par
the required level of complexity for each given
\par
application, preferably being as small as possible
\par
for the sake of computational simplicity. Figure 1
\par
shows a simple situation modelled as a Markov
\par
process.
\par
In this example, the manager of a four-truck
\par
fleet delivery company has to plan the driver's
\par
schedule for the next day. However, truck mainte-
\par
nance is conducted at night and he needs to build
\par
the schedule before noon. In order to predict the
\par
next day's truck availability, the manager de-
\par
signed a model that uses the number of trucks in
\par
a given day to forecast the same information for
\par
the next day. This very simplified model needs
\par
only one variable to describe the situation. Such a
\par
variable would have four possible values: zero,
\par
one, two, or three available trucks, which are also
\par
the states of the system. A transition probability
\par
matrix for this case would look like the one
\par
below.
\par
Intrinsic to this model is the key concept that
\par
names the process: the Markov assumption. The
\par
Markov assumption states that the next state s of
\par
the system at time t+1, st+1, is probabilistically
\par
dependent upon the current state (st), but condi-
\par
tionally independent of the previous states given
\par
the current state:
\par
P(st+1st, st-1, st-2, . . . , st-n )=P(st+1st)
\par
For the truck manager, the fact that he had two
\par
trucks available this morning is all the manager
\par
needs to know in order to predict tomorrow
\par
morning's probability of having zero (4\%), one
\par
(26\%), two (30\%), or three trucks (40\%). In other
\par
words, due to the Markov assumption, no poste-
\par
rior history prior to this morning's information is
\par
required.
