Copyright \copyright{} 2000 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd. 4.2. Markov DP
\par
The manager's truck model gives only a proba-
\par
bilistic expectation on the next day's truck
\par
availability, which may guide other events like
\par
driver's and delivery schedules. However, even
\par
though we may call it a dynamic probabilistic
\par
model, it is not a `problem' yet, because it acts
\par
just as a predictor of future events. A lot of power
\par
and usefulness may be added to this scheme when
\par
we also model the manager's decisions with out-
\par
comes and values, which affect the system's states,
\par
transforming it into a Markov DP (MDP)
\par
(Howard, 1960, 1971; White, 1992).
\par
That transformation can be achieved by the
\par
introduction of a reward function to the model,
\par
which attaches values for both state occupancies
\par
and transitions. Note that one multi-attribute
\par
value function could be defined over states and
\par
another over transitions. In addition, since both
\par
states and transitions are assumed finite, a reward
\par
function for states and transitions can be precom-
\par
puted. The general form of the reward function is
\par
V=f(O1, O2, . . . , On)=Skg(Sik)+h(Tijk)
\par
where O1, O2, . . . , On are the objectives of the
\par
decision-maker, Sik means state i at time k, and
\par
Tijk means transition from state i to state j at time
\par
k. Such a function allows any algorithm to weight
\par
the benefits of the outcomes and transitions of
\par
every action available to the decision maker, thus
\par
providing it with the necessary criteria for choos-
\par
ing the best among those.
\par
