\par
In addition, because the information received is
\par
not complete, we will have only an estimation of
\par
the system's state so a probability distribution
\par
function (pdf) will specify our current belief of it.
\par
Yet, as we have a dynamic system, there will be a
\par
flow of (partial) information feeding the current
\par
state's pdf, and an updating process should take
\par
place considering the probability of each observa-
\par
tion given each state of the model. In other words,
\par
we still have a (discrete) Markov process, but its
\par
current state is a probability distribution function
\par
updated via Bayes' rule (a non-Markov, continu-
\par
ous process). Such a scheme is portrayed in Fig-
\par
ure 2.
\par
Here, a situation can be modelled as a Markov
\par
process with four states, but the decision-maker
\par
receives only hints about the current system's
\par
state. As we see in the picture, although the
\par
underlying model is discrete, the information flow
\par
feeds a continuously updated probability distribu-
\par
tion function on the discrete model's current state.
\par
Nevertheless, the decisions are usually (but not
\par
necessarily) taken just after the Markov system
\par
makes its transitions.
\par
Partial information about a process reflects the
\par
situation of most real life situations, which makes
\par
the POMDP approach a solid modelling tech-
\par
nique. Unfortunately, the downside is the lack of
\par
efficient algorithms for solving these more com-
\par
plex models. Actually, many different approaches
\par
have been investigated to find a fast, reliable
\par
algorithm. Examples are algorithms based on
\par
value iteration (Sawaki and Ichikawa, 1978; Cas-
\par
sandra et al., 1994), policy iteration (Sondik,
\par
1978; Putterman, 1990; Hansen, 1997, 1998a,b),
\par
accelerated value iteration (White and Scherer,
\par
1989), structured representations (Boutilier and
\par
Poole, 1996), incremental pruning (Zang and Liu,
\par
1996; Cassandra et al., 1997), and extra percep-
\par
tual data (Chrisman, 1992).
