4.3. POMDP
\par
A major pitfall when modelling complex, dynamic
\par
situations with Markov decision models is to
\par
know for sure what the current state of the system
\par
is. Indeed, knowing the system's current state
\par
means to know the exact value of each system
\par
variable at the present time, a strong demand in
\par
highly uncertain environments like the ones found
\par
in multi-sensor data fusion applications.
\par
Further developments in Markov decision pro-
\par
cesses brought about the concept of POMDP. A
\par
POMDP is a hidden Markov model about which
\par
we can receive partial information regarding the
\par
current state, so we are not able to state with
\par
certainty what that state is. Although this kind of
\par
model is more realistic, the lack of certainty about
\par
the current state of the system prevents the use of
\par
the dynamic programming's conventional recur-
