\par
The initial works on influence diagrams were
\par
focused on developing a computer-aided mod-
\par
elling tool for representing decision analysis prob-
\par
lems (Miller et al., 1976; Howard and Matheson,
\par
1981). Olmsted (1983) proposed and defined the
\par
idea of manipulating that tool in order to not
\par
only represent, but also solve decision analysis
\par
problems. Shachter (1986, 1988, 1990) further de-
\par
veloped the necessary mathematical background
\par
for solving decision analysis problems. Multiple
\par
objective problems can be formulated and solved
\par
easily in influence diagrams (Merkhofer, 1988;
\par
Buede and Ferrel, 1993).
\par
Copyright \copyright{} 2000 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd. Once well elaborated, an influence diagram can
\par
be analysed by a combination of graph-theoretic
\par
and mathematical operations for determining the
\par
optimal decision strategy to be taken. The opera-
\par
tions that allow a complete analysis of any in-
\par
fluence diagram are evidence absorption,
\par
deterministic absorption, null reversal, arc rever-
\par
sal and deterministic propagation.
\par
Influence diagrams share the same graph theory
\par
principles as BN; that is, the same representation
\par
of a set of random variables and its joint proba-
\par
bility distribution. However, unlike the unique set
\par
of variables found in BN, influence diagrams
\par
contain four different sets of random variables,
\par
one for each type of node that can be present in
\par
the model: value nodes, decision nodes, chance
\par
nodes, and deterministic nodes.
\par
Insightful discussions about the value of infor-
\par
mation in uncertain decision problems can be
\par
found in La Valle (1968a,b,c), Ziemba and Butter-
\par
worth (1975), and Hilton (1981). Yet Matheson
\par
(1990) shows the advantages of using such influ-
\par
ence diagrams as modelling tools with respect to
\par
their ability to capture the value of information in
\par
the modelling process.
\par
So far, we have analysed the BN as inference
\par
