5.2. The `DDN zone'
\par
The main idea under the concept of a DDN is to
\par
apply both BN and influence diagrams in the
\par
same application, while optimizing the resulting
\par
synergy between these techniques. Here, the focus
\par
will be in exploiting the advantages of each ap-
\par
proach, while eliminating their weaknesses
\par
through the merging process.
\par
DDNs are intended to solve complex, real-time
\par
decision problems that meet specific criteria.
\par
These criteria are an evolving decision window, in
\par
which a specific decision, D0, (e.g. launching a
\par
product in the market or firing a missile) can be
\par
taken or not taken at any time. During this
\par
P.C.G. DA COSTA AND D.M. BUEDE
\par
evolving window, information is being received
\par
that affects the resolution of uncertainty concern-
\par
ing outcomes on the conflicting objectives for D0;
\par
see Figure 3. Finally, an additional decision can
\par
also be made about what information to gather,
\par
D1. D0 addresses the current situation, while D1
\par
recognizes the needs of the future.
\par
The part of the decision problems' domain
\par
shown in this picture is where the DDN technique
\par
clearly outperforms previous approaches, so we
\par
may refer to it as the `DDN zone'. An example of
\par
a problem that can be classified in the `DDN
\par
zone' of the problem domain is the fighter pilot's
\par
weapon and sensors allocation problem (Buede
\par
and Costa, 1999; Costa, 1999), which is discussed
\par
later in this paper.
\par
A DDN can be seen as having three different
\par
parts (see Figure 4): the decision sub-net, the data
\par
fusion sub-net, and the inference sub-nets. These
\par
sub-nets are not constant with respect to time,
\par
and the modifications between two subsequent
\par
time steps can be caused by external changes, like
\par
a new track from a sensor in a tracking control
\par
application, or by internal decisions, as the deci-
\par
sion sub-net may turn a sensor on or off, depend-
\par
ing on the value of its information.
\par
One or more influence diagrams, whose main
\par
objective is to define an optimal set of actions to
\par
be taken during time step `t', comprise the
