\par
`t-1'. As an example, if a track is an enemy wild
\par
weasel aircraft, depending upon the value of in-
\par
formation versus the risk of keeping a radar acti-
\par
vated, the system may decide to turn a given
\par
radar off, which will decrease the number of input
\par
nodes of each inference sub-net's BN.
\par
A DDN system like the one portrayed in Figure
\par
4 may become quite complex, and its creation is a
\par
task that would require a major system engineer-
\par
ing effort. This is the only way of dealing with the
\par
plethora of information that will arise from the
\par
many domain experts involved in the probability
\par
elicitation process. In addition, the wide spectrum
\par
of sensors, weapon systems and other external
\par
interfaces of the system will also make an opti-
\par
mized approach to the system's life cycle a key
\par
issue.
