\par
6.1. The manufacturing machine problem
\par
A hypothetical manufacturing machine is made of
\par
two identical, independent internal components,
\par
and produces a finished product at the end of a
\par
1-h cycle. At the beginning of a cycle, there is a
\par
probability of 0.1 that a given component will be
\par
defective, and a defective component yields the
\par
probability of 0.5 of causing a defective finished
\par
product.
\par
There are four possible control alternatives
\par
available for this process. The first, and simplest
\par
one, is to let the manufacturing process keep
\par
going without inspecting the internal components
\par
or examining the finished product. The second
\par
choice is to examine the finished product for its
\par
quality at the end of each cycle. The third alterna-
\par
tive is to open the machine and inspect each
\par
component, which requires one full cycle to be
\par
executed; any inspected component recognized as
\par
defective is immediately replaced. The last alter-
\par
native is to open the machine and replace both
\par
components without inspection.
\par
The goal here is to find the best policy for two
\par
cases: a three-cycle stage and a four-cycle stage.
