6.2. The POMDP solution
\par
This situation can be modelled with a high level of
\par
dependability by the POMDP technique, as the
\par
output of the process (the finished product) con-
\par
veniently fits in the definition of partial informa-
\par
tion of an internal MDP (the hidden machine
\par
components). That internal process will have
\par
three distinct states, one for each possible combi-
\par
nation of component situation (0, both operable;
\par
1, one defective; and 2, two defectives), while the
\par
observations could be either a good product or a
\par
defective product. In addition, there will be four
\par
possible actions for each stage: manufacture, ex-
\par
amine, inspect and replace, and replace only
\par
(MFG, EX, INSP and REP, respectively). Figure
\par
5, adapted from Smallwood and Sondik (1973),
\par
shows the solution for both scenarios (3 and 4
\par
stages).
\par
Each point in the equilateral triangles of Figure
\par
5 represents the three probabilities for states 0, 1,
\par
and 2, that is, a possible belief of the state vector
\par
y. In this representation, the perpendicular dis-
\par
tance from a given point to the side opposite to
\par
the i-th vertex correspond to the degree that the
\par
process is believed to be in state i. As an example,
\par
if we believe that our current y vector is [0.5, 0.5,
\par
0.0], which means that our knowledge implies
\par
equally likely probabilities of the system being in
\par
either state 0 or 1, and zero probability of being
\par
in state 2, then this point in the triangle will be in
\par
the middle of the line between vertexes 0 and 1.
\par
The equilateral triangles portrayed in Figure 5
\par
are called a policy region diagram, and show
\par
which decision policies are optimal for specific
\par
regions of y. Therefore, Figure 5 illustrates that if
\par
we consider that the belief vector y is in region
\par
MFG, then the manufacturing option will give us
\par
the best expected reward (for a three-stage hori-
\par
zon). Conversely, if it is very likely that both
\par
components are defective (vector y in region
\par
REP), then the replace option should be taken. In
\par
the four-stage case, these are regions in which
\par
each possible alternative is optimal. Increasing the
\par
number of stages results in an even greater divi-
\par
sion of the triangle, where policy mixtures are
\par
more common; see Smallwood and Sondik (1973)
\par
for a more detailed explanation of this subject.
