7. COMPARISON OF APPROACHES FOR
\par
MULTI-SENSOR DATA SYSTEMS
\par
Although simple cases like the one we just vis-
\par
ited are convenient for comparison purposes,
\par
most real life situations can be dependably mod-
\par
elled only by much more complex schemes. A
\par
clear illustration of this fact may be found when
\par
modelling real-time, feature-rich decision prob-
\par
lems like the fighter aircraft's management of
\par
sensors and weapons. For this specific kind of
\par
problem, it is our view that no other optimiza-
\par
tion technique can achieve more tractable, reli-
\par
able models (and results) than the DDN
\par
technique.
\par
Indeed, it was already shown (Costa, 1999)
\par
that the rule-based system approach, though
\par
computationally attractive, does not provide a
\par
flexible and reliable means of dealing with the
\par
higher degree of uncertainty associated with this
\par
situation, usually achieving models that are na\i{} \textasciidieresis ve
\par
at best. Here, our intention is to demonstrate
\par
that DDN also outperforms the POMDP ap-
\par
proach for this type of application. First, the
\par
fighter pilot's problem will be discussed and the
\par
DDN system developed in Costa (1999) will be
\par
presented. Then, the final subsections will convey
\par
our ideas and conclusions on the differences be-
\par
tween the two approaches.
\par
7.1. The fighter pilot's problem
\par
In a typical mission, a fighter aircraft has to
\par
take-off from a friendly aerodrome, perform a
\par
high-altitude flight over friendly territory, de-
\par
scend to a lower altitude preferably before the
\par
enemy's radar coverage, attack the mission's
\par
target(s), and egress home safely. The enemy's
\par
role is to detect the incoming fighter and deny
\par
its attack, using weapons like interceptor aircraft
\par
with air-to-air missiles, ground-based anti-
\par
aircraft artillery (AAA), or ground-to-air mis-
\par
siles.
\par
Behind the scenes lies a high-tech contest be-
\par
tween the intruder fighter and enemy's forces,
\par
usually called electronic warfare. This contest
\par
can be compared to a hide-and-seek game, where
\par
the intruder fighter tries to stay out of enemy's
\par
electronic eyes (e.g. early warning radars, inter-
\par
ceptor sensors, AAA radars) as long as possible.
\par
The ability of the intruder to hide from these
\par
hostile sensors will depend on tactics like low-
\par
level flight and reduction (or elimination) of
\par
Copyright \copyright{} 2000 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd. communication and radar emissions. The first is
\par
intended to use the terrain as a mask against the
\par
enemy's radars, while the latter avoids being dis-
\par
covered by the enemy's passive detectors.
\par
However, flying into enemy territory means to
\par
be vulnerable to a wide array of threats, and for
\par
most of them awareness is the first requisite to
\par
improve the chances of surviving. To be aware,
\par
the pilot counts on the information provided by
\par
it's own sensors, which can usually be grouped
\par
in two distinct types: passive and active. Sensors
\par
in the first group detect all transmissions and
\par
classify the respective sources; thus, they do not
\par
need to make any transmissions by themselves.
\par
Sensors in the second group are those that trans-
\par
mit energy for a period of time and wait for a
\par
reply in order to obtain information.
\par
Although passive sensors are a stealthy way of
\par
gathering information about the enemy, an obvi-
\par
ous drawback is the fact that their efficacy de-
\par
pends on whether the enemy is emitting. In
\par
addition, passive sensors like the radar homing
\par
and warning (RHAW, provides rough informa-
\par
tion on the bearing of the source of an electro-
\par
magnetic signal) do not provide a reliable
\par
measure of distance, at best providing a `line of
\par
sight' detection, at worst a `plane of sight' for a
\par
given azimuth angle and unknown altitude.
\par
Active sensors, on the other hand, usually
\par
provide more accurate measurement. As a conse-
\par
quence, the decision to decrease uncertainty or
\par
detectability is an important trade off to be
\par
made, mainly during a high attention-demanding
\par
situation as a flight sortie. However, there are
\par
other issues on the use of active sensors. Among
\par
them is the management of the sensor's power,
\par
that is, how to direct it (allocate it) for the many
\par
surrounding enemy's targets/aggressors. The pi-
\par
lot performs this allocation wisely, in order to
\par
achieve an optimal use of the aircraft's weapon
\par
systems (offensively and/or defensively). Here,
\par
the level of uncertainty will also guide the pilot's
\par
decisions.
\par
Those decisions are not restrained to electronic
\par
warfare considerations. The pilot also has to
\par
deal with navigation issues, complex aircraft sys-
\par
tems' monitoring, damage control, fuel consump-
\par
tion; ultimately, he still has to pilot his aircraft
\par
in a 540 knots, near-the-ground flight. In addi-
\par
tion, modern aircraft and sophisticated defence
\par
systems have dramatically increased the pilot's
\par
workload, particularly in the most critical phase
\par
of the mission, the attack.
\par
