These aspects more than justify the efforts that
\par
have been made to reduce the number of decisions
\par
a pilot has to make during flight. Systems are
\par
being engineered to function automatically most
\par
of the time, while warning the pilot when there is
\par
a problem. Yet, sensor allocation is a task that is
\par
still much more manual than automatic due to the
\par
great amount of uncertainty faced by the pilot.
\par
The fighter pilot's dilemma is to find an opti-
\par
mal set of decisions for each specific moment of
\par
his mission. As an example, a decision of not
\par
launching a missile against an approaching air-
\par
craft can be the optimal one for a given time and
\par
the worst one just a few seconds latter, all depend-
\par
ing upon the level of uncertainty about that ap-
\par
proaching aircraft being an enemy. Even if an
\par
enemy, if that intruder is not a serious threat for
\par
the aircraft, the pilot would prefer to just avoid it
\par
and save the missile (a scarce resource) for other
\par
situations in the mission.
\par
In short, the fighter pilot's problem is in a
\par
context where the decision parameters may vary
\par
drastically over time, and the decisions taken at
\par
moment `t' have a definitive impact on the
\par
parameters for moment `t+1'.
\par
7.2. The DDN solution
\par
In this sample implementation, the DDN struc-
\par
ture has only one influence diagram for the deci-
\par
sion sub-net, which is attached to a variable-size
\par
BN that comprises the data fusion sub-net. Figure
\par
8 gives an idea of this joint structure.
\par
The four rectangular nodes of Figure 8 are the
\par
decision sub-net's action nodes (decision nodes).
\par
These nodes rely on the information brought by
\par
the data fusion sub-net's nodes and the knowl-
\par
edge and value structure contained in the other
\par
decision sub-net's nodes for making the most
\par
appropriate actions for each moment of the
\par
mission.
\par
In addition to the decision nodes, the decision
\par
sub-net also carries the system's value structure.
\par
The main objective is represented by the node
\par
`accomplish mission', which can be seen as the
\par
strategic objective of the value structure, while the
\par
three nodes that converge to the main objective
\par
complete the fundamental objective structure that
\par
guides the decision-making process (Keeney,
\par
1992). As the fundamental objective hierarchy in a
\par
DDN is explicitly stated, any modification in the
\par
actual problem can be easily transposed to the
\par
