\par
7.3. The equivalent POMDP system
\par
In a very broad comparison, each DDN's infer-
\par
ence sub-net can be modelled as a MDP, in other
\par
words, as an internal process of a POMDP. How-
\par
ever, each BN of the inference sub-net has a total
\par
of 34 variables, some with more than ten possible
\par
states. Even if we assume that each variable has
\par
only four states, there would be a total of 434
\par
possible combinations (or 295 147 905 179 352
\par
825 856 states in a MDP model).
\par
Therefore, a simple translation from the DDN
\par
model to a POMDP in a process similar to the
\par
one we just did in the machine example would
\par
confront us with a thoroughly intractable prob-
\par
lem. On top of that constraint, there is also the
\par
issue of variables with non-constant number of
\par
states, like those in the DDN model that are
\par
dependent on the number of available threats. A
\par
POMDP model, which requires static definitions
\par
of the number of possible states, does not have
\par
the same flexibility of the DDN approach, with its
\par
variable number of inference sub-nets (one for
\par
each threat).
\par
In essence, in order to model that situation
\par
through a POMDP, one would have to use not
\par
only a different synthesis approach, probably cre-
\par
ating a new variable to account for the number of
\par
threats, but also would have to make many sim-
\par
plifying assumptions. Yet, both alternatives would
\par
render the model either too na\i{} \textasciidieresis ve or still in-
\par
Copyright \copyright{} 2000 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd. 8. COMPARISON OF APPROACHES III:
\par
CONCEPTUAL ISSUES
\par
In addition to the lack of feasible modelling solu-
\par
tions in the POMDP approach, there are also
\par
conceptual issues that render the DDN approach
\par
more appropriate for those types of problems that
\par
satisfy the DDN assumptions. Indeed, some of
\par
the advantages mentioned in the DDN system's
\par
explanation just cannot be completely replicated
\par
by the POMDP approach, mostly because it does
\par
not provide the same flexibility and features of the
\par
DDN scheme. The following paragraphs address
\par
these issues.
\par
8.1. The impreciseness of sensors
\par
As we already know, in order to analyse the
\par
current situation the fighter pilot relies on the
\par
aircraft's various sensors, which will give him
\par
information on the various parameters related
\par
with the possible threats to his aircraft. However,
\par
sensor information itself is not perfect, and the
\par
pilot has to be aware of that impreciseness when
\par
deciding what action to perform. Furthermore,
\par
different sensors of an aircraft have distinct levels
\par
of reliability, which is also an issue to be consid-
\par
ered in the decision process.
\par
Likewise, an automated decision system has to
\par
account for this sensor impreciseness before esti-
\par
mating the actual situation. In the DDN `wise
\par
pilot' system, before being used for inference pur-
\par
poses, the information coming from a sensor
\par
passes through a special node called confusion
\par
matrix (Buede and Girardi, 1997), which has the
\par
J. Multi-Crit. Decis. Anal. 9: 243--262 (2000)
\par
