\par
same states as the sensor's input node. The prob-
\par
ability distribution between the input node and
\par
the confusion matrix node will reflect the
\par
(im)preciseness of that sensor.
\par
However, for the POMDP model, many states
\par
will have the same setting for a specific signature
\par
addressed by an aircraft sensor. This complex
\par
relationship between POMDP states and sensor
\par
capabilities must be considered in crafting sensor
\par
reports about the POMDP states.
\par
In addition, computing the synergy between
\par
different sensors through the internal transition
\par
probabilities between states poses a new limitation
\par
to the POMDP approach. Should any sensor be
\par
replaced or upgraded (i.e. for a more precise one),
\par
in a DDN system this will be a matter of readjust-
\par
ing that sensor's respective confusion matrices.
\par
While in a POMDP system, many changes would
\par
have to be made to mostly all of the internal
\par
transition probability matrices.
\par
This can be thought as a consequence of the
\par
differences in the way each scheme works through
\par
the inference process. The DDN's nodes are actu-
\par
ally variables of the system; and most of the belief
\par
updating is a local process (with relation to the
\par
variables). In contrast, in an internal MDP, each
\par
state is a combination of the many variables, so a
\par
transition probability matrix between two states
\par
conveys information about all the variables of the
\par
system (not a local process).
\par
One possible way of overcoming this problem
\par
would be to extract the sensor's impreciseness
\par
from the internal MDP and resolve it in the y
\par
vector updating process. However, in order to
\par
account for the sensors' capabilities and limita-
\par
tions, which are also influenced by factors inside
\par
the MDP process (like aircraft's current position),
\par
a BN system would have to be developed for the
\par
y vector updating. Such a hybrid system is more
\par
likely to add the shortcomings of both techniques
\par
than their advantages, as some variables would
\par
probably have to be duplicated and the added
\par
complexity would only make the solution more
\par
computationally complex.
\par
8.2. Number of tracks
\par
We have already covered this issue, but there are
\par
some interesting points that should be raised as
\par
both techniques present limitations in this aspect.
\par
The POMDP's lack of effective algorithms for
\par
solving highly complex problems was already
\par
Copyright \copyright{} 2000 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd. commented, so we will analyse the DDN's restric-
\par
tions on this issue.
\par
Even though the DDN is theoretically un-
\par
bounded with respect to the number of threats,
\par
practical considerations on both system memory
\par
and performance dictate that there should be a
\par
limit on the number of threats to be considered by
\par
the system. As we have noted in the DDN system
\par
presentation, for each track there will be a sepa-
\par
rate BN in the inference sub-net that consumes
\par
memory and processing power and that also in-
\par
creases the complexity of the merging process in
\par
the data fusion sub-net.
\par
Nevertheless, it should be noted that current
\par
technology allows this limit to be high enough
\par
that functional systems can be made. In addition,
\par
the fast pace at which the processing power and
\par
memory technology is growing tends to render
\par
such a system practically unbounded in this
\par
aspect.
\par
The POMDP does not support a variable num-
\par
ber of threats, since each state in the internal
\par
MDP process has to be defined prior to pro-
\par
gramme execution; in other words, we can not
\par
simply `add an enemy threat' to the internal sys-
\par
tem as it is detected by the aircraft's sensors.
\par
There might be different solutions for this con-
\par
straint, like fixing a certain number of tracks and
\par
filling the state parameters of the non-existing
\par
ones with `dummy' data, or creating a new vari-
\par
able to track the number of threats and activate
\par
different internal MDP's as needed. Yet, no mat-
\par
ter how inventive we are, these would be only
\par
adaptations of a model that would only add com-
\par
plexity with respect to the original. In this case,
\par
the best alternative is to abandon the `variable
\par
number of tracks' model employed in the DDN
\par
system, and go to a completely different ap-
\par
proach. However, such an effort would be cost
\par
effective only after the other huge limitations of
\par
the POMDP approaches were solved.
\par
8.3. Long horizon and the feasibility of
\par
approaches
\par
As we know from dynamic programming, the best
\par
alternative in the short term is not necessarily the
\par
best for the long term. Indeed, we could see that
\par
the best decision policy in the manufacturing ma-
\par
chine would depend on how far we go into the
\par
future. This basic concept also holds for the more
\par
complicated multi-sensor data fusion system
\par
problem, where the decision on whether to turn
