\par
the IFF on or leave it off should be made taking
\par
into account not only its immediate rewards (i.e.
\par
determining whether a given track is a friend or a
\par
foe), but also the future implications.
\par
Apart from its convenient resemblance with
\par
many real life situations, one of the most appeal-
\par
ing reasons that keeps POMDPs as one of the
\par
main topics in scientific research today is its ca-
\par
pacity of choosing an optimal option by a direct
\par
calculation of its future reward. In other words, a
\par
POMDP system will exhaustively visit all possible
\par
paths of states in a given horizon.
\par
DDNs employ a different approach, as not all
\par
possible `states of the world' are visited. With a
\par
structure that takes account of the probabilistic
\par
interrelationships among variables, systems like
\par
the `wise pilot' rely on expert information to make
\par
inferences based upon the available data to calcu-
\par
late the best alternative. Nonetheless, it is impor-
\par
tant to note a subtler issue addressed by DDN,
\par
namely the value of collecting information so as
\par
to resolve key uncertainties for the decisions in
\par
the future. This aspect of the DDN approach
\par
addresses the trade-offs between the immediate
\par
rewards of a given action and its respective future
\par
implications.
\par
Instead of establishing a given number of itera-
\par
tions and computing the optimized policy for that
\par
horizon, as it was done in the influence diagram
\par
systems of Figures 6 and 7, the DDN uses the
\par
dual-control approach for computing both the
\par
best actions and information collection activities
\par
available. This alternative scheme avoids the
\par
problems found in these simple problems, where
\par
the influence diagrams, though fairly resistant to
\par
increases in the number of variables, proved to be
\par
less than practical for a longer horizon (more
\par
stages).
\par
The main idea of the concept of dual-control
\par
employed here is to use expert information as a
\par
basis for inference about both the near-term and
\par
far-term. Indeed, a DDN addresses a limited set
\par
of dynamic decisions in which one alternative
\par
from a specified set needs to be selected and the
\par
big issue is when to make the selection. In the
\par
`wise pilot' system, the short-term implications are
\par
related to the immediate gains of each action, like
\par
firing a missile, while the longer horizon concerns
\par
deal with what kind of information is most impor-
\par
tant if the selection is delayed as well as what will
\par
be the ramifications of collecting that information
\par
with an active sensor.
\par
9. COMPARISON OF APPROACHES IV:
\par
MULTI-CRITERIA ASPECTS
\par
Even though this characteristic is not always eas-
\par
ily perceivable, problems inside the `DDN zone'
\par
involve decisions that cannot be taken with ele-
\par
mentary utility functions. In other words, the
\par
complexity of the situation is also reflected in the
\par
multiple attributes or objectives that guide the
\par
decision process. Indeed, most situations inside
\par
the `DDN zone' will require complex value
\par
structures.
\par
Ultimately, both techniques analysed here offer
\par
a means of dealing with multi-criteria decision
\par
making. However, the methodologies employed
\par
by each approach have totally different character-
\par
istics, and it is based in these particular differ-
\par
ences that we claim the DDN to be more
\par
applicable to multi-criteria decision problems.
\par
9.1. Multi-criteria in the DDN approach
\par
In a DDN model, the value structure of a prob-
\par
lem is represented in the decision sub-net through
\par
a direct translation from the original hierarchy of
\par
attributes to nodes in the decision sub-net. Each
\par
node represents a given attribute/objective. All
\par
nodes that contain value objectives collapse into
\par
an ultimate value node, which contains the utility
\par
vector of the DDN. This vector is a normalization
\par
of the various attributes' weights.
\par
In the case study previously shown, the fighter
\par
pilot's fundamental objective hierarchy contains
\par
three secondary nodes, each carrying its own
\par
mapping from the act space to the n-dimensional
\par
consequence space (Keeney and Raiffa, 1976).
\par
The decisions available to the pilot will have
\par
different, usually opposite consequences for each
\par
objective, so the need of a multi-attribute value
\par
function for dealing with the trade-offs involved
\par
in the decision process is clear.
\par
Figure 10 shows the insertion of the fundamen-
\par
tal objective hierarchy into the decision sub-net of
\par
the `wise pilot' DDN system. Looking at that
\par
picture, one can easily realize that the intercon-
\par
nections between the decision nodes, the value
\par
node and all other nodes inside the decision sub-
\par
net provide a clear mapping of the many sub-
\par
tleties of the decision-making process in the
\par
system. In other words, the technique's graphical
\par
nature provides a means of understanding how
\par
each objective is inserted in the context of the
\par
system, its significance to each decision, which
