\par
Yet, apart from the easiness in communicating
\par
the model's subtitles, the explicit way in which the
\par
DDN approach depicts the problem's value struc-
\par
ture has other important advantages. One advan-
\par
tage is the ability of changing the multi-attribute
\par
value function to reflect changes among the deci-
\par
sion parameters. Indeed, this function reflects the
\par
weight each attribute has in the overall decision
\par
process, a direct outcome of the criteria used to
\par
evaluate these attributes; changes in the evalua-
\par
tion criteria will have immediate consequences to
\par
the multi-attribute value function.
\par
In the example of the fighter pilot's problem,
\par
the relative importance of the objectives will be
\par
dictated by a unique combination of political and
\par
operational issues that are characteristic of a
\par
given conflict. Distinct conflicts would certainly
\par
have different characteristics and concerns that
\par
point to different interrelationships among the
\par
fundamental objectives. Adjusting a DDN model
\par
to reflect those differences is a matter of changing
\par
the weights that are explicitly stated in the `ac-
\par
complish mission' node.
\par
Obviously, such a feature can also be used for
\par
performing sensitivity analysis, as changes to the
\par
multi-attribute function may be easily replicated
\par
in the system and the consequences of such
\par
changes immediately assessed. Indeed, in the `wise
\par
pilot' DDN system, a sensitivity analysis can be
\par
performed in order to make the system suitable
\par
for scenarios that have different political and/or
\par
doctrinal characteristics. In other words, the sys-
\par
tem is able to reflect the changes in the way
\par
airborne operations are conducted, a very desir-
\par
able feature for a decision process with multiple,
\par
volatile criteria to follow.
