9.2. Multi-criteria in the POMDP approach
\par
As stated before, the POMDP approach can also
\par
replicate a multi-criteria decision environment,
\par
since the decisions are explicitly modelled. How-
\par
ever, as it could be noted from Section 4, the
\par
value structure is not replicated in an explicit way
\par
as in the DDN case. Actually, the value structure
\par
is transformed into rewards that are associated
\par
with each state transition or state location.
\par
Such a scheme does not allow direct modifica-
\par
tions to the model in order to reflect changes in
\par
the evaluation criteria. Actually, modifying the
\par
value structure in forms of weights or specific
\par
objectives in such a scheme would require a com-
\par
plete reformulation of the reward function for all
\par
transitions and states.
\par
In other words, attempting to perform a sensi-
\par
tivity analysis, or even to incorporate a singular
\par
change in the evaluation criteria would require an
\par
extensive reengineering effort to the whole system
\par
since its value structure is implicit in every of its
\par
states and transitions.
\par
As a consequence, even though the approach
\par
allows the modelling of a multi-criteria decision
\par
problem, the parameters related with the weight
\par
and relevance of each attribute will be spread
\par
among the system's variables. Apart from the
\par
technique's shortcomings that we have already
\par
covered, and from its intractability for complex
\par
problems, this limitation is the definitive reason
\par
for not using it in multi-criteria decision
\par
problems.
\par
interrelationships between its many variables, re-
\par
lying on Bayesian-based algorithms for updating
\par
the current knowledge of the system. In addition,
\par
a value structure is explicitly stated through spe-
\par
cial nodes, where each possible combination has
\par
its expected utility calculated and stored for use in
\par
decision selection. However, the DDN approach
\par
is limited for use by its assumptions.
\par
The POMDP technique, although providing an
\par
elegant probabilistic scheme and theoretical sup-
\par
port, still has a long way to go before achieving
\par
the same practical results experienced today by
\par
DDN structures. Indeed, the current state of tech-
\par
nology prevents the technique from achieving any
\par
tractable model, apart for the simplest situations,
\par
while DDN systems can present feasible solutions
\par
for complex systems with a similar powerful
\par
background.
\par
Among the issues presented when comparing
\par
the two approaches, the suitability for solving
\par
multi-criteria decision problems was analysed
\par
with no regard to the POMDP's lack of capable
\par
algorithms, and the DDN's explicit modelling of
\par
multi-attribute functions was presented as an in-
\par
valuable asset. Therefore, we claim that even in
\par
the case of a breakthrough in the algorithms for
\par
solving POMDPs, the DDN technique is the pre-
\par
ferred technique when its assumptions are
\par
justified.
\par
10. EPILOGUE
\par
Both POMDP and DDN are analytical models
\par
aimed at solving complex, real-life problems that
\par
rely on the postulates of multi-attribute utility
\par
theory and probability theory.
\par
The POMDP model assumes the situation as an
\par
internal, not directly observable Markov decision
\par
process, and models it by exhaustively listing all
\par
possible states in which the system can be, storing
\par
the probabilities of each state transition and its
\par
respective rewards. In addition, partial observa-
\par
tions of this internal model are used for inferring
\par
the current state of the system, which is proba-
\par
bilistically updated for each new piece of
\par
information.
\par
