@article{fenwickImprovingEfficiencyRelevance2000,
  title = {Improving the Efficiency and Relevance of Health Technology Assessment: The Role of Iterative Decision Analytic Modelling},
  shorttitle = {Improving the Efficiency and Relevance of Health Technology Assessment},
  author = {Fenwick, Elisabeth and Claxton, Karl and Sculpher, Mark and Briggs, Andrew},
  year = 2000,
  journal = {Discussion Paper-university of York Centre for HEALTH Economics},
  publisher = {University of York},
  langid = {english},
  note = {CENTRE FOR HEALTH ECONOMICS
\par
DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC \& RELATED STUDIES
\par
INSTITUTE OF HEALTH SCIENCES, UNIVERSITY OF OXFORD
\par
\textbf{Improving the efficiency and}
\par
\textbf{relevance of health technology}
\par
\textbf{assessment: The role of iterative}
\par
\textbf{decision analytic modelling}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Elisabeth Fenwick}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Karl Claxton}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Mark Sculpher}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Andrew Briggs}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{DISCUSSION PAPER 179}IMPROVING THE EFFICIENCY AND RELEVANCE OF}
\par
\textbf{HEALTH TECHNOLOGY ASSESSMENT: THE ROLE OF}
\par
\textbf{ITERATIVE DECISION ANALYTIC MODELLING}
\par
Elisabeth Fenwick1, Karl Claxton2, Mark Sculpher1, Andrew Briggs3
\par
\emph{1. 2. 3. Centre for Health Economics, University of York}
\par
\emph{Department of Economics and Related Studies, University of York}
\par
\emph{Health Economics Research Centre, Institute of Health Sciences,}
\par
\emph{University of Oxford}
\par
May 2000\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Decision making in health care involves two sets of related decisions: those
\par
concerning appropriate service provision on the basis of existing information; and
\par
those concerned with whether to fund additional research to reduce the uncertainty
\par
relating to the decision. Information acquisition is not costless, and the allocation of
\par
funds to the enhancement of the decision makers' information set, in a budget-
\par
constrained health service, reduces the `pot' of resources available for health service
\par
provision. Hence, a framework is necessary to unify these decisions and ensure that
\par
HTA is subject to the same evaluation of efficiency as service provision.
\par
A framework is presented which addresses these two sets of decisions through the
\par
employment of decision analytic models and Bayesian value of information analysis,
\par
early and regularly within the health technology assessment process. The model
\par
becomes the vehicle of health technology assessment, managing and directing
\par
future research effort on an iterative basis over the lifetime of the technology. This
\par
ensures consistency in decision making between service provision, research and
\par
development priorities and research methods. Fulfilling the aim of the National
\par
Health Service HTA programme, that research is ``produced in the most economical
\par
way'' using ``cost effective research protocols''.
\par
The proposed framework is applied to the decision concerning the appropriate
\par
management of female patients with symptoms of urinary tract infection, which was
\par
the subject of a recent NHS HTA call for proposals. A probabilistic model is
\par
employed to fully characterise and assess the uncertainty surrounding the decision.
\par
The expected value of perfect information (EVPI) is then calculated for the full model,
\par
for each individual management strategy and for particular model parameters.
\par
Research effort can then be focused on those areas where the cost of uncertainty is
\par
high and where additional research is potentially cost-effective. The analysis can be
\par
used to identify the most appropriate research protocol and to concentrate research
\par
upon particular parameters where more precise estimates would be of most value.CHE Discussion Paper 179 1
\par
\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
Decision making in health care is inevitably undertaken in a context of uncertainty concerning
\par
the effectiveness and resource costs of health care interventions and programmes. Therefore,
\par
two related sets of decisions need to be taken: those concerning appropriate service provision
\par
on the basis of existing information; and those concerned with whether to fund additional
\par
research to reduce the uncertainty relating to the decision. Decision analytic models have been
\par
suggested as a systematic means of guiding both types of decision making under conditions of
\par
uncertainty [1,2] and recently Claxton et al [3] have illustrated the benefits of employing
\par
stochastic models incorporating Bayesian Value of Information (VOI) analysis to address these
\par
decisions.
\par
This paper builds on earlier work relating to iterative frameworks for HTA [4,5], the use of
\par
models to prioritise research [6,7] and methods for estimating the VOI from research [3,8,9], by
\par
suggesting that such models should be instigated with the emergence of a new health
\par
technology and updated regularly as more information emerges. In this framework, each
\par
subsequent modelling stage is informed by the preceding model, updated to incorporate
\par
information acquired to date. Thus the model assumes a predominant role in the management
\par
of the HTA process, providing a vehicle to unify iterative decisions concerning efficient services
\par
to patients, the potential cost of uncertainty, the value of additional information and the most
\par
efficient means of acquiring that information throughout the life-cycle of the technology.
\par
Integrating this framework within the HTA process would improve the efficiency of a health
\par
technology assessment programme, ensuring that HTA is subject to the same efficiency criteria
\par
as service provision.2
\par
Improving the efficiency and relevance of health technology assessment
\par
The benefits and practicality of the proposed approach are demonstrated through application to
\par
the decision concerning the management of non-pregnant women with symptoms of urinary
\par
tract infection (Section 3). Prior to the example application, the methods employed within the
\par
framework are introduced in Section 2.
\par
\textbf{2. METHODOLOGICAL BACKGROUND}
\par
Recently a Bayesian decision theoretic framework has been suggested for the evaluation of
\par
health technologies [3,8,9]. This approach distinguishes the decision concerning efficient
\par
service provision, given the existing level of information available, from the decision concerning
\par
whether to fund the generation of new information through further research to inform this
\par
decision in the future.
\par
The decision concerning efficient service provision given existing information involves identifying
\par
the strategy associated with the best decision payoff given no additional information (the \emph{a priori}
\par
act [10]). Within the evaluation of health technologies, decision payoffs are expressed in terms
\par
of the net benefit1 associated with strategy (t) expressed in either health outcome (0) or
\par
monetary terms (:) [8,9,11,12]:
\par
0t = QALY t - (Cost t * 8-1 ) :t = (8 * QALYt ) - Cost t [equation 1a]
\par
[equation 1b]
\par
where: 8 = monetary valuation of health outcome
\par
1 When only two technologies are being assessed the decision payoff can be expressed in terms of incremental net
\par
benefit (see Claxton [3 ,8 ,9])CHE Discussion Paper 179 3
\par
The monetary valuation of health outcome (8) represents the value that society places upon the
\par
health outcome in monetary terms.2 This can be considered exogenous to the model and the
\par
analysis presented for a range of values of 8.
\par
Given an objective to maximise health for a given budget the \emph{a priori} act is identified as the
\par
technology/strategy that generates the maximum expected net benefit.3 Hence, only the mean
\par
of the distribution of net benefit is relevant for this decision [8]. The choice of a strategy other
\par
than that which maximises expected net benefit - for example in order to satisfy the
\par
requirements of traditional statistical significance - imposes costs upon society in terms of net
\par
benefits forgone (see Section 3.2.3) and contradicts the objective of maximising health gain
\par
subject to resource constraints [8].
\par
However, the uncertainty surrounding the net benefits attainable from the strategy, represented
\par
by the distribution of net benefits, is critical for the decision concerning whether to fund the
\par
collection of further information to inform the service provision decision in the future [8]. Basic
\par
measures of uncertainty have previously been suggested [13,14] and employed to provide an
\par
indication of the worth of further information collection. However, the widespread application of
\par
these techniques to the HTA process is limited by their qualitative nature. The framework
\par
suggested in this paper takes a more formal approach to both measuring uncertainty and
\par
determining the worth of further data collection, employing stochastic modelling and Bayesian
\par
VOI analysis [8,9]. Here the decision concerning the worth of further data collection is based
\par
upon the expected cost of uncertainty, which in turn is determined by the extent of the
\par
uncertainty surrounding the \emph{a priori} decision and the consequences of this uncertainty. The
\par
2 Although when valuing information, which is a public good, it is appropriate that 8 represents the societal willingness
\par
to pay for health outcome, a health service decision makers view of 8 may be influenced by the shadow price of the
\par
budget constraint. 3 The maximum expected net benefit decision rule is equivalent to choosing the strategy with the largest ICER below4
\par
Improving the efficiency and relevance of health technology assessment
\par
extent of the uncertainty associated with the \emph{a priori} decision is measured by the error
\par
probability, expressed as the proportion of iterations in which uncertainty results in a decision
\par
other than that which maximises expected net benefit being undertaken. The associated
\par
consequences are measured in terms of the health benefits forgone as a result of this
\par
uncertainty. When these health benefits are valued according to society's willingness-to-pay for
\par
health outcome (8), the approach gives a monetary value for the amount that society is willing to
\par
pay to reduce the uncertainty surrounding the service provision decision. This valuation can
\par
then be compared to the cost of gathering further information to determine whether research is
\par
worthwhile [9].
\par
\textbf{3. APPLICATION}
\par
The benefits and the practicality of the proposed approach are illustrated through an application
\par
to the decision concerning the appropriate management of non-pregnant women presenting to
\par
general practice with the symptoms of uncomplicated urinary tract infection.4 This provides a
\par
suitable example to demonstrate the value of the framework, as it is an area where existing
\par
information is limited and there is considerable variation amongst physicians concerning the
\par
most appropriate method of patient management [15,16]. In addition, the recent introduction of
\par
new methods of diagnosis (dipsticks) should prompt a reassessment regarding optimum service
\par
provision and the need for further research. Recently, the NHS health technology assessment
\par
programme identified the ``Use of dipsticks and diagnostic algorithms in the diagnosis of urinary
\par
tract infection'' as a research priority and called for proposals for primary research (97/14).
\par
the cut-off level when all dominated strategies are excluded [43] 4 Pregnant women; men; children and women presenting with symptoms suggesting upper urinary tract infection
\par
(fever; chills; nausea and loin pain) are excluded from the study due to their increased risk of underlying structural
\par
abnormalities and complications.CHE Discussion Paper 179 5
\par
The aims of the analysis are: (i) to identify the appropriate patient management strategy given
\par
the current level of information (the \emph{a priori} act); (ii) to determine whether the call for primary
\par
research in the area of UTI diagnosis was justified; and (iii) to provide additional information
\par
regarding priorities for the research process.
\par
A deterministic model is presented initially to provide a conventional decision analysis (Section
\par
3.1). Probabilistic analysis is then employed to illustrate the extent of the uncertainty
\par
surrounding the decision based upon existing information (Section 3.2). Finally, a VOI analysis
\par
indicates the elements of the decision where further information is potentially of value to society
\par
(section 3.3).
\par
\textbf{3.1 A deterministic model of the management of UTI}
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.1.1. Introduction}}
\par
The symptoms associated with urinary tract infection (UTI) are common in general practice:
\par
dysuria and frequency have been reported in 27\% and 34\% of women [17]. These symptoms
\par
are a major cause of consultation in general practice and account for 1\%-3\% of all GP
\par
consultations [18] and 2\% of all prescriptions [19]. Although the majority of cases in women are
\par
short-term and self-limiting, this condition does represent a considerable resource burden for
\par
the NHS.
\par
Early discussions with practitioners identified two main approaches for managing UTI in general
\par
practice. The first is empiric antibiotic treatment on presentation of symptoms and the second
\par
involves the use of diagnostic tests to exclude or confirm the presence of UTI prior to antibiotic
\par
treatment. Two test procedures were identified for consideration within the model: the dipstick
\par
test, a near-patient test generating immediate results; and the laboratory test, involving an6
