\textbf{\emph{3.2 3.2.1 Introduction}}
\par
\textbf{A probabilistic model of management of UTI}
\par
The techniques of deterministic sensitivity analysis are not well suited to handling interactions
\par
between parameters6 [25] and, therefore, can only give an indication of the impact of uncertainty
\par
upon the decision.
\par
In order to fully characterise uncertainty surrounding a decision and to value research aimed at
\par
reducing this uncertainty a more formal examination of the uncertainty is required. This involves
\par
the identification, characterisation and incorporation of existing available (prior) information
\par
within the model (Section 3.2.2). Monte Carlo simulation [26] is then used to generate a
\par
distribution of net benefits for each strategy within the model. The expected values of these
\par
distributions are calculated and compared to identify the \emph{a priori} act. The extent of the spread in
\par
each distribution illustrates the extent of the uncertainty concerning the net benefits that accrue
\par
from that strategy. A quantitative measure of the uncertainty surrounding each strategy is
\par
provided by the associated error probability.7
\par
The uncertainty associated with specific parameters or groups of parameters within the model
\par
can be ascertained through the use of conditional probabilistic sensitivity analysis [26] which
\par
involves repeating the Monte Carlo simulation with distributions attached only to the variables of
\par
interest.
\par
6 Joint interactions are ignored within one-way sensitivity analysis, over-estimated by analysis of extremes and difficult
\par
to interpret through multi-way sensitivity analysis [25]. 7 The error probability is the probability that the strategy does not have the maximum expected net benefit. Where
\par
there are more than 2 alternative technologies under consideration the error probability associated with each strategy
\par
is determined as the proportion of iterations within the simulation where that strategy is not optimal. See Claxton [3]
\par
for details on applying the approach when there are only 2 technologies under consideration.CHE Discussion Paper 179 15
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.2.2 Characterising existing information for the UTI model}}
\par
The existing information and hence uncertainty surrounding individual parameters is embodied
\par
within the model through the specification of (prior) probability distributions for each model input.
\par
These distributions represent both the range of values that each parameter can take and the
\par
likelihood that the parameter assumes any specific value.
\par
Probability distributions characterising existing information were assigned to every parameter
\par
within the UTI model, details are given in Table 4. The unit costs, utility and event time
\par
parameters were characterised as lognormal distributions8 with the mean given by the base
\par
case value and the standard deviation derived from the assumption that the range represented
\par
a 95\% confidence interval. The probability parameters were characterised as triangular
\par
distributions with the mode given by the base-case value and the extremes of the distribution
\par
given by the range.9
