\textbf{\emph{3.3 }The value of information}
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.3.1 Introduction}}
\par
Information from additional research is valuable to the decision-maker because it reduces the
\par
uncertainty surrounding the decision concerning efficient service provision. Bayesian VOI
\par
analysis provides a method to measure the expected costs of decision uncertainty and,
\par
therefore value research aimed at reducing this uncertainty. The analysis, which follows from
\par
probabilistic sensitivity analysis, involves formal consideration and valuation of the20
\par
Improving the efficiency and relevance of health technology assessment
\par
consequences associated with the uncertainty to provide an explicit measure of the cost
\par
associated with the uncertainty surrounding a decision [32-34]. In HTA, the consequences
\par
associated with uncertainty are the net benefits forgone when the service provision decision
\par
made upon the basis of existing information is incorrect. Expressing net benefits in monetary
\par
terms (:) gives an explicit monetary valuation of the costs of uncertainty that can be compared
\par
to the cost of collecting further information to determine the worth of research [9]. As the
\par
monetary valuation of health outcomes may not be known with certainty, the analysis can be
\par
presented to the decision-maker for a range of values of 8. When the valuation of health
\par
outcome (8) employed within the analysis is equivalent to that employed in service provision
\par
decisions, the valuation of information approach ensures consistency between research
\par
prioritisation and service provision [9].
\par
\textbf{3.3.2. Methods}
\par
The expected value of perfect information (EVPI) is equivalent to the expected costs of
\par
uncertainty surrounding the service provision decision made upon the basis of existing
\par
information, because perfect information eliminates all uncertainty and associated costs [8,9,32-
\par
34]. Hence, establishing the expected costs of uncertainty surrounding a decision provides a
\par
measure of the maximum possible payoff from research. When 8 represents a societal
\par
willingness to pay for health outcome, the EVPI represents the amount society is willing-to-pay
\par
to eliminate uncertainty associated with the decision, providing an explicit upper limit on the VOI
\par
obtained from further data acquisition. When compared with the cost of research, the EVPI
\par
provides an initial hurdle for determining whether further research is potentially cost-effective [9].CHE Discussion Paper 179 21
\par
Several authors [8,9,32-34] detail a parametric approach to establishing the expected value of
\par
perfect information. Within this paper a non-parametric approach to determining EVPI
\par
employing Monte Carlo simulation is employed [10,35-37]:
\par
EVPIepisode = E[NBt**] - E[NBt*] (equation 2a)
\par
= improvement in expected net benefit associated with
\par
perfect information
\par
where: NBt = net benefit associated with technology t
\par
t* t** = the technology chosen by the decision-maker given no additional
\par
information (\emph{a priori} act)
\par
= the technology chosen by the decision-maker with perfect
\par
information (posterior act)
\par
Each iteration in the simulation represents a realisation of current uncertainty and a position of
\par
perfect information, for which the posterior act can be determined. The improvement in net
\par
benefits associated with this realisation can then be calculated. Taking the expectation of the
\par
improvement values over all iterations gives the expected value of perfect information for an
\par
individual episode of UTI [36] (see Appendix 3 for a simple example of the calculation of
\par
EVPI)[37].
\par
The overall value of perfect information for a population is then determined by applying this
\par
opportunity loss to the overall number of episodes that will be affected by the information
\par
[3,8,9].22
\par
Improving the efficiency and relevance of health technology assessment
\par
EVPI population = EVPI episode* {$\Sigma$} P
\par
p=1 [ Ip / (1+r)p ] (equation 2b)
\par
where: I = incidence in period
\par
p = period
\par
P = total number of periods for which information from research would
\par
inform the decision
\par
r = discount rate
\par
In addition to determining the EVPI for the entire decision, the techniques can be applied to
\par
particular elements of the decision to direct and focus research in the same way as suggested
\par
for the methods employing basic uncertainty measures [13,14]. Calculating the EVPI for
\par
particular elements of the model involves estimating the EVPI for the full model and the EVPI for
\par
the remaining elements of the model, excluding those of interest (calculated as below). The
\par
EVPI for the elements of interest is then isolated as the difference between these two measures
\par
of EVPI.13
\par
To determine the EVPI for a particular strategy involves calculating the EVPI for the model when
\par
this strategy is excluded as an alternative. This in turn involves recalculating the improvement
\par
values associated with perfect information such that the strategy of interest is excluded as a
\par
potential posterior act (equation 2c):14
\par
EVPIepisode\textbackslash strategy 1 = E [NBt**\textbackslash t1 - NBt*\textbackslash t1] (equation 2c)
\par
where: NBt**\textbackslash t1 = net benefit associated with technology t, excluding strategy 1
\par
13 The EVPI for elements of interest is estimated in this manner rather than by excluding those elements which are
\par
not of interest because this would restrict interactions between those elements.
\par
14 It is obvious that an EVPI can not be calculated for a strategy over the range of values of 8 for which it is the \emph{a priori}
\par
choice.CHE Discussion Paper 179 23
\par
To determine the EVPI for a particular parameter or group of parameters the EVPI excluding
\par
this (these) parameter(s) is estimated. This is achieved by repeating the Monte Carlo simulation
\par
assuming no uncertainty surrounding the parameter(s) of interest,15 a procedure equivalent to
\par
conditional probabilistic sensitivity analysis [26,38].
