\textbf{4. DISCUSSION}
\par
\textbf{4.1 Unifying decisions about service provision and about research}
\par
It is clear that resources expended for providing health services cannot be used for research to
\par
generate additional information, and \emph{vice versa}. It is essential, therefore, that a framework is
\par
developed and adopted in which the economic appraisal of service provision is coupled with
\par
assessment of the cost-effectiveness of future research. This paper argues that iterative
\par
decision modelling provides such a framework. The value of this framework has been illustrated
\par
using a contemporary decision problem -- the management of non-pregnant women presenting
\par
to general practice with the symptoms of uncomplicated UTI.
\par
As regards the optimal treatment \emph{based on current information, }the results of the stochastic
\par
model identify the `empiric' strategy as the optimal decision for valuation of health benefits (8)
\par
below \pounds{} 300,000 per QALY, and the empiric plus laboratory strategy for values of 8 above \pounds CHE Discussion Paper 179 27
\par
300,000 per QALY.17
