The secondary decision problem is whether research to provide further information to inform the
\par
service provision decision in the future is worthwhile. The stochastic model measures the
\par
uncertainty associated with choosing the `empiric' strategy as varying between 1\% - 66\% as the
\par
valuation of health benefit increases to \pounds{} 300,000. The uncertainty associated with the empiric
\par
plus laboratory strategy falls from 65\% as the value of health benefit increases from \pounds{} 300,000
\par
(Figure 5). The VOI analysis generates explicit valuations that can be compared to the cost of
\par
further investigation to determine whether additional research is potentially worthwhile. Figure 9
\par
illustrates the relationship between the level of uncertainty (as represented by the cost-
\par
effectiveness frontier) and the expected value of perfect information.
\par
17 The deterministic model suggests that the empiric strategy is optimal up to a 8 value of \pounds 271,256. The difference is
\par
due to some of the parameters having non-symmetric probability distributions so that the mean in the stochastic
\par
model is not equal to the base case value within the deterministic model.28
\par
Improving the efficiency and relevance of health technology assessment
\par
The decision over which strategies/technologies to include within proposals for further research
\par
can be guided by the expected value of perfect information about individual strategies. The
\par
EVPI for each strategy is linked with the probability that the strategy is optimal and therefore is
\par
related to the cost-effectiveness acceptability curve (Figure 4). The fact that the dipstick
\par
strategy is associated with the second highest probability of being optimal for a considerable
\par
range of values of 8 indicates that there will be a large EVPI associated with this strategy. This
\par
illustrates that whilst the dipstick strategy is never optimal within the analysis it may be worth
\par
collecting further information about the strategy. Therefore it does not necessarily follow that
\par
there will be no value associated with acquiring more information about a dominated strategy.
\par
Indeed this example demonstrates that the concept of dominance in a deterministic analysis can
\par
not be used to exclude an alternative from further research. In fact, it is possible that the value
\par
of information surrounding a non dominated but non optimal strategy will be lower than a
\par
dominated alternative. In these circumstances using the concept of dominance to identify
\par
`relevant' alternatives for further evaluation will be very misleading [39].
\par
Research can be focused upon the particular areas of the decision for which a reduction in
\par
uncertainty via further information is of most value by calculating the expected value of perfect
\par
information for individual parameters and groups of parameters. The EVPI analysis for particular
\par
parameters suggests that further information concerning utilities and side effects provide most
\par
value, whilst information about prevalence of UTI given symptoms, probability of natural
\par
resolution of symptoms and unit costs provide the least value.
\par
It is of interest that the value of information associated with the accuracy of the laboratory test
\par
is higher than that for the unit costs. This result contradicts the one-way sensitivity analysis that
\par
found no impact for these parameters, due mainly to the domination of the strategies involvingCHE Discussion Paper 179 29
\par
laboratory tests. Hence a standard approach to handling uncertainty would have missed the
\par
importance of these parameters. This discrepancy within the results is due to the fact that one-
\par
way sensitivity analysis investigates the impact of parameter uncertainty on strategies
\par
individually, whilst the VOI analysis investigates the cost of uncertainty within the model. The
\par
laboratory test is included within four of the seven patient management strategies and hence
\par
uncertainty surrounding the accuracy of this test will have a large associated cost.
\par
The value of perfect information provides a necessary, but not sufficient, condition for the worth
\par
of further information. To determine the value of specific research, it is necessary to value the
\par
reduction in uncertainty that is actually achievable from the research, in terms of the reduction in
\par
the expected costs of uncertainty. When measured in monetary terms this gives society's
\par
willingness-to-pay for the specific research proposal, which is considered worthwhile where the
\par
valuation exceeds the cost. The techniques can also be employed to design technically efficient
\par
research proposals in terms of optimal sample size and allocation [3,8] thus improving the
\par
efficiency in HTA.
\par
\textbf{4.2 Using iterative decision analysis in research commissioning}
\par
Where early stage modelling is not undertaken, implicit judgements must be made about which
\par
parameters are important for the purposes of HTA, and the extent of evidence required. As a
\par
result, proposals for further data acquisition may either lack focus, leading to an unnecessarily
\par
large information requirement, or fail to provide information about key parameters for estimation.
\par
This can result in inefficient and potentially uninformative programmes of data acquisition.
\par
As an illustration, the results of the UTI model presented here can be used to assess whether
\par
the call for primary research in the area of UTI diagnosis from the NHS HTA programme was
\par
justified. It is not clear whether a formal systematic review was undertaken prior to the call for30
\par
Improving the efficiency and relevance of health technology assessment
\par
primary research - apparently none was formally commissioned. The early stage model,
\par
presented here, used available clinical evidence and clinical opinion to estimate parameters. A
\par
systematic review of published literature may reduce the uncertainty in the model in general,
\par
and of specific parameters, markedly. If such a review were commissioned, this model would
\par
provide an indication of the parameters on which it should focus, with the parameters relating to
\par
utilities and side effects most highly valued. Indeed, the model could provide a valuable insight
\par
into the most efficient search strategy for each parameter. An economic rationale can only be
\par
established for devoting additional resources to searching less accessible literature (e.g. grey
\par
and foreign language literature) with reference to the VOI associated with a given parameter
\par
and the probability that a study is going to be identified which significantly alters parameter
\par
estimates based on prior information.
\par
If we assume that a systematic review had been undertaken and no additional information
\par
identified, further primary research would seem justified on the basis of the model's results.
\par
Although the NHS HTA call does not presume that a randomised trial would be the preferred
\par
design, this may be the preferred form of data acquisition for some parameters, in particular the
\par
effectiveness of antibiotics. The EVPI about antibiotics is \pounds{} 58,000, given a 8 value of \pounds 10,000
\par
per QALY, and since a trial is likely to cost in excess of this it is unlikely that it will be
\par
worthwhile. Although we have not presented data on the optimal sample size of these trials, the
\par
model and VOI analysis could have been employed for that purpose [8 ,9 ,40], providing crucial
\par
information to potential trialists.
\par
However, the parameters for which the value of additional information is greatest (utilities and
\par
side effects) would not require measurement in expensive trials, given that the problem of
\par
selection bias is not expected to be significant in the estimation of these parameters. SplitCHE Discussion Paper 179 31
\par
sample designs would be appropriate to provide further data on dipstick and laboratory test
\par
accuracy and non-experimental designs would be appropriate for the probability of resolution of
\par
UTI without intervention. Given a limited budget for the NHS HTA programme, early stage
\par
modelling would have indicated that a trial was not the most urgent research design in this area
\par
and that further information on the most important parameters may have been generated more
\par
efficiently using non-experimental designs.
\par
\textbf{5. Conclusions}
\par
Information acquisition is not costless, and the allocation of funds to the enhancement of the
\par
decision makers' information set, in a budget-constrained health service, reduces the `pot' of
\par
resources available for health service provision. Hence, it is necessary to ensure that the
\par
process of HTA is subject to the same evaluation of efficiency as is service provision. This
\par
paper argues that employing decision analytic models, early and on an iterative basis in HTA, to
\par
evaluate the efficiency of health care technologies, will assist in the management of health
\par
services R\&D and help to ensure that both service provision and R\&D provide good value for
\par
money. The approach has been illustrated using an application to the decision problem of the
\par
management of UTI, which shows that these methods are practical and generate valuable
\par
information that is unlikely otherwise to be available.32
