\textbf{Appendix 1: Simplifying assumptions used within the UTI model}
\par
{$\bullet$} A proportion of the women who present with symptoms of UTI in general practice will
\par
have other disorders. A lack of quantitative information of other possible causes of
\par
symptoms in this group 1 led us to treat all non-UTI cases identically in the model.
\par
{$\bullet$} We make the assumption that those with non-UTI will not benefit from any of the
\par
strategies considered, and the only possible health outcome for these patients is that
\par
symptoms will persist, as illustrated in Figure 1. However, as these patients are not
\par
immediately identifiable the resources used in the management of their symptoms are
\par
included within the analysis of each strategy.
\par
{$\bullet$} Uncomplicated UTI tends to be a self-limiting condition, with 50\% of cases resolving
\par
naturally after 3 days [20] and the remainder get better after a week on average (base
\par
case).
\par
{$\bullet$} Where no treatment is given to patients with UTI, either as a deliberate strategy or as the
\par
result of an incorrect test result, symptoms are assumed to either disappear after 3 days
\par
or to persist for 7 days (sub-tree 1).
\par
{$\bullet$} Where UTI is the cause of symptoms, antibiotics may resolve symptoms after 2 days from
\par
the start of the course [20], otherwise the patient experiences the same outcomes as
\par
apply where antibiotics are not used.
\par
{$\bullet$} It is assumed that patients given antibiotics fully comply with the course of treatment.
\par
{$\bullet$} When used, test results dictate the subsequent management of the patient. Antibiotics
\par
(and possibly a confirmatory laboratory culture) follow a positive result and no further
\par
treatment follows a negative one. When laboratory tests are undertaken an initial
\par
positive/negative result can confirm the presence/absence of UTI and further analysis of
\par
positive results provides details of bacterial sensitivities that can direct prescribing. Where
\par
available, this information is employed to manage patients whose symptoms persist (sub-
\par
tree 3).
\par
{$\bullet$} 10\% of those receiving antibiotics are expected to experience side effects as a result [22],
\par
which prolong the period of symptoms by an extra 2 days [23].
\par
{$\bullet$} We assume that there is no worsening of symptoms or progression to pyelonephritis due
\par
to withholding or delaying antibiotic treatment within this patient population.
\par
{$\bullet$} Patients are assumed to return to the GP where symptoms persist, and the resources
\par
associated with these visits are included within the model. However, as the model deals
\par
with the primary management of uncomplicated UTI in women any subsequent
