decision is to undertake Treatment A.
\par
However, the distributions of net benefits are skewed. This results in a situation where the
\par
error probability associated with the choice of Treatment A is 66\%.
\par
This simple example illustrates that when there are skewed distributions of net benefit the
\par
treatment/strategy/technology with the maximum expected net benefit will not necessarily
\par
have the minimum error probability (maximum probability of being optimal). When using
\par
cost-effectiveness acceptability curves this translates to a position where the uppermost
\par
curve does not necessarily relate to the optimal strategy.CHE Discussion Paper 179 39
\par
\textbf{Appendix 3: Calculating the EVPI}
\par
The following table gives 3 iterations from a Monte Carlo simulation involving 2 treatments (A
\par
and B).
\par
\textbf{Net benefits in monetary terms}
\par
Treatment A Treatment B Perfect Information
\par
Iteration 1 15 9 15
\par
Iteration 2 11 12 12
\par
Iteration 3 13 15 15
\par
\textbf{Expected NB 13 12 14}
\par
Given current information the\emph{ a priori} decision is to choose Treatment A which generates an
\par
expected net benefit of 13.
\par
However, given perfect information at each iteration Treatment A would only be chosen for
\par
the first iteration, with Treatment B chosen in each of the other two iterations. Hence,
\par
perfect information generates an expected net benefit of 14.
\par
The difference between the expected net benefit given perfect information and that given
\par
current information represents the expected improvement associated with the perfect
\par
information. Hence it represents the value in decision payoff terms (net benefit) of the perfect
\par
information.
\par
EVPI =\textbf{ }E(NB given perfect information) - E(NB given current information)
\par
= 14 - 13
\par
