@article{pohIntelligentDecisionSupport2000,
  title = {An Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis},
  author = {Poh, Kim-Leng},
  year = 2000,
  journal = {Knowledge and Information Systems},
  volume = {2},
  pages = {340--358},
  publisher = {Springer},
  doi = {10.1007/PL00011646},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Knowledge and Information Systems (2000) 2: 340--358
\par
c ⃝ 2000 Springer-Verlag London Ltd.
\par
An Intelligent Decision Support System for
\par
Investment Analysis
\par
K. L. Poh
\par
Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, National University of Singapore,
\par
Singapore
\par
Abstract. We present an intelligent decision support system that combines decision analysis
\par
and traditional investment evaluation and analysis. The system brings to the ordinary
\par
user expertise in both decision analysis and investment evaluation techniques, as well as
\par
domain knowledge about the market. The system supports the entire decision analysis
\par
cycle and provides facilities and tools for decision modeling, probability assessment, model
\par
evaluation, and sensitivity analysis. An example based on the Shanghai Stock Market is
\par
presented. We demonstrate how an investment decision model for the stock market is
\par
constructed by the system and how the optimal decision is obtained. Sensitivity and value
\par
of information analysis were also carried out by the system.
\par
Keywords: Decision analysis; Intelligent decision system; Investment analysis; Know-
\par
ledge-based decision support system
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Investment decisions in the real world are complex processes which are often
\par
complicated by the vast amount of information as well as the high level of risk
\par
involved. Hence, in developing any automated investment decision support system
\par
or tool, the representation and management of this vast amount of information
\par
must be carefully and effectively addressed so that sound and timely decisions
\par
may be made by the investors. This paper presents an intelligent decision support
\par
system for investment decision making in which normative decision theory and
\par
decision analysis (Howard, 1996a; Raiffa, 1968; Savage, 1972; Clemen, 1996)
\par
are combined with traditional investment analysis and evaluation techniques
\par
(Reilly and Brown, 1997) to provide an integrated intelligent decision support
\par
Received 12 April 1999
\par
Revised 8 February 2000
\par
Accepted 2 March 2000An Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 341
\par
environment. The system brings to the user expertise in decision and investment
\par
analyses as well as domain knowledge of specific financial markets.
\par
The management of investments in the global financial market has recently
\par
experienced a lot of changes with advances in information and communication
\par
technology. In the global stock markets, almost all stock exchanges in the world
\par
have become either fully or partially computerized. This resulted in a number
\par
of significant changes in the market. First, with electronic trading, transaction
\par
volumes have increased tremendously and transaction times have significantly
\par
shortened. For example, in 1992, the world's oldest and largest trading system,
\par
INSTINET, reported that its volume had doubled within a relatively short period,
\par
while the Barra's Portfolio System for Institutional Trading (POSIT) reported a
\par
50\% increase (Winninghoff, 1993). Second, advances in information and commu-
\par
nication technology have provided investors with access to the global markets,
\par
and an increasing number of stocks have secondary listings on foreign stock ex-
\par
changes. Finally, with the vast amount of information generated from the financial
\par
markets, companies such as Reuters and Standard \& Poor, which specialize in
\par
the sales of market information, have emerged.
\par
Investment managers operating in the dynamic market must have the ability
\par
to extract relevant information in the shortest possible time and identify key
\par
variables which have significant impact on the return to investment in order
\par
to make timely decisions. Researchers in portfolio management have developed
\par
models which attempt to value assets and estimate the expected return of secu-
\par
rities or portfolios. Two well-known models are the Capital Asset Pricing Model
\par
(CAPM) (Sharpe, 1964) and the Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT) (Ross, 1976).
\par
While these two models are widely accepted, their applications require the user
\par
to be formally and adequately trained in investment theory and financial man-
\par
agement.
\par
Our system combines decision analysis with traditional investment evalua-
\par
tion approaches. The system has an architecture which can be considered as a
\par
form of Klein and Methlie's knowledge-based decision support system (KB-DSS)
\par
(Klein and Methlie, 1990, 1995) as well as Holzman's intelligent decision system
\par
(IDS) (Holtzman, 1989). As a KB-DSS, the system brings to the user expertise in
\par
both decision and investment analyses as well as domain knowledge of specific
\par
financial markets. In particular, the knowledge required to perform the decision
\par
analysis cycle (Matheson and Howard, 1968) as practiced by professional decision
\par
analysts is supported by the system together with investment analysis methodolo-
\par
gies. As an IDS, the system dynamically builds decision models using influence
\par
diagrams according to the user's inputs, and does not assume a `fixed' or `static'
\par
decision model structure for all situations. However, unlike Holtzman's original
\par
IDS, our system does not employ a rule-based inference engine for decision
\par
model construction. Instead, our system employs a model construction procedure
\par
that will dynamically build a comprehensive influence diagram representing the
\par
situation-specific decision problem which includes factors that are considered in
\par
well-established financial analysis procedure.
\par
This paper is organized as follows: In Section 2, we provide a review of related
\par
work on investment analysis, portfolio management, and decision analysis. In
\par
Section 3, the system architecture and the various subsystems and modules are
\par
described. In Section 4, we show how the system may be applied to solve an
\par
investment decision problem related to the Shanghai Stock Market. Finally, in
\par
Section 5 we conclude with a discussion on the system's limitations and suggest
\par
possible extensions to the system.342 K. L. Poh
\par
2. Investment Analysis, Portfolio Management, and Decision
\par
Analysis
\par
Investment may be defined as the current commitment of financial resources or
\par
funds for a period of time in order to derive future payments that will compensate
\par
the investor for the time the funds were committed, the expected rate of inflation,
\par
and the uncertainty of the future payments (Reilly and Brown, 1997). Most
\par
investments have expected cash flows and a stated market price. In evaluating the
\par
value of an investment, an investor considers the investment's expected cash flows
\par
and the required rate of return. The estimated values of the securities are then
\par
compared to their market prices before a decision is made on which securities
\par
to buy. Reilly and Brown (1997) proposed a three-step evaluation process for
\par
investment analysis as follows:
\par
1. Analysis of alternative economies and security markets
\par
2. Analysis of alternative industries
\par
3. Analysis of individual companies and stocks
\par
The first step is concerned with the analysis of security markets which will
\par
reflect the state of the economy. The value of an investment is determined by
\par
its expected cash flows and its required rate of return. Both of these factors are
\par
strongly influenced by the state of the economy. For example, the earnings of a
\par
firm operating in a strong and fast expanding economy is expected to increase,
\par
and the investor's rate of return on the company stock is expected to be high.
\par
On the other hand, if the economy is in recession, the earnings of the same firm
\par
would not be expected to increase in the short run and its stock price would be
\par
either stable or declining. Therefore, when assessing the value of a security, it is
\par
necessary to analyze the aggregated economy together with the security markets.
\par
The second step is concerned with industry analysis, which is important
\par
for a number of reasons. First, returns may vary widely over any time period.
\par
Second, the rates of return for individual industries may also vary over time.
\par
Hence investors cannot simply extrapolate past industry performances into the
\par
future. Thirdly, risk levels for different industries may vary widely during any time
\par
period. Industry analysis can therefore help investors isolate profitable investment
\par
opportunities from those that are non-profitable.
\par
The third and final step is concerned with company analysis. This step helps
\par
the investors find opportunity stocks whose values are under-priced. Company
\par
analysis is separated from stock analysis because the stock of a good company
\par
is not necessarily a good investment. For example, the stock of a good firm with
\par
superior management measured by its current sales and earnings growth may be
\par
over-priced such that the actual value of the stock is below its market value. On
\par
the other hand, the stock of a company with less success based on its current sales
\par
and earnings growth may have a stock market value that is below its intrinsic
\par
value. In this case, although this company is not as good as the first, its stock
\par
could be a good addition to the investor's portfolio. Hence, it is necessary to
\par
distinguish between growth companies and growth stocks. A growth company
\par
is one with an able management that is capable of identifying opportunities
\par
to make investments that yield rates of return greater than the firm's required
\par
rate of return. A growth stock, on the other hand, is one with a rate of return
\par
that is higher than other stocks in the market with similar risk characteristics
\par
(Ezra, 1963).An Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 343
\par
Portfolio management deals with the selection of portfolios that maximize
\par
expected returns at some acceptable levels of risk (Fabozzi, 1995). Markowitz
\par
(1952, 1959) laid the foundation for modern portfolio analysis and provided for
\par
the first time a systematic framework for selecting securities and portfolios on the
\par
basis of their expected returns and risks. He also pioneered the measurement of
\par
security and portfolio risk in terms of the well-known statistical measure known as
\par
the variance. The variance of a random variable is a measure of the dispersion of
\par
the possible outcomes around its expected value. Markowitz defined the variance
\par
of a portfolio as the sum of the weighted variances of the individual assets
\par
plus the sum of the weighted covariances of the assets. Based on the expected
\par
values and variances of possible portfolios, optimization techniques can be used
\par
to determine the Markowitz efficient portfolio. A Markowitz efficient portfolio is
\par
one which has the highest expected return among all feasible portfolios with the
\par
same level of risk. The Markowitz efficient frontier is then the collection of all
\par
Markowitz efficient portfolios at different levels of risk. The investor's utility is
\par
then maximized when the indifferent utility curve is tangential to the Markowitz
\par
efficient frontier.
\par
Based on Markowitz's portfolio theory, the Capital Asset Pricing Model
\par
(CAPM), which attempts to estimate the expected rate of return to securities or
\par
portfolios, was developed (Sharpe, 1964, 1985; Litner, 1965). CAPM assumes that
\par
the expected return to any security or portfolio is determined by its relationship
\par
with a special portfolio known as the market portfolio. This is based on the
\par
observation that the returns to most securities or portfolios tend to move together
\par
with the returns to the market portfolio. The market portfolio consists of all assets
\par
available to investors, and each asset must be held in proportion to its market
\par
value relative to the total market value of all assets.
\par
The CAPM model has been severely criticized for its dependence on the
\par
existence of a market portfolio which cannot be observed in practice. This is
\par
because such a portfolio contains all the assets in the economy, and an asset
\par
or a portfolio's expected return is dependent on the sensitivity of the asset or
\par
portfolio's return to the excess returns on more than one factor. In 1976, another
\par
model, called Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT), was developed (Ross, 1976). The
\par
APT model seems to be better in explaining the apparent puzzle as to why the
\par
estimated returns to securities issued by small firms tend to be higher than the
\par
estimated expected returns to securities issued by large firms (Sivalingam, 1990).
\par
Although the APT suffered from difficulties in identifying the required factors, it
\par
required fewer assumptions and considered multiple factors to explain the risk of
\par
an investment, in contrast to the single-factor CAPM.
\par
Decision analysis (Howard, 1996a; Raiffa, 1968; Clemen, 1996) is a term used
\par
to describe a body of knowledge and professional practice for the logical illumi-
\par
nation of decision problems. Decision analysis has a history of more than 30 years
\par
and was developed from statistical decision theory (Savage, 1972), operations re-
\par
search and systems analysis. Decision analysis seeks to apply logical, mathemati-
\par
cal, and scientific procedures to complex decision problems through mathematical
\par
modeling and structuring of the decision situation, computational implementation
\par
of the model and, finally, quantitative evaluation and comparison of the alterna-
\par
tive courses of action (Matheson, 1968). Much of the power of decision analysis
\par
lies in its ability to integrate effectively many factors that affect a decision, and to
\par
clearly and concisely represent decision problems which are necessary for helping
\par
decision-makers gain insight into the problems (Clemen, 1996). Decision analysis
\par
has been applied to a wide range of problems including plant expansion planning344 K. L. Poh
\par
(Braunstein, 1983), new product development (Matheson, 1970), space program
\par
planning (Matheson, 1970), investment in water supply systems (Rabinowitz et
\par
al, 1988), selection of residence (Hoepfner and Mata, 1993), energy investment
\par
(Huang et al, 1995, 1997), facility investment (Spetzler and Zamora, 1983), etc.
\par
In general, decision analyses are expensive and time consuming to perform.
\par
Usually, a decision analysis needs one or more decision-makers, one or more
\par
domain experts, and one or more decision analysts (Heckerman, 1991). Much of
\par
the cost of building a decision model is in capturing the expert knowledge into
\par
the model. In recent years, researchers have attempted to reduce the high cost of
\par
decision analyses by providing intensive domain knowledge in computer-based
\par
decision systems based on normative decision theory. Examples of such systems
\par
are the normative expert systems (Heckerman, 1991) and the intelligent decision
\par
systems (Holtzman, 1989). The latter have been identified by Klein and Methlie
\par
(1990, 1995) as special forms of knowledge-based decision support systems.
\par
Normative and intelligent decision systems differ from other traditional deci-
\par
sion support systems in that they combine the knowledge-based paradigm from
\par
artificial intelligence for domain knowledge representation, and normative deci-
\par
sion theory and analysis for inference. It should also be noted that these systems
\par
have been realizable only recently due to the development of an efficient decision
\par
modeling and inference language, namely the influence diagram and coupled with
\par
efficient probabilistic reasoning techniques for building automated decision aids
\par
and computer-based systems (Miller et al, 1978; Owen, 1978). Influence diagrams
\par
(Howard and Matheson, 1984) are directed acyclic graphs (DAG) for expressing
\par
and representing decision models. The nodes in an influence diagram represent
\par
the variables or events that are relevant to the decision problem, while the arcs
\par
represent the relations between the variables. Influence diagrams are compact
\par
and easy to manipulate by computers, unlike the traditional decision trees which
\par
grow exponentially in size for large problems. Furthermore, influence diagrams
\par
are capable of explicitly representing probabilistic dependencies, in contrast to
\par
the decision trees, which cannot do so.
\par
In a normative expert system, the domain knowledge and decision model are
\par
typically pre-engineered and represented as an influence diagram. Inference is then
\par
performed on the influence diagram and the structure or topology of the diagram
\par
does not change very much. An intelligent decision system, on the other hand,
\par
does not have a decision model pre-built. Instead, general domain knowledge is
\par
captured in a knowledge base and a customized situation-specific decision model
\par
in the form of an influence diagram is built during run time. Recent research has
\par
focused on the representation of domain knowledge and techniques for decision
\par
model construction (Breese et al, 1991).
\par
In our system, we combine decision analysis with traditional investment
\par
analysis and portfolio management. Traditional investment evaluation techniques
\par
do not explicitly consider the risk attitudes of investors. Some investors may
\par
be risk averse and their degrees of risk aversion can be measured using risk
\par
tolerances. Traditional investment analysis also does not consider probabilistic
\par
dependencies among key variables.
\par
Our system attempts to overcome the limitations of traditional investment
\par
analysis by modeling the investment decision-making problem at three levels
\par
corresponding to Reilly's three-step evaluation approach described earlier. The
\par
three levels of analysis are economy and security analysis, industry analysis,
\par
and company analysis. Each level of analysis progressively tries to capture the
\par
variables which are relevant to the problem, and at the same time enablesAn Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 345
\par
Fig. 1. The system architecture.
\par
the probabilistic dependency relations among all the variables to be captured
\par
in the form of an influence diagram. Conditional probability distributions for
\par
all uncertain variables are assessed and the risk preferences and attitude of
\par
the decision-maker are captured accurately in a utility function. Based on the
\par
constructed model, the system can determine the optimal decision policy as well
\par
as the optimal expected return. In addition, sensitivity analysis can be performed.
\par
3. System Architecture and Implementation
\par
Figure 1 shows the architecture of the system, which comprises four major
\par
subsystems, namely the model generation subsystem, the deterministic sensitivity
\par
subsystem, the probabilistic analysis subsystem, and the informational analysis
\par
subsystem. Each of these four subsystems corresponds to a distinct phase of
\par
the decision analysis cycle (Howard, 1983). The system was developed with the
\par
Microsoft Visual C++ development studio using standard Windows-based dialog
\par
boxes and interfaces.
\par
It should be noted that many of the features provided by our system can
\par
also be found in industry-standard software such as DPL, DATA and Preci-
\par
sion Trees for professional decision modeling and analysis. These include model
\par
editing functions, sensitivity analysis tools, risk profiles generator, and value of
\par
information analyzer. However, these commercial software packages are generic
\par
decision analysis tools for professional decision analysts and do not provide any
\par
intelligent guidance for building situation-specified models, nor do they provide
\par
any specific domain knowledge for the building decision models.
\par
3.1. The Model Generation Subsystem
\par
The model generation subsystem is concerned with creating a decision model
\par
based on inputs from the decision-maker. Inputs to this subsystem include the346 K. L. Poh
\par
name of the stock market or the industry to be analyzed. Decision models in the
\par
system are represented as influence diagrams. The model generation subsystem is
\par
further made up of three sub-modules. They are the influence diagram generator,
\par
the influence diagram editor, and the value model assessment sub-modules.
\par
The influence diagram generator sub-module generates a preliminary influence
\par
diagram comprising three levels corresponding to stock market analysis, industry
\par
analysis, and company analysis. The three-level structure of the influence diagram
\par
allows the decision-maker to concentrate on developing the model on a level-
\par
by-level basis, and avoids being overloaded with a large amount of information.
\par
A user may select any one of the three levels of analysis from a dialog box.
\par
Alternatively, the user can choose to create an entirely new model without the
\par
help of the domain knowledge base.
\par
The influence diagram editor sub-module allows the user to customize the
\par
decision model by directly editing the model via a set of influence diagram
\par
operations. These operations include addition and deletion of nodes and arcs
\par
from the influence diagram. If a decision node is added, the user is asked to
\par
specify the alternatives corresponding to the newly added node. If a chance node
\par
is added, the user is asked to specify the set of possible outcomes or states for
\par
the new node. In addition to providing influence diagram editing facilities, this
\par
sub-module also provides files management functions for saving and loading of
\par
models from external storage devices.
\par
Finally, the value model assessment sub-module determines the outcomes and
\par
consequences that the decision-maker cares about. Preferences and value trade-off
\par
are the core of any decision problem. The final decision recommended by the
\par
system ultimately depends on the values and preference trade-offs specified by the
\par
decision-maker. Typically, each alternative can produce many possible outcomes
\par
and these outcomes are subject to uncertainties. The fundamental aim of value
\par
assignment is to specify the preferences of the decision-maker for each outcome
\par
compared with the others. In our system, the value model is based on the profit
\par
return of investment. The value model assessment sub-model helps to identify
\par
the state variables that have direct impact on profit return. In our system, the
\par
decision-maker either assigns the profit values directly to each combination of
\par
the outcomes for the state variables, or he/she can specify a multi-attribute value
\par
function.
\par
3.2. Deterministic Sensitivity Subsystem
\par
The deterministic sensitivity subsystem is concerned with deterministic sensitivity
\par
analysis of the decision model. Its main aim is to reduce the decision model
\par
generated by the model generation subsystem to a manageable size by eliminating
\par
unimportant variables or variables that have insignificant impact on the outcomes
\par
of the problem. The deterministic sensitivity analyzer is the main module in this
\par
subsystem. For each uncertain variable, we let its value vary from its low to high
\par
value while keeping all the other uncertain variables at their expected values, and
\par
the change in profit return is computed. If the change in the profit return for
\par
an uncertain variable is large or significant when compared with those obtained
\par
from the other variables, then that uncertain variable has a significant impact
\par
on the problem and should be retained in the model. Otherwise, if the change
\par
in profit is small or insignificant, then the variable concerned will be fixed atAn Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 347
\par
Fig. 2. A reference lottery for assessing risk tolerance.
\par
its expected value in the model for all subsequence analyses. The end result is a
\par
smaller model with only the important variables.
\par
3.3. Probabilistic Analysis Subsystem
\par
The probabilistic analysis subsystem is concerned with the probabilistic phase
\par
of the decision analysis cycle. Here, all the required probability distributions
\par
are assessed, and the influence diagram is then solved for the optimal decision
\par
policy. This subsystem comprises five main sub-modules. They are the probability
\par
assessment, the risk attitude assessment, the influence diagram evaluation, the
\par
stochastic sensitivity analysis, and the risk profile generation sub-modules.
\par
The probability assessment sub-module encodes probability or conditional
\par
probability distributions for all the important chance nodes in the model. It also
\par
provides tools for assessment of probability distributions for discrete as well as
\par
continuous variables.
\par
The risk attitude assessment sub-module determines the decision-maker's at-
\par
titude towards risk and constructs a utility function for the decision model.
\par
Decision theory allows a decision-maker to make rational choices by taking into
\par
consideration both returns and attitude to risks. A person's attitude towards risk
\par
is characterized by the shape of his utility function over a range of possible value
\par
outcomes. A commonly used utility function has the following exponential form:
\par
u(x) = 1- e-x/R (1)
\par
where x represents the return and R is a constant known as the risk tolerance.
\par
Although this utility function is valid only under the condition of constant degree
\par
of absolute risk aversion, it is often a good approximation for practice purposes.
\par
Our system provides tools for assessing the decision-maker's risk tolerance.
\par
This is performed by posing the reference lottery as shown in Fig. 2 to the
\par
decision-maker. Here the decision-maker is asked for the dollar value of y for
\par
which he or she is undecided between accepting and not accepting a lottery in
\par
which he or she has a 50\% chance of winning y dollars or a 50\% chance of
\par
losing half of y dollars. It can be shown if the decision-maker utility function
\par
can be approximated by the exponential form, then the value of y provided
\par
by the decision-maker is approximately equal to the risk tolerance R of the
\par
decision-maker with an error of only 4\% (Howard, 1988).
\par
The influence diagram evaluation sub-module computes the optimal decision
\par
policy based on the principle of maximum expected utility. The system uses an
\par
extended version of the Shachter node reduction algorithm (Shachter, 1986) to
\par
solve the decision model. The extensions include preprocessing of the decision
\par
model to eliminate null information nodes in addition to the elimination of barren
\par
nodes as originally suggested by Shachter. A null information node is one which
\par
does not have any impact on the optimal decision policy. It may be eliminated
\par
prior to any node reduction operations to reduce the overall computation time.348 K. L. Poh
\par
The risk profile generation sub-module constructs a risk profile for each of the
\par
alternatives so that the risk-return characteristics of the investment alternatives
\par
may be compared. The risk profile of an alternative is a plot usually in the form
\par
of a cumulative or excess probability curve that shows the probability associated
\par
with all possible outcomes of that alternative. Stochastic dominance analysis may
\par
be carried out to eliminate alternatives that will never be selected under any valid
\par
utility function.
\par
Finally, the stochastic sensitivity analysis sub-module performs probabilistic
\par
sensitivity analysis by varying selected model parameters over ranges of possible
\par
values. Stochastic sensitivity analysis determines which uncertainty matters in the
\par
decision. In our system, any chance node can be selected by the user for stochastic
\par
sensitivity analysis. The probability of an outcome for that node can be varied
\par
over any range between 0 and 1, and the system will systematically analyze the
\par
optimal decision and the maximum expected utility over the specified range of
\par
values. The results are presented in a sensitivity graph often called a `rainbow
\par
diagram'.
\par
3.4. Informational Analysis Subsystem
\par
The informational analysis subsystem is concerned the final phase of the decision
\par
analysis cycle. The main sub-module here performs expected value of information
\par
analyses. When a decision-maker is faced with uncertainty, he or she often has
\par
the option of gathering information with the intention of reducing or eliminating
\par
the uncertainty so as to improve the decision made. The economic worth of
\par
completely eliminating the uncertainty in a chance variable is measured by its
\par
expected value of perfect information (Howard, 1966b, 1967). If a variable has
\par
an expected value of perfect information that exceeds the cost of acquiring it,
\par
then an information-gathering activity may be designed to reduce or eliminate the
\par
uncertainty surrounding that variable. The expected value of perfect information
\par
of a variable indicates the maximum amount of money one should spend on
\par
acquiring information concerning that variable.
\par
The system supports computation of expected values of perfect information
\par
as well as expected values of imperfect information. The modeling of the decision
\par
problem with perfect information can be represented through appropriate addition
\par
of an information arc from the selected chance node to the appropriate decision
\par
node. For the imperfect information case, an additional chance connected to
\par
the original node is added in the influence diagram to represent the imperfect
\par
information that is being observed. An appropriate information arc is also added
\par
and the computation is similar to that for the case of perfect information.
\par
4. An Application to the Shanghai Stock Market
\par
4.1. The Shanghai Stock Market
\par
The Shanghai Stock Market was founded on December 19, 1990. At its opening,
\par
it had only one trading hall, 46 seats, eight listed companies and 25 members. The
\par
total capitalization was only 1.3 billion yuan (US \$150 million) and there were
\par
only 30,000 investors. At the end of 1993, within three years of its establishment,
\par
the Shanghai Stock Market had grown to six trading halls, 2600 seats, 119 listedAn Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis Index
