\par
billion yuan (US \$28.7 billion) with 4.6 million investors, and the trading volume
\par
was as high as 500 billion yuan (US \$57.5 million) (Zhang, 1993).
\par
Our study here focuses on the so-called `A' shares, which can only be owned by
\par
Chinese nationals. The so-called `B' shares are meant for foreign ownership. Most
\par
stock traded as `A' shares in China are issued in three categories, namely state-
\par
owned stock, institutionally owned stocks, and freely traded stocks (Liao, 1994b).
\par
In terms of volume, state-owned stocks and institution-owned stocks are much
\par
more numerous than freely traded stocks (Liao, 1994a).
\par
The Shanghai Stock Market is still in its early stage of development compared
\par
with the mature stock markets in other parts of the world. Some market observers
\par
have remarked that the Shanghai Stock Market development trend is contradic-
\par
tory to general economic development trends (Guo, 1994a). At the same time,
\par
the Chinese Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC), being new at its job, is
\par
still learning and experimenting with ways of tackling problems that emerged
\par
from the stock market. For instance, the Bao Yan event in 1993 involving the
\par
acquisition of Shanghai-listed Yanzhong Co. Ltd by the Shenzhen-listed Baoan
\par
Co. Ltd caused the price of Yanzhong to rise rapidly from about 10 yuan to over
\par
40 yuan per share within a few days. The lack of proper directions and regulatory
\par
interventions by the CSRC in the early stage of the acquisition have contributed
\par
to the rapid rise in the share price of Yanzhong.
\par
The investment awareness of Chinese investors is lower than that of other
\par
developed countries. The financial capital already committed in investment of
\par
different sorts by the Chinese citizens amounts to only 1.4 trillion yuan (US
\par
\$161 billion). The amount of bank deposits and cash at hand are as high as 1350 K. L. Poh
