\par
The Chinese economic system is in a transition stage from a planned economy
\par
to a market economy. State-owned companies operate under a safety net provided
\par
by the government, which is a characteristic of the old socialist system. These
\par
companies tend to worry less about their longer-term financial health. Some
\par
of these companies park a substantial portion of their funds in equities and
\par
real estates including the building of infrastructure. The day-to-day operational
\par
capital is then raised from the capital market in terms of loans. When the banks'
\par
liquidities are driven up and loan facilities are tightened, these companies will
\par
find it hard to raise capital for their day-to-day operations (Guo, 1994a). As it is
\par
estimated that about up to 80\% of the stock market investors are such companies
\par
(Liao, 1994b), it is not difficult to understand why the index fell sharply when
\par
the Central Government tightened the bank loan policy in 1993, forcing these
\par
companies to sell their shares to raise operational capital or to repay their debts
\par
(Liao, 1994a, 1994c). Figures 3 and 4 show examples of index fluctuations for the
\par
Shanghai Stock Market for the periods from December 1993 to January 1994,
\par
and from March 1994 to April 1994, respectively.
\par
4.2. The Decision Model
\par
4.2.1. The Basic Model
\par
A general basic financial investment decision model for an investor in the Shang-
\par
hai Stock Market is generated by the system as shown in Fig. 5. The domainAn Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 351
\par
Fig. 5. General financial investment model.
\par
knowledge for the problem was gathered from the literature and publications such
\par
as those cited in this paper, and through consultations with financial analysts
\par
familiar with the market. We shall describe in detail the various nodes in the
\par
influence diagram of Fig. 5.
\par
{$\bullet$} Retail investment (Retail ): This variable reflects the uncertainty of the invest-
\par
ment commitments in terms of the trading volume made by individual investors
\par
in the stock market. On average, 20\% of the trading volume is attributed to
\par
retail investment (Liao, 1994b).
\par
{$\bullet$} Corporate investment (Corporate): This variable reflects the investments made
\par
by companies, factories and other state-owned enterprises. On average, 80\% of
\par
the trading volume is attributed to corporate investment (Liao, 1994b).
\par
{$\bullet$} Stock market policy (Policy): This variable is concerned with policies introduced
\par
by the CSRS. As there are few investment regulations in China, the CSRS will
\par
enforce certain stock market policies according to specific situations on an ad
\par
hoc basis and these policies usually cause fluctuations of the stock market.
\par
{$\bullet$} General economic indicators (Indicators): This variable captures the various
\par
microeconomic factors which affect both retail investment and corporate in-
\par
vestment, such as inflation, interest rate, and GNP growth rate.
\par
{$\bullet$} Stock market fluctuation (Fluct): This variable denotes the fluctuation of the
\par
stock market.
\par
{$\bullet$} Investment environment (Environ): This is a deterministic variable which is
\par
derived from the integration of the `stock market policies' and `general economic
\par
indicators' chance nodes.
\par
{$\bullet$} Investment decision (Decision): This decision variable represents the various352 K. L. Poh
\par
Fig. 6. Decomposition of general decision model.
\par
investment alternatives. In our models two alternatives are considered. These
\par
are `investment in the stock market' and `saving account with fixed interest'.
\par
{$\bullet$} Value (Value): This value node represents the profit return and is a function of
\par
the uncertain variables retail investment, corporate investment, and investment
\par
decision.
\par
4.2.2. Refining the Basic Model
\par
The basic model can be refined by the system by decomposing some of the chance
\par
nodes. For instance, Fig. 6 shows how the two chance nodes for general economic
\par
indicators (Indicators) and stock market policy (Policy) of the basic model may
\par
be extended by adding a number of direct predecessor nodes.
\par
In the extended model, the general economic indicators (Indicators) node is
\par
decomposed according to a set of economic variables based on the APT (Rolla
\par
and Ross, 1980). The added nodes, which are inflation (Inflation), risk premium
\par
(Premium), interest rates (I Rates), and industrial production (I Prod ), have been
\par
observed to have a significant impact on stock market returns. Also in the
\par
extended model, the stock market policy (Policy) node is extended to include new
\par
listings of stocks on the market (N Stock), issuing of treasury bills (T Bill ), and
\par
trading of state and institutionally owned stocks (IO Stock).An Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 353
\par
Fig. 7. The risk profile for the optimal decision.
\par
4.2.3. The Value Model
\par
The value model (profit return) is a function of retail investment (Retail ), corpo-
\par
rate investment (Corporate), and the investment decision (Decision). On average,
\par
80\% of the trading volume is due to corporate investment and only 20\% to retail
\par
investment.
\par
4.2.4. Probability Assessments
\par
Probability assessments for all chance nodes were performed through dialog boxes.
\par
For example, probabilities were assigned to the three possible outcomes of stock
\par
market policy (Policy), namely, favorable, neutral, and unfavorable. A favorable
\par
policy refers to an index-boosting policy where there will be new issues of stocks
\par
and treasury bills with properly regulated trading of state and institutionally
\par
owned stocks.
\par
Probability distributions for the chance nodes general economic indicators
\par
(Indicators), investment environment (Environ), retail investment (Retail ), and
\par
corporate investment (Corporate) were also assessed.
\par
4.2.5. Risk Attitude Assessment
\par
Using the risk attitude assessor, the following lottery was posed to the decision-
\par
maker:
\par
{$\bullet$} Win \$Y with probability of 0.5.
\par
{$\bullet$} Lose \$Y /2 with probability of 0.5.
\par
The value of Y for which the decision-maker was just indifferent between accept-
\par
ing and rejecting the lottery was found to be \$10,000. Hence, the risk tolerance
\par
of the decision-maker is approximately \$10,000.
\par
4.3. The Optimal Decision and Risk Profiles
\par
The decision model was solved by an extended version of Shachter's nodes
\par
reduction algorithm (Shachter, 1986). After the reduction is completed, only the
\par
value node will be left in the influence diagram. The maximum expected value was
\par
found to be \$10,448.2, and the optimal decision policy was found to be `investment354 K. L. Poh
\par
Fig. 8. Probabilistic sensitivity analysis on stock market policy.
\par
Fig. 9. Probabilistic sensitivity analysis on general economic indicators.
\par
in the stock market'. Figure 7 shows the risk profile for the optimal decision where
\par
the horizontal axis indicates the return in thousands of dollars while the vertical
\par
axis shows the excess probability, i.e., the probability of achieving at least so much
\par
of the value indicated by the horizontal axis.
\par
4.4. Sensitivity Analysis
\par
The decision-maker selected the chance nodes for stock market policy (Policy),
\par
general economic indicators (Indicators), and market fluctuation (Fluct) for prob-
\par
abilistic sensitivity analysis. Figures 8--10 show the graphical plots of probabilistic
\par
sensitivity analysis on stock market policy, general economic indicators, and mar-
\par
ket fluctuation, respectively when the probability of the outcome in each variable
\par
is varied between 0 and 1. From the sensitivity graphs, we observe that stockAn Intelligent Decision Support System for Investment Analysis 355
\par
Fig. 10. Probabilistic sensitivity analysis on market fluctuation.
\par
Fig. 11. Expected value of imperfect information analysis.
\par
market policy has the greatest impact on profit return while the general economic
\par
indicators have the least impact on profit return.
\par
4.5. Expected Value of Information Analysis
\par
Expected value of imperfect information analysis (Howard, 1996b, 1997) were
\par
performed for the chance nodes for stock market policy (Policy) and general
\par
economic indicators (Indicators). The influence diagram for the analysis is shown
\par
in Fig. 11. In Fig. 11, two new chance nodes are added to the influence dia-356 K. L. Poh
\par
gram, namely stock market policies forecast (P Forecast) and general economic
\par
indicators forecast (I Forecast), to indicate the imperfect information sources for
\par
the two variables, respectively. Conditional probabilities indicating the accuracy
\par
or reliability of the information are entered. The modified model was evaluated,
\par
yielding a maximum expected value of \$14,526.70. Hence the expected value of
\par
information is the difference between \$14,526.70 and \$10,448.20, or \$4078.50.
\par
In summary, based on the inputs from the decision-maker, the system has rec-
\par
ommended that the optimal policy is to invest in the Shanghai Stock Market with
\par
an expected profit return of \$10,448.2. It has also shown that stock market policy
\par
influences the value (profit return) greatly, while the general economic indicators
\par
have little impact on profit return. This is consistent with the observation that the
\par
stock market in Shanghai, and in general, China, is a largely policy-influenced
\par
market.
\par
5. Conclusion
\par
We have presented an intelligent decision support system that combined decision
\par
analysis and theory with traditional investment evaluation procedures to help
\par
decision-makers make better investment decisions. The system provides tools such
\par
as influence diagram generator, probability assessor, value function generator
\par
etc. to help decision-makers model the decision problem. It delivers domain
\par
expert knowledge within a decision theoretic framework to improve the quality
\par
of decisions. The intelligent decision support system developed here differs from
\par
other traditional investment decision and analysis systems in that it takes decision
\par
maker's risk attitude and preferences into consideration. Another major feature
\par
of the system is the use of influence diagram as a modeling and communication
\par
tool for the decision problem.
\par
A limitation of our system is that it cannot help dealers allocate investment
\par
funds among the picked stocks such as BARRA's world markets model, but it can
\par
provide supplementary supports for decision-makers. Although we have shown
\par
how the system may be applied to an investment decision problem concerning
\par
the Shanghai Stock Market, the system would still require extensive and rigorous
\par
testing to prove its worth, and also to assess its portability to other stock markets.
\par
A possible extension of our system is the inclusion of technical analysis (TA).
\par
In practice, technical analysis of sentiment and supply and demand through the
\par
analysis of actual trading data is often used by investors besides the relative eval-
\par
uation of the company. We believe that a system that combines both fundamental
\par
and technical analyses would be a really useful and powerful one.
