Executive summary
\par
\textbf{Background}
\par
Bayesian methods may be defined as the explicit
\par
quantitative use of external evidence in the design,
\par
monitoring, analysis, interpretation and reporting
\par
of a health technology assessment. In outline, the
\par
methods involve formal combination through the
\par
use of Bayes's theorem of:
\par
1. a prior distribution or belief about the value of
\par
a quantity of interest (for example, a treatment
\par
effect) based on evidence not derived from the
\par
study under analysis, with
\par
2. a summary of the information concerning the
\par
same quantity available from the data collected
\par
in the study (known as the likelihood), to yield
\par
3. an updated or posterior distribution of the
\par
quantity of interest.
\par
These methods thus directly address the question
\par
of how new evidence should change what we
\par
currently believe. They extend naturally into
\par
making predictions, synthesising evidence from
\par
multiple sources, and designing studies: in
\par
addition, if we are willing to quantify the value of
\par
different consequences as a `loss function',
\par
Bayesian methods extend into a full decision-
\par
theoretic approach to study design, monitoring
\par
and eventual policy decision-making. Nonetheless,
\par
Bayesian methods are a controversial topic in that
\par
they may involve the explicit use of subjective
\par
judgements in what is conventionally supposed to
\par
be a rigorous scientific exercise.
\par
4. criteria for assessing the reporting of a Bayesian
\par
analysis
\par
5. a comprehensive list of published `three-star'
\par
examples, in which a proper prior distribution
\par
has been used for the quantity of primary
\par
interest
\par
6. tutorial case studies of a variety of types
\par
7. recommendations on how Bayesian methods
\par
and approaches may be assimilated into health
\par
technology assessments in a variety of contexts
\par
and by a variety of participants in the research
\par
process.
\par
\textbf{Methods}
\par
The BIDS ISI database was searched using the
\par
terms `Bayes' or `Bayesian'. This yielded almost
\par
4000 papers published in the period 1990--98. All
\par
resultant abstracts were reviewed for relevance to
\par
health technology assessment; about 250 were so
\par
identified, and used as the basis for forward and
\par
backward searches. In addition EMBASE and
\par
MEDLINE databases were searched, along with
\par
websites of prominent authors, and available
\par
personal collections of references, finally yielding
\par
nearly 500 relevant references. A comprehensive
\par
review of all references describing use of `proper'
\par
Bayesian methods in health technology assessment
\par
(those which update an informative prior distribu-
\par
tion through the use of Bayes's theorem) has
\par
been attempted, and around 30 such papers are
\par
reported in structured form. There has been very
\par
limited use of proper Bayesian methods in practice,
\par
and relevant studies appear to be relatively easily
\par
identified.
\par
\textbf{Objectives}
\par
This report is intended to provide:
\par
1. a brief review of the essential ideas of Bayesian
\par
analysis
\par
2. a full structured review of applications of
\par
Bayesian methods to randomised controlled
\par
trials, observational studies, and the synthesis of
\par
evidence, in a form which should be reasonably
\par
straightforward to update
\par
3. a critical commentary on similarities and differ-
\par
ences between Bayesian and conventional
\par
approaches
\par
\textbf{Results}
\par
\textbf{Bayesian methods in the health}
\par
\textbf{technology assessment context}
\par
1. Different contexts may demand different
\par
statistical approaches. Prior opinions are
\par
most valuable when the assessment forms
\par
part of a series of similar studies. A decision-
\par
theoretic approach may be appropriate where
\par
the consequences of a study are reasonably
\par
predictable.
\par
\textbf{iii}\emph{Executive summary}
\par
\textbf{iv}
\par
2. The prior distribution is important and not
\par
unique, and so a range of options should be
\par
examined in a sensitivity analysis. Bayesian
\par
methods are best seen as a transformation from
\par
initial to final opinion, rather than providing a
\par
single `correct' inference.
\par
3. The use of a prior is based on judgement,
\par
and hence a degree of subjectivity cannot be
\par
avoided. However, subjective priors tend to
\par
show predictable biasses, and archetypal priors
\par
may be useful for identifying a reasonable
\par
range of prior opinion. For a prior to be taken
\par
seriously, its evidential basis must be explicitly
\par
given.
\par
4. The Bayesian approach provides a framework
\par
for considering the ethics of randomisation.
\par
5. Monitoring trials with sceptical and other
\par
priors may provide a unified approach to
\par
assessing whether the results of a trial should
\par
be convincing to a wide range of reasonable
\par
opinion, and could provide a formal tool for
\par
data-monitoring committees.
\par
6. In contrast to earlier phases of development, it
\par
is generally unrealistic to formulate a Phase III
\par
trial as a decision problem, except in circum-
\par
stances where future treatments can be accu-
\par
rately predicted.
\par
7. Observational data will generally require more
\par
complex analysis: the explicit modelling of
\par
potential biasses may be widely applicable but
\par
needs some evidence-base in order to be
\par
convincing.
\par
8. A unified Bayesian approach is applicable to
\par
a wide range of problems concerned with
\par
evidence synthesis, for example in pooling
\par
studies of differing designs in the assessment
\par
medical devices.
\par
9. Priors for the degree of `similarity' between
\par
alternative designs can be empirically informed
\par
by studies comparing the results of randomised
\par
controlled trials and observational data.
\par
10. Increased attention to pharmaco-economics
\par
should lead to further investigation of decision-
\par
theoretic models for research planning,
\par
although this will not be straightforward.
\par
11. Regulatory agencies are acknowledging
\par
Bayesian methods and have not ruled out their
\par
use, and the regulation of medical devices is
\par
leading the way in establishing the role of
\par
evidence synthesis.
\par
12. `Comprehensive decision modelling' is likely
\par
to become increasingly important in policy
\par
making.
\par
13. The BayesWatch criteria described in this
\par
report may provide a basis for structured
\par
reporting of Bayesian analysis.
\par
14. Summaries of fully fledged (`three-star')
\par
applications of Bayesian methods in health
\par
technology assessment contain few prospective
\par
analyses but provide useful guidance.
\par
15. Four case studies show:
\par
a. Bayesian analyses using a sceptical prior can
\par
be useful to the data-monitoring committee
\par
of a cancer clinical trial.
\par
b. Bayesian methods can be used to temper
\par
overoptimistic conclusions based on meta-
\par
analysis of small trials.
\par
c. Modern graphical software can easily handle
\par
complex assessments previously analysed
\par
using the `confidence profile' method.
\par
d. Bayesian methods provide a flexible tool
\par
for performance estimation and ranking
\par
of institutions.
\par
\textbf{Recommendations and implications for}
\par
\textbf{future research and development}
\par
Bayesian methods could be of great value within
\par
health technology assessment, but for a realistic
\par
appraisal of the methodology, it is necessary to
\par
distinguish the roles and requirements for five
\par
main participant groups in health technology
\par
assessment: methodological researchers, sponsors,
\par
investigators, reviewers and consumers. Two
\par
common themes for all participants can immedi-
\par
ately be identified. First, the need for an extended
\par
set of case studies showing practical aspects of the
\par
Bayesian approach, in particular for prediction
\par
and handling multiple substudies, in which mathe-
\par
matical details are minimised but details of imple-
\par
mentation are provided. Second, the development
\par
of standards for the performance and reporting of
\par
Bayesian analyses, possibly derived from the
\par
BayesWatch checklist.
\par
Some specific potential areas of research and devel-
\par
opment include:
\par
1. Design. Realistic development of payback
\par
models and consideration of `open' studies.
\par
2. Priors. Investigation of evidence-based prior
\par
distributions appropriate to the participant
\par
group, as well as reasonable default priors in
\par
non-standard situations.
\par
3. Modelling. Efficient use of all available evidence
\par
by appropriate joint modelling of historical
\par
controls, related studies, and so on.
\par
4. Reporting. Development of criteria along the
\par
lines of the BayesWatch checklist, so that future
\par
users can reproduce analyses.
\par
5. Decision-making. Increased integration with
\par
a health-economic and policy perspective,
\par
together with flexible tools for implementation.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
