\textbf{Chapter 1}
\par
Introduction
\par
\textbf{What are Bayesian methods?}
\par
Bayesian statistics began with a posthumous publica-
\par
tion in 1763 by Thomas Bayes,36 a non-conformist
\par
minister from Tunbridge Wells.237 His work was
\par
formalised as Bayes's theorem, which, when
\par
expressed mathematically, is a simple and uncontro-
\par
versial result in probability theory. However, certain
\par
specific uses of the theorem have been the subject of
\par
continued controversy for over a century,128,170 giving
\par
rise to a steady stream of polemical arguments in
\par
a number of disciplines. In recent years a more
\par
balanced and pragmatic perspective has developed.
\par
The basic idea of Bayesian analysis can be illus-
\par
trated by a simple example and, although we shall
\par
try to keep mathematical notation to a minimum in
\par
this review, it will be very helpful if we are allowed
\par
one Greek letter q (theta), to denote a currently
\par
unknown quantity of primary interest. Suppose our
\par
quantity q is the median life-years gained by using
\par
an innovative rather than a standard therapy on
\par
a defined group of patients. A clinical trial is
\par
carried out, following which conventional statistical
\par
analysis of the results would typically produce a
\par
P value, an estimate and a confidence interval as
\par
summaries of what this particular trial tells us
\par
about q. A Bayesian analysis supplements this by
\par
focusing on the question `How should this trial
\par
change our opinion about q?' This perspective
\par
forces the analyst to explicitly state
\par
{$\bullet$} a reasonable opinion concerning q excluding
\par
the evidence from the trial (known as the prior
\par
distribution)
\par
{$\bullet$} the support for different values of q based solely
\par
on data from the trial (known as the likelihood)
\par
and to combine these two sources to produce
\par
{$\bullet$} a final opinion about q (known as the posterior
\par
distribution).
\par
The final combination is done using Bayes's
\par
theorem.
\par
What, then, is a Bayesian approach to health tech-
\par
nology assessment? We have defined it 415 as ``the
\par
explicit quantitative use of external evidence in the
\par
design, monitoring, analysis, interpretation and
\par
reporting of a health technology assessment''.
\par
\textbf{Reasons for conducting a review}
\par
Much of the standard statistical methodology used
\par
in health technology assessment revolves around
\par
that for the classical randomised controlled trial:
\par
these include power calculations at the design
\par
stage, methods for controlling type I error within
\par
sequential monitoring, calculation of P values and
\par
confidence intervals at the final analysis, and meta-
\par
analytic techniques for pooling the results of
\par
multiple studies. Such methods have served the
\par
medical research community well.
\par
The increasing sophistication of health technology
\par
assessment studies is, however, highlighting the
\par
limitations of these traditional methods. For
\par
example, when carrying out a clinical trial, the
\par
many sources of evidence and judgement available
\par
before a trial may be inadequately summarised by a
\par
single `alternative hypothesis', monitoring may be
\par
complicated by simultaneous publication of related
\par
studies, and multiple subgroups may need to be
\par
analysed and reported. Evidence from multiple
\par
sources may need to be combined in order to
\par
inform a policy decision, such as embarking or
\par
continuing on a research programme, regulatory
\par
approval of a drug or device, or recommendation
\par
of a treatment at an individual or population level.
\par
Standard statistical methods are designed for single
\par
studies, and have difficulties dealing with this
\par
pervading complexity.
\par
A Bayesian perspective leads to an approach to
\par
clinical trials and observational studies that is
\par
claimed to be more flexible and ethical than tradi-
\par
tional methods,262 and to elegant ways of handling
\par
multiple substudies, for example when simulta-
\par
neously estimating the effects of a treatment on
\par
many subgroups.72 Proponents have also argued
\par
that a Bayesian approach enables one to provide
\par
conclusions in a suitable form for making deci-
\par
sions: whether for specific patients, for planning
\par
research, or for public policy.299
\par
The increasing interest in the Bayesian approach is
\par
reflected both in the medical and statistical
\par
\textbf{1}\emph{Introduction}
\par
literature and in the popular scientific press.318
\par
Pharmaceutical companies are beginning to
\par
express an interest, possibly helped by the recent
\par
international regulatory authority statistical guide-
\par
lines248 explicitly mentioning the possibility of a
\par
Bayesian analysis. However, many outstanding
\par
questions remain: in particular, to what extent will
\par
the scientific community, or the regulatory authori-
\par
ties, allow the explicit introduction of evidence that
\par
is not totally derived from observed data, or the
\par
formal pooling of data from studies of differing
\par
designs?
\par
