\textbf{Review methodology}
\par
\textbf{What do we mean by a `systematic'}
\par
\textbf{review?}
\par
In common with all such methodological reviews, it
\par
is essential to define what we mean by `systematic'.
\par
We have identified three levels of review:
\par
Comprehensive. This seeks to identify all relevant
\par
references, and has only been attempted for what
\par
we have termed `three-star' Bayesian health tech-
\par
nology assessment studies, which we define as those
\par
1. intending to confirm the value of a technology
\par
2. using an informative, carefully considered prior
\par
distribution for the primary quantity of interest
\par
3. updating, or planning to update, this prior distri-
\par
bution by Bayes's theorem.
\par
Such three-star studies have been summarised
\par
according to a standard pro forma, and are
\par
reported in appendix 1. We note that we do not
\par
require such studies to be prospective, in that
\par
currently most Bayesian examples are re-analyses
\par
of previous studies.
\par
The following are therefore not considered as
\par
three-star studies:
\par
1. exploratory or Phase II studies
\par
2. those using a `minimally informative' or
\par
reference prior, or only using an informative
\par
prior for a nuisance parameter such as between-
\par
study heterogeneity
\par
3. decision analyses in which expected utilities are
\par
assessed without any updating of beliefs using
\par
Bayes's theorem.
\par
Systematic. This is based on a structured search
\par
and reporting of the literature, and covers many
\par
areas which are not comprehensively reviewed,
\par
such as Bayesian analyses using reference priors
\par
and Phase I and Phase II studies. An attempt has
\par
been made to identify the majority of the relevant
\par
literature.
\par
Peripheral. Minimal references are provided on
\par
topics such as using Bayes's theorem for prognostic
\par
or diagnostic statements, `empirical Bayes' analysis
\par
which uses elements of Bayesian modelling without
\par
giving a Bayesian interpretation to the conclusions,
\par
preclinical work, pharmacokinetics, decision
\par
analysis, and descriptive studies of clinician's
\par
personal beliefs.
\par
We emphasise that our definition of `Bayesian'
\par
may be more restrictive than that of other
\par
researchers who may, for example, place a much
\par
higher emphasis on decision-making using subjec-
\par
tive probabilities, without necessarily requiring
\par
the use of Bayes's theorem.
\par
\textbf{The search procedure}
\par
A Bayesian approach can be applied to many scien-
\par
tific issues, and a search of the BIDS ISI database
\par
using the term `Bayes' or `Bayesian' yielded nearly
\par
4000 papers over the period 1990--98. All of the
\par
abstracts were handsearched for relevant material,
\par
and about 300 of these were relevant to health
\par
technology assessment. These were used as asource for forward and backward searches, and
\par
further techniques included searching other
\par
databases (EMBASE and MEDLINE), personal
\par
collections, handsearching recent journals, and
\par
internet searches of prominent authors.
\par
Since it is very difficult to identify appropriate
\par
health technology assessment literature from
\par
keywords, we would recommend anyone con-
\par
ducting a search for Bayesian methods in health
\par
technology assessment to use `bayes' and `bayesian'
\par
in all searches and then view all abstracts.
\par
We identified about 450 relevant papers, including
\par
around 30 reports of studies taking a `three-star'
\par
Bayesian perspective. The published studies are
\par
dispersed throughout the literature, apart from
\par
one recent collection of papers,45 and the only
\par
general textbook which might be considered as
\par
Bayesian health technology assessment is on the
\par
confidence profile approach.150
\par
It will be clear that the studies are mainly demon-
\par
strations of the approach rather than complete
\par
assessments, and in spite of numerous articles
\par
promoting the use of Bayesian methods the prac-
\par
tical take-up seems very low, although increasing.
\par
Possible reasons for this will be discussed in our
\par
review. There is also a preponderance of articles
\par
in the literature on methodology for clinical trials,
\par
and an apparent lack of articles on the more
\par
complex issues of synthesising data from studies
\par
of different designs, in spite of this being an
\par
area where Bayesian methods may have much to
\par
offer.
\par
\textbf{Structure of the review}
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 2 briefly reviews the `classical' statistical
\par
approach to health technology assessment, and
\par
then outlines the main features of the Bayesian
\par
philosophy, including the subjective inter-
\par
pretation of probability, the relation to diag-
\par
nostic testing, predictions, decision-making
\par
and design. There is a brief description of
\par
computational methods, complex Bayesian
\par
modelling, and the theoretical justification for
\par
the approach. Schools of Bayesians are identi-
\par
fied, and a commentary attempts to sort out the
\par
major ideological issues.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 3 deals in detail with the possible
\par
sources of prior distributions and their possible
\par
criticism in the light of data, and introduces the
\par
concept of exchangeability and its relation to
\par
hierarchical or multilevel prior distributions.
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 4 attempts to structure the large litera-
\par
ture on Bayesian approaches to all aspects of
\par
randomised controlled trials, including sequen-
\par
tial analysis, reporting, cost-effectiveness analysis
\par
and the stages of drug development.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 5 covers observational studies, such
\par
as case--control designs, the use of historical
\par
controls, and non-randomised comparisons of
\par
institutions.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 6 considers meta-analysis and its gener-
\par
alisations, in which evidence from multiple
\par
studies, possibly of different designs, is pooled
\par
using a statistical model.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 7 examines how Bayesian analyses for
\par
randomised or non-randomised studies may be
\par
placed in a concrete decision-making context
\par
in order to inform either commercial or public
\par
policies, possibly with explicit costs on the conse-
\par
quences of alternative strategies. The view of
\par
alternative `actors' is emphasised.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 8 discusses the reporting of Bayesian
\par
studies, sets out criteria for assessing the quality
\par
of a Bayesian analysis, and provides an example.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 9 is a case study in which a sequential
\par
cancer clinical trial, the continuous hyper-
\par
fractioned accelerated radiotherapy (CHART)
\par
study, was monitored using a Bayesian procedure.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 10 is a case study concerning the much-
\par
studied issue of magnesium for acute myocardial
\par
infarction (AMI), in which a meta-analysis
\par
conflicted with a mega-trial. We show that a
\par
reasonably sceptical Bayesian meta-analysis
\par
would not have found the initial meta-analysis
\par
convincing evidence.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 11 shows by four small case studies
\par
how modern Bayesian software can deal with
\par
the complex modelling problems previously
\par
analysed using the confidence profile approach.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 12 provides an example of a institu-
\par
tional comparison, in which the success rates
\par
of UK in vitro fertilisation (IVF) clinics is
\par
compared.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chapter 13 provides a final summary, general
\par
discussion and some suggestions for future
\par
research.
\par
{$\bullet$} Appendix 1 summarises and lists the `three-star'
\par
applications in a structured format, using the
\par
criteria outlined in chapter 9.
\par
\textbf{3}\emph{Introduction}
\par
{$\bullet$} Appendix 2 briefly describes available software
\par
and internet sites of interest.
\par
Most of the chapters in the review finish with a
\par
critical commentary, in which the arguments
\par
against the Bayesian perspective are summarised,
\par
and a list of key points for each chapter are
\par
repeated in chapter 13. Mathematical and computa-
\par
tional methods will be barely mentioned, and
\par
details should be sought in the references provided.
\par
The review is therefore structured by context rather
\par
than methods, although some methodological
\par
themes inevitably run throughout; for example,
\par
what form of prior distribution is appropriate, and
\par
is it reasonable to adopt an explicit loss function?
\par
Finally, we should be quite explicit as to our own
\par
subjective biases, which will doubtless be apparent
\par
from the organisation and text of this review. We
\par
favour the Bayesian philosophy, and would like to
\par
see its use extended in health technology assess-
\par
ment, but feel that this should be carried out
\par
cautiously, with critical appraisal, and in parallel
\par
with the currently accepted methods.
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. Bayesian methods are defined as the explicit
\par
quantitative use of external evidence in the
\par
design, monitoring, analysis, interpretation and
\par
reporting of a health technology assessment.
\par
2. Bayesian methods are a controversial topic in
\par
that they may involve the explicit use of subjec-
\par
tive judgements in what is conventionally
\par
supposed to be a rigorous scientific exercise in
\par
health technology assessment.
\par
3. There has been very limited use of proper
\par
Bayesian methods in practice, and relevant
\par
studies appear to be relatively easily identified.
\par
4. The potential importance of Bayesian methods
\par
to a topic is not necessarily reflected in the
\par
volume of published literature: in particular,
\par
publications on the design and analysis of
\par
single clinical trials dominate those on the
\par
synthesis of evidence from studies of multiple
\par
designs.
\par
\textbf{4}\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Chapter 2}
\par
An overview of the Bayesian philosophy in the
\par
context of health technology assessment
\par
In this chapter we give an overview of some of the
\par
generic features of the Bayesian philosophy that
\par
find application in health technology assessment.
\par
Limited references to the literature are given at
\par
this stage, but relevant sections within randomised
\par
trials, observational studies and evidence synthesis
\par
are identified. We shall use a simple running
\par
example to illustrate the general issues: the
\par
Grampian Region Early Anistreplase Trial
\par
(GREAT)204 of early thrombolytic treatment for
\par
myocardial infarction, which reported a 49\%
\par
reduction in mortality (23/148 deaths on control
\par
versus 13/163 deaths on active treatment).
\par
periodically analysed and the trial stopped
\par
if sufficiently convincing results obtained.
\par
Repeated analysis of the data has a strong effect
\par
on the type I error, since there are many oppor-
\par
tunities to obtain a false-positive result, and thus
\par
the P value and the confidence interval need
\par
adjusting477 (although this rarely appears to be
\par
carried out in the published report of the trial).
\par
Such sequential analysis is just one example of a
\par
problem of `multiplicity', in which adjustments
\par
need to be made due to multiple analyses being
\par
carried out simultaneously. A standard example
\par
is the use of Bonferroni adjustments when esti-
\par
mating treatment effects in multiple subsets.
\par
Clinical trials are generally designed from a
\par
Neyman--Pearson standpoint, but analysed from a
\par
Fisherian perspective.398 Methods used for observa-
\par
tional methods and evidence synthesis tend to be
\par
more Fisherian.
\par
Advantages of the traditional framework include
\par
its apparent separation of the evidence in the
\par
data from subjective factors, the general ease in
\par
computation, its wide acceptability and established
\par
criteria for `significance', its relevance to the drug
\par
regulatory framework in which quality control of
\par
statistical submissions must be ensured, the avail-
\par
ability of software, and the existence of robust
\par
non- and semi-parametric procedures.
\par
\textbf{The `classical' statistical approach}
\par
\textbf{in health technology assessment}
\par
It would be misleading to dichotomise statistical
\par
methods as either `classical' or `Bayesian', since
\par
both terms cover a bewildering range of tech-
\par
niques. It is a little more fair to divide conventional
\par
statistics into two broad schools, Fisherian and
\par
Neyman--Pearson; different Bayesian approaches
\par
will be discussed later in this chapter.
\par
{$\bullet$} The Fisherian approach to inference on an
\par
unknown intervention effect q is based on the
\par
likelihood function mentioned previously,
\par
which expresses the relative support given to the
\par
different values of q by the data. This gives rise to
\par
an estimate comprising the `most-likely' value for
\par
q, intervals based on the range of values of q most
\par
supported by the data, and the evidence against
\par
specified null hypotheses summarised by P values
\par
(the chance of getting a result as extreme as that
\par
observed were the null hypothesis true).
\par
{$\bullet$} The Neyman--Pearson approach is focused on
\par
the chances of making various types of error so
\par
that, for example, clinical trials are designed to
\par
have a fixed type I error a (the chance of incor-
\par
rectly rejecting the null hypothesis), usually
\par
taken as 5 or 1\%, and fixed power (one minus
\par
the type II error b, the chance of not detecting
\par
the alternative hypothesis), often 80 or 90\%.
\par
The situation is made more complex if a sequen-
\par
tial design is used, in which the data are
\par
\textbf{Critique of the classical approach}
\par
\textbf{Hypothesis testing and \emph{P }values}
\par
Overemphasis on hypothesis testing has been
\par
strongly criticised (although shifting attention
\par
to confidence intervals does not avoid all the
\par
problems, since these are just the set of hypotheses
\par
that cannot be rejected at a certain a level). P
\par
values are explicitly concerned with the chance of
\par
observing the data (or something more extreme)
\par
given certain values of the unknown quantity q,
\par
and use an inverse (and frequently misunderstood)
\par
argument for deriving statements about q.
\par
Arguments against this procedure include: the null
\par
hypothesis may be neither plausible nor of great
\par
interest, the arbitrariness of the 0.05 and 0.01 level,
