\textbf{Type I and type II error}
\par
Both Bayesians and Fisherians can express strong
\par
criticism of Neyman--Pearson theory. Anscombe,13
\par
quoted by Herson,232 says ``the concept of error
\par
probabilities of the first and second kinds \dots{}
\par
has no direct relevance to experimentation. The
\par
formalism of opinions, decisions concerning
\par
further experimentation and other required
\par
actions, are not dictated in a simple prearranged
\par
way by the formal analysis of the experiment, but
\par
call for judgement and imagination'', while Healy224
\par
asks ``Why the invariable 5\% for a? Conditional on
\par
this, why the larger 10\% or even 20\% for b? Is it
\par
really more important not to make a fool of
\par
yourself than it is to discover something new?''.
\par
Criticism of fixed type I error has particularly
\par
been aimed at sequential analysis (see pages
\par
10 and 29), and relevance of hypothesis testing
\par
and decision-making to health technology
\par
assessment will be a running theme of this review
\par
(see page 14).
\par
\textbf{Multiple testing}
\par
We have already identified the crucial issue that
\par
arises in any context in which simultaneous analysis
\par
of multiple studies, or multiple analyses of the
\par
same study, is required. The traditional approach
\par
warns that repeated hypothesis testing is bound to
\par
raise the chance of a type I error (wrongly rejecting
\par
a true null hypothesis), and so suggests some
\par
adjustment, such as Bonferroni, to try to retain a
\par
specified overall type I error. This will typically give
\par
larger P values and wider confidence intervals: it
\par
has been shown that such results would be consis-
\par
tent with a rather odd prior distribution in which a
\par
constant probability is given to all the null hypoth-
\par
eses being true, regardless of their number.472
\par
The need for any such adjustment, which neces-
\par
sarily depends on the number of hypotheses being
\par
tested, has been strongly questioned from a non-
\par
Bayesian perspective, particularly in epidemi-
\par
ology;356,382 Cole108 states that ``in every study, every
\par
association should be evaluated on its own merits:
\par
its prior credibility and its features in the study at
\par
hand. The number of other variables is irrelevant'',
\par
while Cook and Farewell110 say that it is generally
\par
reasonable to report unadjusted conclusions.
\par
However, Greenland and Robins207 are among the
\par
many who have argued that some adjustment is
\par
necessary, but rather than be based on type I errors
\par
it should be derived from an explicit model that
\par
reflects assumptions about variability. We shall
\par
return to this theme on page 12.
\par
Other criticisms of conventional statistical analysis
\par
include that it fails to incorporate formally the
\par
inevitable background information that is available
\par
both at design and analysis and, from a more
\par
ideological perspective, that it disobeys certain
\par
reasonable axioms of rational behaviour (see
\par
page 13). Finally, there is no doubt that classical
\par
inferences are often inappropriately interpreted
\par
in a Bayesian way, in that P values are mistaken
\par
for probabilities of null hypotheses being true,\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
