\textbf{Bayes's theorem}
\par
Suppose q is some quantity that is currently
\par
unknown, for example a specific patient's true
\par
diagnosis or the true success rate of a new therapy,
\par
and let p(q) denote the probability of each possible
\par
value of q (where for the moment we do not
\par
concern ourselves with the source of that proba-
\par
bility). Suppose we have some observed evidence y
\par
whose likelihood of occurrence depends on q,
\par
for example a diagnostic test or the results of a
\par
clinical trial. This dependence is formalised by a
\par
probability p(y\textbar q), which is the (conditional) proba-
\par
bility of y for each possible value of q. We would
\par
like to obtain the new probability for different
\par
values of q, taking account of the evidence y; this
\par
probability has the conditioning reversed, and is
\par
denoted p(q\textbar y). Bayes's theorem simply says
\par
p(q\textbar y)\textmu{} p(y\textbar q) \texttimes{} p(q)
\par
(The proportionality is made into an equality by
\par
making probabilities for all possible values of q
\par
add to 1.) The usual term for p(q) is the prior, for
\par
p(y\textbar q) the likelihood, and for p(q\textbar y) the posterior,
\par
and hence Bayes's theorem simply says that the
\par
posterior distribution is proportional to the
\par
product of the prior times the likelihood.
\par
\textbf{7}\emph{Overview of Bayesian philosophy}
\par
\textbf{Example}
\par
Pocock and Spiegelhalter363 discuss the GREAT
\par
trial, in which the unknown quantity q is the true
\par
percentage change in risk of mortality from using
\par
home thrombolytic therapy. They obtained a prior
\par
distribution for q expressing belief that ``a 15--20\%
\par
reduction in mortality is highly plausible, while the
\par
extremes of no benefit and a 40\% reduction are
\par
both unlikely''. This prior is shown in Figure 1a,
\par
while Figure 1b shows the likelihood expressing the
\par
support by the data (23/148 deaths on control
\par
versus 13/163 deaths on active treatment) for
\par
various values of q. In contrast to the prior distribu-
\par
tion, Figure 1b displays strong support for values of
\par
q representing a 40--60\% risk reduction.
\par
Figure 1c shows the posterior distribution, obtained
\par
by multiplying the prior and likelihood together
\par
and then making the total area under the curve be
\par
equal to one (i.e. `certainty'). The evidence in the
\par
likelihood has been pulled back towards the prior
\par
distribution -- a formal representation of the belief
\par
that the results were `too good to be true'.
\par
\textbf{8}
\par
\textbf{Reporting probability statements}
\par
Having obtained a posterior distribution, to produce
\par
probabilities of exceeding certain thresholds, or
\par
lying in certain intervals, is only a computational
\par
task. In Figure 1, the posterior distribution provides
\par
an easily interpretable summary of the evidence,
\par
and probabilities for hypotheses of interest can then
\par
be read off the graph by calculating the relevant
\par
areas under the curve. For example, the most likely
\par
benefit is around a 24\% risk reduction (half that
\par
observed in the trial), the posterior probability that
\par
the reduction is at least 50\% is only 5\%, and a 95\%
\par
interval runs from a 43\% to 0\% risk reduction.
\par
Such an interval is generally termed a `credible
\par
interval' -- unlike a confidence interval, it can be
\par
directly interpreted as saying that, given the prior
\par
assumptions, the model and the data, there is a
\par
95\% chance that the true reduction lies between 0
\par
and 43\%.
\par
In many standard situations a traditional confi-
\par
dence interval is essentially equivalent to a credible
\par
interval based on the likelihood alone, and hence
\par
equivalent to using a `flat' prior. Burton82 claims
\par
that ``it is already common practice in medical
\par
statistics to interpret a frequentist confidence
\par
interval as if it did represent a Bayesian posterior
\par
probability arising from a calculation invoking a
\par
prior density that is uniform on the fundamental
\par
scale of analysis''.
\par
\textbf{The subjective interpretation of}
\par
\textbf{probability}
\par
The standard use of probability describes long-run
\par
frequency properties of repeated random events.
\par
This is known as the frequency interpretation of
\par
probability, and so both Fisherian and Neyman--
\par
Pearson schools are often referred to as
\par
`frequentist'. We have allowed probability to
\par
refer to generic uncertainty about any unknown
\par
quantity, and this is an application of the subjec-
\par
tivist interpretation of probability.
\par
This subjective view of probability is not new, and
\par
used to be standard. Fienberg170 points out that
\par
Jakob Bernoulli in 1713 introduced ``the subjective
\par
notion that the probability is personal and varies
\par
with an individual's knowledge'', and that
\par
Laplace and Gauss both worked with posterior
\par
distributions, which became known as `the inverse
\par
method'. However from the mid-nineteenth
\par
century the frequency approach started to
\par
dominate, and controversy has sporadically
\par
continued. Dempster128 quotes Edgeworth in 1884
\par
as saying the critics who ``heaped ridicule upon
\par
Bayes's theorem and the inverse method'' were
\par
trying to elicit ``knowledge out of ignorance, some-
\par
thing out of nothing''. Polemical opinions are still
\par
expressed: in defence of the explicit introduction
\par
of subjective judgement into scientific research,
\par
Matthews319 states that ``it simply makes no sense to
\par
take seriously every apparent falsification of a plau-
\par
sible theory, any more than it makes sense to take
\par
seriously every new scientific idea''.
\par
The Bayesian perspective thus extends the remit of
\par
standard statistical analysis, in that there is explicit
\par
concern for what it is reasonable for an observer to
\par
believe in the light of data. Thus the perspective of
\par
the consumer of the analysis is explicitly taken
\par
into account; for example, in a trial on a new drug
\par
being carried out by a pharmaceutical company,
\par
the viewpoints of the company, the regulatory
\par
authorities and the medical profession may be
\par
substantially different. The subjective nature of the
\par
analysis is therefore unapologetically emphasised.
\par
Berger and Berry38 state that ``Bayesian statistics
\par
treats subjectivity with respect by placing it in the
\par
open and under the control of the consumer of
\par
data''.
\par
The prior distribution shown in Figure 1a was based
\par
on the subjective judgement of a senior cardiolo-
\par
gist, informed by empirical evidence derived from
\par
one unpublished and two published trials. Of
