\textbf{The relation to the use of Bayes's}
\par
\textbf{theorem in diagnostic tests}
\par
If q is something that is potentially observable, and
\par
p(q) can be derived from known data, then the use
\par
of Bayes's theorem is uncontroversial. For example,
\par
it has long been established that sensitivity and
\par
specificity are insufficient characteristics to judge
\par
the result of a diagnostic test for an individual --
\par
the disease prevalence is also needed.
\par
From the health technology assessment perspec-
\par
tive, the more interesting and controversial context
\par
is that of an unknown q, which is a quantity that
\par
is not potentially directly observable, such as
\par
the mean benefit of a new therapy in a defined
\par
group of patients. There have been many argu-
\par
ments80,132,361,429 for the connection between the
\par
use of Bayes's theorem in diagnostic testing and in
\par
general clinical research, pointing out that just as
\par
the prevalence is required for the assessment of a
\par
diagnostic test, so the prior distribution on q is
\par
required to supplement the usual information (P
\par
values and confidence intervals) which summarises
\par
the likelihood. We need only think of the huge
\par
number of clinical trials that are carried out, with
\par
few clear successes, to realise that the `prevalence'
\par
of truly effective treatments is low. We should thus
\par
be cautious about accepting extreme results, such
\par
as observed in the GREAT trial, at face value:
\par
indeed, Grieve213 suggests a Bayesian approach
\par
provides ``a yardstick against which a surprising
\par
finding may be measured''.
\par
Brophy and Joseph76 defend a Bayesian approach
\par
to clinical studies by analogy to the differing levels
\par
of certainty which might be demanded by a diag-
\par
nostic test under different circumstances, and
\par
