\par
\textbf{Predictions}
\par
Suppose we wish to predict some future observa-
\par
tions z, which depend on the unknown quantity
\par
q through a distribution p(z\textbar q): for example, z
\par
may be the outcomes to be observed on some
\par
\textbf{Sequential analysis}
\par
Sequential data fall naturally within the Bayesian
\par
framework, as the posterior distribution following
\par
each observation becomes the prior for the
\par
next. This is discussed with regard to clinical
\par
trials in chapter 4, but for illustration we
\par
consider the example of Berry,53 concerning
\par
the 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP) trial for acuteleukaemia.186 In this study patients were allocated
\par
in pairs to 6-MP (A) or placebo (B), and the treat-
\par
ment assigned to whichever stayed in remission
\par
longer was considered the preference for that pair.
\par
Of the first 18 pairs, 15 preferred A, and the trial
\par
was stopped with P {$<$} 0.01; three subsequent pairs
\par
also preferred A. The data are shown in Table 3,
\par
with the frequentist two-sided P values and the
\par
posterior probability of B being the preferred
\par
treatment, assuming an initial uniform prior on
\par
the probability of B being preferable to A.
\par
We note that the posterior probabilities can be
\par
calculated without regard to any stated stopping
\par
procedure, that the posterior probability that
\par
P(B {$>$} A) is simply the prior probability that the
\par
next pair will prefer B, and that the posterior
\par
probabilities suggest the trial could have ended
\par
considerably sooner.
\par
The idea that the data influence the posterior
\par
only through the likelihood is known as the
\par
likelihood principle37,49 (see page 13), and
\par
underlies the strong Bayesian criticism of
\par
sequential analysis.
\par
\textbf{Decision-making}
\par
Suppose we wish to make one of a set of decisions,
\par
and that we are willing to assess some value u(d, q),
\par
known as a utility, of taking the decision d when q
\par
is the true unknown `state of nature'. The theory of
\par
optimal decision-making says we should choose the
\par
decision that maximises our expected utility, where
\par
the expectation is taken with respect to our current
\par
probability distribution for q.
\par
307
\par
The use of Bayesian ideas in decision-making is a
\par
huge area of research and application, in which
\par
attention is focused on the utility of consequences
\par
rather than the use of Bayesian methods to revise
\par
beliefs.471 This activity blends naturally into cost-
\par
effectiveness analysis (see page 51), but neverthe-
\par
less the subjective interpretation of probability
\par
is essential, since the expressions of uncertainty
\par
required for a decision analysis can rarely be
\par
based purely on empirical data. There is a
\par
long history of attempts to apply this theory to
\par
medicine, and in particular there is a large litera-
\par
ture on decision analysis, whether applied to the
\par
individual patient or for policy decisions.301 The
\par
journal Medical Decision Making contains an
\par
extensive collection of policy analyses based
\par
on maximising expected utility, some of which
\par
particularly stress the importance of Bayesian
\par
considerations.
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
Therefore there has been a long debate on the use
\par
of loss functions (just the negative of utility), in
\par
parallel to that concerning prior distributions.
\par
Berry53 and others have long argued that the design,
\par
monitoring and analysis of a study must explicitly
\par
take into account the consequences of eventual
\par
decisions. It is important to note that there is also a
\par
frequentist theory of decision-making that uses loss
\par
functions, but does not average with respect to prior
\par
or posterior distributions: the decision-making
\par
strategy is generally `minimax', where the loss is
\par
minimised whatever the true value of the parameter
\par
might be. This can be thought of as assuming the
\par
most pessimistic prior distribution: see, for example,
\par
Bather30 and Palmer.344 Thus all combinations of the
\par
use of prior distributions and/or loss functions are
\par
possible: this is further discussed in the commen-
\par
taries to this chapter and chapter 4.
\par
The explicit use of utility functions within the
\par
design and monitoring of clinical trials is contro-
\par
versial but has been explored in a number of
\par
contexts: for example, Berry and Stangl45 discuss
\par
whether to stop a Phase II trial based on estimating
\par
the number of women in the trial and in the future
\par
who will respond; whether to continue a vaccine
\par
trial by estimating the number of children who will
\par
contract the disease; and the use of adaptive alloca-
\par
tion in a Phase III trial such that at each point the
\par
treatment which maximises the expected number
\par
of responders is chosen. See pages 32 and 37 for
\par
further discussion. A decision-theoretic approach
\par
also leads to formal techniques for assessing the
\par
payback from research (see page 52) and policy
\par
making (see page 54).
\par
\textbf{Hypothesis testing}
\par
Just as the Neyman--Pearson approach focuses on
\par
two competing hypotheses, it is possible to take a
\par
Bayesian hypothesis-comparison approach. This
\par
requires a prior distribution that places a `lump' of
\par
probability on each competing hypotheses, which
\par
is updated to produce a posterior probability in,
\par
say, the null hypothesis that two drugs have equal
\par
effect (note that this is not the same as a P value,
\par
although such a mistaken interpretation of a P
\par
value is often made). Priors that explicitly consider
\par
the `truth' of a null hypothesis are discussed on
\par
page 19. These were particularly promoted by
\par
Cornfield, who emphasised the `relative betting
\par
odds' between two hypotheses, defined as the ratio
\par
of the posterior to the prior odds. This measure of
\par
the relative likelihood of two hypotheses is also
\par
known as the `Bayes factor', and has been the
\par
subject of much research.266
\par
\textbf{11}\emph{Overview of Bayesian philosophy}
\par
\textbf{Design}
\par
Bayesian design of experiments can be considered
\par
as a natural combination of prediction and deci-
\par
sion-making, in that the investigator is seeking to
\par
choose a design which will achieve the desired
\par
goals. Chaloner and Verdinelli99 provide a general
\par
review, while Simes401 emphasises that trials that
\par
are designed to help treatment decisions require
\par
specification of utilities and detailed subgroup
\par
information in order to individualise treatment
\par
decisions. A simple design problem, choosing the
\par
size of a clinical trial, is considered on page 28.
\par
Sequential designs present a particular problem
\par
known as `backwards induction',124 in which one
\par
must work backwards from the end of the study,
\par
examine all the possible decision points that one
\par
might face, and optimise the decision, allowing
\par
for all the possible circumstances in which one
\par
might find oneself. This is computationally very
\par
demanding since one must consider what to do
\par
in all possible future eventualities, but limited
\par
examples will be described on pages 32 and 37.
\par
Early phases of clinical trials have attracted this
\par
approach: Brunier and Whitehead81 consider the
\par
balancing of costs of experimentation and errors
\par
in treatment allocation (see page 38).
\par
We emphasise, however, that the likelihood prin-
\par
ciple states that Bayesian inference is carried out
\par
independently of design, so that the reasons for
\par
obtaining particular data points is irrelevant: the
\par
only aspect of interest is the relative likelihood of
\par
observing those particular data given possible
\par
values of the parameters of interest.
\par
\textbf{12}
\par
\textbf{Multiplicity}
\par
The context of clinical trials may present issues of
\par
``multiple analyses of accumulating data, analyses of
\par
multiple end-points, multiple subsets of patients,
\par
multiple treatment group contrasts and inter-
\par
preting the results of multiple clinical trials''.404
\par
Observational data may feature multiple institu-
\par
tions, and meta-analysis involves synthesis of
\par
multiple studies. The general Bayesian approach
\par
to multiplicity involves specifying a common prior
\par
distribution for the substudies that expresses a
\par
belief in the expected `similarity' of all the indi-
\par
vidual unknown quantities being estimated. This
\par
produces a degree of pooling, in which an indi-
\par
vidual study's results tend to be `shrunk' towards
\par
the average result by an amount depending on the
\par
variability between studies and the precision of the
\par
individual study: relevant contexts include subset
\par
analysis, between-centre variability, and random-
\par
effects meta-analysis. This is essentially a random-
\par
effect approach, often labelled as `empirical Bayes'
\par
or `multilevel' modelling; we shall later distinguish
\par
these from a `fully Bayes' approach.
\par
This is later discussed with respect to hierarchical
\par
models (see page 21), subset analysis (see page 35),
\par
between-centre variability (see page 36), and insti-
\par
tutional comparisons (see page 44 and chapter 12).
\par
\textbf{Complex modelling}
\par
Health technology assessments will generally
\par
involve some synthesis of evidence from a variety
\par
of sources, and a single clinical trial will rarely
\par
provide a definitive conclusion as to a policy
\par
recommendation. Standard statistical methods
\par
are designed for summarising the evidence from
\par
single studies, and although there has been a
\par
huge growth in methods and application of meta-
\par
analysis, these have generally been concerned
\par
with pooling evidence from studies with very
\par
comparable designs, outcome measures and so on.
\par
A Bayesian approach allows evidence from diverse
\par
sources to be pooled through assuming that their
\par
underlying probability models (their likelihoods)
\par
have parameters of interest in common. For
\par
example, the `true' effect of an intervention will
\par
feature in models for both randomised trials and
\par
observational data, even though there may be addi-
\par
tional adjustments for potential biases, different
\par
populations, cross-overs between treatments and so
\par
on. One context in which this has been emphasised
\par
is in the regulation of medical devices (see page
\par
53). This ability to deal with very complex models
\par
is discussed in chapter 6, but in general this great
\par
flexibility brings with it mathematical and computa-
\par
tional challenges.
\par
\textbf{Computational issues}
\par
The Bayesian approach applies probability theory
\par
to a model derived from substantive knowledge
\par
and can, in theory, deal with realistically complex
\par
situations -- the approach can also be termed `full
\par
probability modelling'. It has to be acknowledged,
\par
however, that the computations may be difficult,
\par
with the specific problem being to carry out the
\par
integrations necessary to obtain the posterior
\par
distributions of quantities of interest in situations
\par
where non-standard prior distributions are used,
\par
or where there are additional `nuisance parame-
\par
ters' in the model. These problems in integrationfor many years restricted Bayesian applications
\par
to rather simple examples. However, there has
\par
recently been enormous progress in methods
\par
for Bayesian computation, generally exploiting
\par
modern computer power to carry out simulations
\par
known as Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC)
\par
methods.189,193
\par
Although we will not be particularly concerned
\par
with computational issues, we refer to Carlin et al.90
\par
and Etzioni and Kadane162 who discuss a range
\par
of methods which may be used (normal approxi-
\par
mations, Laplace approximations and numerical
\par
methods including MCMC), Gelman and
\par
Rubin's189 review of MCMC methods in bio-
\par
statistics, and Vanhouwelingen's464 commentary
\par
on the importance of computational methods in
\par
the future of biostatistics.
\par
\textbf{The theoretical justification for}
\par
\textbf{the Bayesian approach}
\par
The theoretical foundations for the optimality of
\par
Bayesian inference have been discussed at length
\par
by Cornfield,112 Degroot,124 Lindley,306 Bernardo
\par
and Smith43 and others. The crucial idea is that if
\par
one accepts certain intuitively plausible behav-
\par
ioural axioms, such as not placing bets that are
\par
guaranteed to lose money, then one should act
\par
according to decision theory based on subjective
\par
probabilities.
\par
Apart from this somewhat ideological argument,
\par
the most important theoretical construct (aside
\par
from probability theory itself) is the likelihood
\par
principle,37 which states that all the information
\par
that the data provides about the parameter is
\par
contained in the likelihood. This entails, for
\par
example, that frequentist stopping rules that retain
\par
type I error (with their consequence that the infer-
\par
ences are influenced by what one would have done
\par
had one observed something different) are entirely
\par
irrelevant.
\par
\textbf{Schools of Bayesians}
\par
It is important to emphasise that there is no such
\par
thing as a single Bayesian approach, and that
\par
many ideological differences exist between the
\par
researchers whose work is discussed in this chapter.
\par
Four levels of Bayesian approach, of increasing
\par
`purity', may be identified:
\par
1. The empirical Bayes approach, in which a
\par
prior distribution is estimated from multiple
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
experiments. Analyses and reporting are in
\par
frequentist terms.
\par
2. The reference Bayes approach, in which a
\par
Bayesian interpretation is given to conclusions
\par
expressed as posterior distributions, but an
\par
attempt is made to use `objective' or `reference'
\par
prior distributions.
\par
3. The proper Bayes approach, in which informa-
\par
tive prior distributions are based on available
\par
evidence, but conclusions are summarised by
\par
posterior distributions without explicit incorpo-
\par
ration of utility functions.
\par
4. The decision-theoretic or `full' Bayes approach,
\par
in which explicit utility functions are used to
\par
make decisions based on maximising expected
\par
utility.
\par
Our focus in this review is primarily on the third,
\par
proper, school of Bayesianism.
\par
\textbf{Making the health technology}
\par
\textbf{assessment context explicit}
\par
Bayesian methods explicitly allow for the possi-
\par
bility that the conclusions of an analysis may
\par
depend on who is conducting it and their available
\par
evidence and opinion, and therefore the context
\par
of the study is vital. Apart from methodological
\par
researchers, at least four different viewpoints can
\par
be identified for any health technology
\par
assessment:
\par
{$\bullet$} sponsors, for example the pharmaceutical
\par
industry, medical charities or granting
\par
agencies
\par
{$\bullet$} investigators, that is, those responsible for the
\par
conduct of a study, whether industry or publicly
\par
funded
\par
{$\bullet$} reviewers, for example regulatory bodies or
\par
journal editors
\par
{$\bullet$} consumers, for example agencies setting health
\par
policy, clinicians or patients.
\par
An analysis which might be carried out solely
\par
for the investigators, for example, may not be
\par
appropriate for presentation to reviewers or
\par
consumers, and Racine et al.370 point out that
\par
``experimentalists tend to draw a sharp distinction
\par
between providing their opinions and assessments
\par
for the purposes of experimental design and
\par
in-house discussion, and having them incorpo-
\par
rated into any form of externally disseminated
\par
report''.
\par
