\textbf{Commentary}
\par
As mentioned on page 11, we need to carefully
\par
distinguish the debate as to whether prior distribu-
\par
tions should be used for inferences, from the
\par
question of whether our objective should be esti-
\par
mation, hypothesis testing or a decision requiring
\par
a loss function of some kind. Table 4 considers all
\par
possible combinations of these elements.
\par
All six approaches have been investigated in theory
\par
and, to some extent, in practice, and the resulting
\par
arguments become complex. Here we shall only
\par
give a very brief overview; see the commentaries in
\par
each chapter for further detailed objections.
\par
\textbf{Prior or no prior?}
\par
There have been generic warnings about a
\par
Bayesian approach, and Feinstein167 claims that
\par
``a statistical consultant who proposes a Bayesian
\par
analysis should therefore be expected to obtain a
\par
suitably informed consent from the clinical client
\par
whose data are to be subjected to the experiment''.
\par
However, some seem to misunderstand the
\par
Bayesian explicit acceptance of subjectivity: Lane280
\par
states that ``We are told that elimination of subjec-
\par
tivity by use of Bayesian inference paves the way to
\par
truly objective, evidence-based practice. Yet who
\par
but a statistically minded minority can begin to
\par
interpret Bayesian analysis?''.In general, the objections to the use of a prior
\par
distribution have been pragmatic, and strict ideo-
\par
logical standpoints have been played down: Cox
\par
and Farewell118 say it would be ``a great pity if differ-
\par
ences of technical approach were exaggerated
\par
into differences about qualitative issues'', while
\par
Armitage19 maintains it is not appropriate to
\par
polarise the argument as a choice between two
\par
extremes. Practical difficulties in obtaining and
\par
using prior opinions will be described in the
\par
commentary to succeeding chapters: Fisher171 and
\par
O'Rourke343 provide lists of general objections,
\par
while Tukey454 suggests that the Bayesian approach
\par
to multiplicity has little to contribute due to it being
\par
rarely appropriate to assume exchangeability.
\par
Following the discussion on page 13, many
\par
authors have argued that the appropriateness of
\par
the Bayesian approach depends crucially on the
\par
circumstances and the precise question being
\par
asked: for example, both Koch272 and Whitehead476
\par
claim that a proper Bayesian approach may be
\par
reasonable at early stages of a drug's development
\par
but is not acceptable in Phase III trials.
\par
\textbf{Estimation, hypothesis testing or}
\par
\textbf{decision-making?}
\par
Whether or not to incorporate an explicit loss
\par
function would appear to depend on the question
\par
being asked, and the extent to which a health tech-
\par
nology assessment should lead to an inference
\par
about a treatment effect or a decision as to future
\par
policy has been strongly debated.15,72,223,249,402
\par
Important objections that have been raised to a
\par
decision-theoretic approach include the lack of a
\par
coherent theory for decision-making on behalf of
\par
multiple audiences with different utility functions,
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
the difficulty of obtaining agreed utility values,
\par
and the fact that future treatments would be
\par
recommended on the basis of even marginal
\par
expected gains, without any concern with the
\par
confidence with which such a recommendation is
\par
made (see also page 39). We also note Kass and
\par
Greenhouse's267 argument against the Bayes factor
\par
approach, claiming that ``in most RCTs, estimation
\par
would be more appropriate than testing''.
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. Claims of advantages and disadvantages of
\par
Bayesian methods are now largely based on
\par
pragmatic reasons rather than blanket ideolog-
\par
ical positions.
\par
2. A Bayesian approach can lead to flexible
\par
modelling of evidence from diverse sources.
\par
3. Bayesian methods are best seen as a transforma-
\par
tion from initial to final opinion, rather than
\par
providing a single `correct' inference.
\par
4. Different contexts may demand different statis-
\par
tical approaches, both regarding the role of
\par
prior opinion and the role of an explicit loss
\par
function. It is vital to establish contexts in which
\par
Bayesian approaches are appropriate.
\par
5. A decision-theoretic approach may be appro-
\par
priate where the consequences of a study are
\par
predictable, such as when dealing with rare
\par
diseases treated according to a protocol, within
\par
a pharmaceutical company, or in public health
\par
policy.
\par
