\par
\textbf{Chapter 3}
\par
Where does the prior distribution come from?
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
There is no denying that quantifiable prior beliefs
\par
exist in medicine. For example, in the context of
\par
clinical trials, Peto and Baigent358 state that ``it is
\par
generally unrealistic to hope for large treatment
\par
effects'' but that ``it might be reasonable to hope
\par
that a new treatment for acute stroke or AMI could
\par
reduce recurrent stroke or death rates in hospital
\par
from 10\% to 9\% or 8\%, but not to hope that
\par
it could halve in-hospital mortality''. However,
\par
turning informally expressed opinions into a math-
\par
ematical prior distribution is perhaps the most
\par
difficult aspect of Bayesian analysis. Four broad
\par
approaches are outlined below, ranging from the
\par
elicitation of subjective opinion to the use of
\par
statistical models to estimate the prior from data.
\par
Sensitivity analysis to alternative assumptions is
\par
considered vital, whatever the method used to
\par
construct the prior distribution.
\par
From a mathematical and computational perspec-
\par
tive, it is extremely convenient if the prior
\par
distribution is a member of a family of distributions
\par
that is conjugate to the form of the likelihood, in
\par
the sense that they `fit together' to produce a pos-
\par
terior distribution that is in the same family as the
\par
prior distribution. For example, many likelihoods
\par
for treatment effects can be assumed to have an
\par
approximately normal shape,421 and thus in these
\par
circumstances it will be convenient to use a normally
\par
shaped prior (the conjugate family), provided it
\par
approximately summarises the appropriate external
\par
evidence. Similarly when the observed data are to be
\par
proportions (implying a binomial likelihood), the
\par
conjugate prior is a member of the `beta' family
\par
of distributions which provide a flexible way of
\par
expressing beliefs about the magnitude of a true
\par
unknown frequency. Modern computing power is,
\par
however, reducing the need for conjugacy, and in
\par
this section we shall concentrate on the source of
\par
the prior rather than its precise mathematical form.
\par
We should repeat the statements made on page 9
\par
regarding the fact that there is no `correct' prior:
\par
Bayesian analysis can be seen as a means of trans-
\par
forming prior into posterior opinions, rather than
\par
producing the posterior distribution. It is therefore
\par
vital to take into account the context and audience
\par
for the assessment (see page 13).
\par
\textbf{Elicitation of opinion}
\par
A true subjectivist Bayesian approach requires only
\par
a prior distribution that expresses the personal
\par
opinions of an individual but, if the health tech-
\par
nology assessment is to be generally accepted by a
\par
wider community, it would appear to be essential
\par
that the prior distributions have some evidential or
\par
at least consensus support. In some circumstances
\par
there may, however, be little `objective' evidence
\par
available and summaries of expert opinion may be
\par
indispensable.
\par
There is a very large literature on eliciting subjec-
\par
tive probability distributions from experts, with
\par
some good early references on statistical391 and
\par
psychological aspects,456 as well as on methods
\par
for pooling distributions obtained from multiple
\par
experts.190 The fact that people are generally not
\par
good probability assessors is well known, and the
\par
variety of biases they suffer are discussed by Kadane
\par
and Wolfson.265 Nevertheless it has been shown
\par
that training can improve experts' ability to
\par
provide judgements that are `well calibrated', in
\par
the sense that if a series of events are given a
\par
probability, say 0.6, then around 60\% of these
\par
events will occur. Murphy and Winkler331 discuss
\par
this issue with regard to weather forecasting.
\par
Chaloner97 provides a thorough review of methods
\par
for prior elicitation in clinical trials, including
\par
interviews with clinicians, postal questionnaires,
\par
and the use of an interactive computer program to
\par
draw a prior distribution. She concludes that fairly
\par
simple methods are adequate, using interactive
\par
feedback with a scripted interview, providing
\par
experts with a systematic literature review, basing
\par
elicitation on 2.5 and 97.5\% percentiles, and using
\par
as many experts as possible.
\par
Berry and Stangl45 discuss methods for eliciting
\par
conjugate priors and checking whether the assess-
\par
ments are consistent with each other. Kadane and
\par
Wolfson260 distinguish between experiments to
\par
learn for which only the prior of the experimenter
\par
needs to be considered, and experiments to prove,
\par
in which the priors (and utilities) of an audience to
\par
be persuaded need to be considered. They discuss
\par
general methods of elicitation, and give an
\par
example applied to an observational study in
\par
\textbf{17}\emph{The prior distribution}
\par
\textbf{18}
\par
healthcare. They emphasise the method by which
\par
experts may be asked for predictions about future
\par
observations; from these assessments an implicit
\par
prior distribution can be derived.
\par
The methods used in case studies can be divided
\par
into four main categories using increasingly formal
\par
methods:
\par
1. Informal discussion. Examples include Rosner
\par
and Berry,381 who consider a trial of paclitaxel
\par
(Taxol\textregistered ) in metastatic breast cancer, in which
\par
a beta prior for the overall success rate was
\par
assessed using a mean of 25\% and 50\% belief
\par
that the true success rate lay between 15 and
\par
35\%. Similarly, Lilford and Braunholtz299
\par
describe how priors were obtained from two
\par
doctors for the relative risk of venous throm-
\par
bosis from oral contraceptives. In the context of
\par
meta-analysis, Smith et al.412 thought that it was
\par
unlikely that the between-study odds would vary
\par
by more than an order of magnitude, and hence
\par
derived a prior distribution on the hetero-
\par
geneity parameter.
\par
2. Structured interviewing and formal pooling of
\par
opinion. Freedman and Spiegelhalter180
\par
describe an interviewing technique in which
\par
a set of experts were individually interviewed
\par
and hand-drawn plots of their prior distribu-
\par
tions elicited. Deliberate efforts were made
\par
to prevent the opinions being over-confident
\par
(too `tight'). The distributions were converted
\par
to histograms, and averaged to produce a
\par
composite prior. This was carried out twice for
\par
trials of thiotepa in superficial bladder cancer
\par
and these priors used later in discussing initial
\par
and interim power of the study,416,418 (see pages
\par
28 and 29). A similar exercise was carried out
\par
before a trial in osteosarcoma414 and used in a
\par
discussion of the power of the trial.420 Gore198
\par
introduced the concept of `trial roulette', in
\par
which 20 gaming chips, each representing 5\%
\par
belief, could be distributed amongst the bins of
\par
a histogram: in a trial of artificial surfactant
\par
in premature babies, 12 collaborators were
\par
interviewed using this technique to obtain
\par
their opinion on the possible benefits of the
\par
treatment.442 Using an electronic tool so that
\par
individuals in a group could respond without
\par
attribution, Lilford et al.297 presented collabora-
\par
tors in a trial with a series of imaginary patients
\par
in order to elicit their opinions on the benefit
\par
of early delivery.
\par
3. Structured questionnaires. The `trial roulette'
\par
scheme described above was administered by
\par
post by Hughes242,243 for a trial in treatment of
\par
oesophageal varices, and by Abrams et al.1,421 for
\par
a trial of neutron therapy.
\par
Parmar et al.348 elicited prior distributions for
\par
the effect of a new radiotherapy regime
\par
(CHART), in which the possible treatment
\par
effect was discretised into 5\% bands and the
\par
form was sent by post to each of nine clinicians.
\par
Each provided a distribution over these bands
\par
and an arithmetic mean was then taken. Parmar
\par
et al. provide a copy of the form in their paper,
\par
and results are provided for both lung348 and
\par
head and neck cancer.421
\par
4. Computer-based elicitation. Chaloner et al.98
\par
provide a detailed case study of the use of a
\par
rather complex computer program that interac-
\par
tively elicited distributions from five clinicians
\par
for a trial of prophylactic therapy in AIDS (see
\par
also Carlin et al.90). Kadane258 reports the results
\par
of an hour-long telephone interview with each
\par
of five clinicians, using software to estimate
\par
prior parameters from the results of a series of
\par
questions eliciting predictive probability distri-
\par
butions for responses of various patient types.
\par
When a second round of elicitation became
\par
necessary, the proposal was met by ``little enthu-
\par
siasm''. Kadane and Wolfson260 provide an
\par
edited transcript of a computerised elicitation
\par
session in a non-trial context. Dumouchel141
\par
describes a computer program for eliciting
\par
prior distributions in hierarchical models, in
\par
which beliefs about contrasts give rise to infor-
\par
mative prior distributions on between-unit
\par
variability.
\par
\textbf{Summary of evidence}
\par
If the results of previous similar studies are avail-
\par
able it is clear that may be used as the basis for a
\par
prior distribution. Three main approaches have
\par
been used:
\par
1. Use of a single previous experimental result as a
\par
prior distribution. Brown et al.78 and
\par
Spiegelhalter et al.421 both provide examples of
\par
prior distributions proportional to the likeli-
\par
hoods arising from a single pilot trial.
\par
Korn et al.276 could not identify a beta prior
\par
matching the characteristics of their past
\par
empirical experience, but Bring75 pointed out
\par
that had they fitted the mode rather than the
\par
mean of the prior to their past data then the
\par
problem would have disappeared.2. Direct use of a meta-analysis of many previous
\par
studies. Lau et al.284 point out that cumulative
\par
meta-analysis can be given a Bayesian interpreta-
\par
tion in which the prior for each trial is obtained
\par
from the meta-analysis of preceding studies,
\par
while Dersimonian129 derives priors for a trial of
\par
the effectiveness of calcium supplementation in
\par
the prevention of pre-eclampsia in pregnant
\par
women by a meta-analysis of previous trials using
\par
both random effects and fixed effects models.
\par
Smith et al.410 and Higgins and Whitehead233
\par
both use a review of past meta-analyses as a basis
\par
for a prior distribution for the degree of hetero-
\par
geneity in a current meta-analysis. Gilbert et al.192
\par
report an empirical distribution of past trial
\par
results in surgery which could be used as a prior.
\par
3. Use of previous data in some discounted form.
\par
Previous studies may not be directly related to
\par
the one in question and we may wish to discount
\par
its influence. Kass and Greenhouse267 state that
\par
``we wish to use this information, but we do not
\par
wish to use it as if the historical controls were
\par
simply a previous sample from the same popula-
\par
tion as the experimental controls'', and they
\par
investigate a range of such discounting methods
\par
(see appendix 1). Berry and Stangl45 discuss
\par
the use of historical information with trial data,
\par
giving an example of a Bayesian logistic regres-
\par
sion analysis with historical data weighted by
\par
various amounts: see page 5.4 for discussion of
\par
historical controls in randomised trials. Brophy
\par
and Joseph76 pool two previous studies to form a
\par
prior but investigate the effect of downweighting
\par
their influence to 50 and 10\%, Cronin et al.121
\par
report a mixture of past evidence and `prior
\par
belief of what is a reasonable range of values',
\par
while Greenhouse and Wasserman206 down-
\par
weight a previous trial with 176 subjects to have
\par
weight equivalent to only 10 subjects.
\par
\textbf{Default priors}
\par
It would clearly be attractive to have prior distribu-
\par
tions that could be taken `off-the-shelf', rather than
\par
having to consider all available evidence external
\par
to the study in their construction. Four main
\par
suggestions can be identified.
\par
\textbf{`Non-informative' or `reference' priors}
\par
There has been substantial research in the
\par
Bayesian literature into so-called non-informative
\par
or reference priors, which usually take the form of
\par
uniform distributions over the range of interest,
\par
possibly on a suitably transformed scale of the
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
parameter.70 Formally, a uniform distribution
\par
means the posterior distribution has the same
\par
shape as the likelihood function, which in turn
\par
means that the resulting Bayesian intervals and
\par
estimates will essentially match the traditional
\par
results, so that posterior tail areas will match one-
\par
sided P values (ignoring any adjustment for multi-
\par
plicity). Hughes243 points out that a careful choice
\par
of scale is necessary and that a uniform prior on,
\par
say, a log(relative risk) might entail an inappro-
\par
priate prior on a risk difference, while Kass and
\par
Wasserman268 review the current status of such
\par
reference priors.
\par
Results with reference priors are generally quoted
\par
as one part of a Bayesian analysis. In particular,
\par
Burton82 suggests that most doctors interpret
\par
frequentist confidence intervals as posterior distri-
\par
butions, and also that information prior to a study
\par
tends to be vague, and that therefore results from
\par
a study should be presented by performing a
\par
Bayesian analysis with a non-informative prior and
\par
quoting posterior probabilities for the parameter
\par
of interest being in various regions. He gives
\par
examples from a hypothetical logistic regression
\par
setting and from the evaluation of the effectiveness
\par
of a Haemophilus influenzae type b (HIB) vaccine in
\par
Aboriginal children.
\par
Other applications of reference priors include
\par
Achcar et al.,
\par
7 Briggs73 on net benefit in cost-
\par
effectiveness studies, and almost all the confidence
\par
profile examples in Eddy et al.150 However, Fisher171
\par
points out that ``there is no such thing as a
\par
``noninformative'' prior. Even improper priors
\par
give information: all possible values are equally
\par
likely''.
\par
\textbf{`Sceptical' priors}
\par
Informative priors that express scepticism about
\par
large treatment effects have been put forward both
\par
as a reasonable expression of doubt, and as a way
\par
of controlling early stopping of trials on the basis
\par
of fortuitously positive results. Kass and Green-
\par
house267 suggest that a ``cautious reasonable sceptic
\par
will recommend action only on the basis of fairly
\par
firm knowledge'', but that these sceptical ``beliefs
\par
we specify need not be our own, nor need they
\par
be the beliefs of any actual person we happen to
\par
know, nor derived in some way from any group of
\par
``experts'' ''.
\par
Mathematically speaking, a sceptical prior about a
\par
treatment effect will have a mean of zero and
\par
some spread which determines the degree of
\par
scepticism. Fletcher et al.173 use such a prior, while
\par
Spiegelhalter et al.421 argue that a reasonable
\par
\textbf{19}\emph{The prior distribution}
\par
\textbf{20}
\par
degree of scepticism may be equivalent to feeling
\par
that the alternative hypothesis is optimistic, forma-
\par
lised by a prior with only a small probability (say
\par
5\%) that the treatment effect is as large as the
\par
alternative hypothesis (see Figure 2).
\par
This approach has been used in a number of case
\par
studies,183,348 and has been suggested as a basis
\par
for monitoring trials166 (see page 29) and when
\par
considering whether or not a confirmatory study
\par
is justified (see page 33). Other users of sceptical
\par
priors include Dersimonian129 and Heitjan227 in the
\par
context of Phase II studies, while a senior US Food
\par
and Drugs Administration (FDA) biostatistician339
\par
has stated that he ``would like to see [sceptical
\par
priors] applied in more routine fashion to provide
\par
insight into our decision making''.
\par
\textbf{`Enthusiastic' priors}
\par
As a counterbalance to the pessimism expressed
\par
by the sceptical prior, Spiegelhalter et al.421 suggest
\par
an `enthusiastic' prior centred on the alternative
\par
hypothesis and with a low chance (say 5\%) that
\par
the true treatment benefit is negative. Use of such
\par
a prior has been reported in case studies183,227 and
\par
as a basis for conservatism in the face of early
\par
negative results166 (see page 31). Digman et al.133
\par
provide an example of such a prior, but call it
\par
`optimistic'.
\par
\textbf{Priors with a point mass at the null}
\par
\textbf{hypothesis (`lump-and-smear' priors)}
\par
The traditional statistical approach expresses a
\par
qualitative distinction between the role of a null
\par
hypothesis, generally of no treatment effect, and
\par
alternative hypotheses. A prior distribution that
\par
retains this distinction would place a `lump' of
\par
probability on the null hypothesis, and `smear' the
\par
remaining probability over the whole range of
\par
alternatives: Cornfield112 uses a normal distribution
\par
centred on the null hypothesis, while Hughes243
\par
uses a uniform prior over a suitably restricted
\par
range. The resulting posterior distribution retains
\par
this structure, giving rise to a posterior probability
\par
of the truth of the null hypothesis: this is appar-
\par
ently analogous to a P value but is neither
\par
numerically nor conceptually equivalent (see
\par
page 11).
\par
Cornfield repeatedly argued for this approach,
\par
which naturally gives rise to the `relative betting
\par
odds' as a sequential monitoring tool, defined as
\par
the ratio of the likelihood of the data under the
\par
null hypothesis to the average likelihood (with
\par
respect to the prior) under the alternative.113 The
\par
relative betting odds is independent of the `lump'
\par
of prior probability placed on the null (although it
\par
does depend on the shape of the `smear' over the
\par
alternatives), and does not suffer from the problem
\par
of `sampling to a foregone conclusion' (see page
\par
32). He suggests a `default' prior under the alterna-
\par
tive as a normal distribution centred on the null
\par
hypothesis and with expectation (conditional on
\par
the effect being positive), equal to the alternative
\par
hypothesis d, that is, with prior standard deviation
\par
\textdiv (p/2d). This is essentially equivalent to the formu-
\par
lation of a sceptical prior described above, but with
\par
probability of exceeding the alternative hypothesis
\par
of g = 0.21 -- this is larger than the value of 5\%
\par
often used for sceptical priors, but the lump of
\par
probability on the null hypothesis is already
\par
expressing considerable scepticism. Values for
\par
these prior distributions for 11 outcome measures
\par
are reported for the Urokinase Pulmonary
\par
Embolism Trial.389 This method was used in a
\par
number of major studies alongside more standard
\par
approaches,114,389,458 although relative betting odds
\par
were dropped from the final report of the
\par
Coronary Drug Project.115
\par
A mass of probability on the null hypothesis has
\par
also been used in a cancer trial182 and for sensitivity
\par
analysis in trial reporting243 (see page 33).
\par
Although such an analysis provides an explicit
\par
probability that the null hypothesis is true, and so
\par
appears to answer the question of interest, the
\par
prior might be somewhat more realistic were the
\par
lump to be placed on a small range of values repre-
\par
senting the more plausible null hypothesis of `no
\par
clinically effective difference' (although Corn-
\par
field112 points out that the `lump' is just a mathe-
\par
matical approximation to such a prior). Lachin277
\par
has extended the approach to this situation where
\par
the null hypothesis forms an interval.
\par
\textbf{`Robust' priors}
\par
To save all the effort of eliciting a prior distribu-
\par
tion, it seems reasonable when reporting or
\par
monitoring a study to identify how the prior affects
\par
the conclusions. This essentially means identifying
\par
a class of prior distributions, and then seeing how
\par
the posterior conclusions vary with priors within
\par
that class. Greenhouse and Wasserman206 carry out
\par
two such case studies, while Carlin and Sargent93,388
\par
use `prior partitioning' to identify the set of priors
\par
that would lead, say, to stopping a trial according
\par
to specified tail-area posterior probabilities. Those
\par
in authority then simply have to assess whether
\par
their prior lies in the appropriate partition. See
\par
pages 31 and 33 for further discussion of this
\par
approach in clinical trials.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
