\textbf{Exchangeability, hierarchical}
\par
\textbf{models and multiplicity}
\par
Suppose we are interested in making inferences on
\par
many parameters q1,\dots, qk measured on k `units'
\par
which may, for example, be true treatment effects
\par
in subsets of patients, multiple clinics, or for each
\par
of a series of trials. We can identify three different
\par
assumptions:
\par
1. All the qs are identical, in which case all the data
\par
can be pooled and the individual units
\par
forgotten.
\par
2. All the qs are entirely unrelated, in which case
\par
the results from each unit are analysed inde-
\par
pendently (for example using a fully specified
\par
prior distribution).
\par
3. The qs are assumed to be `similar' in the
\par
following sense. Suppose we were blinded as to
\par
which unit was which, and all we had was a label
\par
for each, say A, B, C and so on. Then our prior
\par
opinion about any particular set of qs would not
\par
be affected by only knowing the labels, in that
\par
we have no reason to think specific units are
\par
systematically different. Such a set of qs are
\par
called `exchangeable',43 and this assumption can
\par
be shown under broad conditions to be mathe-
\par
matically equivalent to assuming they are drawn
\par
at random from some population distribution,
\par
just as in a traditional random effects model.
\par
Note that there does not need to be any actual
\par
sampling -- perhaps these k units are the only
\par
ones that exist -- since the probability structure
\par
is a consequence of the belief rather than a
\par
physical randomisation mechanism.
\par
We emphasise that an assumption of
\par
exchangeability is a judgement based on our
\par
knowledge of the context. If there are known
\par
reasons to suspect specific units are systematically
\par
different, then those reasons need to be modelled.
\par
Dixon and Simon134 discuss the reasonableness of
\par
exchangeability assumptions.
\par
The Bayesian approach to multiplicity is thus to
\par
integrate all the units into a single model, in which
\par
it is assumed that q1,\dots, qk are drawn from some
\par
common prior distribution whose parameters are
\par
unknown: this is known as a hierarchical model.
\par
These unknown parameters may then be estimated
\par
directly from the data using the technique of
\par
`marginal maximum likelihood' (known as the
\par
`empirical Bayes' approach), or given a (usually
\par
minimally informative) prior distribution (known
\par
as the `full Bayes' approach). The results from
\par
either an empirical or full Bayes analysis will gener-
\par
ally be similar, and lead to the inferences for each
\par
unit having narrower intervals than if they are
\par
assumed independent, but biased towards mean
\par
response. Thus there is a similar conservatism to
