\textbf{Empirical criticism of priors}
\par
The ability of subjective prior distributions to
\par
predict the true benefits of interventions is clearly
\par
of great interest, and Box69 suggested a method-
\par
ology for comparing priors with subsequent data.
\par
The prior is used to create a predictive distribution
\par
of the future summary statistic, which provides the
\par
prior predictive probability of observing data as
\par
extreme as that actually seen. This probability may
\par
be termed Box's P value. Spiegelhalter416 showed
\par
how this could be applied to a clinical trial, and
\par
applications have been reported for discrete
\par
data,369,422 while Spiegelhalter et al.421 display predic-
\par
tive distributions and Box's P values in all their
\par
analyses (see chapter 9 for an application in the
\par
CHART trial.) For normal prior and likelihood,
\par
Box's P value is simply derived from the standard-
\par
ised difference (denoted Z) between the two
\par
distributions (difference in means divided by the
\par
square root of the sum of their variances).421
\par
There have been a number of prospective elicita-
