\textbf{Commentary}
\par
Here we shall only consider comments on how
\par
priors may be best obtained: whether they should
\par
be used or not is discussed elsewhere (see pages 14
\par
and 39).
\par
There have been many criticisms of the process of
\par
eliciting subjective prior distributions in the health
\par
technology assessment context. Claims include:
\par
1. Subjects are biased in their opinions. Gilbert et
\par
al.192 state that ``innovations brought to the stage
\par
of randomised trials are usually expected by the
\par
innovators to be sure winners'', and Hughes242
\par
points out that the very fact that clinicians are
\par
participating in a trial is likely to suggest they
\par
expect the new therapy to be of benefit; this
\par
appears to be borne out in the results shown in
\par
Table 5. Altman12 warns that investigators may
\par
even begin to exaggerate their prior beliefs in
\par
order to make their prospective trial appear
\par
more attractive (although this appears to already
\par
happen both in public and industry-funded
\par
studies). Fisher171 believes the effort put into
\par
elicitation is misplaced, since the measured
\par
beliefs are likely to be based more on emotion
\par
than scientific evidence.2. The choice of subject biases results. The biases
\par
discussed above mean that the choice of subject
\par
for elicitation is likely to influence the results. If
\par
we wish to know the distribution of opinions
\par
among well-informed clinicians, then trial inves-
\par
tigators are not a random sample and will give
\par
biased conclusions. Lewis292 says statisticians
\par
reviewing the literature should provide much
\par
better prior distributions than clinicians, while
\par
Chalmers95 suggests even lay people are biased
\par
towards believing new therapies will be
\par
advances, and therefore we need empirical
\par
evidence on which to base the prior probability
\par
of superiority.
\par
Fisher171 claims that uncertainty as to whose
\par
prior to use militates against any use of Bayesian
\par
methods. However, it can be argued that taking
\par
context into account (see page 13) means that
\par
it is quite reasonable to allow for differing
\par
perspectives.
\par
3. Timing of elicitation has an influence. Senn397
\par
objects to any retrospective elicitation of priors
\par
as ``present remembrance of priors past is not
\par
the same as a true prior'', while Hughes242 points
\par
out that opinions are likely to be biased by what
\par
evidence has recently been represented and by
\par
whom.
\par
4. Subjective judgement of exchangeability may be
\par
inappropriate. Tukey454 says that ``to treat the true
\par
improvements for the classes concerned as a
\par
sample from a nicely behaved population \dots{} does
\par
not seem to me to be near enough the real world
\par
to be a satisfactory and trustworthy basis for the
\par
careful assessment of strength of evidence''.
\par
These concerns have led to a call for the evidential
\par
basis for priors to be made explicit, and for effort
\par
to go into identifying reasons for disagreement and
\par
attempting to resolve these.171 Even advocates of
\par
Bayesian methods have suggested that the biases in
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
clinical priors suggest more attention to empirical
\par
evidence from past trials, possibly represented as
\par
sceptical priors: Fayers164 asks, given the long expe-
\par
rience of negative trials, ``should we not be using
\par
priors strongly centred around 0, irrespective of
\par
initial opinions, beliefs and hopes of clinicians?'',
\par
while Spiegelhalter et al.421 say that elicited priors
\par
from investigators show predictable positive bias
\par
and may possibly be replaced by archetypal `enthu-
\par
siastic' priors.
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. The use of a prior is based on judgement and
\par
hence a degree of subjectivity cannot be
\par
avoided.
\par
2. The prior is important and not unique, and so a
\par
range of options should be examined in a sensi-
\par
tivity analysis.
\par
3. The intended audience for the analysis needs to
\par
be explicitly specified.
\par
4. The quality of subjective priors (as assessed by
\par
predictions) show predictable biases in terms of
\par
enthusiasm.
\par
5. For a prior to be taken seriously, its evidential
\par
basis must be explicitly given, as well as any
\par
assumptions made (e.g. downweighting of past
\par
data). Care must, however, be taken of bias in
\par
published results.
\par
6. Archetypal priors may be useful for identifying a
\par
reasonable range of prior opinion.
\par
7. Great care is required in using default priors
\par
intended to be minimally informative.
\par
8. Exchangeability assumptions should not be
\par
made casually.
\par
