\textbf{Chapter 4}
\par
A guide to the Bayesian health technology
\par
assessment literature: conduct
\par
of randomised controlled trials
\par
A Bayesian: one who asks you what you think before a
\par
clinical trial in order to tell you what you think afterwards.
\par
Stephen Senn398
\par
\textbf{General arguments}
\par
Randomised controlled trials have provided fertile
\par
territory for arguments between alternative statis-
\par
tical philosophies. There are many specific issues
\par
in which a distinct Bayesian approach is identi-
\par
fiable, such as the ethics of randomisation, power
\par
calculations, monitoring, subset analysis, alterna-
\par
tive designs and so on, and these are dealt with in
\par
separate sections below.
\par
There are also a number of general discussion
\par
papers on the relevance of Bayesian methods to
\par
trials. These include tutorial introductions at a
\par
non-technical295 and slightly more technical level,1
\par
while Brophy and Joseph76 explain the Bayesian
\par
approach by demonstrating its use in re-analysing
\par
data from the Global Utilization of Streptokinose
\par
and t-PA for Occluded Coronary Arteries
\par
(GUSTO) trial of different thrombolytic regimes,
\par
using prior information from other studies. More
\par
detailed but non-mathematical discussions are
\par
given by Cornfield113 and Kadane,262 who particu-
\par
larly emphasises the internal consistency of the
\par
Bayesian approach, and welcomes the need for
\par
explicit prior distributions and loss function
\par
as producing scientific openness and honesty.
\par
Pocock and Hughes365 again provide a non-
\par
mathematical discussion concentrating on estima-
\par
tion issues in trials, while Armitage22 attempts a
\par
balanced view of the competing methodologies.
\par
Particular emphasis has been placed on the ability
\par
of Bayesian methods to take full advantage of
\par
the accumulating evidence provided by small
\par
trials.302,317 A special issue of Statistics in Medicine on
\par
`methodological and ethical issues in clinical
\par
trials' contains papers both for53,420,459 and
\par
against476 the Bayesian perspective, and features
\par
incisive discussion by Armitage, Cox and others.
\par
Palmer and Rosenberger346 review non-standard
\par
trial designs and suggest circumstances where they
\par
may be appropriate.
\par
Somewhat more technical reviews are given by
\par
Spiegelhalter and colleagues.420,421 Berry52,53,55 has
\par
long argued for a Bayesian decision-theoretic basis
\par
for clinical trial design, and has described in detail
\par
methods for elicitation, monitoring, decision-
\par
making and using historical controls.
\par
Earlier (see page 14), we identified the funda-
\par
mental dual issues of prior distributions and loss
\par
functions, and we have followed this division by
\par
focusing on inferences from a clinical trial in this
\par
chapter, and delaying discussion of subsequent
\par
decisions, whether based on randomised or non-
\par
randomised evidence, to chapter 7. We realise this
\par
is a somewhat artificial separation, as the potential
\par
role for explicit statement of a loss function is a
\par
running theme throughout discussions on design,
\par
sample size, sequential analysis, adaptive allocation
\par
and payback from research programmes, and many
\par
would argue that the eventual decision is insepa-
\par
rable from design and analysis of a study. However,
\par
the health technology assessment context often
\par
means that the investigators who design and carry
\par
out a study are generally not the same body who
\par
make decisions on the basis of the evidence (see
\par
page 13), and so if we take a pragmatic rather than
\par
ideological perspective, then the attempt at separa-
\par
tion appears reasonable.
\par
\textbf{Ethics and randomisation}
\par
\textbf{Is randomisation necessary?}
\par
Randomisation has two traditional justifications: it
\par
ensures treatment groups are directly comparable
\par
(up to the play of chance), and it provides a funda-
\par
mental basis for the probability distributions
\par
underlying conventional statistical procedures.
\par
Since Bayesian probability models are derived
\par
from subjective beliefs and do not require any
\par
underlying random mechanism, the latter require-
\par
ment is irrelevant, and this has led some to
