question the need for randomisation at all,
\par
provided alternative methods of balancing groups
\par
can be established. For example, Urbach459
\par
argues
\par
that a ``Bayesian analysis of clinical trials affords a
\par
valid, intuitively plausible rationale for selective
\par
controls, and marks out a more limited role for
\par
randomisation than it is generally accorded'', while
\par
Kadane261 suggests updating clinician's priors and
\par
only assigning treatments that at least one clini-
\par
cian considers optimal. Berry goes further in
\par
claiming46 ``Randomised trials are inherently
\par
unethical''. Papineau347 refutes Urbach's position
\par
and claims that despite it not being essential for
\par
statistical inference, experimental randomisation
\par
forms a vital role in drawing causal conclusions
\par
(see also Rubin383). The relationship between
\par
randomisation and causal inferences is beyond the
\par
scope of this chapter, but in general the need for
\par
sound experimental design appears to dominate
\par
philosophical statistical issues.246 In fact Berry and
\par
Kadane65 suggest that if there are several parties
\par
who make different decisions and observe
\par
different data, randomisation may be a strictly
\par
optimal procedure since it enables each observer
\par
to draw his or her own appropriate conclusions.
\par
Kadane and Seidenfeld263 make a useful distinc-
\par
tion between experiments to learn and those to
\par
prove, which we will find useful when it comes to
\par
discussing confirmatory trials.
\par
That careful analysis of databases can to some
\par
extent replace randomised trials has been argued
\par
by Howson and Urbach240 and Hlatky.235 Byar84 puts
\par
an opposing view.
\par
\textbf{When is it ethical to randomise?}
\par
If we agree that randomisation is in theory useful,
\par
then the issue arises of when it is ethical to
\par
randomise. This is closely associated with the
\par
process of deciding when to stop a trial (discussed
\par
further on page 29) and is often represented
\par
as a balance between individual and collective
\par
ethics.346,362 The ethics of randomisation and
\par
clinical trials have been covered in Edwards et al.155Freedman176 introduced the idea of professional
\par
equipoise, in which disagreement among the
\par
medical profession makes randomisation ethical.
\par
The trial design of Kadane258 (see appendix 1) is
\par
an expression of this principle, in that only a
\par
treatment that at least one clinician thought
\par
optimal could be given to a patient (although it
\par
unfortunately turned out that a computer bug
\par
meant that some patients were allocated to
\par
treatments that all clinicians felt were suboptimal).
\par
Perhaps a more appealing approach is the
\par
`uncertainty principle', which is often argued
\par
as a basis for ethical randomisation85: this may be
\par
thought of as `personal equipoise'154 in which the
\par
clinician is uncertain as to the best treatment for
\par
the patient in front of him or her. However, a
\par
quantified degree of uncertainty has not been
\par
specified.
\par
The Bayesian approach can be seen as formalising
\par
the uncertainty principle by explicitly representing,
\par
in theory, the belief of an individual clinician that
\par
a treatment may be beneficial to a specific patient --
\par
this could be provided by superimposing the
\par
clinician's posterior distribution on the range of
\par
equivalence (see page 27) relevant to a particular
\par
patient.421 It has been argued that a Bayesian
\par
model naturally formalises the individual ethical
\par
position,300,345 in that it explicitly confronts the
\par
personal belief in the clinical superiority of one
\par
treatment. Berry,53 however, has suggested that if
\par
patients were honestly presented with numerical
\par
values for their clinician's belief in the superiority
\par
of a treatment, then few might agree to be random-
\par
ised. One option might be to randomise but with
\par
a varying probability that is dynamically weighted
\par
towards the currently favoured treatment: such
\par
adaptive allocation designs are discussed on
\par
page 37.
\par
Kass and Greenhouse267 argue that ``the purpose
\par
of a trial is to collect data that bring to conclusive
\par
consensus at termination opinions that had been
\par
diverse and indecisive at the outset'', and go on
\par
to state that ``randomisation is ethically justifiable
\par
when a cautious reasonable sceptic would be
\par
unwilling to state a preference in favour of either
\par
the treatment or the control''. This approach leads
\par
naturally to the development of sceptical prior
\par
distributions (see page 19) and their use in moni-
\par
toring sequential trials (see page 30).
\par
\textbf{Specification of null hypotheses}
\par
Attention in a trial usually focuses on the null
\par
hypothesis of treatment equivalence expressed by
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
q = 0, but realistically this is often not the only
\par
hypothesis of interest. Increased costs, toxicity and
\par
so on may mean that a certain improvement would
\par
be necessary before the new treatment could be
\par
considered clinically superior, and we shall denote
\par
this value qS. Similarly, the new treatment might
\par
not actually be considered clinically inferior unless
\par
the true benefit were less than some threshold
\par
denoted qI. The interval between qI and qS has
\par
been termed the `range of equivalence'179 (see
\par
Figure 3): often qI is taken to be 0.
\par
This is not a specifically Bayesian idea,22 and there
\par
are several published examples of the elicitation
\par
and use of such ranges of equivalence.172,180
\par
\textbf{Using historical controls}
\par
A Bayesian basis for the use of historical controls
\par
in clinical trials, generally in addition to some
\par
contemporaneous controls, is based on the idea
\par
that it is wasteful and inefficient to ignore all past
\par
information on control groups when making a new
\par
comparison. This was first suggested by Pocock,360
\par
and has since been particularly developed within
\par
the field of carcinogenicity studies.386 The crucial
\par
issue is the extent to which the historical informa-
\par
tion can be considered equivalent to contemp-
\par
oraneous data: essentially the inclusion of histor-
\par
ical controls is indistinguishable from using such
\par
data as the basis for a prior opinion (see page 18).
\par
Five broad approaches have been taken:
\par
1. Assume the historical control individuals are
\par
exchangeable with those in the current control
\par
group, which leads to a complete pooling of
\par
historical with experimental controls.
\par
2. Assume the historical control groups are
\par
exchangeable with the current control group,
\par
and hence build or assume a hierarchical model
\par
for the response within each group.127,440 This
\par
leads to a degree of pooling between the
\par
control groups, depending on their observed or
\par
assumed heterogeneity. Gould199 suggests using
\par
past trials to augment current control group
\par
information, assuming exchangeable control
\par
groups. Rather than directly producing a
\par
posterior distribution on the contrast of interest,
\par
he uses this historical information to derive
\par
predictive probabilities of obtaining a significant
\par
result were a full trial to have taken place (see
\par
page 28) (this paper carefully avoids the term
\par
`Bayesian', and we might suspect this was a delib-
\par
erate policy).
\par
\textbf{27}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
3. Assume that the parameter being estimated in
\par
historical data is some function of the parameter
\par
that is of interest, thus explicitly modelling
\par
potential biases, as in the confidence profile
\par
method150 (see page 49).
\par
Atkinson25 suggests this technique in a context
\par
where the historical control data is available on
\par
a general population, but experimental therapy
\par
is only given to an identifiable subset of new
\par
patients. A posterior distribution for the benefit
\par
of treatment can be obtained by constructing a
\par
model for what would have been the control
\par
response on that subset.
\par
4. If there is only one historical group, then
\par
assume a parameter representing the probability
\par
that any past individual is exchangeable with
\par
current individuals, so discounting the contribu-
\par
tion of past data to the likelihood, as used by
\par
Berry44,45 in reanalysis of the extracorporeal
\par
membrane oxygenation (ECMO) study (see
\par
page 37).
\par
5. Assume a certain prior probability that the
\par
historical control group exactly matches the
\par
contemporaneous controls and hence can be
\par
pooled. Racine et al.370 provide a Bayes's factor
\par
formulation within the context of
\par
bioequivalence studies.
\par
\textbf{28}
\par
\textbf{Design: sample size of}
\par
\textbf{non-sequential trials}
\par
Here we only deal with trials of fixed sample size:
\par
see page 29 for sequential designs.
\par
We described a taxonomy of six different broad
\par
approaches to health technology assessment
\par
studies earlier (see page 14). Here we focus on how
\par
the four main concepts (ignoring the Bayesian
\par
hypothesis testing and the classical decision-theory
\par
approach) deal with selecting the size of an
\par
experiment.
\par
1. Fisherian. In principle there is no need for
\par
preplanned sample sizes, but a choice may be
\par
made by selecting a particular precision of
\par
measurement and informally trading that off
\par
against the cost of experimentation.
\par
2. Neyman--Pearson. Trials have traditionally been
\par
designed to have reasonable power, defined as
\par
the chance of correctly detecting an alternative
\par
hypothesis, best defined as being both `realistic
\par
and important'. Power is generally set to 80 or
\par
90\%.
\par
3. Proper Bayesian. As in the Fisherian approach,
\par
there is in principle no need for preplanned
\par
sample sizes, as pointed out by Lilford et
\par
al.153,300,302 Alternatively, it is natural to focus
\par
on the eventual precision of the posterior
\par
distribution of the treatment effect. There is
\par
an extensive literature on non-power-based
\par
Bayesian sample size calculations which may be
\par
very relevant for trial design.9,247,255,256
\par
Considerable attention has been paid to a
\par
hybrid between Neyman--Pearson and Bayesian
\par
approaches, in which the prior might be used
\par
in the design but not in reporting the analysis.
\par
Thus the requirement for a trial being large
\par
enough to detect a plausible difference naturally
\par
leads to the use of prior distributions: either the
\par
prior mean could be taken as the alternative
\par
hypothesis or the power for each value of q
\par
could be averaged with respect to the prior
\par
distribution to obtain an `expected' or `average'
\par
power, which should be a more realistic assess-
\par
ment of the chance that the trial will yield a
\par
positive conclusion.
\par
Prior distributions can thus be used for sample
\par
size calculations for trials that will be analysed in
\par
a traditional frequentist or Bayesian manner.421
\par
The prior distributions might be from any
\par
of the sources described in chapter 3, and
\par
examples include sets of subjective assess-
\par
ments,150,180,414,418,442 a single previous study,78
\par
or
\par
a meta-analysis of previous results.96,129
\par
Brown78 predicts the chance of correctly
\par
detecting a positive improvement, rather than
\par
the overall chance of getting a positive result,
\par
and this might be considered a more reasonable
\par
target. Predictive power can also be applied to
\par
null hypotheses defined as ranges of equiva-
\par
lence.328 Gould considers nuisance parameters,
\par
for example overall response rate, and uses
\par
historical data200 or interim data201 for power
\par
calculations.
\par
It is natural to express a cautionary note on
\par
projecting from previous studies,273 and possible
\par
techniques for discounting past studies are very
\par
relevant (see page 27).
\par
4. Decision-theoretic Bayesian. If we are willing
\par
to express a utility function for the cost of
\par
experimentation and the potential benefit of
\par
the treatment, then sample sizes can chosen to\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
maximise the expected utility. This was consid-
\par
ered by Canner,88 while Thompson452 estimated
\par
that a trial of electronic foetal monitoring with
\par
180,000 cases per arm would cost US\$22 million
\par
but would be expected to provide a benefit of
\par
US\$118 million. Detsky131 conducted an early
\par
attempt to model the impact of a trial in terms
\par
of future lives saved, which required modelling
\par
beliefs about the future number to be treated
\par
and the true benefit of the treatment, while Tan
\par
and Smith439 fit the ideas in with ranges of equiv-
\par
alence. In other examples,107,238,239 sample sizes
\par
are explicitly determined by a trade-off between
\par
the cost of the trial and the expected future
\par
benefit: see page 39 for some comments on the
\par
consequences. This approach also attempts to
\par
answer the question `what is the expected net
\par
benefit from carrying out the trial?', which is
\par
discussed further on page 52.
\par
Instead of attempting to model the future
\par
benefit of a trial, Lindley305 considers a utility
\par
function based only on the final interval.
\par
\textbf{Design and monitoring of}
\par
\textbf{sequential trials}
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Whether or not to stop a trial early is a complex
\par
ethical, financial, organisational and scientific
\par
issue, in which statistical analysis plays a consider-
\par
able role. Furthermore the monitoring of
\par
sequential clinical trials can be considered the
\par
`front line' between Bayesian and frequentist
\par
approaches, and Etzioni and Kadane state that the
\par
reasons for their divergence ``reach to the very
\par
foundations of the two paradigms''.162 Pocock,362
\par
O'Brien336 and Whitehead478 provide good
\par
reviews.
\par
Four main statistical approaches can be identified,
\par
again corresponding to the four main entries in
\par
Table 4.
\par
1. Fisherian. This is perhaps best exemplified in
\par
trials influenced by Richard Peto's group, in
\par
which protocols state109 that the data-monitoring
\par
committee should only alert the steering
\par
committee to stop the trial on efficacy grounds
\par
if there is ``both (a) `proof beyond reasonable
\par
doubt' that for all, or for some, types of patient
\par
one particular treatment is clearly indicated\dots,
\par
and (b) evidence that might reasonably be
\par
expected to influence the patient management
\par
of many clinicians who are already aware of the
\par
results of other main studies''.
\par
2. Neyman--Pearson. This classical method
\par
attempts to retain a fixed type I error through
\par
pre-specified stopping boundaries. These may
\par
be considered as stopping guidelines: Demets126
\par
states that ``while they are not stopping rules,
\par
such methods can be useful in the decision-
\par
making process'', although regulatory authori-
\par
ties require good reasons for not adhering to
\par
such boundaries.248 Whitehead476,477 is a major
\par
proponent, and Jennison and Turnbull250
\par
provide a detailed review.
\par
3. Proper Bayesian. Probabilities derived from a
\par
posterior distribution may be used for moni-
\par
toring, without formally prespecifying a stopping
\par
criterion -- see page 30. There is no real need
\par
in this framework even to prespecify a sample
\par
size.53 As for fixed sample size trials, prior distri-
\par
butions have been used at the design stage but
\par
assuming a Neyman--Pearson analysis.180,321
\par
4. Decision-theoretic Bayesian. This assumes we are
\par
willing to explicitly assess the losses associated
\par
with consequences of stopping or continuing
\par
the study (see page 32), and therefore requires
\par
a full specification of the `patient horizon', the
\par
allocation rule and so on. This approach also
\par
quantifies the expected benefit of the trial and
\par
therefore helps decide whether to conduct the
\par
trial at all.
\par
Here we briefly describe some of the huge litera-
\par
ture on this subject.
\par
\textbf{A brief critique of Neyman--Pearson}
\par
\textbf{methods in sequential trials}
\par
As introduced on page 10, the need to adjust
\par
conclusions because the data have been looked
\par
at during the study has been roundly criticised.
\par
Anscombe13 baldly states that ``Sequential analysis is
\par
a hoax'', and Meier323 considers that ``provided the
\par
investigator has faithfully presented his methods
\par
and all of his results, it seems hard indeed to
\par
accept the notion that I should be influenced in
\par
my judgement by how frequently he peeked at
\par
the data while he was collecting it''. The crucial
\par
technical point is that Neyman--Pearson theory
\par
disobeys the likelihood principle (see page 13),
\par
and hence there is no need for Bayesians or
\par
Fisherians to take any account of what would have
\par
happened had something other been observed.50
\par
If we were to assign weights to the relative impor-
\par
tance of the two types of error that could be made,
\par
any resulting design would seek to minimise a
\par
linear combination of the type I error rate a and
\par
type II error rate b. The fact that such a design
\par
\textbf{29}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
\textbf{30}
\par
would obey the likelihood principle led Corn-
\par
field111 to point out that ``the entire basis for
\par
sequential analysis depends upon nothing more
\par
profound than a preference for minimising b for
\par
given a rather than minimizing their linear combi-
\par
nation. Rarely has so mighty a structure, and one
\par
so surprising to scientific common sense, rested on
\par
so frail a distinction and so delicate a preference''.
\par
Freedman177 points out that there is no agreed
\par
method of estimation following a sequential trial,
\par
and Hughes and Pocock244,364 argue that frequentist
\par
sequential rules are ``prone to exaggerate magni-
\par
tude of treatment effect'' since they would tend
\par
to stop when on a random high. However,
\par
Whitehead478 says one can adjust to get rid of such
\par
biases, and Hughes and Pocock are really looking
\par
at inconsistency with clinical opinion.
\par
Armitage17 agrees that adjusted P values are ``too
\par
tenuous to be quoted in an authoritative analysis of
\par
the data'', but still considers frequency properties
\par
of stopping rules may be useful guides for ``mental
\par
adjustment''. Heitjan et al.228 says the loss function
\par
implicit in a sequential analysis reflects a focus on
\par
inference rather than decisions.
\par
Pocock and Hughes364 say that ``control of the
\par
overall type I error is a vital aid to restricting the
\par
flood of false positives in the medical literature'',
\par
but this appears to introduce the extraneous issue
\par
of selective reporting.
\par
From a practical perspective, the responsibility for
\par
recommending the early termination of a trial is
\par
increasingly vested in an independent data and
\par
safety monitoring committee, which will need to
\par
take into account multiple sources of evidence
\par
when making their judgements. Classical sequen-
\par
tial analysis may be a useful warning to them
\par
against overinterpretation of naive P values.
\par
\textbf{Monitoring using the posterior}
\par
\textbf{distribution}
\par
Following the `proper Bayesian' approach, it is
\par
natural to consider terminating a trial when one
\par
is confident that one treatment is better than the
\par
other, and this may be formalised by assessing the
\par
posterior probability that the treatment benefit q
\par
lies above or below some boundary, such as the
\par
ends of the range of equivalence. It is generally
\par
recommended that a range of priors are consid-
\par
ered, and applications have been reported in a
\par
variety of trials.38,51,77,90,129,182,183,191,348,381 Explicit
\par
comparison with boundaries obtained by
\par
frequentist procedures have been displayed,129,181,183
\par
and the similar conservativism noted. Armitage17
\par
and Simon405 discuss the choice of an appropriate
\par
boundary.
\par
The informal stopping procedure described above
\par
explicitly takes into account the impact of the
\par
results on a range of clinical opinion, and so
\par
follow Kass and Greenhouse267 in claiming that a
\par
successful trial should contain sufficient evidence
\par
to bring both a sceptic and an enthusiast to broadly
\par
the same conclusions (see page 26). This may
\par
be formalised by using the concept of sceptical
\par
and enthusiastic priors (see page 19), in which
\par
stopping with a positive result might be considered
\par
if a posterior based on a sceptical prior suggested
\par
a high probability of treatment benefit, whereas
\par
stopping without a negative result may be based on
\par
whether the results were sufficiently disappointing
\par
to make a posterior based on an enthusiastic prior
\par
rule out a treatment benefit: in other words we
\par
should stop if we have convinced a reasonable
\par
adversary that he or she is wrong. Fayers et al.166
\par
provide a tutorial on such an approach, and
\par
Freedman et al.178 consider it as part of an exercise
\par
for a data-monitoring committee. Digman et al.133
\par
give an example in which the data have over-
\par
whelmed an optimistic prior centred on a 40\% risk
\par
reduction, and hence justifies assuming a negative
\par
result and early stopping.
\par
Greenhouse and Wasserman206 and Carlin and
\par
Sargent93,388 consider stopping rules based on
\par
`robust priors': Carlin89 argues that this enables
\par
statements of the form ``given the data so far, the
\par
prior would have to place a mass of at least p on
\par
the range where the new treatment is considered
\par
superior, in order to avoid stopping now and
\par
rejecting this treatment as inferior''. Emerson159
\par
retrospectively examines what class of priors would
\par
have been needed to replicate stopping used in a
\par
study (see page 20). Posterior probabilities of two
\par
responses can be monitored jointly, and stopping
\par
considered when an event of interest, such as
\par
either outcome occurring,161 exceeds a certain
\par
threshold.
\par
A specific problem occurs when additional infor-
\par
mation becomes available as a trial is continuing,
\par
such as the publication of similar studies. Brophy
\par
and Joseph77 argue that this is a good opportunity
\par
for Bayesian methods, generating considerable
\par
discussion271 and letters.
\par
This monitoring scheme has also been proposed
\par
for single-arm studies, and is discussed within
\par
the context of Phase I and II trials (see page 38).
\par
Criticisms of this procedure include its sampling\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
