\par
\textbf{Monitoring using predictions}
\par
Investigators and funders are often concerned
\par
with the question `Given the data so far, what is
\par
the chance of getting a `significant' result?' The
\par
traditional approach to this question is `stochastic
\par
curtailment',221 which calculates the conditional
\par
power of the study, given the data so far, for a
\par
range of alternative hypotheses.250
\par
Digman et al.133 point out that it is not, however,
\par
reasonable to condition on a hypothesis that is no
\par
longer tenable. From a Bayesian perspective it is
\par
natural to average such conditional powers with
\par
respect to the current posterior distribution, just as
\par
the pretrial power was averaged with respect to the
\par
prior to produce the average or expected power
\par
(see page 28). The methodology has been illus-
\par
trated in a variety of contexts103,104,286,368,419 in which
\par
initial trial results are used to predict the
\par
probability of eventually gaining `significance'.
\par
Since data are available at an interim stage a mini-
\par
mally informative pretrial prior may be used, and
\par
hence the method does not strictly speaking
\par
require a proper Bayesian justification. Armitage22
\par
points out circumstances in which the predictions
\par
can be based on a pivotal quantity that does not
\par
depend on the parameter, and Lan and Wittes's278
\par
`B value' enables calculation of predictive
\par
probability of significance. Chang and Shuster100
\par
use non-parametric predictions of survival
\par
times without a prior, while Frei et al.185 and
\par
Hilsenbeck234 provide practical examples of
\par
stopping studies due to the futility of continuing.
\par
The fact that predicted probabilities of success are
\par
often surprisingly low has been emphasised and is
\par
shown in Figure 4.
\par
420 The chance that the result will
\par
even change sign may also be reported.48 The tech-
\par
nique has been used with results that currently
\par
show approximate equivalence to justify the `futil-
\par
ity' of continuing,470 and may be particularly useful
\par
for data-monitoring committees and funders275
\par
\textbf{31}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
\textbf{32}
\par
when accrual or event rates are lower than
\par
expected.5
\par
Nevertheless, Armitage17,18,22 warns against using
\par
this predictive procedure as any kind of formal
\par
stopping rule as it gives an undue weight to `signifi-
\par
cance', and makes strong assumptions about the
\par
direct comparability of future data with those
\par
already observed -- for example if future data
\par
involve extended follow-up there may be undue
\par
reliance on an assumption of proportional
\par
hazards.421
\par
\textbf{Monitoring using a formal loss function}
\par
The full Bayesian decision-theoretic approach
\par
requires the specification of losses associated with
\par
all combinations of possible true underlying states
\par
and all possible actions. The decision whether
\par
to terminate a trial is then, in theory, based on
\par
whether termination has a lower expected loss than
\par
continuing, where the expectation is with respect
\par
to the current posterior distribution, and the
\par
consequences of continuing have to consider all
\par
possible future actions -- this `backwards induction'
\par
requires the computationally intensive technique
\par
of `dynamic programming'.
\par
Reasonably straightforward solutions can be found
\par
in some circumstances. For example, Anscombe13
\par
considers n pairs of patients randomised equally
\par
to two groups, a total patient horizon of N, a
\par
uniform prior on true treatment benefit, and loss
\par
function proportional to the number of patients
\par
given the inferior treatment times the size of
\par
the inferiority: he concludes it is approximately
\par
optimal to stop and give the `best to the rest'
\par
when the standard one-sided P value is less than
\par
n/N -- half the proportion of patients already
\par
randomised. Berry and Pearson60 and others59,120,228
\par
have extended such theory to allow for unequal
\par
stages and so on. Backwards induction is extremely
\par
computationally demanding, but Carlin et al.91
\par
do a retrospective analysis on a trial,90 and claim
\par
it is computationally feasible using MCMC
\par
methods, in which forward sampling is used as
\par
an approximation to the optimal strategy. There
\par
is also an extensive theoretical literature on trials
\par
designed from a non-Bayesian decision-theoretic
\par
perspective.30
\par
As a worked (but retrospective) example, Berry et
\par
al.64 consider a trial of influenza vaccine for Navajo
\par
children. They construct a model consisting of
\par
priors for the effectiveness of the vaccine and the
\par
placebo treatment, the probability of obtaining
\par
regulatory approval and the length of time taken to
\par
obtain it, and the probability of a superior vaccine
\par
appearing in the next 20 years and the length of
\par
time taken for it to appear. After each month the
\par
expected number of cases of the strain amongst
\par
Navajo children in the next 20 years is calculated
\par
in the case of stopping the trial, and continuing
\par
the trial (the latter being calculated by dynamic
\par
programming). The trial is stopped when the
\par
former exceeds the latter.
\par
The level of detail required for such an analysis
\par
has been criticised as being unrealistic,72,421 but it
\par
has been argued that trade-offs between benefits
\par
for patients within and outside the trial should be
\par
explicitly confronted.162 See page 39 for further
\par
discussion.
\par
A recent development is reported by Kadane et
\par
al.,
\par
264 who are to be allowed to elicit prior distribu-
\par
tions and utilities from members of the data-
\par
monitoring committee for a large collaborative
\par
cancer trials group National Surgical Adjuvant
\par
Breast and Bowel Project (NSABP). They intend to
\par
do individual elicitations using predictive methods
\par
(see page 17) and use the forward sampling
\par
approach to solve the dynamic programming
\par
problem.91 Their success at this ambitious venture
\par
remains to be seen.
\par
\textbf{Frequentist properties of sequential}
\par
\textbf{Bayesian methods}
\par
Although the long-run sampling behaviour of
\par
sequential Bayesian procedures is irrelevant from
\par
a strict Bayesian perspective, a number of investi-
\par
gations have taken place which generally show
\par
good sampling properties.236,293,294,381 In particular,
\par
Grossman et al.217 show that a sceptical prior (see
\par
page 19), centred on zero and with precision equiv-
\par
alent to that arising from an `imaginary' trial of
\par
around 26\% of the planned sample size, gives rise
\par
to boundaries that have type I error around 5\%
\par
for five interim analyses, with good power and
\par
expected sample size. Thus an `off-the-shelf'
\par
Bayesian procedure has been shown to have good
\par
frequentist properties: essentially the conservative
\par
behaviour of a Neyman--Pearson approach is
\par
mirrored by that obtained from assuming a
\par
sceptical prior. The sampling properties of
\par
Bayesian designs has been particularly investigated
\par
in the context of Phase II trials (see page 28).
\par
One contentious issue is `sampling to a foregone
\par
conclusion'.21 This mathematical result proves that
\par
repeated calculation of posterior tail areas will,
\par
even if the null hypothesis is true, eventually lead a
\par
Bayesian procedure to reject that null hypothesis.
\par
This does not, at first, seem an attractive
\par
frequentist property of a Bayesian procedure.Nevertheless, Cornfield111 argued that ``if one is
\par
seriously concerned about the probability that a
\par
stopping rule will certainly result in the rejection
\par
of a null hypothesis, it must be because some
\par
possibility of the truth of the hypothesis is being
\par
entertained'', and if this is the case then they
\par
should be placing a lump of probability on it,
\par
as discussed on page 19, and so fit within the
\par
Bayesian hypothesis testing framework (see
\par
page 11). He shows that if such a lump, however
\par
small, is assumed then the problem disappears in
\par
the sense that the probability of rejecting a true
\par
null hypothesis does not tend to one. Breslow72
\par
agrees with this solution, but Armitage is not
\par
persuaded,16 claiming that even with a continuous
\par
prior distribution with no lump at the null
\par
hypothesis, one might still be interested in type I
\par
error rates at the null as giving a bound to those at
\par
non-null values.
\par
\textbf{The role of `scepticism' in}
\par
\textbf{confirmatory studies}
\par
After a clinical trial has given a positive result for
\par
a new therapy, there remains the problem of
\par
whether a confirmatory study is needed. Fletcher et
\par
al.173 argue that the results of the first trial might be
\par
treated with scepticism, and Berry57 points out that
\par
using a sceptical prior is a means of dealing with
\par
`regression to the mean', in which early extreme
\par
results tend to return to the average over time.
\par
\textbf{Example}
\par
Parmar et al.349 consider two trials and show that,
\par
when using a reasonable sceptical prior, doubt can
\par
remain after both the first trial and the confirma-
\par
tory trial about whether the new treatment
\par
provides a clinically important benefit.
\par
Figure 5 shows an example with a sceptical prior
\par
distribution with a median of 0 months benefit,
\par
which is equivalent to an `imaginary' trial in which
\par
33 patients died on each treatment. The dashed
\par
vertical lines display the null hypothesis of no
\par
improvement and the minimum clinically worth-
\par
while improvement of 4 months: between these lie
\par
what can be termed the `range of equivalence', and
\par
the figure shows that the sceptical prior expresses a
\par
probability of 41\% that the true benefit lies in the
\par
range of equivalence, and only 9\% that the new
\par
treatment is clinically superior.
\par
The likelihood plot shows the inferences to be
\par
made from the data alone, assuming a `uniform'
\par
prior on the range of possible improvements:
\par
Parmar et al. call this an `enthusiastic' prior. The
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
probability that the new treatment is actually
\par
inferior is 0.004 (equivalent to the one-sided P
\par
value of 0.008/2.) The probability of clinical
\par
superiority is 80\%, which might be considered
\par
sufficient to change treatment policy.
\par
The posterior plot shows the impact of the
\par
sceptical prior, in that the chance of clinical
\par
superiority is reduced to 44\% -- hardly sufficient
\par
to change practice. In fact, Parmar et al. report
\par
that the National Cancer Institute (NCI) Inter-
\par
group Trial investigators were unconvinced by the
\par
Cancer and Leukemia Group B (CALGB) trial
\par
due to their previous negative experience, and so
\par
carried out a further confirmatory study. They
\par
found a significant median improvement but of
\par
only 2.4 months, suggesting the sceptical approach
\par
might have given a more reasonable estimate.
\par
\textbf{Reporting, sensitivity analysis, and}
\par
\textbf{robustness}
\par
The only `guidelines' available for reporting
\par
Bayesian analyses appear to be those of Lang and
\par
Secic:281
\par
1. Report the pretrial probabilities and specify how
\par
they were determined.
\par
2. Report the post-trial probabilities and their
\par
probability intervals.
\par
3. Interpret the post-trial probabilities.
\par
These seem very limited: see chapter 8 for an
\par
attempt to set more stringent standards for
\par
reporting.
\par
An integral part of any good statistical report is
\par
a sensitivity analysis of assumptions concerning
\par
the form of the model (the likelihood). Bayesian
\par
approaches have the additional concern of sensi-
\par
tivity to the prior distribution, both in view of its
\par
controversial nature and because it is by definition
\par
a subjective assumption that is open to valid
\par
disagreement. As part of the general discussion
\par
of priors in chapter 3, the need to consider the
\par
impact of a `community of priors'267 was stressed,
\par
and three main types of `community' may be
\par
identified:
\par
1. Discrete set. Many case studies carry out sensi-
\par
tivity analysis to a limited list of possible priors,
\par
possibly embodying scepticism, enthusiasm,
\par
clinical opinion and `ignorance': the studies
\par
described in appendix 1 provide many
\par
examples.
\par
\textbf{33}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
2. Parametric family. O'Rourke343 emphasises that
\par
posterior probabilities ``should be clearly and
\par
primarily stressed as being a `function' of the
\par
prior probabilities and not the probability of
\par
treatment effects''. If the community of priors
\par
can be described by one varying parameter, then
\par
it is possible to graphically display the depend-
\par
ence of the main conclusion to that parameter.
\par
Hughes242 suggested examining sensitivity of
\par
conclusions to priors based on previous trial
\par
results and that reflecting investigators'
\par
opinions, and later243 gives an example which
\par
features a point-mass prior on zero, and an
\par
explicit plot of the posterior probability against
\par
the prior probability of this null hypothesis.
\par
Hughes's approach of plotting prior and against
\par
posterior summaries is an example of the
\par
`robust' Bayesian approach, in which an attempt
\par
is made to characterise the class of priors
\par
leading to a given decision (see appendix 1).
\par
3. Non-parametric family. The `robust' Bayesian
\par
approach has been further explored by allowing
\par
the community of priors to be a non-parametric
\par
family in the neighbourhood of an initial prior
\par
(see page 20). For example, Gustafson219
