\par
plotted, providing a sensitivity analysis. Such
\par
an approach has also been explored by
\par
Greenhouse and Wasserman206 and Carlin
\par
and Sargent.93
\par
Stangl and Berry426 emphasise the need for a
\par
fairly broad community, with sensitivity analysis
\par
not just to the spread of the prior but also its
\par
location, while they also stress that sensitivity to
\par
exchangeability and independence assumptions
\par
should be examined. In fact, the `community'
\par
approach to prior distributions of treatment
\par
effects can also be applied to the distribution
\par
of random effects in hierarchical models: for
\par
example, Gustafson219 considers a non-parametric
\par
approach to the effect of non-normality in a
\par
random effects distribution. Stangl and Berry426
\par
finish by saying that while sensitivity analysis is
\par
important, it should not serve as a substitute for
\par
careful thought about the form of the prior
\par
distribution.
\par
There is limited experience of reporting such
\par
analyses in the medical literature, and it has been
\par
suggested by Koch,272 Hughes242 and Spiegelhalter
\par
et al.421 that a separate `interpretation' section is
\par
required to display how the data in a study would
\par
add to a range of currently held opinions. It would
\par
be attractive for people to be able to carry out their
\par
own sensitivity analysis to their own prior opinion:
\par
Lehmann and Shachter290 describe a computing
\par
architecture for this, and available software and
\par
web pages are described in appendix 2.
\par
\textbf{Subset analysis}
\par
The discussion on multiplicity on page 12 has
\par
already described how multiple simultaneous infer-
\par
ences may be made by assuming a common prior
\par
distribution with unknown parameters, provided
\par
an assumption of exchangeability (the prior does
\par
not depend on units' identities) is appropriate.
\par
Within the context of clinical trials this has imme-
\par
diate relevance to the issue of estimating treatment
\par
effects in subgroups of patients.
\par
By placing an identical prior on each subgroup,
\par
the unknown between-subgroup variability is essen-
\par
tially estimated from the data. As Cornfield113
\par
points out, this procedure ``leads to 1) pooling
\par
subgroups if the differences among them appear
\par
small, 2) keeping them separate if differences
\par
appear large, and 3) providing intermediate results
\par
for intermediate situations''.
\par
\textbf{35}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
\textbf{36}
\par
Published applications are generally based on
\par
assigning a reference (uniform) prior on the
\par
overall treatment effect, and then assuming the
\par
subgroup-specific deviations from that overall
\par
effect have a common prior distribution with zero
\par
mean. This prior expresses scepticism about widely
\par
differing subgroup effects, although the variability
\par
allowed by the prior is usually estimated from the
\par
data. Thus this specification avoids the need for
\par
detailed subjective input, which may be seen as an
\par
attractive feature. Many applications consider this
\par
an empirical Bayes's procedure123,309,365 which gives
\par
rise to traditional confidence intervals which are
\par
not given a Bayesian interpretation. Donner139
\par
sets out the basic ideas, and Dixon, Simon and
\par
co-authors elaborate the techniques in several
\par
examples.134,135,136,403,405,406 Dixon and Simon134
\par
discuss the reasonableness of the exchangeability
\par
assumption.
\par
\textbf{Example: Bayes's theorem for subset}
\par
\textbf{analysis}
\par
Dixon and Simon135 describe a Bayesian approach
\par
to dealing with subset analysis in a clinical trial in
\par
advanced colorectal cancer. The solid horizontal
\par
lines in Figure 6 show the standardised treatment
\par
effects within a range of subgroups, using tradi-
\par
tional methods for estimating treatment by
\par
subgroup interactions. Four of the 12 intervals
\par
exclude zero, although due to the multiple hypoth-
\par
eses being tested an adjustment technique such as
\par
Bonferroni might be used to decrease the apparent
\par
statistical significance of these findings.
\par
The Bayesian approach is to assume subgroup-
\par
specific deviations from the overall treatment effect
\par
have a prior distribution centred at zero but with
\par
an unknown variability: this variability parameter
\par
is then given its own prior distribution. Since the
\par
degree of scepticism is governed by the variance of
\par
the prior distribution, the observed heterogeneity
\par
of treatment effects between subgroups will influ-
\par
ence the degree of scepticism being imposed.
\par
The resulting Bayesian estimates are shown as
\par
dashed lines in Figure 6. They tend to be pulled
\par
towards each other, due to the prior scepticism
\par
about substantial subgroup-by-treatment interac-
\par
tion effects. Only one 95\% interval now excludes
\par
zero: the subgroup with non-measurable metastatic
\par
disease. Dixon and Simon mention that this was
\par
the conclusion of the original trial, but that the
\par
Bayesian analysis has the advantage of not relying
\par
on somewhat arbitrary adjustment techniques,
\par
being generalisable to any number of subsets, and
\par
provides a unified means of both providing esti-
\par
mates and tests of hypotheses.
\par
\textbf{Multicentre analysis}
\par
Methods for subset analysis (see page 35) naturally
\par
extend to multicentre analysis, in which the centre-
\par
by-treatment interaction is considered as a random
\par
effect drawn from some common prior distribution
\par
with unknown parameters. Explicit estimation of
\par
individual institutional effects may be carried out,
\par
which in turn relates strongly to the methods used
\par
for institutional comparisons of patient outcomes
\par
(see page 44).
\par
There have been numerous examples of this proce-
\par
dure,203,218,409,425,427,428 generally adopting MCMC
\par
techniques due to the intractability of the analyses.
\par
Recent case studies include Gould,202 who provides
\par
BUGS code (see appendix 2) for Gibbs sampling
\par
analysis, and Jones et al.,
\par
251 who compare estima-
\par
tion methods. Matsuyama et al.316 allow a random
\par
centre effect both on baseline hazard and treat-
\par
ment effect, and examine the centres for outliers
\par
using a Student's t prior distribution for the
\par
random effects.
\par
Senn398 discusses the general issue of when a
\par
random-effects model for centre-by-treatment
\par
interaction is appropriate, emphasising the
\par
possible difficulty of interpreting the conclusions,
\par
particularly in view of the somewhat arbitrary defi-
\par
nition of `centre'.
\par
\textbf{Multiple end-points and}
\par
\textbf{treatments}
\par
Multiple end-points in trials can often be of
\par
interest when dealing with, say, simultaneous
\par
concern with toxicity and efficacy. This tends
\par
to occur in early phase studies, and a Bayesian
\par
approach allows one to create a two-dimensional
\par
posterior distribution over toxicity and
\par
efficacy.137,161,451 General random effects models for
\par
more complex situations can be constructed.288
\par
Naturally, a two-dimensional prior is required, and
\par
particular care must be taken over the dependence
\par
assumptions.
\par
A similar situation arises with many treatments: if
\par
one is willing to make exchangeability assumptions
\par
between treatment effects, then a hierarchical
\par
model can be constructed to deal with the multiple
\par
comparison problem. This was proposed long ago
\par
by Waller and Duncan.468 Brant et al.71 update this
\par
procedure by assuming exchangeable treatments
\par
and setting the critical values for the posterior
\par
probabilities of treatment effects by using a\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
decision-theoretic argument based on specifying
\par
the relative losses for type I to type II error.
\par
\textbf{Data-dependent allocation}
\par
\textbf{`Bandit' problems}
\par
So far we have only covered standard
\par
randomisation designs, but a full decision-theoretic
\par
approach to trial design would consider data-
\par
dependent allocation so that, for example, in order
\par
to minimise the number of patients getting the
\par
inferior treatment, the proportion randomised to
\par
the apparently superior treatment could be
\par
increased as the trial proceeded. Such `adaptive'
\par
designs would appear to satisfy ethical consider-
\par
ations for the patients under study303 (see page
\par
25). These designs can pose so-called `bandit'
\par
problems, as they are analogous in theory to a
\par
gambler deciding which arm of a two-armed bandit
\par
to pull in order to maximise the expected return.
\par
An extreme example is Zelen's `play-the-winner'
\par
rule in which the next patient is given the currently
\par
superior treatment, and randomisation is
\par
dispensed with entirely;485 there is an extensive
\par
literature on this and other designs.47
\par
There has been considerable criticism of these
\par
ideas as not being practically rooted in the realities
\par
of clinical trials. Byar et al.86 identify as objections:
\par
(1) responses have to be observed without delay,
\par
(2) adaption depends on a one-dimensional
\par
response, (3) sample sizes may have to be bigger
\par
and (4) patients may not be homogeneous
\par
throughout the trial. Armitage15 and Peto357 add
\par
that clinicians are likely to be unhappy with
\par
adaptive randomisation, the trial will be complex
\par
and may deter recruitment, and estimation of the
\par
treatment contrast will lose efficiency. Palmer and
\par
Rosenberger346 suggest that unbalanced allocation
\par
will make blinding difficult as clinicians may guess
\par
which treatment is `in the lead', but point out that
\par
modern technology is making such designs more
\par
feasible. Finally, Senn398 emphasises that future
\par
patients, greatly outnumbering those in the trial,
\par
would value a more precise treatment estimate.
\par
A careful analysis has been carried out by Berry
\par
and Eick,58 who conclude that such adaptive
\par
designs are more likely to yield a large improve-
\par
ment in the expected number of successful
\par
treatments when a large proportion of patients
\par
with the disease are likely to be in the trial. Tamura
\par
et al.438 report one of the few adaptive clinical trials
\par
to take place, in patients with depressive disorder:
\par
the trial designed by Kadane258 also adapts its
\par
allocation rules, in a somewhat complex way, to the
\par
current evidence.
\par
Although there are specific aspects of adaptive
\par
allocation that cause practical problems, it is
\par
possibly the formulation of a trial as a decision
\par
rather than an inference that leads to most
\par
objections -- see page 39.
\par
\textbf{The ECMO studies}
\par
Two studies of extracorporeal membrane oxygena-
\par
tion (ECMO) have been considered by many
\par
authors. ECMO is a rescue therapy for severe lung
\par
disease in newborns which carries the chance of
\par
greatly improved survival but at the risk of side-
\par
effects. An initial Zelen play-the-winner study was
\par
followed by the Harvard two-phase adaptive study
\par
designed to minimise the number of infants
\par
exposed to an inferior treatment: the design was
\par
to randomise between ECMO and conventional
\par
medical treatment (CMT) in balanced blocks
\par
of four, until one treatment had four deaths, at
\par
which point all subsequent patients were to receive
\par
the currently superior therapy. Nineteen patients
\par
were enrolled in the first phase, with 4/10 deaths
\par
under CMT and 0/9 deaths on ECMO -- in the
\par
second phase,20 patients were randomised to
\par
ECMO and one died. Interest has focused on
\par
what might have been concluded during the first
\par
phase of the study, and Bayesian analyses have
\par
been provided by Ware,469 Berry,45,46,51 Kass and
\par
Greenhouse,267 Greenhouse and Wasserman,206
\par
and Gustafson.219 Each of these is considered in
\par
detail in appendix 1.
\par
Ware469 and his discussants46,47,267 provide a range
\par
of views on the ethics and appropriate analysis of
\par
this trial, and how existing information both about
\par
ECMO and the control therapy could have been
\par
used in formulating a prior opinion.
\par
\textbf{Trial designs other than two}
\par
\textbf{parallel groups}
\par
\textbf{Equivalence trials}
\par
There is a large statistical literature on trials
\par
designed to establish equivalence between
\par
therapies. From a Bayesian perspective the solution
\par
is straightforward: define a region of equivalence
\par
(see page 27) and calculate the posterior proba-
\par
bility that the treatment difference lies in this
\par
range -- a threshold of 95 or 90\% might be chosen
\par
to represent strong belief in equivalence. Several
\par
examples of this remarkably intuitive approach
\par
have been reported.31,72,174,211,314,332,370,394,395 Racine et
\par
\textbf{37}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
al.371 consider two-stage designs, Metzler324 studies
\par
sample size while Lindley308 takes a decision-
\par
theoretic approach.
\par
\textbf{Cross-over trials}
\par
The Bayesian approach to cross-over designs, in
\par
which each patient is given two or more treatments
\par
in an order selected at random, is fully reviewed
\par
by Grieve.212 More recent references concentrate
\par
on Gibbs sampling approaches175 and sensitivity
\par
analysis to different assumptions about carry-over
\par
effects.11,209,210,214,215,370
\par
\textbf{\emph{N}-of-1 trials}
\par
N-of-1 studies can be thought of as repeated cross-
\par
over trials in which interest focuses on the response
\par
of an individual patient, and Zucker et al.488 pool
\par
23 N-of-1 studies using a hierarchical model
\par
producing the standard shrinkage of the individual
\par
conclusions towards the average result. This can
\par
be thought of as an extreme example of the subset
\par
procedure described previously, in which the
\par
subsets have been reduced to individual patients.
\par
\textbf{Factorial designs}
\par
Factorial trials, in which multiple treatments are
\par
given simultaneously to patients in a structured
\par
design, can be seen as another example of multi-
\par
plicity, and hence a candidate for hierarchical
\par
models. Simon and Freedman407 and Miller and
\par
Seaman325 suggest suitable prior assumptions that
\par
avoid the need to decide whether interactions do
\par
or do not exist.
\par
\textbf{38}
\par
\textbf{Other aspects of drug}
\par
\textbf{development}
\par
\textbf{Pharmacokinetics}
\par
The `population' approach to pharmacokinetics, in
\par
which the parameters underlying each individual's
\par
drug clearance curve are viewed as being drawn
\par
from some population, is well established and is
\par
essentially an empirical Bayes procedure. Proper
\par
Bayesian analysis of this problem has been exten-
\par
sively reported by Wakefield and colleagues,372,466
\par
emphasising MCMC methods for estimating both
\par
population and individual parameters, as well as
\par
individualising dose selection.467
\par
\textbf{Phase I trials}
\par
Phase I trials are conducted to determine that
\par
dosage of a new treatment which produces a level
\par
of risk of a toxic response which is deemed to be
\par
acceptable. The primary Bayesian contribution to
\par
the development of methodology for Phase I trials
\par
has been the continuous reassessment method
\par
(CRM) developed by O'Quigley and colleagues.342
\par
In CRM a parameter underlying a dose--toxicity
\par
curve is given a proper prior which is updated
\par
sequentially and used to find the current `best'
\par
estimate of the dosage which would produce the
\par
acceptable risk of a toxic event if given to the
\par
next subject, as well as giving the probability of a
\par
toxic response at the recommended dose at the
\par
end of the trial.340 High sensitivity of the posterior
\par
to the prior distribution187 has been reported in a
\par
similar procedure. Numerous simulations and
\par
modifications of the method have been
\par
proposed.10,102,163,197,222,274,326,329,341,445,479
\par
Etzioni and Pepe161 suggest monitoring a Phase I
\par
trial with two possible adverse outcomes via the
\par
joint posterior distribution of the probabilities of
\par
the two outcomes with frequentist inference at the
\par
end of the trial.
\par
\textbf{Phase II trials}
\par
Phase II clinical trials are carried out in order to
\par
discover whether a new treatment is promising
\par
enough (in terms of efficacy) to be submitted to a
\par
controlled Phase III trial, and often a number of
\par
doses may be compared. Bayesian work has focused
\par
on monitoring and sample size determination.
\par
Monitoring on the basis of posterior probability of
\par
exceeding a desired threshold response rate has
\par
been recommended by Mehta and Cain,322 while
\par
Heitjan227 adapts the proposed use of sceptical and
\par
enthusiastic priors (see page 30) in Phase III
\par
studies. Korn et al.276 consider a Phase II study
\par
which was stopped after three out of four patients
\par
exhibited toxicity; Bring75 and Greenhouse and
\par
Wasserman206 re-examine their problem from a
\par
Bayesian perspective.
\par
Herson231 used predictive probability calculations
\par
to select among designs with high power in
\par
regions of high prior probability. Thall and co-
\par
workers have also developed stopping boundaries
\par
for sequential Phase II studies based on posterior
\par
probabilities of clinically important events,
\par
but where the designs are selected from the
\par
frequentist properties derived from extensive
\par
simulation studies.160,408,444,446,447,448,449,450 However,
\par
Stallard424 has criticised this approach as being
\par
demonstrably suboptimal when evaluated using a
\par
full decision-theoretic model with a monetary loss
\par
function.
\par
Finally, Whitehead and colleagues81,473,474,477 have
\par
taken a full decision-theoretic approach to allo-
\par
cating subjects between Phase II and Phase III
\par
studies. For example, Brunier and Whitehead81\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
