\par
performing or not performing a Phase III trial,
\par
using a cost function which includes consideration
\par
of the costs to future patients if the inferior treat-
\par
ment is eventually used, the power of the possible
\par
Phase III trial (which they assume will be carried
\par
out by frequentist methods), and the costs of
\par
experimentation. They show how to determine,
\par
for given parameter values, the expected cost of
\par
performing a Phase II trial of any particular size,
\par
and thus the optimal size for a trial. (The work is
\par
said to correct earlier work of Sylvester and
\par
Staquet.430,435,436,437)
\par
When faced with selecting among a list of treat-
\par
ments and allocating patients, Pepple and Choi355
\par
have considered two-stage designs, Yao et al.482
\par
deal with screening multiple compounds and
\par
allocating patients within a programme, while
\par
Straus and Simon432 use a prior distribution and
\par
horizon.
\par
\textbf{Phase IV -- safety monitoring}
\par
A considerable literature exists on Bayesian
\par
causality assessment in adverse drug reactions: see
\par
for example Hsu et al.241 and Lanctot et al.279
\par
\textbf{Commentary}
\par
Table 6 briefly summarises some major distinctions
\par
between the Bayesian and the frequentist approach
\par
to trial design and analysis.
\par
\textbf{Use of prior distributions}
\par
As well as the general issues concerning the specifi-
\par
cation and use of prior distributions (see page 22),
\par
specific questions arise in the context of clinical
\par
trials. These include:
\par
1. Whose prior? Pocock366 states that the
\par
``hardened sceptical triallist, the hopeful
\par
clinician and the optimistic pharmaceutical
\par
company will inevitably have grossly different
\par
priors'', while Fisher171 also emphasises the
\par
differing views of participants in health tech-
\par
nology assessment.
\par
As mentioned on page 13, context is an essential
\par
aspect of any assessment, and chapter 7
\par
describes the relevant perspectives of regulatory
\par
authorities and health policy makers.
\par
2. What about type I error? A common objection
\par
to Bayesian methods is their apparent lack of
\par
concern with type I error, with the eventual
\par
certainty of rejecting a true null hypothesis.249
\par
Counter-arguments to this position were given
\par
on page 29 and 32.
\par
\textbf{39}\emph{Randomised controlled trials}
\par
\textbf{40}
\par
3. Stopping rule dependence. A somewhat more
\par
subtle objection, well described by Rosenbaum
\par
and Rubin,380 is that a Bayesian stopping rule
\par
based on posterior tail areas may be over-
\par
dependent on the precise prior distribution.249
\par
A possible response is that Bayesian stopping
\par
should not be based on a strict rule derived
\par
from a single prior, and instead a variety of
\par
reasonable perspectives investigated and a trial
\par
stopped only if there is broad convergence of
\par
opinion.
\par
4. What are the implications for the size of trials?
\par
There is no single implication for trial size.
\par
Matthews317 and Edwards et al.153 have suggested
\par
that small, open, trials fit well into a Bayesian
\par
perspective in which all evidence contributes
\par
and there is no demand for high power to reject
\par
hypotheses.300 Alternatively, monitoring with a
\par
sceptical prior may demand larger than standard
\par
sample sizes in order to convince an archetypal
\par
sceptic about treatment superiority.
\par
The distinction made by Kadane and Seidenfeld263
\par
between `experiments to learn' and `experiments
\par
to prove' appears useful: trial design must naturally
\par
reflect one's objectives and different contexts may
\par
therefore demand differing designs.
\par
Whitehead475.476,478 argues that where a limited
\par
group of investigators are engaged on a project,
\par
for example in a drug development programme,
\par
then a full Bayesian approach with a loss function
\par
may be sensible, but that there is no place for
\par
prior opinions in publicly scrutinised Phase III
\par
studies.
\par
As a final comment, O'Rourke343 suggests that
\par
all methods have arbitrary aspects; a Bayesian
\par
approach has at least the simplicity of collecting
\par
all of this arbitrariness into the prior.
\par
\textbf{Use of a loss function: is a clinical trial}
\par
\textbf{for inference or decision?}
\par
There has been a long and intense dispute about
\par
whether a clinical trial should be considered as a
\par
decision problem, with an accompanying loss
\par
function, or as an inference.
\par
1. A clinical trial should be a decision. Lindley304
\par
categorically states that ``Clinical trials are not
\par
there for inference but to make decisions'',
\par
while Berry54 states that ``deciding whether to
\par
stop a trial requires considering why we are
\par
running it in the first place, and this means
\par
assessing utilities''. Healy223 considers that ``in
\par
my view the main objective of almost all trials
\par
on human subjects is (or should be) a decision
\par
concerning the treatment of patients in the
\par
future''.
\par
Claxton106,107 argues from an economic perspec-
\par
tive that a utility approach to clinical trial design
\par
and analysis is necessary in order to prevent
\par
conclusions based on inferential methods
\par
leading to health or monetary losses. He points
\par
out that ``Once a price per effectiveness unit has
\par
been determined, costs can be incorporated,
\par
and the decision can then be based on
\par
(posterior) mean incremental net benefit
\par
measured in either monetary or effectiveness
\par
terms''. The assumptions that need to be made
\par
about rapid dissemination of superior treat-
\par
ments are reasonable, he claims, since we
\par
should be designing trials that are `best' for a
\par
healthcare system: the need for incentives or
\par
arrangements to persuade decision makers is a
\par
separate issue.
\par
See page 52 where methods for evaluating the
\par
`payback' of health research are discussed.
\par
2. A clinical trial provides an inference. Armitage,15
\par
Breslow,72 Demets,125 Simon402 and O'Rourke343
\par
all describe how it is unrealistic to place clinical
\par
trials within a decision-theoretic context,
\par
primarily because the impact of stopping a trial
\par
and reporting the results cannot be predicted
\par
with any confidence: Peto357 states that ``Bather,
\par
however, merely assumes \dots{} ``it is implicit that
\par
the preferred treatment will then be used for all
\par
remaining patients'' and gives the problem no
\par
further attention! This is utterly unrealistic, and
\par
leads to potentially misleading mathematical
\par
conclusions''. Peto goes on to argue that a
\par
serious decision-theoretic formulation would
\par
have to model subsequent dissemination of
\par
treatment -- attempts to do this will be discussed
\par
on page 52.
\par
3. It depends on the context. Whitehead478 points
\par
out that the theory of optimal decision-making
\par
only exists for a single decision-maker, and
\par
that no optimal solution exists when making
\par
a decision on behalf of multiple parties with
\par
different beliefs and utilities. He therefore
\par
argues that internal company decisions at
\par
Phase I and Phase II of drug development can
\par
be modelled as decision problems, but that
\par
Phase III trials cannot be.476 Koch272 also
\par
provides a non-dogmatic discussion, in which
\par
the relevant approach depends on the question
\par
being asked.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. The Bayesian approach provides a framework
\par
for considering the ethics of randomisation.
\par
2. Monitoring trials with a sceptical and other
\par
priors may provide a unified approach to
\par
assessing whether a trial's results would be
\par
convincing to a wide range of reasonable
\par
opinion, and could provide a formal tool for
\par
data monitoring committees.
\par
3. Various sources of multiplicity can be dealt with
\par
in a unified and coherent way.
\par
4. In contrast to earlier phases of develop-
\par
ment, it is generally unrealistic to formulate a
\par
Phase III trial as a decision problem, except in
\par
circumstances where future treatments can be
\par
accurately predicted.
\par
5. An empirical basis for prior opinions in clinical
\par
trials should be investigated, but archetypal
\par
prior opinions play a useful role.
\par
6. The structure in which trials are conducted must
\par
be recognised, but can be taken into account by
\par
specifying a range of prior opinions.
\par
\textbf{41}\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
