\textbf{Chapter 5}
\par
A guide to the Bayesian health technology
\par
assessment literature: observational studies
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Since the probability models used in Bayesian
\par
analysis do not need to be based on actual
\par
randomisation, non-randomised studies can be
\par
analysed in exactly the same manner as random-
\par
ised comparisons, perhaps with extra attention to
\par
adjusting for covariates in an attempt to control
\par
for possible baseline differences in the treatment
\par
groups with respect to uncontrolled risk factors
\par
or exposures. For example, the results from an
\par
epidemiological study, whether of a cohort or
\par
case--control design, provide a likelihood which
\par
can be combined with prior information using
\par
standard Bayesian methods. The dangers of this
\par
approach have been well described in the medical
\par
literature,86 but nevertheless there are circum-
\par
stances where randomisation is either impossible
\par
or where there is substantial valuable information
\par
available in historical data. There is, of course,
\par
a degree of subjective judgement about the
\par
comparability of groups, which fits well into the
\par
acknowledged judgement underlying all Bayesian
\par
reasoning. It is possible that the effect of unmea-
\par
sured confounders can be modelled by means of
\par
a prior distribution, which could be considered
\par
as part of the explicit modelling of biases discussed
\par
on page 41. In this chapter we consider four
\par
aspects of non-randomised comparisons: case--
\par
control studies, complex epidemiological models,
\par
explicit modelling of biases, and comparisons of
\par
institutions on the basis of their outcomes. The
\par
discussion of historical controls within randomised
\par
trials (see page 27) is also relevant to the situation
\par
in which no contemporaneous controls are
\par
available.
\par
Hodgkin's disease, and consider the consequences
\par
of various prior distributions based on past studies,
\par
possibly downweighted. Lilford and Braunholtz's
\par
tutorial article concerns potential side effects of
\par
oral contraceptives, with likelihoods arising from
\par
case--control studies299 (see appendix 1 for further
\par
details).
\par
\textbf{Case--control studies}
\par
Case--control designs have been considered in
\par
detail by a number of authors,24,313,335,486,487 generally
\par
relying on analytic approximations in order to
\par
obtain reasonably simple analyses. For example,
\par
Ashby et al.24 examine two case--control studies
\par
(one being very small) and a cohort study of
\par
leukaemia following chemotherapy treatment for
\par
\textbf{Complex epidemiological models}
\par
One approach to assessing the value of an inter-
\par
vention is to construct a model for the natural
\par
history of a chronic disease, and predict the
\par
consequences of implementing a specific policy.
\par
Such models can be developed by synthesising
\par
evidence from multiple sources (see chapter 6)
\par
in order to provide a `comprehensive decision
\par
model' for cost-effectiveness analyses. However,
\par
Craig et al.119 point out that predictions based on
\par
such a model require assumptions of parameter
\par
independence which do not need to be made if
\par
estimation and prediction are carried out
\par
simultaneously.
\par
Such simultaneous analysis can be carried out if
\par
a large cohort database is available and the joint
\par
posterior distribution of the parameters of the
\par
model obtained, say through MCMC techniques.
\par
Craig et al.119 describe such an analysis of a
\par
population-based cohort of patients with diabetic
\par
retinopathy in order to evaluate different
\par
screening policies. They construct a Markov model
\par
for transitions between disease states, using reason-
\par
ably non-informative prior distributions and
\par
MCMC estimation.
\par
There is also a substantial literature on Bayesian
\par
methods for complex epidemiological modelling,
\par
particular concerning problems spatial correla-
\par
tion,23,40,226,377 measurement error376 and missing
\par
covariate data.373 Analysis is now almost universally
\par
by MCMC methods, and considerable use has
\par
been made of Bayesian graphical modelling tech-
\par
niques,417 which are further explored in the
\par
Magnesium (see chapter 10) and confidence
\par
profile (see chapter 11) case studies.
\par
\textbf{43}\emph{Observational studies}
\par
\textbf{44}
\par
\textbf{Explicit modelling of biases}
\par
The fullest Bayesian analysis of non-randomised
\par
data for health technology assessment is probably
\par
the confidence profile method of Eddy and
\par
colleagues.150 This is seen primarily as a tool for
\par
evidence synthesis (discussed in chapter 6), but
\par
also emphasises the ability to explicitly model
\par
potential biases that occur both within studies and
\par
in the attempt to generalise studies outside their
\par
target population.
\par
They identify as `biases to internal validity'
\par
those features of a study that may mean that
\par
the effect of interest is not being appropriately
\par
estimated within the circumstances of that
\par
study: these include dilution and contamination
\par
due to those who are offered a treatment not
\par
receiving it (see chapter 11), errors in measure-
\par
ment of outcomes, errors in ascertainment of
\par
exposure to an intervention, loss to follow-up,
\par
and patient selection and confounding in which
\par
the groups differ with respect to measurable
\par
features. These biases may occur singly or in
\par
combination.
\par
`Biases to external validity' concern the ability of
\par
a study to generalise to defined populations or to
\par
be combined with studies carried out on different
\par
groups. These include `population bias' in which
\par
the study and general population differ with
\par
respect to known characteristics, `intensity bias' in
\par
which the `dose' of the intervention is varied when
\par
generalised, and differences in lengths of follow-
\par
up. Finally, when combining studies it is possible
\par
to downweight the likelihood, much as historical
\par
control data can be downweighted when forming
\par
a prior (see page 27).
\par
Eddy et al.150 show how each of these biases can be
\par
given a mathematical formulation, and hence can
\par
be used to adjust the findings of any study. Two
\par
requirements are necessary. First, analytic solu-
\par
tions are rarely possible, and so approximations
\par
or simulations are necessary: chapter 11 shows
\par
how these are now reasonably straightforward.
\par
More serious are the necessary assumptions
\par
required concerning the extent of the biases.
\par
Data may be available on which to base accurate
\par
estimates, but there is likely to be considerable
\par
judgemental input. Any unknown quantity can,
\par
of course, be given a prior distribution, and Eddy
\par
et al. claim this obviates the need for sensitivity
\par
analysis. Rittenhouse378 has argued that explicit
\par
allowance for external biases is necessary for
\par
cost-effectiveness studies.
\par
\textbf{Institutional comparisons}
\par
A classic `multiplicity' problem arises in the use of
\par
performance indicators to compare institutions
\par
with regard to their health outcomes or use of
\par
particular procedures. Analogously to subset
\par
estimation (see page 35) and meta-analysis (see
\par
page 47), hierarchical models can be used to make
\par
inferences based on estimating a common prior
\par
or `population' distribution.195,333 An additional
\par
benefit of using MCMC methods (see page 12) is
\par
the ability to derive uncertainty intervals around
\par
the rank order of each institution.312 Fully Bayesian
\par
methods have also been used in the analysis of
\par
panel agreement data on the appropriateness of
\par
coronary angiography.27
\par
The case study in chapter 12 describes an analysis
\par
of success rates in in vitro fertilisation clinics, in
\par
which Bayesian methods are used both to make
\par
inferences on the true rank of each clinic, as well
\par
as estimating the true underlying success rates with
\par
and without an exchangeability assumption.
\par
\textbf{Commentary}
\par
To date there has been relatively little work done
\par
on Bayesian health technology assessment in an
\par
epidemiological setting compared with that in
\par
randomised controlled trials. One important
\par
reason for this is the often complex regression
\par
models that are used routinely in epidemiology
\par
to, for example, adjust for known confounders,
\par
with corresponding computational difficulties
\par
of Bayesian analysis. The advent of computer-
\par
intensive methods (see page 12) has largely
\par
overcome that problem.
\par
In the future we can expect demands for increas-
\par
ingly complex analyses, such as of institutional
\par
comparisons and epidemiological data concerned
\par
with gene--environment interactions, which will
\par
place great demands on traditional hypothesis
\par
testing and estimation procedures which
\par
were essentially intended for fairly simple low-
\par
dimensional problems. This is likely to lead to
\par
increased demands for Bayesian analyses.
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. Epidemiological studies tend to demand a more
\par
complex analysis than randomised trials.
\par
2. Computer-intensive Bayesian methods in
\par
epidemiology are becoming more common.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
3. There are likely to be increased demands
\par
for Bayesian analyses, particularly in areas
\par
such as institutional comparisons and gene--
\par
environment interactions.
\par
4. The explicit modelling of potential biases in
\par
observational data may be widely applicable
\par
but needs some evidence base in order to be
\par
convincing.
\par
\textbf{45}\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Chapter 6}
\par
A guide to the Bayesian health technology
\par
assessment literature: evidence synthesis
\par
\textbf{Meta-analysis}
\par
Bayesian meta-analysis follows the idea that has
\par
already been explored in other contexts of multi-
\par
plicity, such as subset analysis (see page 35),
\par
multicentre trials (see page 36) and multiple
\par
outcomes (see page 36), in considering each trial
\par
to be a `unit' of analysis within a hierarchical struc-
\par
ture. The `true' treatment effect in each trial is
\par
considered, assuming that the trials are exchange-
\par
able, as a random quantity drawn from some
\par
population distribution. This is exactly the same
\par
as the standard random-effects approach to meta-
\par
analysis,130 but the latter tends to focus on esti-
\par
mating an overall treatment effect while a full
\par
Bayesian approach also concentrates on estimating
\par
trial-specific effects. The Bayesian approach also
\par
requires prior distributions to be specified for the
\par
mean effect size and for the between- and within-
\par
study variances: these will generally be default
\par
`reference' priors, but including the uncertainty of
\par
all the parameters will tend to give wider interval
\par
estimates than a classical random effects analysis.
\par
Sutton et al.434 review the whole area of meta-
\par
analysis and Bayesian methods in particular:
\par
other reviews are provided by Jones,254 Normand334
\par
and Hedges.225
\par
Empirical Bayes approaches have received most
\par
attention in the literature until recently, largely
\par
because of computational difficulties in the use of
\par
fully Bayesian modelling.374,431 However, the full
\par
Bayesian hierarchical model has been investigated
\par
extensively by Dumouchel and colleagues142,143,144,145
\par
and Abrams and Samso4 using analytic approxima-
\par
tions, and also using MCMC methods.327,412 Carlin,94
\par
for example, considers meta-analyses of both
\par
clinical trials and case--control studies; he examines
\par
the sensitivity to choice of reference priors, and
\par
explores checking the assumption of normal
\par
random effects. There have been many compara-
\par
tive studies of the full Bayesian approach,
\par
including trials,379,433,455 and observational
\par
studies.66,433,457 These comparative studies between
\par
different approximations show few substantial
\par
effects: the primary finding is that when there are
\par
few studies, and hence the between-study variability
\par
cannot be accurately estimated from the data
\par
alone, the prior for this parameter becomes impor-
\par
tant and the empirical Bayes approach, in which
\par
the uncertainty about the between-study variability
\par
is ignored, tends to provide intervals that are too
\par
narrow.
\par
It is natural to use a cumulative meta-analysis as
\par
external evidence when monitoring a clinical
\par
trial,230 and cumulative meta-analysis can also be
\par
given a Bayesian interpretation as providing a prior
\par
distribution284 (see page 18): in this situation the
\par
Bayesian approach relies on the assumption of
\par
exchangeability of trials but avoids concerns with
\par
retaining type I error over the entire course of the
\par
cumulative meta-analysis.
\par
Others have investigated relationship of treatment
\par
effect to underlying risk320,393,453 -- see the accompa-
\par
nying case study for an application of this approach
\par
(see chapter 10). Priors on the heterogeneity
\par
parameter were considered in chapter 3: Higgins
\par
and Whitehead233 use proper priors derived from
\par
a series of meta-analyses. Another application is
\par
investigation of publication bias, which has been
\par
modelled by Begg et al.34 and Givens et al.,
\par
194 while
\par
Daniels and Hughes122 pool studies in order to
\par
model a joint distribution of a surrogate end-point
\par
and eventual response.
\par
Sutton et al.434 summarise the potential advantages
\par
of the Bayesian approach to meta-analysis as
\par
including:
\par
1. Unified modelling. The conflict between fixed
\par
and random effect meta-analysis is overcome
\par
by explicitly modelling between-trial variability
\par
(which could be assumed to be small), as well as
\par
allowing regression models for the treatment
\par
effect in each trial.
\par
2. Borrowing strength. As in all areas in which
\par
Bayesian hierarchical modelling is adopted,
\par
the exchangeability assumption leads to each
\par
experimental unit `borrowing' information
\par
from the other units, leading to a shrinkage of
\par
the estimate towards the overall mean, and a
\par
\textbf{47}\emph{Evidence synthesis}
\par
reduction in the width of the interval estimate.
\par
This degree of pooling depends on the
\par
empirical similarity of the estimates from the
\par
individual units.
\par
3. Allowing for all parameter uncertainty. The full
\par
uncertainty from all the parameters is reflected
\par
in the widths of the intervals for the parameter
\par
estimates.
\par
4. Allowing for other sources of evidence. Other
\par
sources of evidence can be reflected in the prior
\par
distributions for parameters, or in pooling
\par
multiple types of study (see below).
\par
5. Allowing direct probability statements. As with
\par
all Bayesian analyses, quantities of interest can
\par
be directly addressed, such as the probability
\par
that the true treatment effect in a typical trial is
\par
greater than 0.
\par
6. Predictions. The ease of making predictions
\par
allows, for example, current meta-analyses to be
\par
used in designing future studies.
\par
\textbf{48}
\par
\textbf{Cross-design synthesis}
\par
The previous section has dealt with meta-analysis
\par
of studies with similar basic design, but a more
\par
general approach allows mixing of different types
\par
of study. Rubin384 emphasises pooling evidence
\par
through modelling in order to ``build and extrapo-
\par
late a response surface'', which models the true
\par
treatment effect conditional on both the design
\par
of the study and on subgroup factors.
\par
As noted when discussing observational studies
\par
(see chapter 5), in some circumstances random-
\par
ised evidence will be less than adequate due to
\par
economic, organisational or ethical consider-
\par
ations.67 Considering all the available evidence,
\par
including that from non-randomised studies, may
\par
then be necessary or advantageous. Droitcour
\par
et al.140 describe the limitations of using either
\par
randomised controlled trials or databases alone, in
\par
that randomised controlled trials may be rigorous
\par
but restricted, whereas databases have a wider
\par
range but may be biased. They introduce what
\par
they term cross-design synthesis, an approach for
\par
synthesising evidence from different sources, with
\par
the aim ``not to eliminate studies of overall low
\par
quality from the synthesis, but rather to provide
\par
the information needed to compensate for specific
\par
weaknesses''.140 Although not a Bayesian approach,
\par
they are following the explicit modelling of biases
\par
considered by the confidence profile method (see
\par
pages 44, and 49), and work on generalising the
\par
results of clinical trials for broader populations.
\par
Cross-design synthesis was outlined in a report
\par
from the US General Accounting Office,337 but a
\par
Lancet editorial152 was critical of this approach,
\par
suggesting it would deflect attention from carrying
\par
out serious controlled trials: this was denied in a
\par
subsequent reply.101 A review by Begg33 suggested
\par
they had underestimated the difficulty of the task,
\par
and appeared to assume that randomised trials and
\par
databases could be reconciled by statistical adjust-
\par
ments, whereas selection biases and differences in
\par
experimental rigour could not be eliminated so
\par
easily.
\par
It is natural to take a Bayesian approach to the
\par
synthesis of multiple trial designs, and a hierar-
\par
chical model can specifically allow for the quanti-
\par
t ative within- and between-sources heterogeneity,
\par
and for a priori beliefs regarding qualitative differ-
\par
ences between the various sources of evidence. A
\par
full Bayesian version of cross-design synthesis was
\par
subsequently applied to data on breast cancer
\par
screening.3,411 The concept of combining different
\par
types of study in a model has also been termed
\par
`grouped meta-analysis'; Li and Begg296 combine
\par
controlled and uncontrolled studies, Larose and
\par
Dey283 integrate open and closed studies within a
\par
single model, while Dominici et al.138 pool open
\par
and closed studies on migraine using a graphical
\par
model, different treatment contrasts and different
\par
designs. Muller et al.330 combine case--control and
\par
prospective studies.
\par
Other examples include Berry et al.,
\par
62 who consider
\par
a complex synthesis of studies concerning breast
\par
cancer therapy, facing up to issues such as
\par
unplanned analyses, multiple variables, lack of
\par
exchangeability across and within studies, and the
\par
problem of convincing practitioners on the basis
\par
of such a complex analysis. Belin et al.35 combine
\par
observational databases in order to evaluate inter-
\par
ventions to increase screening rates, needing
\par
to impute missing data in some studies. Such
\par
integrated analyses naturally lead on to the
\par
`comprehensive decision models' discussed in
\par
chapter 7.
\par
It has yet to be established when such analyses are
\par
appropriate, as there is concern that including
\par
studies with poorer designs will weaken the
\par
analysis, though this issue is partially addressed
\par
by conducting sensitivity analyses under various
\par
assumptions. However, an example of such a
\par
synthesis is provided in the context of regulatory
\par
approval of medical devices (see page 53).\textbf{Confidence profile method}
\par
This approach was developed by Eddy and
\par
colleagues and promulgated in a book with
\par
numerous worked examples and accompanying
\par
software,150 as well as tutorial articles.146,148,149,151,399
\par
They use directed conditional independence
\par
graphs to represent the qualitative way in which
\par
multiple contributing sources of evidence relate to
\par
the quantity of interest, explicitly allowing the user
\par
to discount studies due to their potential internal
\par
bias or their limited generalisability (see page 44).
\par
Their analysis is essentially Bayesian, although it is
\par
possible to avoid specification of priors and use
\par
only the likelihoods.
\par
The software for carrying out the confidence
\par
profile method, FAST*PRO, has been used in
\par
meta-analysis of the benefits of antibiotic therapy,28
\par
mammography of those aged under 50 years147 and
\par
angioplasty.8
\par
The need to make explicit subjective judgements
\par
concerning the existence and extent of possible
\par
biases, and the limited capacity and friendliness of
\par
the software, has perhaps restricted the application
\par
of this technique. However, we show in chapter 11
\par
that modern software can allow straightforward
\par
implementation of their models promulgated by
\par
Eddy and co-workers.
\par
\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. A unified Bayesian approach appears to be
\par
applicable to a wide range of problems
\par
concerned with evidence synthesis.
\par
2. In the past, prospective evaluation of clinical
\par
interventions concentrated on randomised
\par
controlled trials, but more recent interest
\par
has focused on more diffuse areas, such as
\par
healthcare delivery or broad public health
\par
measures. This means methods that can
\par
synthesise totality of evidence are required,
\par
for example in assessing medical devices.
\par
3. Evaluations of current technologies may often
\par
be seen as unethical subjects for randomised
\par
controlled trials, and hence modelling of
\par
available evidence is likely to be necessary.
\par
4. Perhaps one reason for lack of uptake is that
\par
syntheses are not seen as `clean' methods, with
\par
each analysis being context-specific, less easy to
\par
set quality markers for, easier to criticise as
\par
subjective and so on.
\par
5. Priors for the degree of `similarity' between
\par
alternative designs can be empirically informed
\par
by studies comparing the results of randomised
\par
controlled trials and observational data.
\par
\textbf{49}\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Chapter 7}
\par
A guide to the Bayesian health technology
\par
assessment literature: strategy, decisions
\par
and policy making
\par
\textbf{Contexts}
\par
Throughout this report we have emphasised that it
\par
was vital to take into account the context of health
\par
technology assessment is being made. The appro-
\par
priate prior opinions, and the possibility of explicit
\par
loss functions, depend crucially on whose behalf
\par
any analysis is being reported or decision being
\par
made.
\par
In this chapter we specifically address this issue
\par
using the broad categories of `actors' introduced in
\par
chapter 2:
\par
{$\bullet$} Sponsors, for example the pharmaceutical
\par
industry, medical charities or granting agencies.
\par
In deciding whether to fund studies, they will be
\par
concerned with the potential `payback' from
\par
research (see page 52), which within industry
\par
takes the form of a drug development
\par
programme.
\par
{$\bullet$} Investigators, that is, those responsible for the
\par
conduct of a study, whether industry or publicly
\par
funded. In previous chapters we have focused
\par
primarily on those carrying out a single study,
\par
who are primarily concerned with the accuracy
\par
of the inferences to be drawn from their work.
\par
{$\bullet$} Reviewers, for example regulatory bodies
\par
(see page 53) or journal editors. They will be
\par
concerned with the appropriateness of the infer-
\par
ences drawn from the studies, and so may adopt
\par
their own prior opinions and reporting
\par
standards (see page 33).
\par
{$\bullet$} Consumers, for example agencies setting health
\par
policy, clinicians or patients. Healthcare organi-
\par
sations may be concerned with the cost-
\par
effectiveness of an intervention (see page 51)
\par
although the sponsor or investigator may carry
\par
out this analysis on their behalf. Decisions about
\par
health policy, whether at a community or indi-
\par
vidual level, may involve explicit consideration
\par
of costs and benefits (see page 54) -- here we
\par
distinguish between those based only on prior
\par
opinions (forming a decision analysis), and
\par
those requiring a full Bayesian statistical analysis.
\par
In the remainder of this chapter we examine the
\par
potential Bayesian contribution to these different
\par
perspectives.
\par
\textbf{Cost-effectiveness within trials}
\par
The traditional tool for dealing with uncertainty in
\par
cost-effectiveness analysis has been sensitivity
\par
analysis, although there has been considerable
\par
recent work on developing classical confidence
\par
intervals for cost-effectiveness ratios. This area has
\par
recently been reviewed by Briggs and Gray,74 who
\par
mention the use of probabilistic sensitivity analysis,
\par
in which prior probability distributions are placed
\par
over uncertain inputs into the analysis and the
\par
resulting distribution of potential cost-effectiveness
\par
ratios is generated by simulation. This is a partic-
\par
ular case of a Bayesian method: the general way in
\par
which uncertainty is handled by the Bayesian
\par
approach has been emphasised by Manning et al.311
\par
and Jones.252,253
\par
From a technical perspective, Grieve216 shows
\par
how pharmaco-economics naturally gives rise to
\par
a bivariate posterior distribution of costs and
\par
effectiveness, which can be plotted and from
\par
which the probability of specific conclusions may
\par
be obtained. Heitjan et al.229 illustrate this bivariate
\par
approach with a number of examples, while
\par
Briggs73 avoids the problem of possible zero
\par
denominators in the cost-effectiveness ratio by
\par
working directly with the prior and likelihood for
\par
net benefit relative to a specified baseline ratio.
\par
Luce and Claxton310 present a strong argument
\par
that Bayesian methods should be applied in cost-
\par
effectiveness studies and pharmaco-economics in
\par
general. They point out that hypothesis testing is
\par
of limited relevance in economic studies, and that
\par
additional evidence outside a study is likely to be
\par
relevant. Furthermore, when a cost-effectiveness
\par
analysis is being used as one of the inputs into a
\par
\textbf{51}\emph{Strategy, decisions and policy making}
\par
formal decision concerning drug regulation
\par
or health policy, then they recommend a full
\par
decision-theoretic approach in which an explicit
\par
loss function of the decision maker is assessed.
\par
This view has led to a Bayesian initiative in
\par
pharmacoeconomics (see appendix 2). Felli and
\par
Hazen168,169 extend this utility perspective to sensi-
\par
tivity analysis, suggesting that an analysis should be
\par
considered sensitive to a particular uncertain input
\par
if the expected gain in utility from eliminating
\par
the uncertainty about that input exceeds a certain
\par
specified threshold. The use of such an expected
\par
value of perfect information (EVPI) approach has
\par
also been recommended when deciding research
\par
priorities (see page 52).
\par
Finally, Rittenhouse378 suggests that trial results
\par
may need to be adjusted to in order to generalise
\par
the cost-effectiveness analysis to other populations
\par
of interest. This essentially is concerned with the
\par
type of adjustments used in cross-design synthesis
\par
(see page 48) and the explicit modelling of biases
\par
in observational studies (see page 44).
\par
\textbf{52}
\par
\textbf{Cost-effectiveness of carrying out}
\par
\textbf{trials -- `payback models'}
\par
\textbf{Research planning in the public sector}
\par
Any organisation funding clinical trials must
\par
make decisions concerning the relative importance
\par
of alternative proposals, and there have been
\par
increased efforts to measure the potential
\par
`payback' of expenditure on research. Buxton and
\par
Hanney83 review the issues and propose a staged
\par
semi-quantitative structure, while Eddy146 suggests
\par
a fully quantitative model based on assessing
\par
the future numbers to benefit and the expected
\par
benefit, with a subjective probability distribution
\par
over the potential benefits to be shown by the
\par
research. However, Eddy's146 limited approach was
\par
not adopted by its sponsors, the US Institute of
\par
Medicine, who preferred a less structured model
\par
that employed weights.
\par
It is clearly possible to extend this broad approach
\par
to increasingly sophisticated models within a
\par
Bayesian framework, and Hornberger and
\par
Eghtesady state that ``by explicitly taking into
\par
consideration the costs and benefits of a trial,
\par
Bayesian statistical methods permit estimation
\par
of the value to a healthcare organisation of
\par
conducting a randomised trial instead of contin-
\par
uing to treat patients in the absence of more
\par
information''.238 Clearly this is a particular example
\par
of a decision-theoretic Bayesian approach, applied
\par
at the planning stage of a trial (see page 28) rather
\par
than at interim analyses (see page 32). Examples
\par
given on page 28 by Detsky,131 Hornberger238,239
\par
and others explicitly calculate the expected utility
\par
of a trial in order to select sample sizes, and such
\par
calculations can also, in theory, be used to rank
\par
studies that are competing for resources, and
\par
hence to decide whether the trial is worth doing
\par
in the first place.
\par
Detsky's early analysis131 assumed that a trial
\par
would need to achieve statistical significance in
\par
order to have an impact on future treatments, but
\par
Claxton106 strongly argues that dependence on
\par
such inferential methods, whether classical or
\par
Bayesian, will lead to suboptimal use of health
\par
resources. He recommends a full decision-
\par
theoretic approach to both fixed107 and sequen-
\par
tial106 trials, basing his analysis on a univariate
\par
scale comprising the net benefit relative to a
\par
prespecified standard measure of effectiveness
\par
per unit cost. The EVPI must be higher than the
\par
cost of research in order to pass the first `hurdle'
\par
for a proposed programme to overcome, and
\par
the expected value of sample information (EVSI)
\par
(essentially the EVPI allowing for the sampling
\par
error of a trial) must exceed the sample costs
\par
to overcome the hurdle for a specific proposed
\par
trial. This model allows for unbalanced allocation
\par
of patients between arms, and the ability to
\par
revise design based on interim analyses,452 in
\par
order to optimise the expected net benefit from
\par
sampling (ENBS), which is the EVSI minus
\par
sample costs.
\par
The standard criticisms of decision-theoretic
\par
approaches to trials apply (see page 40), particu-
\par
larly regarding unrealistic assumptions concerning
\par
the impact of research results (which may not
\par
even be `significant') on clinical practice. Claxton
\par
replies that the first step should be to establish a
\par
normative framework that best meets the needs of
\par
a system, and separately to conduct studies to see
\par
how to get the research into practice.106
\par
More generally, the role of decision analysis in
\par
all aspects of health services research has been
\par
emphasised by Lilford and Royston.298
\par
\textbf{Research planning in the}
\par
\textbf{pharmaceutical industry}
\par
Berry49,59 has stressed the decision-theoretic
\par
approach to sequential trial design as being
\par
relevant to a a pharmaceutical company seeking to
\par
maximise profit. However, here we are concerned
\par
with a whole research programme in which there
\par
are multiple competing projects at different stages\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
of drug development. It is natural that the pharma-
\par
ceutical industry would wish to organise its
\par
programme in a cost-effective way, and Bergman
\par
and Gittins39 review quantitative approaches to
\par
planning a pharmaceutical research programme.
\par
Many of the proposed methods are sophisticated
\par
uses of bandit theory in order to allocate resources
\par
in a dynamically changing environment, but
\par
Senn396,398 suggests a fairly straightforward scheme
\par
based on the Pearson index, which is the expected
\par
net present value divided by expected net present
\par
costs. He discusses the difficulties of eliciting
\par
suitable probabilities for the success of each stage
\par
of a drug development programme, conditional
\par
on the success of the previous stage, but suggests
\par
formal Bayesian approaches involving subjective
\par
probability assessment and belief revision should
\par
be investigated in this context.
\par
\textbf{The regulatory perspective}
\par
\textbf{Regulation of pharmaceuticals}
\par
Regulatory bodies have a duty to protect the public
\par
from unsafe or ineffective therapies, and increas-
\par
ingly are taking on responsibility for assuring cost-
\par
effectiveness.
\par
Opinions on the relevance of Bayesian methods to
\par
drug or device regulation cover a broad spectrum:
\par
Whitehead478 and Koch272 see any use of priors as
\par
being controversial and inappropriate, while on
\par
the other hand Matthews319 claims that the use of
\par
sceptical priors ``should not be optional but manda-
\par
tory''. Keiding269 criticises the ``ritual dances''
\par
currently prescribed for regulation, but wonders
\par
whether Bayesian methods will allow anything less
\par
ridiculous. Claxton105 suggests that agencies take
\par
on a full decision-theoretic approach to regulation,
\par
that evaluates the expected value of further investi-
\par
gation in order to assess whether sufficient
\par
evidence is available to permit approval. O'Neill,339
\par
as a senior US FDA statistician, acknowledges the
\par
appropriate conservatism arising out of the use
\par
of sceptical priors, and considers that Bayesian
\par
methods should be investigated in parallel with
\par
other techniques.
\par
The website of the FDA allows one to search for
\par
references to Bayesian methods among their
\par
published literature (see appendix 2). Much of the
\par
discussion concerns medical devices (see page 53).
\par
Guidelines for population pharmacokinetics are
\par
provided,462 which can be thought of as an empir-
\par
ical Bayes procedure (see page 38). There is also
\par
an interesting use of a Bayesian argument in the
\par
approval of the drug enoxaparin (Lovenox\textregistered ). The
\par
transcript of the Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs
\par
Advisory Committee meeting on 26 June 1997461
\par
shows the pharmaceutical company had been
\par
asked to make a statement about the effectiveness
\par
of enoxaparin plus aspirin as compared to placebo
\par
(aspirin alone), whereas their clinical trial had
\par
used an active control of heparin plus aspirin.
\par
They therefore used meta-analysis data comparing
\par
heparin plus aspirin versus aspirin alone in order
\par
to produce a posterior distribution on the treat-
\par
ment comparison of interest: an example of
\par
cross-study inference (see page 7.2). Analyses
\par
were repeated using the meta-analysis data
\par
directly, but also expressing scepticism about its
\par
relevance and reducing its influence, with results
\par
being expressed as posterior probabilities of treat-
\par
ment superiority over placebo. The committee
\par
welcomed this analysis and voted to approve the
\par
drug.
\par
It is important to note that the latest international
\par
statistical guidelines for pharmaceutical submis-
\par
sions to regulatory agencies state that ``the use of
\par
Bayesian and other approaches may be considered
\par
when the reasons for their use are clear and when
\par
the resulting conclusions are sufficiently robust''.248
\par
Unfortunately, they do not go on to define what
\par
they mean by clear reasons and robust conclusions,
\par
and so it is still open as to what will constitute an
\par
appropriate Bayesian analysis for a pharmaceutical
\par
regulatory body.
\par
\textbf{Regulation of medical devices}
\par
The greatest enthusiasm for Bayesian methods
\par
appears to be in the FDA Center for Devices and
\par
Radiological Health (CDRH). They co-sponsored
\par
a workshop on Bayesian methods in November
\par
1998, and are currently proposing to produce the
\par
document Statistical Guidance on Bayesian Methods in
\par
Medical Device Clinical Trials.
\par
460
\par
Campbell recently described the potential for
\par
Bayesian methods in assessing medical devices,87
\par
emphasising that devices differed from
\par
pharmaceuticals in having better understood
\par
physical mechanisms, which meant that effective-
\par
ness was generally robust to small changes. Since
\par
devices tended to develop in incremental steps,
\par
a large body of relevant evidence existed, and
\par
companies did not tend to follow established
\par
phases of drug development. The fact that an
\par
application for approval might include a variety of
\par
studies, including historical controls and registries,
\par
suggests that Bayesian methods for evidence
\par
synthesis might be appropriate. However, the
\par
standard conditions apply that the source and
\par
robustness of the prior information must be
\par
\textbf{53}\emph{Strategy, decisions and policy making}
\par
assessed, and that Bayesian analysis does not
\par
compensate for poor science and experimental
\par
design.
\par
Campbell draws attention to the Transcan Breast
\par
Scanner\textregistered, which was approved by the CDRH in
\par
April 1999.463 A primary `intended use' study on
\par
72 women was supplemented by two additional
\par
studies of differing designs, using a hierarchical
\par
multinomial logistic regression model with study
\par
introduced as a random effect. MCMC simulation
\par
methods were used by means of the BUGS
\par
software.423
\par
\textbf{Policy making and `comprehensive}
\par
\textbf{decision modelling'}
\par
The primary advantage of a Bayesian approach is
\par
that it allows the synthesis of all available sources of
\par
evidence, whether from RCTs, databases or expert
\par
judgement, into a single model that can then be
\par
used to evaluate the cost-effectiveness of alternative
\par
policies. The approach has been termed `compre-
\par
hensive decision modelling', and can be thought
\par
of as extending the evidence synthesis methods
\par
described in chapter 6 to allow for costs in partic-
\par
ular and utilities in general.
\par
Parmigiani and colleagues350,352 apply this idea to
\par
screening for breast cancer, in which many sources
\par
of evidence are brought together in a single model
\par
that predicts the consequences of alternative
\par
screening policies, while Cronin et al.121 use micro-
\par
simulation at the level of the individual patient to
\par
\textbf{54}
\par
predict the consequences of different policy deci-
\par
sions on lowering expected mortality from prostate
\par
cancer. Samsa et al.387 consider ischaemic stroke,
\par
and construct a model for natural history using data
\par
from major epidemiological studies, and a model
\par
for the effect of interventions based on databases,
\par
meta-analysis of trials and Medicare claim records.
\par
They also use micro-simulation of the long-term
\par
consequences of different stroke prevention
\par
policies in order to compare their cost-effectiveness:
\par
Matchar, Parmigiani and colleagues315,351,353 consider
\par
further use of the stroke prevention policy model
\par
(SPPM), developed under the auspices of the
\par
Stroke PORT (Patient Outcomes Research Team).
\par
\textbf{Key points}
\par
1. A Bayesian approach allows explicit recognition
\par
of multiple perspectives.
\par
2. Increased attention to pharmaco-economics
\par
may lead decision-theoretic models for research
\par
planning to be explored, although this will not
\par
be straightforward.
\par
3. There appears to be great potential for formal
\par
methods for planning in the pharmaceutical
\par
industry.
\par
4. The regulation of devices is leading the way in
\par
establishing the role of evidence synthesis.
\par
5. `Comprehensive decision modelling' is likely to
\par
become increasingly important in policy making.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Chapter 8}
\par
BayesWatch: a Bayesian checklist for health
\par
technology assessment
\par
In this chapter we present a checklist against
\par
which published accounts of Bayesian assess-
\par
ments of health technologies can be compared. We
\par
aim to ensure that an account which adequately
\par
contains all the points mentioned here would have
\par
the property the analysis could be replicated by
\par
another investigator who has access to the full data.
\par
These guidelines should be seen as complementary
\par
to the CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of
\par
Reporting Trials) guidelines, in that they focus on
\par
those aspects crucial to an accountable Bayesian
\par
analysis, in addition to standard sections
\par
concerning the technology, the design and the
\par
results.
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
1. The technology. The intervention to be
\par
evaluated must, of course, be clearly described
\par
with regard to the population of interest and so
\par
on.
\par
2. Objectives of study. It is important that a clear
\par
distinction is made between desired inferences
\par
on any quantity or quantities of interest, repre-
\par
senting the parameters to be estimated, and any
\par
decisions or recommendations for action that
\par
are to made subsequent to the inferences. The
\par
former will require a prior distribution, while
\par
the latter will require explicit or implicit consid-
\par
eration of a loss function/utility.
\par
\textbf{Methods}
\par
1. Design of study. This is a standard requirement,
\par
but when synthesising evidence, particular
\par
attention will be necessary to the similarity of
\par
studies in order to justify assumptions of
\par
exchangeability.
\par
2. Statistical model. The probabilistic relationship
\par
between the parameter(s) of interest and the
\par
observed data should be explicitly described.
\par
The relationship should either be given
\par
mathematically, or its structure should be
\par
described in such a way as to allow its mathemat-
\par
ical form to be unambiguously obtained by a
\par
competent reader. If this likelihood has been
\par
obtained by a method of model selection,
\par
whether Bayesian or not, this should be stated
\par
and the method described.
\par
3. Prospective analysis? It needs to be made clear
\par
whether the prior and any loss function were
\par
constructed preceding the data collection, and
\par
whether analysis was carried out during the
\par
study.
\par
4. Loss function. If an explicit method of
\par
deducing scientific consequences is decided
\par
prior to the study, this should be explicitly
\par
stated. This will often be a range of equivalence
\par
(a range of values such that if the parameter of
\par
interest lies in that range, two different technol-
\par
ogies may be regarded as being of equal effec-
\par
tiveness), or a loss function whose expected
\par
value is to be minimised with respect to the
\par
posterior distribution of the parameter of
\par
interest yielding an estimated value of the
\par
parameter. If these have been obtained by an
\par
elicitation process from experts, this should be
\par
stated and the process described. Any intention
\par
to investigate the dependence of the final
\par
conclusion on the range of equivalence, etc.,
\par
should be described.
\par
5. Prior distribution. Explicit priors for the param-
\par
eters of interest should be given, clearly showing
\par
whether an informative or `non-informative'
\par
prior is being used. If they have been obtained
\par
by an elicitation process this should be stated
\par
and described. If it is intended to examine the
\par
effect of using different priors on the conclusion
\par
of the study, the alternative priors explicitly
\par
should be stated. Any empirical evidence
\par
underlying the prior assessment should be
\par
provided.
\par
6. Computations. These need to be described to
\par
the extent that a mathematically competent
\par
reader could, if necessary, repeat all the
\par
\textbf{55}\emph{BayesWatch}
\par
calculations and obtain the required results.
\par
Details of any software used to obtain the
\par
results should be given. If MCMC methods are
\par
being used, the choice of starting values, the
\par
number and length of runs and convergence
\par
diagnostics to be used should be clearly stated
\par
and justified
\par
\textbf{Results}
\par
1. Evidence from study. As much information
\par
about the observed data -- sample sizes, measure-
\par
ments taken -- as is compatible with brevity and
\par
data confidentiality should be given.
\par
\textbf{Interpretation}
\par
1. Reporting. The posterior distributions should be
\par
clearly summarised. In most cases, this should
\par
include a presentation of posterior credible
\par
intervals and a graphical presentation of the
\par
posterior distribution. If either a formal or
\par
informal loss function has been described, the
\par
results should be expressed in these terms.
\par
It is also essential that the likelihood can be
\par
reconstructed, usually through information
\par
given under `evidence from study', so that subse-
\par
quent users can establish the contribution from
\par
the study to, say, a meta-analysis. There should
\par
be a careful distinction between the report as
\par
a current summary for action, in which case a
\par
synthesis of all relevant sources of evidence is
\par
appropriate, and the report as a contributor of
\par
information for future action.
\par
2. Sensitivity analysis. If alternative priors and/or
\par
expressions of the consequences of decisions
\par
have been given in the sections above, the
\par
