\textbf{Example}
\par
The following is a single example from the litera-
\par
ture summarised using the BayesWatch headings. A
\par
full list of `three-star' Bayesian health technology
\par
assessment studies is provided in appendix 1.
\par
Author. Abrams K, Ashby D and Errington D.1
\par
Title. Simple Bayesian analysis in clinical trials --
\par
a tutorial.
\par
Year. 1994.
\par
The technology. High-energy neutron therapy
\par
against standard photon therapy in treatment of
\par
pelvic cancers.
\par
Objectives of study. To estimate the odds ratio for
\par
12 month survival.
\par
Design of study. Randomised controlled trial.
\par
Separate trials were ran concurrently for cancers
\par
of the rectum, bladder, colon and cervix. Subjects
\par
were randomised in a ratio of 3:1 towards neutron
\par
therapy from 10 February 1986 until 11 January
\par
1988 and then in a ratio of 1:1 until 12 February
\par
1990.
\par
Evidence from study. Twelve-month survival: 61
\par
subjects on photon treatment, 36 alive, 25 dead;
\par
90 subjects on neutron treatment, 44 alive, 46
\par
dead.
\par
Statistical model. Binomial model with 12 month
\par
mortality rate qp on photon treatment and qn on
\par
neutron treatment. Inference is on the log(odds
\par
ratio) log[(qn(1 -- qp)/qp(1 -- qn)].
\par
Prospective analysis? Partly: prior distributions
\par
elicited prospectively, analysis performed
\par
retrospectively.
\par
Loss function. No explicit loss function. An
\par
average of 10 clinicians demanded an odds ratio of
\par
less than 0.63 before routinely preferring neutron
\par
therapy.
\par
Prior distribution. Ten clinicians were asked to
\par
specify their prior for 12 month mortality on
\par
neutron therapy by the roulette method assuming
\par
12 month mortality on photon therapy to be 0.5.
\par
The arithmetic mean of these was taken and a
\par
beta prior superimposed by equating moments:
\par
qp \textasciitilde{} Beta(5.25, 6.17). The 12 month survival on
\par
proton therapy was taken to have a beta prior
\par
with a mean of 0.5 (specified by clinicians) and a
\par
variance of 0.01: qn \textasciitilde{} Beta(12, 12) (suggested by
\par
variability of previous studies).
\par
Computations. Conjugate analysis for qp and qn,
\par
and normal approximation for the posterior of the
\par
log(odds ratio).
\par
Reporting. Kaplan--Meier survival curves. Plots
\par
are shown of the prior, likelihood (`reference
\par
prior') and posterior. Medians, 95\% credible
\par
intervals and probabilities of the odds ratio being
\par
less than 1, and less than the clinical demand
\par
(0.63), are given.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
Sensitivity analysis. The analysis was repeated using
\par
a reference prior.
\par
Comments. Likelihood conflicts with the prior
\par
distribution in the direction of effect. There is
\par
deliberate use of an analytically tractable model for
\par
tutorial purposes. This study is also considered by
\par
Spiegelhalter et al.421 using a log(hazard ratio)
\par
scale.
\par
