\textbf{Chapter 9}
\par
Case study 1: the CHART (lung cancer) trial
\par
This case study briefly describes the prospective
\par
use of an informal Bayesian monitoring
\par
procedure. The information has been kindly
\par
provided by Dr Mahesh Parmar, MRC Clinical
\par
Trials Unit (Cancer Division), Cambridge. Primary
\par
references are Saunders et al.390 and Parmar et al.348
\par
The technology. In 1986 a new radiotherapy tech-
\par
nique called CHART was introduced. Its concept
\par
was to give radiotherapy continuously (no weekend
\par
breaks), in many small fractions (three a day) and
\par
accelerated (the course completed in 12 days). It
\par
should be clear that there are considerable logis-
\par
tical problems in efficiently delivering CHART.
\par
Promising non-randomised and pilot studies led
\par
the UK Medical Research Council to instigate two
\par
large randomised trials for head-and-neck and lung
\par
cancer. Only the lung cancer trial390 is considered
\par
here.
\par
Objectives of study. To estimate the change in
\par
survival in lung cancer patients when given CHART
\par
compared with conventional radiotherapy, in
\par
particular the probability that it provides a clini-
\par
cally important difference in survival that
\par
compensates for any additional toxicity and
\par
problems of delivering the treatment.
\par
Design of study. A 60:40 randomisation in favour
\par
of CHART was selected, with 90\% power (with a
\par
two-sided 5\% significance level) to detect a 10\%
\par
improvement in 2 year survival over the 15\%
\par
expected under conventional treatment. This
\par
would require around 600 patients with 470
\par
expected events, with accrual expected to last
\par
4 years and a further 1 year of follow-up. The
\par
alternative hypothesis of 10\% is the mean of the
\par
subjective prior distribution expressed by 11
\par
clinicians (see below).
\par
The trial began recruitment in January 1990, with
\par
planned annual meetings of the Data Monitoring
\par
Committee (DMC) to review efficacy and toxicity
\par
data. No formal stopping procedure was specified
\par
in the protocol.
\par
Evidence from study. The data reported at each
\par
meeting of the DMC is shown in Table 7.
\par
Recruitment stopped in early 1995 after 563
\par
patients had entered the trial. It is clear that the
\par
extremely beneficial early results were not retained
\par
as the data accumulated, although a clinically
\par
important and statistically significant difference
\par
was eventually found.
\par
The statistical model. We assume a proportional
\par
hazards model where the hazard ratio is defined as
\par
the hazard under standard treatment to the hazard
\par
under CHART, and hence hazard ratios greater
\par
than 1 indicate the superiority of CHART. If
\par
randomisation were balanced between the arms of
\par
the trial, the likelihood for the log(hazard ratio) q
\par
may be assumed to be normally distributed with
\par
mean 4L/m and variance 4/m, where L is the log-
\par
rank statistic (observed number of deaths in the
\par
CHART group minus the expected number under
\par
the null hypothesis) and m is the total number
\par
of deaths.421 Since the randomisation ratio is
\par
60:40 in favour of CHART, this variance must
\par
be changed to 3.84/m. The log(hazard ratio)
\par
q can be transformed, under the proportional
\par
hazard assumption, to an improvement in
\par
2 year survival rate d through the relationship
\par
e
\par
q = log p0/log(d + p0), where p0 is the 2 year
\par
survival rate under conventional treatment,
\par
assumed to be 15\%. This relationship allows us to
\par
obtain estimates and intervals for d from normal
\par
posterior distributions calculated on the q scale.
\par
