\textbf{Chapter 10}
\par
Case study 2: meta-analysis of magnesium sulphate
\par
following acute myocardial infarction
\par
The technology. This example has been consid-
\par
ered at length in the medical and statistical
\par
literature, as it features an apparent contradiction
\par
between a meta-analysis and a `mega-trial'. An
\par
abbreviated history follows. Epidemiology, animal
\par
models and biochemical studies have suggested
\par
intravenous magnesium sulphate may have a protec-
\par
tive effect in patients with AMI, particularly through
\par
preventing serious arrhythmias.443 A series of small
\par
randomised trials culminated in a meta-analysis in
\par
1991443 which showed a highly significant (P {$<$} 0.001)
\par
55\% reduction in odds of death. The authors
\par
concluded that ``further large scale trials to confirm
\par
(or refute) these findings are desirable'', and in
\par
1992 the Second Leicester Intravenous Magnesium
\par
Intervention Trial (LIMIT-2)481 published results
\par
showing a 24\% reduction in mortality in over 2000
\par
patients. An editorial in Circulation was entitled `An
\par
effective, safe, simple and inexpensive treatment',484
\par
but recommended further trials to obtain ``a more
\par
precise estimate of the mortality benefit''. Early
\par
results of the massive Fourth International Study of
\par
Infarct Survival (ISIS-4) trial pointed, however, to a
\par
lack of any benefit, and final publication of this trial
\par
on 58,000 patients showed a non-significant adverse
\par
mortality effect of magnesium. ISIS-4 found no
\par
effect in any subgroups, and concluded that
\par
``Overall, there does not now seem to be any good
\par
clinical trial evidence for the routine use of
\par
magnesium in suspected acute MI''.109
\par
There have been many responses to this apparent
\par
contradiction between meta-analysis and mega-
\par
trial, which can be summarised under four broad
\par
headings:
\par
{$\bullet$} Essential scepticism about large effects. In
\par
response to the ISIS-4 results, Yusuf, the main
\par
author of the optimistic Circulation editorial,
\par
claimed ``since most treatments produce either
\par
no effect or at least moderate effects on major
\par
outcomes such as mortality, investigators should
\par
be sceptical if the results obtained deviate
\par
substantially from this expectation (``too good to
\par
be true'')''.483 This expression of prior scepticism
\par
was echoed by Peto and colleagues,359 who
\par
argued that the risk reduction of the initial
\par
overview was ``implausibly large'', and that even
\par
when combined with the LIMIT-2 data ``still
\par
indicated an implausibly large reduction of one-
\par
third in mortality''. However, Peto reports that
\par
the ISIS-4 steering committee was convinced
\par
there would be at least some benefit, right up
\par
until they were shown the results.
\par
{$\bullet$} Criticism of the meta-analysis. Egger and
\par
Davey-Smith157,158 claim that the meta-analysis
\par
was flawed, as a funnel plot (of numbers of
\par
participants against observed treatment effect)
\par
suggested smaller negative studies might not
\par
have been published, and sensitivity analysis
\par
could have prevented the misleading conclu-
\par
sions. Using a different argument, Pogue and
\par
Yusuf367 claim that a frequentist stopping rule
\par
applied to the meta-analysis, designed to have
\par
high power to detect a moderate effect (15\%
\par
reduction in mortality), would also have led to
\par
the meta-analysis not being significant at the 1\%
\par
level even taking into account the LIMIT-2 data.
\par
{$\bullet$} Criticism of the mega-trial. Woods480 has argued
\par
that a mega-trial such as ISIS-4 will tend to bias
\par
results towards the null due to protocol viola-
\par
tions and inaccurate data. In addition, he claims
\par
that the benefit of magnesium is to prevent
\par
reperfusion injury, and yet the ISIS-4 protocol
\par
expected all patients to be given thrombolytic
\par
therapy (which tends to induce reperfusion)
\par
before randomisation, and hence magnesium
\par
would generally be given too late to provide
\par
benefit. He claims the subgroup who did not
\par
receive thrombolytic therapy did not provide
\par
sufficient power to detect an important
\par
difference.
\par
{$\bullet$} Treatment effect depending on baseline risk.
\par
Antman14 and others have pointed out that in
\par
ISIS-4:
\par
-- the control group mortality was 7.2\% in
\par
contrast to 11.0\% observed in the data
\par
available at the time of the 1993 Circulation
\par
editorial
\par
-- patients were randomised at a median of 8
\par
hours after onset
\par
-- 70\% received thrombolytics and 94\%
\par
received antiplatelets.
\par
