Results and sensitivity analysis.
\par
Fixed effect analysis. Figure 9 shows the prior, likeli-
\par
hood and posterior for two sceptical priors: a
\par
`reasonable' one and one designed to produce a
\par
posterior distribution with 5\% chance that there is
\par
no benefit from magnesium.
\par
The prior necessary not to have found the meta-
\par
analysis `significant', even at a one-sided 5\% proba-
\par
bility, is clearly a very extreme form of scepticism.
\par
Table 10 shows that a reasonably sceptical prior
\par
even finds the meta-analysis quite convincing
\par
concerning a clinically worthwhile improvement, in
\par
that there is 97\% chance that the treatment benefit
\par
is at least 10\%.
\par
Random effect analysis. The random effects analysis
\par
leads to a different conclusion. Figure 10 shows the
\par
95\% posterior credible intervals for the mortality
\par
odds ratio associated with magnesium, for both
\par
fixed and random effect analysis, and a `flat' refer-
\par
ence prior and the reasonably sceptical prior. The
\par
random effects analysis combined with a `flat'
\par
reference prior, and the fixed effect analysis with a
\par
flat or sceptical prior (as also shown in Figure 9), all
\par
lead to highly `significant' results. However the
\par
random effects analysis with a sceptical prior leads
\par
to a 95\% interval that includes one, and hence the
\par
cautious result sought by Yusuf. Inclusion of the
\par
ISIS-4 results has a strong impact on the fixed
\par
effect analysis, but little influence on the random
\par
effect (see discussion below).
\par
We therefore can reject Yusuf and Flather's claim
\par
that a sceptical approach applied to their analysis
\par
would have led to caution.
\par
Figure 11 shows the consequences of a fully Bayesian
\par
sceptical random effects analysis on the estimates
\par
given to the individual trials. The LIMIT-2 results
\par
