\par
Discussion. We conclude that one would need to
\par
have been unreasonably extremely sceptical not to
\par
have found the 1993 meta-analysis convincing, if
\par
one had carried out a standard Peto fixed-effect
\par
analysis. Reasonable scepticism and a random
\par
effects meta-analysis would have led to appropriate
\par
caution. There was limited evidence available in
\par
1993 that treatment effect was related to sample
\par
size or underlying risk, but both have been
\par
confirmed by ISIS-4.
\par
It is important to recognise the limitations of such
\par
a statistical analysis. There is obvious heterogeneity
\par
between the studies, and it is vital to investigate the
\par
possible reasons for this using substantive knowl-
\par
edge, through inclusion of covariates and so on.
