\textbf{Chapter 11}
\par
Case study 3: confidence profiling revisited
\par
Here we revisit four short examples provided by
\par
Eddy et al. in their text on the confidence
\par
profile method150 (see chapter 6 for a discussion of
\par
their work and possible reasons for its lack of
\par
impact).
\par
The confidence profile method made extensive
\par
use of graphical models for communication of
\par
the essential features of a model, although their
\par
software, FAST*PRO, was menu-driven. In contrast,
\par
more recent developments in Bayesian graphical
\par
modelling have led to software, for example
\par
WinBUGS (see appendix 2), in which the graph
\par
explicitly drives the analysis through generating
\par
the code for performing the required simulations
\par
(see chapter 2 for background on simulation-based
\par
Bayesian analysis). The re-analysis of some of their
\par
examples thus serves two purposes: to emphasise
\par
the versatility and power of their approach, and to
\par
show how current (freely available) software can
\par
make its implementation reasonably
\par
straightforward.
\par
The four specific applications have been selected
\par
to illustrate various facets of Bayesian graphical
\par
modelling applied to evidence synthesis in health
\par
technology assessment, and each example is
\par
structured according to the BayesWatch criteria.
\par
However, they are clearly rather dated and hence
\par
should not be taken as having any substantive
\par
value, and we have simply reproduced the
\par
original description and have not attempted to
\par
carry out a full analysis to appropriate quality
\par
standards.
\par
\textbf{Analysis of surveillance of}
\par
\textbf{colorectal cancer patients: a}
\par
\textbf{modelling exercise based entirely}
\par
\textbf{on judgements}
\par
Reference. This example forms chapter 29 of
\par
Eddy et al.
\par
150
\par
The technology. Surveillance of colorectal cancer
\par
patients in order to reduce the risk of liver meta-
\par
stases. No direct evidence for the effectiveness of
\par
this intervention is available.
\par
Objectives of analysis. To estimate the reduction in
\par
mortality rate from liver metastasis due to introduc-
\par
tion of surveillance, denoted dsurv.
\par
Statistical model. We assume that a death is
\par
prevented if:
\par
1. a case has a solitary liver metastasis at the time of
\par
examination (with probability pexist)
\par
2. the metastasis is detected at examination (with
\par
probability pdetect.if.exist)
\par
3. the detected metastasis is treatable (with proba-
\par
bility ptrt.if.detect)
\par
4. the treatment leads to the patient surviving
\par
5 years, who would not otherwise have survived
\par
without surveillance (survival probability has
\par
increased dsurv.if.trt)
\par
The overall improvement in survival rate is given by
\par
the logical product:
\par
dsurv
\par
= dsurv.if.trt \texttimes{} ptrt.if.detect \texttimes{} pdetect.if.exist \texttimes{} pexist
\par
The graph in Figure 13 shows this logical depend-
\par
ence, using the DoodleBUGS graph drawing
\par
facility in WinBUGS.
\par
Prior distributions. The subjective prior distribu-
\par
tions shown in Table 13 are those provided by Eddy
\par
and colleagues.
\par
Computations. Since no data are involved a
\par
forwards Monte Carlo simulation can be carried
\par
out without a burn-in stage and without concerns
\par
with convergence. A total of 100,000 iterations
\par
were carried out, taking 2 seconds on a 400 MHz
\par
personal computer.
\par
Results. Summary statistics for the simulated poste-
\par
rior distributions are shown in Table 14.
\par
The posterior distributions are the same as the
\par
prior distributions (up to simulation error) since
\par
no data have been observed. We estimate 0.022\%
\par
increase in survival (95\% interval --0.010 to
\par
0.092\%), compared with Eddy and colleagues'
\par
estimate of 0.025\% (95\% interval --0.031 to
\par
0.075\%). Their estimate is based on assuming a
\par
normal posterior distribution for dsurv, and the
\par
\textbf{69}\emph{Case study 3}
\par
skewness of the posterior distribution displayed in
\par
Figure 14 clearly shows this is inappropriate.
\par
Sensitivity analysis. The whole case study can be
\par
thought of as a sensitivity analysis to the assump-
\par
tion of known parameter values -- if the best
\par
guesses had been used the conclusion would have
\par
been an increase in survival, with no allowance for
\par
uncertainty.
\par
Comments. This analysis illustrates the propagation
\par
of uncertainty through a logical model, akin to
\par
placing probability distributions on the inputs to
\par
a spreadsheet. The resulting skewness shows the
\par
dangers of making normal approximations to poste-
\par
rior distributions of logically transformed quantities.
\par
