\par
The technology. Neonatal screening for MSUD, an
\par
inborn error in amino acid metabolism, for which
\par
early detection should lead to reduced rates of
\par
retardation.
\par
Objectives of analysis. To estimate the probability
\par
of retardation without screening, and the change
\par
in retardation rate associated with screening. The
\par
latter is denoted ed = qs-- qn, where qn is the retarda-
\par
tion rate in those not screened, and qs is the rate in
\par
those screened.
\par
Design of study. Modelling exercise using results
\par
from multiple epidemiological cohort studies.
\par
Available evidence. There was no direct evidence
\par
on the change in retardation rate in screened
\par
and unscreened populations. The data shown in
\par
Table 18 was used (references provided by Eddy
\par
and colleagues).
\par
Statistical model. The data described in Table 18
\par
are all assumed to arise from binomial distributions
\par
with the appropriate parameters. The functional
\par
relationships shown in Table 19 then exist. The
\par
graphical model is shown in Figure 16.
\par
Prior distribution. The prior distributions for all
\par
the binomial parameters provided by Eddy and
\par
colleagues are `non-informative' (Jeffreys priors --
\par
Beta(0.5, 0.5)).
\par
Computations. A total of 100,000 iterations were
\par
carried out, taking 3 seconds on a 400 MHz
\par
personal computer.
\par
Results. The posterior distribution of ed had the
\par
properties shown in Table 20.
\par
Eddy and colleagues display a normal approxima-
\par
tion to the posterior distribution for ed, with an
\par
\textbf{73}\emph{Case study 3}
\par
estimate of --0.35 (95\% interval --0.69 to --0.19).
\par
Our wider interval accurately reflects the skewed
\par
posterior distribution.
\par
Comments. This example illustrates the synthesis
\par
of evidence from multiple studies, with appropriate
\par
allowance for the uncertainty of the parameter esti-
\par
mates. Further extensions could include allowance
