\par
(the power), assuming a two-sided type I error (a)
\par
of 0.05. This will be calculated assuming 50,000
\par
individuals per group as an example. The analysis
\par
will be by intention-to-treat, but we want to
\par
adjust for the possibility of some of those offered
\par
screening `crossing over' to the unscreened group,
\par
and some of those not offered screening crossing
\par
over to the screened group. Eddy terms this
\par
`dilution'.
\par
Design of study. Proposed randomised controlled
\par
trial.
\par
Available evidence in study. None yet, but the final
\par
evidence will have the form shown in Table 21.
\par
Statistical model. Let pt and pc be the underlying
\par
mortality rate of those offered and not offered
\par
screening, respectively, so that rc \textasciitilde{} Binomial(nc, pc),
\par
rt \textasciitilde{} Binomial(nt, pt). Let qt and qc be the assumed
\par
mortality rates in those actually obtaining and not
\par
obtaining screening, respectively. Under the cross-
\par
over assumption, we have the relationships shown
\par
in Table 22. The graph of the model is shown in
\par
Figure 17, from which the WinBUGS analysis is
\par
driven.
\par
Prior distribution. Eddy and colleagues assume
\par
the following point estimates for the parameters:
\par
qc = 0.005, qt = 0.004, dt = 0.3, dc = 0.2. Thus they
\par
are attempting to detect a 20\% mortality reduction,
\par
assuming 30\% of those offered screening refuse,
\par
and 20\% of those not offered screening are
\par
screened outside the trial.
\par
Computations. Sampling from the binomial
\par
distribution with a large denominator is slow, and
\par
so 5000 iterations took nearly 3 minutes on a
\par
400 MHz personal computer.
\par
Results. These are shown in Table 23, with the results
\par
of Eddy et al. in bold; `dilution' refers to the allow-
\par
ance for cross-over between intervention groups.
\par
Comments. The results of Eddy et al. appear to be
\par
incorrect for the trial with dilution. It is clear that
\par
if a moderate amount of cross-over is plausible,
\par
then a clinical trial needs to be very large in order
\par
to have a reasonable chance of correctly obtaining
\par
a significant conclusion.
\par
\textbf{Commentary}
\par
The confidence profile technique can easily be
\par
transformed into the form of Bayesian graphical
\par
modelling used by the WinBUGS software. It is a
\par
very powerful method, but has perhaps been
\par
limited by its implementation, and WinBUGS
\par
appears to allow straightforward application.
\par
However, there are inevitably dangers with such a
\par
modelling exercise, which can only be as good as
\par
its structural assumptions and the quality of the
\par
data going into it.
