\textbf{75}\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
\textbf{Chapter 12}
\par
Case study 4: comparison of \emph{in vitro}
\par
fertilisation clinics
\par
The UK Human Fertilisation and Embryology
\par
Authority (HFEA) has a responsibility to
\par
monitor clinics in the UK licensed to carry out
\par
donor insemination (DI) and in vitro fertilisation
\par
(IVF). Their annual publication -- The Patients
\par
Guide to DI and IVF Clinics -- is designed to help
\par
people who are considering fertility treatment to
\par
understand the services offered by licensed clinics
\par
and to decide which clinic is best for them.245
\par
One of the statistics provided regarding IVF treat-
\par
ment at each clinic is an adjusted live-birth rate per
\par
treatment cycle started. A live birth is defined as any
\par
birth event in which at least one baby is born and
\par
survives for more than 1 month, and a treatment
\par
cycle begins with the administration of drugs to
\par
induce superovulation. The adjustment, which is
\par
intended to take account of the mix of patients
\par
treated by the clinic by using factors such as age,
\par
duration of infertility, number of previous treat-
\par
ment cycles and so on,441 varies from year to year
\par
and is based on a pooled logistic regression of all
\par
IVF treatments carried out in the UK in the relevant
\par
year. Also provided are associated 95\% confidence
\par
intervals for each adjusted live-birth rate.
\par
Marshall and Spiegelhalter312 analyse the data
\par
published in 1996, using Figure 18 to show the
\par
substantial range of success rates displayed by the
\par
clinics.
\par
They then use MCMC techniques to derive poste-
\par
rior distributions for the ranks of the institutions:
\par
this is easily done by calculating the current rank of
\par
each institution at each iteration of the simulation,
\par
and then summarising the distribution of these
\par
calculated ranks after many thousands of iterations.
\par
Figure 19 shows that there is considerable uncer-
\par
tainty in the true rank of an institution, even when
\par
they show substantial differences in performance.
\par
We now assume the clinics are fully exchangeable
\par
(see page 21) with the true rates (on a logit scale)
\par
being drawn from a common normal distribution:
\par
if, after adjusting for case mix, we can find no
\par
other contextually meaningful way to differentiate
\par
between the institutions a priori, then the assump-
\par
tion of their exchangeability seems justified. It
\par
is clear from Figure 20 that there is substantial
\par
`shrinkage' towards the overall mean performance,
\par
although there are still a number of clinics that
\par
would be considered `significantly' above or below
\par
average. It can be argued that this adjustment is an
\par
appropriate means of dealing with the problem of
\par
multiple comparisons. In addition, this shrinkage
\par
should deal with `regression-to-the-mean', in which
\par
extreme institutions will tend back towards the
\par
overall average when they recover from their
\par
temporary run of good or bad luck.
\par
The consequence of assuming exchangeability is to
\par
reduce the differences between clinics and hence
\par
to make their ranks even more uncertain. Figure 21
\par
shows this is the case to a limited extent, although
\par
since many of the extreme clinics are also fairly
\par
large, their rank is not unduly affected.
\par
