\textbf{105}\emph{Appendix 1}
\par
\textbf{What is a three-star application}
\par
In chapter 1 we defined `three-star' Bayesian health
\par
technology assessment studies as those
\par
1. intending to confirm the value of a technology
\par
2. using an informative, carefully considered prior
\par
distribution for the primary quantity of interest
\par
and
\par
3. updating, or planning to update, this prior
\par
distribution by Bayes's theorem.
\par
Table 24 summarises the three-star applications
\par
identified by the review with respect to the
\par
BayesWatch criteria outlined in chapter 8,
\par
although we have placed `evidence from study'
\par
earlier in the list. Out of the 30 studies identified,
\par
all but two considered the application of Bayesian
\par
methods in a randomised controlled trial setting,
\par
with 17 studies adopting a normal approximation
\par
to the appropriate likelihood and a corresponding
\par
conjugate analysis. This is particularly interesting
\par
since the majority of papers appeared after 1993
\par
yet only two used a MCMC technique, and high-
\par
lights the wide applicability of a normal--normal
\par
conjugate model. Perhaps more disappointingly
\par
only nine studies conducted the analysis prospec-
\par
tively, of which four also undertook elicitation of
\par
subjective prior beliefs from experts, though an
\par
additional four studies undertook some form of
\par
elicitation exercise before conducting a retrospec-
\par
tive analysis. Although 11 of the 30 studies
\par
considered some form of clinical/policy demand,
\par
only two of these did so using a formal loss
\par
function. Particularly encouraging was the fact
\par
that all but five of the studies undertook some
\par
form of sensitivity analysis.
\par
