0--0.414 in log(hazard ratio), corresponding to a
\par
rise from 25 to 40\% improvement in 2 year primary
\par
control.
\par
Prior distribution. Default reference, sceptical and
\par
enthusiastic priors.
\par
Computations. Conjugate normal analysis.
\par
Reporting. Posterior distributions and probabilities
\par
relative to a range of equivalence.
\par
Sensitivity analysis. Reference, clinical and
\par
sceptical priors.
\par
Comments. The probability of clinical superiority
\par
was low even with an enthusiastic prior. In fact,
\par
the trial was terminated at this third analysis.
\par
This paper also considers `three-star' analyses
\par
of the neutron study1 and the levamisole plus
\par
5-fluorouracil study.183
\par
Author. Stangl DK.427
\par
Title. Prediction and decision-making using
\par
Bayesian hierarchical-models.
\par
Year. 1995.
\par
The technology. Imipramine hydrochloride for
\par
prevention of or delaying a return to depression.
\par
Objectives of study. To compare the time to recur-
\par
rent depression with (new regimen) and without
\par
(existing regimen) imipramine.
\par
Design of study. Five-centre randomised controlled
\par
trial.
\par
Evidence from study. Survival data from five centres.
\par
Statistical model. Exponential survival model, with
\par
the option of a change point to allow for a non-
\par
constant hazard.
\par
Prospective analysis? No.
\par
Loss function. No.
\par
Prior distribution. Gamma priors were obtained
\par
by considering the first gamma parameter to be a
\par
sample size, and making the second have a gamma
\par
prior which itself has a second parameter equal to
\par
the maximum likelihood estimate. Three different
\par
sets of priors were used.
\par
Computations. MCMC (Gibbs sampling).
\par
Reporting. The expectation of the time to recur-
\par
rence for patient in each treatment group, and
\par
the expected difference between the treated and
\par
untreated group and at each clinic, were provided
\par
under each model and prior, and the predictive
\par
distribution of the difference at each clinic under
\par
one model and prior was plotted.
\par
Sensitivity analysis. Three different priors were
\par
explored.
\par
Comments. The use of decision theory is explored
\par
in deciding which treatment to give to a patient.\emph{Health Technology Assessment }2000; Vol. 4: No. 38
\par
This study was also analysed using Laplace approxi-
\par
mations425 in which further sensitivity analysis to
\par
prior assumptions is carried out.
\par
Author. Ware J.469
\par
Title. Investigating therapies of potentially great
\par
benefit: ECMO (with discussion).
\par
Year. 1989.
\par
The technology. ECMO.
\par
Objectives of study. To estimate the difference in
\par
mortality associated with ECMO patients.
\par
Design of study. Adaptive randomised controlled
\par
trial.
\par
Evidence from study. Four deaths out of 10 controls,
\par
and zero deaths out of nine ECMO patients.
\par
Statistical model. Model/likelihood: (pC, pE), the
\par
survival probabilities in the control and ECMO
\par
groups, respectively, such that a beta prior is
\par
assumed for pC, and the conditional distribution of
\par
pE, given pC, is such that P(pC {$<$} pE) = P(pC = pE) =
\par
P(pC {$>$} pE) = 1/3.
\par
Prospective analysis? No.
\par
Loss function. No.
\par
Prior distribution. Historical clinical series of 13
\par
patients receiving conventional medical therapy, of
\par
whom two survived.
\par
Computations. Due to the model formulation,
\par
posterior probabilities could be obtained in a
\par
closed form.
\par
Reporting. Posterior probabilities: P(pC {$<$} pE),
\par
P(pC = pE), P(pC {$>$} pE).
\par
Sensitivity analysis. Both an informative data-based
\par
prior and a vague prior for pC were used.
\par
Comments. None.
