\par
This report describes research into the impact of the school library resource centre (SLRC) on
\par
learning, funded by Resource: The Council for Museums, Archives and Libraries. The study
\par
was conducted in Scottish secondary schools between August 1999 and February 2001.
\par
The research was undertaken at a time of increasing presssure to measure performance in all
\par
educational establishments, including libraries and information services (ILS) and to ensure
\par
the service is not only \emph{efficient} but also \emph{effective.}
\par
The study looked at learning in its broadest sense, including motivation, progression,
\par
independence, and interaction. The first phase of the study used focus group discussions with
\par
teachers and pupils and interviews with librarians to identify their perceptions of how the
\par
SLRC can contribute to learning. The second phase used case studies of specific examples of
\par
SLRC activity to establish whether this perceived impact on learning was indeed taking place;
\par
to identify indicators which might be used as evidence of learning; and to examine the process
\par
of assessing the impact of the SLRC on learning.
\par
The report identifies:
\par
{$\bullet$} stakeholder groups' perceptions of the wide range of possible learning experiences which
\par
might be affected by the SLRC;
\par
{$\bullet$} the broad range of potential impacts on the learning experience which were observed in
\par
practice;
\par
{$\bullet$} examples of indicators of learning which can provide a starting point for individual
\par
teachers and librarians to examine the relationship between their own SLRC and learning;
\par
{$\bullet$} examples of techniques used to monitor the impact on learning;
\par
{$\bullet$} a range of factors which influence the effectiveness of learning in the SLRC, and which
\par
will help practitioners make decisions about aims, objectives and activities to optimise the
\par
learning potential.
\par
\textbf{AUTHORS}
\par
\textbf{Dorothy Williams} is Professor at the School of Information and Media, The Robert Gordon
\par
University, Aberdeen and has considerable experience in research at the interface between
\par
education and information, in particular information skills development, information
\par
management and the impact of information on decision-making in education.
\par
\textbf{Caroline Wavell} is a Research Assistant at the School of Information and Media, The Robert
\par
Gordon University, Aberdeen and has worked in the museum and school library sectors
\par
developing a keen interest in education in its broadest sense.\textbf{ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS}
