\textbf{EXECUTIVE SUMMARY}
\par
This report presents the findings of a recent study which investigated the impact of the school
\par
library resource centre (SLRC) on learning. The study had two phases:
\par
1. Focus group discussions with teachers and pupils and interviews with librarians identified
\par
teachers', pupils' and librarians' perceptions of how the SLRC can contribute to learning.
\par
2. Case study research, using the framework developed from the first phase, identified
\par
learning experiences that the SLRC impacted upon; investigated indicators to be used as
\par
evidence of learning; and examined the process of assessing the impact of the SLRC on
\par
learning.
\par
The research was conducted in secondary schools in Scotland between August 1999 and
\par
February 2001. The study was commissioned by the Library and Information Commission,
\par
which was absorbed into Resource - The Council for Museums, Archives and Libraries in
\par
April 2000.
\par
\textbf{Perceptions of learning impacts (Section 5)}
\par
The teachers represented in the focus groups covered a wide cross-section of subject
\par
departments as well as members of senior management. Their accumulated perceptions of the
\par
impact of the SLRC on learning covered a broad range of potential learning experiences but it
\par
was clear that their views were based on broad expectations of what the impact \emph{might} be,
\par
rather than any in-depth analysis or monitoring of \emph{actual} learning impact.
\par
The pupils participating in the focus group sessions found the concept of how the SLRC
\par
might contribute to learning more difficult to grasp but were able to identify a range of
\par
potential learning impacts.
\par
The librarians interviewed were aware of what they were trying to achieve but were not sure
\par
whether their objectives were being met.
\par
The collective perceptions of impact were expressed as:
\par
{$\bullet$} The acquisition of information and wider general knowledge;
\par
{$\bullet$} Skills development in the areas of finding and using information, ICT skills and reading
\par
skills and their cross-curricular use and potential for equal opportunities;
\par
{$\bullet$} Higher achievement in school work;
\par
{$\bullet$} Developing a study and reading habit encouraging independent working;
\par
{$\bullet$} Motivation to learn and enjoyment of learning;
\par
{$\bullet$} The ability to use these skills confidently and independently and the ability to transfer
\par
these skills across the curriculum and beyond school;
\par
{$\bullet$} The development of interpersonal and social skills, including working collaboratively.
\par
The three groups of participants shared a common perception of how the SLRC could
\par
contribute to learning. The discussions also highlighted factors which were thought to
\par
influence the learning impact and these factors tended to reflect constraints imposed by
\par
finances, environmental conditions, personalities and social background, which were not
\par
easily controlled by individual teachers or the librarian.
\par
These potential areas of impact on learning formed the foundation of an impact framework of
\par
potential learning experiences, which related areas of impact to established educational and
\par
ilearning classifications. The result was a list of potential learning experiences that were
\par
categorised under the learning themes of motivation, progression, independence and
\par
interaction and a corresponding list of possible indicators which could be used to look for
\par
evidence that such learning experiences were taking place. This framework provided the basis
\par
for a qualitative, observational approach to case study investigations, in which evidence of the
\par
learning experiences was identified and the validity of the indicators was examined.
\par
\textbf{Findings from the case studies (Sections 6 \& 7)}
\par
The case study units covered a range of learning across the curriculum and broader
\par
educational goals and they also represented a wide range of year groups. The activities
\par
selected for investigation covered a range of curriculum related project work, a skills course,
\par
the promotion of reading for pleasure, pupil librarians, special events and ad hoc use of the
\par
SLRC.
\par
The investigation examined the learning experiences and evidence of impact was identified in
\par
the learning themes of motivation, progression, independence and interaction. Examples of
\par
indicators that might be used to look for evidence of impact were identified. The various
\par
methods of monitoring impact and the value these methods have for particular learning
\par
experiences was examined. Finally the case studies highlighted a number of conditioning
\par
factors which influence the impact on learning.
\par
\textbf{Evidence of motivation} was seen across all the case units by pupil enjoyment and
\par
participation and absorption in the tasks set whether that was a project, looking for reading
\par
material or in the commitment of the pupil librarians. The indicators were identified as:
\par
{$\bullet$} Verbal and written expression of enthusiasm by pupils;
\par
{$\bullet$} Pupil willingness to participate in the activity set;
\par
{$\bullet$} Pupil application and absorption in the task;
\par
{$\bullet$} Willingness of pupils to continue their work either by returning to the SLRC or at home;
\par
{$\bullet$} A change in attitude towards work over a period of time.
\par
\textbf{Evidence of progression} was most easily identified as awareness of or ability to use a
\par
specific skill associated with the finding of information and sometimes the use and
\par
presentation of information. It was also possible to identify examples of the application of
\par
skills in new contexts. The indicators were identified as:
\par
{$\bullet$} Awareness of or the ability to use specific skills associated with finding, using and
\par
presenting information;
\par
{$\bullet$} The use of new knowledge in work or discussion of new knowledge;
\par
{$\bullet$} Personal achievement or quality of work;
\par
{$\bullet$} The ability to apply skills or knowledge in a new situation.
\par
\textbf{Evidence of independence} was identified in individual pupils who appeared to have
\par
mastered a skill and were seen to have the confidence and competence to proceed and
\par
progress unaided, either in the class session or in their own time. The pupil librarians were
\par
able to use their initiative by instigating their own activities and tasks. The indicators were
\par
identified as:
\par
{$\bullet$} The ability and confidence to continue and progress with a task unaided;
\par
{$\bullet$} Awareness of the need for help and the confidence to seek it;
\par
{$\bullet$} Awareness of the need for organisation and time management in work;
\par
{$\bullet$} Use of initiative;
\par
{$\bullet$} Increased self-esteem.
\par
\textbf{Evidence of interaction} was particularly relevant in the junior curriculum related activities
\par
and with the pupil librarians, where discussion and interaction were encouraged. Interaction
\par
iiwas seen as a learning experience in itself, as a means of enhancing the learning in other
\par
themes and as a means of establishing the learning that individuals were engaged in. The
\par
indicators were identified as:
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion with others about the task;
\par
{$\bullet$} Peer co-operation;
\par
{$\bullet$} Ability to mix with other groups;
\par
{$\bullet$} Use of appropriate behaviour.
\par
\textbf{The methods used for monitoring} impact on learning in the SLRC were varied and some
\par
were easier to use within the class situation while others required discussion or questioning
\par
beyond the immediate environment. The methods identified as potentially useful tools for
\par
practitioners in schools were:
\par
{$\bullet$} Observation of pupils at work in the SLRC and connecting their learning both to the
\par
immediate context and to previous experiences;
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion with and questioning of pupils about their work both during and at the end of
\par
the activities;
\par
{$\bullet$} Examination of work in progress and, if available and when appropriate, submitted
\par
written work;
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion with other members of teaching staff about work, attitudes and related
\par
incidences;
\par
{$\bullet$} Examination of reader records.
\par
\textbf{The factors that were seen to influence} effective learning were varied and were often seen
\par
to be associated with the immediate context. An examination of the influencing factors may
\par
help teachers and librarians to make decisions about their aims, objectives and activities to
\par
optimise the learning potential. Interestingly, these factors reflect the need for joint teacher
\par
and librarian planning. The influencing factors identified during the case units included:
\par
{$\bullet$} Interest, enthusiasm and appreciation shown by others;
\par
{$\bullet$} Appropriate intervention to ensure progress could proceed;
\par
{$\bullet$} Familiarity with surroundings;
\par
{$\bullet$} Foundation of necessary skills to proceed;
\par
{$\bullet$} Understanding of task or tasks;
\par
{$\bullet$} Opportunity to try again and build on understanding;
\par
{$\bullet$} New stimuli, for instance the use of computers;
\par
{$\bullet$} Frustration caused by problems;
\par
{$\bullet$} Tensions within groups;
\par
{$\bullet$} Time constraints or inappropriate use;
\par
{$\bullet$} Lack of focus, skills, background theory.
\par
\textbf{The way forward (Section 8)}
\par
The project developed a framework of potential learning experiences and examples of
\par
indicators which can be used to evaluate learning. The framework provides a basic
\par
understanding of the SLRC learning context, which could provide a useful starting point for
\par
teachers, librarians and schools to begin looking at effective learning associated with the
\par
SLRC.
\par
The report highlights a number of key issues which need to be resolved if the SLRC is to
\par
make an effective impact on learning:
\par
{$\bullet$} the need for local and collaborative planning and decision-making. The report provides
\par
an understanding of how the learning experiences interact and interrelate with each other
\par
and within the curriculum and school context. There is evidence of learning beyond the
\par
immediate goals set by the individual teacher using the SLRC and, although these are
\par
iiinot necessarily readily visible, they are part of the overall learning experience within the
\par
school environment and play a vital role in the development of the cross-curricular, core
\par
skills needed to fulfil the educational aims of the current political and social climate. The
\par
framework could be used within the overall planning and evaluation processes at a
\par
school, subject department, SLRC and course level to help identify appropriate roles for
\par
the SLRC. An understanding of the influencing factors identified in the case units may
\par
help teachers, librarians and senior management to prioritise conflicting and competing
\par
agendas when using the SLRC.
\par
{$\bullet$} the need for more understanding or use of the skills involved in information handling
\par
and a mutual understanding of how the library and teaching professions can support each
\par
other and collaborate together in order to encourage effective use of the SLRC.
\par
{$\bullet$} the need to look beyond the immediate SLRC for impact. The evidence of the impact on
\par
learning is not always easily visible but, if it is thought to be valuable, measures need to
\par
be put in place to identify impact across learning contexts, beyond the immediate
\par
situation and over appropriate timescales. This requires some form of evaluation of
\par
courses or activities undertaken in the SLRC to ensure effective teaching and learning.
\par
There is also a need to assess individuals to ensure progression of skills and progress
\par
over time. It is not suggested that every use of the SLRC requires formal evaluation or
\par
assessment but that the stakeholders in the local context will be in a position to decide
\par
their own priorities.
\par
There is potential value in applying the experiences gained from the SLRC impact study to
\par
other information services (such as public libraries, museums and archives) or learning
\par
contexts (such as homework clubs). The framework could provide the starting point for
\par
practitioners to examine the provision of learning experiences against the impact on the
\par
learning of the user in a variety of educational environments. However, the findings of the
\par
research are expected to be particularly relevant to all members of the teaching and library
\par
professions who are interested in the use of the SLRC for effective learning and teaching,
\par
from policy-makers, senior management to individual teachers and librarians working directly
\par
with pupils in schools.
\par
iv\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
This report describes research carried out in secondary schools in Scotland to assess the
\par
impact of the school library resource centre (SLRC) on learning. The research was undertaken
\par
between August 1999 and February 2001. The project was commissioned by the Library and
\par
Information Commission, which was absorbed into Resource - The Council for Museums,
\par
Archives and Libraries in April 2000.
\par
The research has been undertaken at a time when value and impact studies are trying to
\par
establish the contribution made by library and information services in different community
\par
contexts including that of supporting traditional education, independent study and lifelong
\par
learning. At the same time there is increasing interest in ways in which links between the
\par
public libraries and the educational sector can promote lifelong learning1. A recent
\par
consultation document has suggested the need for a cross-sectoral learning and access
\par
standard for museums, archives and libraries2 and this requires an understanding of the
\par
relationship between the user, the context and the learning in educational environments
\par
outside the traditional school or college setting. While this study focuses on Scottish schools,
\par
the interest in establishing the impact of information and library services in schools is world-
\par
wide, not least because of the increasing pressures to justify the resource allocation from a
\par
school's budget or the existence of adequate professional staffing within the SLRC. While
\par
there may be an intuitive knowledge that the SLRC makes a valuable contribution, more
\par
concrete evidence is required to convince decision-makers of its importance.
\par
At the same time, the position of the SLRC within the school environment is changing. In the
\par
UK, recent government initiatives such as the National Grid for Learning (NGfL)3 and the
\par
implementation of information and communications technology (ICT) networks within
\par
schools has increased awareness of information support for the curriculum. The volume of
\par
information and the speed with which it is available has also raised an awareness of the need
\par
to understand information in terms of its delivery, evaluation of purpose and its ultimate use.
\par
Information scientists have a key role to play in developing the skills for effective information
\par
use and librarians in schools have found their roles being redefined to enable schools to fulfil
\par
government objectives for the NGfL, and to develop a clearer strategy for integrating the
\par
work of the SLRC within the wider educational experience.
\par
While ICT initiatives have already resulted in new opportunities to enhance the work of the
\par
SLRC in support of the curriculum, such developments have also dramatically increased the
\par
expenditure in the area of ICT. Such expenditure requires careful consideration and with
\par
recent devolved management within schools the pressures to justify expenditure in terms of
\par
educational benefits are increasing. There is a growing awareness of the need to examine
\par
carefully how effective the various inputs and outputs are in terms of enhancing the quality of
\par
learning and raising achievements within schools as well as creating better links with the
\par
wider community.
\par
This focus on evaluation of the quality of learning within schools, shifts the emphasis from
\par
one of measuring outputs (as has traditionally been the case when measuring SLRC
\par
performance) to that of the \emph{outcomes}, particularly the nature and quality of the learning
\par
experience, resulting from the provision of the SLRC and how this relates to the goals and
\par
aspirations of the school itself. It may never be possible or even desirable to attempt to
\par
"measure" learning impact in the same way as academic achievement may be measured, but it
\par
is the understanding of the impact on the learning experience which will ultimately be needed
\par
if the work of the SLRC is to be fully integrated within the curriculum and thus contribute
\par
effectively to achievement.
\par
1All these factors have led to the realisation that greater understanding is required in the
\par
relationship between information and learning and between the role that the SLRC and the
\par
librarian can play in enhancing the quality of the learning experience. This research focuses
\par
on this relationship with the aim of identifying the impact the SLRC can have on learning as
\par
well as the challenges involved in evaluating that impact.
\par
The aim of the study was:
\par
{$\bullet$} to assess the impact of a School Library Resource Centre (SLRC) on learning.
\par
The main objectives were:
\par
{$\bullet$} to develop frameworks describing pupils', teachers' and librarians' perceptions of
\par
a) the ways in which the SLRC contributes to the learning experience;
\par
b) direct and indirect benefits to learning from a SLRC;
\par
and using the frameworks as a starting point
\par
{$\bullet$} to identify learning outcomes to be used as indicators of SLRC impact;
\par
{$\bullet$} to assess the impact of a SLRC on specific aspects of learning.
\par
The research was conducted within secondary schools, examining the issues from the
\par
perspectives of librarians, teachers and pupils in semi-structured focus groups and a series of
\par
case studies. Sections 2 and 3 examine the background to the study and the rationale behind
\par
the chosen research approach, while Section 4 describes in more detail the methods used. The
\par
outcomes of the focus group phase (Section 5) and the case studies (Section 6) are analysed
\par
separately before the fuller implications are discussed and interpreted in Sections 7 and 8.
