2\textbf{2. BACKGROUND}
\par
\textbf{2.1 THE SLRC AND LEARNING}
\par
The emphasis of the research is on learning and the relationship between a SLRC and the
\par
learning experience. The mission and goals of any SLRC indicate that support for the learning
\par
process is seen as central to the development of information and library services. SLRCs aim
\par
not only to provide a rich learning environment in terms of an appropriate range of resources
\par
but also in terms of developing the learning skills of pupils. Yet the research and professional
\par
literature of recent years indicates that the relationship between the provision of the SLRC
\par
and the quality of the learning experience remains unclear1
\par
.
\par
The need for greater understanding of this relationship emerges from a consideration of recent
\par
curriculum related developments, which have placed greater emphasis on the development of
\par
transferable skills. In 1999, the Scottish Office identified key priorities for the curriculum in
\par
developing essential skills to foster the personal and social development of the individual in
\par
order to equip them for life2
\par
. The implementation of the new Higher Still (post-16, S5-S63)
\par
curriculum is intended not only to build upon these foundations within the 5-14 (primary - S2)
\par
and Standard Grade (S3-S4) education but to increase employability by encouraging the
\par
development of core skills in communication, numeracy, problem solving, information
\par
technology and working with others. The English National Curriculum has similar
\par
developments in learning opportunities across the curriculum to encourage a lifelong
\par
learning4. The SLRC is seen as having an active and supporting role in achieving these
\par
educational priorities but for librarians as well as teachers, the nature of the potential SLRC
\par
contribution remains vaguely defined and raises wider questions about the relationship
\par
between the SLRC and the learning experience.
\par
Alongside these educational initiatives are the management issues of quality assurance and
\par
benchmarking. The use of performance indicators to self-evaluate the quality of educational
\par
provision in school has been paralleled by education and library organisations' attempts to
\par
develop guidelines and indicators for evaluating the SLRC5, within the context of the school
\par
as a whole. One key area identified for evaluation is that of Learning and Teaching, within
\par
which one Performance Indicator is that of "Quality of pupils' learning". However, the
\par
evaluation of the contribution made by the SLRC to quality of learning within the school
\par
requires an understanding of the relationship between SLRC activities and the learning goals
\par
of the curriculum and the school as a whole.
\par
This is all part of the wider pressure on managers of library services in the public sector,
\par
including schools, to move beyond measuring efficiency of performance into assessment of
\par
effectiveness. The Government's response to a report by The Library and Information
\par
Commission (LIC) recognises `` \emph{the important role played by high quality school library}
\par
\emph{services in supporting teaching and learning within schools}''6. However, how to recognise
\par
``quality'' provision, particularly in respect of learning, presents challenges. Traditionally
\par
attempts to measure performance have focused on activities which are essentially to do with
\par
the running of the SLRC rather than the impact these activities and services have on the work
\par
of the school and its learners. Measures of \emph{outputs} from a library perspective (covering
\par
expenditure, resource provision and levels of library use, e.g. numbers of library-related
\par
transactions, learning skills transactions and users) may tell us much about the efficiency with
\par
which the SLRC operates but tells us little about the \emph{outcomes} in terms of the learning
\par
experience and achievement. It would appear that a new approach is needed if schools are to
\par
begin to assess the impact of the SLRC on learning.
\par
3In the UK, recent guidelines produced by Information Management Associates of assessing
\par
\emph{Best Value} and better performance encourages managers of public and school library services
\par
to move beyond attempts to benchmark or measure performance in output terms and to focus
\par
more on assessment of outcomes, in other words \emph{impact.} However, the guidelines also
\par
acknowledge that while there is growing interest in benchmarking on the basis of impact and
\par
achievement (rather than outputs) in a wide range of public sector services and contexts
\par
"...\emph{nobody is doing it well - yet!"7}
\par
Clearly a better understanding of the relationship between SLRC activities and the learning
\par
experience could be useful as a way of demonstrating the value of, and attaining appropriate
\par
recognition for, the work of the SLRC, an issue of concern to the Information and Library
\par
Science (ILS) profession world-wide8. However, a more fundamental need for such
\par
understanding relates to the future planning of an effective SLRC in support of the
\par
curriculum. Effective integration within the work of the school requires some sharing of a
\par
common ``language'' and vision between the library and teaching professions of what they
\par
want from their SLRC in relation to the learning process. Yet, curriculum guidelines offer
\par
little guidance to either teachers or librarians on how the services of the SLRC should be
\par
incorporated into curriculum and lesson planning in support of the achievement of targets. It
\par
has become increasingly clear that there is a need for research which can provide some sort of
\par
framework or starting point within which schools can begin this dialogue and more effective
\par
planning.
\par
\textbf{2.2 OTHER STUDIES}
\par
Attempts to measure SLRC impact in terms, which will be understood by educators and
\par
decision-makers, have been emerging in studies in the USA in recent years. As in the UK,
\par
much of the impetus for such studies in the USA has come from the greater pressures on
\par
librarians to justify their existence and the pressure on decision-makers to justify and
\par
prioritise expenditure in relation to the achievement of learning goals9. Key studies such as
\par
the original "Colorado Study"10 and the follow up Second Colorado Study11 have already
\par
reported a strong statistically relevant correlation between the provision of an SLRC and
\par
academic achievement, in research conducted over a large sample of schools in the state of
\par
Colorado. In particular it appears that the quantity of professional staffing is a key factor.
\par
Other studies have also pointed to a positive impact on achievement albeit on a more localised
\par
scale, for example in schools involved in the Library Power programme initiative12
\par
.
\par
Such studies have provided the kind of evidence that will be reassuring to librarians, and
\par
convincing to decision-makers. However, by focusing on academic achievement they do not
\par
necessarily take account of the broader learning experience. Nor do they fully explain the way
\par
in which the SLRC activities and programmes interact with, and contribute to, the learning
\par
experience. While providing positive evidence of the overall impact on achievement, their
\par
value in terms of planning and integrating the work of a SLRC within the curriculum is more
\par
limited. This present study could be seen as complementary to, but different from, the
\par
Colorado and more recent follow up studies carried out in the USA13 in that it attempts
\par
interaction between the SLRC and the learning experience.
\par
Research by Kuhlthau14 and Pitts15 has also focused much more on the learning experience,
\par
specifically in relation to the information search process. By revealing and describing the
\par
inquiry process as a series of stages that relate to the cognitive and affective domains of
\par
learning, this research has been influential in helping to establish a relationship between
\par
information and learning. Building on these earlier investigations, Kuhlthau's more recent
\par
studies in relation to the Library Power programme16 have gone on to identify indicators of
\par
learning and frameworks for evaluating libraries' provision of opportunities for learning
\par
which take account of attitudes, development of information skills and the use of resources
\par
4for learning through enquiry. Though this research has concentrated on evaluating the
\par
provision of learning opportunities, the approach taken is closely related to the aims of the
\par
present study. By engaging librarians in the process of identifying indicators of learning it
\par
begins to move our understanding on from a focus on library \emph{outputs }to a language and
\par
understanding which relate to \emph{outcomes} and impact on learning.
\par
The work of Kuhlthau and Pitts, provides an invaluable reference point in this study,
\par
however, these studies tend to focus on particular aspects of the SLRC role, in particular those
\par
of developing information skills and the information retrieval process. In other words they
\par
focus on the information handling aspects of the learning process. This is clearly a focus for
\par
much of the planned activity in the SLRC but does not address the wider learning goals which
\par
schools as a whole seek to achieve. In Scottish schools, for example, recent evaluation
\par
guidance to schools defines quality of learning under the broad themes of motivation,
\par
progress in learning, independence and interaction17
\par
. The curriculum for primary and early
\par
secondary defines learning in terms of a broad range of knowledge and understanding,
\par
dispositions to learning, social and personal responsibility, and core skills and capabilities18;
\par
while the new post 16 Higher Still curriculum seeks to develop a broad range of core skills
\par
(communication, application of number, information technology, working with others,
\par
improving own learning and performance, and problem solving)19. These broader definitions
\par
of the goals and aspirations of schools, teachers and learners provide a backdrop for the work
\par
of the SLRC, yet it is currently not clear how the kinds of services and activities of the SLRC
\par
relate to these various learning experiences and goals. It is with this in mind that the present
\par
study set out to examine the wider impact of the SLRC, not just in terms of the impact on
\par
pupils' ability to find and use information, important though that clearly is in this context, but
\par
also on their ability to learn effectively in the broadest sense.
