6\textbf{3. ASSESSING IMPACT}
\par
\textbf{3.1 INDICATORS OF IMPACT}
\par
Some of the challenges faced in studying the impact of the SLRC within its community are on
\par
the face of it similar to the challenges encountered in attempts to assess impact of any kind of
\par
information and library service, namely identifying reliable, valid indicators of impact, and
\par
the methods to assess them within a complex environment. Indicators have to be appropriate
\par
to the type of information and library service or programme being studied and the
\par
environment within which users operate. Menou recognises this need in his framework for
\par
measuring impact of information on development1. This framework advocates an in-depth
\par
qualitative study of the Information Use Environment (IUE) in order to identify a suite of
\par
indicators that can be used for evaluation at several levels. In dealing with impact of the
\par
SLRC on learning, the indicators chosen have to reflect the learning goals of the curriculum
\par
and the school as a whole since this is the context within which learning activities in the
\par
SLRC will be planned and undertaken.
\par
Moreover, the impact of any SLRC will be an individual impact depending on the learner and
\par
on local circumstances and the indicators of impact will tend to be localised and specific. To
\par
some extent learning in an information context can be seen as a decision-making process in
\par
that the learner has choices to make and will tend to make those choices in an individualistic
\par
way depending on previous experiences, motivation, confidence, information handling skills,
\par
and so on2. Studies of real-world decision-making indicate that the point at which information
\par
may influence a decision will depend on the accumulated experience, knowledge, values and
\par
preferences of the decision-maker and not simply on their awareness of a particular piece of
\par
information3. The way in which information is interpreted and used, and the timescale for
\par
information to have an impact, will vary from individual to individual. Thus the indicators
\par
selected should, ideally, allow for the assessment of SLRC impacts which may be direct or
\par
indirect, immediate or longer term.
\par
The complexity is compounded by the need to examine learning in its broadest sense (see
\par
Sections 2.2 and 3.2) identifying indicators which are appropriate to a wide range of learning
\par
experiences. While some may relate to the uptake and use of information others will relate
\par
more to motivation, interaction and communication with others.
\par
The choice of appropriate indicators will also be influenced by the decisions made by the
\par
teacher as an intermediary in the learning experience. Local interpretation of the curriculum,
\par
local setting of learning goals, and individual teaching styles will undoubtedly influence the
\par
kind of value and impact the SLRC will have. Conversely, it is only by developing an
\par
understanding of the potential for SLRC impact on the learning experience that the librarian
\par
and teacher can plan effectively to ensure that the learning goals of the SLRC are appropriate
\par
to the pedagogical approaches and to local teaching and learning styles. The work of
\par
Streatfield and Markless4 clearly points to the wide variation in teaching approaches within
\par
schools, which will require appropriate but differing information and library provision. This
\par
once again stresses the need for research which will not simply assess impact in general terms
\par
but will provide frameworks for individual schools to evaluate the contributions made by their
\par
own SLRC within their own particular learning and educational environment. This has been a
\par
central consideration in the development of the approach taken within this study.
\par
It is clear, then, that the examination of the context of the "Information Use Environment" and
\par
identification of appropriate indicators of impact requires an understanding of the uses and
\par
expectations of the SLRC from the perspective of the learner as well as those involved in
\par
7mediating the learning experience, i.e. the librarians and teachers. However, it was recognised
\par
that this in itself was likely to raise some challenges within the study. Both teachers and
\par
librarians have some responsibility for the effective uptake of information and library
\par
provision in relation to the curriculum. Yet little clear guidance is provided to teachers (in, for
\par
example, curriculum guidelines) on what their expectations or plans should be when using the
\par
SLRC to support the curriculum or learning. Similarly while librarians have guidelines and
\par
standards to follow5 these tend to relate more to outputs (services and targets) and do very
\par
little to help relate such services and outputs to the goals of learners and teachers. Therefore,
\par
while it was important to study impact on learning within a framework, which represented the
\par
uses, demands and expectations of the school as a whole, it was clear that defining what these
\par
expectations were in any degree of specificity might prove problematic. The approach taken
\par
was to conduct semi-structured focus groups with teachers and pupils and interviews with
\par
librarians to establish the framework of learning perceptions.
\par
\textbf{3.2 SCOPE OF IMPACT ASSESSMENT}
\par
A further element of complexity is associated with the fact that the study sought to identify
\par
the scope and breadth of impact of the SLRC rather than impact of particular resources or
\par
information. The outputs from the SLRC which may impact on the learning experience are
\par
broad and varied. Alongside the provision of information, the SLRC provides planned
\par
instruction in information skills, individualised advice in the handling and presentation of
\par
information, provides advice and support within planned curriculum activities, promotion of
\par
reading for pleasure, afterschool activities and clubs, opportunities for library helpers and
\par
prefects, support for the professional development of the teacher, a social environment for
\par
meeting outwith timetabled classes, and so on.
\par
One way to make the process manageable is to restrict impact studies to specific aspects of
\par
the information and library service. It is interesting in this respect that recent guidance from
\par
Information Management Associates on " \emph{Best Value} and Better Performance in Libraries" in
\par
the school and public sector, clearly signal the need for managers to restrict impact
\par
assessment to key aspects of the information and library service and not to attempt to assess
\par
impact of the whole service6. This follows also the approach taken in many previous impact
\par
studies where the focus has often been restricted to the impact of specific information
\par
provision. For example, attempts to examine impact of information services in the health
\par
sector (e.g. Marshall7 and Urquhart8), financial sector (e.g. Marshall9) or on development (e.g.
\par
Menou10) have tended to examine the issues from the perspective of the impact of information
\par
on decision making.
\par
Moreover, as Streatfield and Markless point out, to be useful in any particular evaluation of
\par
impact indicators should:
\par
"\emph{be as few as possible}
\par
\emph{allow meaningful comparisons to be made over time}
\par
\emph{cover significant parts of the activities of the service}[\emph{...}]
\par
\emph{reflect the existence of competing priorities."11}
\par
This being the case, the study sought to provide guidance for individual schools to develop a
\par
manageable and practical approach by focusing on particular aspects of the SLRC's work
\par
while still providing a broad framework for considering the impact of the SLRC as a whole.
\par
The approach adopted was a multi-site case study design in which specific examples of
\par
learning tasks, identified during the discussions with practitioners, were used as the case
\par
units. The analysis of these specific examples was complemented by relatively open reflection
\par
on the overall impact by practitioners and learners in focus groups and interviews. This
\par
allowed for an iterative building and refining of a framework which describes the ways in
\par
8which the SLRC can impact on the learning experience, based on the evidence of this study,
\par
and the ways in which schools can begin to monitor the impact of their own SLRC.
\par
\textbf{3.3 SOURCES OF EVIDENCE}
\par
Any study which seeks to examine the \emph{outcomes} of the use of an information and library
\par
service, faces further challenges in gaining insights into processes and experiences which may
\par
take place well outwith the initial library visit or transaction.
\par
Previous studies of the impact of information on decision-making have often relied on users
\par
being able to isolate and make judgements about the impact of information distinct from all
\par
the other influences on decision-making. Thus Marshall's work relied on self-reporting by
\par
clinicians on the way in which specific information gained from their information and library
\par
service had enhanced their ability to make sound judgements and decisions related to the
\par
diagnosis and treatment of patients12. Similarly, Usherwood's study of the impact of the public
\par
library13 relied on adult respondents, who identified the potential breadth of the impact of a
\par
library and information service in which the wider social as well as informational impacts can
\par
be seen. However, the question arises as to whether it is feasible and practical to build this
\par
kind of approach into a study of the impact of the information and library service within a
\par
school.
\par
Such studies provide useful pointers for examining the impact of information and library
\par
services in the educational sector but care has to be taken when attempting to adapt their
\par
methods for a school environment, when dealing with younger learners and a very broad
\par
range of potential areas for impact. Self-assessment of impact can be difficult enough for
\par
users when faced with relatively isolated instances of information and decisions, but is made
\par
much more problematic when dealing with the very wide ranging learning experiences of
\par
interest in the present study.
\par
In addition, previous impact studies in other sectors have tended to focus on mature adult
\par
users, whereas many of the users of the SLRC will be relatively immature and are likely to
\par
find it much more difficult to articulate their ideas, thoughts and feelings about the learning
\par
experience. Thus, while it was important to attempt to gain some feedback from learners,
\par
teachers and librarians themselves, it was also important to develop an approach which was
\par
not solely reliant on individuals' ability to isolate and make judgements about the SLRC
\par
distinct from all the other influences on learning.
\par
The approach taken was:
\par
{$\bullet$} one of triangulation, gathering data from different sources (learner, teacher and librarian)
\par
using a range of data collection techniques some of which relied on self-reporting, as
\par
well as direct observation and examination of the recorded (usually written) work of
\par
learners;
\par
{$\bullet$} not to attempt to separate impacts due to SLRC input from all the other influences, but
\par
instead to monitor the learning experiences taking place in \emph{SLRC related activities}. (This
\par
became particularly important after it became clear that the teachers and librarians had
\par
quite general expectations and plans for SLRC activities and that it was going to be
\par
extremely difficult to develop a detailed picture prior to case studies of how the SLRC
\par
activity "fitted" into the planned curricular activities. (see Section 4.3.2))
\par
Thus the study sought evidence of the kinds of learning which a range of SLRC-related
\par
activities could potentially stimulate, encourage and support, with the knowledge that the
\par
impact of the SLRC will itself be influenced by a wide range of other factors in the school.
\par
9This approach, once again, places emphasis on the interaction between the work of the SLRC
\par
and that of the teacher in the classroom.
\par
\textbf{3.4 THE PROCESS OF IMPACT ASSESSMENT}
\par
Finally, the process of assessing impact was considered as important to the research as finding
\par
actual evidence of impact on learning. Examination of the case units considered how the
\par
SLRC can contribute effectively to the learning experience and had the potential to give
\par
examples of how SLRC input might contribute to effective learning. However, as discussed
\par
above, the impact of any SLRC on learning will be context specific and indeed will change
\par
with time. The study therefore sought to provide both examples and suitable frameworks or
\par
guidance as a starting point for schools to examine the impact of their own SLRC over time.
\par
With this in mind, it was not the intention to draw comparisons between the relative impact of
\par
different SLRCs. Instead the study sought to identify the range of learning impacts, as well as
\par
specific examples of impact, which could reasonably be expected to be produced in any
\par
SLRC which aspired to the standards of resource provision and staffing expected within a
\par
"good" SLRC. For this reason, the SLRCs chosen for the study were valued by their schools
\par
and considered themselves to be broadly complying with, or at least actively aspiring to, the
\par
standards set out in the recently published CoSLA document "Standards for School Library
\par
Resource Services in Scotland."14 Each had a professionally qualified librarian, provided a
\par
broad range of learning resources, had capacity for individual, group and class activities and
\par
had some planned input to the development of information literacy.
