1112\textbf{4. METHODOLOGY}
\par
The Research i was undertaken in two phases:
\par
{$\bullet$} a series of focus groups with teachers and pupils, and interviews with librarians, to
\par
examine the use, expectations and perceptions of the potential impact of the SLRC on
\par
learning. More specifically the feedback from this phase provided the basis for the
\par
development of frameworks describing:
\par
\"y pupils', teachers' and librarians' perceptions of the ways in which the SLRC
\par
contributes to the learning experience and the benefits they might expect from use of
\par
the SLRC;
\par
\"y examples of potential indicators which could be used to assess the impact on the
\par
quality of the learning experience;
\par
{$\bullet$} case studies of specific SLRC related activities in order to assess the impact of the SLRC
\par
on the learning experience and factors which influence that impact. As well as providing
\par
examples of effective learning taking place in SLRC related activities, this phase allowed:
\par
\"y the verification and refinement of the initial frameworks;
\par
\"y evaluation of the process involved in monitoring and assessing the learning
\par
experience within the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{4.1 SELECTION OF SCHOOLS}
\par
The Research Project is set in the context of the Scottish educational system and schools for
\par
inclusion were restricted to secondary schools in mainland Scotland. This provided a
\par
cohesive educational and curriculum context within which to examine learning as well as a
\par
range of geographical locations that could be visited regularly within the timescale of the
\par
project. In addition, the Scottish Library Association acted as a collaborative partner
\par
throughout the study, providing advice and support in identifying contacts and opportunities
\par
to disseminate information to, and gain informal feedback from, Heads of School Library
\par
Services across Scotland.
\par
However, in order to ensure that the study was seen as relevant across as wide a basis as
\par
possible, representatives of the ILS and educational communities in England and Scotland
\par
were included in the Advisory Group, which comprised representatives from:
\par
{$\bullet$} the school library profession;
\par
{$\bullet$} the teaching profession, in particular senior management in schools;
\par
{$\bullet$} Scottish Library Association;
\par
{$\bullet$} Learning and Teaching Scotland, the main curriculum development and guidance body in
\par
Scotland;
\par
{$\bullet$} the English and Welsh ILS community;
\par
{$\bullet$} the research community;
\par
{$\bullet$} the funding agency.
\par
The main criteria for selecting participating schools were that:
\par
{$\bullet$} the school felt their SLRC met the standards set out in the recently published document
\par
by CoSLA1
\par
;
\par
i Research(er) and Project used with capital letters refer to the Impact of SLCR on Learning Project and
\par
Research to distinguish this from research and projects carried out by pupils in the case studies.
\par
13{$\bullet$} teachers and senior management as well as librarians were willing to commit some time
\par
to the project.
\par
Fliers and brief questionnaires [Appendix 1] were sent to the Heads of School Library
\par
Services in mainland Scotland for distribution to schools via their librarians. The
\par
questionnaire was designed to:
\par
{$\bullet$} identify schools who might be interested and committed to the project;
\par
{$\bullet$} ensure that the schools selected measured up, at least in part, to the standards felt
\par
appropriate for a school library, following the guidelines set out by CoSLA in the
\par
document ``Standards for School Library Services in Scotland''.
\par
The distribution of the questionnaires was left to the individual School Library Services.
\par
Some sent a copy to all the school libraries under their jurisdiction while others selected
\par
schools that were either likely to be interested or had a good reputation. It was understood that
\par
some were sent directly to the librarians concerned while others were sent to the head teachers
\par
for consideration.
