\textbf{4.2 FOCUS GROUPS}
\par
\textbf{4.2.1 Design}
\par
The aims of the focus groups and interviews were:
\par
{$\bullet$} to establish the perceptions of teachers and senior management, librarians and pupils of
\par
the expected impact (benefits or not) of the SLRC;
\par
{$\bullet$} to identify where possible specific direct and indirect, short and long-term potential
\par
benefits to learning and how these might show up.
\par
The focus groups were semi-structured, employing a mixed strategy, which allowed for some
\par
individual reflection as well as group discussions and enabled a broad spectrum of evidence
\par
to be gathered. The discussions were recorded but identities were not, thus ensuring
\par
anonymity. This restricted comparative analysis between the focus groups and case study
\par
examples but anonymity was felt to be more important for some individuals.
\par
The groups were interviewed separately to allow for some comparative analysis of the
\par
perceptions of teachers, librarians and learners, and not because of concerns about lack of
\par
confidentiality of disclosures.
\par
The format and instruments for the focus groups were piloted at a local school where it was
\par
felt the approach and wording were acceptable.
\par
Focus group discussions were conducted during December 1999 and January and February
\par
2000.
\par
15\textbf{Librarian participants}
\par
Each librarian was given a semi-structured interview with open-ended questions for guidance
\par
[Appendix 2]. In the case of the job-share librarians both were present at the interview. A
\par
further group discussion was conducted during the first librarians' meeting, when all the
\par
participating librarians were invited to a meeting to discuss progress at the end of the focus
\par
group phase. No new evidence was gathered from this meeting.
\par
\textbf{Teacher participants}
\par
Each school was asked to invite six to eight members of staff to participate in the focus group
\par
discussion. A range of curriculum areas was requested and when possible one member of
\par
senior management and/or a head of department. Non teaching staff were also encouraged but
\par
none were represented.
\par
The schools varied in their approach to selecting participants. Sometimes the librarian invited
\par
keen users and in other cases the member of senior management with responsibility for the
\par
SLRC selected appropriate members of staff. The schools where senior management took an
\par
active interest in the focus group discussion tended to have more productive discussions. It
\par
also became apparent that some of the librarians who were left to organise the groups
\par
experienced difficulties in timetabling and commitment.
\par
The participating teachers were given a letter in advance of the focus group and asked to
\par
consider how they used the SLRC and what they felt the learning outcomes might be
\par
[Appendix 3].
\par
The discussion, lasting approximately one hour, began with teachers explaining what they
\par
used the SLRC for. They were then prompted to reflect on what they would expect or hope
\par
the learning outcomes to be and to try to break the outcomes further into direct, indirect,
\par
immediate and delayed outcomes. A ``learning outcome'' grid [Appendix 4] was used to help
\par
them think this through and some of the teachers either completed the grid at the time or
\par
returned it at a later date after reflection. The grid served its purpose of trying to make the
\par
teachers think beyond their immediate requirements but feedback from the teachers at the end
\par
and through the librarians indicated that they found the grids difficult or confusing to use.
\par
\textbf{Pupil participants}
\par
Each school was invited to select six to eight senior pupils to participate in focus group
\par
discussions. It was considered that senior pupils would be able to reflect back over a number
\par
of years of using the SLRC and might be in a better position to communicate their ideas than
\par
junior pupils. However, this caused some discussion because many of the librarians felt the
\par
senior pupils were not always the most frequent users or were tied up with exams. In two
\par
schools both senior and junior pupils were present together. In one of these schools, where
\par
senior management had been involved, the discussion was productive because the pupils were
\par
given the opportunity to meet each other and discuss what was required before the event. In
\par
the other school, the mix of ages and maturity was felt to limit the discussion to a certain
\par
extent.
\par
The pupils were given more structured prompting to encourage discussion and reflection and
\par
the discussions usually lasted about half an hour. At the end of the session each pupil was
\par
asked to write their individual ideas down, both to give more structure to the views and to
\par
encourage the less confident pupils to express themselves [Appendix 5].
\par
Some pupils were reluctant to participate in the discussion, indicating their relief after the tape
\par
recorder had been switched off.
\par
16\textbf{4.2.2 Logistics and quality of discussion}
\par
Librarians found it difficult to gather teachers together at the same time because of
\par
timetabling and cover issues. Schools in which senior management was involved found this
\par
easier.
\par
The quality of the discussion varied depending on the numbers, key members of staff and the
\par
preparation of the teachers in the groups, and the confidence and mix in the pupil groups.
\par
School C, where one male and one female pupil from each of the S2-S6 year groups were
\par
involved, had prepared the pupils by getting them together, introducing each other and
\par
discussing the issues in advance. In School E the mix of pupils caused minor problems of
\par
distraction and intimidation.
\par
The schools where the senior management have an active interest in the SLRC and a member
\par
of senior management was present tended to be more focused in their discussion.
\par
\textbf{4.2.3 Focus group analysis}
\par
Full verbatim transcription of tapes was not considered necessary for the purposes of this
\par
stage of the study but detailed notes were made from each focus group or interview to allow
\par
the main ideas and perceptions to be identified. The partial transcripts were analysed
\par
manually. Main ideas from the transcripts were noted and the school and participant group
\par
code was recorded against the idea. The transcripts were not coded according to individual
\par
participants' contribution.
\par
The findings were discussed with the Advisory Group and any observations taken into
\par
account.
\par
\textbf{4.3 CASE STUDY DESIGN}
\par
\textbf{4.3.1 Choice of case study units}
\par
During the focus group discussions teachers were identified who were keen to make effective
\par
use of the SLRC, who worked well with their librarian and who might be interested in
\par
participating in some action research. The librarians were invited to identify curriculum units
\par
or activities being run during the case study period where teachers could be approached with a
\par
view to collaborating in the case study research with the librarian. The aim in selecting case
\par
study units was to attempt to cover a breadth of SLRC use and age range, while taking into
\par
account the logistics for visits.
\par
A variety of potential curriculum related projects and library skills courses were identified
\par
involving first and second year pupils and one information skills course was identified in
\par
which third year pupils were involved. The Researchers were interested in investigating the
\par
impact of some of the significant areas of SLRC input that are less directly linked to the
\par
curriculum. Case study units were selected to reflect the impact of the SLRC on the
\par
promotion of reading for pleasure; the impact of the SLRC on pupil librarians' learning; and
\par
an attempt was made to try to get a clearer picture of the impact of ad hoc use of the SLRC.
\par
Curriculum related project work is part of the 5-14 curriculum, and a few subjects (for
\par
instance Religious and Moral Education) continue investigations up to Standard Grade. In the
\par
Higher Curriculum investigations have an important role in most subjects. Curriculum based
\par
project courses were chosen to cover a variety of subjects and year groups and it was
\par
expected to see evidence of previous experience of using the SLRC in these case study units.
\par
All the focus group schools had a skills course in the first year and some form of follow up
\par
course in the senior years. It was decided to target the middle years for a skills course because
\par
there were fewer project options available in the Research timescale. The lower school skills
\par
17courses vary in emphasis across the schools but they generally cover some form of
\par
introduction to the SLRC and the resources and begin to tackle the information handling skills
\par
of finding appropriate information, note taking, reading for information and bibliographic
\par
details. The emphasis on the middle years skills course is on developing the research skills,
\par
and organisation and time management associated with study skills.
\par
The SLRC has traditionally had close links with the English department and the library
\par
profession has traditionally played an important part in the promotion of literature as well as
\par
providing access to information. The national curriculum and political trends have highlighted
\par
the need to maintain the significant part libraries can make to encourage a reading habit and
\par
an enjoyment of reading for pleasure as well as for study purposes. The 5-14, Standard and
\par
Higher Curriculum all include the encouragement of reading for pleasure and information and
\par
this goes hand in hand with government initiatives of lifelong learning, National Year of
\par
Reading, literacy hour in English primary schools and an emphasis in encouraging reading to
\par
community groups through the public libraries. Standard Grade and Higher English require
\par
detailed reading of a variety of genres, i.e. poetry, drama, novels, short stories, Scottish
\par
works, and critical analysis of them. This is based upon the foundation of the 5-14 Reading
\par
Strands aimed at encouraging reading for enjoyment, reading for information, reflection on
\par
the writer's ideas and craft, an awareness of genre and knowledge about language2
\par
.
\par
English teachers in the focus groups suggested that the SLRC helped pupils select reading
\par
material and that this indirectly helped the understanding of characterisation and plot. Some
\par
of the focus group SLRCs had more involvement in the promotion of reading for pleasure
\par
than others; in two schools the librarians' role was thought to be significant and one of these
\par
was chosen for this case study unit. Although the school was involved in encouraging reading
\par
in both the lower and senior years and the first year library course was considered the most
\par
suitable in terms of timing, input and contact with pupils and staff.
\par
Pupil librarians were encouraged in five of the focus group schools and in two of these
\par
schools the pupil librarians were seen to play a prominent role. Discussions with the Advisory
\par
Group established that this was an area where the SLRC was thought to provide a significant
\par
role in preparing pupils for work responsibilities as well as supporting other forms of learning
\par
and social development. It was agreed that this would make a suitable contribution to the
\par
understanding of the impact on learning as a case study unit.
\par
Another significant area of SLRC support is providing facilities for personal, leisure and
\par
study activities, for both pupils and staff, before, during and after school. Lunchtime use in
\par
many of the focus group SLRCs was considerable and it was decided that an attempt would
\par
be made to assess the impact of the SLRC on the more individual learning experiences. The
\par
aim was to conduct this in all the case study schools over a short period of time during the
\par
lunch break.
\par
The use of careers information is another aspect of the SLRC and this was considered as a
\par
possible case study unit but none of the schools provided obvious opportunities for this and
\par
the learning experiences of some of the other activities provided more scope for examination.
\par
\textbf{4.3.2 Methodology}
\par
The aim of the Research was to take a grounded approach inviting those involved in each case
\par
study to work with the Research team to select appropriate aspects of learning and associated
\par
indicators to suit the local learning context. The framework [see Section 5.4.1] developed
\par
from the focus groups was to provide the starting point. The design of each case study would
\par
therefore be determined by the local conditions and discussions with the librarians and
\par
teachers involved was important in order to establish the potential impact and decide the
\par
appropriate methods of monitoring before implementing the design. However, the
\par
18unpredictable nature of school life meant that plans were often changed unexpectedly and
\par
there was not always the opportunity for the full discussions expected with the teachers. It
\par
was expected to take a qualitative approach for the case studies, using a variety of methods,
\par
such as questionnaires to elicit responses from users, observations of learners and the analysis
\par
of records of assessments and achievements. It was expected that the teachers and librarians
\par
would be involved in the data collection but not all the teachers were able to give the
\par
commitment or time to the Project and the librarians were not always comfortable with the
\par
data collection process.
\par
Where the teachers and librarians were fully involved in the initial discussions [see
\par
Appendices 6-9 for planning instruments], it became apparent that there was a lack of
\par
understanding of the learning goals expected from the use of the SLRC. The Research team
\par
had to take a more observational approach to the case studies than was originally intended to
\par
try and understand what the learning processes and their indicators might be. After initial
\par
discussions with the teacher and/or librarian, the Researcher used the initial impact
\par
framework from the focus group phase [see Section 5, figure 2] to complete a specific grid
\par
with potential learning experiences and possible indicators for that particular activity [see
\par
Appendix 9 for an example of a completed framework]. This acted as a basic framework to
\par
work with but was neither exclusive nor prescriptive. The Researcher observed pupils'
\par
learning experiences using descriptive recording methods and schedules detailing specific
\par
activities where possible [Appendix 10]. Pupils were questioned about their experiences,
\par
sometimes informally as they worked and in other instances a questionnaire [see example
\par
Appendix 11] was used with both open and closed questions. The questionnaire was based
\par
upon the aspects of learning identified in the impact framework that was developed from the
\par
focus group discussions. Wherever possible the jotters or notes, submitted work and
\par
assessment grades were examined in consultation with the teacher. A schedule [Appendix 12]
\par
was used to check the work against possible criteria drawn up to reflect the initial learning
\par
themes. The participating teachers and librarians were asked to reflect on the impact of the
\par
SLRC on learning during the project and were questioned informally about their reactions at
\par
the end of the activity [Appendices 14 and 16].
\par
It was decided to adapt the Marshall3 approach to look at the ad hoc use of the SLRC by both
\par
pupils and staff; looking at the impact of a specific visit to the resource centre and how the
\par
information or resource might have had an influence on the user. Marshall, and other
\par
researchers4, looked at the impact of a piece of information on the decision making of doctors
\par
in the medical profession and managers in a variety of other financial and business sectors.
\par
This research used a critical incident technique, asking practitioners to assess the value of
\par
information to their particular need. A questionnaire was designed in a simplified format,
\par
based on the potential learning experiences. It was piloted at one of the local focus group
\par
schools not involved in the case studies and changes were made to the design and wording to
\par
avoid ambiguity. The questionnaire [Appendix 13] was tried again in two of the case study
\par
schools, but had to be abandoned. The intrusive nature of the questioning interfered with the
\par
personal nature of pupil and staff use of the SLRC on an ad hoc basis. Pupils and staff found
\par
it difficult to see their use of the SLRC in terms of learning and the interruption of a leisure
\par
activity caused unease. The use made of information or resources in the SLRC by pupils in
\par
schools does not necessarily have the same foundation of need to satisfy a problem or to
\par
enable a decision to be made that is the case for professionals seeking information. The use of
\par
the careers library and the staff development materials is the nearest the SLRC comes to
\par
fulfilling the decision making function of the professional library but the decision was made
\par
not to examine these as case studies in this Research.
\par
\textbf{4.3.3 Case study analysis}
\par
The data for each case study was gathered and examined together as a separate unit. Initial
\par
analysis was begun as each type of data was collected and examples of impact and the
\par
19conditioning factors began to emerge. When all the data was collated for each case unit, the
\par
value and relevance of the indicators used was examined and initial conclusions about the
\par
learning experience and impact of the SLRC were drawn. Each case unit was also evaluated
\par
for the effectiveness of the monitoring process and revisions were made to the instruments
\par
and approach for use in the next case unit both according to these observations and
\par
considering the context of the next case unit. [See Appendices 17-25 for questionnaire
\par
responses, sample observation notes and statistical data from reader records and jotters.]
\par
During the final writing up of all the case study units, further analysis of the evidence of
\par
impact and the indicators was made and more general conclusions were drawn from the
\par
accumulated findings and conditioning factors. Finally, the monitoring techniques were
\par
assessed as a whole and conclusions drawn about the relative merits in terms of practical
\par
application, evidence sought and the quality of data obtained.
\par
A second meeting was arranged for all the participating librarians to give them feedback on
\par
the case study investigation and to get feedback from them about the usefulness of the initial
\par
framework as a potential planning and evaluation tool.
\par
The framework and findings were discussed with the Advisory Group and comments were
\par
noted.
\par
Finally the findings from the case studies and feedback from librarians and Advisory Group
\par
were used to revise and review the initial impact framework as a way of a) summarising the
\par
learning experiences and potential indicators of impact and b) providing a starting point for
\par
schools themselves to evaluate the impact of their SLRC.
