\textbf{5.1 FINDINGS FROM TEACHERS' FOCUS GROUPS}
\par
The curriculum subjects were well represented in the focus group discussions. Different
\par
subject teachers placed different emphasis on their use and expected outcomes but during
\par
discussion and on the individual sheets the majority expressed an awareness of issues beyond
\par
their own subject areas. Despite this, the teachers found it difficult to think of impact as direct
\par
and indirect but some examples of the less tangible delayed and indirect impact were
\par
identified. The English teachers typically placed an emphasis on fiction and library skills and
\par
the skills were taken up by Guidance and science. RE and social subjects needed information.
\par
Art seems to use the resources in an ad hoc way and in one school the art teacher also wanted
\par
to develop search skills and a respect for books. Modern languages had a variety of uses
\par
including using the Internet to access French and German newspapers by Sixth Year Study
\par
pupils in school E, using the video conferencing facilities in school F and encouraging
\par
cultural awareness in school A.
\par
22The librarian's role was mentioned in all the teacher focus groups. The librarian was seen as
\par
having a role in supporting teaching; finding and supplying resources; supporting teachers in
\par
their learning, especially ICT skills; supporting pupil use of all resources; a role of central
\par
maintenance and distribution of information within the school and being prepared to
\par
participate in activities beyond the call of duty.
\par
It was recognised by many teachers that the personality of the librarian provides the basis for
\par
positive or negative experiences when using the SLRC and therefore has an impact on the
\par
learning potential.
\par
\textbf{5.1.1 Teachers' use of the SLRC}
\par
\textbf{English}
\par
All the schools had representatives from the English department but the emphasis placed on
\par
the SLRC varied, some stressed the importance of developing information skills or the variety
\par
of fiction titles to encourage reading for pleasure in the lower years and others emphasised the
\par
need for up to date information on topical subjects for discursive essays and the range of
\par
quality literature for critical analysis by senior pupils. Some of the English teachers had close
\par
links with the librarians and felt their use of the SLRC to develop skills was a service to other
\par
departments but others had less easy relations with the SLRC. One teacher expressed concern
\par
that her department insisted on silent reading in the SLRC when this was felt to be an
\par
inappropriate use in their particular circumstances (a large area which could be used by
\par
several classes at once).
\par
\textbf{Modern Languages}
\par
One teacher was introducing a cultural element to language teaching in the early years to
\par
enable the pupils to put their language learning into context. Another used the SLRC for
\par
video and audio conferencing to teach remote pupils and expressed a reliance on the librarian
\par
for technical support. The other two language teachers saw the value in accessing foreign
\par
newspapers via the Internet for use by senior pupils and they assumed the pupils had the skills
\par
to carry out this task.
\par
\textbf{Home Economics}
\par
The home economics departments use the SLRC to support curriculum related projects, to
\par
develop information handling skills either done formally or informally to reinforce skills
\par
developed in the library induction or skills courses covered earlier. The opportunity was taken
\par
by this department to encourage group work and the SLRC was thought to provide a suitable
\par
environment for collaborative work.
\par
\textbf{Religious and Moral Education}
\par
This is one of the few subject areas where investigations are continuous throughout the year
\par
groups. The approach taken by these teachers was different, depending on their own
\par
experience and the school policy. One teacher was quite clear that pupils needed grounding in
\par
information handling for successful research outcomes and had developed a close relationship
\par
with the librarian. Another couple of teachers saw it as an opportunity to introduce ICT skills,
\par
particularly using the Internet. Yet another teacher assumed the pupils already had the skills
\par
required to make effective independent use of the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Science}
\par
The science teachers were divided in their use as a means of developing ICT skills for data
\par
presentation and those wanting to access information for project work in the lower years.
\par
They were all aware of the need to develop information handling skills even if they did not
\par
have a formal means of tackling this process.
\par
23\textbf{Social Subjects }(History, Geography, Modern Studies)
\par
These subjects tended to use the SLRC for project work in geography and history in the lower
\par
school, for more sophisticated historical investigations by Sixth Year Studies pupils and the
\par
need for current information from the Internet to support modern studies. All these subject
\par
teachers recognised the importance of developing the skills to access information, with some
\par
indicating they could see the evidence of skills introduced in subjects lower down the school
\par
in their pupils' work. However, these teachers, although aware of the need for the
\par
development of skills in order to prepare pupils for effective learning, were not in a position
\par
to quantify any progress made in this area of learning.
\par
\textbf{Guidance}
\par
Guidance teachers in some of the schools mentioned their role in reinforcing study skills in S3
\par
and some saw this as an opportunity to use resources while others concentrated use on career
\par
information in the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Support for Learning}
\par
One support for learning teacher used the librarian as an expert in sourcing information to aid
\par
the special needs of individual pupils; another was keen to emphasis the social development
\par
pupils gained from access to the SLRC; and this department was also aware of role the SLRC
\par
has in providing appropriate material for all abilities enabling the learning potential of less
\par
able pupils to be realised. One support for learning base adjoined the SLRC helping the
\par
teacher to motivate pupils by encouraging them to find out interesting facts whenever
\par
possible. This teacher also thought the proximity of the base to the SLRC enabled pupils to
\par
avoid the stigma of being seen to enter the base directly.
\par
\textbf{Art and Technical}
\par
The teachers in these departments used the SLRC for ad hoc sourcing of images and
\par
background information. Two teachers were keen to use the opportunity to encourage pupils
\par
to explore resources and understand the concept of finding information or images by thinking
\par
round the possibilities and another used the librarian as a gateway to external sourcing of
\par
artefacts to draw and hoped to encourage pupils to develop \emph{"their own personality...their own}
\par
\emph{expressive feelings about themselves"} through exploration of the works of others. This latter
\par
teacher expressed the view that the Internet access in the classroom had diminished her need
\par
for the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Mathematics}
\par
One school had a representative from the maths department and this teacher wanted to
\par
encourage pupils to view maths not just as an academic subject but also as a subject with an
\par
interesting history. This teacher was also aware of the potential value of stressing the SLRC
\par
as a place to revise and to promote the study habit.
\par
\textbf{PE,} \textbf{Music and Drama}
\par
Guidance teachers represented PE but PE use of the SLRC was not mentioned in discussion.
\par
Music and drama departments were not represented in the focus groups but two librarians
\par
mentioned use by these departments, one mentioned a CD-ROM course which was developed
\par
to support music and another that the drama teacher was involved in lunchtime support.
\par
\textbf{5.1.2 Teachers' perceptions of the impact on learning}
\par
The teachers' perceptions of how the SLRC can contribute to learning can be summarised as
\par
the learning impacts identified below but it must be remembered that this has been compiled
\par
from analysis of all the responses from all the focus groups, it does not necessarily represent
\par
the perceptions of any one individual.
\par
24\textbf{Learning impacts perceived by teachers}
\par
{$\bullet$} The acquisition of \textbf{information }to complete a subject specific task.
\par
{$\bullet$} The teachers\textbf{ }thought\textbf{ skills development} was a high priority for pupils using the SLRC.
\par
The skills covered a range of library, information, ICT and reading skills.
\par
{$\bullet$} Despite the inclusion of English teachers in every discussion group (no other subject was
\par
represented in each school) there was not a high emphasis on the \textbf{encouragement of}
\par
\textbf{reading} for pleasure but there was awareness that the SLRC provided a variety of
\par
suitable material. Where reading for pleasure was mentioned it was viewed as a constant
\par
challenge to get reluctant readers into a reading habit.
\par
{$\bullet$} Using the SLRC was felt to \textbf{motivate} pupils to produce a piece of work, some teachers
\par
felt the introduction of computers of particular benefit in this and the SLRC has an
\par
important social role in making computers available to all pupils.
\par
{$\bullet$} Teachers recognised the role the SLRC has to play in encouraging \textbf{independent}
\par
\textbf{learning}, encouraging an awareness of learning as an activity outside the classroom and
\par
the confidence needed to pursue learning outside the confines of the school environment.
\par
{$\bullet$} Some teachers were aware of the \textbf{cross-curricular} nature of the SLRC, enabling
\par
maximum use of expensive or limited resources across subject areas and also the role in
\par
building skills to use these resources to be transferred across the curriculum and beyond
\par
school. Included in this could be the building of \textbf{social skills }of using appropriate
\par
behaviour in particular situations and an environment where co-operation and interaction
\par
is encouraged.
\par
{$\bullet$} There was widespread recognition of the important role the SLRC could contribute to
\par
the \textbf{social development and interpersonal skills} of vulnerable pupils by providing a
\par
safe environment with the opportunity to build confidence and make friends.
\par
{$\bullet$} The SLRC was seen to have a role in providing \textbf{equal opportunities} in terms of
\par
computer equipment, book resources or the just the understanding of the information
\par
world.
\par
{$\bullet$} Several teachers also highlighted aspects of the SLRC or the contribution of the librarian
\par
that could be considered \textbf{enriching} to school life and thus helping school ethos.
\par
These are an accumulation of ideas raised from across the spectrum of teachers and schools;
\par
individual teachers presented their perceptions in the context of their own experiences. One
\par
science teacher made the comment during the focus group discussion, \emph{"I think another issue}
\par
\emph{that has to be examined is the efficiency of the learning in the resource centre,''} suggesting
\par
awareness of the need for a more considered approach to SLRC use. The Research team
\par
thought the focus groups provided a welcome opportunity for teachers to come together and
\par
discuss their use and the learning potential of the SLRC, and feedback from the librarians
\par
confirmed this view. Some teachers clearly gave their use of the SLRC considerable thought
\par
already but did not always have the means to develop this beyond their own subject area and
\par
build this into an integrated approach across the curriculum. Some schools recognised the
\par
need for this to happen in order to maximise the potential learning. Other teachers used the
\par
SLRC to give pupils the opportunity to carry out research without checking that pupils had
\par
the necessary skills required in order to complete the task satisfactorily.
\par
The teachers were encouraged to think of themselves as learners and how the SLRC might
\par
have an impact in this capacity. Although, suggestions were not readily forthcoming there
\par
was an indication that the teachers used the resources for themselves for professional
\par
development, to support their teaching, to develop their own skills and for personal reading,
\par
whether fiction, non-fiction or newspapers. The librarians in two of the schools made a
\par
conscious effort to promote reading amongst the members of staff; one held a monthly book
\par
club and the other highlighted recommended titles through the Intranet. Several teachers
\par
mentioned using the resources to support their own children's learning.
\par
25\textbf{5.2 FINDINGS FROM THE PUPILS' FOCUS GROUPS}
\par
The quality of the discussions with the pupils was variable but analysis of the discussions and
\par
the individual SLRC Use Sheet [Appendix 5] completed at the end of the focus group
\par
sessions provided enough information to give a perception of how pupils view the
\par
contribution made by the SLRC to learning. Some pupils, as expected, were initially reluctant
\par
