\textbf{5.3 FINDINGS FROM THE LIBRARIANS' INTERVIEWS}
\par
The librarians were interviewed individually, except for the job share librarians in school A,
\par
who were interviewed together and their responses are taken as one. The discussion started
\par
with role of librarian, moving on to the objectives the librarians had for the pupils (and staff)
\par
as users and learners, and finally trying to gather their perceptions of how the SLRC impacts
\par
on learning and what the evidence might be.
\par
The librarians found it relatively easy to identify what they hoped pupils would achieve by
\par
using the SLRC, although some found it easier to give objectives for the SLRC rather than for
\par
the pupils. They were less sure of how to achieve these objectives or whether they were being
\par
achieved. They were not generally looking for evidence of learning or able to come up with
\par
the means of checking the learning, except librarian D who had collaborated with a teacher to
\par
identify criteria according to 5-14 curriculum for information skills. It was easier for them to
\par
identify where they felt their objectives were not being met. The main objective they
\par
identified was to develop confident and independent users.
\par
Collaboration with teaching staff was not always felt to be as good as it should or could be.
\par
The librarians were not always informed of the teacher's requirements and intended goals and
\par
they were not always informed of how effective the teacher found the experience. Librarian I
\par
expressed the idea that the more involved the librarian was in the planning the more smoothly
\par
a curriculum task progressed, and librarians A and F felt the more prepared the teacher was
\par
the more motivated the pupils were and the harder they worked. Librarian F was aware of the
\par
need for a whole school policy towards information skills.
\par
There appeared to be a lack of knowledge or emphasis on the process of finding information
\par
and more emphasis was placed on the library skills, with a few of the librarians citing the lack
\par
of a computerised catalogue for the pupils a disadvantage, inhibiting independence. The
\par
librarians generally held the view that the SLRC was no longer the place for the traditional
\par
silent reading. There was a general feeling that time and energy did not always allow more to
\par
be done.
