\textbf{5.4.1 Development of the impact framework}
\par
The potential learning impacts emerging during the focus group discussions and interviews
\par
were analysed and grouped using a number of well-established classifications relevant to the
\par
learning process. This was partly to allow some evaluation of the breadth of the potential
\par
learning impacts that were emerging at this stage and partly to provide a useful framework to
\par
facilitate further discussion and debate with teachers and librarians. The organisation of the
\par
potential learning impacts into a framework was not only seen as a way of describing what
\par
the participants were discussing in the focus groups but also intended to help practitioners
\par
identify how aspects of learning in the SLRC might fit into the curriculum. While it is not
\par
possible to claim that it encompasses all possible learning impacts nevertheless, as will be
\par
seen, the range of impacts identified was varied and broad in scope.
\par
30Initial attempts at classifying the learning impacts into some form of basic framework, looked
\par
at the work of Benjamin Bloom in his Taxonomy of Educational Objectives1 on the cognitive,
\par
affective and psychomotor domains of learning and at the more recent work of Carol
\par
Kuhlthau2 on the cognitive, affective and physical realms of learning. Thus, for instance,
\par
under the cognitive domain were placed the learning outcomes of finding and using new
\par
information, under affective were the ideas of motivation and specific skills, such as using a
\par
CD-ROM under the physical or psychomotor domains. The interpersonal skills, which were
\par
sited as potential impacts, were added as an additional category (see table 8).
\par
\textbf{Table 8 Initial classification of learning impacts}
\par
Higher Cognitive Improved achievement
\par
Understanding need to work
\par
Independence \& confidence to work independently
\par
Transference of skills, knowledge \& understanding
\par
Decision making
\par
Lower Cognitive Information skills
\par
Thinking, life, study skills
\par
New knowledge
\par
Reading skills
\par
Psychomotor or Physical Library skills
\par
ICT skills
\par
Affective Motivation
\par
Personal interests
\par
Interpersonal Social \& interpersonal skills
\par
This classification was then considered in relation to the objectives set out in Scottish
\par
curriculum documents, the context within which the Research was being conducted. The 5-14
\par
documentation indicates that attainment goals in all the curriculum areas are placed in the
\par
wider context of knowledge and understanding, skills, and aspects of personal and social
\par
development3
\par
. A more recent 5-14 document identifies these wider educational contexts as
\par
the knowledge, skills and understanding and the cross-curricular requirements of dispositions
\par
(towards learning, respect for oneself and others and social responsibility), core skills and
\par
capabilities,4 and this is echoed in the general guidelines "Curriculum Design for the
\par
Secondary Stages"5, which links the 5-14 curriculum to more advanced curriculum levels.
\par
The Research Project was being conducted in the context of a growing awareness of the need
\par
to evaluate the quality of services in schools, including the SLRC. It was decided, therefore,
\par
to examine how the various educational and curriculum classifications would compare with
\par
the way in which learning is described within the recently published performance indicator
\par
documents6. The Performance Indicator 3.2 looks at the "Quality of pupils' learning"
\par
identifying the themes of motivation, progress in learning, personal responsibility for learning
\par
and interaction with others. Figure 1 presents the initial hierarchical combination of these
\par
educational and curriculum classifications.
\par
This classification was then used in order to analyse and group the potential learning impacts
\par
identified during the focus group discussions and interviews. As already explained, the
\par
teachers had expressed some concern over the use of the term ``learning outcome'' and as a
\par
result the general phrase ``learning impact'' was used at this stage. In order to stimulate
\par
feedback on the more practical recognition of learning within the SLRC context, a further
\par
level of the framework attempted to provide examples of possible indicators which could be
\par
used to identify learning impact.
