\textbf{Figure 2 Aspects of learning areas upon which the SLRC can impact}
\par
The identification of learning indicators was something which was seen as important for the
\par
planning of the case studies. The original plan for the case study investigation had been to use
\par
the frameworks to help teachers and librarians to identify appropriate indicators to evaluate
\par
impact in specific learning activities. This had proved problematic during the focus groups
\par
and interviews themselves when the teachers and librarians had not been forthcoming with
\par
more specific ideas of how to identify the impact of the SLRC on learning. Several teachers
\par
expressed the view that the impact of the SLRC could not, and perhaps should not, be isolated
\par
from other impacts on learning and one expressed concern as to whether the impact the SLRC
\par
could be measured. It also appeared that teachers and librarians generally found it difficult to
\par
focus at a very specific level on the kind of learning experiences that they would associate
\par
with SLRC based activities.
\par
In an attempt to elicit further discussion and feedback on this issue, and to facilitate planning
\par
of case studies, the Research team examined the learning impacts and used them to identify
\par
\emph{possible} indicators of learning drawing also on the work of Kuhlthau8 as well as the
\par
performance indicator documents9 and 5-14 curriculum guidelines10. Both the list of learning
\par
impacts and the potential indicators of learning were classified using the figure 1
\par
classification. The resulting framework, which was to provide the basis for the case study
\par
investigations, is presented in Figure 2.
\par
This initial impact framework was sent back to the schools inviting comments from the
\par
participating members of staff. This approach did not elicit any significant feedback and the
\par
framework was then explained in face-to-face meetings with individual members of senior
\par
management, teachers and librarians. This second approach proved more successful and the
\par
members of senior management responded favourably to the learning experiences, identified
\par
during the focus groups as examples of impact of the SLRC on learning, to the potential
\par
indicators of learning, developed by the Research team, and the classification within the
\par
framework. However, it was thought that the framework appeared daunting and difficult to
\par
understand without detailed explanation. This exercise did little to confirm or validate the
\par
potential learning indicators other than to gain general approval and agreement that the
\par
frameworks had some potential for helping teachers and librarians start to explore the SLRC
\par
contribution to learning.
\par
At this stage it became apparent that the approach to the case studies would have to be altered
\par
to take account of the fact that it was unlikely that the Research team would be able to engage
\par
teachers and librarians in the specific identification of appropriate learning indicators for use
\par
within the SLRC. Instead the approach had to become a more open observation of the
\par
learning experiences of the pupils, seeking evidence and examples across the full breadth of
\par
the learning experience. In other words, rather than evaluating the learning experience within
\par
32the SLRC in relation to planned goals and specifically identified indicators, the role of the
\par
case studies was to enable more specific examples of learning and indicators to be identified.
\par
Thus the case study phase was to enable the initial framework to be refined and used to
\par
describe the kinds of learning experiences upon which a SLRC might expect, if not plan, to
\par
have an impact. Given this subtle shift in the nature of the Project outcomes, one further
\par
change to the terminology of the impact framework was made prior to its use within the case
\par
studies. As already explained, teachers had expressed concern over the use of the term
\par
``learning outcome'', which has a specific meaning in curriculum terminology, and this had
\par
been initially replaced in focus group discussions with the term ``learning impact''. However,
\par
in using and refining the framework during the case study phase the term ``learning
\par
experience'' was chosen. This was seen as a more appropriate description of the findings
\par
which emerged and signals the fact that, rather than providing measures of impact as such, the
\par
final framework (figure 3) identifies examples of more learning experiences upon which an
\par
SLRC might expect to have an impact.
\par
.
\par
