\textbf{Figure 2 Initial impact framework6. CASE STUDY FINDINGS}
\par
Each case study is introduced with brief factual information about the school and its SLRC.
\par
This is followed by a description of what the teacher and librarian were seeking to achieve
\par
and the activities they planned. One teacher forwarded a detailed lesson plan for her use of the
\par
SLRC to the Research Team and other information for these sections was accumulated from
\par
the initial discussions with teachers and librarians. The rationale for the methodology used in
\par
this Research Project has already been explained in section 4.3 but in each case study a brief
\par
summary of the specific methods used to examine the learning impacts is included to explain
\par
the variations in individual circumstances. The findings are then described in general terms of
\par
observation and the more specific evidence of impact. The evidence of impact has been
\par
broken down into the learning themes described in the Performance Indicator 3.2, the "quality
\par
of pupils' learning",1: motivation (" \emph{extent to which pupils are motivated by their learning}
\par
\emph{experience}"); progression ("\emph{progress in learning}"); independence ("\emph{personal responsibility}
\par
\emph{for learning, independent thinking, and active involvement in learning}"); and interaction
\par
("\emph{interaction with others}"). In the observation and evidence of impact sub-sections the
\par
evidence of impact on learning has been highlighted in bold italics and the corresponding
\par
indicators identified in the margin. This grouping was chosen because it was thought to
\par
describe the learning experience and can then be related to the way the curriculum is
\par
described in the official documentation; it was not chosen because of the specific use as
\par
performance indicators. Where appropriate these have given further sub-headings. The final
\par
section looks at the issues arising from the tracking or monitoring process and summarises the
\par
evidence of impact.
\par
The layout of each case study:
\par
{$\bullet$} School context - factual information about the participating school;
\par
{$\bullet$} School library resource centre- factual information about the SLRC;
\par
{$\bullet$} Introduction to the case study lesson plan - describing what the teacher or librarian was
\par
setting out to achieve and the activities relating to the aims;
\par
{$\bullet$} Case study methodology - describing the specific methods employed to examine the
\par
particular learning environment;
\par
{$\bullet$} Observation - general description of what happened;
\par
{$\bullet$} Evidence of impact - evidence of impact under the headings: Motivation; Progression;
\par
Independence and Interaction. These were subdivided still further where appropriate to
\par
describe more specific types of learning skills.
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion - examines the issues arising from the process of tracking or monitoring the
\par
evidence and summarises the examples of impact on learning.
\par
The Section 8 within the Case Study Findings examines a few examples of special events
\par
organised by the SLRC and the significant use made of the SLRC for ad hoc personal
\par
requirement. The special events in the SLRC were observed in order to investigate how
\par
impact might be indicated and examination of the ad hoc visits to the SLRC used a
\par
questionnaire based on Marshall's critical incident methodology2 [Appendix 12] already
\par
discussed in the methodology (Section 4.3.2).
