37\textbf{6.1 CASE STUDY 1}
\par
\textbf{SCHOOL C: S1 ENGLISH}
\par
\textbf{6.1.1 School context}
\par
\textbf{Location}
\par
School C serves a small town and the outlying country districts. The school
\par
has served the town and district since the early nineteenth century and
\par
moved to its present site on the edge of the town in 1965.
\par
\textbf{Size}
\par
Pupil roll of 1165 and approximately 125 members of staff.
\par
\textbf{Free meals}
\par
12\% free meal entitlement.
\par
\textbf{Bussed pupils}
\par
25\% bussed pupils.
\par
\textbf{Associated primaries}
\par
Ten (three other primaries have the option to send pupils).
\par
\textbf{School aim}
\par
"\emph{The general purpose of [C] Academy is to enable pupils to enrich their}
\par
\emph{lives by engaging in work and activities which will assist them to make}
\par
\emph{sense of their society, to make more sophisticated appraisals of their own}
\par
\emph{role in the world, and to cope with the demands and pressures which that}
\par
\emph{world imposes.}"
\par
\textbf{6.1.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
During the year 2000, the SLRC was extended to allow seating for 32
\par
pupils in the class area, including 12 networked computers, seating for 10
\par
in comfortable chairs and a study area with tables and study booths for 25
\par
pupils. The career resources are in an adjoining room with seating for 20
\par
pupils. The atmosphere is spacious, bright and welcoming.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
The SLRC is staffed by one chartered librarian, who has been in the post
\par
for approximately two years. The librarian has clerical assistance 2.5 hours
\par
per week, volunteer teacher help at lunchtimes and volunteer pupil
\par
librarians help whenever they are free before, after school and at lunchtime.
\par
The head teacher stressed the importance he placed on the SLRC. There is a
\par
library committee and keen interest from all members of senior
\par
management. This school places an emphasis on the development of
\par
information skills in the lower school with an integrated Information
\par
Technology Course (IT Skills Course) directed by senior management with
\par
input from the librarian.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The school made a conscious decision to have open access to the SLRC for
\par
all pupils. Lunchtime is particularly busy and is staffed with teacher
\par
volunteers. The SLRC is not open at break time and is not used extensively
\par
after school. Individual pupil use during class time is discouraged, except in
\par
the case of seniors.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
Some departments use the SLRC extensively while others are located too
\par
far away but all departments give positive encouragement for pupil use of
\par
the SLRC. Pupils are encouraged to work in the SLRC, not necessarily
\par
silently but with purpose, and games and general socialising are
\par
discouraged.
\par
38\textbf{6.1.3 Introduction to case study 1}
\par
The teacher involved in this case study was not a participant in the focus
\par
group discussions.
\par
\textbf{Lesson plan}
\par
\textbf{Subject}
\par
English, 5-14 functional writing to convey key facts.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
Two S1 classes taken by the same teacher, one class with 31 pupils, the
\par
other with 28 pupils. All the pupils have done other projects using the
\par
SLRC during the course of the year and one third of the pupils in each class
\par
were engaged in the library part of the integrated IT Skills Course during
\par
the case study period, which concentrated on types of information and the
\par
use of a variety of resources. The others will have covered the library
\par
modules in previous terms.
\par
\textbf{Aims}
\par
Each pupil was required to produce a poster or leaflet conveying interesting
\par
facts about an animal they had researched. The teacher specified her aims
\par
as the following:
\par
{$\bullet$} To produce a piece of functional writing for the pupils' folios
\par
(Functional writing, according to the 5-14 English Language
\par
Guidelines1, is used to convey information.);
\par
{$\bullet$} To encourage reading for information (skimming and scanning);
\par
{$\bullet$} Selecting information from a range of reference sources (practice in
\par
notetaking techniques);
\par
{$\bullet$} To practise redrafting work.
\par
The librarian's aim for the pupils was to ensure they found relevant
\par
information from appropriate resources.
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
The teacher initially booked the SLRC for both English periods for each S1
\par
class for one week. After the first week there was a break of a week while
\par
the pupils took part in a Readathon in class time.
\par
Towards the end of the first week the teacher decided more time would be
\par
needed for using the resources and booked more sessions in the SLRC. This
\par
extra time was eventually reduced when she and the librarian felt the pupils
\par
were beginning to get bored and had begun to exhaust the resources. The
\par
pupils spent four periods (of 50 minutes each) researching their animal in
\par
the SLRC.
\par
After gathering information in the SLRC, the teacher expected to give the
\par
pupils another week to complete the work in the classroom. Pupils were not
\par
set homework but were verbally encouraged to look for information beyond
\par
the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
The teacher consulted with the librarian beforehand to ensure there was
\par
enough appropriate material available in the SLRC on all the animals she
\par
had selected for research.
\par
39In the first session the teacher explained the task and handed out a card to
\par
each pupil with the name of an animal to research. The librarian introduced
\par
the new library arrangement and gave a refresher on the type of resources
\par
available, the use of index and content pages, the library catalogue and
\par
codebuster (an alphabetical chart of commonly used topics and their Dewey
\par
classification number). At the beginning of subsequent sessions the class
\par
was reminded of what they were trying to achieve.
\par
Pupils were allowed to sit in friendship groups and used the class or non-
\par
fiction area of the SLRC, and were only moved if the teacher felt there was
\par
too much distraction.
\par
The teacher encouraged all pupils to use the CD-ROMs, and a rotation of
\par
computer use was ensured.
\par
\textbf{Resources required}
\par
Pupils were given the freedom to use whatever resources they needed:
\par
reference books, non-fiction books, magazines, CD-ROMS, and the
\par
Internet.
\par
\textbf{Differentiation/extension}
\par
One class had a support for learning teacher with them for some of the
\par
research lessons. This teacher was also involved in delivering the IT Skills
\par
Course, which all S1 pupils complete.
\par
At the end of the project, some pupils were given a poem or piece of
\par
imaginative writing to do using the information found on the animal.
\par
\textbf{Assessment/evaluation}
\par
The submitted posters or leaflets were marked according to the 5-14
\par
criteria, which included looking at the information and its presentation in
\par
poster or leaflet form.
\par
Informal discussion about the sessions was carried out between the librarian
\par
and teacher during and after the project.
\par
\textbf{6.1.4 Case study methodology}
\par
The researcher observed the pupils at work using the resources in the SLRC
\par
and the first lesson back in the classroom. This involved descriptive and
\par
schedule recording.
\par
The jotters and final work were studied at the end of the project, to
\par
investigate the progress of the research process, and a checklist of possible
\par
learning experiences was used to aid data collection. In total 57 examples
\par
of submitted work were examined and 37 jotters were examined.
\par
The pupils in both classes completed a simple questionnaire (51 returned)
\par
at the end of the sessions in the SLRC to assess their views on using the
\par
SLRC and to provide an indication of what they felt they had learned.
\par
Discussions were carried out informally with the teacher and librarian
\par
during the course of the project (there was little opportunity to have more
\par
formal discussions with the teacher because of her tight timetable).
\par
40\textbf{6.1.5 Observation}
\par
The teacher gave each pupil an animal to research and introduced the task
\par
explaining what was expected in the SLRC and as a finished piece of work.
\par
The librarian introduced the new layout of the SLRC, reminding the pupils
\par
about the use of reference books, index and contents pages, the library
\par
catalogue and codebuster.
\par
The teacher started with a brainstorming session to establish the type of
\par
information that would be required and subsequent lessons began with a
\par
brief reminder of the aims. When the pupils were allowed to begin their
\par
research, the five \textbf{\emph{computers}} (loaded with a variety of CD-ROMS) were
\par
\textbf{\emph{immediately occupied}}, one pupil \textbf{\emph{used the catalogue}} (with help from the
\par
librarian) and the rest made their way to the non-fiction books, the
\par
reference section and the Wildlife Magazines.
\par
In one class, all but two of the 31 pupils \textbf{\emph{found some resource}} to work from
\par
showing a degree of \textbf{\emph{confidence in use}} of the SLRC and its resources. The
\par
remaining two pupils displayed a lack of enthusiasm for the resources and
\par
had made no tangible start to the research by the end of the first lesson. In
\par
the other class, again the pupils made their way \textbf{\emph{straight to the resources}}
\par
but this time a group had difficulty finding the encyclopaedias. They had
\par
gathered on the wrong side of the shelves and seemed to be taking the lead
\par
from each other. It was observed that some pupils struggled with
\par
alphabetical order and understanding the instructions within the
\par
encyclopaedia.
\par
The pupils using the hard copy resources \textbf{\emph{engaged in discussion}}, and
\par
helped each other find the correct volume of an encyclopaedia or the basic
\par
information highlighted in one particular encyclopaedia. The pupils were
\par
allowed to sit where they liked around the tables, and the arrangement of
\par
comfortable chairs helped the communication flow. The teacher
\par
commented that this communication is not always possible or encouraged
\par
in the formal classroom setting.
\par
The pupils who used the computers during the first lesson were not those
\par
who had recently covered the CD-ROM searching in the IT Skills Course.
\par
One pupil was observed to \textbf{\emph{work systematically}} through all the CD-ROMs
\par
and was \textbf{\emph{selective in his notes}}, he consulted his jotter and wrote additional
\par
information as he found it. He also \textbf{\emph{helped his neighbour}} who was having
\par
difficulty finding his animal on his particular CD-ROM. Those on the
\par
computers were using them as a resource, \textbf{\emph{taking relevant information and}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{moving on}}. One pupil had problems with his password logging onto the
\par
computer and was observed to lose interest in his research for the rest of the
\par
first lesson.
\par
Pupils were observed using resources to \textbf{\emph{find interesting and more unusual}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{facts}}, for instance one pupil found information about the tail of a lemur and
\par
the librarian helped another find out about the effects of poison but this
\par
type of information was not always evident in the final piece of work or
\par
even in the jotters.
\par
In the classroom the teacher began the lesson with a brainstorming session
\par
on the way information is portrayed in leaflets and on posters to help the
\par
pupils develop ideas for presenting the information they found. When the
\par
discussion was over pupils began working in their jotters or started to
