\textbf{6.1.7 Issues arising from the process of monitoring impact}
\par
Observation was a useful tool to investigate what the pupils were doing and
\par
how they were tackling the project but some of the pupils were wary of the
\par
Researcher and reluctant to discuss how they were tackling the research
\par
process. The teacher and librarian were in a better position to understand
\par
individual problems when discussing progress with the pupils. The
\par
observation schedule gave a more quantitative measure of pupils' actions
\par
and gave an indication of how the class as a whole was progressing rather
\par
than a more individual evaluation when observing particular pupils.
\par
Detailed discussion with the teacher proved difficult in this case study
\par
because of her full timetable. However, discussion during the SLRC
\par
sessions was valuable and allowed changes to be made immediately where
\par
necessary, such as the decision to end the sessions because the pupils were
\par
beginning to exhaust the resources and get bored.
\par
46The questionnaire provided useful evidence to support the observation
\par
about how pupils enjoyed the experience (motivation), how they found the
\par
experience of using the SLRC and the type of resources used, what they
\par
thought they had learned (progression and independence) and how they felt
\par
they had interacted with each other (interaction). However, it was clear that
\par
there were ambiguities in the responses.
\par
Examination of final work gave a good indication of how the pupils chose
\par
to present the information they found but the jotters gave a clearer picture
\par
of what information was found and how individuals tackled some aspects
\par
of the information handling process. However the teacher indicated that she
\par
did not have the criteria to assess this aspect of the work and the jotters are
\par
not readily available for the librarian to examine.
\par
The librarian felt the main criteria for success was whether the teacher was
\par
satisfied with the project and the pupils' experience in the SLRC but this is
\par
not necessarily an indicator of impact on learning.
\par
