48\textbf{6.2 CASE STUDY 2}
\par
\textbf{SCHOOL A: S1 MODERN LANGUAGE}
\par
\textbf{6.2.1 School context}
\par
\textbf{Location}
\par
School A serves a small town and the surrounding rural and coastal area.
\par
\textbf{Size}
\par
902 pupils and 100 staff.
\par
Projected roll for August 2001 is 944.
\par
\textbf{Free meals}
\par
70 pupils representing 8\%
\par
\textbf{Bussed pupils}
\par
340 pupils representing 38\%
\par
\textbf{Associated primary schools}
\par
Eight
\par
\textbf{School aim}
\par
"\emph{Our Aim is to enable pupils to achieve all they can and gain the skills and}
\par
\emph{knowledge they will need to cope with a rapidly changing world}."
\par
\textbf{6.2.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
The SLRC covers an area of approximately 315 square metres; allowing
\par
seating for 40 pupils, including 10 networked computers within a careers
\par
area and a separate staff development area. The atmosphere is bright and
\par
airy.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
Two chartered librarians work on a job share basis with part-time assistance
\par
for 12 hours per week. Pupil librarians help at lunchtime.
\par
Senior Management is fully committed to the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The SLRC is open before and after school and most lunch times. Whole
\par
classes, groups of pupils or individuals use the resources throughout the
\par
day.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
The majority of departments make use of the resources either for the pupils
\par
or for staff development.
\par
\textbf{6.2.3 Introduction to case study 2}
\par
The language teacher involved in the case study had participated in the
\par
focus group and expressed an interest at that time in the potential of using
\par
the SLRC to develop a cultural awareness in the pupils alongside their study
\par
of the language. Her experience of using the SLRC in this way was limited
\par
to one project during the previous year. The teacher was invited to take part
\par
in the case study and accepted, developing an ambitious project centring on
\par
co-operative learning methods introduced at a staff development workshop.
\par
\textbf{Lesson plan}
\par
\textbf{Subject}
\par
Modern language, French cultural project.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
Two S1 classes doing a project at the beginning of the school year. They
\par
49had all started their library induction course during English. The previous
\par
language experience of these first year pupils will depend on whether they
\par
were introduced to French or German in primary school and they spend the
\par
first year studying one or other language but not necessarily the one
\par
introduced in primary.
\par
\textbf{Aims}
\par
The aim of the French project was to give the pupils some cultural
\par
background to their study of the language. The pupils were in small groups
\par
and given a topic to research. Each group had to complete a worksheet
\par
giving information on that topic to be assessed by the teacher. The groups
\par
then gave a presentation, using a prepared poster, to other groups linked by
\par
a common theme. These groups became new clusters and prepared
\par
presentation using the accumulated information to give to the whole class.
\par
Aims of the project expressed by the teacher were to:
\par
{$\bullet$} develop awareness of French culture
\par
{$\bullet$} develop research skills
\par
{$\bullet$} develop independent learning
\par
{$\bullet$} develop group work and positive interaction
\par
{$\bullet$} develop reporting skills in small group, clusters of groups and
\par
whole class.
\par
The aim was to use the SLRC:
\par
{$\bullet$} as a research centre: books, CD-ROMS, Internet, TV
\par
{$\bullet$} as a space
\par
and to have access to librarians:
\par
{$\bullet$} for research
\par
{$\bullet$} for encouraging small groups interaction
\par
{$\bullet$} for pupil support
\par
{$\bullet$} for teacher support
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
The whole project was scheduled to take eight lessons, with the research
\par
being done in one 40-minute period and finished at lunchtime or after
\par
school. The research time was extended for another period when it was
\par
discovered that the pupils needed more time to complete their research.
\par
During the other lessons the pupils were to work in groups to present their
\par
findings and report to other members of the class.
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
Initial discussions took place between the teacher and librarians and the
\par
teacher drew up detailed lesson plans for the project. These were passed on
\par
to the librarians and Researcher and further discussions were held to clarify
\par
the project.
\par
The two S1 French classes taught by the participating language teacher
\par
were organised into groups of about three pupils (using a system to ensure
\par
random grouping). The groups were given a topic to research and spent two
\par
periods in the SLRC looking at resources selected by the librarians in
\par
advance. The groups reported their findings back to the teacher or librarian
\par
in both a written summary and brief presentation. Some of the groups
\par
continued to work in the SLRC using the space. The groups then joined
\par
with others to form new clusters united by a common topic. Each group
\par
nominated one member to work with another group member of the cluster
\par
to reorganise and refine the information to be presented to the whole class.
\par
50These members of the cluster groups worked in the SLRC. Meanwhile the
\par
remaining members of the clusters worked in the classroom and used the
\par
information gathered to help devise a quiz for the teacher to test their
\par
knowledge and understanding of the cultural aspects researched.
\par
The teacher designed a class evaluation that each pupil completed at the end
\par
of the project. Each pupil wrote a sentence or so about what they had
\par
learned, what they had enjoyed, what they did not like during the project
\par
and what the group could have done better.
\par
\textbf{Resources required}
\par
The librarians located suitable book and Internet resources and a checklist
\par
was made available for each group identifying the most appropriate
\par
resources. The non-fiction books were taken off the shelves and kept
\par
separately. Two groups of pupils needed to use a video to extract
\par
information.
\par
\textbf{Assessment/evaluation}
\par
The teacher gave instructions on what was required and assessed the
\par
information written on the worksheet. The teacher and librarians assessed
\par
the reporting skills of the various groups, according to criteria specified in a
\par
checklist and the pupils themselves were involved in peer and self-
\par
evaluation.
\par
\textbf{6.2.4 Case study methodology}
\par
Although two classes were involved in the same project only one class was
\par
observed as the case study unit. In this class, all the groups were observed
\par
doing their research in the SLRC. The groups that were allocated to work in
\par
the SLRC at the poster and reporting stage were observed as they used the
\par
information gathered to make a poster and then reported that information to
\par
the larger group or cluster.
\par
Informal discussions were carried out with the pupils, librarians and teacher
\par
during the sessions. At the end of the project the teacher and librarian
\par
evaluated the project with the Researcher present. The teacher discussed her
\par
impressions of the project with the Researcher. Observation schedules were
\par
not used in this case study because of the complex nature of the project
\par
itself.
\par
A questionnaire was completed by the pupils of both classes (50 returned)
\par
and the self-evaluation sheets completed for the teacher were also
\par
examined.
\par
The presentations were observed and the posters and worksheet examined.
\par
The librarians were asked to keep a record of their observations.
\par
\textbf{6.2.5 Observation}
\par
The librarian reminded the pupils that they were to work together and tried
\par
to establish what role each individual had within the group (resource
\par
manager, timekeeper, and noise controller). The teacher was using an
\par
unfamiliar co-operative learning model for this project and this caused
\par
initial uncertainty about what was expected of the pupils and the librarians.
\par
51The librarians had prepared research cards for each group listing appropriate
\par
resources to ensure that pupils could begin quickly. The pupils were
\par
reluctant to refer back to instructions given in the classroom by the teacher
\par
to establish what they were supposed to be doing. The roles they were given
\par
within the group caused confusion at the start and they were not good at
\par
communicating with each other or organising themselves to ensure all the
\par
tasks were covered. The project commenced at the beginning of the school
\par
year when the new S1 pupils were unfamiliar with the SLRC, the librarians
\par
and other members of the group and this was thought to have a bearing on
\par
how quickly and confidently the research process was undertaken.
\par
Pupils using the book resources appeared to understand the need to \textbf{\emph{use the}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{contents and index pages}} and made a reasonable start at reading and taking
\par
notes. A few of these pupils had begun to \textbf{\emph{make connections}} with their
\par
topic and the information they were finding by the end of the lesson. Those
\par
using the computers needed more help, were less able to search efficiently
\par
and reluctant to take notes but printed as much information as they could.
\par
Although the librarian reminded them to read these printouts, this was not
\par
observed and by the end of the lesson not much appeared to have been done
\par
with this information. A few pupils were disappointed not to have had the
\par
chance to use the computers. Some pupils still appeared unsure of what was
\par
required by the end of the first session.
\par
During the second session in the SLRC the groups were gathering their
\par
information together onto a worksheet and beginning to think about their
\par
presentation. This time they appeared to be more aware of what was
\par
required and were more focused, although individual members within
\par
groups were not always collaborating as well as they should. When the
\par
teacher checked the worksheets and explained why goals had not been met
\par
there appeared to be a greater understanding of what had been required and
\par
in some cases a clearer idea of how to proceed, but others were less sure
\par
because they had not understood their topic sufficiently or because the
\par
process of presenting the information caused renewed uncertainty.
\par
Most of the groups managed to progress onto the next stage with a little
\par
input from the librarian or another helper (the teacher had sent two
\par
assistants to the SLRC to help the librarians).
\par
