The school is committed to success, raising expectations and standards and
\par
providing opportunities for our young people and adult learners.
\par
\textbf{6.3.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
The SLRC covers an area of approximately 500 square metres with seating
\par
for 90 pupils and 27 networked computers (20 situated in an adjacent IT
\par
room). The accommodation includes a class area, a partitioned careers area
\par
and senior study area with study carrels and tables. A separate IT suite is
\par
available for classes using the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
The SLRC is staffed with one chartered librarian, who has been in the post
\par
since the school was built, and a part-time library assistant.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The SLRC is open during day, before and after school and at lunchtime.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
``The Resource Centre is used to promote the English reading programme,
\par
teaching information skills, a wide variety of research projects both in
\par
subject and year groups, careers lessons and interviews, supported study
\par
after school, reading club at lunchtime.''
\par
\textbf{6.3.3 Introduction to case study 3}
\par
The teacher involved in the case study was a participant in the focus group
\par
discussions and was known to work closely with the librarian to plan
\par
courses conducted in the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Lesson plan}
\par
\textbf{Subject}
\par
Geography, natural disasters including volcanoes, earthquakes, tidal waves,
\par
floods and famine.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
All seven of the geography classes in S2 were doing this project. One class
\par
(30 pupils) was used as the case unit and another class, with a different
\par
teacher, was observed on two occasions.
\par
60\textbf{Aims}
\par
The aim of the course was a personal investigation into a natural disaster
\par
and how this affects the local population. The course was designed to
\par
encourage independent research using a variety of resources in the SLRC,
\par
to develop mapping and diagram skills, to motivate pupils and to develop
\par
an enjoyment of geography. The theory of volcanoes and earthquakes was
\par
covered in class before the research project.
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
This course had been initially planned to run for one 57-minute period per
\par
week over five weeks. In fact the pupils had eight research sessions that
\par
lasted over a period of eleven weeks, including holidays, and two other
\par
days when the SLRC was not available.
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
During the first session in the SLRC, the teacher explained the task and the
\par
librarian reminded the class about the resources and the research sequence.
\par
Pupils had to choose a natural disaster, such as volcanoes, earthquakes,
\par
floods, and follow guidance in a research project booklet to complete a 600
\par
word project. The pupils decided a title and then selected approximately
\par
four questions to answer on the chosen topic and these would be used to
\par
make up the sections of the written project report.
\par
During the remaining lessons, the whole class worked in the SLRC
\par
researching their topic independently with support from the teacher and
\par
librarian.
\par
\textbf{Resources required}
\par
The non-fiction books were selected by the librarian and remained on a
\par
trolley for the duration of the project. This ensured the resources were
\par
readily available for all the pupils but were only available for loan during
\par
the holiday and at weekends. Project boxes with newspaper cuttings,
\par
leaflets, etc. were also available. CD-ROMS and the Internet were
\par
introduced from the third week in the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Differentiation/extension}
\par
Pupils would be directed to suitable resources as required.
\par
\textbf{Assessment/evaluation}
\par
The teacher assessed the pupils' work according to criteria clearly marked
\par
in the research project booklet handed out at the start of the project. The
\par
marking covered information content, information gathering techniques and
\par
diagrams and maps. The librarian was consulted on the research process if
\par
required.
\par
The course was evaluated by informal discussion between teacher and
\par
librarian.
\par
Examples of work were displayed on the library notice board at the end of
\par
the project period.
\par
\textbf{6.3.4 Case study methodology}
\par
The first, second, fourth and last session in the SLRC were observed. An
\par
observation schedule was not used in this case study because the pupils
\par
61were too spread out to make timed observation valid but the Researcher
\par
used the criteria in the schedule as a reference for observation.
\par
The Researcher met with the teacher and librarian before the start of the
\par
project and informal discussions took place with the teacher, librarian and
\par
pupils during the project.
\par
The pupils completed a questionnaire (25 returned) at the end of the
\par
project. It was noticed that the pupils did not respond to the question on
\par
using `non-fiction' resources and it was explained that the librarian used the
\par
term `information book' but the aim of this question was to establish the
\par
variety of resources used and evidence was available from the responses
\par
given.
\par
The Researcher examined the submitted work (15 pieces) and a few jotters
\par
but very little work in progress was available.
\par
The librarian indicated that keeping a log of enquiries or resources used by
\par
the case study pupils was impractical.
\par
\textbf{6.3.5 Observation}
\par
The teacher explained to the Researcher that the case study class had not
\par
covered the theory of volcanoes and earthquakes by the start of the project.
\par
The SLRC was not available for two sessions at the start of the course
\par
because of strike action and another school event and it was observed from
\par
the jotters that the teacher took this opportunity to cover the theory.
\par
It took a long time for the pupils to get settled in the classroom area of the
\par
SLRC during the first lesson and this was partly due to the presence of an
\par
S2 home economics class who were doing a group project on countries.
\par
The librarian explained to the class what they were expected to do during
\par
the geography investigation. Each pupil was given a research project
\par
booklet which set out the investigation guidelines in steps covering choice
\par
of topic, planning, where to find information, techniques for gathering
\par
information and guidelines on what was required in each section of the
\par
submitted booklet and how the work would be marked. The librarian then
\par
explained what resources were available and where they were located in the
\par
SLRC.
\par
The pupils were then encouraged to start thinking about and planning their
\par
topic and questions. The pupils were told to use their jotters for making
\par
notes and that space in the SLRC was available to keep finished work if
\par
required. While pupils considered their choices and thought of their
\par
research questions, the teacher discussed choices with individual pupils.
\par
Pupils were told they could share information as long as each submitted
\par
their own piece of work at the end.
\par
Once they were allowed the freedom to begin their research, some pupils
\par
\textbf{\emph{settled quickly using the topic boxes and books}}. Other pupils were
\par
distracted by friends in the S2 class researching countries for home
\par
economics. By the end of the first lesson the \textbf{\emph{majority of the pupils were on}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{task}} and the teacher reminded them to take a note of the resources they
\par
were using. The teacher checked jotters between this lesson and the next
\par
\textbf{\emph{Motivation/Progression}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Motivation}}
\par
62lesson in the SLRC three weeks later.
\par
During subsequent lessons, pupils were given the freedom to research
\par
independently with the teacher going round discussing progress
\par
individually.
\par
Once the computers were made available to the pupils (during the third
\par
session in the SLRC), the teacher and librarian spent the majority of time
\par
helping these pupils in the adjoining computer lab. The rest of the class
\par
were expected to continue working independently in other areas of the
\par
SLRC and pupils were observed to get on with their work. However, some
\par
pupils took advantage of this freedom to chat and find excuses not to get on
\par
with their work. The teacher explained that it is school policy to
\par
concentrate on those pupils who want to get on with their work.
\par
