67\textbf{6.4 CASE STUDY 4}
\par
\textbf{SCHOOL K, S6 BIOLOGY}
\par
\textbf{6.4.1 School context}
\par
\textbf{Location}
\par
This is an independent, fee-paying, co-educational day school situated in a
\par
city centre. The school has a history dating back to the thirteenth century
\par
and a unique role in the community serving villages and towns in the
\par
adjoining regions. The school is housed in a complex of buildings situated
\par
on adjacent sites.
\par
\textbf{Size}
\par
The total school roll is about 1,100 pupils, of which 700 are in the
\par
secondary school and a teaching staff of about 90 in the secondary school.
\par
\textbf{School aim}
\par
"\emph{The aim is to provide a secure and caring environment in which each}
\par
\emph{individual has the opportunity to fulfil his or her potential. This is a}
\par
\emph{predominantly academic school where the majority of pupils go on to}
\par
\emph{university. However, the education of the whole pupil involves more than}
\par
\emph{high standards in academic fields. There is a wide range of out-of-school}
\par
\emph{activities, many carried to high levels of achievement and a strong}
\par
\emph{corporate life - both essential in the development of a pupil's personality}."
\par
\textbf{6.4.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
The SLRC is housed in one of the adjacent sites and covers an area of
\par
approximately 272 square metres. There is seating for approximately 80
\par
pupils, including careers and senior study area, 20 networked computers
\par
and a specific area for the junior school.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
The SLRC is staffed by one full-time chartered librarian and one full-time
\par
library assistant.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The SLRC is open all day, before and after school and at break and lunch
\par
time and allows for whole class use, individual research and leisure or
\par
homework activities.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
The SLRC is used by a variety of subject departments in the senior school.
\par
\textbf{6.4.3 Introduction to case study 4}
\par
This school was not involved in the focus group discussions. The biology
\par
teacher was keen to encourage his sixth form pupils to make effective use
\par
the SLRC and other libraries to help with their academic work.
\par
\textbf{Subject}
\par
Biology investigation.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
Sixth year Higher level pupils, seventeen in the class.
\par
\textbf{Aims}
\par
The following aims were expressed by the teacher:
\par
{$\bullet$} To raise awareness of how the library can help;
\par
68{$\bullet$} To learn how to use a library properly;
\par
{$\bullet$} To encourage regular use;
\par
{$\bullet$} To find information for use in investigation.
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
At the beginning of the school year the pupils were given a refresher course
\par
in using the SLRC. After the October holiday, the pupils began their
\par
biology investigation and the submission date for the first draft of their
\par
introduction to their investigation was early December.
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
As part of their biology Higher course the pupils were required to carry out
\par
an investigation on a topic of their own choice. The investigation required
\par
the pupils to design and carry out an experiment and research the relevant
\par
background to their chosen topic. The pupils were given written guidelines
\par
for the biology investigation and during their refresher course in using the
\par
SLRC they were given a booklet to guide them through the research
\par
process.
\par
The pupils were expected to make independent use of the SLRC and other
\par
resources during their investigation period.
\par
\textbf{Assessment/evaluation}
\par
The teacher would assess the pupil's work.
\par
The use of the SLRC would be evaluated informally by the teacher and
\par
librarian.
\par
\textbf{6.4.4 Case study methodology}
\par
The pupils using the SLRC for their investigation were expected to work
\par
independently and during study periods and therefore observation was not
\par
possible by the Researcher. The SLRC staff was asked to keep an informal
\par
record of their observations of which pupils used the SLRC and how they
\par
tackled the task.
\par
Pupils were given a library research checklist during their refresher period
\par
in the SLRC by the librarian, to help with the research process and to be
\par
used for feedback but at the end of the case study the pupils indicated that
\par
they had not used the checklist.
\par
After their draft introductions for their investigations had been submitted
\par
the pupils completed a questionnaire to give the Research team information
\par
about their experiences of tackling the research. They were also asked to
