73\textbf{6.5 CASE STUDY 5}
\par
\textbf{SCHOOL F: S3 SKILLS COURSE}
\par
\textbf{6.5.1 School context}
\par
\textbf{Location}
\par
This is a mixed comprehensive school serving an extensive area of rural
\par
and urban pupils. The school was founded in 1792 but moved to its present
\par
site on the outskirts of the city in 1977.
\par
\textbf{Size}
\par
Pupil role of 779 and 65 members of staff.
\par
\textbf{Free meals}
\par
8.09\%
\par
\textbf{Bussed pupils}
\par
6.55\%
\par
\textbf{Associated primaries}
\par
Ten
\par
\textbf{School aim}
\par
The school aims " \emph{to provide each pupil with a wide range of challenging}
\par
\emph{experience in a safe, caring environment, so that each individual pupil can}
\par
\emph{make the best of his or her talents}."
\par
\textbf{6.5.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
The SLRC is on two floors and has an area of approximately 115 square
\par
metres, with seating for 45 pupils. The lower floor is arranged with tables
\par
for class work and has 12 networked computers, and the senior study area
\par
and careers library is located on the upper floor.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
The SLRC is staffed by one chartered librarian with volunteer parental help
\par
and a library assistant has recently been appointed to work 15 hours per
\par
week.
\par
The SLRC has full backing from Senior Management.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The SLRC is open all day, before and after school and at lunchtime.
\par
Lunchtime is particularly busy and members of staff help, the SLRC is
\par
closed at break time.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
All departments use the SLRC and positively encourage pupil use.
\par
\textbf{6.5.3 Introduction to case study 5}
\par
\textbf{Lesson plan}
\par
\textbf{Subject}
\par
Personal and Social Education (PSE), using the subject of healthy living to
\par
develop study skills introduced in class by the guidance teacher.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
S3, all PSE pupils.
\par
\textbf{Aims}
\par
A personal investigation into a Healthy Lifestyle and how this affects the
\par
body. Pupils to choose a topic from smoking, alcohol, drugs, healthy diet,
\par
exercise and produce a poster, leaflet or short talk on how this affects the
\par
74body. The course is designed to refresh pupils in how to use the library, to
\par
encourage independent researchers, to meet deadlines and develop ICT
\par
skills, as well as health education.
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
One 40-minute period per week for a three-week block.
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
Lesson 1, the librarian reminded pupils about resources and the research
\par
sequence.
\par
Lesson 2, the pupils used the resources to research their selected topic, and
\par
the project was continued as homework so that it was finished for lesson 3.
\par
Lesson 3, the pupils explained their work to other members of a small
\par
group who peer evaluated the work according to set criteria. This was
\par
followed by a class discussion about the results, considering the
\par
information found and its relevance to the topic chosen and the task, the
\par
presentation and appropriateness of design, and the bibliography. The
\par
librarian then asked the pupils to complete a self-evaluation sheet noting
\par
down what they had learned about the research process.
\par
The librarian introduced the library resources and the research process, with
\par
teacher supporting the librarian and pupils.
\par
\textbf{Resources required}
\par
Research checklist for each table.
\par
All book resources available and access to the Internet.
\par
\textbf{Assessment/evaluation}
\par
Informal discussion after the course between teacher and librarian, review
\par
of final product, peer and self-evaluation sheets.
\par
Examples of work were displayed on the library notice board. Pupils were
\par
recommended for mention for the `record of achievement' for good or
\par
outstanding work.
\par
\textbf{6.5.4 Case study methodology}
\par
One class was observed for all three lessons and the Researcher discussed
\par
the research process with the teacher and pupils informally during these
\par
sessions.
\par
The final work of this class was examined as well as the work of the other
\par
five classes. Very few pupils submitted any notes for examination.
\par
The peer and self-evaluation sheets were also examined.
\par
\textbf{6.5.5 Observation}
\par
Session 1:
\par
The librarian explained what the pupils would be doing over the three
\par
weeks. The librarian reminded the class about the arrangement of resources
\par
in the SLRC, choosing a book, skimming and scanning, notetaking and
\par
keywords, the research process, discussion of the Internet, presentation and
\par
recording the source of information and summarised it all before the pupils
\par
got started.
\par
75The teacher reminded the pupils about the study skills introduced in class.
\par
During brief discussion, the librarian commented that she should also watch
\par
the video the pupils had seen in class to refresh her memory of what was
\par
covered.
\par
The pupils had ten minutes at the end of the session to make a start and
\par
most began \textbf{\emph{looking for resources}} but three pupils began on their poster.
\par
Session 2:
\par
The librarian's introduction explained that the pupils should all have chosen
\par
their topics and made a start the previous week; that they would need to be
\par
highly focused; and the work would have to be finished for homework so
\par
that the completed poster, leaflet or talk could be presented to their group
\par
the following week. All the pupils went to \textbf{\emph{get resources quickly}}; nine boys
\par
\textbf{\emph{went straight to the computers}} and spent time logging on. The librarian
\par
helped them find search engines and reminded them to keep focused on the
\par
effects on the body of their particular health issue. Most of the sites that
\par
pupils tried were drug related and were therefore barred. The librarian, still
\par
working with those on the computers, helped individuals define their search
\par
and think of keywords. The other thirteen pupils were \textbf{\emph{working with book}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{resources}} and only one was recorded as not focused on the project. The
\par
teacher helped by encouraging pupils to think about what information they
\par
would need on their poster. One pupil was observed to \textbf{\emph{move on from the}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{computer and use an encyclopaedia}}. Another pupil found a useful looking
\par
site and was copying the information directly onto paper.
\par
The lesson provided the opportunity for instruction in developing search
\par
skills using the Internet. The pupils were not confident at seeking help, one
\par
had trouble printing and was finally spotted and helped; another spent a
\par
long time looking for a book in the wrong section of the library and was not
\par
spotted having trouble; and another told the Researcher that he did not
\par
know what he was supposed to be doing because he was absent the
\par
previous week but did not seek help.
\par
Session 3
\par
Two pupils were absent the previous week and had produced no work and
\par
the librarian commented that some other pupils had produced very little.
\par
The pupils started peer evaluation at their tables. Four of the seven groups
\par
\textbf{\emph{took the exercise seriously}} and engaged in discussion about the work
\par
presented. The teacher helped one of the less committed groups.
\par
The librarian led the general discussion on the work, commenting on
\par
quality, the use of resources, the use of style for presentation but there was
\par
little response from the pupils on this occasion (although the librarian had
\par
indicated that other classes had contributed more to this discussion). The
\par
pupils were then asked to do a self-evaluation writing briefly what they had
\par
learned about the research process.
