\par
discuss the use of information in an informal way but some pupils
\par
found this task difficult to carry out objectively.
\par
80{$\bullet$} The teacher helped one group evaluate more realistically and this
\par
highlighted information that pupils had copied without understanding.
\par
\textbf{6.5.7 Issues arising from the process of monitoring impact}
\par
If used carefully peer and self-evaluation can be an effective tool for
\par
assessing the understanding and implementation of the pupil's learning.
\par
A questionnaire was not appropriate in this case study because of time
\par
constraints but the use of peer and self-evaluation did give valuable insight.
\par
Discussion with guidance staff was limited.
\par
Two pupils handed in their notes and this made it much easier to see what
\par
they had done and how the notes were reflected in the final work. This is an
\par
important part of the process and is valuable for assessing the work and
\par
evaluating the course properly. The pupils were not asked to submit their
\par
notes from the research and therefore the Researcher was not able to look at
\par
the planning or information process.
\par
The librarian was trying out a new format for this course and adapted it
\par
during the process. Not all the classes received quite the same treatment.
\par
The evaluation sheet was refined so that the pupils had a clearer idea of
\par
what they were assessing. However, there were problems associated with
\par
peer evaluation, such as the influence of personalities in the assessment.
\par
The self-evaluation cards often reflected the discussion rather than genuine
\par
reflection on the pupils' part.
\par
The librarian expressed the view that the changes prompted by the case
\par
study Research had improved the course and the self-evaluation comments
\par
were considered to be particularly valuable.
\par
The librarian in this school mentioned to the Research team that being
\par
involved in the Impact Project had prompted reflection on her own work
\par
and she understood the benefit of using the self-evaluation comments for
\par
further development of the SLRC. As a result of the changes to the S3 skills
\par
course the librarian discussed the possibility of developing ICT skills in the
\par
S1 library periods with senior management.
\par
