82\textbf{6.6 CASE STUDY 6}
\par
\textbf{SCHOOL F: S1 READING FOR PLEASURE}
\par
\textbf{6.6.1 School context}
\par
\textbf{Location}
\par
This is a mixed comprehensive school serving an extensive area of rural
\par
and urban pupils. The school was founded in 1792 but moved to its present
\par
site on the outskirts of the city in 1977.
\par
\textbf{Size}
\par
Pupil roll of 779 and 65 members of staff.
\par
\textbf{Free meals}
\par
8.09\%
\par
\textbf{Bussed pupils}
\par
6.55\%
\par
\textbf{Associated primaries}
\par
Ten
\par
\textbf{School aim}
\par
The school aims " \emph{to provide each pupil with a wide range of challenging}
\par
\emph{experience in a safe, caring environment, so that each individual pupil can}
\par
\emph{make the best of his or her talents}."
\par
\textbf{6.6.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
The SLRC is on two floors and has an area of approximately 115 square
\par
metres, with seating for 45 pupils. The lower floor is arranged with tables
\par
for class work and has 12 networked computers, and the senior study area
\par
and careers library is located on the upper floor.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
The SLRC is staffed by one chartered librarian with volunteer parental help
\par
and a library assistant has recently been appointed to work 15 hours per
\par
week.
\par
The SLRC has full backing from Senior Management.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The SLRC is open all day, before and after school and at lunchtime.
\par
Lunchtime is particularly busy and members of teaching staff help. The
\par
SLRC is closed at break time.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
All departments use the SLRC and positively encourage pupil use.
\par
\textbf{6.6.3 Introduction to case study 6}
\par
\textbf{Lesson plan}
\par
\textbf{Subject}
\par
A weekly library course during English.
\par
The first year library course introduces the main library skills as well as a
\par
number of other information handling skills and activities to encourage the
\par
enjoyment of reading.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
S1 classes
\par
\textbf{Aims}
\par
The aim of the library course is to give S1 pupils the basic skills they need
\par
83to use the library independently for finding information, and to promote
\par
confidence in selecting fiction and enjoying reading.
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
S1 pupils go to the SLRC for one period a week during the first year. The
\par
case study period lasted from the beginning of the school year (in August)
\par
to the end of the second term (in December).
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
Each week the librarian introduces different aspects of library skills. Some
\par
weeks focus on the selecting and reading of books and other weeks involve
\par
a short activity on information handling, as well as time to change books.
\par
This librarian in conjunction with other school librarians in the authority
\par
has contributed to the development of three web-based databases of modern
\par
fiction. The Power Readers database is designed for S1 and S2; Keep
\par
Reading encourages the S3 and S4; and for the senior years a Higher Still
\par
Contemporary English Literature website. The Power Readers database
\par
encourages the pupils to work towards a progression of awards by reading a
\par
variety of genres; the head teacher then presents the pupils with their
\par
awards. The bronze level award required the pupils to read one poetry
\par
anthology, one book of short stories, one Scottish novel, one award winner
\par
and two genres not tried before and finally the pupils are asked to write a
\par
brief review of one of the books. The database gives details of a selection
\par
of books under genre headings, and links to author sites. All the S1 pupils
\par
were introduced to the Power Readers database during a library session.
\par
All pupils have a library jotter in which they keep a record of their reading
\par
with an indication of their enjoyment of the book.
\par
\textbf{Resources required}
\par
The fiction books and the database of recommended contemporary fiction.
\par
\textbf{Differentiation/extension}
\par
As required.
\par
\textbf{6.6.4 Case study methodology}
\par
This case study investigated the promotion of reading for pleasure based
\par
upon the introduction to fiction given by the librarian during the first term
\par
of the library course. The aim was to examine whether the SLRC has an
\par
impact on the reading habit; whether it helps the selection process; whether
\par
it encourages pupils to try reading different genres and authors; and
\par
whether this in turn might help the way pupils read, use language and their
\par
enjoyment.
\par
For the purposes of this case study the librarian changed the emphasis of
\par
her course. For one class, the librarian gave pupils a list of suggested
\par
authors and titles tailored for the individual, using information on hobbies
\par
and reading habits expressed in the questionnaires completed during P7
\par
visits. Two other classes were required to work towards the awards on the
\par
Power Readers database. The other three classes were left to select their
\par
library books from the shelves or the database.
\par
The case study followed the individualised class (individualised class E),
\par
one of the classes given the database as a compulsory activity (database
\par
class F) and a control class left to their own selection (control class D).
\par
84This case study school traditionally handed out questionnaires to the P7
\par
visiting pupils to get a feel for their likes, attitudes and time spent on
\par
reading. This would then be discussed during the course of the library
\par
programme, which is taken in English time, and again at the end of the year
\par
to see if attitudes had changed.
\par
The case study S1 pupils completed a questionnaire at the end of the
\par
autumn term to determine how the SLRC might have had an impact on
\par
pupils' reading. The questionnaire included questions about the number of
\par
books borrowed and the number of books read to try to ensure students
\par
made the distinction between the two.
\par
The library jotters were examined for the case study pupils and for the
\par
previous (1999) S1 pupils.
\par
Pupils were observed and questioned during a few of the library sessions
\par
and occasionally when they came to the SLRC at lunchtime.
\par
Informal discussions were carried out with the librarian and teachers to
\par
ascertain any changes noticed in pupil reading habits over the case study
\par
period. The teachers did not feel able to comment on how the SLRC might
\par
impact on reading or writing ability because of all the other complex factors
\par
influencing an individual's learning.
\par
An examination of a sample of 1999 S1 reader records suggests that pupils
\par
borrow books during the English class both for reading for pleasure and to
\par
support a curriculum subject. It is not possible to know whether these books
\par
are actually read but knowledge of the individual and careful examination
\par
of the issue and return dates can give some reasonable indication. The time
\par
of borrowing can also give an indication of the motivation and
\par
independence of the pupils. The reader records provide a link between what
\par
is borrowed and activities that are taking place elsewhere in the curriculum
\par
or school. In this case study school, the library records are maintained
\par
centrally and the reader records are not readily available for inspection as
\par
the library service were concerned about data protection issues.
\par
\textbf{6.6.5 Observation}
\par
The librarian introduced the Power Readers database to all the S1 classes
\par
and each pupil was given the URL in their homework diary enabling them
\par
to log on at different locations. Each class was introduced to the
\par
arrangement of fiction books in the SLRC and the books on the Power
\par
Readers database were out on display. The individualised class was also
\par
given a sheet listing up to ten books and/or authors that the librarian
\par
thought each pupil might like to try reading. After the introduction to the
\par
fiction books and the database, the pupils were left to make their own
\par
selection of books to borrow.
\par
All three of the observation classes responded to the introduction to the
\par
database in a similar way and there was not an immediate rush to use the
\par
computers. These S1 pupils would have had very different experiences of
\par
ICT and this introduction to the database was at the beginning of their
\par
secondary schooling and the reluctance by some pupils to try the database
\par
may have been due to a lack of understanding or confidence in the
\par
technology. One pupil (control D) was observed to send another away so he
\par
85could use the computer alone. A few pupils in each class did try to select
\par
books from the database but the time taken to log on caused problems and
\par
detracted from the stimulating experience intended.
\par
The problems with the database were sorted later in the term. The librarian
\par
took a liberal approach to the awards and allowed pupils to succeed as long
\par
as they had broadly fulfilled the criteria although they may not all have
\par
used the database to select their choice.
\par
In the individualised class one pupil was observed to be \textbf{\emph{very keen to try the}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{database and asked questions}} about the genre of books for the award. He
\par
appeared to be the less interested in the list of recommended books and
\par
authors. The pupils in the compulsory database class were observed to be
\par
more interested in the display of database books than using the computers
\par
or they asked advice from the librarian rather than use the computers. The
\par
control class were the most stimulated by the computers and had gathered
\par
in small groups by the end of the lesson but time was limited for them to do
\par
much exploration. Pupils using the database were observed to be interested
\par
in exploring the links and it did stimulate limited interest in the books but
\par
then some pupils found it difficult to make a selection.
\par
During the following lessons the librarian treated each of the case study
\par
classes in the same manner. In the second lesson observed, the librarian
\par
introduced the different genres in fiction by inviting discussion of possible
\par
types already known by the pupils, showing examples and describing the
\par
plot. These titles were then offered for pupils to try and very often the
\par
pupils were keen to accept the recommendations although the books were
\par
sometimes rejected later when the pupils had the chance to select for
\par
themselves.
\par
Another library session concentrated on poetry to coincide with National
\par
Poetry Day when this SLRC made an effort to celebrate the event by
\par
encouraging staff and pupils alike to share their favourite poems. The class
\par
observed (control) \textbf{\emph{borrowed about twelve poetry books}} and this would, of
\par
course, count towards their award.
\par
It became apparent after observing the individualised class for a few
\par
sessions that they were a class of particularly able and keen readers and this
\par
was confirmed by discussion with the teacher and librarian. Many already
\par
knew which books they wanted to borrow but there were others who
\par
consulted their list of suggestions. The jotters suggested that many of them
\par
were using their individualised list, using some of these to work towards
\par
their Power Readers award and were also selecting titles of their own
\par
choice.
\par
During the lunch break it was observed that several pupils requested their
\par
library jotters to update, particularly those from the individualised class but
\par
not exclusively.
\par
During the course of the second term the teacher and librarian decided that
\par
one particularly well read pupil should be given an extension exercise and
\par
he was encouraged to set up a website for book reviews.
