\emph{them" [E]}
\par
\emph{"I sometimes come in and tell my friend about a book or use the}
\par
\emph{computer" [E]}
\par
\textbf{6.6.7 Issues arising from the monitoring process}
\par
This librarian felt that the full timetable and busy lunchtime in the SLRC
\par
was a good indicator of learning. The teachers certainly valued the SLRC
\par
and the librarian and were constantly visiting to discuss school matters or to
\par
use the resources themselves but this is less an indicator of learning than an
\par
indicator of the value placed upon it.
\par
The reader record showed clear indication that the SLRC played a
\par
significant part in the selection of material borrowed by pupils. Poetry,
\par
science, war novels, audio material and horror stories were all borrowed in
\par
greater numbers when the librarian had some input into the discussion
\par
about a subject. It also reveals the influence of peers in the selection of
\par
books to read: clusters of borrowers from the same class were reading
\par
Horrible Histories, River Boy, Skellig, the Machine Gunners to mention
\par
just a few, at about the same time.
\par
The issue statistics also reflect a very high rate of borrowing and the growth
\par
over the past ten years since this librarian has been in the post. The number
\par
of books issued and the variety may not make an immediate and
\par
89recognisable impact on pupil learning but it does enable pupils to
\par
experiment with books and allows keen readers to acquire substantial
\par
amounts of reading material without undue expense or inconvenience.
\par
Discussions with the teachers proved difficult. There was a reluctance to
\par
make any judgements, they were reluctant to think of ways of finding out
\par
and there appeared to be an assumption that there would be no clear
\par
evidence of impact on learning.
\par
An analysis of the number of books claimed to have been read in jotters
\par
between 1999 and 2000 did not reveal any significant differences over the
\par
period August to December. The very slight decrease in 2000 (which was
\par
expected) might be due to the more realistic claims made this year than the
\par
previous year.
\par
The questionnaire enabled pupils to comment on their responses and this
\par
gave some valuable indication of what the pupils themselves thought the
\par
impact of the SLRC had been on their reading habits.
\par
It was interesting to note that there were not as many differences between
\par
the three groups as might have been expected but the positive response
\par
from all the groups would suggest that the additional input into this part of
\par
the library course did have a positive impact on pupil selection of texts and
\par
their enjoyment.
\par
90\textbf{6.6.8 Case study 6: summary of the evidence of impact on learning}
\par
\textbf{Indicators of Motivation:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Returning to SLRC in own time to borrow books, continue activity;
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion with teacher, librarian or others about books;
\par
{$\bullet$} Recommending books;
\par
{$\bullet$} Enjoyment of book, change in attitude;
\par
{$\bullet$} Working towards award.
\par
\textbf{Indicators of Progression:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Willingness to try different genre or author;
\par
{$\bullet$} Change in reading habit;
\par
{$\bullet$} Understanding of relationship between reading and other learning;
\par
{$\bullet$} Sense of achievement.
\par
\textbf{Indicator of Independence:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Independent choice of quality fiction.
\par
\textbf{Indicator of Interaction:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion of books.
\par
\textbf{Summary of factors influencing the impact on learning}
\par
\textbf{Factors enhancing impact:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Interest taken by librarian and teacher.
