91\textbf{6.7 CASE STUDY 7}
\par
\textbf{SCHOOL J: PUPIL LIBRARIANS}
\par
\textbf{6.7.1 School context}
\par
\textbf{Location}
\par
School J is a modern, purpose-built city school on a green field site.
\par
\textbf{Size}
\par
Pupil roll was 940 pupils in 2000 with 72 members of teaching staff.
\par
\textbf{Free meals}
\par
Not available.
\par
\textbf{Bussed pupils}
\par
Not available.
\par
\textbf{Associated primaries}
\par
The school has four associated primaries in the city with another six
\par
primaries outside the city with the option to attend.
\par
\textbf{School aim}
\par
"\emph{Our aims are to encourage your child to play a full part in the life of the}
\par
\emph{school and to provide learning facilities which will enable all our pupils,}
\par
\emph{whatever their abilities, to realise their potential and to become responsible}
\par
\emph{caring adults. To achieve our aims we encourage self-discipline and self-}
\par
\emph{motivation in all our pupils, respect for others and others' points of view}."
\par
\textbf{6.7.2 School library resource centre}
\par
\textbf{Accommodation}
\par
The SLRC is situated centrally in the school and acts as a corridor between
\par
classrooms and the staff area and has constant activity throughout the day.
\par
The room has tables for class use, comfortable seating and study carrels. A
\par
careers section and networked computers are situated to the side of the
\par
room.
\par
\textbf{Staffing}
\par
The SLRC is staffed with one full-time chartered librarian. A library
\par
assistant is shared with other schools and the pupil librarians help every day
\par
when they are available.
\par
The head teacher was particularly interested in the SLRC because of his
\par
experience of Canadian education and their use of teacher/librarians. He
\par
felt the tradition of teacher training in this country did not always recognise
\par
the importance of resources as a learning aid and felt teachers often
\par
undervalued the SLRC. The head teacher was fully committed to the work
\par
of the SLRC and saw ICT as a means of affecting change in the way
\par
information, and the management of that information, is viewed in schools.
\par
\textbf{Availability}
\par
The SLRC is open all day, before and after school on most days and at
\par
morning break and lunchtime. The librarian encourages class use,
\par
individual borrowing and research and leisure use by pupils and teachers.
\par
The atmosphere is noisy and enthusiastic.
\par
\textbf{Use}
\par
All pupils in S1 and S2 complete Library and Information Skills Course
\par
modules during English lessons and the majority of subject departments use
\par
the SLRC for research.
\par
92\textbf{6.7.3 Introduction to case study 7}
\par
\textbf{Aims}
\par
Pupil ownership of the SLRC;
\par
Increased self-esteem and use of initiative;
\par
Interaction with others;
\par
Development of ICT skills by using the various computer applications and
\par
sorting problems.
\par
\textbf{Level}
\par
All pupils may apply to become pupil librarians and during the case study
\par
there were eleven (three girls, eight boys) ranging from S5 to S2.
\par
\textbf{Time scale}
\par
Pupil librarians help in the SLRC during morning break, lunchtime and
\par
after school whenever they are available. They continue in the post for as
\par
long as they are able or remain interested.
\par
\textbf{Activities}
\par
The pupil librarians are involved in library duties, such as returning books
\par
to shelves, general tidying, issue and return of loan items using the
\par
computerised library management system.
\par
The group helps with the processing of new stock and repairs. They help
\par
users of the SLRC, particularly with ICT, by loading CD-ROMS, sorting
\par
difficulties, and explaining processes. In return, the pupil librarians have
\par
privileges of access and loans.
\par
Candidates apply for the chance to become a pupil librarian and are
\par
selected on basis of a simple application form and interview with the
\par
librarian. New recruits are trained by the librarian and by established pupil
\par
librarians. The pupil librarians have designed the application form and a
\par
multi-media library quiz to help with their training.
\par
The librarian tends to leave organisation of the group to the pupil librarians
\par
themselves.
\par
\textbf{6.7.4 Case study methodology}
\par
The Researchers met with the pupil librarians and discussed aims of the
\par
Project and how they might help. During this initial meeting the pupil
\par
librarians explained what their duties were and were encouraged to reflect
\par
on how this might have an impact on their learning. The pupil librarians
\par
were then involved in the decisions of how the learning might be tracked. It
\par
was agreed that the pupil librarians would keep a chart detailing the
\par
activities of each individual. All members of the group were also
\par
encouraged to keep a personal log of their activities and reflections on the
\par
processes involved.
\par
After discussions with the head teacher and the librarian it was agreed that
\par
the guidance staff would be approached and asked to reflect on the
\par
individual pupil librarians in their care and whether they could detect any
\par
evidence that being a pupil librarian had an impact on learning.
\par
The pupil librarians were observed carrying out their duties and those in S2
\par
were also observed in their classes in the S2 Library and Information Skills
\par
93Course based on research in the SLRC.
\par
The initial timescale for the case study was two weeks but this was
\par
extended to six weeks to encourage greater involvement and reflection of
\par
the Impact Project.
\par
The librarian was encouraged to consider what the pupil librarians were
\par
doing and how this might influence their learning.
\par
\textbf{6.7.5 Observation}
\par
Two of the pupil librarians were not present at the initial meeting and
\par
during the case study period, seven of the eleven pupil librarians were seen
\par
to be \textbf{\emph{particularly active}} and the others appeared to contribute little.
\par
The librarian reported that two of the pupil librarians had been very
\par
involved with the preparation of the chart and had \textbf{\emph{explained the Project to}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{the absent members}} of the group. On the first observation day after the
\par
initial meeting with the pupil librarians, another member of the group was
\par
\textbf{\emph{particularly keen to show}} the Researcher the chart they had prepared and
\par
started to complete.
\par
It was apparent that those involved in the preparation of the chart had given
\par
it thought but it was very factual, giving activities carried out by whom and
\par
when. Seven of the group started to fill in the chart during the first two
\par
weeks and then the two senior pupils became less involved in the SLRC as
\par
other activities took on a higher priority. Three pupils never filled in the
\par
chart and one pupil started completing activities during the last week of the
\par
case study although he was frequently observed carrying out duties and
\par
taking an active part.
\par
During the initial two weeks of the case study, the senior pupil librarians
\par
expressed their resentment of the way certain individuals were contributing
\par
to the tasks. It was felt that the senior pupil librarians were doing the more
\par
tedious routine tasks in the SLRC while the younger members of the group
\par
were allowed to spend their time doing more exciting things on the
\par
computers. The pupil librarians who were particularly keen on ICT were
\par
\textbf{\emph{developing a presentation}} of the study skills club to be shown at an
\par
exhibition in the city hall and the others did not see this as part of their
\par
library duties.
\par
During the observation period the pupil librarians were seen to spend the
\par
majority of their time either using the computers, \textbf{\emph{helping other users}} with
\par
computer related tasks or manning the issue desk and only a little time was
\par
spent shelving and tidying the resources, although this was mentioned by
\par
most as one of the major duties of the pupil librarians.
\par
After the initial two weeks, the pupil librarians were encouraged to spend a
\par
little time each day reflecting on their activities and experiences in the
\par
SLRC and during discussions they were more willing to consider their
\par
activities in a more reflective way. However, they still had difficulties
\par
seeing any connections between what they were doing in the SLRC and
\par
their learning experiences. Only one member of the group kept a diary and
\par
was \textbf{\emph{very keen to show}} this to the Researcher.
