\textbf{\emph{Interaction - }}\emph{Friendships}\textbf{6.7.7 Issues arising from the process of monitoring impact}
\par
The pupils found it difficult to understand the learning concepts associated
\par
with this Research Project and the chart and diaries were therefore of a
\par
more factual nature than was really required. Other studies have used pupil
\par
diaries and logs more successfully but they have tended to concentrate on a
\par
particular learning process, such as using ICT for information seeking1
\par
.
\par
Where the interest was in tracking a much wider range of learning
\par
experiences this clearly led to some difficulties for the pupils in trying to
\par
self-evaluate the learning experience. However, observation and discussion
\par
helped to draw some of these learning connections out and the teachers
\par
were able to reflect in a limited way on the potential learning experiences.
\par
It was particularly interesting to observe the pupil librarians in the
\par
classroom situation and see how their experiences were reflected in other
\par
curriculum activities. The two pupil librarians who were keen users of ICT
\par
were confident helping other members of the class to make the most of
\par
Power Point and their group presentations were technically more
\par
competent. The use of resources was not observed but the three
\par
presentations given by groups who had a pupil librarian did make good use
\par
of information and were among the best of the twelve presentations given
\par
by this class, half of which were unfinished.
\par
Motivation may be expressed as a willingness to participate, an enthusiasm
\par
and energy to do something. This motivation might be due to, or reflect, the
\par
immediate activity taking place in the SLRC but it might also extend
\par
beyond the SLRC to motivate the pupil to do or learn something. These
\par
connections are more difficult to trace but when the pupil librarians were
\par
observed in the classroom situation and questioned about other activities,
\par
there did appear to be evidence that their learning experiences were
\par
transferred to other situations.
\par
The comments expressed by other members of teaching staff gave a
\par
valuable insight to how the pupil librarians were seen in other areas of the
\par
school, although one guidance teacher expressed caution about making
\par
connections between attitude and involvement in the SLRC.
\par
Interaction can either be seen as a progressive skill related to
\par
communication and organisation within a group or as a means of promoting
\par
learning by interacting with others and learning from others. This was
\par
particularly significant during the observation of, and discussions with,
\par
participants in this case study.
\par
98\textbf{6.7.8 Case study 7: summary of the evidence of impact on learning}
\par
\textbf{Indicators of Motivation:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Willing participation, enjoyment of working together;
\par
{$\bullet$} Expression of enthusiasm, initiate discussion;
\par
{$\bullet$} Changed attitude to school;
\par
{$\bullet$} Encouragement to others.
\par
\textbf{Indicators of Progression:}
\par
{$\bullet$} Development of skills:
\par
