\par
\textbf{AD HOC USE: SCHOOLS A, C, F, AND J}
\par
\textbf{6.8.1 Introduction}
\par
The school librarians interviewed in the selected focus group schools,
\par
aimed to provide experiences and activities beyond the traditional library
\par
functions to support the curriculum and these activities had the added
\par
advantage of helping to promote themselves and the SLRC with the school.
\par
All the librarians tackled this in some form. Activities ranged from
\par
ensuring their presence at parent meetings and helping with induction
\par
activities, to supervising Duke of Edinburgh expeditions and senior proms
\par
and staging events centred round the SLRC itself or facilitating events
\par
using the SLRC area.
\par
During the period of investigation many of these activities were taking
\par
place and a decision was made to observe a few examples in order to
\par
consider the impact on learning.
\par
It was also recognised that there is a great deal of ad hoc use of the SLRC
\par
by staff and pupils out of class time and it was thought to be worthwhile to
\par
try and understand how this use might impact on learning. Like the pupil
\par
librarians case study, these activities were not planned strictly as class
\par
based, curriculum related activities in the sense of the earlier project based
\par
case studies had been. This in itself created interesting challenges from the
\par
Research perspective in that it was not going to be as easy to observe and
\par
record this type of use of the SLRC or examine work produced by learners.
\par
\textbf{6.8.2 Space Fair}
\par
\textbf{Background}
\par
The Space Fair was organised by the librarian in collaboration with the
\par
science department, the local Astronomical Society and a local enthusiast
\par
and the librarian raised sponsorship money. The aim of the Space Fair was
\par
to support the S2 science curriculum, and the event has been repeated every
\par
three years to enable all pupils to access it either during P7, S1 or S2. The
\par
secondary pupils attended the space fair in their science classes and the
\par
associated primaries were invited to attend for an afternoon.
\par
The exhibition, which lasted for a month, consisted of models of the planets
\par
in the solar system indicating their relative size and another display showed
\par
the relative distances between them. The resources were displayed to
\par
accompany the exhibition; space fiction and poetry and the non-fiction
\par
books; posters, video clips, and satellite photographs were all on display.
\par
There was a special section on the Moon Landing. The computers were set
\par
up with a variety of space related CD-ROMS and there was a hands-on
\par
balloon model of how a rocket is propelled.
\par
\textbf{Observation}
\par
The Researcher spent the second day of the Space Fair observing the
\par
activities. This provided the opportunity to view one S1 class for a double
\par
period and a visiting Primary 7 class using the resources.
\par
\emph{"The pupils were highly motivated by all of the activities. Excellent}
\par
100\emph{selection of books on display - pleased to see a range of fiction with a}
\par
\emph{'space' theme also."}
\par
"\emph{It is so much easier to "see" space facts with the help of the exhibitions}
\par
\emph{and books."}
\par
The pupils were given an introduction to the exhibition and its layout by the
\par
librarian and encouraged to explore the exhibits before choosing a planet to
\par
investigate. The S1 pupils were to write this up as an exercise. The primary
\par
7 pupils listened to a talk given by a local model maker and space
\par
enthusiast and were then encouraged to explore the resources using a
\par
worksheet to find out about a planet chosen from a selection of bookmarks.
\par
This visit provided the pupils with the opportunity to familiarise themselves
\par
with the SLRC in preparation for their move to secondary after the summer.
\par
\emph{"I really enjoyed the model rocket. I am going to try and make it at home."}
\par
\emph{"I really enjoyed this brilliant exhibition I hope they will do something like}
\par
\emph{this again. It was really informitive and I would love to visit Nasa and in}
\par
\emph{future years go into space."}
\par
In discussion with the Researcher the science teacher indicated that he was
\par
not sure whether there was any benefit in using the SLRC in this way. This
\par
class showed signs of a lack of direction, and most of the time was spent
\par
wandering around instead of using the resources in an organised way. The
\par
librarian expressed dissatisfaction and felt there was a need for more
\par
direction but when questioned the teacher felt the pupils had managed
\par
adequately. When approached one pupil said, \emph{"it is better than being in the}
\par
\emph{classroom" }but was not sure that they had learned anything. One pupil did
\par
\textbf{\emph{remain in the SLRC}} and \textbf{\emph{continued his research}} over the lunch break.
\par
During the course of this lunch break eight other pupils were observed
\par
using the resources associated with the Space Fair.
\par
\textbf{Evidence of Impact}
\par
During the Space Fair visitors were invited to make comments in a book.
\par
Nine pages of comments were completed. Comments were not written by
\par
all the participants but it was available for the primary visits, the open
\par
evening and during school time. Spontaneous \textbf{\emph{letters of thanks}} were also
\par
received from two of the visiting primary schools. Ten S1 pupils completed
\par
a brief questionnaire.
\par
The secondary pupils generally treated their class visit to the Space Fair
\par
like any other work situation, they got on it but without great enthusiasm
\par
but those responding to the questionnaire indicated that they felt the visit
\par
had been worthwhile and 82\% said they had \textbf{\emph{learned something new}}.
\par
The primary pupils mentioned finding information, they enjoyed using the
\par
CD-ROMS, the talk and models.
\par
One pupil was particularly enthusiastic at the open evening and mentioned
\par
it to her form teacher the next day and \textbf{\emph{enquired about joining the}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Astronomical Society}}. Two weeks after the fair ended a pupil asked to use
\par
one of the space \textbf{\emph{CD-ROMs during lunch break}} and another wanted to find
\par
out about Black Holes on the Internet. These individuals continued to be
\par
stimulated by the Space Fair after the event and are typical of many of the
