\textbf{6.8.3 TES Newspaper Day}
\par
\textbf{Background}
\par
This is an annual event for this case study school and in the past efforts
\par
have been highly praised.
\par
The aim is for pupils to produce a newspaper in one day using national and
\par
local news.
\par
Twelve pupils were selected from volunteers and suitable candidates put
\par
forward by English teachers. The librarian was keen to have participants
\par
with the appropriate mix of skills required to complete the task to a high
\par
standard. This year the pupils were mainly from S3, younger than in
\par
previous years. Three S5 pupils had been involved in previous years and
\par
had the editorial positions; the other nine were responsible for specific jobs,
\par
such as advertising, fashion, and sport. The team had met with the librarian
\par
before the event to allocate tasks and all were expected to do some
\par
preparation before the day.
\par
\textbf{Observation}
\par
The day began with technical problems with the email not getting through.
\par
This was sorted during the morning. The package for participating schools
\par
had also not arrived on time.
\par
One of the sub-editors was \textbf{\emph{learning how to use the desktop publishing}}
\par
software, while another pupil arrived with examples of advertising that
\par
needed to be scanned, tidied and saved on the network. The pupil
\par
responsible for advertising was \textbf{\emph{particularly keen to show}} the Researcher
\par
what he was doing. Others showed less outward signs of enthusiasm but
\par
were all \textbf{\emph{fully absorbed}} in their individual tasks until the final stages of
\par
production at the end of the school day. Little help was sort from the
\par
librarian or visiting teachers but advice was given on computing techniques
\par
and one teacher helped during her free periods by speed typing pupil's
\par
work if required.
\par
Members of teaching staff looked in periodically to see progress and give
\par
encouragement. The librarian felt the day had been less frantic but more of
\par
her input had been required during the final stages than in previous years.
\par
The newspaper was finished about an hour before the deadline and the
\par
participants were pleased with the final result.
\par
\textbf{Evidence of Impact}
\par
All the participating pupils were asked for their reaction to the day. They all
\par
indicated that it had been an \textbf{\emph{enjoyable experience}}. Five pupils indicated
\par
they had \textbf{\emph{learned ICT skills}}, new desktop publishing and scanning skills as
\par
well as improving other keyboard or word-processing skills. Four felt they
\par
had \textbf{\emph{learned writing skills}}, such as writing in journalistic style. Three pupils
\par
mentioned \textbf{\emph{time management issues}}, working to deadlines and how the
\par
finishing off takes longer than you expect. Nine pupils felt the experience
\par
would \textbf{\emph{help them in the future}}, either with specific skills or for a career in
\par
journalism, and it would be valuable addition to pupils' record of
\par
achievement.
