105106\textbf{7. DISCUSSION}
\par
The study set out to identify the nature of the impact that the SLRC has on learning, firstly
\par
through the perceptions of the key players, i.e. learners, teachers and librarians, and secondly
\par
by monitoring the learning taking place in case studies of specific examples of SLRC related
\par
activities.
\par
This Section of the report examines the range and examples of learning experiences observed
\par
across the case studies; compares these observations with the earlier perceptions of teachers,
\par
learners and librarians (as summarised in figure 2); and finally summarises the nature of the
\par
potential learning impact as well as factors which have been observed to influence that impact
\par
both positively and negatively. While it was clearly seen that a broad range of learning
\par
experiences was being fostered by working in the SLRC, it was also clear that the learning
\par
was not necessarily related to the kind of outcomes the teachers stated as their aims.
\par
The learning experiences observed to be supported and fostered by the SLRC are discussed
\par
under the four broad learning themes used in current evaluation guidelines provided for
\par
Scottish schools1 and used to classify potential learning experiences identified in the focus
\par
groups and interviews (figure 1 and figure 2) and in individual case study analysis, i.e.
\par
\textbf{motivation, progression, independence, interaction. }These in turn relate well to other
\par
established classifications of learning as discussed in Section 5. Within each of these learning
\par
themes the discussion:
\par
{$\bullet$} begins with a reminder of the potential learning experiences identified within focus
\par
groups and interviews (as shown in figure 2) together with the potential indicators
\par
which had been drawn up as an aid to discussion with teachers and librarians and
\par
observation within case studies (see again figure 2);
\par
{$\bullet$} examines the evidence for the existence of these learning experiences within the case
\par
studies as well as the factors which appear to influence the extent of the impact the
\par
SLRC is likely to have on these experiences;
\par
{$\bullet$} examines the indicators which had earlier been identified as potentially useful in
\par
monitoring learning experiences in the SLRC in relation to a) their value as indicators
\par
within the case studies; b) new indicators and/or amendments to the list which have
\par
arisen as a result of case study observation; c) those indicators which, by their nature,
\par
have proved to be easier or more difficult to track;
\par
{$\bullet$} finally summarises the nature of the impact of the SLRC on learning in the form of a
\par
revised framework of learning areas, experiences and indicators. Clearly the
\par
emerging list of indicators are examples only. They would be expected to vary
\par
according to specific activities and local circumstances and the value of the
\par
framework (as discussed more fully in Section 8) is as a starting point for individual
\par
schools, teachers and librarians to plan and evaluate their own SLRC related activities
\par
and services.
\par
The section finishes with a general discussion on the revised framework, and some of the
\par
issues arising from the cases studies that have influenced the refinements and an examination
\par
of the value and feasibility of the various methods for data collection.
\par
107\textbf{7.1 MOTIVATION}
\par
\textbf{7.1.1 Evidence of impact on learning}
\par
Motivation was one of the learning experiences that all the groups of participants identified in
\par
the focus groups and interviews and the SLRC did indeed appear to have a significant role in
\par
fostering this aspect of learning, although the potential impact was also seen to be limited in
\par
certain circumstances. Given favourable circumstances the SLRC can encourage motivation
\par
to learn within the specific activity going on and also stimulate motivation beyond the
\par
immediate task to other learning situations within the school and home environment. The
\par
potential for enthusiasm and fostering a motivation to learn were seen in all the case study
\par
investigations but the other learning experiences identified within this theme were less
\par
obvious and more difficult to trace. Motivation would appear to be closely linked to the
\par
ability to proceed with a task as well as interaction with others. Within this theme the learning
\par
experiences of enthusiasm and motivation to learn are closely related.
\par
In the impact framework (figure 2) the potential learning experiences and indicators
\par
associated with the theme of motivation were identified as those shown in table 12.
\par
