The first two learning experiences, enthusiasm and motivation to learn, were relatively easy to
\par
observe and monitor through discussion and feedback but the other three are less easily
\par
recognised and would usually require further investigation beyond the SLRC.
\par
\textbf{Enthusiasm}
\par
This was observed in the curriculum based project work in S1 and S2 by the initial
\par
enthusiasm with which the pupils began their research. The pupils in case studies 1 and 3
\par
knew their way around the SLRC and were able to locate resources that might be appropriate
\par
immediately. The pupils in case study 2 were unfamiliar with their surroundings and less sure
\par
of how to tackle their task and did not display the same initial enthusiasm to get started.
\par
However, individual pupils did show more excitement when they began using the computers
\par
and the video and when questioned during the second session in the SLRC their response was
\par
very positive with only one pupil indicating that she had not enjoyed the experience. The use
\par
of computers was seen to increase interest and stimulate action to varying degrees in pupils in
\par
case studies 1, 3, 4 and 5.
\par
108This expression of enthusiasm was apparent in other individual pupils when they interacted
\par
with the Researcher in case study 3, or with the teacher and each other in case studies 1 and 2.
\par
The four senior pupils interviewed in case study 4 expressed enthusiasm for their
\par
investigation although there were indications from the questionnaires that some of the pupils
\par
whose investigations had not produced such good results were not as enthusiastic. Although
\par
discussions initiated by pupils about the books they were reading was not observed there was
\par
evidence in the reading records and the questionnaires that this could be an indicator of
\par
enthusiasm for particular books and reading in general. Although one teacher expressed
\par
concerns about using the final piece of work as a means of measuring enthusiasm, there did
\par
appear to be evidence of effort and extra input in some examples of work that could be
\par
interpreted as a form of enthusiasm.
\par
The voluntary return to the SLRC to continue the activity was thought to indicate enthusiasm
\par
and this could also include the effort put into the activity at home. Individual pupils were
\par
observed or reported to return to the SLRC to continue project work (case studies 1 and 2)
\par
and the final work reflected pupil effort at other times (case studies 3, 4 and 5). The pupils in
\par
case study 6 showed their enthusiasm not only by returning to the SLRC to record their
\par
reading in their jotters and to borrow books but by their accomplishments in achieving the
\par
bronze awards for reading. The pupil librarians showed their enthusiasm by continued
\par
commitment to their roles, by the effort they put into the projects they initiated and by the
\par
positive group interaction that was observed. The special events organised in the SLRC
\par
highlighted isolated incidences where individual pupils were enthusiastic enough to pursue an
\par
element of that activity beyond the immediate occasion but these examples are easily missed
\par
as they tend to happen after the event or outside the SLRC.
\par
The factors, which appeared to have an impact on the enthusiasm of the pupils, were
\par
familiarity with their surroundings and the ability to proceed with the task, i.e. to "make
\par
positive progress" with the task in hand. The use of computers was perceived by participants
\par
in the focus group discussions as being a means of motivating pupils to learn and of
\par
stimulating interest. In the case studies this introduction of technology was particularly
\par
stimulating, though not exclusively, to boys but problems with logging on or the speed of
\par
getting results was seen to frustrate individuals. Group interaction was seen to enhance
\par
enthusiasm when relations were good but in case studies 2 and 7 examples of tension were
\par
observed to cause problems. The enthusiasm of the teacher (and librarian) and the willingness
\par
to interact and become involved with pupils was thought to increase the sense of purpose and
\par
motivation. Discussion would appear to stimulate motivation as well as being an indicator of
\par
some of the learning experiences within the motivation theme. A positive experience in the
\par
SLRC can also lead to motivation in other aspects of learning.
\par
\textbf{Motivation to Learn}
\par
The pupils' willingness to get on with the set task and their absorption in that task were
\par
thought to be examples of motivation to learn. This was observed in various forms in all the
\par
case studies. The pupils in case studies 1, 2, 3 and 5 working on curriculum related projects
\par
were seen to be getting on with the task in hand but on closer inspection it appeared that some
\par
individuals were not fully absorbed in what they were doing; some of the discussion was
\par
unrelated to the project work, some pupils were not fully engaged in reading the texts they
\par
had in front of them or able to proceed for some other reason. The pupils in case study 4 were
\par
not observed working but reports from the teacher suggested that many of them had found it
\par
difficult to get down to their investigation.
\par
The two other case studies required a slightly different approach to consider the motivation to
\par
learn, as the need to be fully absorbed in the task was not as great. The pupils librarians'
\par
willingness to learn new skills, transfer those skills to the classroom situation was thought to
\par
reflect a motivation to learn and the comments from the guidance teacher about one pupil's
\par
attitude to work and school would also suggest increased motivation to get on. The pupils in
\par
109case study 6 did appear from observation to be actively involved in selecting books and some
\par
were seen to read the books chosen and the questionnaire gave an insight into how the pupils
\par
themselves thought their attitude to reading had changed. Observation of the ad hoc use of the
\par
SLRC would suggest that pupils can become fully absorbed in their personal activities and the
\par
fact that lunchtimes are so busy in some of the SLRCs, would also indicate a willingness to be
\par
productively occupied.
\par
\textbf{Imagination and experimentation}
\par
These learning experiences to do with promoting the individuality and personality of pupils
\par
appeared to be present in case studies 6, 7 and 8. The potential was evident in the way pupils
\par
in case study 7, who are allowed the freedom to approach activities in their own way and
\par
given the time to experiment, and develop their own ideas. However, the intangible nature of
\par
these learning experiences and the complexities of getting to grips with individual learning
\par
meant that this learning experience was not really identified or explored. The nature of the
\par
task undertaken within the SLRC has an impact on the learning experience most likely to be
\par
affected and therefore different types of activities, such as those associated with art, might
\par
have given more insight into this type of learning experience. The extension work in case
\par
study 1, when pupils wrote a poem or a piece of writing about their animal did reflect
\par
imagination.
\par
\textbf{Satisfaction}
\par
If the experience in the SLRC was positive then satisfaction was expressed either in terms of
\par
an enthusiastic response to questioning or the voluntary display of the pupil's efforts. This
\par
was observed in the curriculum related case studies and with the pupil librarians. Less
\par
obvious was the return of a pupil to the SLRC for another unrelated activity that was inspired
\par
by previous success. Pupils indicated on their questionnaires whether they wanted to return to
\par
the SLRC for another activity and the responses favoured a desire to return but this did not
\par
appear to reflect a true sense of achievement or success except in case study 2 where other
\par
expressions of satisfaction had also been seen.
\par
\textbf{Self-esteem}
\par
Evidence for self-esteem being generated by the SLRC activities was only thought to be seen
\par
in case study 7 where the attitude of the pupil librarians reflected a confidence and ability to
\par
take responsibility within their own environment and a pride in their achievements. The
\par
remarks about one pupil by a guidance teacher regarding a change in attitude towards school
\par
were thought to reflect increased self-esteem. The SLRC may well have a positive role to play
\par
in other ways to enhance self-esteem by enabling pupils to have their work recognised in
\par
some way, for instance through awards or displays, but identifying the impact would appear
\par
to be more difficult and may well rely on other members of staff or the family to identify this
\par
learning experience.
\par
\textbf{7.1.2 Value and feasibility of the indicators}
\par
The indicators that appear to apply to the motivation aspect of learning and were identified in
\par
the case study areas are:
\par
{$\bullet$} expression of enthusiasm (verbally or effort work);
\par
{$\bullet$} willing participation;
\par
{$\bullet$} absorption in the task;
\par
{$\bullet$} continuation of an activity (beyond the immediate occasion);
\par
{$\bullet$} change in attitude.
\par
Although similar to those identified as possible indicators in the impact framework (figure 2),
\par
there are fewer indicators in the list above. The wording was refined as similar indicators
\par
began to emerge from different situations and different tracking mechanisms. "Expression of
\par
110enthusiasm" encompassed observed spontaneous reactions to a situation as well as those
\par
encountered through discussion with individuals or deduced from the effort and additional
\par
qualities displayed in written work. In a similar manner "willing participation" and
\par
"absorption in the task" can be gauged from a range of possible monitoring devices. The
\par
"continuation of an activity" and "change in attitude" require checks to be made in other
\par
contexts, either through keeping brief records in the SLRC itself or by enquiring about
\par
behaviour and activities from other members of teaching staff. Some evidence of the
\par
continuation of an activity may be visible in the final work if this is available for scrutiny by
\par
the librarian. Informal remarks made by teaching staff and family members may also indicate
\par
a pupil's motivation related to a variety of learning experiences. A ``change in attitude'' would
\par
appear to require more extensive monitoring and knowledge of the individual and may, of
\par
course, be influenced by other factors unrelated to the SLRC, such as maturity or friendship
\par
groups. Therefore, those indicators most easily identified were closely related to the activity
\par
under scrutiny but it is possible to identify impact of the SLRC on motivation beyond the
\par
immediate circumstances when steps are taken to specifically look for learning experiences in
\par
this way.
\par
It is interesting to note that the indicators for motivation identified during the case study work
\par
echo those suggested by librarians in research carried out by Kuhlthau on the Library Power
\par
initiative2
\par
.
\par
\textbf{7.2 PROGRESSION}
\par
The theme "progress in learning" identifies learning experiences related to skills, knowledge
\par
and understanding and within the SLRC environment this is associated with the finding and
\par
use of information. The development of skills provide more tangible means of identifying
\par
particular learning experiences and in this case the indicators could be described as "learning
\par
outcomes" a term teachers are more familiar with. The type of skills identified during the
\par
focus groups have been loosely categorised under headings generally used by the librarians in
\par
the interviews (detailed below and listed in table 13) but the definition of terms associated
\par
with information literacy is constantly under debate.
\par
{$\bullet$} Library skills: this includes the traditional skills covered by induction courses such as
\par
understanding of the layout of the SLRC, use of the catalogue, the types of resources
\par
available, and familiarity with their use.
\par
{$\bullet$} Information handling skills: this covers the use of information and various process
\par
models can be found in the literature to help support and develop these skills, such as
\par
Marland3, Eisenberg \& Berkowitz4, Kuhlthau5, to name but a few.
\par
{$\bullet$} Reading skills: in the impact framework this was treated as a separate category because
\par
it was isolated in discussions and was thought by teachers and librarians to cover the
\par
development of a reading habit as well as reading for information including skimming
\par
and scanning.
\par
{$\bullet$} ICT skills: these have been given a separate category in the Report because this was how
\par
they were portrayed in discussions and in the current curriculum literature. There is an
\par
argument that ICT skills, apart from the physical skills of keyboard mastery, are very
\par
similar to the skills required when handling all information that is effective search
\par
strategies, and evaluation for relevance, bias, currency, manipulating and communicating
\par
i.e. the information handling skills.
\par
Increased knowledge and understanding covers the higher order cognitive aspects of
\par
assimilating information and making sense of it within the individual's existing knowledge to
\par
increase recall ability and the range and depth of understanding. The knowledge and
\par
understanding is then used by the pupil to produce a piece of work of a quality that represents
\par
111the ability of the individual. This learning experience is most likely to be related to the direct
\par
outcome set by the subject teacher and is most likely to be assessed by the teacher.
\par
The case study Research identified great potential for the development of learning
\par
experiences in this broad area of learning. The SLRC can have a significant impact on the
\par
development of skills and this in turn encourages knowledge and understanding and the
\par
production of quality work. It would appear from the evidence that the introduction, practise
\par
and mastery of skills can be relatively easily monitored within the SLRC context but that
\par
more work needs to be done to understand what the skills are and how they will be identified.
\par
The other two learning experiences in progression, i.e. increased knowledge and
\par
understanding and the quality of work are more difficult to establish without closer
\par
collaboration with teaching staff.
