\par
\textbf{Awareness and development of skills:}
\par
\textbf{Library skills}
\par
The majority of pupils in all the case studies showed signs of understanding how to go about
\par
finding resources. Observation during the case studies showed pupils' ability to navigate the
\par
SLRC and find a variety of resources and this was taken as evidence of their understanding of
\par
library skills. In case study 1, 3, 4 \& 5 pupils were reminded of the resources at the beginning
\par
of the sessions but there was evidence that pupils had transferred the skills (for instance the
\par
use of contents and index pages, the layout of encyclopaedias and how to go about finding
\par
additional resources using the catalogue and keywords) from previous project work in the
\par
SLRC. The pupils in case study 2, who had not completed their library induction, were
\par
observed using the contents and index pages and were familiar with the layout of an
\par
encyclopaedia and would have transferred these skills from primary school. References to
\par
other sources of information in their final work or responses to the questionnaire and
\par
discussion was taken as evidence that pupils had the ability to find appropriate material from
\par
other sources, such as home or the public library. This transferability of skills was an aspect
\par
of learning that appeared to cause concern in the focus group discussions and interviews.
\par
Individual pupils were observed to have particular problems finding resources and many did
\par
not appear to think of using some types of resources unless reminded but there was enough
\par
evidence of pupils finding information to suggest that reinforcement would have overcome
\par
these difficulties. Although the senior pupils in case studies 4 and 5 did not always make the
\par
connection of how previous experience of using the SLRC might have helped them, there was
\par
112evidence in the responses to the questionnaire and self-evaluation sheets to suggest that
\par
previous mastery of library skills contributed to their ability to find information.
\par
\textbf{ICT skills}
\par
Computers were used in all the case studies and the ability of pupils to master quite quickly
\par
specific skills was evident when watching pupils help each other or when they were given
\par
instructions by the librarians or teachers. The use of particular software applications for
\par
practical purposes by the pupil librarians (case study 7) and those involved in the TES
\par
Newspaper Day (Section 6.8) and the sharing of that knowledge with others are good
\par
examples. However, individuals were also observed to grasp more fundamental skills such as
\par
the importing of pictures, printing selected text, while other pupils indicated they learned
\par
word-processing skills as a result of working in the SLRC. In case studies 1 and 2 pupils were
\par
observed to gain confidence in using a computer to search for information whether from a
\par
CD-ROM or the Internet. In case study 5 the use of search engines and keywords was
\par
introduced to individual pupils and was seen to be successful for some but the problems of
\par
time, access to sites and the ability to handle the information were thought to limit success
\par
and the learning experience. Some of the seniors in case study 4 were very successful in
\par
developing their Internet searching skills to obtain appropriate information but those
\par
interviewed also acknowledged a lack of understanding of some of the authority and
\par
copyright issues associated with the use of information from the Internet.
\par
\textbf{Information handling skills}
\par
The information handling skills were less developed than the library skills but in all the case
\par
study schools doing project work there was evidence of an awareness by pupils of some of the
\par
skills involved. Some pupils in each case study showed signs of developing or mastering
\par
skills indicating some input and transfer of previous information skills introduction. Two
\par
pupils in case study 2 explained their methods of extracting information using highlighters or
\par
crossing out unwanted information. Pupils in case study 1 displayed note taking techniques in
\par
their jotters and when observed using resources. Where the rough work and the final piece of
\par
work was available in case studies 4 and 5, there was evidence of selection of appropriate
\par
material and noting sources of information. The examination of rough and final work
\par
provided useful evidence of what information was found, what was selected and whether the
\par
selection was the most appropriate. From the examples examined it would suggest that those
\par
pupils who were given the higher marks had developed better information handling skills but
\par
there was also evidence in the jotters that some pupils had found significant amounts of
\par
relevant information which they did not use, and there may be several reasons for this, such as
\par
their own interpretation of the usefulness of material 6 or an inability, for whatever reason, to
\par
interpret the material. The teacher in case study 4 expressed concerns that some of his senior
\par
pupils had not mastered the techniques of selecting the most appropriate material and case
\par
study 5 reflected a similar situation where \emph{some} pupils had well-developed information
\par
handling skills, but \emph{many} revealed weaknesses. The Research found evidence that using the
\par
SLRC can encourage the use of the information handling skills and can provide the
\par
opportunity for these skills to be introduced and developed but it also highlighted instances in
\par
each case study (1-5) where a lack of skills limited the effective learning process.
\par
In case studies 2, 5, 7 and 8 indicators of impact were identified which were not specified in
\par
the learning experiences outlined in the original framework (figure 2 and table 13) but are part
\par
of the information handling process described in the literature (Marland, Eisenberg \&
\par
Berkowitz and Kuhlthau). The use of peer and self-evaluation in case study 5 was introduced
\par
as a means of evaluating the success of the course by examining what the pupils indicated
\par
they had learned. This form of reflection can also help pupils understand strengths and
\par
weakness and gives them the opportunity to develop the skills of critical analysis and this was
\par
evident in some comments made during the discussion or on the peer evaluations forms. The
\par
opportunity to develop critical skills was also introduced in case study 2 and some of the
\par
pupils managed reasonably well but others found this task difficult. In case study 2 the pupils
\par
113were evaluating the group presentations of their research findings and this leads on to the
\par
reporting skills.
\par
The skills associated with reporting or presenting in front of an audience were only observed
\par
to be touched on briefly and the librarians were not impressed with the quality of the talks but
\par
they were felt to reflect the quality and understanding of the research. In case study 2 the
\par
course provided the means to do a second presentation and these showed signs of
\par
improvement in both the presentation and the research as well as a greater sense of
\par
achievement. This confirmed the finding that an understanding of the task enables pupils to
\par
progress and highlights the conditioning factor that providing the opportunity for pupils to
\par
reflect and improve their performance can serve as a useful learning tool.
\par
\textbf{Reading skills}
\par
In the original framework (figure 2 and table 13) reading skills were expressed as a separate
\par
learning experience. In the case studies the skimming and scanning techniques were seen as
\par
part of the information handling process although evidence of use of the techniques was very
\par
difficult to establish. The development of a reading habit and the enjoyment of reading was
\par
investigated in case study 6 but again the actual reading skills of fluency and understanding
\par
could not be established easily from the SLRC perspective. The willingness of these pupils to
\par
try a variety of genre or different authors was evident from the responses given to the
\par
questionnaire in which pupils reported that their attitudes had changed and that they had tried
\par
different genres and authors recommended by the librarian or on the database. Their
\par
responses also revealed an awareness of the relationship between reading and other learning,
\par
especially language development, but correlations of this sort are very complex and would
\par
need much greater input from the teaching staff to establish any actual examples of impact of
\par
the SLRC on reading ability. What was evident from the number and variety of books
\par
borrowed and the awards given, was that the SLRC can have an impact on the motivation to
\par
read and the selection of reading material, particularly when emphasis is placed on
\par
introducing new elements to stimulate interest. The evidence that discussion can be a
\par
motivating and monitoring tool from other case study units suggests that this may be of value
\par
in developing and evaluating this learning experience. The questionnaires and anecdotal
\par
evidence would suggest that discussion is more significant than was apparent from the
\par
observation during this case study.
\par
\textbf{Increased knowledge and understanding}
\par
Evidence of increased knowledge and understanding was difficult to judge in the majority of
\par
the case studies without knowing how much the pupils already knew. This emphasises the
\par
importance of joint planning and evaluating the work carried out in the SLRC by both
\par
librarians and teachers. The teacher in case study 1 thought all her pupils had learned
\par
something about their individual animal and the responses to the questionnaire would suggest
\par
that the pupils themselves thought they had learned facts and skills. The pupils in case study 2
\par
displayed a greater knowledge and understanding of their topics as they presented their
\par
information in additional ways throughout the project. The teacher in case study 4 expressed
\par
disappointment in the knowledge and understanding of his pupils because connections
\par
between their research and biology theory or the investigation requirements were not always
\par
made but the final work and the questionnaire would suggest that new knowledge and
\par
understanding had taken place but not necessarily to satisfy the outcomes required by the
\par
exercise. The pupil responses in questionnaires, peer and self-evaluation sheets and the final
\par
work does give an indication of new knowledge but it is limited without greater
\par
understanding of the pupils involved and the work is not always available to the librarians
\par
unless specifically requested. Some evidence of knowledge and understanding comes with the
\par
ability to transfer skills and process and use previous knowledge that was seen in some of the
\par
learning experiences associated with the development of skills.
\par
114\textbf{Quality of work}
\par
The quality of the submitted work and the mark given is an indication of the ability of the
\par
individual and the Research did establish that giving pupils the opportunity to work in the
\par
SLRC is not necessarily enough to improve the quality of work (as in case studies 3 and 4).
\par
The impact the SLRC may have on quality of work is related to pupil motivation and the
\par
ability to proceed with the task set and this in turn is related to the development of skills and
\par
there was evidence that pupils' work was directly related to the amount or quality of
\par
information they had found in the SLRC. The teachers indicated that the work could not have
\par
been done as easily without the availability of the resources in the SLRC but were less aware
\par
of the need to consolidate the skills required to use these resources effectively. However,
\par
there was evidence of pupil effort in submitted written work, of observed improvements in
\par
reporting skills (case study 2), and an expressed sense of achievement by pupils (case studies
\par
6, 7 and 8 in particular). Again the final work is often not available to the librarian and
\par
therefore other methods of monitoring the quality of work need to be established if this is
\par
something that individual schools want to evaluate themselves. The examination of rough
\par
work did give an idea of how individuals were working and over a period of time this could
\par
give an indication of the progression of skills developed by an individual. In case study 6, the
\par
number of pupils who were working towards or had already achieved awards for their reading
\par
was higher than the previous year at the same time and this was an indication that the
\par
database or the individual reading lists promoted by the SLRC had an impact on the
\par
achievements of these individuals. It would be possible for librarians to keep a record of
\par
achievements awarded for work that involved the SLRC and using action research over time
\par
these records could provide valuable evidence of impact.
\par
\textbf{Study skills}
\par
Organisation and time management skills have been groups together as study skills, which is
\par
how they were described in case study 5. Developing an understanding of study skills was
\par
one of the aims in case study 5 and the pupils responded in their self-evaluation with an
\par
awareness of the need for organisation and time management skills in their application to
\par
work. In the framework (figure 2) this learning experience was placed under the theme of
\par
independent learning but seeing how the pupils were being introduced to the idea and became
\par
aware of the skills it was thought to be more appropriate in the progression learning theme.
\par
Once mastered, and pupils are observed to use these skills confidently and competently, the
\par
learning experience would become an indicator of independence. The pupil librarians and the
\par
pupils involved in the newspaper day discussed the need for organisation and time
\par
management skills when their activities had deadlines and awareness was also expressed by
\par
pupils on the evaluation sheets in case studies 2 and 5. The impact of the SLRC on these
\par
learning experiences would be to provide opportunities for pupils to work to deadlines but
\par
more important is the contribution the SLRC can make in the development of other skills
\par
which enable pupils to fulfil the requirements of many of their assignments.
\par
\textbf{7.2.2 Value and feasibility of the indicators}
\par
The indicators that appear to apply to the progression theme and were identified in the case
\par
studies are:
\par
{$\bullet$} Development of skills: awareness of need, leading to ability to practise (leading to
\par
confident and therefore independent use)
\par
{$\bullet$} Library skills: use codebuster, catalogue, index, contents page, encyclopaedia,
\par
{$\bullet$} Information handling skills: use skim \& scan, note taking appropriate material for task
\par
{$\bullet$} ICT skills: use unfamiliar software (including search the Internet)
\par
{$\bullet$} Study skills: organise task, submit task on time, evaluate own or others' work
\par
{$\bullet$} Reading skills: try different genre or author, relationship between reading and other
\par
learning
\par
{$\bullet$} New knowledge
\par
115{$\bullet$} Achievement (leading to self-esteem)
\par
{$\bullet$} Ability to apply skills or knowledge in different context (leading to independence)
\par
In the progression area of learning the indicators needed to establish evidence of learning
\par
were more specific than those for motivation and required a breakdown of the identified
\par
learning experiences into particular skills. The Research identified a few basic indicators,
\par
very similar to the examples given in the original framework (figure 2 and table 13), to
\par
establish evidence of some aspects of the possible learning experiences; the ability to use the
\par
contents and index pages to locate information and highlighting information or the use of
\par
bullet points indicated an ability to select information from a resource. On some occasions an
\par
individual displayed an awareness of a skill and at other times a pupil displayed an ability to
\par
use a skill. Once a skill was mastered, the individual appeared confident in that learning
\par
experience and could proceed without assistance, these skills became an indicator of
\par
independence. However, there is a need for more development in this area and the indicators
\par
used in the Project are examples only. There was evidence that some of the skills overlap
\par
between the learning experiences, such as the use of ICT as a resource overlaps with the
\par
information handling process and more development should clarify the learning experiences
\par
and the indicators that would be most appropriate within the local context. The learning
\par
experiences of increased knowledge and understanding and the quality of work are more
\par
easily examined by members of the teaching staff but there are ways for the librarian to
\par
establish indicators by using rough work and keeping records of achievements (of any kind)
\par
and by establishing close working relations with members of teaching staff.
\par
\textbf{7.3 INDEPENDENCE}
\par
Throughout the Research Project professionals warned of the problems of cause and effect
\par
and this was summarized by one librarian who said, " \emph{Confident pupils use the library, the}
\par
\emph{library does not make confident pupils"}. The case studies suggested that independence is
\par
closely associated with the confident and competent handling of a skill, the ability to proceed
\par
without assistance. It is also dependent upon motivation and the desire to proceed, the
\par
recognition of a need and the confidence to tackle something learned either in a new
\par
environment or under new circumstances. The SLRC can help to develop confidence and
\par
independence by providing the opportunity for pupils to learn, develop and master skills in a
\par
cross- and extra- curricular environment. Table 14 identifies the learning experiences from the
\par
focus group discussions with the associated indicators.
\par
