\par
116There was evidence of independence in all the case studies but the indicators are also
\par
associated with other aspects of learning and the distinctions are not always clear. There were
\par
quite a few learning experiences identified within this learning area but some of the learning
\par
experiences were less easily observed or the situations were not conducive to that particular
\par
learning experience.
\par
\textbf{Serendipity and exploration}
\par
Evidence of serendipity and exploration was not forthcoming in the case studies although
\par
some aspects were touched upon in the special events and ad hoc section. The freedom
\par
associated with lunchtime use of the SLRC and the role of the pupil librarians did allow for a
\par
few examples of exploration to be identified. At lunchtime pupils were observed to explore
\par
the Internet in school F where pupils were observed to look up an author site and others were
\par
reported to have found the official examination timetable before it was given in class. One of
\par
the pupil librarians explained that he found books of interest when doing the shelving.
\par
However, these were isolated incidences and as already explained in Section 4, the Research
\par
did not establish any consistent means of establishing evidence.
\par
\textbf{Reinforcement}
\par
Reinforcement was observed to happen both in the more structured class time and at
\par
lunchtime. The intended meaning of this learning experience was in the unsupervised use of
\par
the SLRC to reinforce some aspect that the pupil was aware needed practice or wanted to
\par
develop further. During class time the reinforcement of skills was more evident and is more
\par
closely related to the progress in learning theme and the indicators developed for the
\par
progression framework would be most appropriate. The ad hoc use of the SLRC to develop
\par
skills or reinforce class work was observed in all the schools where the lunch period was
\par
reviewed. However, again the Research did not establish any means of finding evidence in
\par
any consistent manner except by observing how pupils were occupied and relating that back
\par
to other activities.
\par
\textbf{Transference of skills \& knowledge}
\par
The transfer of skills and knowledge has already been discussed in the previous learning
\par
theme and this highlights one the difficulties of using defined areas of learning to look for
\par
impact. The ability to transfer skills and knowledge is viewed as evidence of independence
\par
because the pupil had mastered the process enough to use confidently without assistance. This
\par
can be seen as the ultimate step in the progression theme of learning leading to independent
\par
learning. The indicator identified as evidence of the transfer of skills was observed use of
\par
skills or previous knowledge in a new situation and this required knowledge of what the
\par
pupils already knew or had covered on other occasions. Independent use of these skills and
\par
knowledge in their own time was thought to be another indicator of independence and again
\par
this required looking at how pupils were using the SLRC in their own time or how they were
\par
using skills developed in the SLRC elsewhere. The latter required discussion with pupils
\par
about their experiences (case study 4) or an examination of their work (case study 5), which is
\par
not always an option for the librarian.
\par
\textbf{Confidence in use}
\par
Evidence for confident use of resources was identified while pupils worked in the SLRC
\par
either during class time or in their own time. Those pupils with well-developed information
\par
handling skills, in case studies 1-5, appeared to make effective use of the SLRC, and
\par
examples of confident SLRC users were observed in all the case studies where individual
\par
pupils continued and progressed with their work unaided either during the class time or their
\par
own time. In some cases this confident use may only apply to a few specific tasks within a
\par
learning experience, such as the ability to find a site on the Internet but not locate the most
\par
relevant information from that site. There were also indications of confident use of resources
\par
beyond the SLRC environment, whether at home, or using the public library. This was
\par
evident from extra visits pupils made to the SLRC in their own time and from additional
\par
117information or effort in submitted work. Confident use of the SLRC was also observed during
\par
the lunch break when pupils had the opportunity to develop their own interests; this was often
\par
associated with the computers, which is where much of the evidence of learning was observed
\par
during the lunchtime, such as the use of the Power Point application in school J. The ability to
\par
transfer skills across the curriculum and in different situations has already been mentioned
\par
under progression but is also seen as an indication of independence because the skills have
\par
been mastered and the individual is able to apply them unaided.
\par
Another aspect of confidence identified within the Report is the ability to appreciate the need
\par
for help and ask for it from the teaching or library staff. There was a mixed response in the
\par
questionnaires to this question between pupils who lacked confidence and would rather ask a
\par
friend and those who recognised the expertise of either the librarian or the teacher. This may
\par
well reflect the local context and the organisation of the particular projects but the observation
\par
did indicate that pupils, particularly those using computers, were often unaware of their
\par
needs. It was noticeable that the S3 pupils were more reluctant to seek help and some of the
\par
sixth form pupils had the impression that the SLRC and the librarians would not be able to
\par
help with such specialist and individual investigations they were undertaking. However, these
\par
sixth form pupils showed evidence of confidence by being aware of other sources of
\par
information and indicating that they had pursued their search at university libraries.
\par
An extension of this is the evidence from the teacher in case study 2 that her classes had
\par
developed more confidence to interact in class because of their project work. Although the co-
\par
operative learning model used is likely to have had more impact than the use of the SLRC it is
\par
an interesting example of innovative use that had an impact in a number of ways on learning.
\par
\textbf{Study ethos}
\par
The development of study skills has been discussed under the Progression theme and
\par
evidence that pupils were aware of these skills was highlighted there. The practice of these
\par
skills reflects an independent approach to study and the evidence for this was inferred from
\par
the timely submission of well-presented and well-organised work in any of the case studies
\par
and appeared to reflect those pupils who had well-developed information handling skills. The
\par
flow charts completed for the Researcher by the senior pupils in case study 4 confirmed this
\par
view.
\par
\textbf{Awareness}
\par
The awareness of the wider information world and the need for it has been covered in other
\par
aspects of independence, such as confidence and the study ethos. Although it was considered
\par
a learning experience in its own right the evidence from the case studies would suggest that it
\par
is closely related to other aspects of independence and might not warrant such significance.
\par
\textbf{Initiative}
\par
Case study 7 revealed the most evidence for the use of initiative, where the pupils were given
\par
responsibility, given recognition of their skills and ideas and the chance to develop them.
\par
There was anecdotal evidence of the use of initiative in ad hoc use of the SLRC and again
\par
some of these examples reflected the freedom, support and availability of space and resources
\par
in the SLRC to develop personal ideas and is closely linked to the learning experiences in the
\par
motivation theme. Indicators for the use of initiative were identified as the pupils' ability to
\par
initiate and follow through an idea and this was seen for example in the way the pupil
\par
librarians developed multi-media programmes to train new librarians.
\par
118\textbf{7.3.2 Validity and feasibility of the indicators}
\par
The indicators that appear to apply to the independence theme and were identified in the case
\par
studies are:
\par
{$\bullet$} Ability and confidence to continue and progress with task unaided either in SLRC
\par
sessions or in own time, beyond SLRC
\par
{$\bullet$} Awareness when help is required and confident to seek help
\par
{$\bullet$} Awareness of need for time management and organisation for independent learning
\par
{$\bullet$} Transfer skills from elsewhere in curriculum
\par
{$\bullet$} Continuing project elsewhere
\par
{$\bullet$} Increased self-esteem
\par
{$\bullet$} Use of initiative
\par
{$\bullet$} Independent choice of quality fiction
\par
The indicators identified during the case studies were mainly associated with confidence. This
\par
encompassed unsupervised use of the SLRC, appropriate use of resources either in the SLRC
\par
or elsewhere and confidence developed within the SLRC that was reflected in the ability to
\par
tackle an activity in other areas of the school or outside. Given the right circumstances this
\par
confidence can then be expressed as initiative either within the SLRC or elsewhere. The most
\par
easily recognisable examples were when pupils had mastered a skill and could proceed
\par
without help and observation of the lunchtime use of the SLRC, including the work of the
\par
pupil librarians, provided examples of confident use. However, during observation of the
\par
project work case studies it became evident on closer inspection that some pupils displayed an
\par
air of confidence that in reality was either misplaced or masked an obstacle of some sort. One
\par
of the indicators of independence was thought to be the ability of an individual to understand
\par
the need for help and seek it from the most appropriate source of assistance.
\par
\textbf{7.4 INTERACTION}
\par
The SLRC was perceived by the teachers and librarians as having an impact on group
\par
interaction, peer support and providing a valuable environment for individuals to develop
\par
social interaction. All the case studies, except the senior pupils biology project, revealed
\par
aspects of interaction where the SLRC had a role to play. The most obvious examples were
\par
the peer support, social interaction with other pupils and members of staff and awareness of
\par
the needs for others. There was not only evidence that this was taking place but also evidence
\par
that this interaction had an impact on other learning experiences, particularly motivation and
\par
progression.
\par
The evidence for interaction was in the amount of relevant discussion and sharing of ideas
\par
amongst the pupils and teaching staff and the indicators identified in the table 15 below were
\par
the sort of evidence seen in the case studies.
\par
