\par
119\textbf{Peer support \& teamwork}
\par
The project work provided opportunities for discussion, peer support and sharing of ideas and
\par
resources. This was very evident in case study 1, where the teacher interacted with the pupils
\par
and encouraged discussion and the sharing of ideas and resources. In case study 2, where this
\par
was encouraged to happen, the uncertainty limited effectiveness of interaction initially, but as
\par
the groups became more at ease relationships began to develop. The need to understand your
\par
own task before you can interact with others was thought to have a bearing in both case
\par
studies 2 and 3, where again isolated instances of peer support were observed, in the sharing
\par
of resources and help with computer skills, but the productive interaction seen in case study 1
\par
was not observed in case studies 2 and 3. In case study 4 and 5, the pupils were more
\par
independent in their approach to the research exercise but during the latter, there was some
\par
evidence from the discussion in session 3 that this helped the learning process.
\par
\textbf{Develop communication skills}
\par
The ability to discuss the task or a book with peers or a member of staff was thought to be an
\par
indication of interaction and the developing of social and communication skills. Pupils
\par
indicated that they discussed books they had read with others in case study 6 and discussion
\par
about books was also observed in the ad hoc use of the SLRC at lunchtime, either between
\par
pupils or with the librarian. In schools A and F this discussion about books was also observed
\par
amongst the staff. The pupil librarians in school J were also observed to discuss their own
\par
activities and to include other pupils and staff in their discussions.
\par
\textbf{Social interaction}
\par
The ability to mix with other groups was particularly noticeable with the pupil librarians who
\par
interacted with each other across the year groups and with other SLRC users. The librarian in
\par
school F had concerns about the isolated social behaviour of one of the pupils seeking refuge
\par
in the SLRC and, together with the English teacher, was attempting to integrate the pupil into
\par
a small group during English library sessions using the mutual interest of reading. The
\par
librarian is not alone in being able to identify individual pupils like this but does have a
\par
unique position in being able to observe pupils during recreation time and this might give an
\par
insight into both behaviour and possible courses of action.
\par
\textbf{Awareness of others}
\par
The policy in school A encouraged a respect for the needs of others while working in the
\par
SLRC and this calm, studious atmosphere was reflected in the pupils' behaviour when
\par
compared with the classroom and other schools where there was not the same emphasis. In
\par
school A, there was also evidence of pupils using appropriate behaviour in the SLRC. One
\par
librarian expressed concern when the pupils arrived for their first session because a few had
\par
caused problems on a previous occasion. However both librarians indicated that pupils were
\par
well behaved and generally considerate of each other.
\par
The co-operation within groups was particularly relevant to the project in school A and by the
\par
end of the course there was evidence that individuals were working better in their groups and
\par
the self-evaluation sheets revealed an awareness of the need for co-operation. The class
\par
teacher also thought the classes were working better as a whole after the course than previous
\par
classes at this stage of their first year in French.
\par
\textbf{Awareness \& knowledge of others in the wider world}
\par
There were limited opportunities for this learning experience to be displayed in the activities
\par
investigated in the Project. However, case studies 2 and 3 did provide the opportunity for
\par
some awareness of the wider world to be expressed in the factual content of the project work
\par
and there was some evidence, particularly in case study 2. In these circumstances it would
\par
appear to be more appropriate for awareness and knowledge of others in the wider world to be
\par
incorporated within knowledge and understanding within the progression theme.
\par
120\textbf{7.4.2 Validity and feasibility of the indicators}
\par
The indicators that appear to apply to the interaction theme and were identified in the case
\par
studies are:
\par
{$\bullet$} Discussion about activity/task with peers, staff
\par
{$\bullet$} Peer co-operation, sharing resources, explaining processes, working together, helping
\par
each other, evaluation
\par
{$\bullet$} Friendships and ability to mix with other groups
\par
{$\bullet$} Use of appropriate behaviour and respects for needs of others
\par
The willingness of pupils to help others and share ideas and resources were good indicators of
\par
co-operation and interaction and there was also evidence of respect for others in the way the
\par
pupils behaved towards each other but the awareness of others in the wider world was more
\par
difficult to establish unless it formed part of the overall aims for the project. The development
\par
of communication skills can be identified either in the social context described here where the
\par
SLRC can contribute to the way in which individuals interact and develop social skills or in
\par
terms of a development of communication skills which are more appropriate as the ultimate
\par
aim of the information handling process under the progression theme of learning.
\par
\textbf{7.5 REVISED FRAMEWORK}
\par
During the case study work the initial framework (figure 2) was explained to members of
\par
senior management in two schools and case study teachers in four of the schools. The
\par
teachers were preoccupied with the organisation of their own courses and showed little
\par
enthusiasm for the framework. Members of senior management responded in a more positive
\par
manner recognising the potential for identifying the learning experiences and indicators in
\par
this way. The framework continued to be reviewed and refined during the case study period in
\par
the light of feedback and one of the developments was a planning and evaluation cycle which
\par
attempted to place the framework into the school, departmental and course evaluation system.
\par
This is represented in figure 3. At the end of the case study work but before the final analysis
\par
had been carried out all of the participating librarians from the case study and focus group
\par
schools were invited to a meeting for a presentation of the preliminary findings. Two
\par
exercises for the librarians were organised for the day and were designed to provide the
\par
Project with feedback on a) the librarians' responses to issues arising from the Project and
\par
whether they reflected their own experiences and b) the value of the framework (figure 2) as a
\par
planning and evaluation tool.
\par
The librarians were given two exercises, working in small groups and sharing ideas. The first
\par
activity invited them to look at problems in an imaginary curriculum project scenario and
\par
consider ways of making the learning experience more effective. The aim was to examine the
\par
kind of ideas they came up with and whether they were reinforced or differed from the
\par
findings emerging from the case studies and the extent to which they were focusing on the
\par
learning process. Some of the librarians did pick up on issues from the initial findings from
\par
the research and these tended to be the librarians who throughout the Project had been ready
\par
to tackle the issues of impact on learning. The discussion concentrated on how the librarians
\par
themselves could improve the lessons by giving the pupils more targets to work towards, by
\par
giving more instruction and help with the information handling experiences. One group
\par
discussed how they might influence what teachers do but full collaboration was not
\par
mentioned and this was observed during the case studies to be a challenging area. The
\par
timetable does not permit enough development time to enable detailed collaboration and
\par
during focus group and case study discussions it was noted that the two professions appear to
\par
be working along parallel lines and not really fully engaging with each other's knowledge and
\par
expertise. During the librarians' meeting, the dicussion concentrated on how the librarian
\par
could improve the lessons by giving the pupils more targets, instruction and help with their
\par
121information handling experiences. The groups were then asked to consider how they might
\par
evaluate the changes they had made in the imaginary scenario and their ideas reflected some
\par
of the instruments used during the case study work, in particular the use of questionnaires
\par
both for the pupils' and the teachers' reactions to the course.
\par
The second activity was undertaken in order to see how easy or difficult the librarians found
\par
the process of using the framework of potential learning experiences and possible indicators
\par
(figure 2) to plan and evaluate reading for pleasure activities. The librarians were given a
\par
completed example of the framework for reading promotion and a blank framework (figures 4
\par
and 5) to complete for the planning and evaluation of their contribution to reading for
\par
pleasure using the impact framework (figure 2) as a starting point. The initial reaction was
\par
that the framework was daunting but by the end of the exercise most expressed the feeling
\par
that the learning experiences and indicators were helpful. One librarian expressed a dislike for
\par
using the blank grid as a format for organising ideas, but another felt the framework helped
\par
her articulate what she already does, and yet another indicated that she would like to try using
\par
the framework with a teacher in the near future. The discussion in one group suggested that
\par
the framework identified a great number of potential learning experiences for the reading
\par
activity and the challenge would be to keep the selection of target learning experiences within
\par
manageable proportions. One group expressed a reluctance to interfere with personal reading
\par
in any way that might make it too formal. This exercise reflected the different ways individual
\par
schools use the SLRC in the local context: for example, one English department was reported
\par
to use the SLRC for fiction selection and silent reading with little input from the librarian;
\par
another librarian reported that she only saw pupils on an individual basis for personal reading
\par
because the English department did not take the pupils to the SLRC; and in other schools the
\par
librarians were more actively involved with promotion of reading for pleasure during library
\par
periods. This was seen to have a bearing on how the librarians approached this exercise.
\par
The exercises provided useful feedback on the findings and framework. However, it is
\par
recognised that the framework will only provide one possible model for tackling the planning
\par
and evaluation of the SLRC on learning and this model will need to work well with the
\par
existing development planning process used in schools. This is an area for further research
\par
and there has also been interest in looking at the framework in relation to the primary sector.
\par
Positive feedback was received spontaneously from several librarians after the meeting and
\par
one immediately organised an evaluation meeting with the case study class and teacher in her
\par
school.
\par
David Streatfield, in his recently published work on quality assurance in the SLRC7, suggests
\par
that in order to be effective and manageable, indicators need to be kept to a minimum and this
\par
was borne in mind when revising the framework in the light of the case study investigations.
\par
The refinements made to the impact framework in the light of case study analysis and
\par
responses during the librarians' meeting have been brought together in figure 6. This
\par
framework provides a foundation upon which individual schools, teachers and librarians may
\par
begin to plan and evaluate their own SLRC related activities and services.
\par
The learning experiences defined in the two frameworks (figure 2 and 6) are similar but the
\par
terminology has been simplified and some of the areas of overlap have been clarified from the
\par
case study investigations. Not all the learning experiences were identified in great depth but
\par
the range and variety encountered did validate the range projected by the focus group
\par
participants. The skills required to achieve success in the progression theme need to be
\par
identified as specific learning outcomes or targets and appropriate indicators established in
\par
order to assess the progress of individual pupils or evaluate the success of the course in terms
\par
of learning achievements. The standards published by the American School Library
\par
Association8 give a useful starting point for breaking down the information handling process
\par
into specific skills and learning objectives. The 5-14 documentation also covers many of the
\par
skills required to handle information effectively. Areas where clear evidence or indicators
\par
122were not established tend to be the areas where the learning is more abstract or where the
\par
learning is expressed outside the SLRC. It does not however, imply that the SLRC does not
\par
have an impact on these learning experiences. It is anticipated that the refined framework will
\par
be adapted and developed still further (by the Research team or individual schools) to provide
\par
a working tool for schools, teachers and librarians to use as a starting point for closer
\par
examination of how the SLRC can impact on learning and how to look for evidence that the
\par
SLRC has had an impact.
\par
Analysis of the focus group discussions had earlier revealed a variety of conditioning factors
\par
identified by participants as influencing the impact of the SLRC on learning (see table 9).
\par
These were analysed and identified as factors that were outside the control of the individual
\par
members of teaching staff but having a bearing on the organisation of the SLRC service
\par
provision. During observation and analysis of the case studies other conditioning factors
\par
began to emerge and these are summarised below (table 16).
