\par
\textbf{7.6 VALUE OF MONITORING TECHNIQUES}
\par
One of the objectives of this Research Project was to examine how impact might be measured
\par
and ease with which this might be achieved in everyday practice. The methods used during
\par
the Project have been discussed in the individual case studies but some general points are
\par
worth summarising.
\par
123Observation provides a valuable tool to assess what is happening in the class situation but it is
\par
recognised as being impractical in situations where the librarian is occupied with a number of
\par
activities during the same session in the SLRC. However, it is worth taking a step back
\par
occasionally and looking at the situation objectively. In order to do this effectively it is
\par
important to have the aims and objectives for the course or activity clearly identified so that
\par
the observation and reflection is focused. In one of the case studies an observation schedule
\par
was used to give more accurate quantitative measurement and this required marking
\par
individual activities at timed intervals. This would also be impractical under normal
\par
circumstances but adapting this for use as a checklist to assess how individuals are coping
\par
with itemized tasks would be more easily administered.
\par
Discussion during the course of the activities in the SLRC was not only seen to stimulate
\par
interest, to encourage and help individuals overcome obstacles, but it is also an effective
\par
means of gauging understanding and progress. The remarks made by pupils when interest was
\par
shown in their work provide a valuable indication of their motivation as well as their
\par
understanding of the task they are engaged in. Listening to the discussion between individuals
\par
helps to gauge motivation and understanding and gives an idea of how able the pupils are at
\par
co-operating with one another, their understanding of other peoples' needs and how these
\par
skills are developing.
\par
Verbal questioning of pupils or giving them the chance to express their ideas and feelings in
\par
open-ended questions on the questionnaire or evaluation sheets proved a more effective way
\par
of obtaining evidence than the closed questions. The latter provided useful quantitative data
\par
but the responses were found to be contradictory on occasions and the younger pupils had
\par
difficulty understanding the questions, partly because of their own reading and
\par
comprehension levels and partly because of the abstract nature of many of the questions.
\par
Throughout the Project it was evident that pupils often do not think about or are not able to
\par
reflect on their learning or the processes they go through to achieve their aims. This inability
\par
to express the learning expectations and achievements was particularly evident when the
\par
Research team attempted to capture the impact of the SLRC on ad hoc learning. It was
\par
recognised that the impact of the SLRC within the learning situation is more to do with a need
\par
imposed upon the individual by the learning context or to do with a leisure activity than a
\par
conscious attempt by the individual to find information to aid decision-making. The impact
\par
research carried out by Marshall9 required the individual to identify a prospective need for
\par
information to enable decision-making or further action to take place. The information
\par
seeking contexts within the school environment that reflect a similar need to enable decision-
\par
making are the pupils' use of the career section and the teaching staff need for educational
\par
documentation and references. These may prove more fruitful areas for further
\par
experimentation with Marchall's approach to evaluating impact in the SLRC.
\par
In normal circumstances a pupil's finished work is not available for the librarian to examine
\par
and, although useful evidence was gained in this way, it was not thought to be essential as
\par
long as the teacher is aware of the need for feedback. It may prove useful to keep a record of
\par
the final marks or achievements of pupils if evidence was required over a long period to
\par
evaluate particular courses or the progress of individuals across a series of courses.
\par
Developments in the United States have begun to look at this more closely and the standards
\par
set out in Information Power10 provide a useful starting point for identifying the processes and
\par
skills required for information handling. The publication on assessment in the SLRC, edited
\par
by Kuhlthau11 gives interesting techniques for identifying pupil progress as well as providing
\par
the basic evidence to evaluate courses undertaken within the SLRC. Kuhlthau12 successfully
\par
used diaries and logs maintained by senior pupils to gather evidence on how they approached
\par
searching the Internet. This method was tried in this Impact Project but with less success. This
\par
is again thought to reflect the more abstract nature of the learning data sought from the pupil
\par
librarians where this approach was tried. The librarians themselves were encouraged to keep
\par
logs of events happening in the SLRC that reflected the learning experiences in the case study
\par
124units. While this was only taken up in case study 2, it did provide useful data and the
\par
librarians reported that the reflection process itself helped to focus their attention on the
\par
pupils' activities.
\par
The use of automated library management systems enables borrowing records and the loan
\par
history of individual resources to be examined and this was investigated in case study 6.
\par
There are data protection issues regarding the use of borrower records which need to be
\par
examined but the data provides useful information on links between resources and courses,
\par
between the use of resources in particular classes and how peer groupings influence
\par
borrowing, and the time and date of borrowing allows for a greater understanding of how the
\par
resources might have been used than the statistics alone. This information helps to
\par
substantiate evidence accumulated from the observational methods.
\par
It was not expected that any one simple easy way would be found to assess impact but the
\par
experience gives an insight into the practicalities of some of the possible methods. The case
\par
study work identified discussion, observation, and pupil evaluation sheets as useful methods
\par
for gathering evidence and there is also a need for more formal assessment of individual
\par
pupil's progress in the skills identified as being part of the learning experiences within the
\par
SLRC. Some evidence needs to be sought from sources outwith the SLRC environment and
\par
requires the collaboration of teaching staff and links with families.
