\textbf{Figure 5 Revised impact framework}130\textbf{8. CONCLUSION}
\par
\textbf{8.1 RESEARCH IN CONTEXT}
\par
This Research Project aimed to investigate the impact of the SLRC on learning by looking
\par
firstly at what the major stakeholders perceived to be the learning experiences taking place
\par
within this environment and secondly to examine whether this learning was indeed happening
\par
and how it might be monitored through a number of case studies. The Project was undertaken
\par
within the context of the current educational climate with increasing pressure to provide
\par
evidence of quality assurance, and at a time when there is interest amongst practitioners and
\par
research communities in evaluating and understanding the impact of information and library
\par
services.
\par
The recent government initiatives to raise educational standards, encourage social inclusion
\par
and introduce information technology throughout the curriculum, have led to a shift in
\par
emphasis towards the development of core skills in pupils who will be able to learn
\par
independently both in the school environment and beyond. The SLRC has been recognised as
\par
having a key role in supporting the ICT initiatives and the development of the skills required
\par
to extract and use information effectively. Alongside this, local authorities, including
\par
educational establishments and public libraries, are required to demonstrate public
\par
accountability in terms of service to clients and value for money. The increased expenditure
\par
on technology as well as the traditional hard copy resources within the SLRC has prompted a
\par
need for quality assurance in the SLRC as well as in the school as a whole. The school library
\par
profession has embraced this and has sought to integrate its approach with that required of
\par
schools as a whole. However, the inclusion of the "quality of pupils' learning" amongst the
\par
performance indicators established for the SLRC1 revealed an area that librarians found
\par
difficult to tackle. Traditional evaluation of a library service has looked at outputs in terms of
\par
statistical information relating to expenditure, resources and use but has until recently not
\par
tackled the evaluation of impact in terms of service outcomes. Teachers understand the need
\par
to encourage use of the SLRC and its resources both through the curriculum and to develop
\par
independence beyond the classroom but do not necessarily place sufficient emphasis on how
\par
the SLRC can contribute most effectively to pupil learning.
\par
This study compliments to some extent other recent studies in the USA where the provision of
\par
a SLRC has been linked to academic achievement2. Within the Library Power programme3
\par
improvements in the quality of provision of service have provided greater opportunities for
\par
pupils to develop information handling skills through an integrated approach to enquiry. The
\par
need to understand the SLRC's contribution to effective learning was the context in which this
\par
study began.
\par
The challenges of measuring impact were recognised from other studies looking at the impact
\par
of information in other environmental4, business and professional 5 contexts and the need to
\par
identify reliable, valid indicators of impact and the means of monitoring them within the
\par
particular environment. Therefore, the indicators used in this study had to reflect the stated
\par
curricular goals and the approach taken by individual schools to achieving those goals. The
\par
learning activities within the SLRC could not and should not be viewed in isolation. The pupil
\par
or staff users of the SLRC are similar to other information users in their need for information
\par
and the resultant decision-making process will depend on previous accumulated experience
\par
and knowledge and the timescale for impact will vary according to the individual and the
\par
specific circumstances. However, the complexity of the learning environment must also take
\par
into account, the maturity of the users, the involvement of the teacher as intermediary and the
\par
need to weigh up competing priorities. Previous impact studies have relied on the user being
\par
131able to articulate their information need and being able to evaluate the implications their
\par
information use has on their future course of action. The immaturity of some of the
\par
information users and the involvement of the teaching staff and librarians as intermediaries in
\par
the school context posed significant new challenges when trying to assess the impact of the
\par
SLRC on the learning of the users.
\par
The aim was not to compare the relative impact in different schools but to examine a range of
\par
learning situations within SLRCs where attempts had been made to provide a good standard
\par
of service as specified in recent standards published by CoSLA6. By focusing on a range of
\par
learning contexts the emphasis was placed on the impact of the SLRC as a whole within those
\par
contexts rather than the impact of specific information or resources. Traditional impact
\par
studies in other ILS sectors have taken a critical incident approach to examine how
\par
information helped with decision-making and progress along a planned course of action.
\par
Although the use of information in the school context can have such applications there are
\par
other more complex issues associated with the impact on learning that meant the use of
\par
traditional impact methods was more challenging and less easily implemented.
\par
The methodology involved the gathering of data from the learner, the teacher and the librarian
\par
using collection techniques such as self-reporting, observation and examination of recorded
\par
work executed by the learner and this ensured a triangular approach. There was no attempt to
\par
try and isolate the impact on learning from the multitude of other influencing factors but to
\par
monitor the learning experiences within SLRC related activities over a relatively short period
\par
of time. The process of monitoring impact was considered as important to the Research as
\par
finding actual evidence of impact on learning and the findings from this part of the Research
\par
may provide a useful starting point for practitioners to examine their own situation over a
\par
longer period of time.
\par
\textbf{8.2 LEARNING IN THE SLRC}
\par
The Research examined a relatively small range of specific activities conducted in the SLRC
\par
but these examples highlighted the potential for impact on a very wide range of learning
\par
experiences. The range of learning experiences identified were classified according to various
\par
educational theories and the resultant framework (figure 2) placed the learning experiences
\par
into learning themes which accorded well with the breadth of learning described in other
\par
learning classifications, for example Bloom7, the Scottish curriculum documentation8 and
\par
``Taking a Closer Look at the School Library Resource Centre''9. Thus the range of learning
\par
experiences upon which the SLRC can have an impact includes all the areas of learning
\par
expressed in current educational documents as forming part of the curriculum goals within
\par
schools, i.e. motivation, progression, independence and interaction.
\par
The Research highlighted how the SLRC can contribute to the development of a variety of
\par
skills associated with the handling of information and how pupils can demonstrate a
\par
knowledge and understanding of these skills when intervention is appropriately applied. It has
\par
also shown that contributions can be made to cross-curricular reinforcement of core skills and
\par
can encourage the "disposition"10 of pupils towards social and individual responsibility. The
\par
SLRC provides those who are equipped with the skills to progress to independent learning
\par
with the opportunities to increase their knowledge, understanding and help to fulfil personal
\par
achievements. Use of the SLRC alone does not motivate pupils to learn, they need to
\par
understand what is required of them and have the skills or an understanding of the skills in
\par
order to proceed with the task set. Pupils who have mastered some of the processes are able to
\par
take advantage of the opportunity to work independently (although many appear to do so in
\par
their own time). Those pupils who are not confident in the information handling skills need
\par
additional support to make their use of the SLRC more effective. The case study investigation
\par
provides more evidence to support the work already undertaken11 in understanding the
\par
132processes involved in the handling of information and the skills required to tackle enquiry
\par
related activities. The library, information handling, ICT, reporting, study and critical skills
\par
are all interrelated and more development work needs to be done within individual schools to
\par
identify the most appropriate way to tackle the introduction and reinforcement of these skills
\par
within the local context. This may then enable more effective learning to be achieved within
\par
the SLRC by all pupils. The relative ease with which pupils were able to locate appropriate
\par
sources of information but the difficulty they appeared to have using the information would
\par
suggest that a greater emphasis needs to be placed on the latter than the former activity. The
\par
schools where the senior management was seen to be actively involved in the development
\par
and integration of resource-based learning into the whole school, appeared to have a greater
\par
understanding of the issues involved and were in a better position to engage in the practical
\par
implementation of reviews and revision of practice.
\par
The impact of the SLRC does not need to be confined to the areas of learning progression (i.e.
\par
information handling skills, increased knowledge and understanding and improved quality of
\par
work) unless school policy and local circumstances indicate this to be the most appropriate
\par
course of action. It may be appropriate for the main emphasis to be on progression skills and
\par
knowledge but the SLRC can contribute, alongside other departments, to the learning in its
\par
wider sense of motivation, independence and interaction. The Research has contributed to an
\par
understanding of the relationship between the SLRC and learning and opens the way for
\par
greater reflection by schools, teachers and librarians on what the learning potential is and how
\par
this potential might be maximised. The examples of impact give an indication of the breadth
\par
of the learning experiences and the framework (figure 5), along with a knowledge of the
\par
influencing factors provides one way for practitioners to begin the process of evaluating what
\par
is being achieved in the SLRC and planning for further developments. Factors which enhance
\par
or detract from the impact will always be present but need to be borne in mind when planning
\par
and evaluating courses, thus enabling the most appropriate activities to be organised for the
\par
specific requirements in relation to the overall learning potential. Therefore, the SLRC
\par
contribution to enhancing the quality of learning must not be evaluated in isolation from the
\par
rest of the school and planning must be a joint process to make educational sense.
\par
\textbf{8.3 MONITORING AND EVALUATING IMPACT ON LEARNING}
\par
Some elements of learning are easier to monitor than others. It was found to be easier to
\par
examine the immediate learning context, i.e. the work done within the SLRC and its relation
\par
to the curriculum goal set by the teacher. The indicators can be very specific and lend
\par
themselves to tracking techniques which are relatively easily managed and relevant to the
\par
main focus of activity. However, the Research looked at connections beyond the immediate
\par
SLRC learning environment and saw evidence of the transfer or development of skills and
\par
learning to other areas of the curriculum and other aspects of learning not directly related to
\par
the immediate goal. It is important for librarians and teachers to at least be aware of these
\par
other impacts on learning, which may be happening. Whether planned for or just taken into
\par
account as part of the teaching and learning process, these factors contribute to a greater
\par
understanding of the nature of the end product of pupils' work. A greater understanding and
\par
awareness of these instances could also help develop a more integrated learning environment,
\par
particularly for some of the core skills, which are so prominent in current educational
\par
thinking.
\par
The indicators to be used as evidence of learning should be context specific and therefore
\par
need to be examined at a local level, but some general points have been made on the type of
\par
indicators which are easier and more difficult to track (see Section 8.2.3) and use of the
\par
framework may help with this process. It would not be possible or desirable to evaluate
\par
everything and the local context is likely to restrict evaluation to a particular learning theme,
\par
to specific programmes or in some form of rotation. However this is tackled, collaboration
\par
133between the teacher and librarian is necessary to provide an understanding of the common
\par
goals and how the integration of the two professions can enhance learning.
\par
Although the two are interrelated, there may be a distinction between the aims of a project or
\par
course and the aims for using the SLRC and the impact this might have on learning. The
\par
study provides evidence that the latter are not necessarily fully explored by teachers and
\par
librarians. Teachers are using the SLRC for a specific curriculum goal and this is being
\par
assessed formally but the skills that are needed to complete that specific task are not being
\par
consistently analysed and reinforced and therefore the support is not necessarily forthcoming
\par
at the most appropriate times. Similarly, some of the indirect and less immediate learning
\par
experiences are not given due consideration and in many cases these have a direct bearing on
\par
the cross-curricular, core skills emphasised in recent educational documents.
\par
As indicated in the Government's response to the ``Empowering the Learning Community''12
\par
there is a need for cross-disciplinary training to ``allow people from different professions to
\par
learn alongside one another and to exchange skills.''
\par
\textbf{8.4 PLANNING FOR EFFECTIVE LEARNING THROUGH THE SLRC}
\par
Teachers and librarians do not yet have a common vision or language to describe the way the
\par
SLRC interacts with the classroom. The SLRC is not an end in itself but can be seen as a step
\par
towards an end, with the start and conclusion taking place outside the SLRC. The joint
\par
planning which exists is limited and the "outcome" of the activity tends to be measured in
\par
terms which relate to the end product but the skills or processes which contribute to the end
\par
product are not assessed or evaluated. Part of this may relate to a sense of detachment which
\par
teachers feel towards the SLRC, they do not have or expect to have control over the way the
\par
SLRC operates and they may not plan the activity accordingly. Librarians on the other hand
\par
may assume that more has been planned than is in fact the case. They may not have a clear
\par
understanding of what the teacher expects, assuming the teacher does have specific
\par
expectations, and therefore are not in a position to give support in the most appropriate way.
\par
The framework may provide the means for teachers and librarians to begin the planning and
\par
evaluation process. Some form of course evaluation provides valuable information on what
\par
has been learned about the processes as well as subject matter, it provides useful information
\par
on where difficulties lie for further development or concentration of effort. The need for
\par
intervention at appropriate time helps to avoid disillusionment and frustration, and early
\par
intervention increases the chances of developing independent learners at a later stage.
\par
In order to ensure the programmes of study provide effective learning experiences the
\par
activities and/or pupils need some form of evaluation or assessment to provide evidence of
\par
impact on learning. This need not be confined to the skills developed within curriculum based
\par
activities. In discussing Higher Still core skills, the head teacher of one case study school saw
\par
the potential for the SLRC to contribute in areas outwith the planned curriculum. For
\par
example, he felt there would be value in some form of accreditation of pupil librarians,
\par
itemising the duties and matching these to individual abilities to help guidance staff compile
\par
the personal records of pupils. Clearly with potential for impact in different contexts and
\par
settings there is a need for both teaching staff and librarians to be involved in evaluating that
\par
impact. There are areas of learning that each group is in a better position to assess and the
\par
accumulated evidence is important. However, decisions about the evaluation priorities and
\par
where the responsibilities for evaluation rest may need to be made locally depending on the
\par
particular approaches individual schools take to teaching and learning and ethos.
\par
The SLRC does impact on learning and on learning in its broadest sense. The learning can be
\par
monitored provided the teacher and librarian understand what they are looking for and what
\par
they are hoping to achieve by monitoring the learning. There is no need to evaluate
\par
134everything at once but unless there is some understanding of the kind of learning experiences
\par
being fostered by the SLRC activity, whether these are planned or not, then there can be no
\par
real attempt to plan for more effective use of the SLRC. A great deal of learning is happening
\par
that is not planned for and it is up to the stakeholders in the local environment to decide
\par
which aspects are worth planning for, what their priorities should be and what and how
\par
evaluation should take place. This should be done at various levels to be integrated into the
\par
school development plan, departmental planning and the planning and evaluation of
\par
individual courses and activities based within the SLRC.
\par
The findings of this research are relevant to all members of the teaching and library
\par
professions who are interested in the use of the SLRC for effective learning and teaching,
\par
from policy-makers, through senior management to individual teachers and librarians working
\par
directly with students in schools.
\par
Clearer guidelines could be developed on how to integrate the use of the SLRC into
\par
curriculum practice, in particular with regard to the current educational emphasis on key skills
\par
in England and Wales and core skills in Scotland. Senior management need to take a more
\par
active role to ensure greater collaboration between departments (including the SLRC) in order
\par
to encourage greater understanding of the potential learning, progression of skills and
\par
activities, and consistency of terminology and aims. Teachers and librarians need to develop a
\par
common language and understanding of their respective expertise and the roles each
\par
profession can play to provide effective support for pupils who use the SLRC. Teacher
\par
training institutions and information and library schools could incorporate a greater awareness
\par
of the potential learning associated with the SLRC into courses. Education departments and
\par
library services could take up the opportunity to disseminate current research into joint and
\par
profession specific staff development programmes, as recognised by the Government
\par
``Training of librarians, resource managers and teachers should be co-ordinated and should
\par
include ways of developing mutual support''13
\par
.
\par
There is also potential for this research to provide the foundation for further studies into the
\par
impact of wider educational and recreational facilities on the learning of users, as suggested in
\par
the Learning and Access Standard consultation document14
\par
.
\par
In conclusion, the study found evidence of impact across a broad spectrum of learning areas
\par
but found that the impact was not always directly related to the intended aims for SLRC use.
\par
Some form of evaluation needs to be put in place to ensure the learning and teaching is
\par
effective in the areas of recognised and stated priority and that the roles and process by which
\par
evaluation is carried out needs to be established. The Research offers a framework for
\par
evaluation of impact not only in the SLRC but potentially useful in any learning context. In
\par
addition, because the study has been firmly embedded within the context of \emph{learning,} the
\par
findings may provide some basis for reflection on the impact of information services, in the
\par
broadest sense, on education and lifelong learning.
