\par
\textbf{The impact of the school library resource centre on learning}
\par
The School Library Resource Centre (SLRC) is actively involved in pupil's learning, not only by
\par
developing library and information skills but also by supporting the curriculum and by encouraging
\par
independent decision-making and critical analysis. The current focus on developing transferable skills
\par
as the basis for lifelong learning suggests that SLRCs should be very much at the heart of school-
\par
based initiatives promoting this integrated approach to learning.
\par
The SLA, COSLA and SCCC have all recently been involved in developing appropriate standards and
\par
methods of evaluating the performance of school libraries. However, the relationship between the
\par
performance of the SLRC and the outcomes in terms of learning remains unclear and there is interest
\par
in establishing real examples of the impact of the SLRC on learning. The key to the adoption of
\par
standards and the integration of school libraries within the planning and decision-making process in
\par
schools lies in a shared understanding of the value and impact of the school library on learning.
\par
Researchers at RGU are beginning a project, complementing the work of the SLA, COSLA and
\par
SCCC, which aims to assess the impact of a good school library resource centre on learning. The
\par
challenge of this research is to find meaningful ways to express impact.
\par
The project team will follow self-evaluation practice, grounding the research in the local context of the
\par
individual school and its community. This research will, therefore, invite schools themselves to
\par
participate in discussions about the aspects of learning that they feel are appropriate as indicators of
\par
impact.
\par
The project will establish `impact frameworks' of learning benefits based on the way pupils, teachers
\par
(including senior management), and librarians value their SLRC, a discreet and valuable exercise.
\par
These frameworks will help to identify indicators of impact on learning, which schools will then be
\par
able to use as the basis of local self-assessment of impact under the guidance and assistance of the
\par
project team. Approximately six secondary schools will be selected from across Scotland.
\par
Dr Dorothy Williams leads the project working in partnership with the Scottish Library Association.
\par
Dr Williams is actively involved in research in education and information; in particular, information
\par
skills development, information management, and the impact of information on decision making in
\par
education. Mrs Caroline Wavell is Research Assistant for the project. The project, which is funded by
\par
the Library and Information Commission, started in September 1999 and is due to run for eighteen
\par
months.
\par
