\textbf{IMPACT OF A SCHOOL LIBRARY RESOURCE CENTRE}
\par
\textbf{ON LEARNING}
\par
We would like to thank you for agreeing to take part in the teachers' focus group to support the above
\par
research.
\par
The School Library Resource Centre (SLRC) is actively involved in pupil's learning, not only by
\par
developing library and information skills but also by supporting the curriculum and by encouraging
\par
independent decision-making and critical analysis. The current focus on developing transferable skills
\par
as the basis for lifelong learning suggests that SLRCs should be at the heart of school-based initiatives
\par
promoting this integrated approach to learning.
\par
\textbf{Aims and objectives of the research}
\par
{$\bullet$} to assess the impact of the School Library Resource Centre (SLRC) on LEARNING by:
\par
{$\bullet$} assessing the outcomes of using the SLRC as perceived by teachers, librarians and pupils
\par
{$\bullet$} identifying learning outcomes to be used as indicators of impacts
\par
{$\bullet$} and evaluating the impact on specific aspects of learning.
\par
\textbf{Methodology}
\par
{$\bullet$} Secondary schools from across mainland Scotland were invited to complete a simple questionnaire
\par
to indicate their willingness to participate in this project. Ten schools have been selected to take
\par
part in the first phase of the study.
\par
{$\bullet$} The aim of the first phase is to identify what teachers, librarians and pupils perceive to be the
\par
impact of the library on learning. This involves an interview with the school librarian(s) in the
\par
SLRC, and a focus group discussion with a small number of teachers and pupils.
\par
{$\bullet$} The aim of the second phase is to evaluate the impact of the school library on specific aspects of
\par
learning. The frameworks established from the focus groups will help to identify indicators of
\par
impact on learning to be used as the basis of self-assessment under the guidance and assistance of
\par
the project team.
\par
\textbf{Focus Groups}
\par
{$\bullet$} The focus groups will be held during December 1999, January and February 2000. A small group
\par
of teachers from across the curriculum areas and representing teachers, senior management and
\par
support staff will be invited to consider their use of the SLRC and to reflect on the direct, indirect,
\par
immediate and delayed outcomes of using the library. A similar exercise will be carried out with
\par
the senior pupils and each group will be analysed separately.
\par
{$\bullet$} \textbf{It will be of great help to us and to yourselves if, before the focus group, you would reflect on}
\par
\textbf{these issues and note down any thoughts you have on the attached sheet.} A discussion of the
\par
learning outcomes will form the basis of the focus group. \textbf{Please bring the completed sheets with}
\par
\textbf{you.}
\par
Your help is very much appreciated and it is hoped that your participation will prove to be rewarding
\par
for your own and the pupils' use of the SLRC. It will certainly provide the project team with valuable
\par
information to identify frameworks of perceived learning outcomes. The finished research will help
\par
librarians and teachers consider how to evaluate the use of the SLRC in terms of learning.
\par
Dr. Dorothy Williams, Project Leader and Mrs Caroline Wavell, Research Assistant\textbf{Appendix 3}
