\textbf{EXTRACT FROM OBSERVATION NOTES}
\par
\textbf{CASE STUDY 2}
\par
\textbf{Day 2 Session 1 researching in the SLRC}
\par
Librarian reminded the pupils that they were to work together and to have good manners.
\par
\textbf{Group 1 - researching mountains, rivers, regions.}
\par
Confusion over their roles, one member of the group was absent. Girl (A) seemed to know
\par
what she was doing, found information quickly. Boy (B) working on the Internet looking up
\par
regions, found a map of France, then librarian helped find the regions and reminded him to go
\par
through the information.
\par
Librarian reminded all pupils to put the information into their own words. The instruction
\par
sheet was felt to be too broad in its scope because the pupils found it difficult to focus on
\par
what was required. There was little evidence that the pupils referred to the instruction sheet
\par
much.
\par
\textbf{Group 9 - researching sporting personalities}
\par
The group appeared unsure of what they were supposed to be doing.
\par
Boy (D) worked on Corbertin and the Olympics from a book.
\par
Girl (E) knew what she was doing on the computer, she grasped the idea of selecting text for
\par
printing but not sure about printing a photo, after help from the librarian understood
\par
immediately. Boy (F) was watching (E) on the computer, less capable but demonstrated an
\par
understanding of the process when questioned.
\par
(D) took a long time reading and noting information, unsure of how much information was
\par
needed, writing the information in sentences. He was disappointed not to be on the computer.
\par
(F) not sure what he was supposed to be doing and was not working collaboratively.
\par
At the end of break, one of the group rushed back to collect information and thanked the
\par
librarian for her help.
\par
\textbf{Session 2 producing a worksheet with the information \& preparing an oral presentation}
\par
Librarian checked the pupils had gathered all the information and explained that they needed
\par
to get the main points on an A4 sheet of paper of submission to the teacher and that they
\par
should consider who they would present the information. The librarian did not feel this was
\par
her role. The teacher visits the SLRC to see how things are going, she mentions keywords,
\par
mind maps and spider diagrams. These are not topics covered by the librarians.
\par
\textbf{Group 1}
\par
One girl (C) was away last week when the research began.
\par
All three were looking at the information found last week, attempting to show the girl who
\par
was off what they had found.
\par
They got the instructions out and discussed what had been found but were unsure how to
\par
proceed. It seemed clearer when researcher suggested they needed to find the main points
\par
from the instruction sheet for the presentation.
\par
The boy (B) was proud of his contribution, text printed from the Internet, and wanted to go
\par
back on the computer. They looked at this and the girl (A) decided it was not relevant and
\par
thought they needed to use the books again. (A) appeared to know what was needed but failed
\par
to find enough information on the Alps. The suggestion of using an atlas to locate the
\par
mountains, rivers and regions was not taken up. (A) continued to work on her own during
\par
break and without consulting the other two.
\par
They asked about Napoleon, another group's topic.
\par
(C) never really got into the subject.
\par
\textbf{Group 9}
\par
(E) getting on with writing information.
\par
They appeared to be organised and working as a team.
\par
The boys had already written their pieces on the worksheet and were waiting for the girl so
\par
they could sign the sheet. [They had not thought of doing anything else while waiting, e.g.\textbf{Appendix 19}
\par
starting the poster.]
\par
(F) was not contributing as much as the other two.
\par
\textbf{Day 3 Session 3 Preparing an oral presentation}
\par
Librarian checked the pupils had gathered all the information needed and explained.
\par
\textbf{Group 1}
\par
This group had achieved the goal although the teacher commented that they had not
\par
mentioned the Alps or the river Seine. By the end of this period they were still not organised
\par
for their talk. (A) was reluctant to use any other resource than the encyclopaedia.
\par
\textbf{Group 9}
\par
The group did not achieve the goal because they missed information on the Tour de France.
\par
They said they had the information but failed to include it on the worksheet. They felt they
\par
were working better as a group than at the start and they had decided to swop around and talk
\par
about different information than they had found. They were the most organised group and
\par
started practising their talk earliest. The girl said she enjoyed finding new information and
\par
one of the boys enjoyed going on the Internet best.
\par
\textbf{Session 4 Giving a talk}
\par
The audience did not appear to be listening except those doing the evaluation. It was difficult
\par
to understand because the information was not organised and the presentation was poor in
\par
some cases. The evaluation sheets were not shown to the appropriate group although they did
\par
appear to understand what was written. One member was then elected to stay in the SLRC to
\par
work with others from the cluster group to prepare the information for another presentation to
\par
the class. The member chosen was not always the most competent.
\par
\textbf{Group 1}
\par
(C) did the introduction to the talk, (B) read from the sheet about the rivers and (A) read about
\par
the mountains. The information was not clearly delivered but they did cover the main points.
\par
\textbf{Group 9}
\par
They were organised and had better information. Both the boys read information and were
\par
less clear in their presentation. They did not look at the audience.
\par
They evaluated group 8, the girl (E) knew what she was doing and involved the others.\textbf{Appendix 20}
