@article{woerdmanProjectbasedEmissionsTrading2001,
  title = {Project-Based Emissions Trading: The Impact of Institutional Arrangements on Cost-Effectiveness},
  shorttitle = {Project-Based Emissions Trading},
  author = {Woerdman, E. and Van Der Gaast, W.},
  year = 2001,
  journal = {Mitigation and Adaptation Strategies for Global Change},
  volume = {6},
  number = {2},
  pages = {NaN-NaN},
  doi = {10.1023/A:1011330609725},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Export Date: 05 May 2024; Cited By: 13
\par
\textbf{PROJECT-BASED EMISSIONS TRADING: THE IMPACT OF}
\par
\textbf{INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS}
\par
EDWIN WOERDMAN1 and WYTZE VAN DER GAAST2
\par
1\emph{Department of Economics and Public Finance (ECOF), Faculty of Law, University of Groningen,}
\par
\emph{PO Box 716, 9700 AS Groningen, The Netherlands}
\par
2\emph{Joint Implementation Network (Foundation JIN), Meerkoetlaan 30A, 9765 TD Paterswolde, The}
\par
\emph{Netherlands}
\par
(Received 5 October 1999; accepted in final form 19 February 2001)
\par
\textbf{Abstract. }In this paper we demonstrate that the institutional arrangement (or: design) of Joint
\par
Implementation (JI) and the Clean Development Mechanism (CDM) has a decisive impact on their
\par
cost-effectiveness. We illustrate our arguments by statistically analyzing the costs from 94 Activities
\par
Implemented Jointly (AIJ) pilot phase projects as well as by adjusting these data on the basis of
\par
simple mathematical formulas. These calculations explicitly take into account the institutional differ-
\par
ences between JI (sinks, no banking) and the CDM (banking, no sinks) under the Kyoto Protocol and
\par
also show the possible effects on credit costs of alternative design options. However, our numerical
\par
illustrations should be viewed with caution, because AIJ is only to a limited extent representative
\par
of potential future JI and CDM projects and because credit costs are not credit prices. Some of the
\par
main figures found in this study are: an average cost figure per unit of emission reduction for AIJ
\par
projects of 46 dollar per ton of carbon dioxide equivalent (\$/Mg CO2-eq)1, an average potential JI
\par
credit cost figure which is lowered to 37 \$/Mg CO2-eq by introducing banking and an average of
\par
6 \$/Mg CO 2-eq per credit for potential low-cost CDM projects which includes sinks. However, at
\par
CoP6 in November 2000 in The Hague (The Netherlands), the Parties to the Framework Convention
\par
on Climate Change (FCCC) did not (yet) reach consensus on the institutional details of the project-
\par
based mechanisms, such as the possible arrangement of early JI action or the inclusion of sinks under
\par
the CDM.
\par
\textbf{Keywords: }Activities Implemented Jointly (AIJ), Clean Development Mechanism (CDM), credit
\par
prices, design, emission reduction costs, institutions, Joint Implementation (JI)
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
In the Kyoto Protocol of 1997 the Parties to the U.N. Framework Convention on
\par
Climate Change (FCCC) agreed on legally-binding greenhouse gas (GHG) emis-
\par
sion targets for industrialized countries as listed in Annex B of the Protocol. These
\par
so-called Annex B Parties shall individually or jointly reduce their overall GHG
\par
emission level by at least five percent below 1990 levels in the first commitment
\par
period 2008 to 2012 (Article 3.1). In order to achieve this overall target the indus-
\par
trialized countries have been assigned with an amount of GHG units that they are
\par
allowed to emit during the first commitment period of the Kyoto Protocol (2008--
\par
2012). These assigned amounts differ for most of the countries. The European
\par
\emph{Mitigation and Adaptation Strategies for Global Change }\textbf{6: }113--154, 2001.
\par
\copyright{} 2001 \emph{Kluwer Academic Publishers. Printed in the Netherlands.}114 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
Union (EU) as a whole, for example, agreed on an assigned amount of emissions
\par
that is eight percent lower than the level of 1990. At the meeting of its Council of
\par
Environment Ministers of 16 June 1998 the EU translated its common commitment
\par
under the Kyoto Protocol to individual targets for each Member State (referred to
\par
as `burden sharing'), such as 21\% reduction for Germany, stabilization for France
\par
and 27\% allowable emission growth for Portugal. The United States (US) adopted a
\par
GHG emission reduction commitment of seven percent, whereas the Russian Fed-
\par
eration's assigned amount is determined at the emissions level of 1990, implying a
\par
stabilization of emissions rather than a reduction.
\par
The Kyoto Protocol allows Annex B Parties to meet their commitments partly
\par
by achieving emission reductions abroad in order to improve the cost-effectiveness
\par
of climate change policy. Several studies show that the marginal costs of reducing
\par
GHG emissions vary substantially among the Parties (e.g. Richels et al. 1996,
\par
Hourcade et al. 1996). Such a variation of marginal costs enables countries to
\par
reduce the overall costs of GHG abatement by working together on abatement
\par
activities. To do so, a country can acquire units of assigned amounts or GHG
\par
emission reductions (that can be transfered in such units if they are considered
\par
to be fungible) from another country by implementing one or more of the so-called
\par
Kyoto Mechanisms:
\par
{$\bullet$} Joint Implementation (JI) under Protocol Article 6;
\par
{$\bullet$} Clean Development Mechanism (CDM) under Protocol Article 12;
\par
{$\bullet$} International Emissions Trading (IET) under Protocol Article 17.
\par
Through JI, Annex B Parties may acquire or transfer emission reduction units
\par
(ERU) resulting from jointly implemented GHG abatement projects, for instance if
\par
an industrialized country in Northern America or Western Europe implements an
\par
emission reduction project in and in cooperation with an Eastern European coun-
\par
try, where the marginal costs of such abatement are lower. As defined under the
\par
Kyoto Protocol, JI envisages a cooperation among Annex B Parties only. The CDM
\par
defines a form of GHG emission reduction project cooperation between industrial-
\par
ized countries and developing countries in order to assist developing countries in
\par
achieving sustainable development and to enable industrialized countries to acquire
\par
so-called certified emission reductions (CER).
\par
IET is a top-down approach according to which the emission reduction is meas-
\par
ured from the national commitment under Annex B of the Kyoto Protocol. Ac-
\par
cording to Article 17 of the Protocol, countries could trade parts of their assigned
\par
amounts with other Annex B countries. Although each Annex B Party could estab-
\par
lish a domestic trading system by dividing its assigned amount via the allocation of
\par
`permits' to domestic individual companies or sectors, it is still a topic for discus-
\par
sion whether and how these domestic private parties can and should be able to trade
\par
directly with foreign private parties. Article 17 of the Protocol refers to industrial-
\par
ized countries which could trade parts of their assigned amounts with other Annex
\par
B countries. This might suggest that this legal text is limited to intergovernmental
\par
emissions trading, while others think that permit trading is somehow implicitlyTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 115
\par
included. Nevertheless, the principles, modalities, rules and guidelines for IET
\par
have not yet been elaborated under Article 17. Hence, it is still to be decided to
\par
what extent the private sector in countries can become involved in IET. A potential
\par
political complication of implementing an (inter)national permit trading system is
\par
the inevitable (re)distribution at the \emph{macro-level }of property or user rights for emis-
\par
sions. Firms will lobby extensively for their most favourable assignment, partly
\par
because the allocation of permits could change, or even distort, their competitive
\par
relations (Woerdman 2000a). According to Woerdman (2000b), this is one of the
\par
reasons why JI and CDM, of which the implementation rather requires \emph{micro-level}
\par
negotiations between the project partners, is likely to be politically more acceptable
\par
-- at least in the short term -- than permit trading.
\par
In this paper we focus on JI and the CDM as project-based types of cooperation
\par
between countries. The emission reductions achieved through such projects are
\par
measured bottom-up as the difference between the estimated future emissions at
\par
the project location in absence of the investment under the project (the baseline)
\par
and the project's actual GHG emissions (Van der Gaast and Woerdman 1997). In
\par
spite of their similarity as project-based types of cooperation, JI and CDM differ on
\par
at least four points. These differences may give a comparative advantage to either
\par
JI or CDM, depending on the relative weight for each of the differences.
\par
Firstly, the current text of Article 6 implies that for JI only GHG emission
\par
reductions achieved between the commitment period 2008--2012 can be transfered
\par
between Parties for the fulfillment of Annex B Parties' commitments. Contrary
\par
to JI, Article 12 indicates that also emission reductions which accrue from CDM
\par
projects between 2000 and 2008 can be banked in order to use them for the com-
\par
mitment period (Article 12.10).
\par
Secondly, in contrast to Article 6, a specific text on sink enhancement projects
\par
has not been included for CDM projects in Article 12. There is a debate at the
\par
level of the Conference of the Parties (CoP) about whether this implies that sink
\par
enhancement in Article 12 is explicitly excluded or still implicitly included via
\par
Article 3 as a possible CDM project type. Article 3.3 states, among other things,
\par
that removals by sinks resulting from human-induced land use change and forestry
\par
activities (limited to afforestation, reforestation and deforestation since 1990) shall
\par
be used to meet the Parties' commitments.
\par
Thirdly, the requirements under the CDM in terms of supporting sustainable
\par
development in the host countries are much stronger than under JI. Under Article
\par
12 the establishment of a supervising Executive Board supported by so-called op-
\par
erational entities is defined. It is yet unclear what the role of these institutions will
\par
be and to what extent the passage on sustainable development will make CDM
\par
project development more complicated than setting up JI projects.
\par
Fourthly, baseline determination could (or should) be easier for JI projects than
\par
for CDM projects. Contrary to developing countries, the host country of a JI project
\par
has commited itself to an assigned amount, which implies that the government has
\par
to define environmental policy targets for its domestic emitters. If it has done so,116 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
the JI baseline to calculate the additional emission reductions would be the defined
\par
environmental policy for the host firm or sector involved. Nevertheless, project-
\par
level baseline determination still remains necessary for JI projects, since baseline
\par
inflation, while not in the interest of both Parties under the double bookkeeping of
\par
assigned amounts, is still in the interest of (private) investors.
\par
With respect to the project-based approach (JI and CDM), experience has been
\par
gained in the framework of the pilot phase for JI called Activities Implemented
\par
Jointly (AIJ), which started in 1995 on the basis of decision 5/CP.1 by the first
\par
Conference of Parties (CoP1) and which is scheduled to be completed shortly after
\par
2000. As part of the AIJ pilot phase, it was decided that Parties could not use
\par
the GHG emission reductions achieved through AIJ projects for the fulfilment of
\par
commitments under the FCCC: the AIJ emission reductions could not be `cred-
\par
ited'. At CoP4 in 1998, the Parties drew up the Buenos Aires Plan of Action
\par
(BAPA). Decision 7/CP.4 of this work programme calls for a further elaboration of
\par
the Kyoto Mechanisms at CoP6 in November 2000. Additionally, decision 6/CP.4
\par
underlined the necessity to review the AIJ pilot phase. The latter was done at
\par
CoP5 in 1999, were it was decided to continue the AIJ pilot phase during the first
\par
years after 1999 on the basis of decision 13/CP.5. At CoP6 in The Hague (The
\par
Netherlands) the FCCC Parties were not (yet) able to reach consensus when they
\par
tried to work out the institutional details of the Kyoto Mechanisms, but a second
\par
attempt will be made in July 2001 in Bonn (Germany) before CoP7 will take place
\par
in October/November 2001 in Marrakech (Morocco).
\par
The cost-effectiveness of (project-based) flexible instruments critically depends
\par
on past and future decisions of policy-makers concerning their design and institu-
\par
tional context (e.g. Woerdman 2000b; Tietenberg 1999; Zhang and Nentjes 1999).
\par
For instance, explicitly including sinks in CDM Article 12, which has -- so far --
\par
been left out because of the associated environmental uncertainties, would make
\par
such developing country projects more cost-effective, notably because forestry is
\par
known to be a relatively cheap sequestration option (e.g. Trexler and Kosloff 1998).
\par
Numerous other examples can be provided. For instance, a quantitative ceiling on
\par
the use of the Kyoto Mechanisms, as proposed by the EU prior to CoP6, would (on
\par
average) lower their cost-effectiveness. Or, baseline standardization would lower
\par
the transaction costs of CDM projects, whereas crediting early JI action is likely
\par
to raise the cost-effectiveness of potential Eastern European emission reduction
\par
projects. Such examples clearly indicate that the institutional arrangement of a
\par
(project-based) flexible instrument is crucial for its cost-effectiveness, which is the
\par
topic of this paper.
\par
\textbf{2. Objective, Assumptions and Methods}
\par
Since cost-effectiveness is one of the main arguments to implement JI and CDM
\par
projects, it is our purpose to illustrate this aspect empirically. The objective of thisTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 117
\par
paper is to provide both qualitative and quantitative indications of the potential ef-
\par
fects of current and alternative institutional arrangements on the cost-effectiveness
\par
of JI and CDM projects. Hence, we want to scale the potential impact on JI and
\par
CDM credit costs of (a) current institutional arrangements of project-based emis-
\par
sions trading under the Kyoto Protocol as well as (b) alternative institutional ar-
\par
rangements of project-based emissions trading which would amend or elaborate
\par
the Kyoto Protocol.
\par
Institutional arrangements are defined as design characteristics of intended or
\par
implemented policy. Consequently, different institutional arrangements are diver-
\par
ging policy design features, which are likely to have dissimilar effects on the credit
\par
costs of a particular (group of) JI or CDM project(s). An example is the effect of
\par
banking on credit costs. The introduction made clear that the institutional arrange-
\par
ment of banking is available for CDM projects, but not (yet) for JI projects. Our
\par
interest is to demonstrate what this means for the credits costs of these projects
\par
and what it could do with the credit costs if policy-makers would decide to include
\par
banking in JI Article 6.
\par
To provide some indications of the potential effects of current and alternative
\par
institutional arrangements on possible JI and CDM credit costs, we make use (and
\par
specify the limitations) of the qualitative and quantitative data on the emissions
\par
reduction costs of 94 AIJ pilot phase projects. In order to enable the CoP to carry
\par
out a review of the AIJ pilot projects, it was decided in March 1996 to establish
\par
the Uniform Reporting Format (URF), so that technical and financial information
\par
about AIJ projects could be presented in a standard format and projects could be
\par
compared systematically. The cost figures we use are obtained from these URFs
\par
submitted by AIJ project partners to the FCCC Secretariat in 1998.
\par
The crucial assumption of our calculations of possible credit costs on the basis
\par
of AIJ cost data is that current pilot phase projects are sufficiently representative of
\par
future JI and CDM projects. This assumption must be made if one wants to use AIJ
\par
data to say something about JI and the CDM, but it undeniably poses a substantial
\par
limitation to the empirical analysis, because it is clear that AIJ projects are \emph{not }fully
\par
representative of JI and CDM projects for the following reasons.
\par
Firstly, the AIJ pilot phase was mainly set up, according to decision 5/CP.1/1(b)
\par
\& (d) of CoP1 in Berlin in 1995, to gain experience with the potential environ-
\par
mental and cost-effectiveness aspects of cross-border GHG abatement, and not
\par
so much to directly or solely test the costs of such projects. Secondly, the data
\par
reported in the URFs are to some extent inconsistent, partly missing and sometimes
\par
unclearly defined (e.g. Jepma and Eisma 1998; Ellis 1999; Michaelowa 1999).
\par
Moreover, some of the data are not necessarily updated in the URFs, which then
\par
reflect the planning stage rather than the implementation of the project, making
\par
some of the data actually older than they appear to be. Thirdly, AIJ projects are not
\par
credited, whereas JI and CDM projects will be credited. This means that the market
\par
for JI and the CDM will attract more investors than the AIJ pilot phase, because
\par
crediting implies a strong incentive to participate in (low-cost) projects. Fourthly,118 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
it should be stressed that credit costs are not credit prices. The invested amount per
\par
unit of emission reduction is not necessarily (or is even unlikely to be) equal to
\par
the price for which the credit will be sold on the market, which will be determined
\par
by both supply and demand. The cost data from several of the AIJ projects may
\par
not be representative for the marginal costs of GHG emission reductions in the
\par
countries were the projects take place. In addition, using the cost information from
\par
URFs does neither provide full insight into the structure of supply and demand of
\par
GHG credits during the commitment period 2008--2012 nor, consequently, in their
\par
market price (e.g. Trexler and Kosloff 1998).
\par
Does this mean that AIJ projects do not say anything about future JI and CDM
\par
projects at all? We don't think so. With the aforementioned reservations in mind,
\par
we are convinced that the AIJ cost data still can give us some idea, albeit to a
\par
limited extent, of the cost-effectiveness of JI and CDM projects for the following
\par
reasons.
\par
Firstly, the AIJ pilot phase was set up, according to decision 5/CP.1/1(b)\&2(a)
\par
of CoP1 in Berlin in 1995, to gain `practical experience' on the `economic, social
\par
and environmental impacts' of cross-border GHG abatement projects, which are
\par
explicitly required -- among other things -- to `contribute to cost-effectiveness'.
\par
Indeed, the costs per unit of emission reduction in real-life AIJ projects attracted
\par
much attention from decision-makers and scientists (in particular from the indus-
\par
trialized countries), precisely because it would give them a practical indication of
\par
what to expect from a post-2000 credited scheme in terms of cost-savings com-
\par
pared to domestic action (e.g. Harmelen et al. 1997; Trexler and Kosloff 1998;
\par
Begg et al. 1999). Some authors even state that cost-effectiveness is the primary
\par
motivation for project-based emissions trading (e.g. Jackson 1995). By comparing
\par
the CDM to AIJ, also Davidson and Sokona (1999), from Sierra Leone and Senegal
\par
respectively, state that both mechanisms are market-based for the transfer of finan-
\par
cial and technological resources, that both have emerged from the concept of GHG
\par
offset transfer or trading and that both rely on major disparities in abatement costs
\par
among Parties.
\par
Secondly, although the AIJ data reported in the URFs is imperfect, as indicated
\par
above, it has improved markedly over the years both in terms of homogeneity,
\par
scope, detail and structure (Jepma and Eisma 1998; SB 2000a), so that some now
\par
even conclude that `(. . .) we can establish a credible and consistent picture of AIJ
\par
costs' (Schwarze 1998: 13--14). Furthermore, the Subsidiary Bodies of the FCCC
\par
noted in 1999 that AIJ cost data are estimates of varying accuracy, so that their
\par
usefulness for an analysis is limited, which also logically implies, one might argue,
\par
that it is not completely absent (cf. SB 1999a).
\par
Thirdly, the fact that JI and the CDM are credited in contrast with AIJ means that
\par
project activity will increase -- possibly distributed over a wider and more diverse
\par
geographical area -- so that experience and insight will grow with respect to the
\par
(investment en transaction) costs of such projects. On the one hand, it is likely that
\par
JI and CDM project costs will decrease relative to AIJ, not only because creditingTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 119
\par
will provide an incentive to search for low-cost options, but also because compet-
\par
ition and transparency increases as the market matures. Furthermore, various AIJ
\par
projects were either set up as simulation studies in the margin of already existing
\par
bilateral or multilateral cooperation programmes which focused on environmental
\par
integrity rather than cost-effectiveness, or were chosen because of their low cost to
\par
ensure that the investment would become profitable relatively quickly. The former,
\par
more costly, projects will probably not be (the first to be) implemented in a credited
\par
scheme. On the other hand, potentially interested investors simply did not want to
\par
invest in AIJ projects with higher marginal costs, precisely because they would not
\par
obtain GHG emission reduction credits for their investments during the AIJ pilot
\par
phase. Crediting would then mean that the more expensive projects have better
\par
chances to be carried out after all under JI and the CDM. Consequently, the wide
\par
range of AIJ credit costs, varying from cheap to expensive, probably provides a
\par
relevant albeit incomplete picture of the cost-effectiveness of project-based emis-
\par
sions trading, although such information will clearly enhance in qualitative and
\par
quantitative terms as the JI and CDM market develops and the design of the URF
\par
improves.
\par
Fourthly, we do not suggest that our numerical illustrations of the effects of
\par
policy design on credit costs are calculations or predictions of market prices which
\par
would require an incorporation of both supply and demand curves. Instead, our
\par
calculations should give some indication of the project costs per unit of emission re-
\par
duction and what different design features might do with these costs. Furthermore,
\par
although our calculations of average costs for various samples of AIJ projects are
\par
not equal to the marginal costs of JI and CDM projects, they still give some useful
\par
information about their marginal costs. If the average costs of a certain project
\par
region or type are higher than the average costs of another, then it is likely that also
\par
the marginal costs of the former are higher. In addition, the marginal cost curve
\par
always converges on the minimum of the average cost curve, so that marginal and
\par
average costs are (almost) equal when the project is so cheap that it has (almost)
\par
reached this minimal point.
\par
Interestingly, although several specialists know that existing project-specific es-
\par
timates of mitigation costs must be interpreted cautiously for purposes of either
\par
comparing with permit prices and tax levels or extrapolating to the future cost-
\par
effectiveness of project-based emissions trading activities, this is precisely what
\par
many explicitly or implicitly do. For instance, although they correctly pay much
\par
attention to the limitations of such extrapolations, Trexler and Kosloff (1998) write:
\par
`The asserted costs of ongoing and proposed offset projects range from negative
\par
carbon dioxide (CO2) costs (. . .) to more than \$40/Mg. Most ongoing private-
\par
sector projects appear to be in \$ 0.20---2.00 per ton of CO2 range (. . .), far less
\par
than the \$20-- 30/Mg CO2 taxes often discussed as being necessary to achieve
\par
significant emissions reductions' (Trexler and Kosloff 1998). Another, different,
\par
example in the context of representativeness is provided by Begg et al. (2000),
\par
who refer to AIJ projects in developing countries as `CDM type projects'.120 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
It should be clear that we do not so much want to calculate either marginal costs
\par
or supply and demand, but that we rather want to use the data from real-life AIJ
\par
projects to provide some numerical insight into the potential effects on the credit
\par
costs of the current policy design differences between JI and CDM as well as of
\par
different possible institutional arrangements under Article 6 and 12. We believe
\par
that this is possible for the aforementioned reasons, although it should be stressed
\par
that conclusions can only be drawn cautiously because of the limitations of the
\par
analysis as discussed above. Nevertheless, we think that it is worthwhile to consider
\par
(and criticize) the cost data from AIJ, exactly because these real-life projects are
\par
intended -- in which it largely succeeded -- to provide useful information on the
\par
basis of `learning by doing' (e.g. Michaelowa et al. 1999, SB 1999b). In sum, we
\par
want to use the valuable AIJ cost data to get some idea of what different policy
\par
designs could do with JI and CDM credit costs during the first commitment period.
\par
To demonstrate the potential influence of policy design on project costs, we apply
\par
the following methods in the sections which are indicated hereafter.
\par
First, we show that some (but not all) AIJ projects have been initiated expli-
\par
citly with a view to their cost-effectiveness, which is illustrated on the basis of
\par
some real-life projects (section 3). Second, we calculate possible JI and CDM
\par
credit costs on the basis of reported and unadjusted AIJ data, differentiated by
\par
host country and project type, on the basis of the assumption (which has been and
\par
will be thoroughly criticized) that the AIJ projects in our study are sufficiently
\par
representative of future JI and CDM projects (section 4). Third, we calculate the
\par
possible effects on JI as well as CDM credit costs by adjusting the AIJ projects
\par
on the basis of simple mathematical formulas as if these projects were credited as
\par
post-2000 projects (sections 5 and 6). These calculations are interesting, not only
\par
because they are easy to follow for non-mathematicians, but also because they
\par
explicitly take into account the institutional differences between JI Article 6 which
\par
includes sinks (and not banking) and CDM Article 12 which includes banking
\par
(and not sinks). Thereafter, we adjust the AIJ cost data by using other formulas to
\par
give some idea of the possible quantitative effects on the credit costs if banking
\par
would be included under JI Article 6 and if sinks would be included in the CDM.
\par
Moreover, within each Kyoto Mechanism the low-cost options, which are likely
\par
to attract most investors after 2000, are specifically considered. The idea to use
\par
simple formulas to calculate the costs of emission reduction projects, such as AIJ,
\par
to be able to compare different projects, was already applied by Begg et al. (2000).
\par
However, they incorporated various cost elements in their formulas rather than the
\par
institutional characteristics of these projects which is the focus of our study in this
\par
paper. Jansen et al. (1999) did not so much calculated project costs, but rather a
\par
market clearing price for energy sector CDM credits on the basis of supply and
\par
demand. However, they also did not incorporate institutional elements (such as
\par
banking or sinks) in their projections, which again underlines the relevance and
\par
originality of our institution-oriented calculations. Fourth, we go from credit costs
\par
to credit prices by considering supply and demand factors. In particular, we com-THE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 121
\par
plement the micro-analysis with a macro-perspective, which not only emphasizes
\par
the incomparability between costs and prices, but which also gives us some idea
\par
of potential prices. We also pay attention to the conditions under which the credit
\par
price on a GHG offset market may rise or fall. Finally, we present a conclusion
\par
which underlines the impact of institutional arrangements on the cost-effectiveness
\par
of project-based emissions trading.
\par
\textbf{3. The Role of Cost-Effectiveness in the AIJ Pilot Phase}
\par
Those who believe that cost-effectiveness played either a dominant role or no role
\par
at all in the AIJ pilot phase, are both wrong. We will demonstrate that the truth lies
\par
somewhere in the middle. We already cited the official AIJ objectives in the pre-
\par
vious section, which included environmental impacts as well as cost-effectiveness.
\par
In this section, we present some real-life projects to illustrate that some, but not all
\par
AIJ projects have been initiated explicitly with a view to their cost-effectiveness.
\par
This is also the explanation for the fact that the AIJ data show a huge cost spread,
\par
which will be illustrated with concrete figures in the next section.
\par
The main purpose of the AIJ pilot phase was to learn from actual project ex-
\par
perience how to deal with several technical implementation issues. During the
\par
preparatory meetings of the Intergovernmental Negotiating Committee (INC) held
\par
from May 1992 through February 1995, the Parties pointed at potential technical
\par
problems with the JI concept, such as baseline determination, monitoring and veri-
\par
fication, certification and credit sharing. While the political time was not ripe yet
\par
for a full-blown JI system, Parties were given an opportunity to experiment with
\par
the concept during the AIJ pilot phase.
\par
Potentially interested investors simply did not want to invest in AIJ projects
\par
with relatively high marginal costs, because during the AIJ pilot phase they would
\par
not obtain GHG emission reduction credits for their investments. Due to this ab-
\par
sence of crediting, several Parties wanted to become involved in pilot projects, but
\par
only at the lowest costs possible. As a result, the projects were set up in different
\par
ways. Roughly, two types of projects can be discerned: pilot projects and simu-
\par
lation projects. Pilot projects were usually initiated by firms (such as US utility
\par
companies) that were looking for cost-effective carbon offset opportunities abroad.
\par
Other initiatives were called simulation projects. Strictly speaking, one could argue
\par
that all projects set up during the pilot phase are `simulation' projects because of
\par
their testing character, but in our paper this term is used if a JI component -- such
\par
as baseline determination or CO2 emission reduction measuring -- is added to a
\par
project which had already been set up in the framework of existing bilateral or mul-
\par
tilateral cooperation programmes between governments (such as the Netherlands
\par
or the group of Nordic countries on the one hand and several Central and Eastern
\par
European countries on the other hand). Such a JI component was added to these
\par
already ongoing projects (mostly fuel switching and energy efficiency projects)122 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
