3.1. THE DECIN AIJ PILOT PROJECT: COST-EFFECTIVENESS AS A MAJOR
\par
MOTIVATION
\par
The Decin project in the Czech Republic is a fuel switching project with total
\par
investment costs of \$9,058,000 to which three US utility companies (Wisconsin
\par
Electric Power Company, NIPSCO Development Company and Commonwealth
\par
Edison Company) each contributed \$200,000 in the form of a 25 year loan with no
\par
interest in return of CO2 abatement credits. The CO2 emission reductions from the
\par
coal-to-gas conversion for the heating system in a district of Decin are estimated to
\par
amount to 608,952 tons in total over 20 years, of which 133,827 can be attributed
\par
to the fuel switch and 475,125 to the co-generation effects of the project. Although
\par
crediting is not allowed for AIJ projects, negotiations still took place on creditTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 123
\par
sharing, since the US utilities were interested in receiving credits in the future
\par
for their contribution to CO2 emission reduction, also because they either had a
\par
(voluntary) offset agreement at the state level or an internal offset target. Initially,
\par
the negotiations were held between the utilities and the local partners, but later
\par
on the Czech government became involved, although no official credit transfer
\par
took place between the Czech government and the US government. The emission
\par
reduction and cost figures of the Decin project are presented in Table I.
\par
Table I reveals that six different cost figures per tonne CO2 emission reduc-
\par
tion could be calculated (cf. SEVEn/JIN 1997). The US investors could argue that
\par
although they only contribute to about 6\% of the project costs, without their assist-
\par
ance (covering the incremental costs) the project might not have been implemented
\par
at all. In that case, they could claim the credits for the total CO2 emission reduction,
\par
so that for them the costs per credit (assuming that one credit = 1 ton CO2 emission
\par
reduction) would be as low as \$0.99. However, the project developers and/or the
\par
Czech government could argue that the total amount of CO2 emission reduction
\par
should be credited to all contributors to the project. In that case the cost of a credit
\par
would amount to \$9,058,000 / 608,952 tons CO2 = \$14.87. Initially, when devel-
\par
oping the Decin project during the years 1992 to 1994, it was agreed that the US
\par
investors would receive the credits on the basis of the 133,827 ton CO2 emission
\par
reduction due to the fuel switch. This would imply that for the US investors the
\par
costs per ton CO2 would amount to \$4.48. Although the figures in Table I indicate
\par
that several possibilities seem to make sense in determining the credit costs, they
\par
also show that already very early in the AIJ pilot phase some project partners were
\par
explicitly negotiating on the basis of cost-effectiveness.
\par
3.2. THE HUNGARIAN-DUTCH AIJ SIMULATION PROJECT:
\par
COST-EFFECTIVENESS AS A MINOR MOTIVATION
\par
In March 1995, the governments of Hungary and the Netherlands expressed their
\par
wish to jointly realize a series of AIJ projects. Their aim was to learn about the
\par
practical aspects of project-based emissions trading and to promote JI as a feasible
\par
concept. Two joint projects (already under way in Hungary within the framework
\par
of the Dutch Bilateral Cooperation Program with Central and Eastern European
\par
countries) were therefore designated as simulation projects and linked to a monit-
\par
oring study. One of these projects involves the transfer of technological know-how
\par
to enable the production of buses that run on compressed natural gas (CNG) instead
\par
of diesel oil. The transfer of technology already used in the Netherlands to applic-
\par
ations in Hungary (the so-called `hardware' of the project) was thus supplemented
\par
with a JI simulation component (the so-called `software' of the project). The sim-
\par
ulation is referred to as the RABA/Ikarus project, referring to the two Hungarian
\par
industries which would place the cleaner engines in the buses.
\par
In order to make the transfer of CNG technology applicable to JI projects, the
\par
effect of the transfer had to be measured in an agreed manner. Therefore, a baseline124 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
had to be established for the emissions of about 5 buses for which the engine would
\par
be replaced. The simulation study found that the project would mainly reduce
\par
carbon monoxide (CO) and nitrogen oxide (NOx) emissions (next to other local
\par
pollutants) rather than carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions, so that the project would
\par
mainly benefit the local environment. The emission reduction would occur despite
\par
a slight increase in total fuel consumption due to a lower fuel efficiency of each
\par
CNG bus.
\par
In terms of GHG abatement costs, the project can hardly be considered as cost-
\par
effective. The total investment costs of installing the project's `hardware' amounted
\par
to \$1.3 million (calculated in 1996 US\$). The expected GHG emission reduction
\par
over the total lifetime of the project is estimated at 5200 to 13,000 tons of CO2-
\par
equivalent (depending on the baseline assumptions), which results in a cost-per-
\par
tonne-abatement figure of as high as \$100 to \$250. Nevertheless, the AIJ simulation
\par
costs for the Netherlands' government were, of course, relatively low (as explained
\par
above) and the aim of the project was not so much to achieve a low-cost emis-
\par
sion reduction abroad, but rather to provide both governments with institutional
\par
and technical AIJ expertise. Because both governments considerably gained such
\par
expertise, it must be concluded that this simulation project was a success, even
\par
though it was not cost-effective.
\par
\textbf{4. Costs per Unit of Emission Reduction in the AIJ Pilot Phase}
\par
The data base used in our study consists of 94 AIJ pilot phase projects of which
\par
68 are implemented in Central and Eastern Europe (Annex I Parties) and 26 in
\par
developing countries (non-Annex I Parties). Within the Annex I region, 56 projects
\par
are financed by governments and 11 by firms. In developing countries there are 21
\par
private sector AIJ projects and only 5 projects are financed with public funds. To
\par
analyze these projects we used the project categories defined by the FCCC Secret-
\par
ariat. This categorization has been criticized, though, for being rather condensed
\par
and indistinct (e.g. Ellis 1999).
\par
4.1. GENERAL CHARACTERISTICS OF THE DATA SET
\par
The AIJ projects implemented in Annex I countries mainly consist of energy ef-
\par
ficiency (34\%) and renewable energy projects (32\%). Energy efficiency projects
\par
mainly consist of fugitive gas capture, demand-side management and energy ef-
\par
ficiency improvements, whereas renewable energy projects largely concern mit-
\par
igation activities related to landfill, biomass, coal-to-gas conversion as well as
\par
wind and solar energy. Fuel switching (3\%) as well as forestry and agriculture
\par
projects (3\%) play a minor role. However, only a small number of projects which
\par
were explicitly reported as fuel switching projects have been categorized as such,
\par
while several fuel switching projects (e.g. boiler conversion) were reported andTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 125
\par
classified as renewable energy projects. Furthermore, forestry consists of both af-
\par
forestation, reforestation and forest preservation. Of the AIJ projects implemented
\par
in non-Annex I countries the majority is of the type of renewable energy (12\%)
\par
and forestry or agriculture (12\%). The share of energy efficiency projects (4\%) in
\par
developing countries is relatively small. Of all 94 projects in the survey, renew-
\par
able energy projects (44\%) are the most common, followed by energy efficiency
\par
projects (38\%) and forestry or agriculture (15\%). Note that the aforementioned
\par
percentages are all rounded percentages of the \emph{total }of projects.
\par
A project's lifetime (as stated in most URFs) is considered as an indicator for
\par
its duration period. It turns out that the duration period of the reported projects
\par
varies from 1 to 60 years, with an average of 16.5 years. On average, the AIJ
\par
projects in developing countries have a longer duration period and reduce more
\par
GHG emissions than projects in Central and Eastern Europe, which can to a large
\par
extent be explained by the relatively large share of forestry and agriculture projects
\par
(42\%) implemented in cooperation with non-Annex I Parties. For the definition of
\par
the emission reduction commitments, the Kyoto Protocol has identified six GHGs
\par
which are listed in Annex A of the Protocol. In the URFs all GHG emission reduc-
\par
tions have been recalculated using IPCC conversion factors into CO2 equivalents
\par
(abbreviated as eq). In general, forestry and agriculture projects result in a high
\par
average and total amount of emission reductions over a relatively longer period
\par
of time. Particularly, projects with reported emission reductions higher than one
\par
million Mg CO2-eq are mainly forest preservation projects. As a consequence, an
\par
average project in an Annex I country yields an emission reduction of 771,190 Mg
\par
CO 2-eq over 14 years, while the average AIJ project with a non-Annex I Party as
\par
host produces an emission reduction of 3,944,384 Mg CO2-eq over 23 years. Of all
\par
the AIJ projects reported to the FCCC the average emission reduction amounts to
\par
1,658,320 Mg CO2-eq, with a minimum of 13 tons and a maximum of 57,467,271
\par
tons.
\par
The average total invested amount in projects in Annex I countries is \$2,878,484
\par
and in developing countries \$16,110,770. The mean total investment in an AIJ
\par
project, irrespective of the host country type, is \$6,298,065 with a minimum of
\par
\$73,500 and a maximum of \$130,000,000. Interestingly, governments invest an
\par
average of \$2,227,260 in an AIJ project, while private partners invest \$15,198,560.
\par
Moreover, the sum of investments is about \$133 million for all government-financed
\par
projects and about \$425 million for the total of private projects. Hence, although
\par
the investor is more often a government (66\%) than a firm (34\%), the contribution
\par
of private projects to AIJ is relatively large in terms of investments (and also, for
\par
instance, in terms of emission reductions).
\par
4.2. THE COSTS PER TONNE OF EMISSION REDUCTION
\par
In order to determine the cost-effectiveness of a project each investor is interested
\par
in the costs per tonne of GHG emission reduction acquired from a project. The126 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
most straightforward way to calculate these costs is to relate the total amount of
\par
investment funds with the total GHG emission reductions for each project.
\par
However, such cost figures have to be put into perspective (as indicated in the
\par
previous section), among other things, because some projects were initially under
\par
construction in the framework of another -- often non-GHG -- programme, but
\par
were lacking the funding for full financing. By adding a GHG emission reduction
\par
component to these projects, governments could experiment with the concept of
\par
project-based emissions trading. As such, the projects were approved as AIJ by the
\par
governments involved and reported to the FCCC as pilot projects.
\par
How should the cost per ton GHG emission reduction be calculated in the latter
\par
case: should the total investment be divided by the total amount of emission re-
\par
duction or should only the `AIJ component' be related to the emission reduction?
\par
Some project developers chose the first option, while others used the second one
\par
(whereas there are some who did not present any figure at all). Obviously, this
\par
complicates a comparison of the projects. Therefore, this study not only considers
\par
the costs per unit as reported by the project developers themselves (see Table II),
\par
but also calculates these costs on the basis of uniform formulas for all projects in
\par
order to facilitate a more systematic comparison (see Table III). All cost figures
\par
(except those between \$0 and \$1) are rounded off an amount to the nearest dollar.
\par
Table II makes clear that, according to the project partners themselves, the
\par
average emission reduction unit costs are about \$ 28 per ton of CO2 equivalent
\par
(\$/Mg CO2-eq), with a minimum of --66 \$/Mg CO2-eq (note that this negative
\par
cost is a profit) and a maximum of 320 \$/Mg CO2-eq. The average unit costs as
\par
reported by the partners in government projects are about 26 \$/Mg CO2-eq and
\par
in private projects 39 \$/Mg CO2-eq. Forestry projects seem to be relatively cheap
\par
and fuel switching projects expensive, but the basis for such a conclusion (only
\par
five forestry projects and two fuel switching projects) is rather small. The average
\par
reported costs per tonne GHG emission reduction in Annex I countries are about
\par
26 \$/Mg CO2-eq and in non-Annex I countries 40 \$/Mg CO2-eq. Unfortunately,
\par
figures for afforestation and agriculture projects were not reported at all, while
\par
reported data were not available for Annex I Parties on reforestation projects and
\par
for non-Annex I Parties on fuel switching and renewable energy. Although the
\par
