4.3. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS
\par
During the last years, several studies have been conducted to analyze the experi-
\par
ences during the AIJ pilot phase. As a comparison, it may be instructive to have a
\par
look at the cost figures presented in some of these studies. The following overview
\par
is not intended to be exhaustive.
\par
SEVEn/JIN (1997) and Van Harmelen et al. (1997) analyzed the emission re-
\par
duction costs for 31 AIJ projects in Central and Eastern Europe. These costs varied
\par
between 0.01 and 101 \$/Mg CO2 with an average of about 12 \$/Mg CO2. Schwarze
\par
(1998) analyzed the AIJ projects on the basis of URFs of August 1998. He ascer-
\par
tains the abundance of low-cost projects, especially those concerning fugitive gas
\par
capture, energy efficiency improvement and sink enhancement, which all cost less
\par
than 4 \$/Mg CO2. He stresses that this is not only at the lower end of previous price
\par
predictions by other authors, which ranged from 2 to 22 \$/Mg CO2, but also that
\par
these data are generally in line with engineering-based estimates from bottom-up
\par
studies (cf. Hourcade et al. 1996). Likewise, Trexler and Kosloff (1998) asserted
\par
that most ongoing private-sector projects appear to be in 0.2--2 \$/Mg CO2 range.
\par
The analysis in this paper generally confirms their findings, although the average
\par
emission reduction costs, also for several project types, are considerably higher
\par
than Schwarze (1998) and Trexler and Kosloff (1998) suggest.
\par
Begg et al. (1999) remark that for determining the costs of GHG emission reduc-
\par
tion units not only the choice of the technology/fuel baseline, but also other factors,THE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 131
\par
such as the discount rate, future fuel prices, and the project's lifetime should be
\par
taken into consideration. They have investigated the effect of these factors on the
\par
cost per tonne (specific incremental cost) of a selection of energy sector projects
\par
undertaken during the AIJ pilot phase. Their analysis shows that there is a large
\par
range of uncertainty in costs for a given AIJ project. Even for projects of a similar
\par
type within the same country (e.g. the biomass heat supply projects in Estonia),
\par
the ranges of uncertainty vary significantly. The main reason for this, with respect
\par
to the projects investigated in their analysis, tended to be different possibilities for
\par
feasible baselines as well as local variations in observed fuel prices.
\par
Further investigation by Begg et al. (1999) indicated that variations in other
\par
factors could lead to significant changes in the specific incremental costs. For ex-
\par
ample, the range given for the fuel switching project in Decin was calculated using
\par
a 4\% discount rate and a lifetime of 25 y. If a 10\% per 10 y combination would be
\par
used (based on an economic lifetime and market discount rate), the range becomes
\par
43 to 64 \$/Mg CO2, which is around a three-fold increase in the central value.
\par
Fuel price changes are also an important factor. In some of the Swedish-funded
\par
AIJ biomass projects in Estonia local biomass prices increased by 100\% over the
\par
first few years of operation due to the large scale domestic economic restruc-
\par
turing that occurred during this period. This considerably reduced the expected
\par
cost-effectiveness of these projects.
\par
A year later, Begg et al. (2000) released another report in which, among other
\par
things, the costs per unit of CO2 emission reduction were calculated for existing
\par
ecological projects in developing countries, such as AIJ. Some CDM type projects,
\par
as they called them, appeared to have negative costs, such as improved cooking
\par
stoves and micro-hydro power projects. Other projects had positive costs, such as
\par
biogas projects, varying from 10 to 20 \$/Mg CO2, or solar home systems which
\par
appeared to be rather costly in a range between 120 and 770 \$/Mg CO2.
\par
\textbf{5. Joint Implementation, Policy Design and Emission Reduction Costs}
\par
Under Article 6 of the Kyoto Protocol, an Annex I industrialized country is allowed
\par
to acquire ERU resulting from a Joint Implementation (JI) project in another Annex
\par
I country. As was explained above, project cooperation under Article 6 (between
\par
Annex I countries) and under Article 12 (between Annex I and non-Annex I coun-
\par
tries) differs in some respects. Theoretically, therefore, a GHG emission reduction
\par
project or sink enhancement project set up in, say, the Russian Federation (an
\par
Annex I Party), could be valued in a completely different way than a similar project
\par
(assuming a comparable invested amount) in China (a non-Annex I Party), for
\par
instance.
\par
First, if the projects were implemented as of 2000 with a lifetime up to 2012, the
\par
project in Russia could, according to the current language of the Kyoto Protocol,
\par
only result in credits between 2008--2012 (during the first commitment period),132 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
whereas the project in China could, as a CDM project, result in emission reduction
\par
credits already as of the year 2000. In other words, the Chinese project could
\par
result in a factor 2.6 (13 years / 5 years) more credits than a comparable project in
\par
Russia. Second, if the projects were both sink enhancement projects and if Article
\par
12 should be interpreted as if sink projects are not eligible under the CDM, the
\par
Chinese project would not result in credits at all, whereas the Russian project
\par
would. Third, the Russian government representing an Annex I Party may wish to
\par
keep part of the project's emission reduction for the fulfillment of its own domestic
\par
commitments under the Kyoto Protocol. The Chinese government (representing a
\par
non-Annex I Party without a commitment) may be less interested in the emission
\par
reduction titles and thus inclined to transfer more credits to the investing country.
\par
These differences may significantly influence the projects' cost-effectiveness for
\par
the investor per tonne of GHG emissions reduced. As an illustration, this and the
\par
following section, among others, present an analysis of the emission reduction
\par
costs on the basis of AIJ data given the institutional differences between JI and
\par
CDM.
\par
