\par
5.2. DECREASED ADDITIONALITY
\par
The determination of the additionality of a JI project, as well as of a CDM project,
\par
is one of the most complicated issues to be solved before the mechanisms can
\par
become operational under the Kyoto Protocol. The issue of additionality deals with
\par
the question whether, and to what extent, an investment under a JI or CDM project
\par
would have been carried out anyway. For example, a project may be commercially
\par
attractive and would have attracted project partners in the commercial market. Ap-
\par
proving such a project as a JI or CDM project would not result in additional GHG
\par
emission reductions. The complication of determining additionality via a so-called
\par
baseline for the project is that the reference scenario is counterfactual, so that the
\par
situation that is referred to will never exist because of the project.
\par
By analyzing different AIJ projects (e.g., Nordic Council of Ministers 1997),
\par
it has been found that the projects often only speed up investments that would
\par
have been carried out by the host countries themselves in the mid- or longer term.
\par
This implies that the project is additional only for that period of time during which
\par
the project has been moved forward by implementing it earlier than the countries
\par
would do themselves. It is generally assumed that this period is longer for the
\par
relatively poor developing countries than for the countries in Central and Eastern
\par
Europe. In this section it is therefore assumed that projects in developing countries
\par
are additional during their full economic lifetime, whereas for projects in some
\par
Central and Eastern European countries the `additionality period' is assumed to
\par
be shorter than the investment's economic lifetime. Also in practice, some URFs
\par
already report a project activity duration or loan period for projects in Central and
\par
Eastern Europe which is several years shorter than its predicted lifetime.
\par
For simplicity and illustration purposes, it is assumed that the additionality for JI
\par
projects will be 50\% of the economic lifetime of AIJ projects in countries with eco-
\par
nomies in transition. Furthermore, it is assumed that this additionality is 100\% for
\par
Russia given a relatively low expected economic growth. Of course, this situation
\par
also holds for some other countries with economies in transition, but practically
\par
none of them have hosted AIJ projects so far. To calculate the JI credit price with
\par
