5.3. LOW-COST OPTIONS
\par
Cost-minimizing investors will preferably focus on JI projects with credit costs
\par
which are as low as possible. In particular, they will probably only consider the
\par
implementation of projects whose costs are within a range of low-cost options.
\par
This would exclude options with cost figures higher than a certain critical level. For
\par
simplicity and illustration purposes, we assume that this critical level is, say, 100136 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
\$/Mg CO2-eq. We have chosen this (somewhat arbitrary) level, because a lower
\par
level would exclude too many projects from the analysis, thereby diminishing its
\par
robustness, whereas a higher level would be rather unlikely given the potential
\par
presence of alternative and cheaper investment options in developing countries
\par
and in Central and Eastern Europe. Furthermore, we think that it is wise, as an
\par
illustrative exercise, to exclude the few projects with sometimes extremely high
\par
costs, precisely because they are small in number and exceptions in terms of costs
\par
and because they have been set up as simulation studies within the framework of
\par
already existing bilateral or multilateral cooperation programmes which did not so
\par
much focus on cost-effectiveness. Given this critical level, the resulting average
\par
low-cost JI credit costs, as portrayed in Table VI, amounts to about 26 \$/Mg CO2-
\par
eq (with a minimum of 0.03 \$/Mg CO2-eq and a maximum of 90 \$/Mg CO2-eq).
\par
Including banking in JI Article 6 would reduce its price to 20 \$/Mg CO2-eq (with
\par
a minimum of 0.01\$/Mg CO2-eq and a maximum of 95 \$/Mg CO2-eq).
\par
5.4. INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS AND JI COST-EFFECTIVENESS
\par
Trexler and Kosloff (1998) correctly state that `. . . the issues associated with offset
\par
quantification and verification (. . .) clearly could have significant implications for
\par
the scope and cost-effectiveness of JI opportunities' (Trexler and Kosloff 1998: 36).
\par
For instance, a politically important topic for the CoP is whether the whole project
\par
cycle (including baseline determination, accreditation and monitoring) should be
\par
similar for JI and the CDM or not. If decision-makers would decide to make the
\par
project cycle `easier' for JI projects than for CDM projects, the cost-effectiveness
\par
of the former would be enhanced.
\par
This has, in fact, been proposed by the Umbrella group (which includes the US
\par
and Russia, among others) and the group of Central and Eastern European countries
\par
prior to CoP6. They wanted to let the acceptance of JI projects -- including their
\par
baselines -- only be subject to checking a Party's compliance with Articles 5 and 7
\par
of the Protocol (dealing with national GHG inventory systems as well as commu-
\par
nication and information). The main argument for this proposal is that JI projects
\par
are implemented in countries with an assigned amount under the Kyoto Protocol.
\par
Miscalculations of JI project baselines will therefore, at most, become visible in the
\par
national emissions inventory of the host country. If, due to a wrongly determined JI
\par
baseline, the national emissions of the host country during the commitment period
\par
will be higher than the host's assigned amount, a non-compliance procedure under
\par
the Protocol will start. Given the character of this still to be decided procedure, the
\par
host or the investor country, or both, will have to compensate for the deficit. The
\par
EU and the G77 including China, instead, supported applying a CDM-type project
\par
cycle for JI as well, in order to ensure environmental integrity and credibility. An
\par
additional argument could be that an `easier' project cycle for JI only, would give
\par
JI projects a competitive advantage over CDM projects, possibly leading to the
\par
crowding out of the latter in a global market. According to the latter proposal, JITHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 137
\par
projects need to be validated and certified by an independent operational entity,
\par
possibly supervised by the Executive Board under the CDM.
\par
The calculations and remarks made above provide an illustration of the po-
\par
tential effects of current and alternative institutional arrangements on the cost-
\par
effectiveness of possible JI projects. However, the numbers presented should be
\par
viewed with caution, because the underlying data are incomplete, fragmented and
\par
only partly representative of future JI projects. Nevertheless, it could be demon-
\par
strated that the absence of banking in the Kyoto Protocol is an institutional limita-
\par
tion which considerably lowers the cost-effectiveness of Eastern European projects
\par
when comparing AIJ to JI. Of course, crediting such early JI action would enhance
\par
the financial attractiveness of JI projects, but Parties failed to reach consensus at
\par
CoP6 in November 2000 in The Hague (The Netherlands) on working out the in-
\par
stitutional details of JI. Furthermore, the specific determination of additionality in
\par
relation to the economic lifetime of a project appears to have a significant impact on
\par
the cost-effectiveness of JI as well. Finally, the AIJ figures suggest that JI is likely
\par
to incorporate various low-cost options, in particular in the field of forestry and re-
\par
newable energy projects. Nevertheless, the next section will show that the low-cost
\par
potential in developing countries is even bigger, although the cost-effectiveness of
\par
such projects will be affected by the particular institutional arrangements of the
\par
CDM.
\par
\textbf{6. Clean Development Mechanism, Policy Design and Emission Reduction}
\par
\textbf{Costs}
\par
Under Article 12 of the Kyoto Protocol, the CDM is defined. The purpose of the
\par
CDM is (a) to assist Parties not included in Annex I in achieving sustainable de-
\par
velopment and (b) in contributing to the ultimate objective of the Convention, and
\par
(c) to assist Parties included in Annex I in achieving compliance with their quan-
\par
tified emission limitation and reduction commitments (Article 12.2). This seems
\par
to reflect a `win-win-win' philosophy by emphasizing that the host country, the
\par
environment and the investor country should all benefit from a joint emission re-
\par
duction project. The emission reduction credits an Annex I country can acquire
\par
from a CDM project are officially called Certified Emission Reductions (CERs) in
\par
Article 12. The CERs differ from JI ERUs as defined in Article 6 in that the former
\par
require an official certification procedure to be carried out by so-called operational
\par
entities that will be designated by the CoP, whereas Article 6 JI projects do not
\par
require such an official certification procedure.
\par
As was explained before, CERs resulting from CDM projects between 2000
\par
and 2008 can be banked, contrary to JI, in order to use them for the commitment
\par
period (Article 12.10). On the other hand, in contrast with JI Article 6, a text on
\par
sink enhancement has not been included for CDM projects in Article 12. Although
\par
some have argued that the latter should be seen as an editorial omission caused by138 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
