\par
cost-effective GHG emission reduction options in developing countries. However,
\par
although the CDM holds several options to reduce GHGs which are relatively
\par
cheap, it may be difficult for investors to actually implement potential low-cost
\par
GHG emission reduction projects in developing countries, among other things,
\par
because technology transfer is demanded under CDM Article 12 and because the
\par
informational, institutional and infrastructural constraints are higher in developing
\par
countries than in Annex I countries (Davidson and Sokona 1999; Karani 1997).
\par
6.3. INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS AND CDM COST-EFFECTIVENESS
\par
With respect to the CDM project cycle, there seems to be a majority view among
\par
FCCC Parties that it needs to be formalized through a reference manual (e.g. SB
\par
2000b). The technical details for the CDM baseline methodology to be includedTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 141
\par
in the reference manual, which are probably to be decided upon by CoP meetings
\par
a few years from now are expected to affect the projects' cost-effectiveness. A
\par
related issue is the \emph{ex post }revision of baselines which could affect a project's
\par
cost-effectiveness if the baseline emissions are thought to be lower than initially
\par
assumed, which would lower the amount of credits generated. However, there was
\par
a consensus already before CoP6 in 2000 that a validated baseline methodology
\par
shall not be subject to revision during the crediting period of a CDM project except
\par
if recommended by an independent verification entity. Furthermore, a revision of
\par
the methodology shall only be relevant to future crediting periods and not affect
\par
existing projects during the current crediting period.
\par
Inclusion of sinks in Article 12, which would increase the cost-effectiveness
\par
of the CDM, is both a technical and a political topic. The technical perspective
\par
of sinks deals with whether current methodologies are sufficiently developed to
\par
make sure that the C sequestration of a CDM sinks project can be calculated and
\par
monitored adequately. From a political perspective, some countries have argued
\par
against including sinks in the CDM not only for social and environmental reasons,
\par
but mainly because they fear that the relatively low cost of sinks projects may
\par
displace technology-oriented CDM projects. Some FCCC parties are generally in
\par
favour of including sinks under the CDM. The EU is more reserved in that it prefers
\par
to wait for a decision on sinks by the CoP. Non-Annex I Parties' positions are
\par
rather diverse. The Latin American countries, in particular Costa Rica, as well as
\par
the African countries in general seem to support sinks under the CDM, possibly
\par
because sinks projects could provide them with an extra flow of investment. Brazil
\par
does not object to sinks under the CDM, but supports afforestation and reforestation
\par
projects only. Some developing countries, such as India and China, are against
\par
including sinks in the CDM, whereby especially China has expressed the concern
\par
that inexpensive sinks projects could displace technology-oriented CDM projects.
\par
With respect to CDM baselines, the issue is to find a methodology that balances
\par
between maximizing the environmental integrity of a project and minimizing its
\par
transaction costs. Aiming at maximizing the environmental integrity by collect-
\par
ing as much information as possible about a project's reference case, as generally
\par
supported by the non-Annex I countries, may inhibit a project's cost-effectiveness.
\par
Standardized procedures for baselines in order to minimize transaction costs, as
\par
supported by the US, may result in CERs and ERUs that in reality do not take place.
\par
This implies that baseline determination although the projects will certainly lower
\par
the costs of climate policy, still incorporates an unavoidable trade-off between the
\par
economy and the environment.
\par
Standardization implies the development of business-as-usual scenarios for pro-
\par
ject categories differentiated by, for instance, region, time, project and/or techno-
\par
logy type. These scenarios could be determined by a panel of experts. Each specific
\par
project should fit into one of the categories. The (\emph{ex ante }or \emph{ex post}) emission reduc-
\par
tions of a specific project can simply be calculated by substracting the (predicted or
\par
observed) emissions from the baseline emissions of the relevant category. Hence,142 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
project partners do not have to establish a baseline and a third party check for each
\par
baseline is no longer necessary.
\par
A practical example of standardization is the matrix approach, which places
\par
pre-defined default scenarios for several project categories into a matrix. The in-
\par
vestor and the host of a JI or CDM project look up the baseline in the matrix,
\par
for example available on the FCCC Internet homepage, to calculate the credits
\par
which will accrue from the project. Jepma et al. (1998) propose to adopt default
\par
project/technology baselines with a possible differentiation by country or region.
\par
The project participants have the possibility to demand an ad hoc adjustment of the
\par
baseline for their particular case with a view to some unique circumstances. But
\par
they have to bear the costs of this procedure, while facing the risk of loosing the
\par
appeal. The disadvantage of the matrix approach is that the baselines remain `best
\par
guesses' which may lead to systematic (under- or) over-estimating the emission
\par
reduction in projects. Therefore, after some period the baselines could be veri-
\par
fied and adjusted if necessary, thereby introducing an element of \emph{ex post }baseline
\par
corrections in the matrix approach.
\par
The calculations and remarks made above provide an illustration of the potential
\par
effects of a limited number of current and alternative institutional arrangements on
\par
the cost-effectiveness of possible CDM projects. It appeared, among other things,
\par
that the amount and scope of AIJ projects in developing countries is yet too limited
\par
to make solid projections of the costs of possible future CDM projects. Further-
\par
more, the numbers presented should be viewed with caution, because the underly-
\par
ing data are incomplete, fragmented and only partly representative of future CDM
\par
projects. Nevertheless, it could be demonstrated that an alleged absence of sinks in
\par
Article 12 would be an institutional limitation which considerably lowers the cost-
\par
effectiveness of developing country projects when comparing AIJ to the CDM.
\par
On the other hand, the CDM has a competitive advantage over JI because of the
\par
banking provision in Article 12. Particularly, the AIJ figures suggest that the CDM
\par
is likely to incorporate the cheapest options available, in particular in the field of
\par
agriculture and forestry. However, this low-cost potential can only be used to a full
\par
extent if decision-makers decide to allow for sinks under the CDM and to keep the
\par
transaction costs for investors low while at the same time supporting a capacity
\par
building process to facilitate the development, maintainance and monitoring of
\par
such projects as well as the transfer of technologies. At CoP6 in November 2000
\par
in The Hague (The Netherlands) the FCCC Parties failed to reach consensus on the
\par
interpretation and elaboration of CDM Article 12.
\par
