\par
play a role in investment decisions. Firstly, an FCCC Party may decide to invest in
\par
a specific country with relatively high emission reduction costs just to enter a new
\par
market, for example for long-term technology export purposes. Secondly, spatial
\par
distance may create a bias. For example, the US may focus disproportionately
\par
on Latin America, Japan on Asia, and the EU on Central and Eastern Europe.
\par
Such behaviour could be advantageous as it appeared that countries which have
\par
concentrated their AIJ investments in the same country, region or sector, have been
\par
able to reduce transaction costs (Schwarze 1998, Ellis 1999). Thirdly, investment
\par
decisions may be (partly) based on political considerations. Examples are invest-
\par
ments in politically favoured developing countries or a policy to intensify general
\par
trading relations with certain host countries, such as Eastern European countries
\par
with an eye on their future accession to the EU.
\par
7.2. JI AND CDM: HIGH COSTS, LOW PRICES?
\par
Although the market price figures presented in Tables X and XI should only be
\par
considered as approximations of the market price in a GHG offset market based
\par
on perfect competition, they may suggest that JI and CDM projects could be re-
\par
latively expensive to set up, because the average JI cost figure (without banking)
\par
amounts to 97 \$/Mg CO2 and the CDM cost figure (without sinks) to 158 \$/Mg
\par
CO 2. The price estimates considered in Tables X and XI are lower than these cost
\par
figures. However, we have already argued before that such a comparison can be no
\par
more than an illustration, among other things, because the average project costs are
\par
only to a limited extent representative of the marginal costs of future JI and CDM
\par
options which make them incomparable with the marginal costs in the models. Fur-
\par
thermore, the average cost figures of AIJ projects are somewhat skewed upwards
\par
due to the inclusion in the pilot phase of a few relatively expensive projects that
\par
were not so much selected for their cost-effectiveness, but rather for the purpose
\par
of gaining technical and institutional experience and information. Removing the
\par
latter projects from the sample resulted in much lower cost figures (\$26 and \$9 per
\par
ton for JI and CDM respectively), which come much closer to the potential market
\par
prices found in Tables X and XI.THE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 7.3. WHEN WILL MARKET PRICES RISE OR FALL?
\par
147
\par
A market price may well turn out to be higher or lower during the commitment
\par
period than predicted by the overview of studies as presented above. Below, we
\par
will discuss several factors which may cause market prices to rise or fall.
\par
First, the predicted equilibrium prices could be too optimistic, since most of the
\par
underlying models assume that transaction costs are absent, information is com-
\par
plete and competition is perfect (cf. Hanley et al. 1997). For instance, transaction
\par
costs for JI projects and in particular for CDM projects may be considerable, which
\par
will largely depend on their institutional framework. In 1997 the Nordic Council
\par
of Ministers assessed the transaction costs of ten AIJ projects in Eastern Europe.
\par
Total transaction costs, including JI specific transaction costs such as baseline
\par
determination and GHG emission reduction monitoring, ranged from 12 to 19\%
\par
of the total initial investment in energy sector projects, and from 15 to 30\% in
\par
smaller and more complex industrial sector projects. The transaction costs for the
\par
JI acceptance procedure alone ranged from 1\% to 8\% (JIQ 1996, JIQ 1997).
\par
Second, in the long term, some argue that, in general, market prices fall as
\par
offset markets develop (e.g. Tietenberg 1992). This conjecture seems to be suppor-
\par
ted by the actual developments in the US sulfur dioxide (SO2) allowance trading
\par
scheme, in which prices have dropped from about \$ 150/Mg SO2 in 1994 to about
\par
\$ 80 Mg/SO2 in 1997, whereas the initial predictions roughly varied from about
\par
\$ 200 to 1000/Mg SO2 (e.g. Schmalensee et al. 1998). However, the domestic
\par
SO 2 market may not be a sufficient precedent for an international CO2 market,
\par
not only because their scale, coverage and costs differ, but also because market
\par
prices are likely to increase as emitters approach the end of the first commitment
\par
period. Furthermore, future commitment periods are likely to tighten commitments
\par
and restrict assigned amounts further (compared to business-as-usual emissions),
\par
thereby raising demand and, \emph{ceteris paribus}, increasing the market price. If these
\par
conjectures materialize, CO2 market prices will rise instead of fall.
\par
Third, `hot air' will rather cause the market price to drop. `Hot air' has crept into
\par
the Kyoto Protocol, because the assigned amounts for some Parties (such as Russia,
\par
the Ukraine and Australia) have been set higher than their actual and/or business-
\par
as-usual emission levels due to their negotiating power in combination with a lack
\par
of negotiation time and incomplete information. If not restricted, these Parties will
\par
be able to sell this surplus, leading to the trading of emission reductions on paper
\par
which have not taken place in practice and which are not covered by real, additional
\par
emission reductions (e.g. Hamilton 1998; Victor et al. 1998). The larger the amount
\par
of `hot air', the larger the supply of ERUs will be on the emissions market during
\par
the first commitment period, which will, therefore, lower the market price. Some
\par
observers even fear that the market price could become so low, that it may not even
\par
be profitable for project developers to implement JI and CDM projects. This could
\par
result in a situation in which projects producing real and additional GHG emission
\par
reductions are to some extent crowded out by trade in artificial emission units.148 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
Fourth, a restriction on emissions trading would lower the market price. The ne-
\par
gotiating position of the EU with respect to supplementarity prior to CoP6 roughly
\par
implied that 50\% of the commitments should be achieved domestically via a quant-
\par
itative ceiling on the Kyoto Mechanisms. More specifically, the EU proposal (EU
\par
Council, 1999) contains rules for buyers (demand) and rules for sellers (supply). A
\par
restriction on demand lowers the international market price. It also raises domestic
\par
prices, since permit importing countries would have to make larger and hence more
\par
expensive domestic reductions. The proposed trade restriction is also combined
\par
with a limit on the supply of `hot air'. Such a restriction on supply would increase
\par
the market price again, but most analysts seem to agree that the overall supply and
\par
demand effects of the EU proposal is primarily a lower market price (Zhang 2000).
\par
Fifth, an inclusion of sinks under the CDM (assuming its current absence)
\par
would magnify supply and thus lower the market price, whereas an exclusion
\par
of sinks from JI (as proposed by some observers) would reduce supply and thus
\par
increase the market price. Sinks under JI and the CDM are likely to attract many
\par
investors, mainly because forestry projects are known for their large supply and
\par
low-cost potential. Such an increase in demand for forestry projects may raise
\par
their credit price, in particular if the supply of suitable land appears to be finite, if
\par
new plantations are increasingly forced onto land of lower productivity and if the
\par
cheapest options gradually disappear when they are implemented first (Hamilton
\par
1998).
\par
Sixthly, demand for GHG offsets and thus their market price will rise if pol-
\par
luting firms in industrialized countries hedge against the possibility that they will
\par
not be able to meet their domestically imposed emission targets. Their hedging
\par
behaviour will be stronger if they are subject to fixed emission ceilings as in trade-
\par
able allowance schemes, and weaker if they are subject to relative targets as in
\par
benchmarking covenants with energy-efficiency norms. Hedging will also increase
\par
if emitters are more risk-averse.
\par
Seventhly, technological progress will be one of the most important determin-
\par
ants of the market price: rapid progress will lower its price. For given technologies
\par
in use, the demand for allowances and thus the market price is expected to rise
\par
(/fall), for instance if GHG emitting activities increase (/decrease), if the current
\par
costs of abatement options at home are high or increase (/low or decrease) and
\par
if the level and speed of technological innovation is lower (/higher) than expec-
\par
ted. Unfortunately, any prediction of technological change is surrounded by large
\par
uncertainties, although economists (top-down approach) generally are thought to
\par
have less faith in such progress than engineers (bottom-up approach). Nevertheless,
\par
it may be illustrative that unforeseen technological progress is seen as one of the
\par
factors which caused an allowance price to emerge on the US SO2 market which
\par
is lower than initially predicted (Carlson et al. 1998).
\par
Although there are several factors, described above, which may cause market
\par
prices to rise or fall, the fact remains that any prediction of future price devel-
\par
opments or price levels is surrounded by uncertainties. Moreover, some of theTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 149
\par
examples mentioned above make clear that the market price will depend on the
\par
decisions by policy-makers concerning the design of the flexible instruments.
\par
\textbf{8. Conclusions}
\par
Project-based emissions trading in the form of JI and CDM has been incorporated
\par
in the Kyoto Protocol to stimulate the cost-effectiveness of international climate
\par
change policy. The objective of this paper was to provide both qualitative and
\par
quantitative indications of the potential effects of current and alternative institu-
\par
tional arrangements (defined as design characteristics of intended or implemented
\par
policy) on the cost-effectiveness of JI and CDM projects. To do so, we presented
\par
numerical illustrations by using (and criticizing) the data on the emissions reduc-
\par
tion costs of 94 AIJ (Activities Implemented Jointly) pilot phase projects. The data
\par
have been derived from the URFs submitted by AIJ project partners to the FCCC
\par
Secretariat. This poses a substantial limitation to the empirical analysis, because
\par
AIJ projects are \emph{not }fully representative of JI and CDM projects. For instance
\par
because the AIJ data are incomplete and because AIJ projects are not credited in
\par
contrast with JI and the CDM.
\par
Nevertheless, the AIJ cost data still can give us some idea of the cost-effective-
\par
ness of JI and CDM projects for several reasons. First, both AIJ and JI as well as
\par
the CDM are market-based mechanisms. Second, the AIJ data reported in the URFs
\par
has improved over the years. Third, the wide range of AIJ credit costs, varying from
\par
cheap to expensive, probably provides a relevant picture of the cost-effectiveness of
\par
project-based emissions trading. Fourth, although our calculations of average costs
\par
for various samples of AIJ projects are not equal to the marginal costs of JI and
\par
CDM projects, it is still likely that their marginal costs are higher if the average
\par
costs of a certain AIJ project region or type are higher than the average costs of
\par
another. However, we clearly separate credit costs from credit prices which are
\par
determined by both supply and demand.
\par
On the basis of the valuable AIJ cost data, we provide numerical illustrations of
\par
what different policy designs could do with JI and CDM credit costs during the first
\par
commitment period. Although we had to make various simplifying assumptions,
\par
our approach has some advantages. In contrast with theoretical reflections and frag-
\par
mented case studies, our analysis is largely based on detailed empirical data from
\par
almost one-hundred real-life intended or implemented emission reduction projects.
\par
Moreover, in contrast with economic studies neglecting the institutional context of
\par
project-based emissions trading, we considered the potential impact of the design
\par
of the Kyoto Protocol, as well as some alternative designs, on the cost-effectiveness
\par
of JI and the CDM.
\par
First, we have calculated possible JI and CDM costs per unit of emission re-
\par
duction on the basis of reported AIJ data as well as by presenting an average
\par
so-called `unadjusted' AIJ cost figure by simply dividing the total amount of invest-150 EDWIN WOERDMAN AND WYTZE VAN DER GAAST
\par
ment funds by the total GHG emission reduction for each project. The underlying
\par
assumption, which has been thoroughly criticized, is that the AIJ projects are suf-
\par
ficiently representative of future JI and CDM projects. Our data set yielded an
\par
average unadjusted AIJ emission reduction unit cost figure of 46 \$/Mg CO2-eq.
\par
The project partners themselves reported lower costs which turned out to be 28
\par
\$/Mg CO2-eq on average. This difference can be partly explained by the fact that
\par
some project developers have not reported costs based on the total invested amount,
\par
but rather on the financial contribution of the project developer or on the project's
\par
hardware only.
\par
Second, we have provided numerical illustrations of the specific institutional
\par
arrangements of the Kyoto Protocol which does not allow for banking under JI
\par
in contrast with the CDM, whereas JI Article 6 explicitly incorporates sinks in
\par
contrast with CDM. We have calculated the possible effects of these design features
\par
on potential JI as well as CDM credit costs by adjusting the AIJ projects on the
\par
basis of simple mathematical formulas. According to our formulas, the average
\par
credit costs for a JI project could become rather high (97 \$/Mg CO2-eq), compared
\par
to average AIJ cost data for projects in Central and Eastern Europe (40 \$/Mg CO2-
\par
eq), due to the absence of banking. Consequently, an alternative design of including
\par
banking -- or: `early JI action' -- in the context of JI, would lower these credit costs
\par
substantially (37 \$/Mg CO2-eq).
\par
The amount and scope of AIJ projects in developing countries is yet too limited
\par
to make firm projections of the costs of possible future CDM projects. Neverthe-
\par
less, our figures illustrate that the possibility of banking under the CDM lowers
\par
its average potential credit costs, but these are raised again if the Kyoto Protocol
\par
implies that sinks are exluded from the CDM (158 \$/Mg CO2-eq). This figure is
\par
high compared to the JI average (97 \$/Mg CO2-eq), but the average obscures the
\par
fact that there are several potential CDM projects which are more cost-effective
\par
than JI projects. In particular, when considering the low-cost options (calculated by
\par
excluding AIJ projects with costs per unit of emission reduction of 100 \$/Mg CO2-
\par
eq and higher), the CDM appears to have a huge potential of cheap projects (9 \$/Mg
\par
CO 2-eq), in particular in the field of agriculture and forestry, compared to JI (26
\par
\$/Mg CO2-eq). Furthermore, an alternative institutional arrangement, namely the
\par
explicit inclusion of sinks under the CDM, would lower the credit costs again (103
\par
\$/Mg CO2-eq), also in the realm of pre-defined low-cost options (6 \$/Mg CO2-
\par
eq). The calculations suggest that the low-cost potential in developing countries is
\par
bigger than in Central and Eastern Europe, but also that the cost-effectiveness of
\par
such projects will be strongly affected by the particular institutional arrangements
\par
of the CDM.
\par
Not only the restricted status and rigour of the micro-level AIJ cost data, but
\par
also the fact that credit costs are not credit prices poses a limitation to our analysis.
\par
The invested amount per unit of emission reduction is not necessarily equal to
\par
the price for which the credit will be sold on a global GHG offset market, which
\par
will be determined by both supply and demand. To give the reader some idea ofTHE IMPACT OF INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS ON COST-EFFECTIVENESS 151
\par
a potential market price, we have calculated an average equilibrium permit price
\par
or tax level based on a survey of twenty macro-level emissions trading simulations
\par
and optimal taxation models, such as GREEN, Global 2100, RICE, ER, PRIMES
\par
and MERGE, which roughly turned out to be about 20 to 30 \$/Mg CO2. We have
\par
also described several factors which may cause this potential market price to rise
\par
or fall. For instance, the price will decrease if the supply of `hot air' turns out to be
\par
large or if the level of technological progress is high, whereas a restriction on the
\par
supply of `hot air', as proposed by the EU prior to CoP6, will increase its price.
\par
The institutional arrangements of JI and the CDM have a decisive impact on
\par
their cost-effectiveness. Alternative designs of the flexible instruments have dif-
\par
ferent effects on the costs, and possibly on the prices, of JI and CDM credits.
\par
Decision-makers thus hold the key to the success or failure of project-based emis-
\par
sions trading.
