\par
Multi-sensor management for information fusion:
\par
issues and approaches
\par
N. Xiong, P. Svensson*
\par
Department of Data and Information Fusion, FOI, S-172 90 Stockholm, Sweden
\par
Received 2 July 2001; received in revised form 28 September 2001; accepted 18 October 2001
\par
Abstract
\par
Multi-sensor management concerns the control of environment perception activities by managing or coordinating the usage of
\par
multiple sensor resources. It is an emerging research area, which has become increasingly important in research and development of
\par
modern multi-sensor systems. This paper presents a comprehensive review of multi-sensor management in relation to multi-sensor
\par
information fusion, describing its place and role in the larger context, generalizing main problems from existing application needs,
\par
and highlighting problem solving methodologies. \'O 2002 Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved.
\par
Keywords: Sensor management; Multi-sensor system; Data fusion; Information fusion; Sensor deployment; Sensor behavior assignment; Sensor
\par
coordination
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Multi-sensor systems are becoming increasingly im-
\par
portant in a variety of military and civilian applica-
\par
tions. Since a single sensor generally can only perceive
\par
limited partial information about the environment,
\par
multiple similar and/or dissimilar sensors are required
\par
to provide sufficient local pictures with different focus
\par
and from different viewpoints in an integrated manner.
\par
Further, information from heterogeneous sensors can
\par
be combined using data fusion algorithms to obtain
\par
synergistic observation effects. Thus, the benefits of
\par
multi-sensor systems are to broaden machine percep-
\par
tion and enhance awareness of the state of the world
\par
compared to what could be acquired by a single sensor
\par
system.
\par
With the advancements in sensor technology, sensors
\par
are becoming more agile. Also, more of them are needed
\par
in a scenario in response to the increasingly intricate
\par
nature of the environment to be sensed. The increased
\par
sophistication of sensor assets along with the large
\par
amounts of data to be processed has pushed the infor-
\par
mation acquisition problem far beyond what can be
\par
*Corresponding author. Tel.: +46-855-50-3696;
\par
fax: +46-855-50-3686.
\par
E-mail addresses: n.xiong@ieee.org (N. Xiong), pers@foi.se
\par
(P. Svensson).
\par
1566-2535/02/\$ - see front matter \'O 2002 Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved.
\par
PII: S1566-2535(02)00055-6
\par
handled by a human operator. This motivates the
\par
emerging interest in research into automatic and semi-
\par
automatic management of sensor resources for im-
\par
proving overall perception performance beyond basic
\par
fusion of data.
\par
1.1. The fundamental purpose of sensor management
\par
Multi-sensor management is formally described as a
\par
system or process that seeks to manage or coordinate
\par
the usageof a suite ofsensors ormeasurement devices in
\par
a dynamic, uncertain environment, to improve the per-
\par
formance of data fusion and ultimately that of percep-
\par
tion. It is also beneficial to avoid overwhelming storage
\par
and computational requirements in a sensor and data
\par
rich environment by controlling the data gathering
\par
process such that only the truly necessary data are col-
\par
lected and stored [60]. The why and what issues of both
\par
single-sensor and multi-sensor management were thor-
\par
oughly discussed in the papers [1,7,53,54,56]. To reiter-
\par
ate, the basic objective of sensor management is to select
\par
the right sensors to do the right service on the right
\par
object at the right time. The sensor manager is respon-
\par
sible for answering questions like:
\par
{$\bullet$} Which observation tasks are to be performed and
\par
what are their priorities?
\par
{$\bullet$} How many sensors are required to meet an informa-
\par
tion request?164 N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186
\par
{$\bullet$} When are extra sensors to be deployed and in which
\par
locations?
\par
{$\bullet$} Which sensor sets are to be applied to which tasks?
\par
{$\bullet$} What is the action or mode sequence for a particular
\par
sensor?
\par
{$\bullet$} What parameter values should be selected for the op-
\par
eration of sensors?
\par
The simplest job of sensor management is to choose
\par
the optimal sensor parameter values given one or more
\par
sensors with respect to a given task, see for example, the
\par
paper [72]. This is also called active perception where
\par
sensors are to be configured optimally for a specific
\par
purpose. More general problems of (multi-)sensor man-
\par
agement are, however, related to decisions about what
\par
sensors to use and for which purposes, as well as when
\par
and where to use them. Widely acknowledged is the fact
\par
that it is not realistic to continually observe everything
\par
in the environment and therefore selective perception
\par
becomes necessary, requiring the sensor management
\par
system to decide when to sense what and with which
\par
sensors. Typical temporal complexities, which must be
\par
accommodated in the sensor management process, were
\par
discussed in [46].
\par
respect to identified intelligence requirements. Sensor
\par
management, aiming at improving data fusion perfor-
\par
mance by controlling sensor behavior, plays the role of
\par
level 4 functions in the JDL model.
\par
Sensor management indeed provides information
\par
feedback from data fusion results to sensor operations
\par
[7,54]. The representation of the data fusion process as a
\par
feedback closed-loop structure is depicted in Fig. 1,
\par
where the sensor manager on level 4 uses the informa-
\par
tion from levels 0--3 to plan future sensor actions. The
\par
feedback is intended to improve the data collection
\par
process with expected benefits of earlier detection, im-
\par
proved tracking, and more reliable identification, or to
\par
confirm what might be tactically inferred from previ-
\par
ously gathered evidences. Timeliness is a necessary re-
\par
quirement on the feedback management of sensors for
\par
fast adaptation to environment changes. That is to say,
\par
a prompt decision on sensor functions has to be made
\par
before the development of the tactical situation has
\par
made such a decision obsolete.
\par
As a categorization of process refinement, Steinberg
\par
and Bowman [68] classified responses of resources
\par
(including sensors) as reflexive, feature-based, entity-
\par
relation based, context-sensitive, cost-sensitive, and re-
\par
action-sensitive, in terms of input data/information
\par
types. This categorization is viewed as an expansion of
\par
Dasarathy's model [18] in which data fusion functions
\par
are subdivided considering merely data, features and
\par
objects as possible input/output types.
\par
1.2. The role of sensor management in information fusion
\par
Sensor management merits incorporation in infor-
\par
mation fusion processes. Although terminology has not
\par
yet fully stabilized, it is generally acknowledged that
\par
information fusion is a collective concept comprising
\par
situation assessment (level 2), threat or impact assess-
\par
ment (level 3) and process refinement (level 4) in the so-
\par
called JDL model of data fusion [76]. As pointed out in
\par
[70], in addition to intelligence interpretation, informa-
\par
tion fusion should be equipped with techniques for
\par
proactive or reactive planning and management of own
\par
collection resources such as sensors and sensor plat-
\par
forms, in order to make best use of these assets with
\par
1.3. Multi-sensor management architectures
\par
The architecture of a multi-sensor management sys-
\par
tem is closely related to the form of data fusion unit.
\par
Typically there are three alternatives for system struc-
\par
ture, namely,
\par
1. Centralized.Inacentralizedparadigmthedatafusion
\par
unit is treated as a central mechanism. It collects in-
\par
Fig. 1. Feedback connection via sensor manager in a data fusion process.N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186 formation from all different platforms and sensors
\par
and decides jobs that must be accomplished by indi-
\par
vidual sensors. All commands sent from the fusion
\par
center to respective sensors must be accepted and fol-
\par
lowed with proper sensor actions.
\par
2. Decentralized. In a decentralized system data are
\par
fused locally with a set of local agents rather than
\par
by a central unit. In this case, every sensor or plat-
\par
form can be viewed as an intelligent asset having
\par
some degree of autonomy in decision-making. Sensor
\par
coordination is achieved based on communication in
\par
the network of agents, in which sensors share locally
\par
fused information and cooperate with each other.
\par
Durrant-Whyte and Stevens [23] stated that decen-
\par
tralized data fusion exhibits many attractive proper-
\par
ties by being:
\par
{$\bullet$} scalable in structure without being constrained by
\par
centralized computational bottlenecks or commu-
\par
nicational bandwidth limitations;
\par
{$\bullet$} survivable in the face of on-line loss of sensing
\par
nodes and to dynamic changes of the network;
\par
{$\bullet$} modular in the design and implementation of fu-
\par
sion nodes.
\par
However, the effect of redundant information is a seri-
\par
ous problem that may arise in decentralized data fusion
\par
networks [16]. It is not possible, within most filtering
\par
frameworks, to combine information pieces from mul-
\par
tiple sources unless they are independent or have known
\par
cross-covariance [36]. Moreover, without any common
\par
communication facility, data exchange in such a net-
\par
work must be carried out strictly on a node-to-node
\par
basis. A delay between sender and receiver could result
\par
in transient inconsistencies of the global state among
\par
different parts of the network, causing degradation of
\par
overall performance [28].
\par
3. Hierarchical. This can be regarded as a mixture of
\par
centralized and decentralized architectures. In a hier-
\par
165
\par
archicalsystem there areusuallyseverallevels ofhier-
\par
archy in which the top level functions as the global
\par
fusion center and the lowest level consists of several
\par
local fusion centers[56].Everylocal fusion node isre-
\par
sponsible for management of a sensor subset. The
\par
partitioning of the whole sensor assembly into differ-
\par
ent groups can be realized based on either sensors'
\par
geographical locations or platforms, sensor functions
\par
performed, or sensor data delivered (to ensure com-
\par
mensurate data from the same sensor group).
\par
Two interesting instances of hybrid and hierarchical
\par
sensor management architectures are given in the fol-
\par
lowing for illustration.
\par
The macro/micro architecture proposed by [7] can be
\par
classifiedasatwo-levelhierarchicalsystem.Itconsistsof
\par
a macro sensor manager playing a central role and a set
\par
of micro sensor managers residing with respective sen-
\par
sors. The macro sensor manager is in charge of high-
\par
level strategic decisions about how to best utilize the
\par
available sensing resources to achieve the mission ob-
\par
jectives. The micro sensor manager schedules the tactics
\par
of a particular sensor to best carry out the requests from
\par
the macro manager. Thus it is clear that every managed
\par
sensor needs its own micro manager.
\par
Another hybrid distributed and hierarchical ap-
\par
proach was suggested in [60] for sensor-rich environ-
\par
ments exemplified by an aircraft health and usage
\par
monitoring system. The main idea is to distribute the
\par
management function across system functional or phys-
\par
ical boundaries with global oversight of mission goals
\par
and information requests. One such model for man-
\par
agement of numerous sensors is shown in Fig. 2. At the
\par
top of the model is the mission manager tasked with
\par
converting mission goals to information needs, which
\par
are then mapped by the information instantiator into a
\par
set of measurement patterns in accordance with those
\par
needs. The role of the meta-manager is to enable natural
\par
subdivision of a single manager into a set of mostly
\par
Fig. 2. A distributed and hierarchical sensor management model for a sensor rich environment (cited from [60]).166 N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186
\par
independent local resource managers each being re-
\par
sponsible for a particular sensor subset. Occasionally,
\par
these local managers need to be coordinated by the
\par
meta-manager if there is a request for information,
\par
which cannot be satisfied by a single sensor suite. A
\par
major difficulty in implementing this hybrid architecture
\par
of sensor management lies with the meta-manager. It is
\par
not yet obvious how to best translate global functional
\par
needs into a set of local resource managers and how to
\par
coordinate the disparate local managers distributed
\par
across functional or physical boundaries.
\par
1.4. Classification of multi-sensor management problems
\par
Multi-sensor management is a broad concept refer-
\par
ring to a set of distinct issues of planning and control of
\par
sensor resource usage to enhance multi-sensor data fu-
\par
sionperformance.Variousaspectsofthisareahavebeen
\par
discussed in papers in the open literature. Generally,
\par
these problems fall into three main categories, i.e., sen-
\par
sor deployment, sensor behavior assignment, and sensor
\par
coordination.
\par
1.4.1. Sensor deployment
\par
Sensor deployment is a critical issue for intelligence
\par
collection in an uncertain dynamic environment. It
\par
concerns making decisions about when, where, and how
\par
many sensing resources need to be deployed in reaction
\par
to the state of the world and its changes. In some situ-
\par
ations, it should be beneficial to proactively deploy
\par
sensing resources according to a predicted situation de-
\par
velopment tendency in order to get prepared to observe
\par
an event, which is likely to happen in the upcoming
\par
period.
\par
Sensorplacement[59]needsspecialattentioninsensor
\par
deployment. It consists of positioning multiple sensors
\par
simultaneously in optimal or near optimal locations to
\par
supportsurveillancetaskswhen necessary.Typicallyitis
\par
desired to locate sensors within a particular region de-
\par
termined by tactical situations to optimize a certain
\par
criterion usually expressed in terms of global detection
\par
probability, quality of tracks, etc. This problem can be
\par
formulated as one of constrained optimization of a set
\par
of parameters. It is subject to constraints due to the
\par
following factors:
\par
{$\bullet$} sensors are usually restricted to specified regions due
\par
to tactical considerations;
\par
{$\bullet$} critical restrictions may be imposed on relative posi-
\par
tions of adjacent sensors to enable their mutual com-
\par
munication when sensors are arranged as distributed
\par
assets in a decentralized network;
\par
{$\bullet$} the amount of sensing resources that can be posi-
\par
tioned in a given period is limited due to logistical re-
\par
strictions.
\par
In simple cases, decisions on sensor placement are to
\par
be made with respect to a well-prescribed and stationary
\par
environment. As examples, we may consider such ap-
\par
plication scenarios as:
\par
{$\bullet$} placing radars to minimize the terrain screening effect
\par
in detection of an aircraft approaching a fixed site;
\par
{$\bullet$} arrangement of a network of intelligence gathering
\par
assets in a specified region to target another well-
\par
defined area.
\par
In the above scenarios, mathematical or physical
\par
models such as terrain models, propagation models, etc.
\par
are commonly available and they are used as the basis
\par
for evaluation of sensor placement decisions.
\par
More challenging are those situations in which the
\par
environment is dynamic and sensors must repeatedly be
\par
repositioned to be able to refine and update the state
\par
estimation of moving targets in real time. Typical situ-
\par
ations where reactive sensor placement is required are:
\par
{$\bullet$} submarinetrackingbymeans ofpassivesonobuoysin
\par
an anti-submarine warfare scenario;
\par
{$\bullet$} locating moving transmitters using ESM (electronic
\par
support measures) receivers;
\par
{$\bullet$} tracking of tanks on land by dropping passive acous-
\par
tic sensors.
\par
1.4.2. Sensor behavior assignment
\par
The basic purpose of sensor management is to adapt
\par
sensor behavior to dynamic environments. By sensor
\par
behavior assignment is meant efficient determination and
\par
planning of sensor functions and usage according to
\par
changing situation awareness or mission requirements.
\par
Two crucial points are involved here:
\par
1. Decisions about the set of observation tasks (referred
\par
as system-level tasks) that the sensor system is sup-
\par
posed to accomplish currently or in the near future,
\par
on grounds of the current/predicted situation as well
\par
as the given mission goal;
\par
2. Planning and scheduling of actions of the deployed
\par
sensors to best accomplish the proposed observation
\par
tasks and their objectives.
\par
Owing to limited sensing resources, it is prevalent in
\par
real applications that available sensors are not able to
\par
serve all desired tasks and achieve all their associated
\par
objectives simultaneously. Therefore a reasonable com-
\par
