2. A perspective on sensor management problem solving
\par
2.1. Principles and methodological considerations
\par
Inherently the purpose of sensor management is to
\par
optimize the data fusion performance by feedback
\par
control of sensor resources. The performance index was
\par
called figure of merit in [7]. Its definition depends on
\par
what onewishestooptimize, typicalquantities including
\par
probability of target detection, track/identification ac-
\par
curacy, probability of loss-of-track, probability of sur-
\par
vival, probability of target kill, etc. Basically, we desire
\par
the performance index established for optimization to
\par
transcend the diversity of sensors and to be analytically/
\par
computationally tractable. Further, in order to avoid
\par
potentially myopic sensor management strategies, a
\par
prospective figure of merit should take into account
\par
both short-term and long-term interests so as to arrive
\par
at a good balance in final outcomes. Optimization
\par
according to long-term objectives is critical to the de-
\par
velopment of global improvements of the perception
\par
process with respect to an evolving scenario. Recent
\par
interesting research efforts to realize non-myopic sensor
\par
management functionality can be found in [35,71,73,77].
\par
From the viewpoint of decision theory, sensor man-
\par
agement is a decision making task to determine the most
\par
appropriate sensor action to perform in order to achieve
\par
maximum utility. Such a decision problem was treated
\par
in [41] based upon a Bayesian decision tree where dis-
\par
tinct perception actions were assessed against each other
\par
in terms of combined costs and benefits. Fung et al. [24]
\par
and Musick and Malhotra [54] discussed the applica-
\par
bility of influence diagrams as a graphical modeling
\par
technique in support of decision analysis for the selec-
\par
tion of sensor action. Decision making for sensor
\par
management tends to be a sequential process in the
\par
sense that intermediate decisions have to be made while
\par
a dynamic situation evolves and where penalties and
\par
awards of decisions made are only revealed over time. It
\par
has been recognized that in many cases sensor man-
\par
agement can be modeled as a Markov decision problem
\par
subject to uncertainty, solutions to which were studied
\par
by Castanon [14,15] based on stochastic dynamic pro-
\par
gramming. Although dynamic programming offers a
\par
mathematically tractable structure to find the optimum
\par
sequence of decisions, it is likely to suffer from combi-
\par
natorial explosion when solving practical problems of
\par
even moderate size.
\par
By viewing sensors as constrained communication
\par
channels [33], sensor management is intended to control
\par
the functions of such channels to provide the maximum
\par
quantity of useful information within a limited time
\par
period. That is to say, we would like to optimize the
\par
information passed through sensor channels by proper
\par
arrangement of their operations. Since uncertainty
\par
about the environment is reduced by means of a mea-
\par
surement, each sensor action has the potential merit of
\par
contributing information as it is performed. The key
\par
point here is how to assess the relative merits of candi-
\par
date sensing plans in terms of information gained or
\par
uncertainty reduced. One direct means serving this
\par
purpose is to borrow the concept of entropy from in-
\par
formation theory as a measure of the uncertainty about
\par
the state of the environment. In this way, we can
\par
quantify the amount of information gained due to ac-
\par
complishment of a sensing plan as either entropy change
\par
(in Shannon's definition) or cross-entropy (in Kullback--
\par
Leibler's definition), see Section 6.2.
\par
In recent years, entropy-based information metrics
\par
have been adopted in many studies of sensor manage-
\par
ment in various scenarios, including: controlling a single
\par
sensor to track multiple targets [33], sensor-target pair-
\par
ings in multi-sensor and multi-target tracking [19,61,62],
\par
search area determination [39], and search versus track
\par
trade-offs in a simulated environment [49]. However, all
\par
these papers consider only the amount of informa-
\par
tion rather than the value of information. They aim to
\par
maximize the quantity of information provided by sen-
\par
sors without taking into account whether the informa-
\par
tion derived is useful or interesting. Occasionally, this
\par
might lead to directing sensor attention to be paid to
\par
non-significant aspects of the environment in order to
\par
reach a superficial maximum of information attainment.
\par
Further research is thus needed to develop more com-
\par
prehensive information measures, in order to drive
\par
sensor management towards optimization in terms of
\par
the utility of obtained information with respect to mis-
\par
sion goals rather than merely the gained amount of in-
\par
formation [3]. Regarding recent efforts in this direction
\par
we note the attempts by [32,48], in which goal lattices
\par
were employed to prioritize observation tasks in terms
\par
of their mission-accomplishing value rather than the
\par
quantity of obtained information.
\par
As sensor management plays the role of feedback
\par
controlling sensor resources, the development of opti-
\par
mal control laws and strategies suitable for real appli-
\par
cations is a challenging problem. Pre-designing a
\par
management algorithm ensuring best control behavior
\par
at all times is desirable but can be extremely difficult due
\par
to high uncertainty and complexity of the environment
\par
to be sensed. A perhaps more feasible way of achieving
\par
optimality of sensor management performance is to in-
\par
corporate machine learning techniques to enable sensor
\par
managers to learn from experience. In principle, there
\par
are two general classes of machine learning useful to
\par
arrive at optimal management strategies, namely, off-
\par
line learning and on-line learning. Off-line learning, asN. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186 practiced in [40,65,66], attempts to generalize valuable
\par
control (management) knowledge from a sufficient
\par
amount of examples. It is actually a form of inductive
\par
learning, which desires a comprehensive training dat-
\par
abase of scenarios covering a wide range of situations.
\par
Using a database with only a small number of scenarios
\par
could result in a sensor manager that will be effective in
\par
some cases but ineffective in others. On the other hand,
\par
on-line learning represents the idea of the sensor man-
\par
ager directly learning to manage sensing assets through
\par
its interactions with the environment. This mode of
\par
learning is more difficult to realize than off-line learning
\par
in that an assessment signal has to be created for every
\par
sensing action conducted, in order for management
\par
strategies to be immediately updated upon completion
\par
of a perception. In particular, reinforcement learning
\par
techniques [8,47] were found effective for on-line learn-
\par
ing of sensor search strategies for static targets. Gener-
\par
ally speaking, despite some preliminary work done (as
\par
mentioned above), machine learning for sensor man-
\par
agement is still an under-researched area and much
\par
room remains for further study in this direction.
\par
In addition, in the authors' view, behavior-based ar-
\par
tificial intelligence [13] could be a useful asset in infor-
\par
mation fusion process refinement. As it is known that
\par
behavior-basedsystems[2]exhibitstrongrobustnessand
\par
flexibility against a dynamically changing world, incor-
\par
porating reactive skills in sensor management would
\par
enable the perception system to have reflexes for coping
\par
with eventual deviations and to recover instantaneously
\par
from unexpected events. In realizing this purpose, a
\par
deliberative-reactive architecture needs to be established
\par
inthe process refinement level forintegration ofabstract
\par
planning and behavior-based reactive control in a co-
\par
herent manner.
\par
2.2. Top-down problem solving by a sensor manager
\par
As we know, sensor management is a complex pro-
\par
cedure intended to guide the information collection
\par
process with respect to a broad range of activities. It is
\par
not realistic for a sensor manager to solve its complete
\par
problem by a single mechanism, because the huge and
\par
perplexing space of possible alternatives tends to make
\par
this goal infeasible in real time. On the other hand,
\par
problem solving for sensor management can be made
\par
much easier and more effectiveby partitioning tasks into
\par
activities along different layers. Thus, a sensor manager
\par
mightfollowatop-downpolicyandproceedstepbystep
\par
from meta-issues to detailed reasoning. The following
\par
five activity levels would be characteristic of a sensor
\par
management system having a comprehensive function-
\par
ality along these lines:
\par
Level 4 (mission planning). This is the highest level of
\par
sensor management responsible for deciding system-
\par
169
\par
level tasks based on information generated internally or
\par
externally. It concerns meta-sensor management issues
\par
such as:
\par
{$\bullet$} Which services to perform (e.g. search, target track-
\par
ing or target updating)?
\par
{$\bullet$} Which accuracy level (e.g. desirable error covariance)
\par
to aim at?
\par
{$\bullet$} How frequently to measure?
\par
{$\bullet$} On which area of the environment to focus?
\par
{$\bullet$} Which targets to select?
\par
{$\bullet$} How to rank the importance or priorities of the re-
\par
quired tasks?
\par
Mission planning plays the role of indirectly managing
\par
the behavior of the sensor system but does not deal with
\par
implementation details. Meta reasoning is required here,
\par
to be conducted according to results from other data
\par
fusion functions. At the same time it must also provide
\par
access to human operators, enabling them to impose
\par
their special requests when necessary.
\par
Level 3 (resource deployment). This management level
\par
is needed for surveillance purposes in a dynamic, un-
\par
certain environment. In such cases, new extra sensing
\par
devices have to be deployed whenever necessary in order
\par
to upgrade sensing capabilities or to catch up with quick
\par
environment changes. This involves proactive or reac-
\par
tive planning of sensor assets to be deployed in a sce-
\par
nario. More concretely, thelevel of resource deployment
\par
is responsible for answering questions such as:
\par
{$\bullet$} When are extra sensors required and how many?
\par
{$\bullet$} Where to place the newly required sensors?
\par
Level 2 (resource planning). The purpose of this level
\par
is to propose requests on individual sensors by deciding
\par
what jobs/actionsare expectedtobe performed bythem.
\par
Typical activities on this level are:
\par
{$\bullet$} sensor selection for multi-sensor multi-target track-
\par
ing;
\par
{$\bullet$} sensor allocation for simultaneous classification of
\par
numerous objects;
\par
{$\bullet$} sensor cueing (sensor handing over, targetacquisition
\par
by another aiding sensor);
\par
{$\bullet$} movement planning for mobile sensors and plat-
\par
forms;
\par
{$\bullet$} negotiation and cooperation between sensors in a de-
\par
centralized sensor network.
\par
Level 1 (sensor scheduling). This level has the role to
\par
set up a timeline of commands for every sensor to carry
\par
out based on sensor availability and capabilities, given
\par
job/action requests from level 2. Each sensor is assigned
\par
with a detailed schedule on what to do in each time170 N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186
\par
interval. This is a non-trivial problem considering the
\par
distinct characteristics of jobs/actions to be performed,
\par
such as hard or fluid deadlines, as well as varying job/
\par
action priorities. As important solutions to scheduling
\par
we refer to such methods as brick packing [7], best first
\par
[7], genetic search [17], OGUPSA [78], and the enhanced
\par
version of OGUPSA [51] with its usage demonstrated in
\par
a sensor management simulation [50].
