3. Principles of sensor placement
\par
3.1. General description
\par
As stated in the problem description in Section 1.4.1,
\par
sensor placement is viewed as a parameter optimiza-
\par
tion problem possibly subject to constraints. Two basic
\par
points are of concern here, namely, the goal function
\par
for placement and the strategy to find optimal or near-
\par
optimal solutions. In order to keep pace with environ-
\par
ment changes, e.g., the movements of targets, the
\par
optimization algorithms adopted must not impose a
\par
highcomputationalload.Threeoptimizationalgorithms
\par
that have been employed for real-time sensor placement
\par
aregradientdescentusedin[57,58],greedylocalsearchin
\par
[77], and simulated annealing in [59]. Worth noting is the
\par
fact that both greedy search and simulated annealing
\par
have the sometimes-important merit of requiring no
\par
derivatives of the objective function in their search
\par
procedures. Various optimization criteria for sensor
\par
placement have been suggested. Generally they can be
\par
classified into short-term and long-term strategies de-
\par
pending on whether future performance is taken into
\par
account in the objective function.
\par
Short-term sensor placement seeks to optimize some
\par
performance measure of the fusion system at the current
\par
stage but does not consider its future behavior. In view
\par
of this, sensor positions are determined simply accord-
\par
ing to one of the following criteria:
\par
{$\bullet$} maximizing the probability of detection of the target
\par
[57,58],
\par
{$\bullet$} minimizing the standard deviation in the target esti-
\par
mates [58],
\par
{$\bullet$} maximizing the figure of merit for the sensor net [59].
\par
Long-term sensor placement seeks to prolong the usage
\par
of deployed sensor(s) by taking care of possible future
\par
paths. Such an attempt complies with the general prin-
\par
ciple that sensor management should ``value long-termN. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186 171
\par
goals of survival and success, not just accuracy and
\par
identity'' [54]. Two examples of long-term placement are
\par
described in [34,35,71] and [77], respectively. They both
\par
aim to find sensor locations such that the expected
\par
variance of the target state will be kept below a pre-
\par
scribed limit for as many consecutive time steps as
\par
possible after the present one. However, they differ in
\par
the state estimation methods used. The work by the FOI
\par
group applies Kalman filtering to identify target states
\par
so that it is computationally more efficient. On the other
\par
hand, the method by Williams may be more generally
\par
applicable due to the employment of particle filtering
\par
methodology.
\par
It bears mentioning that sensor placement in this
\par
context only refers to the issue of determining the po-
\par
sitions where sensors are initially located. It does not
\par
involve planning the movements of mobile sensors after
\par
their deployment, which we believe is a kind of sensor
\par
behavior assignment rather than sensor deployment. By
\par
the same token, motion planning for movable sensors
\par
should be a function belonging to level two (resource
\par
planning) of the top-down procedure for sensor man-
\par
agement proposed in Section 2.2. This is an under-
\par
addressed topic but challenging for future research.
\par
3.2. Signal filtering related to sensor placement
\par
In target tracking applications, the sensor placement
\par
algorithm is inherently related to the filtering algorithm
\par
used for state estimation. The most commonly used is
\par
the Kalman filter, which assumes linear system models
\par
with additive Gaussian noise. A more recently investi-
\par
gated alternative is the particle filter [27] employed by
\par
[58,77] for sensor placement in submarine tracking. The
\par
latter has the advantage of being able to handle any
\par
functional non-linearity and any distribution of system
\par
or measurement noise. Despite seeming quite different,
\par
both filtering approaches can be formulated within the
\par
general framework of recursive Bayesian estimation.
\par
The Bayesian signal filter consists of essentially two
\par
stages: prediction and update, in order to construct the
\par
probability density function (pdf) of the state based on
\par
all available information. The prediction stage uses the
\par
system model to make prediction of the state at the next
\par
time step, and later the predicted state pdf is modified in
\par
the update stage using the newly acquired data from
\par
measurement. The update operation is an application of
\par
Bayes' theorem, which provides a mechanism to update
\par
prior knowledge in light of new evidence.
\par
