4.2. Evaluation using neural networks
\par
Komorniczak and Pietrasinki [40] proposed to assign
\par
target priorities using a neural network, which accepts
\par
features of detected targets as its inputs and offers im-
\par
portance levels of these targets as its outputs. Given
\par
sufficient learning examples, the neural network can be
\par
built via offline training based on a learning algorithm
\par
such as back propagation. The authors utilized a rather
\par
simple neural network for this purpose as shown in
\par
Fig. 6, where each input is multiplied with the corre-
\par
sponding weight Wi and these products are summed up
\par
yielding a net linear output u. The activation function to
\par
compute target importance levels is of the form:
\par
f\dh u\TH{} {$\frac{1}{4}$} 1=\dh 1\th{} exp\dh bu\TH\TH{} \dh 11\TH{}
\par
with b being a parameter determining the slope of the
\par
function.
\par
However, to create the required training set, an ex-
\par
perienced human operator is needed to evaluate be-
\par
forehand target importance levels with respect to a large
\par
number of various situations. An issue could be whether
\par
it is possible or convenient to collect sufficient examples
\par
for the network training.
\par
In addition, in our view, identifying significant fea-
\par
tures as inputs before neural network modeling could be
\par
advantageous in order to filter out irrelevant features,
\par
