4.3. Evaluation based on goal lattices
\par
A goal-lattice-based methodology was addressed in
\par
[32,48] with the purpose of quantifying the relative
\par
contribution of actual observation tasks in the context
\par
of a complex mission. In doing this, one has to identify
\par
all goals relevant to that mission and then define an
\par
ordering relation among them, leading to the construc-
\par
tion of a partially ordered set. The ordering relationship
\par
used is a precedence ordering induced by the simple
\par
Fig. 6. Neural network for (target) priority assignment.174 N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186
\par
statement ``this goal is necessary in order for the other
\par
goal to be satisfied''. The established partially ordered
\par
set can be represented as a lattice, allowing for straight-
\par
forward apportionment of goal values from higher rel-
\par
atively abstract goals to lower increasingly concrete
\par
goals. At each layer of the lattice, each goal gets a value
\par
as the sum of the values received from the (higher) goals
\par
from which it is included. On the other hand, a goal also
\par
distributes its value among the (lower) goals that it in-
\par
cludes. In particular, the bottom layer of the lattice
\par
comprises the nodes for actual observation tasks with
\par
the apportioned values reflecting their relative impor-
\par
tance to accomplish the mission. A web browser im-
\par
plementation of a goal lattice constructor has recently
\par
been reported in [30].
\par
Anillustrativeexamplewaspresentedin[48]applying
\par
the goal lattice approach to an Air Force mission in an
\par
``in harm's way'' scenario. Seventeen goals relevant to
\par
the ``in harm's way'' situation were identified with as-
\par
sociated interrelations among them as shown in Table 1.
\par
The resulting goal lattice is depicted in Fig. 7, where the
\par
value of each goal is uniformly distributed among its
\par
included goals presuming that all included goals con-
\par
tribute equally to their including goal. The bottom layer
\par
corresponds to three fundamental observation tasks:
\par
tracking, identifying, and searching with importance
\par
degrees of 0.36, 0.46, and 0.18, respectively. As a further
\par
aside, the 17 goals listed in Table 1 constitute a subset of
\par
applicable Air Force Doctrine goals. In publications of
\par
the United States Air Force, totally 90 Air Force mis-
\par
sion goals were defined within the six mission areas
\par
Offensive Counterair, Defensive Counterair, Air Inter-
\par
diction, Battlefield Air Interdiction, Close Air Support,
\par
and Suppression of Enemy Air Defenses. A global goal
