5.1. Which measurement pattern to use
\par
In the case of search, the characteristics of various
\par
measurement patterns include high- or low-bearing
\par
resolution, high- or low-range resolution, direct velocity
\par
measurement (Doppler), or any combination of these
\par
capabilities. A general idea proposed by [31] is to de-
\par
termine the measurement pattern in two steps. The first
\par
step is to down-select from all available measurement
\par
options that can be performed by the sensor system to
\par
the admissible ones, subject to constraints. Then a local
\par
optimization criterion can be applied in the second step
\par
to make a choice from the set of admissible solutions,
\par
maximizing the incidental information value of the
\par
pattern to be performed.
\par
In response to requests of object identification, table
\par
look-up was suggested in [31] for deciding what pattern
\par
of measurement will enable the desired identification.
\par
For instance, if only the type of target is needed, it is
\par
sufficient to use electronic support measures (ESM) to
\par
observe signals emanating from the platform and then
\par
make classification of the target in terms of the elec-
\par
tronic order of battle (EOB). Facing a more detailed
\par
task of hull-to-emitter correlation, however, we would
\par
have to arrange a longer observation period due to
\par
certain characteristics of ESM sensors. Such prior
