3. RL detection. As a machine learning technique, rein-
\par
forcementlearning(RL) attemptstoarriveatoptimal
\par
system performance by trial and error through inter-
\par
actions with the environment. Certainly there may be
\par
severalpossibilitiesofusingRLtechniquestolearnto
\par
conduct target detection tasks. The scheme proposed
\par
in [47] is to employ an RL-detection network for each
\par
cell to evaluate the reward of making a measurement
\par
there. The inputs to the one-cell network include the
\par
probability estimate pk\dh t\TH{} for the cell and the number
\par
of measurements that have been performed therein.
\par
Based on the network's outputs this strategy selects
\par
the cell with the largest predicted reward to receive
\par
the next measurement. At the beginning, the net-
\par
work's weights are set randomly, so that their pre-
\par
dicted reward values are incorrect. But upon taking
\par
a new measurement, one can acquire an updated
\par
probability distribution of the target location using
\par
the Bayesian evidence rule and then calculate the ex-
\par
pected reward value of this sensory action in terms of
\par
a predefined metric. The difference between the ex-
\par
pected reward value and that, which is predicted by
\par
the network, provides useful information for correct-
\par
ing the weights of the network by means of a back-
\par
propagation algorithm.
