6.3. Decision-theoretic approaches to sensor planning
\par
Kristensen [41] treated the problem of choosing
\par
proper sensing actions from a family of candidates as
\par
one of decision-making, and developed a framework
\par
based on Bayesian decision analysis (BDA) for its solu-
\par
tion. It was strongly suggested in his thesis that uncer-
\par
tainty has to be dealt with in sensor planning since the
\par
ultimate purpose of performing sensory actions is to
\par
reduce uncertainty about the external world. On the
\par
other hand, BDA offers a powerful mechanism for
\par
representing and reasoning under uncertainty. The the-
\par
ory of BDA was primarily applied in the area of eco-
\par
nomics to evaluate various actions, e.g., investments,
\par
against each other.
\par
Fig. 9 shows the decision tree which was used in [41]
\par
for sensor action planning, where we can see two
\par
different types of nodes: boxes and circles. A box is a
\par
