\par
optimized via certain non-differentiable optimization
\par
techniques. The advantage of this approximate strategy
\par
is the reduction of computational complexity by de-
\par
composing the whole decision problem into decoupled
\par
sub-problems at the lower level. However, this benefit is
\par
earned atthe cost of acceptinga weak condition that the
\par
average amount of consumed resources does not exceed
\par
that, which is available. It is controversial whether the
\par
proposal of relaxed resource constraint has practical
\par
value, since it allows violation of the original constraint
\par
in quite a few cases.
\par
In addition to dynamic programming, state-based
\par
search methods were discussed in [10] for fully observ-
\par
able and non-observable Markov decision processes. In
\par
particular, it was pointed out that search techniques can
\par
be applied to partially observable problems as well, by
\par
searching the space of belief states [9], for example. The
\par
presented methodology of state-based search may be
\par
worth investigation in the context of sensor allocation,
\par
to achieve potentially more effective and efficient deci-
\par
sions about sensor distributions in real-time.
\par
7. Towards cooperative sensor behavior
\par
A team-theoretic formulation of multi-sensor systems
\par
was presented in [29] with sensors being considered as
\par
members or agents of a team, able to offer opinions and
\par
to bargain in group decision situations. Coordination
\par
and control under this structure was analyzed and dis-
\par
cussedbasedon atheory ofteamdecision-making.Itwas
\par
assumed that a manager/coordinator makes group de-
\par
cisions according to criteria of maximum team utility.
\par
The most general form of team utility is to incorporate
\par
utility measures of both team member and joint team
\par
actions to combine local and global objectives. Alter-
\par
natively, in some cases, the team utility function can
\par
disregard individual team member opinions, thereby
\par
allowing the manager to make optimum decisions re-
\par
gardless of individual team member losses. It is clear,
\par
however, that since a team utility represents group
\par
preference with respect to an underlying problem in a
\par
specific scenario, its definition is situation dependent.
\par
Thus, establishing a comprehensive function for assess-
\par
ing team utility may become difficult if not impossible in
\par
complex applications.
\par
Bowyer and Bogner [12] analyzed the issue of inte-
\par
grating distributed sensor assets based on the concept of
\par
cooperating machines. It was argued that heterogeneous
\par
sensors in a multi-sensor system should be treated as
\par
cooperative members of a community. Here, the concept
\par
of community refers to a plastic association rather than
\par
a rigid connection of sensors, considering the possible
\par
departure of a previously participating sensor as the
\par
situation unfolds. The authors also pointed out that
\par
certain social behavior principles are desired of a sensor
\par
network to foster cooperation between individual
\par
members and to enhance their mutual awareness. Such
\par
considerations led to the proposal of exploiting agent
\par
technology for managing temporal requirements and
\par
implementing the desired social behaviors within a
\par
community of sensors. A multi-agent based architecture
\par
was suggested for this purpose, allowing agents to in-
\par
teract with the sensor system. As indicated by the pro-
\par
posed agent system shown in Fig. 10, the information
\par
from an existing sensor is wrapped up by its local sensor
\par
agent, which then communicates and negotiates with
\par
other agents via a suitable communication medium.
\par
A multi-agent resource management system was also
\par
studied by Luo et al. [45] to support the integration of
\par
data from multiple sensors and associated important
\par
sensory information located in different agents. Two
\par
kinds of agents were involved in this study, with service
\par
agents handling local sensor information and a global
\par
decision agent conducting necessary coordination ac-
\par
tivities to achieve cooperative behavior of the whole
\par
system. An Internet-based network architecture was
\par
used for communications between service agents and the
\par
global decision agent.
\par
Although approaches based on intelligent agents
\par
seem to offer a useful framework to realize autonomous
\par
cooperation between different sensors, few concrete
\par
policies have been reported in the literature answeringFig. 10. A multi-agent based architecture for sensor integration (cited
\par
from [12]).
\par
the question how such agents could coordinate their
\par
decisions in a complex environment. However, for the
\par
simple problem of switching between sensors when
\par
tracking a moving target, a fuzzy controller has been
\par
designed for the cueing process [55] and shown to be
\par
very successful in simulations.
\par
Currently, the Argus project [22] is carried out by the
\par
Australian Centre for Field Robotics in Sydney, to de-
\par
velop a decentralized ground-based sensor network. The
\par
nodes include vision, multi-spectral cameras, lasers, and
\par
(in the future) radars. The Argus network will be used
\par
for both air-target tracking and hand-off of ground
\par
targets from air to land sensors.
\par
8. Conclusion and discussion
\par
This paper highlights the emerging area of multi-
\par
sensor managementfrom the perspectiveof multi-sensor
\par
information fusion. Characteristics of three problem
\par
classes of multi-sensor management are elaborated.
\par
Furthermore, a general perspective on problem solving
\par
for sensor management is presented in terms of certain
\par
methodological considerations and a top-down working
\par
procedure. As a collection of activities controlling in-
\par
formation gathering, the scope of sensor management is
\par
wide and many problems involved seem to be case-
\par
specific. In view of this, this paper discusses the state-
\par
of-art in terms of solutions to specific problems.
\par
Research in sensor management is still in its infancy.
\par
Recent early work on this topic has been largely focused
\par
on sensor scheduling and sensor resource allocation.
\par
With the advances of information fusion systems into
\par
more and more complex environments, an increasing
\par
demand will likely occur for sensor management func-
\par
N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186 183
\par
tions on high levels such as mission planning, sensor
\par
deployment and sensor coordination. Unfortunately,
\par
these issues have not yet been extensively addressed.
\par
Contemplating sensor management requirements on
\par
high levels leaves us with the sense that methods of
\par
qualitative reasoning may be needed as complementary
\par
tools,todealwithabstractbutimpreciseinformation.In
\par
general, highly strategic decisions in sensor management
\par
might require the involvement of human operators, who
\par
are expected to ``discuss'' and ``negotiate'' with the
\par
software for working out intelligent solutions. Usually
\par
human operators tend to express their opinions in the
\par
form of propositions formulated in natural language
\par
and they will also want to receive information as lin-
\par
guistically understandable descriptions. Moreover, tol-
\par
erance of imprecision seems a necessary requirement
\par
when making high-level decisions for achieving tract-
\par
ability, robustness, low computational cost, and better
\par
rapport with the inexact nature of human thinking.
\par
Wefeelthatthetermsensormanagementisbecoming
\par
inappropriate to capture what it really means to cope
\par
with increasingly high-level functionality in the future.
\par
This term was originally put forward to refer mainly to
\par
single sensor scheduling and control. It does not accu-
\par
rately capture the nature of process refinement in the
\par
context of information fusion. Indeed what we are now
\par
talking about is a procedure to manage data acquisition
\par
from the external world driven by information requests.
\par
Whether a new term is necessary or beneficial to better
\par
reflect the gist of this fast developing area is open for
\par
debate and discussion by the information fusion com-
\par
munity.
\par
Future research work can be conducted on several
\par
interesting but under-researched problems, including:
\par
{$\bullet$} Combined sensor placement and sensor selection in
\par
multi-sensor and multi-target applications. So far in
\par
theliteraturethesetwoproblemshavebeenaddressed
\par
in isolation of each other. Some papers discuss sensor
\par
selection for various targets under the assumption of
\par
known fixed sensor locations, whereas other papers
\par
focus on sensor placement in tracking a single target,
\par
so that sensor selection is not required. However, in
\par
real applications of multi-sensor and multi-target
\par
tracking we frequently have to solve these two prob-
\par
lems in combination.
\par
{$\bullet$} Management of movable sensor assets or platforms
\par
such as unmanned air vehicles for surveillance pur-
\par
poses. An interesting problem may be planning
\par
time-constrained movements of sensor(s) to keep
\par
track of moving targets in a dynamic environment.
\par
{$\bullet$} Cooperation between sensors in a decentralized para-
\par
digm. Cooperation is particularly needed for a team
\par
of sensors and platforms that collectively perform
\par
surveillance tasks across a region. Each team member
\par
is expected to make its own decision about where to184 N. Xiong, P. Svensson / Information Fusion 3 (2002) 163--186
\par
go and what to sense. But these decisions must be co-
\par
ordinated with other sensors and platforms to arrive
\par
at a globally optimal perception for the system as a
\par
whole. Further, in the case of failure of one or more
\par
sensors or platforms, the system should be able to
\par
reconfigure the control and coordination of sensor
\par
behaviors.
\par
{$\bullet$} Sensormanagement under multiple objectives. Preva-
\par
lent inmilitaryapplications isthat there maybe other
\par
objectives to be accounted for, such as remaining co-
\par
vert and recognizing enemy spoofing, in addition to
\par
information fusion quality. Sometimes such demands
\par
conflict with each other, requiring on one hand mea-
\par
suring in a highly covert way and on the other hand
\par
getting as much information as possible. In order to
\par
achieve a good balance between different objectives,
\par
an appropriate arbitration mechanism is needed.
\par
{$\bullet$} Adaptive signal filtering with respect to sensor place-
\par
ment. Up to now Kalman filtering and particle filter-
\par
ing are used in sensor placement for target state
\par
estimation. Both techniques have advantages and
\par
weaknesses. An adaptive mechanism is desirable to
\par
decide which filter to use based on the situation and
\par
the mission goal. Moreover, both Kalman and parti-
\par
cle filters give average performance of state estima-
\par
tion. However, in some cases we may want to
\par
ensure the worst-case accuracy, especially in military
\par
applications. This could make it advantageous to use
\par
so-called H1 filtering (indeed Kalman filtering is
\par
also termed H2 filtering). H1 filtering minimizes the
\par
``worst-case'' estimation error and assumes no priori
\par
knowledge of noise statistics [63]. Automatic switch-
\par
ing among the three filters could be valuable for im-
\par
plementation of adaptive signal filtering.
