@article{zimmermanRiskAssessmentExtreme2002,
  title = {Risk Assessment of Extreme Events},
  author = {Zimmerman, Rae and Bier, Vicki M.},
  year = 2002,
  journal = {Risk Management Strategies in an Uncertain World, Columbia-wharton/penn Roundtable Held in Palisades, New York, April},
  volume = {12},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\textbf{RISK ASSESSMENT OF EXTREME EVENTS}
\par
Rae Zimmerman
\par
Professor of Planning and Public Administration and Director, Institute for Civil Infrastructure
\par
Systems (ICIS) at New York University's Robert F. Wagner Graduate School of Public Service
\par
E-mail: Rae.zimmerman@nyu.edu.
\par
Vicki M. Bier
\par
Professor in the Departments of Industrial Engineering and Engineering Physics and Director of
\par
the Center for Human Performance and Risk Analysis at the University of Wisconsin-Madison
\par
E-mail: bier@engr.wisc.edu.
\par
\textbf{I. Introduction: Scope}
\par
Risk assessment is a means to characterize and reduce uncertainty to support our ability to deal
\par
with catastrophe through risk management. This paper addresses the application of risk
\par
assessment to both the built and natural environments, to improve our understanding and
\par
management of human health, safety, and security within those environments, especially in the
\par
context of extreme and unanticipated events.
\par
Modern risk assessment began over three decades ago with applications in the military and
\par
nuclear power, beginning with the Reactor Safety Study (U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission,
\par
1975). In the late 1970s it gradually expanded, and was applied to the vast array of chemical
\par
risks being regulated under dozens of federal environmental statutes. For example, risk
\par
assessment has been used as the foundation for: setting drinking water, ambient water quality,
\par
and air quality standards; review and renewal of pesticide applications; and determining levels of
\par
site cleanup under the Superfund program. Applications to engineered systems, and in particular
\par
infrastructure, are common; examples are given in Moses (1998) and Lave and Balvanyos
\par
(1998). Blockley (1992) also devotes a number of chapters to civil engineering topics (e.g.,
\par
design codes or risk assessment in structural engineering), and several infrastructure-engineering
\par
applications (e.g., dam safety, marine structures).
\par
\textbf{II. What is Risk Assessment?}
\par
Risk assessment connotes ``a systematic approach to organizing and analyzing scientific
\par
knowledge and information for potentially hazardous activities or for substances that might pose
\par
risks under specified circumstances'' (National Research Council 1994: 4). This definition
\par
reflects the flexibility that has been incorporated into the concept over the years since it was first
\par
introduced.
\par
The overall objective of risk assessment is to estimate the level of risk associated with adverse
\par
health and safety effects of stressors or sources of those effects. Health and safety effects at the
\par
most general level encompass both human and ecological health. Stressors are the broad set of
\par
causes, such as chemicals, biological agents, and physical conditions in the built environment
\par
and the physical structures that pervade our everyday life. Between cause and effect are
\par
numerous steps that link the two, and risk assessment identifies and quantifies those steps.
\par
1Although the steps vary by the setting, the sources, the effects, and the concepts are similar at a
\par
generic level.
\par
A. Definitions of Risk
\par
In their seminal paper, Kaplan and Garrick (1981) define ``risk'' as involving ``both uncertainty
\par
and some kind of loss or damage.'' More specifically, Kaplan and Garrick note that ``Risk
\par
includes the likelihood of conversion of that source into actual\dots loss, injury, or some form of
\par
damage.'' Thus, fundamentally, risk depends both on the probability or frequency of an adverse
\par
outcome, and also on the severity of that outcome. Risk has similarly been defined generally as
\par
``the potential for realization of unwanted, negative consequences of an event'' (Rowe 1976: 24).
\par
More quantitatively, Sage and White (1980: 426) define risk as ``the probability per unit time of
\par
the occurrence of a unit cost burden,'' and state that it ``represents the statistical likelihood of a
\par
randomly exposed individual being adversely affected by some hazardous event.'' Thus, risk has
\par
been defined at many different levels of detail.
\par
More debate has occurred over acceptable levels of risk than over the definition itself. The
\par
debate has occurred with respect to the process of deriving, selecting, and applying acceptable
\par
levels of risk, including determination of who makes the decision. As a consequence of those
\par
debates not being addressed up front, the passage of environmental health risk statutes over the
\par
past two decades has spawned a large number of terms to characterize acceptable risk levels.
\par
These terms include: ``adequate''; ``imminent''; ``substantial''; ``reasonable'' vs. ``unreasonable'';
\par
``posing grave danger''; ``at a zero level''; ``significant'' vs. ``de minimus''; and ``an ample or
\par
adequate margin of safety.'' More important than the differences in terminology is that different
\par
statutes can allow different levels of risk for the same risk factor or agent, such as a hazardous
\par
chemical.
\par
B. Relationship of Risk to Other Concepts
\par
The concept of risk is related to a number of other concepts, including threat, hazard,
\par
vulnerability, uncertainty, and variability. In addition, terms related to the degree of risk (such as
\par
``catastrophes,'' ``extreme events,'' ``unexpected events,'' and ``unanticipated events'') are also
\par
common. It is important to sort these out to ensure a full understanding of how they relate to risk
\par
assessment.
\par
A ``hazard'' has been defined as ``An act or phenomenon that has the potential to produce harm or
\par
other undesirable consequences to humans or what they value'' (Stern and Fineberg 1996: 215).
\par
Similarly, Merriam-Webster's Collegiate Dictionary (2002) defines ``hazard'' simply as ``a source
\par
of danger.'' Kaplan and Garrick illustrate the distinction between hazard and risk by considering
\par
the ocean as a hazard, and noting that the degree of risk undertaken in crossing the ocean will
\par
depend on the level of safeguards adopted (e.g., the method of transportation used).
\par
``Threat'' is another concept closely linked to risk. The definition of threat is often expressed in
\par
the context of vulnerability. The National Research Council (2002) defines ``vulnerability'' as
\par
``an error or a weakness in the design, implementation, or operation of a system.'' The National
\par
Research Council goes on to define ``threat'' as ``an adversary that is motivated to exploit a
\par
2system vulnerability and capable of doing so,'' and ``risk'' as ``the likelihood that a vulnerability
\par
will be exploited, or that a threat may become harmful.'' Thus, their definition of risk is limited
\par
to intentional threats, unlike the more general definition given above.
\par
Closely related to the idea of risk are the ideas of extreme, catastrophic, and/or unexpected
\par
events. These are critical in linking risk assessment to risk management, since they are value-
\par
laden terms that reflect human perceptions. Bier et al. (1999) define ``extreme events'' as being
\par
extreme in terms of both their low frequency and high severity. They note that an event may be
\par
rare but not extreme: ``For example, a room temperature of exactly 72.34571º F may occur
\par
extremely rarely, but would hardly seem to be extreme by most people's definitions.'' Similarly,
\par
a ``catastrophe'' is defined (Merriam-Webster Collegiate Dictionary, 2002) as ``a momentous
\par
tragic event ranging from extreme misfortune to utter overthrow or ruin.'' Catastrophic risks are
\par
often distinguished from ``chronic'' risks, defined by Merriam-Webster as ``marked by long
\par
duration or frequent recurrence.'' Thus, for example, asbestosis would generally be considered a
\par
chronic risk, since it develops gradually over a long period of time, and then persists. Finally,
\par
Shackle (1972) distinguishes between ``counterexpected events'' (events that had been identified,
\par
but were believed to be unlikely or impossible) and ``unexpected events'' (which had not even
\par
been anticipated). Unexpected events are obviously a significant challenge for both risk analysis
\par
and risk management.
\par
It is important to clarify the distinction between the concepts of ``uncertainty'' and ``variability.''
\par
In particular, Kaplan (1983) distinguishes between ``state of knowledge'' uncertainty and
\par
``population variability.'' To illustrate, in the context of equipment failure rates, state of
\par
knowledge uncertainty may refer to a lack of knowledge about the average or mean failure rate
\par
across the entire population of similar items of equipment. By contrast, population variability
\par
would refer to differences among the items in the population. Similarly, in health risk
\par
assessment, state of knowledge uncertainty may refer to a lack of knowledge about the average
\par
effect of a particular chemical on health, while population variability would refer to differences
\par
in susceptibility from one person to another. Thus, one can have state of knowledge uncertainty
\par
even without population variability (e.g., if all items or people in a population have the same
\par
unknown risk level), and also population variability without state of knowledge uncertainty (e.g.,
\par
if the risks to different individuals in the population are known but unequal). This distinction is
\par
discussed at length in Bier (2001), Cullen and Frey (1999), Morgan and Henrion (1990), and the
\par
National Research Council (1994). Similarly, in the context of health risk assessment, state of
\par
knowledge uncertainty may refer to lack of knowledge about the average effect of a particular
\par
chemical on human health, while population variability refers to differences in susceptibility to
\par
the chemical from one person to the next.
\par
C. Systems Analysis Paradigms and Frameworks for Risk Assessment
\par
In their initial formulations, risk assessment and risk management were distinctly separate
\par
functions. The so-called ``Red Book'' (National Research Council 1983: 7) clearly distinguishes
\par
risk assessment and management in the context of risk regulation, recommending that:
\par
regulatory agencies take steps to establish and maintain a clear conceptual
\par
distinction between assessment of risks and consideration of risk management
\par
3alternatives; that is, the scientific findings and policy judgments embodied in risk
\par
assessments should be explicitly distinguished from the political, economic, and
\par
technical considerations that influence the design and choice of regulatory
\par
strategies.
\par
The report, however, clearly recommended that the two processes not be placed in separate
\par
agencies.
\par
Over time, however, fields such as environmental sociology and social psychology infused issues
\par
of risk perception and risk communication into risk assessment, profoundly influencing the
\par
nature of risk assessment. In the 1990s, the two processes began to be more formally merged
\par
(this section is drawn from Zimmerman 1998). The National Research Council (NRC)
\par
Committee on Risk Assessment of Hazardous Air Pollutants \emph{Science and Judgment} report in
\par
1994 (National Research Council, 1994: 24), the NRC \emph{Understanding Risk} study (Stern and
\par
Fineberg 1996: 28), the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) strategic plan for its
\par
Office of Research and Development (U.S. EPA May 1996: 3), and the report of the Presidential/
\par
Congressional Commission on Risk Assessment and Risk Management (1997, Volume 2: 7)
\par
each reflect far more interaction between stakeholders and risk assessors than the approach
\par
described in the ``Red Book.'' The manner in which the various risk assessment steps are
\par
integrated with stakeholder and risk management concerns varies, however, among these reports.
\par
Stern and Fineberg (1996) develop this integration still further, with the introduction of concepts
\par
of analysis and deliberation, both of which are seen as part of the fully integrated risk
\par
assessment/risk management paradigm, and viewed as iterative processes:
\par
Analysis, according to Stern and Fineberg (1996: 214) is defined as ``The systematic application
\par
of specific theories and methods, including those from natural science, social science,
\par
engineering, decision science, logic, mathematics, and law, for the purpose of collecting and
\par
interpreting data and drawing conclusions about phenomena,'' and ``uses rigorous, replicable
\par
methods, evaluated under the agreed protocols of an expert community-such as those of
\par
disciplines in the natural, social, or decision sciences, as well as mathematics, logic, and law- to
\par
arrive at answers to factual questions'' (Stern and Fineberg 1996: 4). Conversely, they state:
\par
``Deliberation uses processes such as discussion, reflection, and persuasion to communicate, raise
\par
and collectively consider issues, increase understanding, and arrive at substantive decisions''
\par
(Stern and Fineberg 1996: 20). The combination or linkage of these two steps is termed the
\par
``analytic-deliberative'' process, an iterative process in which deliberation and analysis are
\par
viewed as complementary (Stern and Fineberg 1996: 3), so that ``deliberation frames analysis and
\par
analysis informs deliberation'' (Stern and Fineberg 1996: 20).
\par
To understand how the integration of these processes and the changes in the boundaries between
\par
them have occurred, it is instructive to view risk assessment and risk management from a
\par
systems perspective. From a systems perspective, the boundaries between risk assessment and
\par
risk management are seen to be flexible. For example, interested stakeholders under such a
\par
framework are more fully included in the process, and can provide inputs into the design of a risk
\par
assessment at the very outset; e.g., in the selection of exposure points and health outcomes. In
\par
the aftermath of September 11th, this in fact has been occurring with respect to air quality and its
\par
effect on human health. Residents and workers have voiced strong concern over the extent to
\par
4which the design of the air quality sampling strategy (including the placement of monitors and
\par
the selection of standards used to interpret the data) reflect what they are experiencing. The
\par
events following the destruction of the buildings and their impact on air quality have little
\par
precedent to guide the controversy.
\par
Fundamentally, risk assessment is a form of systems analysis, and thus follows the systems
\par
approach. Haimes (1998; see also Haimes and Schneiter 1996) lays out the steps in the systems
\par
approach\textbf{ (}including the involvement of stakeholders\textbf{)}, as paraphrased below:
\par
1. Identify the problem
\par
2. Determine the objectives and goals of the analysis
\par
3. Become familiar with the total problem environment
\par
4. Study the system being analyzed
\par
5. Develop models (including multiple models, if necessary)
\par
6. Solve the models
\par
7. Identify various feasible solutions to the identified problem
\par
8. Evaluate the costs and benefits of the identified solutions
\par
9. Communicate the solutions to the client
\par
10. Evaluate the impact of current decisions
\par
11. Implement the selected solution
\par
12. Review the analysis
\par
13. Iterate
\par
Kaplan and Garrick (1981) state that the goal of a risk assessment is to answer three questions:
\par
``What can go wrong?'' ``How likely is it that that will happen?'' ``If it does happen, what are the
\par
consequences?'' Thus, strictly speaking, their definition of risk goes through step 7 in the above
\par
list, with the remaining steps falling into the category of ``risk management.'' However, most
\par
risk assessments in practice do include the identification and evaluation of possible risk
\par
reduction measures; that is, risk assessment and risk management are integrated to a much
\par
greater extent than the portrayal of the process as independent steps would lead one to believe.
\par
The general framework presented above is applicable to both health/environmental and
\par
engineering risk assessment. However, some of the details of the methodology differ between
\par
the two areas. Therefore, the next section presents a discussion of risk assessment
\par
methodologies for each of these areas separately.
\par
\textbf{III. Risk Assessment Methodologies for Health and Engineered/Built Systems}
\par
A. Health and Environmental Risk Assessment
\par
The steps in risk assessment formalized in 1983 in the ``Red Book'' include hazard identification,
\par
dose-response assessment, exposure assessment, and risk characterization. The latter three steps
\par
are sometimes referred to as risk estimation, to distinguish them from the more qualitative
\par
process of hazard assessment. Although these generic steps have for the most part remained
\par
unchanged since the early 1980s, and have been applied in some of the major environmental
\par
statutes (National Research Council 1994), their interpretation and integration with risk
\par
5management has undergone dramatic change. This is reflected in the change in the term used in
\par
the 1996 \emph{Understanding Risk} report (Stern and Fineberg 1996), from ``risk assessment'' to ``risk
\par
analysis,'' to address the breakdown between the production of scientific results and their use by
\par
decision makers and managers (Stern and Fineberg 1996: x). Assessment is usually used in a
\par
way that implies quantification of a rather narrow set of things, whereas analysis is a broader
\par
concept that connotes a separation of the whole into its components to obtain new knowledge.
\par
The components of risk assessment (as used in health and environmental risk assessment) are
\par
described below.
\par
Hazard Identification
\par
The objective of hazard identification in the health and environmental area is to determine which
\par
hazards are to be used as the subject of the risk estimation. Hazard identification in the health
\par
and environmental area draws upon methods such as epidemiology (to identify disease clusters),
\par
structure-activity relationships (linking the structure of one chemical to the possible or likely
\par
biological effects of another similar chemical), and short-term tests.
\par
Risk Estimation
\par
A generic equation for the calculation of the risk estimate is as follows:
\par
Risk = Unit Risk Level (derived from dose-response studies) x Exposure Level
\par
Exposure Assessment
\par
Exposure assessment is used to calculate the level of a hazardous substance to which people
\par
(and/or the environment) may be exposed, and is part of consequence assessment. Exposure
\par
assessment typically is comprised of two distinct stages---one having to do with the migration
\par
and fate of contaminants from sources to sinks, and another having to do with the exposures of
\par
individuals. Uncertainty exists at every stage of the assessment, especially where default values
\par
are used (U.S. EPA, 1999); however, few risk assessments quantify the assessment at each stage
\par
to derive a composite uncertainty estimate. A generic equation for estimating exposure levels is
\par
as follows, similar to the ones used in Superfund risk assessments (U.S. EPA, 1989):
\par
Exposure Level = Contaminant Concentration x Contact Rate x Exposure Duration
\par
Body Weight x Lifetime
\par
Dose-Response
\par
Dose-response relationships are the foundation for estimating a risk level for a given organism
\par
and risk agent under specific conditions. For health and environmental data, this is usually
\par
expressed in terms of a dose-response curve; as one increases the dose, the response changes
\par
accordingly. In this step---the construction of dose-response relationships---many uncertainties
\par
appear. For example, the shape of such a curve is easier to derive at higher doses, but at very
\par
low doses, the shape of the curve has to be modeled which introduces considerable uncertainty.
\par
6Risk Characterization
\par
Stern and Fineberg (1996: 216) define risk characterization as ``A synthesis and summary of
\par
information about a hazard that addresses the needs and interests of decision makers and of
\par
interested and affected parties. Risk characterization is a prelude to decision making and
\par
depends on an iterative, analytic-deliberative process.''
\par
B. Engineered Systems
\par
Like health and environmental risk assessment, the process of risk assessment for engineered
\par
systems begins with hazard identification. In the context of engineering risk assessment, this
\par
generally includes system familiarization, and sometimes also qualitative methods such as hazard
\par
and operability studies or failure modes and effects analysis. Hazard identification is followed
\par
by the assessment of accident occurrence frequencies, and consequence analysis (including the
\par
use of dose-response models as appropriate). As noted above with regard to health risk
\par
assessment, uncertainty analysis is an overarching step that encompasses the entire process, since
\par
uncertainties can arise with regard to both accident frequencies and consequences.
\par
Assessment of Accident Occurrence Frequencies
\par
Bier (1997) summarizes the process of probabilistic risk assessment (PRA) for complex
\par
engineered systems. To structure the list of possible scenarios for what can go wrong, PRA
\par
models are generally hierarchical in nature. Two reliability analysis techniques are commonly
\par
used to represent the combinations of component failures that could lead to an accident: fault
\par
trees; and event trees (McCormick 1981). (Reliability block diagrams, which are logically
\par
equivalent to fault trees, are sometimes also used.) Fault trees and event trees are equivalent in
\par
the sense that it is possible to represent the same system or subsystem either way, and both
\par
techniques are useful. However, each technique has different strengths and weaknesses (Pate-
\par
Cornell 1984), and they therefore tend to be used in different contexts.
\par
As noted by Pate-Cornell, fault trees are constructed using inductive or ``backward'' logic. In
\par
other words, the process starts with a hypothesized system or subsystem failure, and works
\par
backwards to identify which combinations of component failures could give rise to that event.
\par
By contrast, event trees are constructed using deductive or ``forward'' logic. In other words,
\par
rather than hypothesizing a system failure, the process starts by hypothesizing an initiating event
\par
(i.e., a departure from normal operations), then identifies all possible combinations of subsequent
\par
events (i.e., successes or failures of particular components or subsystems), and determines which
\par
sequences of events could cause failure of the system as a whole. Figure 1 shows a simplified
\par
event tree representing an initiating event and the subsequent response of four subsystems (A, B,
\par
C, and D). For each subsystem, the upper branch represents success and the lower branch
\par
represents failure. Thus, the event sequence shown in bold consists of the initiating event I,
\par
followed by success of subsystem A, failure of subsystem B, success of subsystem C, and failure
\par
of subsystem D.
\par
The frequency of this scenario, S, can be quantified as a product of terms according to
\par
7F(S) = F(I) P(A\textbar I) P(B{$\prime\vert$}IA) P(C\textbar IAB{$\prime$}) P(D{$\prime\vert$}IAB{$\prime$}C)
\par
where:
\par
F(S) = Frequency of scenario S;
\par
F(I) = Frequency of initiating event I;
\par
P(A\textbar I) = Conditional probability that subsystem A succeeds given that initiating
\par
event I has happened;
\par
P(B{$\prime\vert$}IA) = Conditional probability that subsystem B fails given that initiating event I
\par
has happened and subsystem A has succeeded;
\par
P(C\textbar IAB{$\prime$}) = Conditional probability that subsystem C succeeds given that initiating
\par
event I has happened, subsystem A has succeeded, and subsystem B has
\par
failed; and
\par
P(D{$\prime\vert$}IAB{$\prime$}C) = Conditional probability that subsystem D fails given that initiating event I
\par
has happened, subsystem A has succeeded, subsystem B has failed, and
\par
subsystem C has succeeded.
\par
\emph{Figure 1. }Sample Event Tree
\par
The conditional probabilities P({$\bullet$}) represent the long-run fraction of times that a scenario will
\par
follow a particular branch of the event tree. The conditional nature of these split fractions
\par
reflects the fact that subsystems may be dependent on each other. For example, if subsystem A
\par
provides electric power to subsystem B, then the failure probability of subsystem B will depend
\par
on whether subsystem A has succeeded or failed. This is also true for the initiating event I; for
\par
example, if the initiating event is an earthquake, this may affect the failure probabilities of other
\par
subsystems.
\par
As mentioned above, while both fault trees and event trees are useful, they have different
\par
strengths and weaknesses. In particular, event trees are well suited for displaying the order of
\par
events, and also dependencies between events (e.g., the fact that the failure probability of
\par
subsystem B may depend on the status of subsystem A). Event trees are therefore useful for
\par
8facilitating communication about the assumptions made in the risk model; e.g., for presenting a
\par
risk model to plant staff for review and discussion. However, because combinations of
\par
subsystem successes and failures are shown explicitly, event tree models can rapidly become
\par
extremely large, including literally billions of sequences. Fault trees, by contrast, provide a more
\par
compact way of representing combinations of events, but can obscure dependencies and the
\par
chronological order in which events unfold. Therefore, most PRA's use event trees to structure
\par
the overall risk assessment. Fault trees can then be used to quantify the failure probabilities of
\par
particular systems or subsystems represented in the event tree.
\par
Available data is then used to help quantify the various quantities that appear in the risk model.
\par
These include: initiating event frequencies; component failure rates (including the likelihood of
\par
common cause failure, in which multiple components fail during a short period of time for the
\par
same reason); maintenance frequencies and durations (to reflect the fact that components are
\par
temporarily out of service while they are being maintained); and human error rates. The data
\par
used to support the quantification process can include component-specific information for the
\par
specific items of equipment being considered, generic information (such as failure rates for
\par
similar components), and expert opinion. Success or exposure data is generally needed in
\par
addition to failure data, to provide the denominators for the desired failure rates.
\par
Bayesian statistics (Martz and Waller 1982) is frequently used for data analysis in support of risk
\par
assessments, since Bayesian statistics are well suited to the analysis of sparse data, and provide
\par
probability distributions for the quantities of interest in addition to point estimates (thus
\par
supporting the overarching task of uncertainty analysis). Quantification of the fault and event
\par
tree models using probability distributions for the various input quantities of interest (component
\par
failure rates, etc.) yields a probability distribution describing the uncertainty about the likelihood
\par
of an accident.
\par
Consequence Analysis
\par
For most types of engineered systems, consequence analysis (like exposure assessment for health
\par
risk assessment, as discussed above) typically is comprised of two distinct stages---one having to
\par
do with the migration of any hazardous materials released in the accident from sources to sinks,
\par
and another having to do with the consequences of those materials for public health and safety
\par
(using dose-response relationships as appropriate, as in health risk assessment). Relevant
\par
consequence measures might include: the structural response of a building to an impact or
\par
explosion; the costs associated with property damage, loss of use, and facility repair; the amount
\par
of hazardous material or energy released to the environment as a result of the accident; and the
\par
numbers of fatalities or other health effects.
\par
Risk Characterization
\par
Risk characterization focuses on how information is portrayed for decision-making. Results can
\par
be presented graphically in a variety of formats. For example, probability distributions are often
\par
used to display uncertainty about quantities such as the frequency of an accident (see Figure 2).
\par
For damage types that can take on differing levels of severity (e.g., repair cost, number of
\par
fatalities), a complementary cumulative distribution function can be used to show the frequency
\par
9of exceeding any given damage level X (see Figure 3); uncertainty about such exceedance
\par
frequencies is sometimes represented by showing multiple complementary cumulative
\par
distributions in the same figure.
\par
\emph{Figure 2. }Probability Distribution for Frequency of Severe Damage
\par
\emph{Figure 3. }Complementary Cumulative Distribution
\par
10After the initial risk assessment has been completed, the model can then be used for risk
\par
management purposes---e.g., to identify the dominant contributors to risk, and assess the benefits
\par
of risk reduction options (such as design and procedure changes). The application of PRA has
\par
resulted in a number of examples of successful risk management; a few examples are given in
\par
Garrick (1987).
\par
C. Spatial Dimensions as Organizing Principles for Health and Engineering Risk: The Relevance
\par
of Proximity
\par
Proximity is a key factor primarily in the exposure portion of the risk equation. With respect to
\par
environmental and health applications, distance from a hazard can dilute or otherwise minimize
\par
risk, except if there are unusual amplifying or concentrating effects distant from the origins.
\par
Moreover, even estimated distances will depend upon how data on the location of human
\par
receptors and sources are defined. Despite the importance of spatial relationships, however, risk
\par
analyses rarely introduce sophisticated spatial concepts.
\par
For example, engineering risk assessments do sometimes include spatial interactions analysis
\par
(Liming and Bennett 1994)---e.g., to assess how the failure of one component can affect the
\par
performance of other nearby components. However, many standard risk assessment references,
\par
such as Kumamoto and Henley (1996) and Modarres et al. (1999), do not even mention spatial
\par
interactions analysis explicitly. Even when risk assessments do include this type of analysis, the
\par
methodology for doing so tends to be relatively ad hoc, and has so far taken little or no
\par
advantage of the more sophisticated types of information that could be available using modern
\par
geographic information systems (GIS) and related technology.
\par
Similarly, the proximity of a damaged or contaminated area to other important facilities could
\par
affect the perceived severity of particular scenarios. For example, if damage can occur at any of
\par
several locations, different facilities and exposed populations could be affected. The distribution
\par
of social and economic consequences can also vary considerably depending on the spatial
\par
distribution of impacts. This raises questions about geographical and environmental equity; for
\par
example, there has been a lot of dispute about how to specify spatial parameters, given the
\par
implication of the methods of specification for equity (Zimmerman 1994b).
\par
Understanding and representing the spatial relationships among the various components of the
\par
system can dramatically alter the structure of the risk assessment model. It will also affect the
\par
conditional probabilities in the model, since failure probabilities in general are spatially
\par
dependent.
\par
\textbf{IV. Understanding Uncertainty}
\par
Uncertainty and risk are interconnected. Without uncertainty, risk loses much of its meaning.
\par
Estimates of risk resulting from risk assessments (especially for extreme or catastrophic events)
\par
will typically be highly uncertain, due in part to the sparse nature of the data on such events.
\par
Therefore, as indicated at the outset of this paper, uncertainty is central to the idea of risk. In
\par
fact, it is the uncertain and probabilistic nature of risk that requires risk assessment
\par
methodologies in the first place. As a result, uncertainty analysis (i.e., the mathematical methods
\par
11for propagating uncertainties through risk models to quantify the uncertainty about the outcome
\par
quantity of interest; Bier 1987) is a significant part of risk analysis, and decision-making under
\par
uncertainty (the theory of how to take those uncertainties into account in an optimal manner in
\par
decisions) is an important part of risk management.
\par
Morgan and Henrion (1990) present what is probably the best available taxonomy of types of
\par
uncertainty. In particular, they note that uncertainty can arise from:
\par
{$\bullet$} Statistical variation (e.g., ``random error in direct measurements of a quantity'');
\par
{$\bullet$} Systematic error (e.g., ``biases in the measuring apparatus and experimental
\par
procedure'');
\par
{$\bullet$} Subjective judgment (e.g., for quantities where empirical data is largely
\par
unavailable);
\par
{$\bullet$} Linguistic imprecision (e.g., translation of verbal phrases into numerical
\par
probabilities);
\par
{$\bullet$} Variability (e.g., quantities that vary over time or space, or from one person to
\par
another);
\par
{$\bullet$} Inherent randomness or unpredictability (which cannot be reduced by further
\par
research);
\par
{$\bullet$} Disagreement (e.g., among multiple experts);
\par
{$\bullet$} Approximation (e.g., due to limits in the spatial resolution of a model); and
\par
{$\bullet$} Uncertainty about the most appropriate model to represent some phenomenon.
\par
The problem of decision-making under uncertainty is addressed by standard decision theory (see
\par
for example Raiffa 1968; Clemen 1991). Decision theory provides guidance not only on how to
\par
select the best of several uncertain options, but also on ``the value of information''---i.e., whether
\par
it is preferable to make a final decision, or gather more information and defer the decision until
\par
that information becomes available. Thus, one important use of risk assessments (especially
\par
those that include an explicit statement of uncertainties) is to assist in determining whether
\par
additional research is needed.
\par
State-of-knowledge uncertainty and population variability have different implications for
\par
decision-making. Thus, the National Research Council (1994) states: ``uncertainty forces
\par
decision makers to judge how probable it is that risks will be overestimated or underestimated
\par
for every member of the exposed population, whereas variability forces them to cope with the
\par
certainty that different individuals will be subjected to risks both above and below any reference
\par
point one chooses.'' For example, if the various plants or facilities in a population differ a great
\par
deal from each other (i.e., high population variability), but the risk at each one is accurately
\par
known (i.e., low state of knowledge uncertainty), it makes sense to target risk reduction efforts at
\par
the facilities with the highest estimated risks. By contrast, when the plants or facilities in a
\par
population have relatively similar risk estimates (i.e., low variability), but those estimates are all
\par
quite uncertain, then the facility with the highest risk estimate may not actually have the highest
\par
risk. In this case, it may be better to spread one's risk reduction efforts across the entire
\par
population, to protect against the possibility that risks have been misestimated and a high-risk
\par
facility has been neglected. Similarly, in human health risk assessment with large population
\par
variability, it makes sense to target protection measures primarily aimed at the most vulnerable
\par
12sub-populations. Large state of knowledge uncertainty suggests that further research may be
\par
desirable before making a decision on how or whether to implement risk reduction actions.
\par
\textbf{V. The Challenges of Predicting Human Perceptions, Behavior, and Performance in}
\par
\textbf{Extreme Events for Risk Assessment}
\par
The World Trade Center disaster and others that have been studied in the risk literature point out
\par
the central role of human performance in risk assessment. This shows up in two crucial areas---
\par
the importance of human behavior in responding to an event (e.g., in evacuations), and the
\par
challenges of assessing and managing intentional (as opposed to natural or accidental) hazards.
\par
Analyzing human behavior poses unique difficulties for risk analysis.
\par
A. Predicting Human Response to Extreme Events
\par
With regard to human response to severe events, evacuation responses in actual emergencies
\par
differ substantially from ideal performance in evacuation tests and simulations (see for example
\par
Turner and Pidgeon 1997; Fahy and Proux 1997). Similarly, it is known that the behavioral
\par
assumptions underlying many building codes and disaster-response strategies are flawed (Pauls
\par
1998; Sime 1999). For instance, building codes regulate the distance to the nearest exit, even
\par
though people often use more distant but familiar exits (Sime 1999). Similarly, Turner and
\par
Pidgeon (1997) discuss the Summerland fire in 1973, noting: ``the stereotyped view of the public
\par
and its likely behaviour in the case of a fire ignored those parents who were separated from their
\par
children\dots on another floor.'' Recent research results (e.g., Peponis et al., 1990; Willham 1992)
\par
provide the start of a more scientific understanding of how people move within buildings, but
\par
will need to be extended to provide a reliable basis for predicting human response.
\par
Note also that human behavior is extremely variable. For example, Brannigan and colleagues
\par
(1998, 2001) have pointed out that a building designed to ensure safe evacuation of hypothetical
\par
``average healthy individuals'' may be not adequate to allow evacuation of people who are
\par
elderly, ill, or disabled. Thus, even a building that had been ``shown'' to be safe based on a risk
\par
assessment could still become the site of a major disaster if a fire or other emergency took place
\par
at a time when individuals with characteristics different than those assumed in the assessment
\par
were in the building.
\par
Familiarity with a particular environment can also vary widely, with possible adverse
\par
consequences in the event of an emergency. For example, Turner and Pidgeon (1997) cite the
\par
Hixon rail accident (which killed 11 people in 1968) and the Summerland fire (which killed 50
\par
people in 1973), both in the United Kingdom, as examples in which either the likelihood or the
\par
consequences of the event were greatly exacerbated by the presence of ``strangers'' who were not
\par
familiar with the environment. In the case of the Hixon rail accident, truckers driving a slow-
\par
moving vehicle were not familiar with the appropriate procedures to use at a new rail crossing.
\par
In the case of the Summerland fire, the fact that the building was a recreational center used by
\par
visitors who were not familiar with the building and its evacuation routes posed a problem in the
\par
evacuation. Turner and Pidgeon (1997) conclude, ``The Summerland fire highlights the special
\par
problems posed for those responsible for hazardous situations where safe operation relies to
\par
some extent upon the safe behaviour of strangers.''
\par
13Historically, risk assessment had been viewed as much more applicable to errors of omission
\par
(e.g., neglecting to perform a step in a well-defined procedure) than to errors of commission
\par
(e.g., deliberately undertaking an action not specified in procedures); see for example Bier
\par
(1999). More recently, it has been recognized that even errors of commission are generally
\par
``rational actions'' governed by an individual's perception of the situation at hand (Julius et al.
\par
1995; Hsueh and Mosleh, 1996). This recognition makes errors of commission more amenable
\par
to assessment than was previously thought, and there have by now been several pilot studies
\par
incorporating errors of commission into risk assessments (see for example Julius et al. 1995).
\par
However, even those methods to date have focused primarily on predicting the behavior of well-
\par
trained operators in occupational settings---e.g., anesthesiologists (Pate-Cornell et al., 1996),
\par
nuclear power plant control room operators (Julius et al. 1995; Hsueh and Mosleh 1996). The
\par
emphasis on well-trained professionals substantially restricts the range of possible behavior that
\par
must be anticipated. Predicting the behavior and capabilities of members of the lay public, under
\par
a wide variety of possible emergency circumstances for which they may not be trained or
\par
prepared, will be a much more difficult challenge (Brannigan and Smidts 1998; Brannigan et al.
\par
2001).
\par
B. Intentional versus Unintentional Hazards
\par
Predicting human behavior in emergency situations is already difficult. However, in attempting
\par
to estimate and manage the risks of intentional attacks, further difficulties become apparent.
\par
First, as pointed out by Woo (2002), ``some idea of event likelihood is needed for intelligent
\par
benefit-cost analysis.'' However, estimating the likelihood and nature of intentional attacks is an
\par
area with which most risk assessors are not yet familiar, although there has been some related
\par
work on this problem in other fields. For example, Dickey (1980) interviewed bank robbers in
\par
custody to understand the criteria that they used in choosing banks to rob; he found that robbers
\par
preferred banks located near major highways, and banks with a single point in the lobby from
\par
which the robber could see all of the employees at once. Similarly, Crowe (1991) and de Becker
\par
(1997) report that criminals choose targets based not only on the attractiveness of the target, but
\par
also on the likelihood that they would be discovered and apprehended. Interviews with
\par
incarcerated terrorists could presumably be used to explore the criteria they use in selecting
\par
targets, to factor into quantitative risk assessments.
\par
More significantly, protection from a knowledgeable and adaptable adversary is a fundamentally
\par
different challenge than protection against accidents or acts of nature. For example, earthquakes
\par
do not get stronger or smarter just because we have defended our buildings against them.
\par
However, if one's adversaries know or can easily learn about one's defensive measures, then
\par
they can actively choose to either bypass or circumvent those defenses. Progress in and
\par
increased reliance upon detection technologies has made this a more important possibility to take
\par
into account. For example, metal-screening devices had prior to September 11th increased the
\par
security and safety of air travel. A network news report early in 2002 suggested that the box
\par
cutters used by the terrorists on September 11th to gain control of the hijacked airplanes are said
\par
to have fallen just below the detection settings of such screening devices.
\par
As noted by Dresher (1961), optimal allocation of defensive resources requires that ``each of the
\par
defended targets yield the same payoff to the attacker.'' Thus, even if some components can be
\par
14hardened quite inexpensively, focusing protective investments only on those can lead to wasted
\par
resources, if adversaries instead choose to attack targets that cannot be hardened as cost-
\par
effectively. In other words, critical assets must be defended against all possible attacks, which is
\par
much more difficult than just shoring up a few ``weak links.'' As a result, Ravid (2001) has
\par
concluded that security improvements are generally more costly than safety improvements:
\par
``investment in defensive measures, unlike investment in safety measures, saves a lower number
\par
of lives (or other sort of damages) than the apparent direct contribution of those measures.''
\par
For the same reason, many defensive measures may be much less effective if they are widely
\par
known (or can be easily discovered by adversaries), since adversaries can then either design
\par
attacks to overcome those defenses, or else shift to less well-defended targets. However, due to
\par
the openness of our society, it may be quite difficult to keep some types of protective measures
\par
secret, since members of the public may demand to know how critical facilities are defended.
\par
This further increases the difficulty of defending high-profile targets that are of significant public
\par
concern. As just one example, the proposal to sterilize mail to protect against future anthrax
\par
attacks might be an effective defense if the installation of sterilization equipment could be kept
\par
secret. However, this would probably not be possible in our open society, especially given the
\par
outcry of public concern about the threat of anthrax spores being sent through the mail. If
\par
installation of anthrax sterilization equipment is public knowledge, it will likely only cause
\par
future attackers to find a different (and possibly more effective) means of delivery instead of the
\par
public mail. In that case, the proposed \$40 million of sterilization equipment may never sterilize
\par
a single anthrax spore!
\par
Similarly, the optimal strategy for reducing vulnerability to intentional hazards may depend
\par
critically on the assumptions made regarding attacker behavior and motivations. Consider the
\par
question of what attackers decide to do when a few ``signature'' buildings have been fortified to
\par
protect them against prospective attacks. If attackers simply move on to target progressively less
\par
well defended buildings, then expensive methods of protecting signature buildings may not
\par
actually be effective at protecting the public health and safety. Kunreuther and Heal (2002) point
\par
out a similar problem with installation of burglar alarms for theft protection, in which installation
\par
of an alarm at one house increases the risk of burglary at neighboring homes.
\par
Thus, methods that are so expensive that they can be employed only in those few buildings that
\par
are perceived to be most ``at risk'' will protect people and assets in those particular buildings, but
\par
may yield little or no overall public health and safety benefit if attackers simply target other
\par
buildings. In that case, only methods that are inexpensive enough for widespread application,
\par
and/or mobile (such as emergency response capabilities), will substantially reduce overall levels
\par
of societal risk. By contrast, if attackers are interested only in the few highest-profile targets,
\par
then expensive methods of protecting those few targets may be potentially worthwhile from a
\par
societal as well as a private point of view. The fact that assumptions regarding attacker behavior
\par
and motivations are important in determining the expected effectiveness of protective measures
\par
illustrates the need for relevant social science research.
\par
15\textbf{VI. Implications of the World Trade Center and Other Disasters for Learning from the}
\par
\textbf{Past}
\par
One challenge of the World Trade Center disaster is that it had been either unexpected or
\par
counterexpected. Thus, some risk management mechanisms that could have either reduced the
\par
likelihood of the event or minimized its consequences were not in place. As just one illustration,
\par
the insurance policy covering the World Trade Center had not anticipated that more than one
\par
building could be destroyed in a single event. As a result of the failure to anticipate this
\par
possibility and draft contract language to cover it, there was a significant dispute about whether
\par
the building owner was entitled to collect insurance on the destruction of the second building
\par
(SR International Business Insurance Co. Ltd. 2001). On the other hand, the configuration of
\par
many of the utility networks and their density, given the overall density of the area, provided the
\par
kind of resiliency, whether expected or not, to rebound relatively quickly (Zimmerman 2001).
\par
Since September 11th, a number of past experiences have been put forth that could perhaps have
\par
helped to identify the risk of such an attack, or could help predict the likelihood of such events in
\par
the future. For example, Barnett (2001) states:
\par
After the disaster, it was widely (and truthfully) reported from the aviation community
\par
that no one there imagined an event like the one that happened. But could that
\par
circumstance reflect a failure of our imaginations?
\par
There were, after all, lots of events that could be interpreted as precursors of the calamity.
\par
In 1994, terrorists tried to hijack an Air France jet in Algiers, with the aim of using the
\par
plane to destroy the Eiffel Tower. (The plan was thwarted, but not without three
\par
passenger fatalities.) A disgruntled FedEx employee tried in 1998 to hijack one of the
\par
company's planes, intending to crash it into the company's Memphis headquarters.
\par
Hijackers had caused disasters on Ethiopian Airlines (1996) and in Mainland China
\par
(1994) that resulted in triple-digit death tolls (including themselves). Suicidal pilots
\par
apparently crashed two crowded jets in the late 1990s, with no survivors. And terrorists
\par
plotted in 1995 to destroy a dozen U.S. jets coming home from Asia. Their plans were
\par
foiled literally at the last minute: Having successfully exploded a small test bomb on a
\par
Philippine Airlines plane, some of them were in line to board an U.S.-bound jet in
\par
Bangkok when the conspiracy was uncovered.
\par
Thus, all the elements of the Sept. 11 catastrophe---the idea of using planes as weapons,
\par
suicidal individuals in the cockpit, and a willingness to take thousands of innocent
\par
lives---had historical precedent.
\par
In addition to the examples cited by Barnett, a B-25 bomber crashed into the Empire State
\par
Building in 1945, resulting in the death of 14 people and many more injured (Mandell 2001), and
\par
a helicopter hit the Pan Am building (now the Met Life building) in 1977, resulting in five
\par
fatalities (Horsley 2002). However, both events were accidents, not acts of terrorism. The
\par
planes were also smaller, traveling at much lower speeds than what have now been estimated as
\par
the speeds of the airplanes that attacked the World Trade Center towers, and probably not loaded
\par
with as much fuel. Perhaps as a result, the structures were more resilient to the levels of impact
\par
16that they experienced. Yet another past analogy that has been suggested is the attack on Pearl
\par
Harbor, the only previous attack on U.S. soil within the last hundred years.
\par
Perhaps the most direct analogy that has been cited is the bombing of the World Trade Center on
\par
February 26, 1993. The 1993 bombing of the World Trade Center led to a number of risk
\par
reduction measures dealing with various aspects of disaster management. These included the
\par
establishment of the Urban Search and Rescue teams in the mid-1990s, and the establishment of
\par
the Center for National Response that opened in December 2000, which includes training in a
\par
collapsed subway tunnel (Gittrich 2002) to increase the likelihood that responders will be able to
\par
find victims, and thus to lower the risk of death immediately following catastrophic events.
\par
For natural hazards, similar examples exist where the occurrence of a catastrophic event could
\par
perhaps have been predicted from events immediately preceding it. For example, the severity of
\par
the Midwest floods in 1993 could have been anticipated from the gradual buildup of soil
\par
moisture to the point of saturation in the months preceding the floods (see references cited in
\par
Zimmerman 1994a).
\par
While it would of course be a mistake to demand perfect foresight on the part of risk managers,
\par
the fact that past experience foreshadowed the possibility of the World Trade Center and other
\par
disasters points out the need for good methods of learning from past experience. March et al.
\par
(1991) discuss numerous strategies for ``learning from samples of one or fewer,'' which is often
\par
necessary in order to successfully avoid severe events. The general strategies they suggest
\par
include ways of viewing past history more ``richly'' (to extract as much information as possible
\par
from unique events), and using near misses (such as those described above) and hypothesized
\par
scenarios to enrich the available database.
\par
Collection, reporting, and analysis of data on near misses or ``precursors'' to severe events (Bier
\par
1998) are important steps to facilitate learning from past experience. For example, van der
\par
Schaaf (1992) notes that reporting of near misses is valuable for several purposes: (1) ``to gain
\par
qualitative insight into how (small) failures or errors develop into near misses'' or more serious
\par
events; (2) ``to arrive at a statistically reliable quantitative insight into the occurrence of factors
\par
or combinations of factors giving rise to incidents''; and (3) ``to maintain a certain level of
\par
alertness to danger'' and avoid complacency. For that reason, William Corcoran, a root cause
\par
analysis consultant, recommends that root cause analyses of undesired events should always
\par
include an analysis of how the observed event could have been made worse than it was.
\par
The analysis of NASA's response to precursors to the Challenger disaster by Vaughan (1996)
\par
also shows the importance of being vigilant to incipient signs of problems, rather than ignoring
\par
or rationalizing them and minimizing the attendant dangers. Thus, for example, Turner and
\par
Pidgeon (1997) note that some past disasters occurred in part because warnings of trouble were
\par
dismissed as coming from ``outsiders''---e.g., members of the lay public. Similarly, sociologist
\par
Ron Westrum notes that ``generative'' organizations actively seek out information, regardless of
\par
its source, rather than dismissing or compartmentalizing it.
\par
In connecting information from prior events more closely to the process of risk assessment, it is
\par
helpful to look at the occurrence frequency and consequence analysis components of risk
\par
17assessment separately. As described earlier, risk is a combination of the probability of
\par
occurrence of an event and the severity of the consequences. Uncertainty reduction depends on
\par
the extent to which we can predict both occurrences and consequences, and the use of past
\par
experiences is one basis for such predictions.
\par
Methods of precursor analysis (Bier 1998) provide one strategy for using prior event information
\par
in estimating the frequencies of extreme events. Other methods have been discussed in Bier et
\par
al. (1999).
\par
While perhaps less well recognized, analogies from prior events are also needed to estimate
\par
health and safety consequences, just as much as to estimate occurrence frequencies, since
\par
modeling or predicting the consequences of an event requires that we have some basis for
\par
predicting the particular conditions that are likely to occur afterwards. Thus, as New York City
\par
moved away from the initial crisis of survival at the World Trade Center site, there have been
\par
burgeoning concerns over air quality and health, and the search for precedents or analogies to
\par
help address this issue has been intense. In particular, concentrated exposure to particulate
\par
matter was a key consequence of the collapse of the buildings. Here some of the intense
\par
exposures to particulates that occurred as a result of natural disasters are instructive. The
\par
eruption of Mount St. Helens, for example, might be an important model for understanding the
\par
nature of the particulate releases and exposures following the collapse of the World Trade Center
\par
buildings. The understanding of how particulate matter behaves and its effects on human health
\par
also benefited considerably from at least two major documents that had been completed just prior
\par
to September 11th: the Criteria Document for Particulate Matter prepared under the Clean Air
\par
Act (U.S. EPA, ORD, October 1999); and a series of three volumes produced by the National
\par
Research Council entitled \emph{Research Priorities for Airborne Particulate Matter} (National
\par
Research Council 1998, 1999, and 2001). However, regardless of the ability to predict
\par
environmental and health consequences from previous events and the gains from previous
\par
scientific knowledge, the extent to which exposure assessments can reflect the range of
\par
individual circumstances and the extent to which preexisting standards are adequate in light of
\par
previously unanticipated exposures have become serious questions.
\par
\textbf{VII. Conclusions Regarding the Relationship of Risk Assessment to Extreme Events}
\par
Risk assessment in its current form (as a systems-oriented method that is flexible enough to
\par
handle a variety of alternative conditions) is a vital tool for dealing with extreme events, for both
\par
health and the built environment. However, the capabilities of traditional risk assessment are
\par
challenged when we attempt to apply it to extreme and unanticipated events of the nature and
\par
magnitude of the September 11th disaster (Bier et al. 1999).
\par
For example, events of extreme, unexpected, and catastrophic proportions, such as the
\par
destruction of the World Trade Center, point out the need for methodological improvements in
\par
risk assessment to improve and more fully incorporate spatial dimensions. Spatial dimensions
\par
directly relate the risk to the exposed populations, and are critical since such relationships can
\par
dramatically alter the consequences of an event. Another aspect of the spatial dimension is the
\par
interconnections and interdependencies among support systems that must be taken into account
\par
in risk assessment, since these relationships can also dramatically affect event consequences.
\par
18Another challenge to risk assessment is the fact that many human considerations---values,
\par
attitudes, beliefs, and behavior---are critical inputs throughout a risk assessment and must be part
\par
of its formulation, not just add-ons.
